## One name→frame-slot entry: the variable's source name span + its slot index (the slot
## lives at `-(off + 1) * 8(%rbp)`). For a **struct** local the entry also records the
## struct's TYPE name span (`sns`/`snl`; 0/0 = a scalar local) — `off` is the struct's base
## slot and the `nfields` field slots occupy `off .. off+nfields` contiguously, so `p.f`
## reads `-(off + field_index + 1) * 8(%rbp)`. The slot map for a function is a
## `Vec(SlotEntry)` built over the params then the body's `Assign` locals; a `Var` resolves
## by content (its span vs each entry's span — last binding wins, mirroring nameres's env).
##
## STRUCT LAYOUT SIMPLIFICATION (struct tier): every field is treated as 8 bytes
## (a word — u64/usize/ptr); field `i` is at byte offset `i * 8` and the struct size is
## `nfields * 8`. The self-host compiler's own structs are essentially all word-sized fields,
## so sub-word packing is intentionally deferred (no `u8`/`bool` field packing yet).
##
## ENUM LAYOUT SIMPLIFICATION (enum tier): an enum value is a discriminant word
## (the variant's declaration index 0,1,2,…) at word 0 followed by `max_arity` payload words
## (the max payload arity over the enum's variants), so size = (1 + max_arity) * 8. Each
## payload word-sized; no sub-word packing. For an enum local `ek == 3`, `sns`/`snl` carry
## the enum TYPE name span (so `match` can resolve a variant name to its discriminant index)
## and `off` is the base slot — word 0 (discriminant) at `off`, payload word `i` at `off+1+i`.
## `ek`: 0 a scalar local, 2 a struct local, 3 an enum local, 4 a str local, 5 an array local.
##
## ARRAY-OF-AGGREGATE (multi-word-element tier): an array local (`ek == 5`) records
## its ELEMENT STRIDE in words (`estride`) and the element kind (`eek`: 0 a word-sized scalar
## element, 2 a struct element, 3 an enum element). Byte-packed local arrays use eek 8 (`u8`),
## 10 (`i8`), or 11 (`bits8`) and keep the same static length metadata while reserving
## `ceil(N / 8)` frame words. For an aggregate-element array, `sns`/`snl`
## carry the element TYPE name span (so `a[i].f` resolves the field index and a whole-element
## copy knows the element width). A word-sized array keeps `estride = 1`, `eek = 0` (the
## pre-existing case — byte-identical behavior); for such a SCALAR (eek 0) / FLOAT (eek 9)
## element array `sns`/`snl` carry no type span (structural), so `snl` instead records the
## element COUNT `nel` (a static length has no runtime word), letting `for x in <array>` bound
## its counted loop. Element `i` lives at element-0's address MINUS
## `i * estride * 8` (the frame grows DOWN); within an element, field `f` is at the element
## address MINUS `f * 8` (matching the struct field layout's down-growing convention).
## `is_ref` marks a BY-REFERENCE aggregate parameter (aggregate-param tier): the
## slot holds a POINTER to word 0 of the caller's aggregate (a struct/array/enum passed by
## reference — the real Alatyr ABI for an `in`/`in out` aggregate param), NOT the aggregate's
## own words. So a field/element access on such an entry must first LOAD the pointer from the
## slot (`movq -(off+1)*8(%rbp), %reg`) and then add the field/element byte offset to it, rather
## than reading the frame slot directly. An ordinary local has `is_ref = false` (its slot IS the
## aggregate's word 0 — `leaq` its frame address). The pointer word is one slot; the caller
## passes the address (a local's word-0 frame address via `leaq`, or another by-ref param's
## pointer threaded through). See `emit_agg_base_addr` / `emit_fn`'s param binding.
pub SlotEntry := struct { ns : usize, nl : usize, off : usize, sns : usize, snl : usize, ek : u8, estride : usize, eek : u8, is_ref : bool, tmod_s : usize, tmod_l : usize }

## `SVec` — a `SlotEntry` vector backed by a plain bump arena (the lean replacement for
## `alloc::SVec` fixpoint). The self-host lower cannot compile the
## generic `alloc::vec` (its `push` uses `allocate`/`get` + a generic element store), so the
## per-function slot map lives here instead. `base` is the ABSOLUTE address of the element
## region, `arena` is the owning bump arena (carried so `svec_push` can grow). Growth doubles
## `cap` and copies the live region WORD by word (a whole-region copy through pointers is not
## lowered, and a `u8` byte copy lowers as a WORD op under the self-host lower — so the copy
## must be word-granular and the element stride word-aligned).
## The element stride (size of one `SlotEntry`, rounded UP to a word) so the region is a whole
## number of words — required for the word-granular growth copy.
svec_stride := fn() -> usize {
  sz := size(SlotEntry)
  r := sz % 8
  if r == 0 { return sz }
  return sz + (8 - r)
}
## An IN-OUT constructor (NOT a by-value return): `SVec` is 4 words {base,len,cap,arena} and
## the self-host lower's struct return rides ≤3 registers (%rax:%rdx:%rcx) — a 4-word return
## falls to the scalar path and delivers a placeholder 0 base (the `ivec_new` fix #8 shape).
## Caller: `mut v : SVec = SVec(base=0,len=0,cap=0,arena=a); svec_new(v, a, cap)`.
## HEADROOM `pad` (= stride-8) is reserved BEFORE `base`. A SlotEntry is read/written DOWN-growing
## (word k at `entry_ptr - k*8`) by the self-host lower, while `svec_at` returns the entry's LOW end
## (`base + i*stride`) — so entry 0's words run down to `base-(8*8)`, BELOW `base`. Without the pad
## those words aliased the arena region before the block, so param 0's nl/off read back as 0 and
## `slot_of(param0)` failed for every function (the §1 fixpoint's dominant divergence). The pad
## keeps entry 0's down-words inside the allocation. The Rust Stage-0 lower reads the SAME `svec_at`
## UP-growing (its aggregate layout), so it stays in the main block and ignores the pad — both
## in-bounds. The growth copy moves the whole `[base-pad, base+len*stride)` union so neither the
## DOWN headroom words nor the UP top words are lost.
svec_new := fn(in out v : SVec, a : ptr(mut rt::Arena), cap : usize) {
  pad := svec_stride() - 8
  off := node_alloc(deref(a), cap * svec_stride() + pad)
  base_abs := unchecked bitcast(usize, deref(a).base) + off + pad
  v.base = base_abs
  v.len = 0
  v.cap = cap
  v.arena = a
}
svec_push := fn(in out v : SVec, x : SlotEntry) {
  st := svec_stride()
  pad := st - 8
  if v.len >= v.cap {
    new_cap := v.cap * 2
    noff := node_alloc(deref(v.arena), new_cap * st + pad)
    nbase := arena_base(v.arena) + noff + pad
    nwords := (v.len * st + pad) / 8
    mut k := 0
    while k < nwords {
      sp : ptr(mut usize) = unchecked bitcast(ptr(mut usize), v.base - pad + k * 8)
      dp : ptr(mut usize) = unchecked bitcast(ptr(mut usize), nbase - pad + k * 8)
      deref(dp) = deref(sp)
      k += 1
    }
    v.base = nbase
    v.cap = new_cap
  }
  p : ptr(mut SlotEntry) = unchecked bitcast(ptr(mut SlotEntry), v.base + v.len * st)
  deref(p) = x
  v.len = v.len + 1
}
## A typed POINTER to slot entry `i` (NOT a by-value `SlotEntry` — that is a 9-word struct, and
## the ≤3-register struct-return convention delivers only words 0-2 (ns/nl/off), leaving sns/snl/
## ek/is_ref GARBAGE; the same wide-return defect fixed for `IVec`/`SVec`). Generic over `T : type`
## so a call site `deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, v, i))` carries the pointee in arg 0 — `call_first_struct_
## span`/`call_ret_ptrstruct_span` (the generic node_ptr/decl_at machinery) then resolve it and the
## deref copies all 9 words into a real struct local. `T` is always `SlotEntry`; the type-arg is the
## carrier, not a real generalization.
svec_at := fn(T : type, v : ptr(SVec), i : usize) -> ptr(mut T) {
  return unchecked bitcast(ptr(mut T), deref(v).base + i * svec_stride())
}
svec_len := fn(v : ptr(SVec)) -> usize { return deref(v).len }
## Drop the vector back to `n` live elements (the scoped-reservation restore: save `len`, push
## temp slots for a sub-expression, truncate back). Backing storage is reclaimed in bulk with the
## arena; truncation just resets the cursor. Mirrors `alloc::vec::truncate`.
svec_truncate := fn(in out v : SVec, n : usize) { v.len = n }

## The per-function lowering context, passed **by pointer** through the emit recursion: the
## source base (for `Var` / field name-span reads), the name→slot map, and the program's
## decl vector (so a struct field name resolves to its declaration-order index via the
## struct's `FieldDecl` list). Bundling them keeps the emit signatures within the
## argument-register budget (the two `in out` params — the output buffer and the fresh-label
## counter — must land in argument registers, so the ordinary inputs are kept to a pointer).
## `epi` is the fresh label number of the function's EPILOGUE — an early `return e` lowers
## the value into %rax then `jmp`s here (the epilogue restores %rsp/%rbp and `ret`s). The
## trailing return expression falls through to it. It is folded into `LCtx` (not a separate
## `emit_stmts` parameter) to stay within the argument-register budget — the two `in out`
## params (the output buffer + the fresh-label counter) must land in registers.
## `ret_enum` is true when the enclosing function declares an ENUM return type — its return
## value (the trailing return expr or an early `return e`, and the `?` failure path) is then
## delivered via the two-register enum convention (`emit_enum_value` → %rax disc / %rdx
## payload) instead of a scalar in %rax. `tslot` is the frame slot offset of a 2-word
## SCRATCH region reserved by `emit_fn` for staging an enum value returned by a CALL (so a
## `match <call>` over an enum result can dispatch on it as a local); -1 = none reserved.
## `LCtx` moved to the shared base `lower_ctx.al` (§6 decomposition) — imported by name at the top.

## The AST arena VALUE from a context's `mar` pointer. A `a := deref(cx.mar)` binding does NOT
## lower as a struct copy (the deref base is a FIELD, not a Var — `deref_struct_span` only matches
## `deref(<var>)`), so the lean-runtime alias sites use this BY-VALUE return instead: `rt::Arena`
## is 3 words, which the ≤3-register struct-return convention (%rax:%rdx:%rcx) delivers exactly, so
## `a := arena_of(cx)` binds `a` as a real 3-word arena local. (Inline `deref(cx.mar)` in ARGUMENT
## position is fine — `deref_ptr_agg` passes the pointer, the proven `node_alloc(deref(pc.arena),…)`
## shape — so only the alias bindings need this.)
arena_of := fn(cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> rt::Arena { return deref(cx.mar) }

## The base ADDRESS of an arena reached through a pointer. Isolated into a fn taking the arena as
## a CLEAN ptr param so the body reads `deref(a).base` (a ptr-to-struct PARAM field read). HISTORICAL:
## this predates the direct-deref-of-field READ support — `deref(v.arena).base` where `v.arena` is a
## `ptr(Struct)` FIELD now lowers correctly through `deref_field_ptrstruct_span` (sema.al's lvec_push
## uses the direct form). svec_push/ivec_push keep this wrapper (correct + already-seeded); collapsing
## them to the direct form is a follow-up, not required.
arena_base := fn(a : ptr(mut rt::Arena)) -> usize { return unchecked bitcast(usize, deref(a).base) }

## Do two source spans denote the same name (content equality), mirroring nameres/sema.
streq := fn(src : ptr(u8), a_s : usize, a_n : usize, b_s : usize, b_n : usize) -> bool {
  ## PERF: length-first fast reject (most scan comparisons differ in length — skip the str views).
  if a_n != b_n { return false }
  ## `src + a_s`/`src + b_s` are POINTER arithmetic (a span start may be a REBASED handle for a
  ## comptime-synthesized name) → route through `rt::addr`, not a checked integer `+` (I11 / CG-8).
  wa := str_at((src + a_s), a_n)
  wb := str_at((src + b_s), b_n)
  wa == wb
}

## MODULE tier — NAME MANGLING. A function's emitted GAS label is `<module>__<fn>` so two
## modules may each define a same-named `helper` without their symbols colliding (the real
## Stage-0's per-submodule mangling in miniature, Modules §1/§3). The `__` separator keeps the
## label a valid GAS symbol (no `::`). A decl with no `module` directive (span 0/0) uses the
## fixed default prefix `main` — so a single-source program's `main` mangles to `main__main`,
## which the driver's `_start` calls.
##
## Modules §6.1 / Manifest §6 — the package-ROOT module. `package.al` is the ANONYMOUS package-root
## module, and "a root-level declaration is UNPREFIXED (`_start` → `_start`)": every declaration in
## that file emits its BARE name as the linker symbol, with no `<module>__` qualification and no
## separator — there is no module name to qualify with. The driver publishes the root module's NAME
## SPAN here (the very `(mod_start, mod_len)` pair every root decl carries, so the test is two integer
## compares) before emission, and republishes `0/0` when the compile list has no root file.
##
## `0/0` — no root module — is the case for EVERY multi-module package (a package's own `package.al`
## is the manifest, excluded from its module list) and therefore for the compiler's OWN build, whose
## `source_dir = "src"` modules are the only things compiled. So this whole facility is DORMANT for
## the self-build: not one emitted symbol moves, and the TOOL-1 fixpoint is unaffected.
mut ROOT_MOD_S : usize = 0
mut ROOT_MOD_L : usize = 0
pub set_root_module := fn(s : usize, l : usize) -> i64 { ROOT_MOD_S = s ; ROOT_MOD_L = l ; return 0 }

## Is this module span the ANONYMOUS package root? (`ROOT_MOD_L == 0` = no root module in this
## compile → always false → every mangling site keeps its pre-existing behaviour byte-for-byte.)
pub is_root_mod := fn(ms : usize, ml : usize) -> bool {
  if ROOT_MOD_L == 0 { return false }
  return ml == ROOT_MOD_L and ms == ROOT_MOD_S
}

## Emit the module QUALIFICATION for a `mod_*` span — the module name AND its `__` separator, so a
## caller appends the bare declaration name straight after. The source slice if non-empty, else the
## fixed default prefix `main`; NOTHING AT ALL (name and separator both) for the anonymous package
## root. The separator lives HERE, not at the call sites, precisely so the unprefixed form cannot
## leave a stray leading `__` behind at a site that was overlooked.
emit_mod_qual := fn(in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, src : ptr(u8), ms : usize, ml : usize) {
  if is_root_mod(ms, ml) { return }
  if ml == 0 { push_str(sb, "main") } else { push_str(sb, str_at((src + ms), ml)) }
  push_str(sb, "__")
}

## Emit a mangled FUNCTION-DEFINITION label `<module>__<fn>` for a decl's module + name spans
## (a plain `<fn>` for a root-level declaration).
emit_mangled_def := fn(in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, src : ptr(u8), ms : usize, ml : usize, ns : usize, nl : usize) {
  emit_mod_qual(sb, src, ms, ml)
  if nl == 0 {
    ## a LIFTED LAMBDA (FN-6): no source name — `ns` is its `fn`-offset id → `<module>__lam<ns>`.
    push_str(sb, "lam")
    push_int(sb, i64(ns))
  } else {
    push_str(sb, str_at((src + ns), nl))
  }
}

## The byte offset (0-based) of the LAST `::` separator within a callee span `[cs, cs+cl)`, or -1
## if the callee is unqualified (a same-module call). The LAST `::` splits a multi-segment path
## `a::b::c` into the module PATH `a::b` (head) and the function name `c` (tail) — so a 3+-segment
## stdlib path `std::io::write` resolves to module `std::io` (mangled `std__io`) + fn `write`. For a
## plain 2-segment `mod::f` the last `::` IS the only one, so this is byte-identical to the former
## first-`::` scan (the entire self-host source is 2-segment → the TOOL-1 fixpoint is unaffected).
colon_pos := fn(src : ptr(u8), cs : usize, cl : usize) -> i64 {
  mut res := -1
  mut i := 0
  while i + 1 < cl {
    if str_at((src + cs + i), 1) == ":" and str_at((src + cs + i + 1), 1) == ":" { res = i64(i); i = i + 2 } else { i = i + 1 }
  }
  res
}

## Compare two MODULE-name spans treating `::` and `__` as the SAME separator — so a qualified
## callee head (`alloc::map`, from source) matches a decl's MANGLED module (`alloc__map`). Both
## separators are two chars, so equal segments give equal-LENGTH spans; a byte differs only at a
## separator, where `:`≡`_`. Real segment names use only alnum + `_` (never `:`), so a decl module
## (always `__`-mangled) compared to another `__`-form spans only `_`↔`_` at separators — identical
## to `streq`; the `:`↔`_` loosening only ever fires for a qualified source head (`::`), so bare-call
## and same-module resolution is byte-for-byte unchanged (fixpoint-safe).
mod_seg_eq := fn(src : ptr(u8), as_ : usize, al : usize, bs : usize, bl : usize) -> bool {
  if al != bl { return false }
  mut i := 0
  while i < al {
    ca := str_at((src + as_ + i), 1)
    cb := str_at((src + bs + i), 1)
    if ca != cb {
      sepa := ca == ":" or ca == "_"
      sepb := cb == ":" or cb == "_"
      if not (sepa and sepb) { return false }
    }
    i += 1
  }
  true
}

## Emit a module-PATH head `[hs, hs+hl)` with any `::` segment separators mangled to `__` (so a
## multi-segment path head `std::io` becomes the GAS-symbol prefix `std__io`). A single-segment head
## (`rt`, `parser`) passes through unchanged — no `::` to rewrite.
emit_mangled_head := fn(in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, src : ptr(u8), hs : usize, hl : usize) {
  mut i := 0
  while i < hl {
    if i + 1 < hl and str_at((src + hs + i), 1) == ":" and str_at((src + hs + i + 1), 1) == ":" {
      push_str(sb, "__")
      i += 2
    } else {
      push_str(sb, str_at((src + hs + i), 1))
      i += 1
    }
  }
}

## Emit the mangled CALLEE label for a `Call`'s callee span `[cs, cs+cl)`, given the calling
## function's module span. If the callee contains `::` (a cross-module qualified call `mod::f`)
## it splits at the `::` into head `mod` and tail `f`, emitting `mod__f`. Otherwise (an
## unqualified same-module call `f`) it emits `<caller-module>__f`.
## Resolve the DEFINING module of a bare call. A bare `f(…)` may name a fn from another module —
## a destructure-import `(f, g) := mod` or an alias `a := mod` — whose label is `mod__f`, NOT
## `<caller>__f`. Search the decls for a fn (kind 1) / syscall-fn (kind 4) named (cs, cl),
## PREFERRING one in the caller's module (ms/ml): the per-module helpers `node_alloc`/`node_ptr`/
## `decl_at`/`streq` are duplicated in every module, so a local definition must win. Returns the
## matching decl index, or -1 (caller falls back to its own module — a same-module or unknown call).
## Does the call's module HEAD (`ms`/`ml`) name the decl's module (`as_`/`al`)? Either a full
## segment-aware match (`alloc::map` ↔ `alloc__map`) OR the head equals the LAST SEGMENT of the
## mangled module (a BARE lib ref `strbuf` ↔ `alloc__strbuf`). The last-segment case resolves
## `string.al`'s bare `strbuf::buf_len` to `alloc::strbuf`'s copy, not a same-tail-name generic
## elsewhere (`base::alloc::buf_len`). src/'s modules are all single-segment (`rt`/`ast`/…), so their
## last segment == the whole name → this reduces to `mod_seg_eq` for the self-host build (fixpoint).
mod_head_matches := fn(src : ptr(u8), as_ : usize, al : usize, ms : usize, ml : usize) -> bool {
  if mod_seg_eq(src, as_, al, ms, ml) { return true }
  mut ls := 0
  mut k := 0
  while k + 1 < al {
    ca := str_at((src + as_ + k), 1)
    cb := str_at((src + as_ + k + 1), 1)
    if (ca == "_" and cb == "_") or (ca == ":" and cb == ":") { ls = k + 2 }
    k += 1
  }
  if ls == 0 { return false }
  streq(src, as_ + ls, al - ls, ms, ml)
}

## The decl index of an `@inline` fn named `[cs, cl)` resolvable from module `[ms, ml)` (else -1) — a
## `callee_decl_idx` that additionally requires the `@inline` marker, so the call-site expander only
## fires for genuinely-inline callees.
inline_callee_idx := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), cs : usize, cl : usize, ms : usize, ml : usize) -> i64 {
  ci := callee_decl_idx(decls, src, cs, cl, ms, ml)
  if ci < 0 { return -1 }
  d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(ci))))
  if d.is_fn and fn_is_inline(src, d.name_start, d.name_len) { return ci }
  -1
}
## The decl index of a comptime-VARIADIC fn named `[cs,cl)` resolvable from module `[ms,ml)` — its LAST
## param is a `...` rest — else -1. The call-site expander fires for such a callee like `@inline` (§7.1):
## leading fixed args bind normally, the trailing args form the comptime PACK the body walks. An `@abi(c)`
## callee whose last param is a bare `...` is instead a §7.3 C-VARIADIC (FFI, `printf`-style) — NOT a
## comptime tuple: exclude it here so it routes to the `@abi(c)` SysV call path (`emit_abi_c_call_args`),
## which passes the trailing args by the C ABI + emits the `%al` XMM count. `src/`+`lib/` declare no
## `@abi(c)` fn, so this guard never changes a self-host call → the TOOL-1 fixpoint stays byte-identical.
variadic_callee_idx := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), cs : usize, cl : usize, ms : usize, ml : usize, a : rt::Arena) -> i64 {
  ci := callee_decl_idx(decls, src, cs, cl, ms, ml)
  if ci < 0 { return -1 }
  if callee_decl_is_abi_c(decls, src, ci) { return -1 }
  d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(ci))))
  if decl_is_variadic(d, src, a) { return ci }
  -1
}
## The decl index of a SLICE-variadic fn named `[cs,cl)` resolvable from module `[ms,ml)` (its LAST
## param is a `...T` rest), else -1. The call site gathers the trailing args into a `{ptr, len}` block.
slice_variadic_callee_idx := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), cs : usize, cl : usize, ms : usize, ml : usize) -> i64 {
  ci := callee_decl_idx(decls, src, cs, cl, ms, ml)
  if ci < 0 { return -1 }
  d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(ci))))
  if decl_is_slice_variadic(d) { return ci }
  -1
}
## The SURFACE SYMBOL of a binary operator opcode (`""` for a non-routable op — the boolean
## short-circuits 40/41 and `not` 42 are control flow, never library operators). Used to match an
## `@inline` operator fn whose DECL NAME is the symbol (`@inline + := fn(a, b) { … }`).
op_symbol := fn(op : i64) -> str {
  if op == 16 { return "+" }
  if op == 17 { return "-" }
  if op == 18 { return "*" }
  if op == 19 { return "/" }
  if op == 29 { return "%" }
  if op == 20 { return "==" }
  if op == 28 { return "!=" }
  if op == 24 { return "<" }
  if op == 25 { return ">" }
  if op == 26 { return "<=" }
  if op == 27 { return ">=" }
  if op == 34 { return "&" }
  if op == 35 { return "|" }
  if op == 36 { return "^" }
  return ""
}
## The exact scalar binary-operator set whose result keeps an operand's integer signedness/width
## through the type-inference and `unchecked` proof paths. Keep this set separate from comparisons and
## logical control operators: it is the OP-2 glyph set that those consumers are allowed to propagate.
is_scalar_bin_op := fn(op : i64) -> bool {
  op == 16 or op == 17 or op == 18 or op == 19 or op == 29 or op == 34 or op == 35 or op == 36
}
## The exact arithmetic subset that can produce an f32/f64 value. Bitwise operators and `%` are integer
## paths here; callers use this helper only for the float detector/emitter seam.
is_float_bin_op := fn(op : i64) -> bool {
  op == 16 or op == 17 or op == 18 or op == 19
}
## Whether a type-name text is a BUILT-IN scalar/brand — an operand of such a type keeps the direct
## built-in lowering (and so does src/'s own scalar arithmetic → fixpoint-neutral). Only USER
## struct/enum operands route through library operators in this slice (§2).
is_builtin_num_name := fn(t : str) -> bool {
  return t == "u8" or t == "u16" or t == "u32" or t == "u64" or t == "usize"
    or t == "i8" or t == "i16" or t == "i32" or t == "i64" or t == "isize"
    or t == "f32" or t == "f64" or t == "bool" or t == "char" or t == "str"
    or t == "bits8" or t == "bits16" or t == "bits32" or t == "bits64"
}
## The pointer-width normalization of a type-name text: `usize`→`u64`, `isize`→`i64`, else the
## text unchanged — so a `usize` operand matches `num.al`'s `u64` operator (they lower identically).
norm_type_name := fn(t : str) -> str {
  if t == "usize" { return "u64" }
  if t == "isize" { return "i64" }
  t
}
## The decl index of an `@inline` binary-operator fn named `op`'s symbol whose FIRST parameter type
## matches the operand type span `[ts, ts+tn)` (with width-alias normalization) — the operator
## resolver (§2). Matches BOTH user struct/enum operators AND `num.al`'s built-in scalar/float
## operators (`+`/`-`/`*`/`/`/`%`); comparisons (`<`/`==`) have no symbol decl in `cmp.al` (they are
## `lt`/`eq`), so those stay on the direct lowering. A `str`/`bool`/`char` operand or an un-inferable /
## non-routable op returns -1.
## The decl index of an `@inline` 2-arg fn NAMED `name` whose first parameter type matches the operand
## type span (with width-alias normalization), else -1. The name-keyed core of the operator resolver.
named_op_decl_idx := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), name : str, ts : usize, tn : usize, a : rt::Arena) -> i64 {
  if tn == 0 { return -1 }
  ont := norm_type_name(str_at((src + ts), tn))
  cnt := rt::vec_len(deref(decls))
  th := str_name_hash(name)
  mut j := dni_lo(cnt, th)
  je := dni_hi(cnt, th)
  while j < je {
    i := dni_at(cnt, j)
    j += 1
    if dnh_skip(cnt, i, th) == false {
      d := deref(decl_get(decls, i))
      if d.is_fn and d.is_generic == false and d.arity == 2 and str_at((src + d.name_start), d.name_len) == name and fn_is_inline(src, d.name_start, d.name_len) {
        fp := first_param_type_span(d, a, src)
        if fp.n != 0 and norm_type_name(str_at((src + fp.s), fp.n)) == ont { return i64(i) }
      }
    }
  }
  -1
}
## The decl index of a NON-`@inline` 2-arg fn NAMED `name` whose first parameter type matches the
## operand type span (-normalized), else -1. The fallback of `named_op_decl_idx`, consulted ONLY
## after it returned -1 (so an `@inline` operator always wins): a plain `+ := fn(a : T, b : T) -> T`
## routes + expands at the site through the SAME `emit_inline_binop` machinery. Without it a
## non-inline operator never routed at all — `a <op> b` fell through to the built-in scalar lowering
## over the aggregate words and the result binding stayed a scalar, a SILENT miscompile
## (test/operator_noinline.al). `src/`+`lib/` declare no non-inline glyph operator → neutral there.
named_op_decl_idx_noinline := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), name : str, ts : usize, tn : usize, a : rt::Arena) -> i64 {
  if tn == 0 { return -1 }
  ont := norm_type_name(str_at((src + ts), tn))
  cnt := rt::vec_len(deref(decls))
  th := str_name_hash(name)
  mut j := dni_lo(cnt, th)
  je := dni_hi(cnt, th)
  while j < je {
    i := dni_at(cnt, j)
    j += 1
    if dnh_skip(cnt, i, th) == false {
      d := deref(decl_get(decls, i))
      if d.is_fn and d.is_generic == false and d.arity == 2 and str_at((src + d.name_start), d.name_len) == name and not fn_is_inline(src, d.name_start, d.name_len) {
        fp := first_param_type_span(d, a, src)
        if fp.n != 0 and norm_type_name(str_at((src + fp.s), fp.n)) == ont { return i64(i) }
      }
    }
  }
  -1
}
## The base type-name span of a generic operator's first NON-comptime (VALUE) parameter — the
## operand-type discriminator of the generic route (`uint` in `a : uint(N)`). Built from SCALARS
## (`rs`/`rn`) at return, never a struct local reassigned in the loop (the lean-lower landmine —
## see `typearg_at`). 0/0 when the decl has no value parameter.
first_value_param_type_span := fn(d : Decl, src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> CSpan {
  mut rs := 0
  mut rn := 0
  mut p := d.params_head
  while p != 0 {
    pm := deref(param_p(p))
    if rn == 0 and param_is_comptime(src, pm.ns) == false {
      bn := base_type_name(src, pm.ts, pm.tl)
      rs = bn.s
      rn = bn.n
    }
    p = pm.next
  }
  CSpan(s = rs, n = rn)
}
## The decl index of a GENERIC binary-operator fn named `name` (TYP-10 slice B) whose first VALUE
## parameter's type BASE HEAD equals the operand type span `[ts, ts+tn)`'s base head — e.g.
## `@inline + := fn(comptime N : u64, a : uint(N), b : uint(N)) -> uint(N)` matches an operand of
## type `uint(192)` (base head `uint`). `want_inline` selects the `@inline` tier (consulted
## first) or the non-inline tier. A candidate must carry ≥1 comptime VALUE parameter and exactly
## 2 value parameters, and the head must resolve to a user STRUCT decl (a generic operator over a
## scalar/builtin head would hijack built-in arithmetic; a generic-ENUM head is a later slice).
## Two candidates sharing the glyph + head are an AMBIGUITY — fail LOUD, never pick one.
generic_op_decl_idx := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), name : str, ts : usize, tn : usize, want_inline : bool, a : rt::Arena) -> i64 {
  if tn == 0 { return -1 }
  ## slice C: canonicalize a TYPE-ALIAS operand (`u128`) to the alias's full instance span
  ## (`uint(128)`) — its base head then matches the operator's `uint(N)` param head.
  oa := alias_rhs(decls, src, ts, tn)
  ob := base_type_name(src, oa.s, oa.n)
  cnt := rt::vec_len(deref(decls))
  mut found := -1
  mut nfound := 0
  th := str_name_hash(name)
  mut jc := dni_lo(cnt, th)
  jce := dni_hi(cnt, th)
  while jc < jce {
    i := dni_at(cnt, jc)
    jc += 1
    if dnh_skip(cnt, i, th) == false {
    d := deref(decl_get(decls, i))
    if d.is_fn and d.is_generic and str_at((src + d.name_start), d.name_len) == name and fn_is_inline(src, d.name_start, d.name_len) == want_inline {
      mut nct := 0
      mut nval := 0
      mut p := d.params_head
      while p != 0 {
        pm := deref(param_p(p))
        if param_is_comptime(src, pm.ns) { nct += 1 }
        if param_is_comptime(src, pm.ns) == false { nval += 1 }
        p = pm.next
      }
      if nct >= 1 and nval == 2 {
        fv := first_value_param_type_span(d, src, a)
        if fv.n != 0 and struct_decl_of(decls, src, fv.s, fv.n) >= 0 and streq(src, fv.s, fv.n, ob.s, ob.n) {
          found = i64(i)
          nfound += 1
        }
      }
    }
    }
  }
  if nfound > 1 { panic("selfhost: ambiguous generic operator — two generic operator fns share the glyph and the operand base head") }
  found
}
operator_decl_idx := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), op : i64, ts : usize, tn : usize, a : rt::Arena) -> i64 {
  sym := op_symbol(op)
  if sym == "" { return -1 }
  mut ii := named_op_decl_idx(decls, src, sym, ts, tn, a)
  if ii >= 0 { return ii }
  ii = named_op_decl_idx_noinline(decls, src, sym, ts, tn, a)
  if ii >= 0 { return ii }
  ## TYP-10 slice B — the GENERIC tiers (a comptime-VALUE-param operator, e.g. `uint(N)`'s `+`):
  ## a CONCRETE operator always wins over a generic one; within the generic tier `@inline` wins
  ## over non-inline (the same inline-first rule as the concrete tiers).
  ii = generic_op_decl_idx(decls, src, sym, ts, tn, true, a)
  if ii >= 0 { return ii }
  generic_op_decl_idx(decls, src, sym, ts, tn, false, a)
}
## Push the comptime-VALUE binding for a routed GENERIC operator `opci` (TYP-10 slice B): the
## operator's comptime parameter NAME (its span in the operator's own source) → the VALUE read
## from the OPERAND's instantiation (its literal value argument sits in the source right after
## the operand's head span `[ts, ts+tn)`, recovered by the slice-A `ct_param_value`). Returns
## TRUE when a binding was pushed — the caller pops with `ct_bind_pop` after the expansion (a
## concrete operator pushes nothing). Fail LOUD when the operator is generic but `N` cannot be
## determined from the operand (a non-literal value argument / no argument at all) — never fall
## through to the scalar lowering over the aggregate's words (the original silent-miscompile).
op_ct_bind := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), opci : i64, ts : usize, tn : usize, a : rt::Arena) -> bool {
  d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(opci))))
  mut nct := 0
  mut cns := 0
  mut cnl := 0
  mut p := d.params_head
  while p != 0 {
    pm := deref(param_p(p))
    if param_is_comptime(src, pm.ns) { nct += 1; cns = pm.ns; cnl = pm.nl }
    p = pm.next
  }
  if nct == 0 { return false }
  if nct > 1 { panic("selfhost: a generic operator with more than one comptime value parameter is a later slice") }
  ## slice C: the alias-canonicalized operand span (`u128` → `uint(128)`) — the value argument
  ## (`128`) sits after the ALIAS's head in source, not after a bare use-site `u128`.
  oa := alias_rhs(decls, src, ts, tn)
  ob := base_type_name(src, oa.s, oa.n)
  sdi := struct_decl_of(decls, src, ob.s, ob.n)
  val := ct_param_value(decls, src, sdi, ob.s, ob.n, cns, cnl, a)
  if val < 0 { panic("selfhost: a generic operator's comptime parameter cannot be bound from the operand type (expected a literal value argument, e.g. `uint(192)`)") }
  ct_bind_push(cns, cnl, usize(val))
  true
}
## The cmp.al FUNCTION NAME a comparison opcode desugars to: `==`/`!=` → `eq`, the four orderings →
## `lt` (with a swap/negate applied by the caller). `""` for a non-comparison op.
cmp_route_name := fn(op : i64) -> str {
  if op == 20 or op == 28 { return "eq" }
  if op == 24 or op == 25 or op == 26 or op == 27 { return "lt" }
  return ""
}
## Whether a type-name text is a built-in INTEGER width (the operand types cmp.al's `lt`/`eq` cover for
## the comparison desugar) — excludes float (own NaN-aware compare path), bool/char/str, and user types.
is_builtin_int_name := fn(t : str) -> bool {
  return t == "u8" or t == "u16" or t == "u32" or t == "u64" or t == "usize"
    or t == "i8" or t == "i16" or t == "i32" or t == "i64" or t == "isize"
}
## The cmp.al `lt`/`eq` decl a built-in-INTEGER comparison `a <cmp> b` desugars to, else -1. Only
## built-in integer operands route (float/str/bool/user keep their own paths); the caller applies the
## operand swap (`>`/`<=`) and result negate (`!=`/`<=`/`>=`).
cmp_route_idx := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), op : i64, ts : usize, tn : usize, a : rt::Arena) -> i64 {
  cn := cmp_route_name(op)
  if cn == "" { return -1 }
  if tn == 0 { return -1 }
  if not is_builtin_int_name(str_at((src + ts), tn)) { return -1 }
  named_op_decl_idx(decls, src, cn, ts, tn, a)
}
## PERF (emit hot path — ~30% of self-build time was O(cnt) decl-by-name scans, perf 2026-08-13): a
## precomputed per-decl NAME HASH pre-filters those scans. `DNH` addresses a `DNH_N`-word array (an FNV-1a
## hash of each decl's name, indexed by decl index); 0 = not built. A scan compares the TARGET name's hash
## to `DNH[i]` and only pays the expensive `decl_get` + `streq` on a hash MATCH. `streq` stays the arbiter,
## so a hash COLLISION merely skips the skip → resolution and the emitted GAS are byte-IDENTICAL
## (fixpoint-neutral, output unchanged; the compiler's own binary just runs faster). Built once at
## `emit_program` start (after lambda lifting finalizes `decls`); a `DNH_N != cnt` mismatch disables the
## fast path (correctness before speed — never skips a real candidate).
mut DNH : usize = 0
mut DNH_N : usize = 0
## Parallel to DNH: `DIC[i]` = 1 iff decl `i` is a `@convert` fn (`d.is_fn and fn_is_convert`). Lets the
## `convert_callee_idx` scans replace their per-decl SOURCE re-scan (`fn_is_convert`, a perf hot spot) with
## an O(1) array read. Same predicate → identical resolution (fixpoint-neutral).
mut DIC : usize = 0
## PERF (name→decl INDEX, perf 2026-08-15): `DNH` made each name scan CHEAPER but left it O(decls) —
## the scan loop itself (`vec_get`/`decl_get`/`rec_get`) was still ~25% of the self-build. `DNI` turns a
## lookup into O(candidates): a bucketed (CSR) map from a decl-name hash to the decl indices carrying it.
## `DNI_B` addresses `DNI_NB + 1` bucket boundaries, `DNI_L` the `DNI_N` decl indices grouped by bucket
## and — critically — kept in INCREASING order WITHIN a bucket, so a scan that walks candidates visits the
## same decls in the same ORDER as the full scan did (first-match / last-match resolution unchanged).
## Every converted scan keeps its `DNH[i] == th` guard and its `streq` ARBITER; the bucket is derived from
## the very word the guard compares, so a real match can never be missed and a bucket collision merely
## costs a wasted candidate → resolution and the emitted GAS are byte-IDENTICAL (fixpoint-neutral).
## Not live (`DNI_N != cnt`) ⇒ the cursors degrade to `[0, cnt)` and `dni_at` is the identity: the
## original full scan, unchanged.
mut DNI_B : usize = 0
mut DNI_L : usize = 0
mut DNI_NB : usize = 0
mut DNI_N : usize = 0
## PERF: the `@convert` decl LIST — `convert_callee_idx` matches a decl's RETURN type, not its NAME, so
## the name index cannot narrow it; but `@convert` fns are a handful, so walking just their indices (in
## increasing order, same predicate) replaces an O(decls) scan with an O(#converts) one.
mut DCV_L : usize = 0
mut DCV_N : usize = 0
## Modules §3 for a BARE call. `mod_head_matches` answers only "is this the caller's OWN module (or a
## last-segment lib path)"; §3 is wider — a declaration is visible to its module AND to every module
## nested within it — so a bare call must also see its ANCESTORS' declarations, nearest first. That is
## exactly the walk `mod_anc_rank_from` performs for a module GLOBAL (`gref_best_rank`), so callee and
## global now agree on what "nameable from here" means: the caller's own module, then its ancestors
## nearest-first, and NOTHING else. A sibling's, a descendant's or an unrelated module's declaration
## ranks -1 — §3 makes it unnameable, so it must not be reachable by accident either.
##
## Because each ancestor of one module is a distinct segment-aligned PREFIX of its path, no two
## ancestors share a rank: "the best rank" names exactly one module, and the first candidate at that
## rank is the same first-wins-within-a-module rule the own-module short-circuit already applied.
##
## PRESERVED DELIBERATELY: the `mod_head_matches` short-circuit, including its LAST-SEGMENT lib-path
## leniency (a caller in module `strbuf` reaching `alloc__strbuf`'s copy). The whole compiler leans on
## that leniency for `m := path` bindings; narrowing it is its own decision, not this one's.
##
## When NO candidate is nameable the historical fallback stands — but only for a name that is UNIQUE
## in the program, which is the sound case it was written for (a listed member projection
## `(f, g) := M`, a module alias, a name whose defining module is not recoverable here). Two or more
## unrelated same-named candidates used to be settled by DECLARATION ORDER — the first won — so a call
## could bind to an unrelated module's non-`pub` duplicate and silently return its answer (measured:
## `geo::child` calling bare `helper` emitted `call aother__helper`, 22 instead of 42). Answer -1
## there instead of guessing; `reject_callee_ambiguous` turns it into a located diagnostic at the one
## emit site that mangles a call, so the ambiguity cannot fail silently either way.
callee_decl_idx := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), cs : usize, cl : usize, ms : usize, ml : usize) -> i64 {
  callee_decl_ranked(decls, src, cs, cl, ms, ml, true)
}
## The same resolution for a QUALIFIED callee, whose `ms`/`ml` is the spelled module HEAD rather than
## the calling module: an ancestor-chain rank of the CALLER means nothing there. A one-segment module
## alias (`gc := geo::child`) is expanded through the existing binding_head_span helper; its head/tail
## fields are contiguous source spans, so the complete RHS path is `geo::child`. The second lookup then
## reuses binding_callee_idx for a named re-export (`re_answer := geo::answer`) instead of emitting a
## symbol for the alias spelling. An unqualified alias (`strbuf := rt`) keeps the historical unique-name
## fallback because binding_head_span has no qualified RHS to expand.
callee_decl_idx_qual := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), cs : usize, cl : usize, ms : usize, ml : usize, caller_s : usize, caller_l : usize) -> i64 {
  mut hs := ms
  mut hl := ml
  rhs := binding_rhs_span(decls, src, ms, ml, caller_s, caller_l)
  if rhs.n != 0 {
    hs = rhs.s
    hl = rhs.n
  } else {
    bh := binding_head_span(decls, src, ms, ml, caller_s, caller_l)
    if bh.n == 0 { hs = ms; hl = ml }
    ## A named `x := M::y` stores M and y separately. A listed projection stores the full RHS in
    ## `s/n` and the projected name before it, so only the named-alias shape can be recombined.
    else {
      if bh.tn != 0 and bh.ts >= bh.s {
        hs = bh.s
        hl = bh.ts + bh.tn - bh.s
      } else {
        hs = bh.s
        hl = bh.n
      }
    }
  }
  idx := callee_decl_ranked(decls, src, cs, cl, hs, hl, false)
  if idx >= 0 { return idx }
  ## The module path may name a comptime re-export rather than a function declaration. Resolve that
  ## alias to its defining declaration; this is the same binding path used by bare imported calls.
  bi := binding_callee_idx(decls, src, cs, cl, hs, hl)
  bi
}
## The shared body. `bare` selects the §3 ancestor ranking (a bare call) over the historical
## head-preference-then-first-definition resolution (a qualified call).
callee_decl_ranked := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), cs : usize, cl : usize, ms : usize, ml : usize, bare : bool) -> i64 {
  cnt := rt::vec_len(deref(decls))
  th := name_hash(src, cs, cl)
  mut found := -1
  mut hits := 0
  mut best := 0 - 1
  mut besti := 0 - 1
  mut j := dni_lo(cnt, th)
  je := dni_hi(cnt, th)
  while j < je {
    i := dni_at(cnt, j)
    if DNH == 0 or DNH_N != cnt or rt::rec_get(unchecked bitcast(ptr(mut u8), DNH), i) == th {
      d := deref(decl_get(decls, i))
      if (d.kind == 1 or d.kind == 4) and streq(src, d.name_start, d.name_len, cs, cl) {
        if mod_head_matches(src, d.mod_start, d.mod_len, ms, ml) { return i64(i) }
        hits += 1
        if found < 0 { found = i64(i) }
        if bare {
          r := mod_anc_rank_from(src, d.mod_start, d.mod_len, ms, ml)
          if r > best { best = r; besti = i64(i) }
        }
      }
    }
    j += 1
  }
  if besti >= 0 { return besti }
  if bare and hits > 1 {
    ## §4.1.1 — the calling module may have SAID which module it means (`(f, g) := M`, `f := M::f`).
    ## Only consulted here, so a name that resolves on the ancestor chain or is unique in the program
    ## never pays for the scan.
    bi := binding_callee_idx(decls, src, cs, cl, ms, ml)
    if bi >= 0 { return bi }
    ## Otherwise the name is genuinely unanswerable ONLY when two of the off-chain candidates are
    ## indistinguishable to the resolvers downstream — same ARITY and same genericity, in two
    ## different modules. When their arities differ, arity resolution still picks one deterministically
    ## (`generic_decl_of`, `variadic_print_target`), so that case keeps the historical answer.
    if callee_arity_collision(decls, src, cs, cl, ms, ml) { return 0 - 1 }
  }
  found
}
## The module HEAD a binding in module `[cs, cl)` gives the bare name `[ns, nl)`, plus the TAIL name
## it binds to in that module (they differ for a renaming alias `f := M::g`). `n == 0` = no binding.
## Split out of `binding_callee_idx` because the GENERIC resolver needs the head as a candidate
## FILTER rather than a single decl index: `lower.al` says `(SVec, node_ptr, LCtx, …) := lower_ctx`,
## yet the bare generic call `node_ptr(Expr, …)` was emitting `call wat__node_ptr__Expr` — nine modules
## declare `node_ptr`, none of them `lower` or an ancestor, so the resolver fell to LAST-declared and
## the projection the program had written was never consulted.
BindHead := struct { s : usize, n : usize, ts : usize, tn : usize }
binding_head_span := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), ns : usize, nl : usize, cs : usize, cl : usize) -> BindHead {
  z := BindHead(s = 0, n = 0, ts = 0, tn = 0)
  if nl == 0 { return z }
  if cl == 0 { return z }
  cnt := rt::vec_len(deref(decls))
  mut hs := 0
  mut hl := 0
  mut ts := 0
  mut tl := 0
  mut i := 0
  while i < cnt {
    d := deref(decl_get(decls, i))
    if d.is_fn == false and d.kind == 0 and d.arity == 0 and d.ret_tl != 0
       and mod_seg_eq(src, d.mod_start, d.mod_len, cs, cl) {
      if d.name_len == 0 {
        pr := projection_head_for(src, d.ret_ts, d.ret_tl, ns, nl)
        if pr.n != 0 { hs = pr.s; hl = pr.n; ts = ns; tl = nl }
      } else {
        if streq(src, d.name_start, d.name_len, ns, nl) {
          g := gref_split(src, d.ret_ts, d.ret_tl)
          if g.qual { hs = g.ms; hl = g.ml; ts = g.ns; tl = g.nl }
        }
      }
    }
    i += 1
  }
  BindHead(s = hs, n = hl, ts = ts, tn = tl)
}

## The complete RHS of a NAMED qualified binding in module `[cs, cl)`, or 0/0. `binding_head_span`
## intentionally splits `M::g` into the module head and member tail for bare-call resolution; a
## qualified call through a MODULE alias needs the original full path (`geo::child`) so navigation can
## continue. This is a span reader, not a second name resolver; the existing binding_head_span and
## binding_callee_idx pair still resolves the final member/re-export.
binding_rhs_span := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), ns : usize, nl : usize, cs : usize, cl : usize) -> CSpan {
  z := CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
  if nl == 0 or cl == 0 { return z }
  cnt := rt::vec_len(deref(decls))
  mut i := 0
  while i < cnt {
    d := deref(decl_get(decls, i))
    if d.is_fn == false and d.kind == 0 and d.arity == 0 and d.ret_tl != 0 and d.name_len != 0
       and mod_seg_eq(src, d.mod_start, d.mod_len, cs, cl)
       and streq(src, d.name_start, d.name_len, ns, nl) {
      g := gref_split(src, d.ret_ts, d.ret_tl)
      if g.qual { return CSpan(s = d.ret_ts, n = d.ret_tl) }
    }
    i += 1
  }
  z
}

## Modules §4.1/§4.1.1 — the declaration a BINDING in module `[cs, cl)` gives the bare name
## `[ns, nl)`, or -1. Two spellings bind a NAME (a module alias `m := path` binds a MODULE, not a
## name, and is deliberately not one of them):
##  - a LISTED MEMBER PROJECTION `(a, b, …) := M` — the parser's nameless kind-0 marker whose `ret`
##    span is the verbatim source, so the name list and the head are recovered from it exactly the
##    way `vis_check_projection` recovers them for the §3 check;
##  - a single ALIAS `f := M::g` — a named kind-0 decl whose `ret` span is the qualified RHS path.
## This is what makes a cross-module bare call DETERMINISTIC rather than a declaration-order lottery:
## `src/aarch64.al` says `(typearg_at, base_type_name) := lower_layout`, so its bare
## `base_type_name(…)` means `lower_layout`'s — not `lower`'s same-named copy, which happens to be
## declared first. Consulted only after the §3 ancestor ranking has found nothing AND the name is
## declared in more than one module, i.e. only on the path that used to guess.
binding_callee_idx := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), ns : usize, nl : usize, cs : usize, cl : usize) -> i64 {
  if nl == 0 { return 0 - 1 }
  cnt := rt::vec_len(deref(decls))
  bh := binding_head_span(decls, src, ns, nl, cs, cl)
  hs := bh.s
  hl := bh.n
  mut ts := ns
  mut tl := nl
  if bh.n != 0 and bh.tn != 0 { ts = bh.ts; tl = bh.tn }
  if hl == 0 { return 0 - 1 }
  ## the bound module names exactly one function of that name — resolve it there.
  th := name_hash(src, ts, tl)
  mut res := 0 - 1
  mut j := dni_lo(cnt, th)
  je := dni_hi(cnt, th)
  while j < je {
    k := dni_at(cnt, j)
    j += 1
    if dnh_skip(cnt, k, th) == false {
      d := deref(decl_get(decls, k))
      if (d.kind == 1 or d.kind == 4) and streq(src, d.name_start, d.name_len, ts, tl)
         and mod_head_matches(src, d.mod_start, d.mod_len, hs, hl) { res = i64(k) }
    }
  }
  res
}

## The module-path HEAD a listed member projection `(a, b, …) := M` (the verbatim span `[rs, rl)`)
## gives the name `[ns, nl)`, or 0/0 when the list does not contain it. The scan mirrors
## `vis_check_projection`'s, which reads the same span for the §3 check.
projection_head_for := fn(src : ptr(u8), rs : usize, rl : usize, ns : usize, nl : usize) -> CSpan {
  z := CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
  if rl < 4 { return z }
  if str_at((src + rs), 1) != "(" { return z }
  mut e := rs + 1
  ende := rs + rl
  while e < ende and str_at((src + e), 1) != ")" { e = e + 1 }
  if e >= ende { return z }
  mut p := e + 1
  while p < ende and str_at((src + p), 1) != "=" { p = p + 1 }
  if p >= ende { return z }
  p = p + 1
  while p < ende and (str_at((src + p), 1) == " " or str_at((src + p), 1) == "\n"
                      or str_at((src + p), 1) == "\t" or str_at((src + p), 1) == "\r") { p = p + 1 }
  if p >= ende { return z }
  hs := p
  hl := ende - p
  if hl == 0 { return z }
  mut q := rs + 1
  mut hit := false
  while q < e {
    while q < e and (str_at((src + q), 1) == " " or str_at((src + q), 1) == ","
                     or str_at((src + q), 1) == "\n" or str_at((src + q), 1) == "\t"
                     or str_at((src + q), 1) == "\r") { q = q + 1 }
    mut r := q
    while r < e and gref_ident_byte(src, r) { r = r + 1 }
    if r == q { q = q + 1 }
    else {
      if streq(src, q, r - q, ns, nl) { hit = true }
      q = r
    }
  }
  if hit == false { return z }
  CSpan(s = hs, n = hl)
}

## Is a BARE call to `[cs, cl)` from module `[ms, ml)` AMBIGUOUS — two or more same-named function
## declarations exist, and §3 lets the calling module name none of them? `callee_decl_ranked` answers
## -1 for exactly that case (and for a name with no declaration at all, which this separates out), so
## the emit site can report it located instead of emitting a call to whichever came first.
callee_ambiguous := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), cs : usize, cl : usize, ms : usize, ml : usize) -> bool {
  cnt := rt::vec_len(deref(decls))
  th := name_hash(src, cs, cl)
  mut hits := 0
  mut j := dni_lo(cnt, th)
  je := dni_hi(cnt, th)
  while j < je {
    i := dni_at(cnt, j)
    if DNH == 0 or DNH_N != cnt or rt::rec_get(unchecked bitcast(ptr(mut u8), DNH), i) == th {
      d := deref(decl_get(decls, i))
      if (d.kind == 1 or d.kind == 4) and streq(src, d.name_start, d.name_len, cs, cl) {
        if mod_head_matches(src, d.mod_start, d.mod_len, ms, ml) { return false }
        if mod_anc_rank_from(src, d.mod_start, d.mod_len, ms, ml) >= 0 { return false }
        hits += 1
      }
    }
    j += 1
  }
  if hits <= 1 { return false }
  if binding_callee_idx(decls, src, cs, cl, ms, ml) >= 0 { return false }
  callee_arity_collision(decls, src, cs, cl, ms, ml)
}

## Do two DIFFERENT off-chain modules declare a function named `[cs, cl)` with the SAME arity and the
## same genericity? That is the shape no downstream resolver can break the tie on, so it is the one a
## bare call must not guess at. Distinct arities are still separated by the arity tie-breaks
## (`generic_decl_of`, `variadic_print_target`), so those keep the answer they have today — e.g.
## `alloc::hashmap`'s bare `eq(existing, key)` sees `alloc::string`'s 2-arg `eq` beside
## `base::derive`'s 3-arg generic one and stays resolvable.
callee_arity_collision := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), cs : usize, cl : usize, ms : usize, ml : usize) -> bool {
  cnt := rt::vec_len(deref(decls))
  th := name_hash(src, cs, cl)
  mut j := dni_lo(cnt, th)
  je := dni_hi(cnt, th)
  while j < je {
    i := dni_at(cnt, j)
    j += 1
    if dnh_skip(cnt, i, th) == false {
      d := deref(decl_get(decls, i))
      if (d.kind == 1 or d.kind == 4) and streq(src, d.name_start, d.name_len, cs, cl)
         and mod_head_matches(src, d.mod_start, d.mod_len, ms, ml) == false
         and mod_anc_rank_from(src, d.mod_start, d.mod_len, ms, ml) < 0 {
        mut k := dni_lo(cnt, th)
        ke := dni_hi(cnt, th)
        while k < ke {
          q := dni_at(cnt, k)
          k += 1
          if q != i and dnh_skip(cnt, q, th) == false {
            e := deref(decl_get(decls, q))
            if (e.kind == 1 or e.kind == 4) and streq(src, e.name_start, e.name_len, cs, cl)
               and mod_head_matches(src, e.mod_start, e.mod_len, ms, ml) == false
               and mod_anc_rank_from(src, e.mod_start, e.mod_len, ms, ml) < 0
               and e.arity == d.arity and e.is_generic == d.is_generic
               and mod_seg_eq(src, e.mod_start, e.mod_len, d.mod_start, d.mod_len) == false { return true }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  false
}
## The LOCATED half: the offending source line, then the rule. Reached only from the bare-call arm of
## `emit_mangled_call`, so the line printed is the CALL, and only when the call is genuinely a call.
reject_callee_ambiguous := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), cs : usize, cl : usize, ms : usize, ml : usize) {
  if callee_ambiguous(decls, src, cs, cl, ms, ml) == false { return }
  lower_show_src_line(src, cs)
  vis_show_str("selfhost: the bare call `")
  vis_show_span(src, cs, cl)
  vis_show_str("` on the source line above names a function declared in MORE THAN ONE module, and Modules §3 lets module `")
  vis_show_span(src, ms, ml)
  vis_show_str("` name none of them.\n")
  panic("selfhost: Modules §3 — a bare name resolves in the calling module and then UP its ancestor chain, nearest first; a sibling's, a descendant's or an unrelated module's declaration is reachable only through a path, and only when it is `pub`. Picking the first declaration in declaration order would call a function this program may not name (a silent wrong value), so the ambiguity is rejected: qualify the call with the module path it means.")
}
## The decl index of a callee that is a COMPTIME-VARIADIC fn (a trailing param whose type text is
## `..` — the bare rest, Functions §7.1), else -1. Drives the `{}`-template call-site expansion.
callee_variadic_idx := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), cs : usize, cl : usize, ms : usize, ml : usize, a : rt::Arena) -> i64 {
  ## resolve by TAIL name (a qualified `std::fmt::print` → `print`) among decls that HAVE a `..`
  ## param — this uniquely picks the variadic `fmt::print` over the non-variadic `io::print`.
  nm := callee_name_span(src, cs, cl)
  cnt := rt::vec_len(deref(decls))
  mut res := -1
  th := name_hash(src, nm.s, nm.n)
  mut j := dni_lo(cnt, th)
  je := dni_hi(cnt, th)
  while j < je {
    i := dni_at(cnt, j)
    if DNH == 0 or DNH_N != cnt or rt::rec_get(unchecked bitcast(ptr(mut u8), DNH), i) == th {
      d := deref(decl_get(decls, i))
      if d.is_fn and streq(src, d.name_start, d.name_len, nm.s, nm.n) {
        mut p := d.params_head
        mut isv := false
        while p != 0 {
          pm := deref(param_p(p))
          if pm.tl == 2 and str_at((src + pm.ts), 2) == ".." { isv = true }
          p = pm.next
        }
        if isv { res = i64(i) }
      }
    }
    j += 1
  }
  res
}
## The number of declared parameters of the fn `Decl` at index `i` (a generic's leading comptime
## type param counts, matching how a call site spells it).
fn_param_count := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), i : usize) -> usize {
  d := deref(decl_get(decls, i))
  mut p := d.params_head
  mut n : usize = 0
  while p != 0 {
    pm := deref(param_p(p))
    n = n + 1
    p = pm.next
  }
  n
}
## The decl index of the comptime-variadic fn a call at `[cs, cl)` ACTUALLY targets, else -1 — the
## question `callee_variadic_idx` does NOT answer. That one reports "SOME decl with this tail name is
## variadic", and `std::io::print` (non-variadic, one `str` param) shares the tail `print` with the
## variadic `std::fmt::print`, so every `io::print(x)` in STATEMENT position was routed into the
## `{}`-template desugar — which returns without emitting anything when arg 0 is not a string literal.
## The whole statement, side effects included, VANISHED with no diagnostic: `io::print(s)` for a `str`
## local printed nothing, and a user-declared `print := fn(x : u64)` never ran (I11). Discriminate:
##  - a QUALIFIED callee (`mod::f`) names exactly one decl — take the variadic route only when the
##    call's OWN resolution (`resolve_callee_idx`, the emitter's) lands on the variadic decl. When
##    resolution finds nothing (-1) keep the tail-name answer, so nothing that works today changes.
##  - a BARE callee keeps the tail-name answer UNLESS a decl of that name lives in the CALLER's own
##    module: a user-declared `print`/`format` SHADOWS the prelude's variadic (the built-in
##    names are ordinary prelude identifiers), so the call is an ordinary call to the user's fn.
##  - Either way the override needs the call's ARITY to FIT the resolved decl: `io::print("v = {}", x)`
##    spells two arguments where `std::io::print` declares one, so it cannot be that fn — keep the
##    template route there (it is what makes such a call render today).
## (A 3+-segment spelling like `std::fmt::print` never reaches here at all: `callee_variadic_idx`
## keys on `callee_name_span`, which splits at the FIRST `::`, so its tail is `fmt::print`.)
variadic_print_target := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), cs : usize, cl : usize, na : usize, ms : usize, ml : usize, a : rt::Arena) -> i64 {
  vi := callee_variadic_idx(decls, src, cs, cl, ms, ml, a)
  if vi < 0 { return -1 }
  cp := colon_pos(src, cs, cl)
  if cp >= 0 {
    ri := resolve_callee_idx(decls, src, cs, cl, ms, ml)
    if ri >= 0 and ri != vi and na <= fn_param_count(decls, usize(ri)) { return -1 }
    return vi
  }
  si := callee_decl_idx(decls, src, cs, cl, ms, ml)
  if si >= 0 and si != vi and na <= fn_param_count(decls, usize(si)) {
    sd := deref(decl_get(decls, usize(si)))
    if mod_head_matches(src, sd.mod_start, sd.mod_len, ms, ml) { return -1 }
  }
  vi
}
## The decl index of the generic fn named `[nm]` in module `[mod]` (both literal texts), else -1 —
## for resolving `std::fmt::print_one` / `std::io::print`, which the `{}`-template desugar emits by
## mangled label (there is no source span for the name at the call site).
decl_by_lit_name := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), nm : str) -> i64 {
  cnt := rt::vec_len(deref(decls))
  mut res := -1
  mut i := 0
  while i < cnt {
    d := deref(decl_get(decls, i))
    if d.is_fn and str_at((src + d.name_start), d.name_len) == nm { res = i64(i) }
    i += 1
  }
  res
}
## Emit ONE literal run of a `{}`-template (`std::io::print(<run>)`): the run is `[.Lstr<flbl>+roff,
## rlen)` — a byte slice of the template's own rodata string (no new rodata). Materialize its
## {ptr, len} into the str-temp block and pass its address as `io::print`'s by-ref `str` arg. %rsp
## stays 16-aligned (no pushes), so the `call` is ABI-correct.
emit_tpl_run := fn(in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, cx : ptr(LCtx), flbl : usize, roff : usize, rlen : usize) {
  push_str(sb, "  leaq ")
  push_lstr(sb, flbl)
  push_str(sb, "+")
  push_int(sb, i64(roff))
  push_str(sb, "(%rip), %rax\n  movq %rax, -")
  push_int(sb, i64((cx.str_tmp + 1) * 8))
  push_str(sb, "(%rbp)\n  movq $")
  push_int(sb, i64(rlen))
  push_str(sb, ", %rax\n  movq %rax, -")
  push_int(sb, i64(cx.str_tmp * 8))
  push_str(sb, "(%rbp)\n  leaq -")
  push_int(sb, i64((cx.str_tmp + 1) * 8))
  push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rdi\n  call std__io__print\n")
}
## The WORD SIZE of `e` IFF it is a `Var` naming a BY-VALUE aggregate slot — a struct (`ek == 2`) or
## enum (`ek == 3`) local/param whose `sns`/`snl` carry the type — else 0. Drives the bare-comparison
## soundness guard: a >1-word result there means `a <cmp> b` would compare WORD 0 ONLY. A pointer-to-
## aggregate (`ek == 7`) is deliberately 0 (a `ptr` value is one word; `p != 0` is a correct null check).
agg_value_var_words := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena) -> usize {
  vn := var_name_span(e)
  if vn.n == 0 { return 0 }
  ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, vn.s, vn.n)))
  if streq(cx.src, ent.ns, ent.nl, vn.s, vn.n) == false { return 0 }
  if ent.ek == 2 { return struct_words(cx.decls, cx.src, ent.sns, ent.snl, a) }
  if ent.ek == 3 { return 1 + enum_inst_words(cx.decls, cx.src, ent.sns, ent.snl, a) }
  0
}
## The WORD COUNT of `e` IFF it is a `Var` naming a BY-VALUE TUPLE / fixed-ARRAY block whose elements
## are SINGLE-WORD INTEGER words (`ek == 5`, `is_ref == false`, `eek == 0`, `estride == 1`) — else 0.
## A tuple parses as an `ArrayLit`, so it has NO struct/enum decl and `agg_value_var_words` (which
## reads the SLOT kind 2/3) can never see it: that dropped fact is why `(5,7) == (5,9)` and
## `[5,7] == [5,9]` both compared WORD 0 ONLY and read EQUAL. Such a slot records its element COUNT
## in `snl` (a scalar-element array has no nominal type span — see `bind_array_slot`), which IS the
## word count at stride 1. A SLICE VIEW (`is_ref`) is a runtime `{ptr, len}` pair, NOT a by-value
## block, so it is deliberately excluded; so is every non-integer element kind (`eek != 0`) — see the
## fail-loud in the routing for why word-wise equality is not the answer for those.
## The SOURCE START of the declared type of the enclosing fn's parameter named (ns,nl) — the raw
## `pm.ts`, NOT its base name, so a TUPLE param's recorded FIRST-COMPONENT position survives. 0 when
## the name is not a parameter of `penv`.
param_type_start := fn(ns : usize, nl : usize, penv : usize, src : ptr(u8)) -> usize {
  mut pc := penv
  while pc != 0 {
    pm := deref(param_p(pc))
    if streq(src, pm.ns, pm.nl, ns, nl) { return pm.ts }
    pc = pm.next
  }
  0
}
## The COMPONENT COUNT of a by-value TUPLE parameter, recovered by a SOURCE SCAN. An ARRAY param
## `[T; N]` has its static length captured into the param's free `pps` slot (→ the slot's `sns`), but
## a TUPLE param `(T0, T1, …)` records only T0's span and NO length — so the slot carries `sns == 0`
## and the block width was unknown, which is why `p == q` over two tuple params fell through to
## comparing the two block ADDRESSES. Recover N the way the lower recovers every other parser-dropped
## fact: read the source. `ts` sits immediately past the opening `(`, so count the TOP-LEVEL commas
## up to the matching `)`. 0 when `ts` is not preceded by `(` (an ordinary param) or the scan does not
## close — the caller then leaves the operand unrouted, exactly as before.
tuple_param_components := fn(src : ptr(u8), ts : usize) -> usize {
  if ts == 0 { return 0 }
  if str_at((src + ts - 1), 1) != "(" { return 0 }
  mut i := ts
  mut depth := 1
  mut comps := 1
  mut guard := 0
  mut res := 0
  mut scanning := true
  while scanning and guard < 4096 {
    c := str_at((src + i), 1)
    if c == "" { scanning = false }
    else if c == "(" or c == "[" { depth = depth + 1 }
    else if c == ")" or c == "]" {
      depth = depth - 1
      if depth == 0 { res = comps; scanning = false }
    }
    else if c == "," and depth == 1 { comps = comps + 1 }
    i = i + 1
    guard = guard + 1
  }
  res
}
## A BY-VALUE aggregate BLOCK operand: `off` is its base slot, and `isref` says the slot holds a
## POINTER to word 0 (a by-reference PARAM) rather than word 0 itself (a frame LOCAL).
BlockRef := struct { off : usize, isref : bool }
tuple_value_var_words := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> usize {
  vn := var_name_span(e)
  if vn.n == 0 { return 0 }
  ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, vn.s, vn.n)))
  if streq(cx.src, ent.ns, ent.nl, vn.s, vn.n) == false { return 0 }
  if ent.ek != 5 { return 0 }
  if ent.eek != 0 { return 0 }
  if ent.estride != 1 { return 0 }
  if ent.is_ref {
    ## A by-value TUPLE / fixed-ARRAY **PARAM** (`t : (u64, u64)`, `a : [u64; N]`): the slot holds the
    ## caller block's ADDRESS, and comparing the SLOTS compared those two addresses — always unequal
    ## for two distinct blocks, so `p == q` over field-EQUAL tuple params read UNEQUAL (the same
    ## "compare the block address, not its contents" bug `agg_cmp_param_not_address` locks for structs).
    ## Such a param records its STATIC length N in `sns` with `snl == 0` (see the array-param binding);
    ## a runtime-length SLICE view is `snl == 1` — a `{ptr, len}` pair, NOT a block — and is excluded.
    if ent.snl != 0 { return 0 }
    if ent.sns != 0 { return ent.sns }
    ## A TUPLE param records no length at all — source-scan its component count (see below).
    return tuple_param_components(cx.src, param_type_start(vn.s, vn.n, EMIT_PARAMS, cx.src))
  }
  ent.snl
}
## The block descriptor for an operand `tuple_value_var_words` has already accepted.
tuple_value_var_ref := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> BlockRef {
  vn := var_name_span(e)
  ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, vn.s, vn.n)))
  BlockRef(off = ent.off, isref = ent.is_ref)
}
## The NAME span of the array a top-level INDEX read `xs[i]` is taken over — 0/0 when `e` is not an
## `Index`, or its base is not a plain `Var`. (The deref-`match` stays in this pointer-PARAM helper,
## the lowerable shape.)
index_base_var_span := fn(e : ptr(Expr)) -> CSpan {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Index(base, idx) => { var_name_span(base) }
    _ => { CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  }
}
## Is `e` an INDEX read `xs[i]` whose array has MULTI-WORD AGGREGATE elements (struct / enum / nested
## tuple)? Such an operand is a whole multi-word element, so the scalar fallthrough compares its WORD 0
## only (`ps[0] == ps[1]` over `[P; 2]` with equal `x` read EQUAL though `y` differed). `str` elements
## (`eek == 4`) are excluded — a str comparison has its own content-comparing route before this point.
index_agg_elem_words := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> usize {
  bv := index_base_var_span(e)
  if bv.n == 0 { return 0 }
  ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, bv.s, bv.n)))
  if streq(cx.src, ent.ns, ent.nl, bv.s, bv.n) == false { return 0 }
  if ent.ek != 5 { return 0 }
  if ent.eek != 2 and ent.eek != 3 and ent.eek != 5 { return 0 }
  ent.estride
}
## The word count of `e` iff it is an INDEX into a fixed array whose elements are plain multi-word
## STRUCTS with native scalar leaves. Locals use the down-growing frame fillers; a concrete `[P; N]`
## parameter uses the already-defined by-reference array ABI (`emit_index_addr` loads its caller-owned
## base). Generic-array params, Slice(P), packed/byte-layout structs, globals, enums and tuple elements
## stay on the fail-loud path until their own representation contracts are proved. The recursive scalar
## predicate is shared by locals and params so a nested struct is admitted only when every word belongs
## to the same plain word-granular layout contract.
index_plain_struct_elem_words := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena) -> usize {
  bv := index_base_var_span(e)
  if bv.n == 0 { return 0 }
  ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, bv.s, bv.n)))
  if streq(cx.src, ent.ns, ent.nl, bv.s, bv.n) == false { return 0 }
  if ent.ek != 5 or ent.eek != 2 or ent.estride <= 1 { return 0 }
  if not index_plain_scalar_struct(cx.src, cx.decls, ent.sns, ent.snl, a) { return 0 }
  if ent.is_ref {
    ## Only a concrete `[P; N]` parameter has the static array contract needed here. A `Slice(P)`
    ## parameter has the same element span in its slot metadata but a runtime length and a different
    ## view contract; a generic `a : T` substituted to an array has no concrete fixed-array contract
    ## at this seam. Keep both loud until their independent equality paths are proved.
    if fixed_array_param_len(bv.s, bv.n, EMIT_PARAMS, cx.src) == 0 { return 0 }
    return ent.estride
  }
  if agg_arr_fill_count(cx.slots, ent.off) <= 1 { return 0 }
  ent.estride
}
## Is `e` a Var bound to a MULTI-WORD BY-VALUE TUPLE / ARRAY block of ANY element kind? The dual of
## `tuple_value_var_words`, used ONLY by the routing's fail-loud: a block whose elements are floats
## (`eek == 9`), `str` views (4), structs (2), enums (3), nested tuples (5) or pointers (7) is NOT
## word-wise comparable — a `str` element compares its {ptr, len} identity rather than its bytes, an
## enum element's unused payload words are indeterminate, a float element mis-answers NaN and ±0 —
## so such a compare must FAIL LOUD rather than keep the word-0 silent miscompile. The element words
## are the anonymous fillers reserved below the base slot (`agg_arr_fill_count` = `nel * estride`).
tuple_value_var_block := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> bool {
  vn := var_name_span(e)
  if vn.n == 0 { return false }
  ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, vn.s, vn.n)))
  if streq(cx.src, ent.ns, ent.nl, vn.s, vn.n) == false { return false }
  if ent.ek != 5 { return false }
  if ent.is_ref { return false }
  agg_arr_fill_count(cx.slots, ent.off) > 1
}
## The base SLOT of the by-value tuple/array block behind Var `e` (its word 0). Only meaningful where
## `tuple_value_var_words(e, cx) > 0` has already answered.
tuple_value_var_slot := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> usize {
  vn := var_name_span(e)
  ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, vn.s, vn.n)))
  ent.off
}
## Emit COMPONENTWISE equality (Stdlib §2.6) of two by-value blocks that live in the frame at base
## slots `loff` / `roff` and span `nw` single-word INTEGER words each: XOR the two words of every
## component and OR the differences together, so the accumulator is zero iff EVERY component matched.
## Branchless (no label allocation) and word-exact — for a block of single-word integer components,
## `a == b` componentwise IS word-for-word equality, which is what `base::derive::eq`'s unrolled
## per-component compare would emit for the same type. `neg` emits `setne` for `!=`.
## Word `k` of a block based at slot `off` lives at `-(off - k + 1) * 8(%rbp)` (the §4 up-growing
## layout) — routed through `push_frame_word`, the single choke point for that offset.
emit_block_word_eq := fn(in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, lb : BlockRef, rb : BlockRef, nw : usize, neg : bool) {
  mut k := 0
  while k < nw {
    emit_block_word_load(sb, lb, k)
    push_str(sb, "  movq %rax, %rdx\n")
    emit_block_word_load(sb, rb, k)
    push_str(sb, "  xorq %rdx, %rax\n")
    if k == 0 { push_str(sb, "  movq %rax, %rcx\n") } else { push_str(sb, "  orq %rax, %rcx\n") }
    k = k + 1
  }
  push_str(sb, "  testq %rcx, %rcx\n")
  if neg { push_str(sb, "  setne %al\n") } else { push_str(sb, "  sete %al\n") }
  push_str(sb, "  movzbq %al, %rax\n  pushq %rax\n")
}
## Load word `k` of the block `b` into %rax. A frame LOCAL's word `k` is at `-(off - k + 1) * 8(%rbp)`
## (the §4 up-growing layout, via `push_frame_word`); a BY-REFERENCE param's slot holds word 0's
## ADDRESS and its word `k` is at `k * 8` past it (the by-ref aggregate ABI — see the `t.N` read).
emit_block_word_load := fn(in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, b : BlockRef, k : usize) {
  if b.isref {
    push_str(sb, "  movq -")
    push_int(sb, i64((b.off + 1) * 8))
    push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rax\n  movq ")
    push_int(sb, i64(k * 8))
    push_str(sb, "(%rax), %rax\n")
    return
  }
  push_str(sb, "  movq -")
  push_frame_word(sb, b.off, k)
  push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rax\n")
}
## Emit structural equality for two LOCAL fixed-array elements whose element type is a plain
## multi-word struct. Kept outside `emit_gas` because that function is already large enough for extra
## temporaries to perturb its self-hosted frame. The two `emit_index_addr` calls retain the normal
## checked dynamic-index path; the addressed words are compared directly, never materialized as a
## by-reference derive call.
emit_local_struct_index_eq := fn(l : ptr(Expr), r : ptr(Expr), nw : usize, neg : bool, in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, cx : ptr(LCtx), in out nl : usize) {
  a := arena_of(cx)
  lp := field_base_index(l)
  rp := field_base_index(r)
  emit_index_addr(lp.arr, lp.idx, sb, cx, a, nl)
  push_str(sb, "  pushq %rax\n")
  emit_index_addr(rp.arr, rp.idx, sb, cx, a, nl)
  push_str(sb, "  popq %rbx\n")
  mut k := 0
  while k < nw {
    push_str(sb, "  movq ")
    push_int(sb, i64(k * 8))
    push_str(sb, "(%rbx), %rdx\n  xorq ")
    push_int(sb, i64(k * 8))
    push_str(sb, "(%rax), %rdx\n")
    if k == 0 { push_str(sb, "  movq %rdx, %rcx\n") } else { push_str(sb, "  orq %rdx, %rcx\n") }
    k = k + 1
  }
  push_str(sb, "  testq %rcx, %rcx\n")
  if neg { push_str(sb, "  setne %al\n") } else { push_str(sb, "  sete %al\n") }
  push_str(sb, "  movzbq %al, %rax\n  pushq %rax\n")
}
## Is `e` a `Slice(T)` VIEW operand — a runtime `{ptr, len}` pair over ELEMENTS — and can `==` / `!=`
## over it be answered by a WORD-WISE CONTENT compare? 0 = not a slice view (the routing's other
## arms own it); 1 = a view over SINGLE-WORD INTEGER elements, for which comparing `len` words of
## each data pointer IS structural equality; 2 = a slice view that is NOT word-wise comparable.
##
## Why this classification exists: a slice view is TWO words, and a bare comparison reached the
## scalar `cmpq` at the bottom of the Bin case and compared WORD 0 ALONE — the DATA POINTER. So two
## views over EQUAL contents at different addresses read UNEQUAL, and two views of the SAME buffer
## with DIFFERENT lengths read EQUAL (measured: both normal-exit wrong values). A slice PARAM was
## worse than word 0: its slot holds a POINTER to the caller's {ptr,len} block, so the compare
## answered on two BLOCK ADDRESSES — the slice twin of the tuple/struct param bug.
##
## `str` is deliberately NOT a slice view here: a str local is `ek == 4`, and a str SUB-VIEW
## (`s[1..4]`) has no array base so `slice_arr_stride` answers 0. Both keep `emit_str_eq_core`, whose
## `len` really is a BYTE count — that route is already correct and stays byte-identical.
slice_view_cmp_kind := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena) -> usize {
  ## A range-slice EXPRESSION `xs[lo..hi]` over a raw ARRAY. This shape was NOT merely comparing word
  ## 0: `is_str_operand` accepts every `Expr::Slice`, so `xs[0..3] == ys[0..3]` was intercepted by the
  ## str route and BYTE-compared `len` bytes — but a typed slice's `len` counts ELEMENTS, so
  ## `[1,2,3]` vs `[1,9,3]` compared three bytes of word 0 and read EQUAL.
  if is_slice_expr(e) {
    st := slice_arr_stride(e, cx.slots, cx.src)
    if st == 0 { return 0 }
    if st != 1 { return 2 }
    if slice_arr_eek(e, cx.slots, cx.src) != 0 { return 2 }
    return 1
  }
  ## A `Slice(T)` STRUCT FIELD (`p.v`) is also a two-word view, but it is an Expr::Field rather than a
  ## Var, so the slot-entry path below cannot classify it. Reuse `str_field_place` for the proven pair
  ## address and `pair_field_elem_stride` for the element representation. Only an 8-byte, one-word
  ## element reaches the word comparer; byte-packed and aggregate elements stay fail-loud. The emitter
  ## below calls `emit_str_pair`, so both the inline-local and by-reference-struct-field pair layouts
  ## use the same already-proven materialization path.
  sfp := str_field_place(e, cx, a)
  if sfp.found {
    fp := field_place_parts(e)
    bv := field_base_var(fp.base)
    if bv.ok {
      se := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, bv.s, bv.n)))
      fts := field_type_span(cx.decls, cx.src, se.sns, se.snl, fp.fs, fp.fl, a)
      ftb := base_type_name(cx.src, fts.s, fts.n)
      if str_at((cx.src + ftb.s), ftb.n) == "Slice" {
        if pair_field_elem_stride(e, cx, a) == 8 { return 1 }
        return 2
      }
    }
  }
  vn := var_name_span(e)
  if vn.n == 0 { return 0 }
  ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, vn.s, vn.n)))
  if streq(cx.src, ent.ns, ent.nl, vn.s, vn.n) == false { return 0 }
  if ent.ek != 5 { return 0 }
  if ent.is_ref == false { return 0 }
  ## `snl == 1` is the RUNTIME-LEN marker of a scalar/float-element slice — a LOCAL view (`sns == 0`,
  ## the {ptr,len} in its own two frame words) or a PARAM (`sns == 1`, a pointer to the caller's
  ## block). Both read through `emit_str_pair`, so both compare the same way. Word-wise only for
  ## single-word INTEGER elements: a FLOAT element (`eek == 9`) mis-answers NaN and ±0 under a bit
  ## compare, so it fails loud rather than keeping a wrong answer.
  if ent.snl == 1 {
    if ent.eek != 0 { return 2 }
    if ent.estride != 1 { return 2 }
    return 1
  }
  ## A STRUCT / ENUM / `str` / pointer-element slice carries the ELEMENT TYPE SPAN in `sns`/`snl`
  ## instead of that marker, so it is told apart from a by-reference fixed-ARRAY param (`snl == 0`,
  ## whose by-value block the tuple/array route owns) exactly the way `emit_str_pair` does: either a
  ## declared `s : Slice(T)` PARAM, or an INFERRED `s := arr[lo..hi]` LOCAL. Its elements are
  ## multi-word aggregates whose padding/unused payload words are indeterminate, so word-wise
  ## equality is not the answer — fail loud.
  if ent.eek == 2 or ent.eek == 3 or ent.eek == 4 or ent.eek == 7 {
    if slice_param_elem_span(cx.src, ent.ns, ent.nl).n != 0 { return 2 }
    if slot_name_is_annotated(cx.src, ent.ns, ent.nl) == false { return 2 }
  }
  0
}
## Emit CONTENT equality of two `Slice(T)` VIEWS over single-word INTEGER elements — pushes 1 (equal)
## / 0. Structurally the WORD-granular twin of `emit_str_eq_core` (which owns `str` and byte views):
## both operands' {ptr, len} pairs come from the one choke point `emit_str_pair` — which already
## reads a slice EXPRESSION (element-scaled data ptr + `hi - lo`), a slice LOCAL (its two frame
## words) and a slice PARAM (deref the slot: ptr @ `(%rax)`, len @ `8(%rax)`) — then the LENGTHS must
## match and word `i` of each data pointer must match for all `i < len`. Fresh labels via `nl`.
emit_slice_word_eq_core := fn(a0 : ptr(Expr), a1 : ptr(Expr), in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena, in out nl : usize) {
  emit_str_pair(a0, sb, cx, a, nl)
  emit_str_pair(a1, sb, cx, a, nl)
  lne := nl          ## not-equal (result 0)
  ldone := nl + 1    ## every word matched (result 1)
  lloop := nl + 2    ## loop head
  nl += 3
  ## pop b (top) then a: blen, bptr, alen, aptr
  push_str(sb, "  popq %rcx\n  popq %rdx\n  popq %rsi\n  popq %rdi\n")
  ## lengths must match — two views of the SAME buffer with DIFFERENT lengths read EQUAL before this.
  push_str(sb, "  cmpq %rcx, %rsi\n  jne ")
  emit_label(sb, lne)
  push_str(sb, "\n")
  push_str(sb, "  movq $0, %r8\n")
  emit_label(sb, lloop)
  push_str(sb, ":\n  cmpq %rsi, %r8\n  jge ")
  emit_label(sb, ldone)
  push_str(sb, "\n")
  ## element i is one WORD (stride 1) — `(%rdi,%r8,8)` vs `(%rdx,%r8,8)`, the only difference from
  ## the byte compare in `emit_str_eq_core`.
  push_str(sb, "  movq (%rdi,%r8,8), %rax\n  movq (%rdx,%r8,8), %r9\n  cmpq %r9, %rax\n  jne ")
  emit_label(sb, lne)
  push_str(sb, "\n  addq $1, %r8\n  jmp ")
  emit_label(sb, lloop)
  push_str(sb, "\n")
  emit_label(sb, lne)
  push_str(sb, ":\n  pushq $0\n  jmp ")
  lend := nl
  nl += 1
  emit_label(sb, lend)
  push_str(sb, "\n")
  emit_label(sb, ldone)
  push_str(sb, ":\n  pushq $1\n")
  emit_label(sb, lend)
  push_str(sb, ":\n")
}
## Is the mono pre-pass's resolved operand TYPE a `Slice(...)`? Such an operand never reaches
## `base::derive::eq` at the emit site (a slice view is compared by CONTENT), so registering a derive
## instance for it only emits a DEAD one — and a dead one that does not even ASSEMBLE: derive's
## struct arm recurses on the field type `ptr(T)` with `T` unsubstituted, emitting the label
## `base__derive__eq__ptr(T)`, which `as` rejects with `junk (T) after expression`. That is why a
## program merely comparing two `Slice(T)` PARAMS failed to BUILD.
collect_ty_is_slice := fn(src : ptr(u8), ts : usize, tl : usize) -> bool {
  if tl == 0 { return false }
  bn := base_type_name(src, ts, tl)
  str_at((src + bn.s), bn.n) == "Slice"
}
## Is `e` a Var bound to an ENUM slot (ek 3)? Used by the bare-comparison routing to distinguish an
## enum operand (whose ORDERING derive `lt` is not yet emittable — see the fail-loud there) from a
## struct operand (whose `lt` works). 0-word / scalar / struct → false.
agg_value_var_is_enum := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> bool {
  vn := var_name_span(e)
  if vn.n == 0 { return false }
  ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, vn.s, vn.n)))
  if streq(cx.src, ent.ns, ent.nl, vn.s, vn.n) == false { return false }
  ent.ek == 3
}
## The decl index of the generic structural derive `eq` (want_lt=false) / `lt` (want_lt=true) in
## `lib/base/derive.al` — a generic fn of arity 3 (`T : type, a : T, b : T`); -1 if absent. Shared by
## the bare-aggregate comparison routing's TWO seams (the mono-collection pre-pass + the emit site) so
## they agree on the `base__derive__<eq|lt>__<T>` instance label. `src/` makes no bare aggregate
## comparison → this is never consulted when self-compiling → fixpoint-neutral.
derive_cmp_decl := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), want_lt : bool) -> i64 {
  cnt := rt::vec_len(deref(decls))
  mut res := 0 - 1
  for i in 0..cnt {
    d := deref(decl_get(decls, i))
    if d.kind == 1 and d.is_generic and d.arity == 3 {
      nm := str_at((src + d.name_start), d.name_len)
      if want_lt {
        if nm == "lt" { res = i64(i) }
      } else {
        if nm == "eq" { res = i64(i) }
      }
    }
  }
  res
}
## The TYPE span needed by a variadic `print` hole when the ordinary expression resolver is neutral.
## Keep this seam local to the variadic lowering: `expr_type_span` intentionally stays conservative for
## native-width array indexes (fixpoint-sensitive overload/routing), but `print("{}", xs[i])` still
## needs the declared element type to select the same `print_one__T` instance in both passes. A call
## hole likewise uses its declared return type when the general resolver cannot recover it.
variadic_hole_type_span := fn(e : ptr(Expr), block_head : ptr(mut Stmt), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> CSpan {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Index(base, idx) => {
      bv := var_name_span(base)
      if bv.n != 0 {
        lt := block_decl_type(block_head, bv.s, bv.n, src, decls, a)
        if lt.n != 0 {
          es := array_elem_span(src, lt.s, lt.n)
          if es.n != 0 { return es }
        }
      }
    }
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, ah) => {
      rt := call_ret_ty_span(e, decls, src, a)
      if rt.n != 0 { return rt }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
}

## Expand a `{}`-template variadic `print` call (Functions §7.1) into a SEQUENCE of `io::print(<run>)`
## (literal segments) + `print_one(<argtype>, <arg>)` (holes, in order) calls — the specified
## interleaving `[seg0, arg0, seg1, …]`. v1: holes fill trailing args in order; each arg needs an
## inferable type (`expr_type_span` plus `variadic_hole_type_span`); `{{`/`}}` escapes + non-literal
## templates are follow-ups.
emit_variadic_print := fn(args_head : ptr(mut Arg), block_head : ptr(mut Stmt), in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena, in out nl : usize) {
  fmt := arg_expr_at(args_head, 0, a)
  fi := str_lit_info(fmt)
  ## A NON-LITERAL template (`t : str = "…"` then `print(t)`): §7.1 specifies the expansion over the
  ## template's LITERAL text, so there is nothing to expand and this desugar has no output. Returning
  ## here emitted NOTHING for the whole statement — the call and its side effects silently deleted,
  ## the one forbidden outcome (I11). Fail LOUD, located on the offending line.
  if fi.is_s == false {
    foff := comptime_cond_src_off(fmt)
    if foff != 0 { lower_show_src_line(cx.src, foff) }
    panic("selfhost: a `{}`-template variadic `print` needs a string-LITERAL template (the source line above); a non-literal template is not expanded yet. Rejected rather than silently emitting nothing.")
  }
  ## SAVE the agg-temp bump pointer: an aggregate hole (e.g. a global struct) materializes into the pool
  ## via `emit_arg` here, but this desugar is NOT an `emit_call_args` (which would save/restore), so
  ## RESTORE it at the end — else the bump leaks into the next statement's call and overflows the pool.
  aggsave := cx.agg_next
  mut ai := 1
  mut runstart := fi.ss
  mut i := 0
  while i < fi.sl {
    mut step := false
    if i + 1 < fi.sl {
      c := str_at((cx.src + fi.ss + i), 1)
      c2 := str_at((cx.src + fi.ss + i + 1), 1)
      if (c == "{" and c2 == "{") or (c == "}" and c2 == "}") {
        ## `{{` / `}}` — an ESCAPED literal brace: flush the run up to and INCLUDING the first brace
        ## (rendered verbatim), then skip the second. (Reuses the template rodata; the doubled brace
        ## in it means the emitted run byte-count differs from the rendered one, so flush per-escape.)
        rlen := (fi.ss + i + 1) - runstart
        if rlen != 0 { emit_tpl_run(sb, cx, fi.lbl, runstart - fi.ss, rlen) }
        i += 2
        runstart = fi.ss + i
        step = true
      } else if c == "{" and c2 == "}" {
        ## a `{}` HOLE — flush the preceding run, render the next argument by its kind.
        rlen := (fi.ss + i) - runstart
        if rlen != 0 { emit_tpl_run(sb, cx, fi.lbl, runstart - fi.ss, rlen) }
        argx := arg_expr_at(args_head, ai, a)
        mut ty := expr_type_span(argx, cx)
        if ty.n == 0 {
          ty = variadic_hole_type_span(argx, block_head, cx.decls, cx.src, a)
        }
        mut is_str_hole := str_lit_info(argx).is_s
        if ty.n != 0 and str_at((cx.src + ty.s), ty.n) == "str" { is_str_hole = true }
        ## a str LOCAL/PARAM records no type span (ek 4, `{ptr, len}`) — detect it by slot kind; a
        ## FLOAT local is ek 9 (its value lives in an xmm register at the call boundary).
        mut is_float_hole := expr_is_floatlit(argx)
        mut is_scalar_hole := false   ## a plain scalar Var (untyped local / scalar global) → int default
        svn := var_name_span(argx)
        if svn.n != 0 {
          sent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, svn.s, svn.n)))
          if streq(cx.src, sent.ns, sent.nl, svn.s, svn.n) {
            if sent.ek == 4 { is_str_hole = true }
            if sent.ek == 9 { is_float_hole = true }
            if sent.ek == 0 { is_scalar_hole = true }   ## a plain scalar LOCAL/param slot
          } else if is_module_mut_global(cx.decls, cx.src, svn.s, svn.n) {
            ## a module GLOBAL (no slot) — a FLOAT global renders via the float path; otherwise scalar
            ## int, but only if its value is neither a struct nor an array (those aren't valid bare
            ## `{}` holes — guarding here avoids rendering their word 0 as int).
            gmv := mut_global_value(cx.decls, cx.src, svn.s, svn.n)
            if unchecked bitcast(usize, gmv) != 0 {
              if float_lit_span(gmv).n != 0 { is_float_hole = true }
              else if struct_lit_info(gmv).is_s == false and array_lit_info(gmv).is_a == false { is_scalar_hole = true }
            }
          }
        }
        ## TUPLE / ARRAY (and any annotated) hole whose type `expr_type_span` couldn't give (a tuple/array
        ## slot reuses `snl` for its element count, not a type span) — recover the declared type by scanning
        ## the enclosing BLOCK (`block_decl_type`, the same resolver the instance-collection pass uses, so
        ## the `print_one__Tuple_…` / `Array_…` call site and definition agree). Renders `(u64, u64)` /
        ## `[u64; N]` holes via structural Display instead of dropping them silently.
        if ty.n == 0 and svn.n != 0 and is_scalar_hole == false {
          ltb := block_decl_type(block_head, svn.s, svn.n, cx.src, cx.decls, a)
          if ltb.n != 0 { ty = CSpan(s = ltb.s, n = ltb.n) }
        }
        if is_str_hole {
          ## a str hole (literal or a `: str` var) → write its bytes verbatim via `io::print` (the
          ## `Str` render). `emit_arg` materializes a literal's {ptr,len} / passes a var's addr by-ref.
          emit_arg(argx, sb, cx, a, nl, cx.str_tmp)
          push_str(sb, "  popq %rdi\n  call std__io__print\n")
        } else if is_float_hole {
          ## a float hole → the non-generic `print_one_float`; the value goes in %xmm0 (float ABI).
          emit_gas(argx, sb, cx, a, nl)
          push_str(sb, "  popq %rax\n  movq %rax, %xmm0\n  call std__fmt__print_one_float\n")
        } else if ty.n != 0 {
          emit_arg(argx, sb, cx, a, nl, cx.str_tmp)
          push_str(sb, "  popq %rdi\n  call std__fmt__print_one__")
          emit_type_arg_tag(sb, cx.src, ty.s, ty.n)
          push_str(sb, "\n")
        } else if expr_is_numlit(argx) {
          ## a bare int-literal hole → the non-generic `print_one_int` (default `i64`), no type-arg tag.
          emit_arg(argx, sb, cx, a, nl, cx.str_tmp)
          push_str(sb, "  popq %rdi\n  call std__fmt__print_one_int\n")
        } else if is_scalar_hole {
          ## a SCALAR Var hole whose type wasn't resolved — an INFERRED local (`L := 42`) or a scalar
          ## module GLOBAL — defaults to the int renderer (§7.1: a `{}` hole's default numeric type is
          ## `i64`). Previously it matched no arm and fell through → SILENT no-output for `print("{}", x)`.
          ## Guarded to plain scalars: a struct/enum/array var is NOT rendered as int (it needs Display).
          emit_arg(argx, sb, cx, a, nl, cx.str_tmp)
          push_str(sb, "  popq %rdi\n  call std__fmt__print_one_int\n")
        } else if expr_is_arith_bin(argx) {
          ## an ARITHMETIC EXPRESSION hole (`a + b`) — an int-valued expression with no type span;
          ## the §7.1 default numeric renderer `print_one_int`. Without this it rendered nothing.
          emit_arg(argx, sb, cx, a, nl, cx.str_tmp)
          push_str(sb, "  popq %rdi\n  call std__fmt__print_one_int\n")
        }
        ai += 1
        i += 2
        runstart = fi.ss + i
        step = true
      }
    }
    if step == false { i = i + 1 }
  }
  rtail := (fi.ss + fi.sl) - runstart
  if rtail != 0 { emit_tpl_run(sb, cx, fi.lbl, runstart - fi.ss, rtail) }
  cx.agg_next = aggsave
}
## The VALUE expr of a module-level scalar CONSTANT `name := <const-expr>` (a non-fn kind-0 decl with
## NO alias/brand type span and no arity), else null. A `Var` use of such a name emits the
## compile-time-known value inline — there is no runtime storage (Memory §: module data is init'd
## with a compile-time-known value). Distinguished from an ALIAS / brand (both carry `ret_tl != 0`).
## PERF: name→result caches for module_const_value / mut_global_value (the profile's remaining name
## scans). These are checked for EVERY `Var` reference (most are locals → scan all decls, find nothing,
## return null — the expensive miss); the result POINTER is stable per compile, so a 512-bucket cache
## keyed by name text (bucket empty ⇔ stored len 0; a real name has n>0) returns it O(1). Byte-neutral.
_lnhash := fn(src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize) -> usize {
  if n == 0 { return 0 }
  bs := bytes(str_at((src + s), n))
  h := unchecked { n * 131 + usize(bs[0]) * 7 + usize(bs[n - 1]) }
  h & 511
}
mut _mcv_s : [usize; 512] = [0; 512]
mut _mcv_n : [usize; 512] = [0; 512]
mut _mcv_src : [usize; 512] = [0; 512]
mut _mcv_r : [usize; 512] = [0; 512]
mut _mcv_ms : [usize; 512] = [0; 512]
mut _mcv_ml : [usize; 512] = [0; 512]
mut _mcv_ctx : [bool; 512] = [false; 512]
mut _mgv_s : [usize; 512] = [0; 512]
mut _mgv_n : [usize; 512] = [0; 512]
mut _mgv_src : [usize; 512] = [0; 512]
mut _mgv_r : [usize; 512] = [0; 512]
mut _mgv_ms : [usize; 512] = [0; 512]
mut _mgv_ml : [usize; 512] = [0; 512]
mut _mgv_ctx : [bool; 512] = [false; 512]

## The declaration-module identity used by module-global lookup. The parser/driver flatten every
## source module into one Decl vector, so a bare `LIMIT` in module `left` must be resolved against
## `left`'s declarations before its initializer is folded or its storage label is emitted. Function
## symbols already carry this identity through `LCtx.mod_s/mod_l`; publish the same pair while a
## function or one module's static initializers are lowered. This does not control visibility or ELF
## linkage: it only selects the declaration whose Modules §6 path owns the value/storage symbol.
mut GLOBAL_REF_MOD_S : usize = 0
mut GLOBAL_REF_MOD_L : usize = 0
mut GLOBAL_REF_MOD_SET : bool = false
## The program's declaration vector, published together with the module identity. The SLOT resolver
## (`entry_of`) has no `decls` parameter, yet it is the one place every place-resolution path funnels
## through when a name is NOT a bound local — exactly where a cross-module global reference used to
## become a fresh frame local. Publishing the vector here lets that resolver ask whether the name is a
## module-level global before it hands back a slot. 0 = no program published (early passes).
mut GLOBAL_REF_DECLS : usize = 0
set_global_ref_module := fn(s : usize, l : usize, dp : usize) {
  GLOBAL_REF_MOD_S = s; GLOBAL_REF_MOD_L = l; GLOBAL_REF_MOD_SET = true; GLOBAL_REF_DECLS = dp
  ## TYPE-ANCESTOR: a bare TYPE name resolves against the same module identity a bare global and a
  ## bare call already do (Modules §3), so the ONE publisher establishes all three. `lower_layout`
  ## owns the type-side pair because it owns the resolvers; the anonymous package root travels with
  ## it because `is_root_mod` is a `lower` fact that base module cannot import back.
  lower_layout::set_type_ref_module(s, l, ROOT_MOD_S, ROOT_MOD_L)
}
global_ref_module_eq := fn(src : ptr(u8), d : Decl) -> bool {
  streq(src, d.mod_start, d.mod_len, GLOBAL_REF_MOD_S, GLOBAL_REF_MOD_L)
}
## How closely does the module span `[ms, ml)` relate to the module being lowered? Modules §3 —
## privacy flows DOWN the tree, so exactly this module and its ANCESTORS may be named without a path.
## Module spans are `__`-joined paths (`geo`, `geo__child`), so an ancestor is a SEGMENT-ALIGNED
## PREFIX of the current path. The rank is the ancestor's own path LENGTH, which increases with depth
## and so orders the chain from farthest to nearest; the anonymous package root (no path at all) ranks
## 0, below every named module. -1 = not on this module's ancestor chain (a sibling, a descendant, or
## an unrelated module), which §3 makes unnameable from here.
mod_anc_rank := fn(src : ptr(u8), ms : usize, ml : usize) -> i64 {
  if GLOBAL_REF_MOD_SET == false { return -1 }
  mod_anc_rank_from(src, ms, ml, GLOBAL_REF_MOD_S, GLOBAL_REF_MOD_L)
}

## The same rank against an EXPLICIT naming module `[cs, cl)` instead of the module currently being
## lowered. Every declaration kind other than a module global is checked from a site that knows its
## naming module outright (the declaration whose signature or body spells the reference), so the
## ranking rule lives here once and `mod_anc_rank` is the published-module wrapper over it.
mod_anc_rank_from := fn(src : ptr(u8), ms : usize, ml : usize, cs : usize, cl : usize) -> i64 {
  if ml == cl and mod_seg_eq(src, ms, ml, cs, cl) { return i64(ml) }
  if is_root_mod(ms, ml) { return 0 }
  if ml == 0 { return -1 }
  if ml + 2 >= cl { return -1 }
  sep := str_at((src + cs + ml), 2)
  if sep != "__" and sep != "::" { return -1 }
  if mod_seg_eq(src, ms, ml, cs, ml) == false { return -1 }
  i64(ml)
}

## Modules §3 — may the module being lowered NAME this declaration? Its own and its ancestors'
## declarations always, `pub` or not (that is what lets a submodule use its parent's internals);
## anything else — a sibling's, a descendant's, an unrelated module's — only when the declaration is
## `pub`, and then only through a path. A file-defined module carries no visibility marker of its own,
## so the continuous `pub` chain of §3 collapses to the declaration's own `pub`.
gref_decl_visible := fn(src : ptr(u8), d : Decl) -> bool {
  if mod_anc_rank(src, d.mod_start, d.mod_len) >= 0 { return true }
  decl_is_pub(src, d.name_start)
}

## The same question asked from an EXPLICIT naming module — the predicate every non-global
## declaration kind shares (Modules §3). `decl_is_pub` is the ONE `pub` reader (it handles both
## `pub mut NAME` and a first-line `pub`, which is why this no longer needs a local copy).
decl_visible_from := fn(src : ptr(u8), d : Decl, cs : usize, cl : usize) -> bool {
  if mod_anc_rank_from(src, d.mod_start, d.mod_len, cs, cl) >= 0 { return true }
  decl_is_pub(src, d.name_start)
}

## The NEAREST module on this module's ancestor chain that declares a global named `[ns, nl)`, as its
## rank, or -1 if none does. A nearer declaration SHADOWS a farther one, so the bare-name resolver
## admits only the winner — otherwise the last declaration in decl order won, which is a different
## global depending on file order.
gref_best_rank := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), ns : usize, nl : usize) -> i64 {
  if nl == 0 { return -1 }
  cnt := rt::vec_len(deref(decls))
  th := name_hash(src, ns, nl)
  mut j := dni_lo(cnt, th)
  je := dni_hi(cnt, th)
  mut best := 0 - 1
  while j < je {
    i := dni_at(cnt, j)
    if dnh_skip(cnt, i, th) == false {
      d := deref(decl_get(decls, i))
      if d.is_fn == false and d.kind == 0 and d.ret_tl == 0 and d.arity == 0
         and streq(src, d.name_start, d.name_len, ns, nl) {
        r := mod_anc_rank(src, d.mod_start, d.mod_len)
        if r > best { best = r }
      }
    }
    j += 1
  }
  best
}

## Which declaration owns the global a reference names? A QUALIFIED reference (`geo::G`, Modules §2)
## names its module outright, so the declaration's module must BE that head and §3 visibility decides.
## A BARE reference resolves in this module first and then UP the ancestor chain (§3: a descendant sees
## its ancestors' items, `pub` or not), nearest declaration winning. Some whole-program analysis passes
## inspect an AST before entering `emit_fn` and therefore have no active module pair; preserve the
## historical, sound case where the bare name has exactly one runtime/global declaration in the
## package. A name present in two modules never takes that fallback — its declaration module is
## required, preventing both the former last-wins scalar fold and concatenated array label.
global_ref_candidate := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), d : Decl, s : usize, n : usize) -> bool {
  g := gref_split(src, s, n)
  if g.qual {
    if mod_seg_eq(src, d.mod_start, d.mod_len, g.ms, g.ml) == false { return false }
    return gref_decl_visible(src, d)
  }
  ## Modules §4.1/§4.1.1 — a bare module-global name may be the TAIL of a comptime
  ## alias/projection in the active module. Reuse the same binding resolver that the
  ## callee and generic paths use; otherwise `(PUB_C) := geo` is checked as legal but
  ## `PUB_C` falls through to the caller's global resolver and is rejected as an
  ## unaddressable cross-module global. The binding was visibility-checked before
  ## emission, so its RHS module is the declaration owner we must select here.
  if GLOBAL_REF_MOD_SET {
    bh := binding_head_span(decls, src, g.ns, g.nl, GLOBAL_REF_MOD_S, GLOBAL_REF_MOD_L)
    if bh.n != 0 and mod_seg_eq(src, d.mod_start, d.mod_len, bh.s, bh.n) { return true }
  }
  if global_ref_module_eq(src, d) { return true }
  if GLOBAL_REF_MOD_SET {
    r := mod_anc_rank(src, d.mod_start, d.mod_len)
    if r < 0 { return false }
    return r == gref_best_rank(decls, src, g.ns, g.nl)
  }
  cnt := rt::vec_len(deref(decls))
  mut hits := 0
  mut i := 0
  while i < cnt {
    q := deref(decl_get(decls, i))
    if q.is_fn == false and q.kind == 0 and q.ret_tl == 0 and q.arity == 0
       and streq(src, q.name_start, q.name_len, s, n) { hits += 1 }
    i += 1
  }
  hits == 1
}

## Is the source byte at `i` an identifier byte (alnum or `_`)? The module-path scan below needs it.
gref_ident_byte := fn(src : ptr(u8), i : usize) -> bool {
  b := bytes(str_at((src + i), 1))[0]
  (b >= 48 and b <= 57) or (b >= 65 and b <= 90) or (b >= 97 and b <= 122) or b == 95
}

## A global REFERENCE split into its module-path HEAD and its NAME: `geo::G` gives head `geo` + name
## `G`, a bare `G` gives an empty head + name `G` (Modules §2/§4 — a `::` path is ordinary navigation;
## there is no `use`). Every module-global lookup keys the declaration-name index on the NAME, so the
## NAME is what must hash; the head is what selects the module.
##
## Two spellings reach here. A span the parser kept WHOLE (a callee head, a type path) still carries
## its `::`, so it splits forward. A qualified VALUE path does NOT: `p_factor` keeps only the LAST
## segment in `Expr::Var` (`geo::G` becomes `Var(G)`), exactly as the parser erases `mut` and `pub`.
## So the head is recovered from SOURCE by scanning back off the name — the same source-metadata
## recovery `local_is_mut` and `decl_is_pub` do, and for the same reason: it needs no change to the
## bootstrap-sensitive AST. A name not preceded by `::` is bare, and a `::`-preceded name whose head
## resolves to no module simply fails the module test downstream and is left exactly as it was.
GRefSpan := struct { ms : usize, ml : usize, ns : usize, nl : usize, qual : bool }
gref_split := fn(src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize) -> GRefSpan {
  z := GRefSpan(ms = 0, ml = 0, ns = s, nl = n, qual = false)
  if n == 0 { return z }
  cp := colon_pos(src, s, n)
  if cp > 0 {
    hl := usize(cp)
    if hl + 2 < n { return GRefSpan(ms = s, ml = hl, ns = s + hl + 2, nl = n - hl - 2, qual = true) }
    return z
  }
  if s < 3 { return z }
  if str_at((src + s - 2), 2) != "::" { return z }
  he := s - 2
  mut p := he
  mut scanning := true
  while scanning {
    mut q := p
    while q > 0 and gref_ident_byte(src, q - 1) { q = q - 1 }
    if q == p { return z }
    p = q
    if p >= 2 and str_at((src + p - 2), 2) == "::" { p = p - 2 } else { scanning = false }
  }
  GRefSpan(ms = p, ml = he - p, ns = s, nl = n, qual = true)
}

## Does `[s, n)` (bare, or `mod::`-qualified) name a module-level VALUE declaration ANYWHERE in the
## program — a `mut` global, a const array, or a folded const — regardless of which module owns it?
## This is deliberately module-BLIND: it answers "is this spelling a global at all", which is what
## separates a genuine local from a cross-module global reference. An ALIAS/import binding
## (`strbuf := rt`, `len := math::vec::length`) is excluded: its RHS is a bare path, it is erased at
## compile time (Modules §4.1), and it never owns storage.
gref_names_global := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize) -> bool {
  g := gref_split(src, s, n)
  if g.nl == 0 { return false }
  cnt := rt::vec_len(deref(decls))
  th := name_hash(src, g.ns, g.nl)
  mut j := dni_lo(cnt, th)
  je := dni_hi(cnt, th)
  mut res := false
  while j < je {
    i := dni_at(cnt, j)
    if dnh_skip(cnt, i, th) == false {
      d := deref(decl_get(decls, i))
      if d.is_fn == false and d.kind == 0 and d.ret_tl == 0 and d.arity == 0
         and streq(src, d.name_start, d.name_len, g.ns, g.nl)
         and unchecked bitcast(usize, d.value) != 0
         and var_name_span(d.value).n == 0 { res = true }
    }
    j += 1
  }
  res
}

## Modules §3 — `[s, n)` names a module-level global, but NO declaration this module may address.
## Before this it fell straight through to a fresh frame local: a READ returned an uninitialised slot
## (silently 0), a WRITE stored into the frame and smashed the stack, and the qualified spelling
## `mod::G = v` was a silent no-op. All three are the forbidden silent outcome, so the reference is a
## hard reject instead. Only ever true while a module is published (`GLOBAL_REF_MOD_SET`) — the early
## whole-program passes keep the historical unique-name fallback.
gref_unresolved := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize) -> bool {
  if GLOBAL_REF_MOD_SET == false { return false }
  if gref_names_global(decls, src, s, n) == false { return false }
  if is_module_mut_global(decls, src, s, n) { return false }
  if unchecked bitcast(usize, module_const_value(decls, src, s, n)) != 0 { return false }
  if unchecked bitcast(usize, const_array_value(decls, src, s, n)) != 0 { return false }
  true
}

## The LOCATED half: print the offending source line, then reject.
reject_gref_unresolved := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize) {
  if gref_unresolved(decls, src, s, n) == false { return }
  lower_show_src_line(src, s)
  panic("selfhost: this name is a module-level GLOBAL that the module being compiled may not address (the source line above) — Modules §3: a module sees its own and its ANCESTORS' declarations, and reaches anything else only through a path. Lowering it as a fresh frame local is a silent miscompile (a read returns an uninitialised slot, a write smashes the frame), so it is rejected.")
}

## The same reject taken from a site that has no `decls` parameter (the slot resolver). Uses the
## declaration vector published beside the active module identity; inert until one is published.
reject_gref_unresolved_pub := fn(src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize) {
  if GLOBAL_REF_DECLS == 0 { return }
  reject_gref_unresolved(unchecked bitcast(ptr(rt::Vec), GLOBAL_REF_DECLS), src, s, n)
}

## ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
## Modules §3 for EVERY declaration kind (the module-global path above is the same rule, specialised
## to a place that also has to be ADDRESSED). §3 is one sentence: privacy flows DOWN the module tree,
## exposure flows UP only through `pub` — a non-`pub` declaration of module M is nameable by M and by
## every module nested within M, and by nobody else. A function, a type, a constant and an
## import/alias binding are all "a declaration" for that rule (§4.1 says so explicitly for a binding),
## so one predicate (`decl_visible_from`) and one located reject serve all of them.
##
## Where the check fires is chosen for EXACT attribution: the naming module is always the module of
## the declaration whose signature, member list, binding RHS or body spells the reference — never the
## module that happens to be mid-emission. That matters because an `@inline` callee's body is expanded
## under its CALLER's module, so an emit-time hook would blame the caller for the callee's own
## internal, legitimately-private calls.
##
## What is deliberately NOT rejected: a reference whose module head this compiler cannot resolve
## exactly (an alias head, an unknown/intrinsic name, a name no declaration in the head module
## defines) is left exactly as it was. A reject requires a fully-resolved reference, so the check can
## only ever turn a working program into a diagnostic when §3 really is violated.

## Write `[s, n)` verbatim to stderr — the span twin of `lower_show_src_line`, so a diagnostic can
## name the offending declaration by its own module + name spans without allocating.
vis_show_span := fn(src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize) {
  w := rt::sys_write(1, 2, unchecked bitcast(usize, rt::addr(src, s)), n)
}
vis_show_str := fn(m : str) {
  w := rt::sys_write(1, 2, unchecked bitcast(usize, m.ptr), m.len)
}

## The LOCATED reject: the offending source line, then the declaration this reference names and the
## rule it breaks. `off` is the reference's own source offset (so the line printed is the USE site).
reject_decl_not_visible := fn(src : ptr(u8), d : Decl, off : usize, what : str) {
  lower_show_src_line(src, off)
  vis_show_str("selfhost: the source line above names the ")
  vis_show_str(what)
  vis_show_str(" `")
  vis_show_span(src, d.name_start, d.name_len)
  vis_show_str("` of module `")
  vis_show_span(src, d.mod_start, d.mod_len)
  vis_show_str("`, which is not `pub`.\n")
  panic("selfhost: Modules §3 — privacy flows DOWN the module tree and exposure flows UP only through `pub`: a non-`pub` declaration of module M is visible to M and to every module NESTED WITHIN M, and to nothing else. The naming module is neither, so this reference is rejected (mark the declaration `pub` to expose it upward).")
}

## Modules §3 for a reference that names module `[hs, hl)`'s declaration `[ns, nl)` from module
## `[cs, cl)`. `k1`/`k2` are the declaration KINDS the reference admits — 1/4 a function, 2 a struct,
## 3 an enum/union, 0 a value/constant/alias — so a type path cannot be answered by a same-named
## function and vice versa. The module head must name the declaring module EXACTLY (`mod_seg_eq`, the
## nominal identity test, not the looser last-segment fallback): an inexact head is an alias or an
## unknown, and those are left alone.
vis_check_pair := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), hs : usize, hl : usize, ns : usize, nl : usize, cs : usize, cl : usize, k1 : u8, k2 : u8, what : str) {
  if hl == 0 { return }
  if nl == 0 { return }
  cnt := rt::vec_len(deref(decls))
  th := name_hash(src, ns, nl)
  mut j := dni_lo(cnt, th)
  je := dni_hi(cnt, th)
  while j < je {
    i := dni_at(cnt, j)
    j += 1
    if dnh_skip(cnt, i, th) == false {
      d := deref(decl_get(decls, i))
      if (d.kind == k1 or d.kind == k2) and d.name_len != 0
         and streq(src, d.name_start, d.name_len, ns, nl)
         and mod_seg_eq(src, d.mod_start, d.mod_len, hs, hl)
         and decl_visible_from(src, d, cs, cl) == false {
        reject_decl_not_visible(src, d, ns, what)
      }
    }
  }
}

## The same check for a reference span the AST carries. `gref_split` is the shared splitter: a span the
## parser kept WHOLE (a callee span, an alias RHS) still carries its `::` and splits FORWARD at the
## last one, so `geometry::vec::answer` is module `geometry::vec` + name `answer`; a span the parser
## reduced to its TAIL — a qualified PARAMETER type keeps only `Priv` of `geo::Priv`, exactly as
## `p_factor` keeps only the last segment of a value path — has its head recovered by scanning BACK off
## the name in source, the same recovery `decl_is_pub` and `local_is_mut` do. A span with neither is
## bare and is left alone.
vis_check_qual := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize, cs : usize, cl : usize, k1 : u8, k2 : u8, what : str) {
  if n == 0 { return }
  g := gref_split(src, s, n)
  if g.qual == false { return }
  vis_check_pair(decls, src, g.ms, g.ml, g.ns, g.nl, cs, cl, k1, k2, what)
}

## A qualified TYPE path in any of the three type positions (a parameter, a return type, an aggregate
## member). The declared span may be parenthesised (`Option(codec::Error)`, `ptr(mut rt::Arena)`), so
## only the BASE name is a type reference here; a wrapped inner argument keeps its own `::` inside the
## parens and is checked when the generic instance itself is resolved.
vis_check_type_span := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), ts : usize, tl : usize, cs : usize, cl : usize) {
  if tl == 0 { return }
  bn := base_type_name(src, ts, tl)
  if bn.n == 0 { return }
  vis_check_qual(decls, src, bn.s, bn.n, cs, cl, 2, 3, "type")
}

## Modules §4.3 — a RE-EXPORT (`pub name := path`) may name only `pub` items. A descendant's down-tree
## access to an ancestor's non-`pub` item is for LOCAL use only; re-publishing it upward would override
## the owner's deliberate privacy, so it is a reject even though §3 alone would allow the read.
vis_check_reexport := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), hs : usize, hl : usize, ns : usize, nl : usize) {
  if hl == 0 or nl == 0 { return }
  cnt := rt::vec_len(deref(decls))
  th := name_hash(src, ns, nl)
  mut j := dni_lo(cnt, th)
  je := dni_hi(cnt, th)
  while j < je {
    i := dni_at(cnt, j)
    j += 1
    if dnh_skip(cnt, i, th) == false {
      d := deref(decl_get(decls, i))
      if d.name_len != 0 and streq(src, d.name_start, d.name_len, ns, nl)
         and mod_seg_eq(src, d.mod_start, d.mod_len, hs, hl)
         and decl_is_pub(src, d.name_start) == false {
        lower_show_src_line(src, ns)
        vis_show_str("selfhost: the `pub` binding above re-exports `")
        vis_show_span(src, d.name_start, d.name_len)
        vis_show_str("` of module `")
        vis_show_span(src, d.mod_start, d.mod_len)
        vis_show_str("`, which is not `pub`.\n")
        panic("selfhost: Modules §4.3 — a re-export may name only `pub` items. A descendant's down-tree access to an ancestor's non-`pub` item is for LOCAL use only; `pub name := path` would re-publish it upward and override the owner's privacy, so it is rejected. Re-export never elevates a private item to public.")
      }
    }
  }
}

## Modules §4.1.1 — a LISTED MEMBER PROJECTION `(a, b) := M` binds each listed name to `M::<name>`,
## and "a listed name that is not a `pub` member of `M` (subject to the §3 visibility rule) is a
## compile error". The parser keeps the projection as a nameless kind-0 marker whose `ret` span is the
## VERBATIM source `(a, b, …) := M`, so the names and the module path are recovered from that span —
## the same source-metadata recovery `decl_is_pub` and `local_is_mut` do. `cs/cl` is the projecting
## module; `is_pub` says whether the projection itself is a re-export (§4.3).
vis_check_projection := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), rs : usize, rl : usize, cs : usize, cl : usize, is_pub : bool) {
  if rl < 4 { return }
  if str_at((src + rs), 1) != "(" { return }
  ## locate the `)` that closes the name list, then the RHS path after `:=`
  mut e := rs + 1
  ende := rs + rl
  while e < ende and str_at((src + e), 1) != ")" { e = e + 1 }
  if e >= ende { return }
  mut p := e + 1
  while p < ende and str_at((src + p), 1) != "=" { p = p + 1 }
  if p >= ende { return }
  p = p + 1
  while p < ende and (str_at((src + p), 1) == " " or str_at((src + p), 1) == "\n"
                      or str_at((src + p), 1) == "\t" or str_at((src + p), 1) == "\r") { p = p + 1 }
  if p >= ende { return }
  hs := p
  hl := ende - p
  if hl == 0 { return }
  ## walk the comma-separated name list between `(` and `)`
  mut q := rs + 1
  while q < e {
    while q < e and (str_at((src + q), 1) == " " or str_at((src + q), 1) == ","
                     or str_at((src + q), 1) == "\n" or str_at((src + q), 1) == "\t"
                     or str_at((src + q), 1) == "\r") { q = q + 1 }
    mut r := q
    while r < e and gref_ident_byte(src, r) { r = r + 1 }
    if r == q { q = q + 1 }
    else {
      vis_check_pair(decls, src, hs, hl, q, r - q, cs, cl, 1, 4, "function")
      vis_check_pair(decls, src, hs, hl, q, r - q, cs, cl, 2, 3, "type")
      vis_check_pair(decls, src, hs, hl, q, r - q, cs, cl, 0, 0, "declaration")
      if is_pub { vis_check_reexport(decls, src, hs, hl, q, r - q) }
      q = r
    }
  }
}

## Modules §3 across the DECLARED surface of the whole program: every declaration's signature types
## and aggregate member types, and every import / alias / projection binding, each checked against the
## module that SPELLS it (that declaration's own module). Expression-position references — a qualified
## call, a qualified struct/enum literal — are checked by the reachability walk, which carries the same
## exact module attribution (`_mark_ms`/`_mark_ml`).
vis_check_program := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8)) {
  cnt := rt::vec_len(deref(decls))
  mut i := 0
  while i < cnt {
    d := deref(decl_get(decls, i))
    ## a fn SIGNATURE: each parameter type, then the return type
    mut pp := d.params_head
    while pp != 0 {
      pm := deref(param_p(pp))
      vis_check_type_span(decls, src, pm.ts, pm.tl, d.mod_start, d.mod_len)
      pp = pm.next
    }
    if d.is_fn and d.ret_tl != 0 {
      vis_check_type_span(decls, src, d.ret_ts, d.ret_tl, d.mod_start, d.mod_len)
    }
    ## struct FIELD / enum VARIANT-PAYLOAD types
    mut f := d.fields_head
    while f != 0 {
      fd := deref(fld_p(f))
      vis_check_type_span(decls, src, fd.ts, fd.tl, d.mod_start, d.mod_len)
      f = fd.next
    }
    ## an IMPORT / ALIAS binding (`m := geo::child`, `String := strbuf::StrBuf`): a kind-0 no-op decl
    ## whose `ret` span holds the RHS PATH. Modules §4.1 — what a binding may name follows §3.
    if d.is_fn == false and d.kind == 0 and d.arity == 0 and d.ret_tl != 0 and d.name_len != 0 {
      vis_check_qual(decls, src, d.ret_ts, d.ret_tl, d.mod_start, d.mod_len, 1, 4, "function")
      vis_check_qual(decls, src, d.ret_ts, d.ret_tl, d.mod_start, d.mod_len, 2, 3, "type")
      vis_check_qual(decls, src, d.ret_ts, d.ret_tl, d.mod_start, d.mod_len, 0, 0, "declaration")
      if decl_is_pub(src, d.name_start) {
        gr := gref_split(src, d.ret_ts, d.ret_tl)
        if gr.qual { vis_check_reexport(decls, src, gr.ms, gr.ml, gr.ns, gr.nl) }
      }
    }
    ## a LISTED MEMBER PROJECTION `(a, b) := M` — the nameless kind-0 marker (§4.1.1).
    if d.is_fn == false and d.kind == 0 and d.arity == 0 and d.ret_tl != 0 and d.name_len == 0 {
      vis_check_projection(decls, src, d.ret_ts, d.ret_tl, d.mod_start, d.mod_len, decl_is_pub(src, d.ret_ts))
    }
    i += 1
  }
}

## Whether the source at `[s, n)` is spelled as an ASSIGNMENT (`x = v`, `x += v`) rather than a
## DECLARATION (`x := v`, `x : T = v`, `x : T`). The parser erases the distinction from `Stmt::Assign`
## (like `mut`, recovered by `local_is_mut`), yet it is exactly what tells a legitimate SHADOWING local
## apart from a write to a global: a declaration always introduces a NEW name, so it may freely reuse a
## global's spelling; only an assignment can be a global write and therefore only an assignment may be
## rejected for naming a global this module cannot address.
local_is_plain_assign := fn(src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize) -> bool {
  mut p := s + n
  end := p + 64
  mut c := str_at((src + p), 1)
  while p < end and (c == " " or c == "\t" or c == "\r") { p += 1 ; c = str_at((src + p), 1) }
  if c == ":" { return false }
  if c == "=" { return str_at((src + p + 1), 1) != "=" }
  if c == "+" or c == "-" or c == "*" or c == "/" or c == "%" { return str_at((src + p + 1), 1) == "=" }
  false
}

module_const_value := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize) -> ptr(Expr) {
  h := _lnhash(src, s, n)
  if _mcv_n[h] == n and _mcv_src[h] == src and _mcv_ctx[h] == GLOBAL_REF_MOD_SET
     and _mcv_ml[h] == GLOBAL_REF_MOD_L
     and streq(src, _mcv_ms[h], _mcv_ml[h], GLOBAL_REF_MOD_S, GLOBAL_REF_MOD_L)
     and streq(src, _mcv_s[h], _mcv_n[h], s, n) { return unchecked bitcast(ptr(Expr), _mcv_r[h]) }
  cnt := rt::vec_len(deref(decls))
  mut res := 0
  gr := gref_split(src, s, n)
  th := name_hash(src, gr.ns, gr.nl)
  mut j := dni_lo(cnt, th)
  je := dni_hi(cnt, th)
  while j < je {
    i := dni_at(cnt, j)
    if dnh_skip(cnt, i, th) == false {
      d := deref(decl_get(decls, i))
      ## exclude a MUTABLE global (`mut NAME := …`) — that is runtime .data storage, not a
      ## compile-time constant; `is_module_mut_global` claims it and the read/write paths address it.
      if d.is_fn == false and d.kind == 0 and d.ret_tl == 0 and d.arity == 0 and n != 0
         and global_ref_candidate(decls, src, d, s, n) and streq(src, d.name_start, d.name_len, gr.ns, gr.nl)
         and local_is_mut(src, d.name_start) == false {
        res = unchecked bitcast(usize, d.value)
      }
    }
    j += 1
  }
  if n != 0 {
    _mcv_s[h] = s; _mcv_n[h] = n; _mcv_src[h] = src; _mcv_r[h] = res
    _mcv_ms[h] = GLOBAL_REF_MOD_S; _mcv_ml[h] = GLOBAL_REF_MOD_L; _mcv_ctx[h] = GLOBAL_REF_MOD_SET
  }
  unchecked bitcast(ptr(Expr), res)
}
## The VALUE expr (`StructLit` / `Num` / …) of a MUTABLE module GLOBAL named `[s, n)`, else null. The
## exact dual of `module_const_value` (same `usize` bitcast round-trip, so the big `Decl`-by-value copy
## stays ISOLATED in this frame — the caller then reads `struct_lit_info` in its own frame, matching the
## proven `const_struct_field` shape and avoiding the deep-stack fragility of returning a span directly).
mut_global_value := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize) -> ptr(Expr) {
  h := _lnhash(src, s, n)
  if _mgv_n[h] == n and _mgv_src[h] == src and _mgv_ctx[h] == GLOBAL_REF_MOD_SET
     and _mgv_ml[h] == GLOBAL_REF_MOD_L
     and streq(src, _mgv_ms[h], _mgv_ml[h], GLOBAL_REF_MOD_S, GLOBAL_REF_MOD_L)
     and streq(src, _mgv_s[h], _mgv_n[h], s, n) { return unchecked bitcast(ptr(Expr), _mgv_r[h]) }
  cnt := rt::vec_len(deref(decls))
  mut res := 0
  gr := gref_split(src, s, n)
  th := name_hash(src, gr.ns, gr.nl)
  mut j := dni_lo(cnt, th)
  je := dni_hi(cnt, th)
  while j < je {
    i := dni_at(cnt, j)
    if dnh_skip(cnt, i, th) == false {
      d := deref(decl_get(decls, i))
      if d.is_fn == false and d.kind == 0 and d.ret_tl == 0 and d.arity == 0 and n != 0
         and global_ref_candidate(decls, src, d, s, n) and streq(src, d.name_start, d.name_len, gr.ns, gr.nl)
         and local_is_mut(src, d.name_start) {
        res = unchecked bitcast(usize, d.value)
      }
    }
    j += 1
  }
  if n != 0 {
    _mgv_s[h] = s; _mgv_n[h] = n; _mgv_src[h] = src; _mgv_r[h] = res
    _mgv_ms[h] = GLOBAL_REF_MOD_S; _mgv_ml[h] = GLOBAL_REF_MOD_L; _mgv_ctx[h] = GLOBAL_REF_MOD_SET
  }
  unchecked bitcast(ptr(Expr), res)
}
## The ARRAY value expr of a CONST (non-`mut`) module global `NAME := [ … ]`, else null. A const array
## needs `.data` STORAGE (unlike a scalar/struct const, which `module_const_value` INLINES) because
## `NAME[i]` is a runtime-indexed read. The emission + Index read treat it like a mut array (read-only
## in use — a write is rejected upstream). Isolated `Decl`-copy frame, like `mut_global_value`.
const_array_value := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize) -> ptr(Expr) {
  cnt := rt::vec_len(deref(decls))
  mut res := 0
  gr := gref_split(src, s, n)
  th := name_hash(src, gr.ns, gr.nl)
  mut j := dni_lo(cnt, th)
  je := dni_hi(cnt, th)
  while j < je {
    i := dni_at(cnt, j)
    if dnh_skip(cnt, i, th) == false {
      d := deref(decl_get(decls, i))
      if d.is_fn == false and d.kind == 0 and d.ret_tl == 0 and d.arity == 0 and n != 0
         and global_ref_candidate(decls, src, d, s, n) and streq(src, d.name_start, d.name_len, gr.ns, gr.nl)
         and local_is_mut(src, d.name_start) == false {
        if array_lit_info(d.value).is_a or fixed_array_byte_eek(src, d.name_start, d.name_len) != 0 { res = unchecked bitcast(usize, d.value) }
      }
    }
    j += 1
  }
  unchecked bitcast(ptr(Expr), res)
}
## Does the global decl `d` need `.data` storage emitted? A `mut` global always; a CONST global only
## if its value is an ARRAY (a runtime-indexed table — scalar/struct consts are inlined). Gates the
## module-global emission loop so a const array is materialized without emitting inlined const scalars.
global_needs_storage := fn(src : ptr(u8), d : Decl) -> bool {
  if d.is_fn or d.kind != 0 or d.ret_tl != 0 or d.arity != 0 { return false }
  if local_is_mut(src, d.name_start) { return true }
  array_lit_info(d.value).is_a or fixed_array_byte_eek(src, d.name_start, d.name_len) != 0
}
## True if expr `v` is a bare `Var` naming a MUTABLE STRUCT global (`p := STATE`). Returns a BOOL (not
## a span) so the deep-stack CSpan fragility never bites; the caller recovers the struct span in its own
## frame via `struct_lit_info(mut_global_value(...))`. Used to bind + word-copy a whole-struct snapshot.
is_mut_struct_global_var := fn(v : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8)) -> bool {
  vn := var_name_span(v)
  if vn.n == 0 { return false }
  mgv := mut_global_value(decls, src, vn.s, vn.n)
  if unchecked bitcast(usize, mgv) == 0 { return false }
  struct_lit_info(mgv).is_s
}
## The inner NAME span of a static binding's `@section("name")` placement attribute (Memory §2.3), else
## 0/0. Scans BACKWARD from the decl name over the prefix region (whitespace + the `mut`/`pub` words) to
## an immediately-preceding `@section("…")` — a source-scan like `local_is_mut` (the marker is not carried
## on the Decl). `src/`+`lib/` name no `@section`, so this never fires for the self-build (fixpoint-neutral).
global_section_name := fn(src : ptr(u8), name_s : usize) -> CSpan {
  mut p := name_s
  mut scanning := true
  while scanning {
    while p > 0 and (str_at((src + p - 1), 1) == " " or str_at((src + p - 1), 1) == "\n" or str_at((src + p - 1), 1) == "\t" or str_at((src + p - 1), 1) == "\r") { p = p - 1 }
    if p >= 3 and str_at((src + p - 3), 3) == "mut" { p = p - 3 }
    else if p >= 3 and str_at((src + p - 3), 3) == "pub" { p = p - 3 }
    else { scanning = false }
  }
  while p > 0 and (str_at((src + p - 1), 1) == " " or str_at((src + p - 1), 1) == "\n" or str_at((src + p - 1), 1) == "\t" or str_at((src + p - 1), 1) == "\r") { p = p - 1 }
  ## p is now just past the `)` of `@section(...)`, if present
  if p < 12 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  if str_at((src + p - 1), 1) != ")" { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  clq := p - 2                                     ## the closing quote (byte before `)`)
  if str_at((src + clq), 1) != "\"" { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  mut oq := clq                             ## scan back to the opening quote
  while oq > 0 and str_at((src + oq - 1), 1) != "\"" { oq = oq - 1 }
  if oq == 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  opq := oq - 1                                    ## the opening quote position
  if opq < 9 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  if str_at((src + opq - 9), 10) != "@section(\"" { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  CSpan(s = oq, n = clq - oq)                      ## the inner name text [oq, clq)
}
## The EXACT linker symbol of a `@export("name")` directive attached to the declaration named at
## `[name_s, name_s+name_l)` (Modules §6.3), or 0/0 if none. Attributes are consumed + discarded by
## the parser, so recover both the declaration-prefix form and the value-position form. The latter
## must scan FORWARD from `name :=`, just like `extern_symbol`; a backward scan can never see it.
pub export_name := fn(src : ptr(u8), name_s : usize, name_l : usize) -> CSpan {
  ## VALUE position: `name := [attributes] fn(…)`.
  mut v := name_s + name_l
  while str_at((src + v), 1) == " " or str_at((src + v), 1) == "\n" or str_at((src + v), 1) == "\t" or str_at((src + v), 1) == "\r" { v = v + 1 }
  if str_at((src + v), 2) == ":=" {
    v = v + 2
    mut attrs := true
    while attrs {
      while str_at((src + v), 1) == " " or str_at((src + v), 1) == "\n" or str_at((src + v), 1) == "\t" or str_at((src + v), 1) == "\r" { v = v + 1 }
      if str_at((src + v), 1) != "@" { attrs = false }
      else {
        if str_at((src + v), 7) == "@export" {
          c := str_at((src + v + 7), 1)
          if c == "(" or c == " " or c == "\n" or c == "\t" or c == "\r" {
            mut q := v + 7
            while str_at((src + q), 1) == " " or str_at((src + q), 1) == "\n" or str_at((src + q), 1) == "\t" or str_at((src + q), 1) == "\r" { q = q + 1 }
            if str_at((src + q), 1) == "(" {
              q = q + 1
              while str_at((src + q), 1) == " " or str_at((src + q), 1) == "\n" or str_at((src + q), 1) == "\t" or str_at((src + q), 1) == "\r" { q = q + 1 }
              if str_at((src + q), 1) == "\"" {
                es := q + 1
                mut ee := es
                while str_at((src + ee), 1) != "\"" { ee = ee + 1 }
                return CSpan(s = es, n = ee - es)
              }
            }
          }
        }
        ## Skip this attribute, including a balanced argument list, and inspect the next one.
        v = v + 1
        while (str_at((src + v), 1) >= "a" and str_at((src + v), 1) <= "z") or (str_at((src + v), 1) >= "A" and str_at((src + v), 1) <= "Z") or (str_at((src + v), 1) >= "0" and str_at((src + v), 1) <= "9") or str_at((src + v), 1) == "_" { v = v + 1 }
        if str_at((src + v), 1) == "(" {
          mut depth := 1
          v = v + 1
          while depth > 0 {
            c := str_at((src + v), 1)
            if c == "\"" {
              v = v + 1
              while str_at((src + v), 1) != "\"" { v = v + 1 }
              v = v + 1
            } else if c == "(" { depth = depth + 1; v = v + 1 }
            else if c == ")" { depth = depth - 1; v = v + 1 }
            else { v = v + 1 }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  ## DECLARATION prefix: preserve `@export("name") name`, including `pub`/`mut`/`@inline` before name.
  mut p := name_s
  mut scanning := true
  while scanning {
    while p > 0 and (str_at((src + p - 1), 1) == " " or str_at((src + p - 1), 1) == "\n" or str_at((src + p - 1), 1) == "\t" or str_at((src + p - 1), 1) == "\r") { p = p - 1 }
    if p >= 3 and str_at((src + p - 3), 3) == "mut" { p = p - 3 }
    else if p >= 3 and str_at((src + p - 3), 3) == "pub" { p = p - 3 }
    else if p >= 7 and str_at((src + p - 7), 7) == "@inline" { p = p - 7 }
    else { scanning = false }
  }
  while p > 0 and (str_at((src + p - 1), 1) == " " or str_at((src + p - 1), 1) == "\n" or str_at((src + p - 1), 1) == "\t" or str_at((src + p - 1), 1) == "\r") { p = p - 1 }
  if p < 11 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  if str_at((src + p - 1), 1) != ")" { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  clq := p - 2
  if str_at((src + clq), 1) != "\"" { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  mut oq := clq
  while oq > 0 and str_at((src + oq - 1), 1) != "\"" { oq = oq - 1 }
  if oq == 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  opq := oq - 1
  if opq < 8 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  if str_at((src + opq - 8), 9) != "@export(\"" { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  CSpan(s = oq, n = clq - oq)
}
## The EXTERNAL linker symbol a `@extern` fn (declared at `name_s`, length `name_l`) resolves to
## (Modules §7.2): the EXACT `@extern("sym")` name if given, else the DECLARED name (the default),
## else 0/0 when the fn is not `@extern`. `@extern` is a VALUE-position attribute (`name := @extern …`),
## so this scans FORWARD from the name over `:= @extern`; `export_name` handles that direction too and
## additionally preserves the declaration-prefix spelling. Drives call routing (`emit_mangled_call`) +
## skipping the (bodyless) definition.
pub extern_symbol := fn(src : ptr(u8), name_s : usize, name_l : usize) -> CSpan {
  mut p := name_s + name_l
  while str_at((src + p), 1) == " " or str_at((src + p), 1) == "\n" or str_at((src + p), 1) == "\t" or str_at((src + p), 1) == "\r" { p = p + 1 }
  if str_at((src + p), 2) != ":=" { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }   ## `@extern` only on a `:=` binding
  p = p + 2
  while str_at((src + p), 1) == " " or str_at((src + p), 1) == "\n" or str_at((src + p), 1) == "\t" or str_at((src + p), 1) == "\r" { p = p + 1 }
  if str_at((src + p), 7) != "@extern" { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  mut q := p + 7
  ## boundary: `@extern` must be a whole attribute (next is `(` or whitespace), not a longer ident.
  bc := str_at((src + q), 1)
  if not (bc == "(" or bc == " " or bc == "\n" or bc == "\t" or bc == "\r") { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  while str_at((src + q), 1) == " " or str_at((src + q), 1) == "\n" or str_at((src + q), 1) == "\t" or str_at((src + q), 1) == "\r" { q = q + 1 }
  ## `@extern("sym")` — exact external name in the parens.
  if str_at((src + q), 1) == "(" {
    q = q + 1
    while str_at((src + q), 1) == " " or str_at((src + q), 1) == "\n" or str_at((src + q), 1) == "\t" or str_at((src + q), 1) == "\r" { q = q + 1 }
    if str_at((src + q), 1) == "\"" {
      oq := q + 1
      mut clq := oq
      while str_at((src + clq), 1) != "\"" { clq = clq + 1 }
      return CSpan(s = oq, n = clq - oq)
    }
  }
  ## bare `@extern` — default form: the symbol is the DECLARED name.
  CSpan(s = name_s, n = name_l)
}
## True if expr `v` is a bare `Var` naming a mutable ENUM global (`s := STATE`, STATE a `mut S :=
## Color.V(…)`). Enables binding `s` as that enum + copying its `[disc, payload…]` words from `.data`.
is_mut_enum_global_var := fn(v : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8)) -> bool {
  vn := var_name_span(v)
  if vn.n == 0 { return false }
  mgv := mut_global_value(decls, src, vn.s, vn.n)
  if unchecked bitcast(usize, mgv) == 0 { return false }
  enum_lit_info(mgv).is_e
}
## The ENUM-element shape of a module-level ARRAY global, read off its INITIALIZER value expr `gv`
## (`mut GE := [E.A(1), E.B(2)]`): `es`/`el` = element 0's enum HEAD span (`E`, `Option(i64)`),
## `stride` = the element's instance width `1 + enum_inst_words` (the discriminant word plus the
## WIDEST variant's payload words), `nel` = the element count. `{is_e = false, …}` for anything else.
## This is the ONE place the four sides of an enum-element array global agree on its layout — the
## `.data` image (`[disc, payload…, pad]` per element), the whole-element READ (`e := GE[i]`), the
## `match GE[i]` scrutinee, and the element WRITE — so a stride mismatch between them is impossible.
## Takes the already-resolved value pointer (never a name) so no caller pays a decl scan for it.
GAEnum := struct { is_e : bool, es : usize, el : usize, stride : usize, nel : usize }
global_arr_enum := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), gv : ptr(Expr), a : rt::Arena) -> GAEnum {
  z := GAEnum(is_e = false, es = 0, el = 0, stride = 0, nel = 0)
  if unchecked bitcast(usize, gv) == 0 { return z }
  ali := array_lit_info(gv)
  if ali.is_a == false { return z }
  if ali.nel == 0 { return z }
  e0 := arg_expr_at(ali.ehead, 0, a)
  if unchecked bitcast(usize, e0) == 0 { return z }
  ei := enum_lit_info(e0)
  if ei.is_e == false { return z }
  GAEnum(is_e = true, es = ei.es, el = ei.el, stride = 1 + enum_inst_words(decls, src, ei.es, ei.el, a), nel = ali.nel)
}
## The `.data` INITIALIZER value expr of a module-level ARRAY global named `[s, n)` — a `mut` global
## first, then a CONST array (`NAME := […]`, which also gets `.data` storage, read-only). Null when the
## name is a FRAME local (tested first, so a local array never pays the const decl SCAN — the `mut`
## lookup is name-hash cached, the const one is not) or names no array global.
global_arr_value := fn(slots : ptr(SVec), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize) -> ptr(Expr) {
  z := unchecked bitcast(ptr(Expr), 0)
  if n == 0 { return z }
  if slot_of(slots, src, s, n) >= 0 { return z }
  mut gv := mut_global_value(decls, src, s, n)
  if unchecked bitcast(usize, gv) == 0 { gv = const_array_value(decls, src, s, n) }
  gv
}
## Resolve the declaration selected by the module-global rules for an ARRAY name. The old callers
## walked every declaration and let `global_ref_candidate` repeat its binding/projection scans for
## each one. The name index is already live by emission time; `dni_*` degrades to that exact full
## declaration-order walk before the index is built, and each bucket remains in original order.
## Returning the FIRST selected declaration preserves the old first-match behavior, including same-name
## declarations in different modules.
global_array_decl_idx := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), ns : usize, nl : usize) -> i64 {
  if nl == 0 { return 0 - 1 }
  gr := gref_split(src, ns, nl)
  cnt := rt::vec_len(deref(decls))
  th := name_hash(src, gr.ns, gr.nl)
  mut j := dni_lo(cnt, th)
  je := dni_hi(cnt, th)
  while j < je {
    i := dni_at(cnt, j)
    if dnh_skip(cnt, i, th) == false {
      d := deref(decl_get(decls, i))
      if global_ref_candidate(decls, src, d, ns, nl) and streq(src, d.name_start, d.name_len, gr.ns, gr.nl) {
        return i64(i)
      }
    }
    j += 1
  }
  0 - 1
}

## The BYTE element kind of an explicitly typed module-level fixed ARRAY global, else 0. The AST's
## ArrayLit carries no scalar annotation, so recover `[u8|i8|bits8; N]` from the declaration source,
## exactly as the local byte-array tier does. Inferred globals intentionally remain on the historical
## word ABI until their type/layout contract is widened.
global_array_byte_eek := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), ns : usize, nl : usize) -> u8 {
  di := global_array_decl_idx(decls, src, ns, nl)
  if di < 0 { return 0 }
  d := deref(decl_get(decls, usize(di)))
  fixed_array_byte_eek(src, d.name_start, d.name_len)
}
## The STATIC ELEMENT COUNT of a module-level fixed ARRAY global. Array literals carry their count in the
## AST; an `embed(...)` initializer is a StrLit, so recover N from the declaration's `[T; N]` annotation.
## Returns 0 for non-array globals. This keeps bounds, address-of, loads, stores, and `.len` on one query.
global_array_len := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), ns : usize, nl : usize, v : ptr(Expr)) -> usize {
  ai := array_lit_info(v)
  if ai.is_a { return ai.nel }
  di := global_array_decl_idx(decls, src, ns, nl)
  if di < 0 { return 0 }
  d := deref(decl_get(decls, usize(di)))
  lt := local_type_span(src, d.name_start, d.name_len)
  if lt.n != 0 and array_elem_span(src, lt.s, lt.n).n != 0 { return parse_arr_len(src, lt.s, lt.n) }
  0
}
## True if `[s, n)` names a MUTABLE module-level GLOBAL — a kind-0 non-fn zero-arity value decl whose
## name is preceded by `mut` in source (`mut COUNTER := 0`). Such a decl gets runtime `.data` storage
## + label-addressed reads/writes, NOT compile-time inlining (`module_const_value` excludes these).
## Scalar (single-word) globals only for now.
is_module_mut_global := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize) -> bool {
  if n == 0 { return false }
  cnt := rt::vec_len(deref(decls))
  mut res := false
  gr := gref_split(src, s, n)
  th := name_hash(src, gr.ns, gr.nl)
  mut j := dni_lo(cnt, th)
  je := dni_hi(cnt, th)
  while j < je {
    i := dni_at(cnt, j)
    if dnh_skip(cnt, i, th) == false {
      d := deref(decl_get(decls, i))
      if d.is_fn == false and d.kind == 0 and d.ret_tl == 0 and d.arity == 0
         and global_ref_candidate(decls, src, d, s, n) and streq(src, d.name_start, d.name_len, gr.ns, gr.nl)
         and local_is_mut(src, d.name_start) {
        res = true
      }
    }
    j += 1
  }
  res
}
## Emit the `.data` LABEL of a mutable module global (`<module>__<NAME>`, mangled like a fn def),
## resolved by name — the addressing target shared by its reads (`LABEL(%rip)`), writes, and the
## `.data` definition, so all three agree.
emit_global_label := fn(in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize) {
  cnt := rt::vec_len(deref(decls))
  gr := gref_split(src, s, n)
  th := name_hash(src, gr.ns, gr.nl)
  mut j := dni_lo(cnt, th)
  je := dni_hi(cnt, th)
  while j < je {
    i := dni_at(cnt, j)
    if dnh_skip(cnt, i, th) == false {
      d := deref(decl_get(decls, i))
      ## `global_needs_storage` covers a `mut` global AND a const ARRAY (both get a `.data` label);
      ## a scalar/struct const is inlined (no label), so it is correctly excluded.
      if global_ref_candidate(decls, src, d, s, n) and streq(src, d.name_start, d.name_len, gr.ns, gr.nl)
         and global_needs_storage(src, d) {
        emit_mangled_def(sb, src, d.mod_start, d.mod_len, d.name_start, d.name_len)
        return
      }
    }
    j += 1
  }
}
## Resolve a `Var` that names a module-level CONSTANT to its value expr (the `StructLit` / `StrLit` /
## `Num` / …), else return `v` unchanged. So `p := ORIGIN` / `s := MSG` bind + copy the const's value
## via the existing struct-lit / str-lit machinery. A non-const Var (a local) passes through.
const_rhs := fn(v : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8)) -> ptr(Expr) {
  vn := var_name_span(v)
  if vn.n != 0 {
    mcv := module_const_value(decls, src, vn.s, vn.n)
    if unchecked bitcast(usize, mcv) != 0 { return mcv }
  }
  v
}
## The VALUE expr of field `[fs, fl)` of a module-level CONST STRUCT named by `base` (`ORIGIN.x`
## where `ORIGIN := Pt(x = …, y = …)`), else null. The const's `StructLit` args are in field
## DECLARATION order (as `emit_struct_assign` places them), so the field's value is the arg at its
## declaration index. Lets a const-struct field access emit the compile-time value inline.
const_struct_field := fn(base : ptr(Expr), fs : usize, fl : usize, decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> ptr(Expr) {
  z := unchecked bitcast(ptr(Expr), 0)
  vn := var_name_span(base)
  if vn.n == 0 { return z }
  mcv := module_const_value(decls, src, vn.s, vn.n)
  if unchecked bitcast(usize, mcv) == 0 { return z }
  sli := struct_lit_info(mcv)
  if sli.is_s == false { return z }
  di := struct_decl_of(decls, src, sli.ss, sli.sl)
  if di < 0 { return z }
  d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(di))))
  mut f := d.fields_head
  mut idx := 0
  mut found := 0 - 1
  while f != 0 {
    fdn := deref(fld_p(f))
    if streq(src, fdn.ns, fdn.nl, fs, fl) { found = i64(idx) }
    idx += 1
    f = fdn.next
  }
  if found < 0 { return z }
  arg_expr_at(struct_lit_fields(mcv), usize(found), a)
}
## The DECLARATION index of field `[fs, fl)` within struct type `[ss, sl)` (-1 if absent) — the shared
## field-ordering that struct layout, const-struct field reads, and mutable struct-global fields use.
struct_field_index := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), ss : usize, sl : usize, fs : usize, fl : usize, a : rt::Arena) -> i64 {
  di := struct_decl_of(decls, src, ss, sl)
  if di < 0 { return 0 - 1 }
  d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(di))))
  mut f := d.fields_head
  mut idx := 0
  mut found := 0 - 1
  while f != 0 {
    fdn := deref(fld_p(f))
    if streq(src, fdn.ns, fdn.nl, fs, fl) { found = i64(idx) }
    idx += 1
    f = fdn.next
  }
  found
}

## Resolve a FIELD CHAIN rooted at a module-level CONST STRUCT to its literal value. `const_struct_field`
## handles the depth-1 scalar reader, but a `str` VALUE operand needs the same fold for `G.name` and
## `G.q.name` before `emit_str_pair` can push the literal's `{ptr,len}` pair. Returning the literal
## expression keeps all runtime paths out of this const-only case; a const field that aliases another
## const is normalized by `const_rhs` at the call site.
const_field_value := fn(e : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> ptr(Expr) {
  z := unchecked bitcast(ptr(Expr), 0)
  mut res : ptr(Expr) = z
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Var(s, n) => { res = module_const_value(decls, src, s, n) }
    Expr::Field(base, fs, fl) => {
      cv := const_field_value(base, decls, src, a)
      if unchecked bitcast(usize, cv) != 0 {
        sli := struct_lit_info(cv)
        if sli.is_s {
          fi := struct_field_index(decls, src, sli.ss, sli.sl, fs, fl, a)
          if fi >= 0 { res = arg_expr_at(struct_lit_fields(cv), usize(fi), a) }
        }
      }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  res
}
emit_mangled_call := fn(in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, src : ptr(u8), cs : usize, cl : usize, ms : usize, ml : usize, decls : ptr(rt::Vec)) {
  cp := colon_pos(src, cs, cl)
  if cp >= 0 {
    hl := usize(cp)                       ## head length (the module name OR a module ALIAS)
    ts := cs + hl + 2                     ## tail (fn) name span, skipping `::`
    tl := cl - hl - 2
    ## qualified `mod::f` — resolve `f` by its TAIL name, PREFERRING the literal head module.
    ## A real module head wins (its own copy of `f`); a module ALIAS head (`strbuf := rt`, lost
    ## as a no-op decl at parse time) matches no decl's module, so prefer falls through to `f`'s
    ## first real definition — e.g. `strbuf::push_str` → `rt__push_str`. Falls back to the literal
    ## head when no decl named `f` exists (an intrinsic / stdlib fn not in the tree).
    idx := callee_decl_idx_qual(decls, src, ts, tl, cs, hl, ms, ml)
    mut out_ns := ts
    mut out_nl := tl
    if idx >= 0 {
      cd := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(idx))))
      ## `@extern` callee (Modules §7): emit the EXTERNAL symbol, not the mangled name.
      esn := extern_symbol(src, cd.name_start, cd.name_len)
      if esn.n != 0 { push_str(sb, str_at((src + esn.s), esn.n)); return }
      emit_mod_qual(sb, src, cd.mod_start, cd.mod_len)
      ## A binding/re-export may resolve the source tail to a differently named declaration
      ## (`re_answer := geo::answer`). The module comes from `cd` above, so the suffix must too.
      out_ns = cd.name_start
      out_nl = cd.name_len
    } else {
      ## no decl named the tail fn — emit the literal module-path head (mangling its `::` → `__`).
      emit_mangled_head(sb, src, cs, hl)
      push_str(sb, "__")
    }
    push_str(sb, str_at((src + out_ns), out_nl))
  } else {
    ## bare call — resolve the defining module (a destructure-import / alias may differ from ms/ml)
    ## Modules §3: if the name is declared in two or more modules and this one may name NONE of them,
    ## the call has no answer. It used to take the first declaration in decl order — an unrelated
    ## module's private duplicate — so reject it located instead (`callee_ambiguous` is silent for
    ## every call that does resolve, so this costs one hash probe on the failing path only).
    reject_callee_ambiguous(decls, src, cs, cl, ms, ml)
    idx := callee_decl_idx(decls, src, cs, cl, ms, ml)
    if idx >= 0 {
      cd := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(idx))))
      ## `@extern` callee (Modules §7): emit the EXTERNAL symbol, not the mangled name.
      esn := extern_symbol(src, cd.name_start, cd.name_len)
      if esn.n != 0 { push_str(sb, str_at((src + esn.s), esn.n)); return }
      emit_mod_qual(sb, src, cd.mod_start, cd.mod_len)
    } else {
      emit_mod_qual(sb, src, ms, ml)
    }
    push_str(sb, str_at((src + cs), cl))
  }
}

## Emit ONE type-argument tag `[ts, ts+tl)` into a mangled label. For a bare type-arg (`u64`) this
## is just its lexeme (byte-identical to the former `push_str`). For a generic INSTANCE type-arg
## (`Option(u64)` — recorded as the bare head `Option` with the `(u64)` intact in source right after,
## per `typearg_at`), the head is followed by `_<arg>` for each top-level type-argument (`Option_u64`,
## `Result_u64_AllocError`), read via the PROVEN `typearg_at` re-scan — so the tag is a valid,
## paren-free GAS symbol that DISTINGUISHES `Option(u64)` from `Option(i64)`. Def + call both route
## through here, so the two always agree. (Nested generic args keep their inner lexeme verbatim — a
## rare case; the top-level flattening covers the common Option/Result payloads.)
## Does the `(` at `lp` open a STRUCT LITERAL `(field = value, …)` rather than a generic type-argument
## list `(T, …)`? A `field =` (a `=` before the first `,`/`)`) marks a struct construction. Used by
## `emit_type_arg_tag` to NOT mangle a struct-lit-initialized local's `Pt(x = 40, …)` initializer (whose
## type span's name sits AT the `Pt` of that lit) as if `(x = 40, …)` were type-arguments.
paren_is_struct_lit := fn(src : ptr(u8), lp : usize) -> bool {
  mut p := lp + 1
  mut res := false
  mut scanning := true
  mut guard := 0
  while scanning and guard < 4096 {
    c := str_at((src + p), 1)
    if c == "=" { res = true; scanning = false }
    else if c == "," or c == ")" or c == "" { scanning = false }
    else { p = p + 1 }
    guard += 1
  }
  res
}
emit_type_arg_tag := fn(in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, src : ptr(u8), ts : usize, tl : usize) {
  ## A TUPLE type-arg `(T0, T1, …)` mangles to the paren-free tag `Tuple_<T0>_<T1>_…` (the `(` sits at
  ## position 0 of the span, so `typearg_at(src, ts, 0, j)` reads the j-th component). Def + call both
  ## route through here, so the two agree and `(u64, u64)` stays a valid GAS symbol distinct from a struct.
  if str_at((src + ts), 1) == "(" {
    push_str(sb, "Tuple")
    mut jt := 0
    mut got := true
    while got {
      ct := typearg_at(src, ts, 0, jt)
      if ct.n == 0 { got = false } else {
        push_str(sb, "_")
        push_str(sb, str_at((src + ct.s), ct.n))
        jt += 1
      }
    }
    return
  }
  ## An ARRAY type-arg `[T; N]` mangles to the paren-free tag `Array_<T>_<N>` — a valid GAS symbol
  ## distinguishing `[u64; 3]` from `[u64; 4]` / `[i64; 3]`. Def + call both route here → they agree.
  if str_at((src + ts), 1) == "[" {
    aes := array_elem_span(src, ts, tl)
    push_str(sb, "Array_")
    push_str(sb, str_at((src + aes.s), aes.n))
    push_str(sb, "_")
    push_int(sb, i64(parse_arr_len(src, ts, tl)))
    return
  }
  push_str(sb, str_at((src + ts), tl))
  ## GENERIC type-arguments `Name(T, …)` → `Name_T_…`. Skip when the `(` after the name opens a
  ## struct LITERAL `(field = value, …)` — that happens when the type span's name sits at a struct-lit
  ## initializer (`p := Pt(x = 40, …)`): those are not type-args, and mangling them yielded an invalid
  ## symbol `Pt_x = 40_y = 2` that didn't match the `print_one__Pt` definition.
  if paren_is_struct_lit(src, ts + tl) == false {
    mut j := 0
    mut going := true
    while going {
      ta := typearg_at(src, ts, tl, j)
      if ta.n == 0 { going = false } else {
        push_str(sb, "_")
        push_str(sb, str_at((src + ta.s), ta.n))
        j += 1
      }
    }
  }
}

## Emit a GENERIC fn INSTANCE label `<module>__<fn>__<typetag>` — the same-module prefix + the fn
## name + each concrete type-argument tag (via `emit_type_arg_tag`), all joined by `__` (a valid GAS
## symbol; no `::`/`$`). Used for BOTH the instance definition (`emit_program`'s mono pass) and the
## call site (the call routes to its instance), so the two always agree.
emit_generic_label := fn(in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, src : ptr(u8), ms : usize, ml : usize, ns : usize, nl : usize, ts : usize, tl : usize, ts2 : usize, tl2 : usize, ts3 : usize, tl3 : usize) {
  emit_mod_qual(sb, src, ms, ml)
  push_str(sb, str_at((src + ns), nl))
  push_str(sb, "__")
  emit_type_arg_tag(sb, src, ts, tl)
  ## SECOND type-arg tag for a 2-type-param generic (`map(T,U)` → `…__map__<T>__<U>`); absent
  ## (byte-identical to the single-tag form) when `tl2 == 0`.
  if tl2 != 0 {
    push_str(sb, "__")
    emit_type_arg_tag(sb, src, ts2, tl2)
  }
  ## THIRD type-arg tag for a 3-type-param generic (`Result::map(T,E,U)` → `…__map__<T>__<E>__<U>`);
  ## absent when `tl3 == 0`.
  if tl3 != 0 {
    push_str(sb, "__")
    emit_type_arg_tag(sb, src, ts3, tl3)
  }
}

## Look up a variable's slot offset by name span (last match wins; -1 if unbound — a
## well-formed program never hits the -1 path, the upstream sema pass rejects unbound names).
## PERF (perf 2026-08-15): the slot scans were 12%+ of self-build flat time, and almost none of it was
## the name compare — each iteration paid a GENERIC `svec_at` call, a `svec_stride()` call, and a NINE-WORD
## `SlotEntry` copy just to look at `ns`/`nl`. Here the stride + region base are hoisted out of the loop and
## the two name words are read THROUGH the entry pointer (no by-value copy), so a non-matching slot costs
## two loads. `streq` remains the arbiter and the last-match/first-match order is unchanged → byte-identical.
slot_of := fn(slots : ptr(SVec), src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize) -> i64 {
  cnt := svec_len(slots)
  st := svec_stride()
  base := deref(slots).base
  mut res := -1
  mut i := 0
  while i < cnt {
    e : ptr(mut SlotEntry) = unchecked bitcast(ptr(mut SlotEntry), base + i * st)
    ## LENGTH pre-check INLINE: `streq` rejects on length too, but only after the call + two `str_at`
    ## span builds. Most slots differ in name length, so this skips the call outright. Same verdict.
    if deref(e).nl == n {
      if streq(src, deref(e).ns, n, s, n) { res = i64(deref(e).off) }
    }
    i += 1
  }
  res
}

## Look up the full slot ENTRY for a name (last match wins), so a `Field` read can recover
## both the base offset and the struct TYPE name (to resolve the field index). Returns a
## sentinel entry (sns = snl = 0, off = 0) if unbound — sema rejects that case upstream.
## The INDEX of the slot entry for a name (last match wins); 0 if unbound (sema rejects that
## upstream, so callers read entry 0's data harmlessly rather than a zero sentinel). Returns the
## index — NOT the `SlotEntry` by value (a 9-word struct return is truncated to 3 words; see
## `svec_at`). Callers recover the full entry with `deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, slots, entry_of(…)))`,
## a 9-word copy resolved by the `SlotEntry` type-arg.
entry_of := fn(slots : ptr(SVec), src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize) -> usize {
  cnt := svec_len(slots)
  st := svec_stride()
  base := deref(slots).base
  mut res := 0
  mut found := false
  mut i := 0
  while i < cnt {
    e : ptr(mut SlotEntry) = unchecked bitcast(ptr(mut SlotEntry), base + i * st)
    if deref(e).nl == n {
      if streq(src, deref(e).ns, n, s, n) { res = i ; found = true }
    }
    i += 1
  }
  ## The name is NOT a bound local/param. Every place-resolution path reaches here, so this is where a
  ## module-global reference the emitter cannot address used to be handed entry 0 (the unbound
  ## sentinel, `off = 0`) and silently lowered as `-8(%rbp)`. Reject it instead; a name that is not a
  ## global anywhere in the program keeps the historical sentinel behaviour untouched.
  if found == false { reject_gref_unresolved_pub(src, s, n) }
  res
}

## ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
## I11 (correct-or-trap) — the NARROW-RESERVATION collision.
##
## Alatyr `:=` locals are FUNCTION-scoped, and every `bind_*_slot` below no-ops on a name it has
## already bound (`existing := slot_of(…); if existing >= 0 { return }`) — that reuse is deliberate
## and pervasive (a name rebound to the same shape must not grow the frame). It is sound only while
## every binding of the name needs the SAME number of words. When a later AGGREGATE binding needs
## MORE words than the first binding reserved, the reservation is silently too narrow and BOTH ends
## of the aggregate go wrong:
##
##  - the STORE writes the aggregate's words outwards from the base slot, so the words past the
##    reservation land on the slots that belong to the NEIGHBOURING locals, and
##  - the FIELD READ addresses word 0 by the aggregate convention — one slot away from where the
##    narrow store actually put it — so it reads a slot NOTHING ever wrote: stale stack content
##    left there by an earlier call at the same depth.
##
## Because the value read is stale STACK data, the miscompile is a FRAME-LAYOUT LOTTERY: it changes
## with the frame size of this function and of every caller above it, and the TOOL-1 fixpoint cannot
## see it — the emitted GAS stays self-consistent, so `seed == Stage1 == Stage2` holds through a red
## state and only a per-file corpus manifest catches the wrong behaviour.
##
## The instance this guard was written for: `emit_gas` bound `tbc` twice — once as a `usize` match
## scratch level, once as a 3-word `TupleByteComponent`. The struct binding was dropped, its
## 3-register return was stored over the two neighbouring locals' slots, and `tbc.ok` read the slot
## one above the base — a stale word. When that word was non-zero the `Index` arm took the
## standard-byte-tuple-component path for a plain nested/array-of tuple: `t.N.M` then failed loud in
## `emit_index_addr`, and `array_of_tuples` returned 1 instead of 42 — a silent wrong answer.
##
## Reserving the MAX width per name would need a whole extra pre-pass over the body (the reservation
## is contiguous, and its neighbours are already placed by the time the second binding is seen), so
## the collision is REJECTED LOUDLY instead of being written next door. The fix at the source is to
## give one of the two locals its own name.
##
## `slot_reserved_words` recovers an existing reservation's width from the slot vector itself: every
## `bind_*_slot` lays a run of ANONYMOUS filler entries (`ns = nl = 0`) immediately BELOW the base
## slot, so the reservation is the base plus that run. A scalar has no fillers → 1 word. Over-
## reporting is possible only for `bind_dyn_store_slot` (its fillers sit ABOVE its base), which
## costs a MISSED diagnostic, never a false one.
slot_reserved_words := fn(slots : ptr(SVec), i : usize) -> usize {
  mut w := 1
  mut k := i
  while k > 0 {
    e : ptr(mut SlotEntry) = unchecked bitcast(ptr(mut SlotEntry), deref(slots).base + (k - 1) * svec_stride())
    if deref(e).ns != 0 { return w }
    if deref(e).nl != 0 { return w }
    w += 1
    k -= 1
  }
  w
}

## Reject a rebinding of `s`/`n` that needs `need` slots when its existing reservation is narrower.
## A FIRST binding (`slot_of < 0`) and a single-slot need are always fine, so the check is inert for
## every ordinary binding and for every same-shape rebinding.
require_reservation := fn(slots : ptr(SVec), src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize, need : usize) {
  if need <= 1 { return }
  e := slot_of(slots, src, s, n)
  if e < 0 { return }
  if slot_reserved_words(slots, usize(e)) >= need { return }
  lower_show_src_line(src, s)
  panic("selfhost: this local NAME is already bound in this function by a NARROWER binding, and this binding needs more words (the source line above). Alatyr `:=` locals are function-scoped and the slot map reuses the FIRST reservation, so the wide store would overwrite the neighbouring locals' slots while the field reads came from a slot nothing ever wrote — a stale-stack read whose result changes with the frame layout (a silent miscompile the TOOL-1 fixpoint cannot see). Give one of the two locals its own name.")
}

## Add a name→slot entry **if the name is not already bound**, returning the slot count
## after the call. A re-assignment (`x = …` of an already-bound local) reuses the existing
## slot rather than allocating a new one — so the frame size = the number of *distinct*
## names (params + first-seen locals). A scalar local (no struct type).
bind_slot := fn(in out slots : SVec, src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize) -> usize {
  existing := slot_of(ptr(slots), src, s, n)
  if existing >= 0 { return svec_len(ptr(slots)) }
  off := svec_len(ptr(slots))
  svec_push(slots, SlotEntry(ns = s, nl = n, off = off, sns = 0, snl = 0, ek = 0, estride = 1, eek = 0, is_ref = false))
  svec_len(ptr(slots))
}

## Like `bind_slot`, but records the scalar's TYPE name span (`ts`/`tl`) in the slot's `sns`/`snl`.
## Used for a scalar VALUE parameter (ek 0) so per-signature overload resolution can recover the
## argument's type at a call site (`arg_type_name`). Harmless for existing paths: `sns`/`snl` on an
## ek-0 slot is only read by field/type resolution, which a scalar never reaches (fixpoint-checked).
bind_slot_typed := fn(in out slots : SVec, src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize, ts : usize, tl : usize) -> usize {
  existing := slot_of(ptr(slots), src, s, n)
  if existing >= 0 { return svec_len(ptr(slots)) }
  off := svec_len(ptr(slots))
  svec_push(slots, SlotEntry(ns = s, nl = n, off = off, sns = ts, snl = tl, ek = 0, estride = 1, eek = 0, is_ref = false))
  svec_len(ptr(slots))
}

## AGGREGATE-PARAM tier — emit the WORD-0 ADDRESS of an aggregate local/param into %rax.
## For an ordinary aggregate LOCAL its slot IS word 0, so `leaq -(off+1)*8(%rbp), %rax`. For a
## BY-REFERENCE aggregate PARAM (`is_ref`) the slot holds a POINTER to the caller's aggregate
## word 0, so `movq -(off+1)*8(%rbp), %rax` LOADS that pointer. Field `f` / element/word `k` is
## then at `-(f*8)(%rax)` / `-(k*8)(%rax)` (the down-growing aggregate layout — word k is at a
## LOWER address than word 0, matching the local frame layout where field/element `i` is at
## `-(off+i+1)*8`). One uniform base-address primitive so the field/index/whole-copy access
## paths work identically for a frame-local aggregate and a by-ref aggregate param.
emit_agg_base_addr := fn(ent : SlotEntry, in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf) {
  if ent.is_ref {
    push_str(sb, "  movq -")
  } else {
    push_str(sb, "  leaq -")
  }
  push_int(sb, (i64(ent.off) + 1) * 8)
  push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rax\n")
}

## Emit the BYTE offset of frame word `k` of an aggregate whose base slot (word 0) is `off` — §4
## UP-GROWING: word 0 lives at `off` (the TOP of the block, lowest address) and word k grows UP to a
## higher address = LOWER slot `off - k`, byte `(off - k + 1) * 8`. The single choke point for the
## flip: every frame word-k site routes through here. (`off >= k` always: the block spans
## `[off-nwords+1, off]` and `k <= nwords-1 <= off`.)
push_frame_word := fn(in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, off : usize, k : usize) {
  push_int(sb, i64((off - k + 1) * 8))
}

## Is the type text `[ts, ts+tl)` a `ptr(...)` whose POINTEE is an aggregate (struct/enum)? Parses
## `ptr(`<opt `mut `>`<pointee>)` and resolves the pointee by tail name. Used to decide whether a
## `deref(p)` argument is a by-reference aggregate (pass the pointer) vs a scalar deref (load `*p`).
ptr_pointee_agg := fn(src : ptr(u8), ts : usize, tl : usize, decls : ptr(rt::Vec), a : rt::Arena) -> bool {
  if tl < 6 { return false }                       ## "ptr(X)" minimum
  if str_at((src + ts), 4) != "ptr(" { return false }
  mut ps := ts + 4                           ## pointee starts past "ptr("
  mut pl := tl - 5                            ## minus "ptr(" (4) and the trailing ")" (1)
  if pl > 4 and str_at((src + ps), 4) == "mut " { ps = ps + 4; pl = pl - 4 }
  if struct_decl_of(decls, src, ps, pl) >= 0 { return true }
  enum_decl_of(decls, src, ps, pl) >= 0
}

## The POINTEE struct type span of a `ptr(<opt mut> Struct)` type span — the span-returning dual of
## `ptr_pointee_agg` (which only says yes/no). 0/0 if the type isn't `ptr(…)` of a known STRUCT.
ptr_pointee_struct_span := fn(src : ptr(u8), ts : usize, tl : usize, decls : ptr(rt::Vec), a : rt::Arena) -> CSpan {
  if tl < 6 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  if str_at((src + ts), 4) != "ptr(" { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  mut ps := ts + 4
  mut pl := tl - 5
  if pl > 4 and str_at((src + ps), 4) == "mut " { ps = ps + 4; pl = pl - 4 }
  if struct_decl_of(decls, src, ps, pl) >= 0 { return CSpan(s = ps, n = pl) }
  CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
}

## The TYPE span (ts, tl) of field `[fs, fs+fl)` of struct `[sns, snl)` — walks the struct's
## `FieldDecl` list (each carries `ts`/`tl`); 0/0 if not found. (Mirrors `field_word_offset`.)
pub field_type_span := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), sns : usize, snl : usize, fs : usize, fl : usize, a : rt::Arena) -> CSpan {
  ## RAW UNION (spec Types §6.3): a member read `u.m` types as the member's payload type `T`. Gated on
  ## `is_union_decl` → an enum/struct is untouched (byte-identical, fixpoint-neutral).
  if is_union_decl(decls, src, sns, snl) {
    umt := union_member_ty(decls, src, sns, snl, fs, fl)
    return CSpan(s = umt.s, n = umt.n)
  }
  di := struct_decl_of(decls, src, sns, snl)
  if di < 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(di))))
  mut f := d.fields_head
  mut res := CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
  while f != 0 {
    fd := deref(fld_p(f))
    ## NESTED-GENERIC: a type-PARAM field of a generic INSTANCE resolves to the instance's type-arg
    ## (`Box(Pair(u64))`'s `v : T` → `Pair`), so a nested read `c.v.a` types `c.v` as the concrete
    ## aggregate. Gated (aggregate type-args only) inside `subst_field_ty` — a scalar-arg instance
    ## returns the param `T` unchanged (typed/sized as a scalar, byte-identical to before).
    if streq(src, fd.ns, fd.nl, fs, fl) {
      eff := subst_field_ty(decls, src, sns, snl, fd.ts, fd.tl, a)
      res = CSpan(s = eff.s, n = eff.n)
    }
    f = fd.next
  }
  res
}

## Is `inner` a pointer-to-AGGREGATE expression? A `Var` whose slot is a ptr-to-enum/struct
## (`ek == 6`/`7`), or a struct `Field` whose declared type is `ptr(<aggregate>)`. The function-body
## match is the lowerable shape. Used so `deref(inner)` passed as a call ARG passes the POINTER.
ptr_target_is_agg := fn(inner : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> bool {
  match deref(inner) {
    Expr::Var(s, n) => {
      ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, s, n)))
      ent.ek == 6 or ent.ek == 7
    }
    Expr::Field(base, fs, fl) => {
      bi := base_struct_span(base, cx)
      if bi.n == 0 { false }
      else {
        fts := field_type_span(cx.decls, cx.src, bi.s, bi.n, fs, fl, deref(cx.mar))
        ptr_pointee_agg(cx.src, fts.s, fts.n, cx.decls, deref(cx.mar))
      }
    }
    _ => { false }
  }
}

## The struct TYPE span of a base expression: a `Var` (its slot's recorded struct type) or a NESTED
## `Field` `b.f` (the declared type of field `f` within `b`'s struct type — recursing for `o.i.j…`).
## 0/0 otherwise. Lets `ptr_target_is_agg` + the nested-field `field_slot` resolve a field's type.
base_struct_span := fn(base : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> CSpan {
  match deref(base) {
    Expr::Var(s, n) => {
      ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, s, n)))
      CSpan(s = ent.sns, n = ent.snl)
    }
    Expr::Field(b, ifs, ifl) => {
      bt := base_struct_span(b, cx)
      field_type_span(cx.decls, cx.src, bt.s, bt.n, ifs, ifl, deref(cx.mar))
    }
    _ => { CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  }
}

## Is `e` a `deref(p)` where `p` is a pointer to an aggregate (so, as a call arg, the by-ref
## aggregate address is `p`'s VALUE — the pointer)? The inner expression (the pointer) is `deref_inner`.
deref_ptr_agg_arg := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> bool {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Deref(inner) => { ptr_target_is_agg(inner, cx) }
    _ => { false }
  }
}
deref_inner := fn(e : ptr(Expr)) -> ptr(Expr) {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Deref(inner) => { inner }
    _ => { e }
  }
}

## AGGREGATE-PARAM tier — lower a CALL ARGUMENT. An aggregate argument (a `Var` whose slot
## entry is a struct `ek == 2` / enum `ek == 3` / array `ek == 5`) is passed BY REFERENCE: push
## its word-0 ADDRESS (`emit_agg_base_addr` → %rax: `leaq` for a frame-local, `movq` the pointer
## for a by-ref param), so the callee's by-ref aggregate param receives a pointer to the caller's
## aggregate. Any other argument (a scalar, a str, an expression) is lowered to a value as before
## via `emit_gas`. (A `str` local `ek == 4` is a 2-word value handled by the str builtins, not a
## by-ref aggregate param — only structs/arrays/enums are passed by reference here.)
## `tmp_off` is the frame slot offset of a reserved 2-word str-materialization temp block for
## THIS argument (assigned by `emit_call_args` from the str-temp pool, distinct per str-literal
## argument of the call), or -1 when the argument is not a str literal. A `StrLit` argument has
## no frame home, so it must be materialized into that temp block (ptr at slot `tmp_off`, len at
## `tmp_off+1` — the same {ptr, len} layout a str LOCAL uses) and its ADDRESS passed by reference,
## exactly as a `Var` str argument passes its slot address. Without this a str literal fell to the
## bare-expression path, which pushes only the data POINTER (one word) and corrupts the call.
## An argument that is a STRUCT/ENUM-typed FIELD access — `base.f` (a `Field`) or the comptime
## `v.(f)` (a `CompField`, `f` = the active `comptime for` member `cf_fld`) — where field `f` is
## ITSELF an aggregate. Such an arg must be passed BY REFERENCE (its ADDRESS), like a struct `Var`
## arg, NOT word-loaded as a scalar. Returns the address-emission inputs: a by-ref base yields its
## slot entry index + the field's word index `fi` (address = base-ptr − fi*8, the down-growing
## pointee layout); a frame-local base yields the field's absolute frame `slot` (address = leaq
## −(slot+1)*8(%rbp)). `ok = false` for a non-field arg or a scalar/str field (those keep the
## value path). This is what makes `display(f.type, v.(f), sb)` recurse into a NESTED struct field.
AggFld := struct { ok : bool, is_ref : bool, ent_idx : usize, slot : i64, fi : i64 }
agg_field_arg_parts := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> AggFld {
  mut res := AggFld(ok = false, is_ref = false, ent_idx = 0, slot = 0, fi = 0)
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Field(base, fs, fl) => { res = agg_field_of(base, fs, fl, cx) }
    Expr::CompField(base, idx) => {
      vn := var_name_span(idx)
      if cx.cf_var_l != 0 and vn.n != 0 and streq(cx.src, vn.s, vn.n, cx.cf_var_s, cx.cf_var_l) {
        res = agg_field_of(base, cx.cf_fld_s, cx.cf_fld_l, cx)
      }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  res
}

## Is a comptime field projection safe in VALUE position? The bounded projection slice emits scalar
## reads only. Aggregate projections remain valid as CALL ARGUMENTS because `emit_arg` handles them by
## address above; reaching `emit_gas` here for an aggregate would load only word zero and silently
## miscompile. Resolve the field type from the concrete `typeinfo(X)` target so a generic field whose
## declared type is `T` is classified by its instantiated layout, not by the unresolved spelling.
comp_field_scalar_value := fn(cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> bool {
  if cx.cf_var_l == 0 { return false }
  mut ts := cx.cf_ty_s
  mut tn := cx.cf_ty_l
  if str_at((cx.src + cx.it_s), 1) != "(" and str_at((cx.src + cx.it_s), 1) != "[" {
    ct := compfor_target_type(cx, cx.cf_var_s, cx.cf_var_l)
    if ct.n != 0 {
      ft := field_type_span(cx.decls, cx.src, ct.s, ct.n, cx.cf_fld_s, cx.cf_fld_l, deref(cx.mar))
      ts = ft.s
      tn = ft.n
    }
  }
  if tn == 0 { return false }
  if str_at((cx.src + ts), 1) == "[" or str_at((cx.src + ts), 1) == "(" { return false }
  if str_at((cx.src + ts), 3) == "str" { return false }
  bn := base_type_name(cx.src, ts, tn)
  if struct_decl_of(cx.decls, cx.src, bn.s, bn.n) >= 0 { return false }
  if enum_decl_of(cx.decls, cx.src, bn.s, bn.n) >= 0 { return false }
  true
}

## A TUPLE/ARRAY aggregate ELEMENT `v.(e)` (`display`'s array/tuple unroll, element type struct/enum)
## whose address must be passed BY REFERENCE. `ok`, plus `v`'s slot offset + the element's WORD offset
## within the container. Scoped to ARRAY containers (uniform element stride = element word count);
## a tuple with aggregate components needs the cumulative (strided) offset — deferred.
## The WORD OFFSET of the current comptime-for member (`cf_fld_s` = index) within the container
## `cx.it`. A TUPLE `(T0, …)` uses the CUMULATIVE sum of the word counts of the components BEFORE
## the index (so a scalar after a struct component lands past it); an ARRAY `[T; N]` uses the uniform
## `index * elemwords`. For an all-scalar tuple / scalar-element array this equals the index (1 word
## each) — byte-identical to the former direct `cf_fld_s` use.
cf_member_woff := fn(cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena) -> usize {
  if str_at((cx.src + cx.it_s), 1) == "(" {
    mut wo := 0
    mut j := 0
    while j < cx.cf_fld_s {
      cj := typearg_at(cx.src, cx.it_s, 0, j)
      cjb := base_type_name(cx.src, cj.s, cj.n)
      if struct_decl_of(cx.decls, cx.src, cjb.s, cjb.n) >= 0 { wo = wo + struct_words(cx.decls, cx.src, cj.s, cj.n, a) }
      else if enum_decl_of(cx.decls, cx.src, cjb.s, cjb.n) >= 0 { wo = wo + 1 + enum_inst_words(cx.decls, cx.src, cj.s, cj.n, a) }
      else { wo = wo + 1 }
      j += 1
    }
    return wo
  }
  bn := base_type_name(cx.src, cx.cf_ty_s, cx.cf_ty_l)
  if struct_decl_of(cx.decls, cx.src, bn.s, bn.n) >= 0 { return cx.cf_fld_s * struct_words(cx.decls, cx.src, cx.cf_ty_s, cx.cf_ty_l, a) }
  if enum_decl_of(cx.decls, cx.src, bn.s, bn.n) >= 0 { return cx.cf_fld_s * (1 + enum_inst_words(cx.decls, cx.src, cx.cf_ty_s, cx.cf_ty_l, a)) }
  return cx.cf_fld_s
}

CompAggElem := struct { ok : bool, voff : usize, woff : usize }
comp_agg_elem_parts := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena) -> CompAggElem {
  mut res := CompAggElem(ok = false, voff = 0, woff = 0)
  if cx.cf_var_l == 0 { return res }
  is_arr := str_at((cx.src + cx.it_s), 1) == "["
  is_tup := str_at((cx.src + cx.it_s), 1) == "("
  if not (is_arr or is_tup) { return res }
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::CompField(base, idx) => {
      vn := var_name_span(idx)
      if vn.n != 0 and streq(cx.src, vn.s, vn.n, cx.cf_var_s, cx.cf_var_l) {
        ## the CURRENT member type (cf_ty) must be a struct/enum to pass by reference.
        bn := base_type_name(cx.src, cx.cf_ty_s, cx.cf_ty_l)
        mut is_agg := struct_decl_of(cx.decls, cx.src, bn.s, bn.n) >= 0 or enum_decl_of(cx.decls, cx.src, bn.s, bn.n) >= 0
        if is_agg {
          bvn := var_name_span(base)
          if bvn.n != 0 {
            bent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, bvn.s, bvn.n)))
            res = CompAggElem(ok = true, voff = bent.off, woff = cf_member_woff(cx, a))
          }
        }
      }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  res
}

## Allocate the next `agg_w`-word slice of the aggregate-value temp pool (bump allocator) and return its
## base slot offset. `emit_call_args` save/restores `cx.agg_next` around a call's args so successive
## aggregate args in one call get DISTINCT slices (no aliasing — §8) and a nested call stacks above. A
## bump past the reserved pool aborts loudly (never a silent miscompile). Only called when a pool exists
## (`cx.agg_tmp >= 0`, so `agg_next >= 0`).
agg_alloc := fn(cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> i64 {
  off := cx.agg_next
  if off + cx.agg_w > cx.agg_end { panic("selfhost: aggregate-value call-arg temp pool overflow (too many or too deeply nested aggregate-value arguments in one call)") }
  cx.agg_next = off + cx.agg_w
  ## §4 UP-GROWING: return the TOP of the agg_w-wide slice as the block base (word 0), so
  ## emit_*_assign writes word w at `base - w` and emit_agg_base_addr(base) yields word 0 (lowest addr).
  off + cx.agg_w - 1
}

## Types §7 — is the call ARGUMENT `e` a two-word `{ptr, len}` VIEW VALUE with no addressable home
## of its own, so `emit_arg` must materialize its pair into a temp block before passing it by
## reference? True for exactly the view-producing shapes `emit_str_pair` can already emit and
## `emit_arg` had no arm for:
##   `sub(s, start, len)`      — the substring VIEW builtin (a str-PRODUCING call, not a decl'd fn,
##                               so the `str_ret_call` arm above cannot see it);
##   `s[lo..hi]`               — a range sub-view (the typed ARRAY-slice arm above already claimed
##                               the `slice_arr_stride != 0` case, so this is the str/view form);
##   `xs[i]` over `[str; N]`   — a str ELEMENT (eek 4) of a fixed array;
##   `xs[i].key`               — a str FIELD of an array-of-struct element;
##   a str mutable GLOBAL      — its pair lives in `.data`, not in a frame slot.
## A view with a real home (a str LOCAL / by-ref PARAM / a struct's view FIELD) is NOT this: those
## already pass an existing address and keep their (unchanged) arms.
view_temp_arg := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena) -> bool {
  if is_str_call(e, cx) { return true }
  ## CLAYOUT S3(b) — `io::print(deref(p))` where `p` points at a §7 VIEW. The pointee IS the pair,
  ## but it lives at the ASCENDING pointee offsets while a by-ref view PARAM reads the DOWN-growing
  ## frame layout, so the pointer cannot simply be forwarded (the `deref_ptr_agg_arg` shortcut below
  ## is only correct for a struct/enum pointee). Materialize the pair into an agg-temp block and pass
  ## its address, like every other homeless view. Before this the argument fell to the scalar default
  ## and `io::print(deref(p))` printed NOTHING — a silent wrong value (I11).
  if deref_view_span_cx(e, cx).n != 0 { return true }
  if is_slice_expr(e) { return true }
  if str_field_arr_elem(e, cx, a).found { return true }
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Index(ibase, iidx) => {
      call_arg_is_str_elem(e, cx)
    }
    Expr::Var(vs, vn) => {
      gmv := mut_global_value(cx.decls, cx.src, vs, vn)
      unchecked bitcast(usize, gmv) != 0 and str_lit_info(gmv).is_s
    }
    _ => { false }
  }
}

## I11 loud boundary — is the ARGUMENT a `str`/`Slice` FIELD of a module-level GLOBAL struct
## (`G.name`, `G` either `mut` or a const)? Such a view's pair lives in `.data`, not in a frame slot,
## so none of `emit_arg`'s arms can hand out an address for it and `emit_str_pair` has no arm either:
## before this the argument fell to the scalar `emit_gas` default and `io::print(G.name)` printed
## NOTHING — a silent wrong value. Addressing a `.data` view pair is the global-place half of the
## view work (it needs the ASCENDING `.data` layout, not the frame's), so until that lands the
## argument is REJECTED with its workaround rather than miscompiled.
global_view_field_arg := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena) -> bool {
  fp := field_place_parts(e)
  if unchecked bitcast(usize, fp.base) == 0 { return false }
  bv := field_base_var(fp.base)
  if not bv.ok { return false }
  mut gv := mut_global_value(cx.decls, cx.src, bv.s, bv.n)
  if unchecked bitcast(usize, gv) == 0 { gv = module_const_value(cx.decls, cx.src, bv.s, bv.n) }
  if unchecked bitcast(usize, gv) == 0 { return false }
  gsl := struct_lit_info(gv)
  if gsl.is_s == false { return false }
  fts := field_type_span(cx.decls, cx.src, gsl.ss, gsl.sl, fp.fs, fp.fl, a)
  if fts.n == 0 { return false }
  if str_at((cx.src + fts.s), fts.n) == "str" { return true }
  fbn := base_type_name(cx.src, fts.s, fts.n)
  str_at((cx.src + fbn.s), fbn.n) == "Slice"
}

## The lowered shape of an aggregate validity-contract construction `R(v)`. The parser deliberately
## keeps require metadata source-only, so this is the single lower-side recognizer for the aggregate
## slice. The underlying aggregate class is derived from `under` at each sink (2 = named struct, 3 =
## ordinary tagged enum) rather than stored in this returned record: keeping the record's ABI unchanged
## is important because the frozen seed already materializes it in several self-host paths. Niche-folded
## enums and raw unions stay outside this increment because their representation is not the tagged
## `[discriminant, payload…]` layout consumed by the existing enum copy/match machinery. `unchecked {
## R(v) }` is carried explicitly so the aggregate path can suppress the predicate without routing the
## value through scalar `emit_gas`.
RequireAgg := struct { ok : bool, unch : bool, under : CSpan, pred : CSpan, arg : ptr(Expr) }

## The complete aggregate class accepted by this require slice. Keep this decision in one helper: the
## same underlying-type fact sizes the destination slot, aggregate scratch, predicate copy, and return
## registers. Returning 0 is deliberate for scalar contracts (which keep their old path), niche-folded
## enums, unions, and every non-struct/non-enum shape.
require_agg_kind := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize) -> u8 {
  bn := base_type_name(src, s, n)
  if bn.n == 0 { return 0 }
  if struct_decl_of(decls, src, bn.s, bn.n) >= 0 { return 2 }
  if enum_decl_of(decls, src, bn.s, bn.n) >= 0 {
    if is_union_decl(decls, src, bn.s, bn.n) { return 0 }
    if is_niche_folded(src, s, n) { return 0 }
    return 3
  }
  0
}

require_agg_words := fn(under : CSpan, decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> usize {
  k := require_agg_kind(decls, src, under.s, under.n)
  if k == 2 { return struct_words(decls, src, under.s, under.n, a) }
  if k == 3 { return 1 + enum_inst_words(decls, src, under.s, under.n, a) }
  0
}

require_agg_parts := fn(e : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> RequireAgg {
  mut q := e
  mut unch := false
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Unchecked(inner) => { q = inner; unch = true }
    _ => {}
  }
  mut z := unchecked bitcast(ptr(Expr), 0)
  mut res := RequireAgg(ok = false, unch = unch, under = CSpan(s = 0, n = 0), pred = CSpan(s = 0, n = 0), arg = z)
  cp := call_parts(q)
  if cp.is_call == false { return res }
  if cp.na != 1 { return res }
  rp := require_pred(decls, src, cp.cs, cp.cl)
  if rp.n == 0 { return res }
  bu := brand_underlying(decls, src, cp.cs, cp.cl)
  if bu.n == 0 { return res }
  k := require_agg_kind(decls, src, bu.s, bu.n)
  if k == 0 { return res }
  res = RequireAgg(ok = true, unch = unch, under = CSpan(s = bu.s, n = bu.n), pred = CSpan(s = rp.s, n = rp.n), arg = arg_expr_at(cp.ah, 0, a))
  res
}

## Scalar predicate for the same recognizer. Keeping the yes/no query scalar avoids making every
## aggregate sink depend on the multiword `RequireAgg` return while the self-host ABI is still being
## used to discover the construction shape.
require_agg_is := fn(e : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> bool {
  require_agg_parts(e, decls, src, a).ok
}

## Located boundary for enum representations that are not the ordinary tagged layout this increment
## proves. Without this guard, a niche/union require constructor would be mistaken for scalar `T(v)`
## and either read only word 0 or report an unrelated conversion error. Scalar require contracts do not
## enter this function: only a resolved enum underlying type is rejected here.
reject_unsupported_require_enum := fn(e : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) {
  mut q := e
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Unchecked(inner) => { q = inner }
    _ => {}
  }
  cp := call_parts(q)
  if cp.is_call == false or cp.na != 1 { return }
  rp := require_pred(decls, src, cp.cs, cp.cl)
  if rp.n == 0 { return }
  bu := brand_underlying(decls, src, cp.cs, cp.cl)
  if bu.n == 0 { return }
  bn := base_type_name(src, bu.s, bu.n)
  if bn.n == 0 or enum_decl_of(decls, src, bn.s, bn.n) < 0 { return }
  if require_agg_kind(decls, src, bu.s, bu.n) != 0 { return }
  lower_show_src_line(src, cp.cs)
  panic("selfhost: the enum @require constructor on the source line above needs the ordinary tagged enum layout; niche-folded and raw-union underlying representations are a separate increment")
}

## Number of aggregate scratch blocks consumed by a require construction in argument/value position:
## one block holds the preserved result and a second, distinct block is the by-value predicate copy. An
## unchecked construction needs only the preserved result. Returning 0 for every other expression keeps
## the existing aggregate pool scan byte-neutral for the self-host source.
require_agg_blocks := fn(e : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> usize {
  ra := require_agg_parts(e, decls, src, a)
  if ra.ok == false { return 0 }
  if ra.unch { return 1 }
  2
}

## Copy `nw` down-growing aggregate words between two frame-slot bases. The address of word 0 is the
## highest slot in each block; word k is at `-(base-k)*8`. Used to preserve the checked R(v) result while
## the predicate consumes a distinct ordinary by-value aggregate copy.
require_copy_frame_words := fn(src_off : i64, dst_off : i64, nw : usize, in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf) {
  for k in 0..nw {
    push_str(sb, "  movq -")
    push_int(sb, (src_off - i64(k) + 1) * 8)
    push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rax\n  movq %rax, -")
    push_int(sb, (dst_off - i64(k) + 1) * 8)
    push_str(sb, "(%rbp)\n")
  }
}

## Copy an assignable aggregate source into a destination frame block. The source class is selected by
## the require underlying kind, so an enum constructor/call/Var uses the complete tagged enum machinery
## rather than falling through to a scalar word-0 read. Every supported source is evaluated once.
require_emit_source := fn(e : ptr(Expr), dst : i64, under : CSpan, kind : u8, in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena, in out nl : usize) {
  mut q := e
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Unchecked(inner) => { q = inner }
    _ => {}
  }
  q = const_rhs(q, cx.decls, cx.src)
  si := struct_lit_info(q)
  if si.is_s {
    if kind != 2 { panic("selfhost: aggregate require enum construction received a struct source") }
    emit_struct_assign(q, dst, sb, cx, a, nl)
    return
  }
  ei := enum_lit_info(q)
  if ei.is_e {
    if kind != 3 { panic("selfhost: aggregate require struct construction received an enum source") }
    emit_enum_assign(q, dst, sb, cx, a, nl)
    return
  }
  vn := var_name_span(q)
  if vn.n != 0 {
    ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, vn.s, vn.n)))
    if ent.ek != kind { panic("selfhost: aggregate require construction source has a different aggregate representation") }
    emit_agg_base_addr(ent, sb)
    nw := if kind == 2 { struct_words(cx.decls, cx.src, under.s, under.n, deref(cx.mar)) } else { 1 + enum_inst_words(cx.decls, cx.src, under.s, under.n, deref(cx.mar)) }
    for k in 0..nw {
      ## The aggregate pointer ABI is UP-GROWING from word 0 (`param[k]` is +k*8), while the
      ## frame slot that owns the local is DOWN-GROWING (`word k` is `off-k`). `emit_agg_base_addr`
      ## returns the address of word 0, so a negative offset here would copy the discriminant and
      ## neighbouring frame words instead of the payload. That was harmless only while this source
      ## arm had no enum fixture; it is a silent payload truncation at the require seam.
      push_str(sb, "  movq ")
      push_int(sb, i64(k * 8))
      push_str(sb, "(%rax), %rcx\n  movq %rcx, -")
      push_int(sb, (dst - i64(k) + 1) * 8)
      push_str(sb, "(%rbp)\n")
    }
    return
  }
  nw := if kind == 2 { struct_words(cx.decls, cx.src, under.s, under.n, deref(cx.mar)) } else { 1 + enum_inst_words(cx.decls, cx.src, under.s, under.n, deref(cx.mar)) }
  if kind == 2 and struct_ret_call(q, cx.decls, cx.src, a) {
    if nw > 7 { panic("selfhost: aggregate require source struct return exceeds the register-return budget; bind the source first") }
    emit_struct_value(q, sb, cx, a, nl)
    for k in 0..nw {
      push_str(sb, "  movq ")
      emit_retreg(sb, k)
      push_str(sb, ", -")
      push_int(sb, (dst - i64(k) + 1) * 8)
      push_str(sb, "(%rbp)\n")
    }
    return
  }
  if kind == 2 and sret_ret_call(q, cx.decls, cx.src, a) {
    ov := cx.sret_call
    cx.sret_call = dst
    emit_struct_value(q, sb, cx, a, nl)
    cx.sret_call = ov
    return
  }
  if kind == 3 and enum_ret_call_d(q, cx.decls, cx.src, a) {
    if enum_sret_ret_call(q, cx.decls, cx.src, a) {
      ov := cx.sret_call
      cx.sret_call = dst
      emit_enum_value(q, sb, cx, a, nl)
      cx.sret_call = ov
      return
    }
    if nw > 7 { panic("selfhost: aggregate require source enum return exceeds the register-return budget; bind the source first") }
    emit_enum_value(q, sb, cx, a, nl)
    for k in 0..nw {
      push_str(sb, "  movq ")
      emit_retreg(sb, k)
      push_str(sb, ", -")
      push_int(sb, (dst - i64(k) + 1) * 8)
      push_str(sb, "(%rbp)\n")
    }
    return
  }
  panic("selfhost: aggregate require construction supports a matching literal, aggregate Var, or aggregate-returning call as its source")
}

## Emit the ordinary internal aggregate ABI call to a require predicate. Aggregate parameters are
## represented internally by a word-0 pointer, while the caller still materializes a distinct complete
## copy, exactly matching the spec's by-value predicate semantics.
emit_require_predicate := fn(pred : CSpan, under : CSpan, arg_off : i64, in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, cx : ptr(LCtx), in out nl : usize) {
  if pred.n == 0 { panic("selfhost: aggregate require has no predicate") }
  mut inline := false
  mut i := 0
  while i < pred.n { if str_at((cx.src + pred.s + i), 1) == "(" { inline = true }; i += 1 }
  if inline == false {
    pi := callee_decl_idx(cx.decls, cx.src, pred.s, pred.n, cx.mod_s, cx.mod_l)
    if pi < 0 { panic("selfhost: aggregate require predicate is not an in-scope function") }
    pd := deref(decl_get(cx.decls, usize(pi)))
    if not pd.is_fn or pd.arity != 1 { panic("selfhost: aggregate require predicate must be an ordinary one-argument function") }
    pm := deref(param_p(pd.params_head))
    pbn := base_type_name(cx.src, pm.ts, pm.tl)
    ubn := base_type_name(cx.src, under.s, under.n)
    pk := require_agg_kind(cx.decls, cx.src, pm.ts, pm.tl)
    uk := require_agg_kind(cx.decls, cx.src, under.s, under.n)
    if pm.pmode != 0 or pbn.n == 0 or ubn.n == 0 or pk != uk or not streq(cx.src, pbn.s, pbn.n, ubn.s, ubn.n) {
      panic("selfhost: aggregate require predicate must take an ordinary parameter of the complete underlying aggregate type")
    }
    if pd.ret_tl == 0 or str_at((cx.src + pd.ret_ts), pd.ret_tl) != "bool" {
      panic("selfhost: aggregate require predicate must return bool")
    }
  } else if pred.n < 2 or str_at((cx.src + pred.s), 2) != "fn" {
    panic("selfhost: malformed inline aggregate require predicate")
  }
  lok := nl
  nl += 1
  push_str(sb, "  leaq -")
  push_int(sb, (arg_off + 1) * 8)
  push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rdi\n  call ")
  if inline {
    emit_mod_qual(sb, cx.src, cx.mod_s, cx.mod_l)
    push_str(sb, "lam")
    push_int(sb, i64(pred.s))
  } else {
    emit_mangled_call(sb, cx.src, pred.s, pred.n, 0, 0, cx.decls)
  }
  push_str(sb, "\n  testq %rax, %rax\n  jne ")
  emit_label(sb, lok)
  push_str(sb, "\n  ud2\n")
  emit_label(sb, lok)
  push_str(sb, ":\n")
}

## Construct an aggregate require value directly into an existing local destination. The source is
## evaluated once into the destination; the predicate sees a separate copy and cannot clobber the value
## preserved for later field reads. The temporary predicate block is reclaimed before the next statement.
emit_require_agg_assign := fn(e : ptr(Expr), dst : i64, in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena, in out nl : usize) {
  ra := require_agg_parts(e, cx.decls, cx.src, a)
  if ra.ok == false { panic("selfhost: malformed aggregate require construction") }
  rk := require_agg_kind(cx.decls, cx.src, ra.under.s, ra.under.n)
  nw := require_agg_words(ra.under, cx.decls, cx.src, deref(cx.mar))
  if nw == 0 { panic("selfhost: aggregate require underlying type has no complete word layout") }
  old := cx.vchk
  if ra.unch { cx.vchk = false }
  require_emit_source(ra.arg, dst, ra.under, rk, sb, cx, a, nl)
  if cx.vchk {
    if cx.agg_tmp < 0 { panic("selfhost: checked aggregate require needs aggregate predicate scratch") }
    save := cx.agg_next
    poff := agg_alloc(cx)
    require_copy_frame_words(dst, poff, nw, sb)
    emit_require_predicate(ra.pred, ra.under, poff, sb, cx, nl)
    cx.agg_next = save
  }
  cx.vchk = old
}

## Aggregate require in ordinary call-argument position: preserve the result in one temp, validate a
## second temp, then pass the preserved word-0 address to the callee. This is the direct-call counterpart
## of `emit_require_agg_assign` and is the path that prevents result/predicate aliasing.
emit_require_agg_arg := fn(e : ptr(Expr), in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena, in out nl : usize) {
  ra := require_agg_parts(e, cx.decls, cx.src, a)
  if ra.ok == false { panic("selfhost: malformed aggregate require argument") }
  if cx.agg_tmp < 0 { panic("selfhost: aggregate require argument needs aggregate scratch") }
  rk := require_agg_kind(cx.decls, cx.src, ra.under.s, ra.under.n)
  nw := require_agg_words(ra.under, cx.decls, cx.src, deref(cx.mar))
  if nw == 0 { panic("selfhost: aggregate require underlying type has no complete word layout") }
  old := cx.vchk
  if ra.unch { cx.vchk = false }
  save := cx.agg_next
  roff := agg_alloc(cx)
  require_emit_source(ra.arg, roff, ra.under, rk, sb, cx, a, nl)
  if cx.vchk {
    poff := agg_alloc(cx)
    require_copy_frame_words(roff, poff, nw, sb)
    emit_require_predicate(ra.pred, ra.under, poff, sb, cx, nl)
  }
  rent := SlotEntry(ns = 0, nl = 0, off = usize(roff), sns = ra.under.s, snl = ra.under.n, ek = rk, estride = 1, eek = 0, is_ref = false)
  emit_agg_base_addr(rent, sb)
  push_str(sb, "  pushq %rax\n")
  cx.agg_next = save
  cx.vchk = old
}

## A small aggregate require value in a return-register context (`return R(v)` or a struct-returning
## tail expression). The same preserved/copy discipline applies; wide aggregate returns remain on the
## existing sret path and are rejected here until that path gets its own contract hook.
emit_require_agg_value := fn(e : ptr(Expr), in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena, in out nl : usize) {
  ra := require_agg_parts(e, cx.decls, cx.src, a)
  if ra.ok == false { panic("selfhost: malformed aggregate require return value") }
  nw := require_agg_words(ra.under, cx.decls, cx.src, deref(cx.mar))
  if nw == 0 or nw > 7 { panic("selfhost: aggregate require return values wider than seven words are deferred to the sret slice") }
  if cx.agg_tmp < 0 { panic("selfhost: aggregate require return value needs aggregate scratch") }
  old := cx.vchk
  if ra.unch { cx.vchk = false }
  save := cx.agg_next
  roff := agg_alloc(cx)
  rk := require_agg_kind(cx.decls, cx.src, ra.under.s, ra.under.n)
  require_emit_source(ra.arg, roff, ra.under, rk, sb, cx, a, nl)
  if cx.vchk {
    poff := agg_alloc(cx)
    require_copy_frame_words(roff, poff, nw, sb)
    emit_require_predicate(ra.pred, ra.under, poff, sb, cx, nl)
  }
  for k in 0..nw {
    push_str(sb, "  movq -")
    push_int(sb, (roff - i64(k) + 1) * 8)
    push_str(sb, "(%rbp), ")
    emit_retreg(sb, k)
    push_str(sb, "\n")
  }
  cx.agg_next = save
  cx.vchk = old
}

emit_arg := fn(e : ptr(Expr), in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena, in out nl : usize, tmp_off : i64) {
  ## A checked aggregate require value has no scalar stack representation. Materialize it into the
  ## preserved aggregate-argument block, run the predicate against a distinct copy, and pass the
  ## preserved block's address through the ordinary aggregate ABI.
  if require_agg_parts(e, cx.decls, cx.src, a).ok {
    emit_require_agg_arg(e, sb, cx, a, nl)
    return
  }
  ## A bare function name is a CODE ADDRESS, never an aggregate place. Keep this
  ## route ahead of the aggregate-shape probes below: an unbound function value
  ## has no SlotEntry, and their historical sentinel entry can otherwise make it
  ## look like the current function's first Slice/aggregate parameter. That turns
  ## `roundtrip(measure, p, q)` into a data-pointer argument. `emit_gas` already
  ## owns the exact qualified-name recovery and emits the code pointer.
  fv := var_name_span(e)
  if fv.n != 0 {
    fref := gref_split(cx.src, fv.s, fv.n)
    mut fvs := fv.s
    mut fvn := fv.n
    if fref.qual { fvs = fref.ms; fvn = fref.ns + fref.nl - fref.ms }
    if slot_of(cx.slots, cx.src, fv.s, fv.n) < 0
       and resolve_callee_idx(cx.decls, cx.src, fvs, fvn, cx.mod_s, cx.mod_l) >= 0 {
      emit_gas(e, sb, cx, a, nl)
      return
    }
  }
  ## A str-CONSTANT `Var` (`MSG := "…"` at module level) as an argument — resolve it to its `StrLit`
  ## value and materialize THAT (a str is a 2-word {ptr,len} passed by-ref; the plain Var path would
  ## push only its word-0). An int/other scalar const is handled by the Var value path (emit_gas).
  scvn := var_name_span(e)
  if scvn.n != 0 {
    scmcv := module_const_value(cx.decls, cx.src, scvn.s, scvn.n)
    if unchecked bitcast(usize, scmcv) != 0 and str_lit_info(scmcv).is_s {
      emit_arg(scmcv, sb, cx, a, nl, tmp_off)
      return
    }
    ## a mutable STRUCT or ARRAY GLOBAL argument — MATERIALIZE it into the agg-temp (a down-growing
    ## local), then pass that block's word-0 ADDRESS by reference. The global's `.data` is ASCENDING
    ## (word k at LABEL+k*8) but the by-ref aggregate ABI is DOWN-GROWING (word k at `-(k*8)` from word
    ## 0), so the global cannot be passed by-ref directly — copy word k → agg-temp slot k (byte
    ## `-(agg_tmp+k+1)*8`) first. The flat word copy serves both a struct (`gnw` fields) and an array
    ## (`nel*stride` element words). Lets a global aggregate be an ordinary argument (`f(S)`, `print`).
    if cx.agg_tmp >= 0 {
      sgmv := mut_global_value(cx.decls, cx.src, scvn.s, scvn.n)
      if unchecked bitcast(usize, sgmv) != 0 {
        mut gnw := 0
        gspn := struct_lit_info(sgmv)
        if gspn.is_s { gnw = struct_words(cx.decls, cx.src, gspn.ss, gspn.sl, a) }
        else {
          gali := array_lit_info(sgmv)
          if gali.is_a {
            ge0s := struct_lit_info(arg_expr_at(gali.ehead, 0, a))
            gstride := if ge0s.is_s { struct_words(cx.decls, cx.src, ge0s.ss, ge0s.sl, a) } else { 1 }
            gnw = gali.nel * gstride
          }
        }
        if gnw > 0 {
          gaoff := agg_alloc(cx)
          push_str(sb, "  leaq ")
          emit_global_label(sb, cx.decls, cx.src, scvn.s, scvn.n)
          push_str(sb, "(%rip), %rax\n")
          mut gk := 0
          while gk < gnw {
            push_str(sb, "  movq ")
            push_int(sb, i64(gk * 8))
            push_str(sb, "(%rax), %rcx\n  movq %rcx, -")
            push_int(sb, i64((usize(gaoff) - gk + 1) * 8))
            push_str(sb, "(%rbp)\n")
            gk += 1
          }
          gaent := SlotEntry(ns = 0, nl = 0, off = usize(gaoff), sns = 0, snl = 0, ek = 2, estride = 1, eek = 0, is_ref = false)
          emit_agg_base_addr(gaent, sb)
          push_str(sb, "  pushq %rax\n")
          return
        }
      }
    }
  }
  ## AGGREGATE ELEMENT of an array `v.(e)` (a struct/enum element) — pass its ADDRESS: `v` holds the
  ## array's word-0 address (by-ref container param), element at `-(woff*8)(ptr)` (down-growing). So
  ## `display(e.type, v.(e))` recurses into the element instead of reading its first word as a scalar.
  cae := comp_agg_elem_parts(e, cx, a)
  if cae.ok {
    push_str(sb, "  movq -")
    push_int(sb, i64((cae.voff + 1) * 8))
    push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rax\n")
    if cae.woff != 0 { push_str(sb, "  addq $"); push_int(sb, i64(cae.woff * 8)); push_str(sb, ", %rax\n") }
    push_str(sb, "  pushq %rax\n")
    return
  }
  ## AGGREGATE FIELD argument (`base.f` / `v.(f)` where `f` is a struct/enum) — pass its ADDRESS,
  ## not the scalar word-load of its first word. THE nested-aggregate case for `display`'s recursion.
  afa := agg_field_arg_parts(e, cx)
  if afa.ok {
    if afa.is_ref {
      aent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, afa.ent_idx))
      emit_agg_base_addr(aent, sb)
      if afa.fi != 0 { push_str(sb, "  addq $"); push_int(sb, afa.fi * 8); push_str(sb, ", %rax\n") }
      push_str(sb, "  pushq %rax\n")
    } else {
      push_str(sb, "  leaq -")
      push_int(sb, (afa.slot + 1) * 8)
      push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rax\n  pushq %rax\n")
    }
    return
  }
  ## AGGREGATE ARRAY-ELEMENT argument (`take(ps[i])` over `[P; N]` / `[E; N]`, or over a by-ref array
  ## PARAM / a `Slice(P)` PARAM) — pass the ELEMENT'S ADDRESS by reference, exactly like every other
  ## by-value aggregate argument. Without this the operand fell to the scalar default at the bottom of
  ## this fn (`emit_gas`'s `Index` arm), which computes the element address correctly and then
  ## DEREFERENCES it (`movq (%rax), %rax`), handing the callee the element's FIRST FIELD VALUE as its
  ## by-reference block pointer. The callee then dereferenced an ordinary integer — a raw SIGSEGV, on
  ## every spelling: a constant or runtime index, a 1-word or multi-word struct element, an enum
  ## element, a frame-local array, a by-ref `[P; N]` param, a `Slice(P)` param, and any argument
  ## position. The dropped fact is that an aggregate ELEMENT is a PLACE, not a scalar value;
  ## `emit_index_addr` already composes its word-0 address for every one of those bases (the `leaq`
  ## for a frame local, the `movq` for a by-ref param, the extra deref for a slice param's {ptr,len}
  ## block), so the address is simply pushed instead of loaded through.
  ## `index_agg_elem_words` gates on an AGGREGATE element kind (struct 2 / enum 3 / nested tuple 5) —
  ## a scalar (0), float (9) or `str` (4) element is a VALUE and keeps the existing path untouched.
  ## `src/` passes no aggregate array element as an argument (it segfaulted), so this never fires on
  ## the self-host build → fixpoint-neutral.
  iaex := field_base_index(e)
  if iaex.is_ix and index_agg_elem_words(e, cx) > 0 {
    emit_index_addr(iaex.arr, iaex.idx, sb, cx, a, nl)
    push_str(sb, "  pushq %rax\n")
    return
  }
  ## `bytes(x)` argument — the spec str→`[u8]` view (appendix 160 §3.5). `str` and `[u8]` share
  ## the identical `{ptr, len}` representation, so `bytes(x)` is a no-op VIEW: pass it exactly as
  ## `x` (a str value) would be passed — a str Var by-ref, a str literal materialized to a temp.
  ## Without this the `bytes(x)` call fell to the generic Call emit and was mangled to a
  ## `<module>__bytes` label (an undefined symbol at link). `src/` uses `bytes(x)` only in the
  ## `bytes(x)[i]` index form (handled elsewhere), never as a bare arg → fixpoint-neutral.
  bva := bytes_view_arg(e, cx.src, a)
  if unchecked bitcast(usize, bva) != 0 {
    emit_arg(bva, sb, cx, a, nl, tmp_off)
    return
  }
  ## `f.name` argument — the comptime field NAME (a `str`): materialize its {ptr, len} into the temp
  ## block (ptr = `.Lfld<cf_fld_s>` in .rodata, len = `cf_fld_l`) and pass the block ADDRESS by
  ## reference, exactly like a str literal. `cf_fld_*` is the current comptime-for member (set during
  ## the unroll), so `display`'s `push_str(sb, f.name)` renders each aggregate field's name.
  cnsa := cf_name_span(e, cx)
  if cnsa.n != 0 and tmp_off >= 0 {
    push_str(sb, "  leaq ")
    push_lfld(sb, cnsa.s)
    push_str(sb, "(%rip), %rax\n  movq %rax, -")
    push_int(sb, (tmp_off + 1) * 8)
    push_str(sb, "(%rbp)\n  movq $")
    push_int(sb, i64(cnsa.n))
    push_str(sb, ", %rax\n  movq %rax, -")
    push_int(sb, tmp_off * 8)
    push_str(sb, "(%rbp)\n")
    tent := SlotEntry(ns = 0, nl = 0, off = usize(tmp_off), sns = 0, snl = 0, ek = 4, estride = 1, eek = 0, is_ref = false)
    emit_agg_base_addr(tent, sb)
    push_str(sb, "  pushq %rax\n")
    return
  }
  mut is_agg := false
  mut ent := SlotEntry(ns = 0, nl = 0, off = 0, sns = 0, snl = 0, ek = 0, estride = 1, eek = 0, is_ref = false)
  si := str_lit_info(e)
  if si.is_s and tmp_off >= 0 {
    ## STR LITERAL argument: materialize {ptr, len} into the temp block (ptr at slot `tmp_off`,
    ## len at `tmp_off+1`), then push the block's ADDRESS — by reference, like a `Var` str local
    ## (an ordinary, non-ref entry at `off = tmp_off`: `leaq -(tmp_off+1)*8(%rbp)`). The callee's
    ## str param derefs that pointer (ptr = `(%rax)`, len = `-8(%rax)`, the down-growing layout).
    emit_str_assign(e, tmp_off, sb)
    tent := SlotEntry(ns = 0, nl = 0, off = usize(tmp_off), sns = 0, snl = 0, ek = 4, estride = 1, eek = 0, is_ref = false)
    emit_agg_base_addr(tent, sb)
    push_str(sb, "  pushq %rax\n")
    return
  }
  if is_strat_call(e, cx.src) and tmp_off >= 0 {
    ## `str_at(base, len)` argument: a str VIEW with no frame home — materialize its {ptr, len} into
    ## the temp block (ptr at slot `tmp_off`, len at `tmp_off+1`) and push the block's ADDRESS, by
    ## reference, exactly like a str literal. This is THE dominant str argument in the emitter
    ## (`push_str(sb, str_at((src + ns), nl))` writes a source span); without it the str pair would be
    ## pushed as two value words and corrupt the callee's by-ref str param.
    emit_strat_assign(e, tmp_off, sb, cx, a, nl)
    tent := SlotEntry(ns = 0, nl = 0, off = usize(tmp_off), sns = 0, snl = 0, ek = 4, estride = 1, eek = 0, is_ref = false)
    emit_agg_base_addr(tent, sb)
    push_str(sb, "  pushq %rax\n")
    return
  }
  ## TYPED ARRAY-SLICE argument (`f(xs[lo..hi])` where `xs` is a raw scalar/float array): a `Slice(T)`
  ## VALUE with no frame home. Materialize its {ptr, len} into the agg-temp with the SAME slot layout as
  ## a str literal (`emit_array_slice_assign` writes ptr @ `-(soff+1)*8`, len @ `-soff*8` — byte-identical
  ## to `emit_str_assign`), then push the block's word-0 ADDRESS by reference. Without this a slice arg fell
  ## to the scalar default (`emit_gas` on an `Expr::Slice`) and passed one garbage word, so a `Slice(T)` PARAM
  ## read ptr/len/elements from junk. `bind_param`'s "Slice" case binds the callee param to deref this block.
  ## `src/` passes no array-slice args → dormant → fixpoint-neutral.
  if is_slice_expr(e) and slice_arr_stride(e, cx.slots, cx.src) != 0 and cx.agg_tmp >= 0 {
    soff := agg_alloc(cx)
    emit_array_slice_assign(e, soff, sb, cx, a, nl)
    sent := SlotEntry(ns = 0, nl = 0, off = usize(soff), sns = 0, snl = 0, ek = 4, estride = 1, eek = 0, is_ref = false)
    emit_agg_base_addr(sent, sb)
    push_str(sb, "  pushq %rax\n")
    return
  }
  ## BYTES bounded return-carrier argument — a supported `[u8; N]` call result has no frame home,
  ## while the existing fixed-array parameter ABI consumes a caller-owned data pointer. Materialize
  ## the one packed `%rax` carrier into one aggregate-temp word, then pass that word's address. This
  ## is deliberately the only return-call argument shape admitted here: array literals, wider/non-u8
  ## returns, tuples, structs and indirect calls keep their existing paths or located rejects.
  byte_ret_n := fixed_array_byte_return_len(e, cx.decls, cx.src, a)
  if byte_ret_n >= 1 and cx.agg_tmp >= 0 {
    emit_byte_array_return_value(e, sb, cx, a, nl)
    baoff := agg_alloc(cx)
    push_str(sb, "  movq %rax, -")
    push_int(sb, (baoff + 1) * 8)
    push_str(sb, "(%rbp)\n")
    baent := SlotEntry(ns = 0, nl = 0, off = usize(baoff), sns = 0, snl = 0, ek = 5, estride = 1, eek = 8, is_ref = false)
    emit_agg_base_addr(baent, sb)
    push_str(sb, "  pushq %rax\n")
    return
  }
  if fixed_array_ret_call(e, cx.decls, cx.src, a) {
    panic("selfhost: a fixed-array return cannot be passed as an aggregate argument yet; only a direct [u8; N] return carrier with 1 <= N <= 8 is supported")
  }
  ## STRUCT/ENUM CTOR argument (a non-place `S(…)` / `E.V(…)`): materialize it into the agg-temp
  ## block (it has no frame home), then push the block's word-0 ADDRESS — by reference, exactly
  ## like a str literal. The callee's by-ref aggregate param receives the pointer.
  sli := struct_lit_info(e)
  eli := enum_lit_info(e)
  if (sli.is_s or eli.is_e) and cx.agg_tmp >= 0 {
    aoff := agg_alloc(cx)
    if sli.is_s {
      emit_struct_assign(e, aoff, sb, cx, a, nl)
    } else {
      emit_enum_assign(e, aoff, sb, cx, a, nl)
    }
    aent := SlotEntry(ns = 0, nl = 0, off = usize(aoff), sns = 0, snl = 0, ek = 2, estride = 1, eek = 0, is_ref = false)
    emit_agg_base_addr(aent, sb)
    push_str(sb, "  pushq %rax\n")
    return
  }
  ## TUPLE-LITERAL argument (a non-place `(a, b, …)`, built as an `ArrayLit`): materialize it into
  ## the agg-temp block (element k at `agg_tmp+k`, the down-growing layout) and push the block's
  ## word-0 ADDRESS by reference — the tuple dual of the struct-ctor case (a tuple passes like a
  ## small struct). Without this a tuple LITERAL passed directly (`display((u64,u64), (3,4), sb)`)
  ## fell through to the scalar default and pushed garbage (segfault / no output); a tuple LOCAL
  ## already worked. Scalar-component tuples; a mixed multi-word-component tuple needs strided layout.
  ali := array_lit_info(e)
  if ali.is_a and cx.agg_tmp >= 0 {
    aoff := agg_alloc(cx)
    emit_array_assign(e, aoff, sb, cx, nl)
    tlent := SlotEntry(ns = 0, nl = 0, off = usize(aoff), sns = 0, snl = 0, ek = 2, estride = 1, eek = 0, is_ref = false)
    emit_agg_base_addr(tlent, sb)
    push_str(sb, "  pushq %rax\n")
    return
  }
  ## A CALL returning a STRUCT passed as a by-ref aggregate arg (`mk().sum()` — a UFCS receiver that
  ## is itself a call, or any `f(…)` struct value forwarded by value): the call has no frame home, so
  ## materialize its register-returned words (`emit_retreg(k)`) into the agg-temp block, then push the
  ## block's word-0 ADDRESS — the by-ref convention, exactly like the ctor case above. Enables the
  ## complex-receiver UFCS the parser now desugars to `Call(method, [receiver-call, …])`.
  if struct_ret_call(e, cx.decls, cx.src, a) and cx.agg_tmp >= 0 {
    csp := call_ret_struct_span(e, cx.decls, cx.src, deref(cx.mar))
    rw := struct_words(cx.decls, cx.src, csp.s, csp.n, a)
    emit_struct_value(e, sb, cx, a, nl)
    caoff := agg_alloc(cx)
    for k in 0..rw {
      push_str(sb, "  movq ")
      emit_retreg(sb, k)
      push_str(sb, ", -")
      push_int(sb, (i64(caoff) - i64(k) + 1) * 8)
      push_str(sb, "(%rbp)\n")
    }
    cent := SlotEntry(ns = 0, nl = 0, off = usize(caoff), sns = 0, snl = 0, ek = 2, estride = 1, eek = 0, is_ref = false)
    emit_agg_base_addr(cent, sb)
    push_str(sb, "  pushq %rax\n")
    return
  }
  ## A WIDE (> 7-word, SRET) struct-returning call passed DIRECTLY as a by-value aggregate argument
  ## (`sumf(mk())`) — the sret dual of the register-return case just above. Such a callee delivers its
  ## result through a hidden result POINTER, so it needs a destination: allocate a fresh agg-temp block
  ## and publish its base on `cx.sret_call` (exactly what a `c := mk()` binding does with the destination
  ## local's base), so `emit_call_args` hands the block's ADDRESS down as the hidden %rdi. Then pass that
  ## same block's word-0 address by reference, like every other aggregate arg. Without this the call fell
  ## through to the scalar `emit_gas` path with NO hidden pointer set up, and the callee wrote the struct
  ## through whatever %rdi happened to hold → SIGSEGV. `src/`+`lib/` pass no such argument → dormant.
  if sret_ret_call(e, cx.decls, cx.src, a) and cx.agg_tmp >= 0 {
    wsoff := agg_alloc(cx)
    ovwsc := cx.sret_call
    cx.sret_call = wsoff
    emit_struct_value(e, sb, cx, a, nl)
    cx.sret_call = ovwsc
    wsent := SlotEntry(ns = 0, nl = 0, off = usize(wsoff), sns = 0, snl = 0, ek = 2, estride = 1, eek = 0, is_ref = false)
    emit_agg_base_addr(wsent, sb)
    push_str(sb, "  pushq %rax\n")
    return
  }
  ## A WIDE (disc + payload > 7-word, SRET) enum-returning call passed DIRECTLY as a by-value aggregate
  ## argument (`use_it(mk(1))`) — the enum dual of the wide-STRUCT case just above, and the x86_64 mirror
  ## of aarch64's `emit_a64_enumsret_arg`. Such a callee has NO return registers: it delivers through a
  ## hidden result POINTER, and in argument position there is no destination local to point it at. So
  ## reserve an agg-temp block FIRST and publish its base on `cx.sret_call` (saved/restored around the
  ## inner emit, so a nested wide-enum argument allocates its OWN distinct block), which makes
  ## `emit_call_args` hand the block's ADDRESS down as the hidden %rdi; then pass that same block's
  ## word-0 address by reference (ek 3), like every other aggregate arg. MUST precede the register-return
  ## `enum_ret_call_d` arm below — that one stages `emit_retreg(k)`, which tops out at %r11 (7 words) and
  ## for a wide callee wired no hidden pointer at all → the callee wrote its words through whatever %rdi
  ## happened to hold (a RAW SIGSEGV). `src/`+`lib/` declare no wide-enum-returning fn → dormant.
  if enum_sret_ret_call(e, cx.decls, cx.src, a) and cx.agg_tmp >= 0 {
    weoff := agg_alloc(cx)
    ovwec := cx.sret_call
    cx.sret_call = weoff
    emit_enum_value(e, sb, cx, a, nl)
    cx.sret_call = ovwec
    weent := SlotEntry(ns = 0, nl = 0, off = usize(weoff), sns = 0, snl = 0, ek = 3, estride = 1, eek = 0, is_ref = false, tmod_s = 0, tmod_l = 0)
    emit_agg_base_addr(weent, sb)
    push_str(sb, "  pushq %rax\n")
    return
  }
  ## An ENUM-returning call passed DIRECTLY as an argument (`f(find_str(s, n))`, `g(parse(x))`) — the
  ## enum dual of the struct case above. Without it the call fell to the scalar `Call` arm and only its
  ## word 0 (the disc) reached the by-ref enum param → the payload was garbage / a crash. Emit the call
  ## (disc/%rax + payload/%rdx… via `emit_enum_value`), stage `1 + max-payload` words into a fresh
  ## agg-temp block, and pass that block's ADDRESS by reference (ek 3). src/ binds such results to a
  ## local first (the idiom), so this never fires on the self-host build → fixpoint-neutral.
  if enum_ret_call_d(e, cx.decls, cx.src, a) and cx.agg_tmp >= 0 {
    esp := call_ret_enum_span_d(e, cx.decls, cx.src, deref(cx.mar))
    rw := 1 + enum_inst_words(cx.decls, cx.src, esp.s, esp.n, a)
    emit_enum_value(e, sb, cx, a, nl)
    eaoff := agg_alloc(cx)
    for k in 0..rw {
      push_str(sb, "  movq ")
      emit_retreg(sb, k)
      push_str(sb, ", -")
      push_int(sb, (i64(eaoff) - i64(k) + 1) * 8)
      push_str(sb, "(%rbp)\n")
    }
    eent := SlotEntry(ns = 0, nl = 0, off = usize(eaoff), sns = 0, snl = 0, ek = 3, estride = 1, eek = 0, is_ref = false, tmod_s = 0, tmod_l = 0)
    emit_agg_base_addr(eent, sb)
    push_str(sb, "  pushq %rax\n")
    return
  }
  ## A str-returning call passed DIRECTLY as an argument (`trim_end(trim_start(s))`, `f(basename(p))`).
  ## The str dual of the struct/enum cases: emit the call (ptr/%rax = word 0, len/%rdx = word 1, via
  ## emit_struct_value's Call arm), stage the 2 words UP-growing into a fresh agg-temp (word 0 at the
  ## top, matching `emit_agg_base_addr` + the callee's by-ref str read: ptr at `(%rax)`, len at
  ## `8(%rax)`), and pass the block's address (ek 4). Without it the call fell to the scalar path and
  ## pushed the data pointer as the by-ref pointer → a garbage len / crash. src/ binds such results to a
  ## local first → the branch never fires on the self-build → fixpoint-neutral.
  if (str_ret_call(e, cx.decls, cx.src, deref(cx.mar)) or generic_inferred_str_ret(e, cx, a)) and cx.agg_tmp >= 0 {
    emit_struct_value(e, sb, cx, a)
    saoff := agg_alloc(cx)
    for k in 0..2 {
      push_str(sb, "  movq ")
      emit_retreg(sb, k)
      push_str(sb, ", -")
      push_int(sb, (i64(saoff) - i64(k) + 1) * 8)
      push_str(sb, "(%rbp)\n")
    }
    sent := SlotEntry(ns = 0, nl = 0, off = usize(saoff), sns = 0, snl = 0, ek = 4, estride = 1, eek = 0, is_ref = false)
    emit_agg_base_addr(sent, sb)
    push_str(sb, "  pushq %rax\n")
    return
  }
  ## Types §7 — a two-word `{ptr, len}` VIEW VALUE argument with NO frame home of its own. The view
  ## ABI passes the ADDRESS of the pair, so an argument that IS a view but owns no addressable pair
  ## must be MATERIALIZED into a fresh agg-temp block first, then passed by reference (ek 4) exactly
  ## like a str LITERAL or a str-returning CALL. `emit_str_pair` is the ONE choke point that already
  ## knows how to produce the pair for every such shape (`sub(…)`, a range sub-view `s[lo..hi]`, a
  ## `[str; N]` element, a str field of an array-of-struct element, a str mutable GLOBAL); this arm
  ## just gives its result a home. WITHOUT it every one of those arguments fell through to the
  ## scalar `emit_gas` default at the bottom of this fn, which pushes ONE word — the view's data
  ## POINTER at best, its LENGTH for a bare `sub(…)` — where the callee's by-ref view param expects
  ## a pointer to the pair. Measured before this arm: `io::print(sub(s, 0, 4))` SIGSEGV (exit 139,
  ## the length 4 arrived as the block pointer), `io::print(s[0..5])` SIGSEGV, `io::print(xs[0])`
  ## over `[str; 2]` / `io::print(xs[i].name)` / `io::print(<mut global str>)` all printed NOTHING —
  ## silent wrong values (I11). The dropped fact is the argument's TYPE: `emit_arg` classified
  ## arguments by SHAPE and had no arm for "this expression IS a view", so anything not on the
  ## hand-written shape list degraded to a scalar. `src/`+`lib/` pass no such argument (they bind
  ## the view to a LOCAL first — the documented idiom), so this never fires on the self-host build
  ## → fixpoint-neutral.
  if deref_call_pointee_unresolved(e, cx.decls, cx.src, a) { reject_deref_call_pointee(e, cx.src) }
  if view_temp_arg(e, cx, a) and cx.agg_tmp >= 0 {
    emit_str_pair(e, sb, cx, a, nl)            ## stack: [ptr (deeper), len (top)]
    vaoff := agg_alloc(cx)
    push_str(sb, "  popq %rax\n  movq %rax, -")
    push_frame_word(sb, usize(vaoff), 1)       ## len  → block word 1
    push_str(sb, "(%rbp)\n  popq %rax\n  movq %rax, -")
    push_frame_word(sb, usize(vaoff), 0)       ## ptr  → block word 0
    push_str(sb, "(%rbp)\n")
    vaent := SlotEntry(ns = 0, nl = 0, off = usize(vaoff), sns = 0, snl = 0, ek = 4, estride = 1, eek = 0, is_ref = false)
    emit_agg_base_addr(vaent, sb)
    push_str(sb, "  pushq %rax\n")
    return
  }
  ## `deref(p)` argument where `p` is a pointer to an AGGREGATE (struct/enum): the callee's by-ref
  ## aggregate param wants the ADDRESS of the pointee, which IS `p`'s value (the pointer). Emit
  ## `p`'s value directly — do NOT word-load `*p` (the type-discard miscompile that crashed
  ## `node_alloc(deref(a))` / `node_ptr(T, deref(pc.arena))`: the lean lower otherwise reads the
  ## first word of the pointee as if it were a scalar).
  if deref_ptr_agg_arg(e, cx) {
    inner := deref_inner(e)
    emit_gas(inner, sb, cx, a, nl)
    return
  }
  ## An `if`/`match` EXPRESSION yielding an AGGREGATE (struct/enum/tuple/str) passed DIRECTLY as an
  ## argument (`f(if c { Rec(…) } else { … })`, `g(match x { … => S(…) })`). The value has NO frame
  ## home — materialize each branch/arm's value into a fresh agg-temp block (down-growing, via the
  ## `emit_val_if/match_to_local` local-delivery primitives — the SAME layout the by-ref aggregate/str
  ## ABI reads), then push the block's word-0 ADDRESS by reference. The branch dual of the struct/enum/
  ## str-CTOR arg case below. Without this the whole `if`/`match` fell through to the scalar default and
  ## passed ONE garbage word as the by-ref pointer → a struct arg SEGFAULTED, a str arg read a garbage
  ## len (Priority-1 silent miscompiles). `src/` binds such branch results to a LOCAL first (the idiom)
  ## — never passes a branch aggregate directly — so this never fires on the self-host build → neutral.
  mak := match_if_agg_kind(e, cx.decls, cx.src, a)
  if mak.kind != 0 and cx.agg_tmp >= 0 {
    boff := agg_alloc(cx)
    bmi := match_info(e)
    if bmi.is_m {
      emit_val_match_to_local(bmi.scrut, bmi.head, boff, sb, cx, a, nl)
    } else {
      bii := if_info(e)
      emit_val_if_to_local(bii.cond, bii.then_e, bii.else_e, boff, sb, cx, a, nl)
    }
    mut bek := 2
    if mak.kind == 4 { bek = 4 }
    if mak.kind == 3 { bek = 3 }
    bent := SlotEntry(ns = 0, nl = 0, off = usize(boff), sns = 0, snl = 0, ek = bek, estride = 1, eek = 0, is_ref = false)
    emit_agg_base_addr(bent, sb)
    push_str(sb, "  pushq %rax\n")
    return
  }
  if global_view_field_arg(e, cx, a) { panic("selfhost: a `str`/view FIELD of a module-level GLOBAL cannot be passed as a call argument yet - bind it to a local first (`v := G.f` then pass `v`)") }
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Var(s, n) => {
      ent = deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, s, n)))
      if ent.ek == 5 and ent.is_ref and (ent.eek == 2 or ent.eek == 3) {
        push_str(sb, "  leaq -")
        push_int(sb, (i64(ent.off) + 1) * 8)
        push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rax\n  pushq %rax\n")
        return
      }
      ## a typed array-SLICE LOCAL (`s := arr[lo..hi]`, ek 5, is_ref, `snl == 1`, `sns == 0`) stores its
      ## {ptr, len} pair DIRECTLY in its two frame words (up-growing). Passed as an arg, the callee's
      ## slice param wants a POINTER to that block — which is the local's ADDRESS, so `leaq` it. Without
      ## this it fell to `emit_agg_base_addr`, which for `is_ref` LOADS word 0 (the data ptr) and passed
      ## THAT as the block pointer → the callee double-deref'd it and read a garbage len (`s.len` = junk,
      ## so `f(s)` where `s` is a slice LOCAL truncated). A slice PARAM (`sns == 1`) already holds a real
      ## block pointer and keeps the `emit_agg_base_addr` load. src/ passes slice args directly (no such
      ## local-then-pass), so this never fires on the self-host build → fixpoint-neutral.
      if ent.ek == 5 and ent.is_ref and ent.snl == 1 and ent.sns == 0 {
        push_str(sb, "  leaq -")
        push_int(sb, (i64(ent.off) + 1) * 8)
        push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rax\n  pushq %rax\n")
        return
      }
      ## A packed fixed-array LOCAL stores byte 0 at its first filler word (`off`), not in the
      ## metadata slot at `off + 1`. Its concrete fixed-array PARAM counterpart already stores the
      ## data pointer in the slot and therefore stays on `emit_agg_base_addr`; only the local-to-param
      ## call edge needs this byte-address form.
      if ent.ek == 5 and ent.is_ref == false and is_byte_array_eek(ent.eek) {
        push_str(sb, "  leaq -")
        push_int(sb, i64(ent.off) * 8)
        push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rax\n  pushq %rax\n")
        return
      }
      ## struct/enum/array (ek 2/3/5) AND a str (ek 4) are passed BY REFERENCE — push the
      ## aggregate's word-0 address. A str local's word 0 is its ptr word; the callee's str
      ## param derefs it. (A str by-ref PARAM re-passed as an arg also rides emit_agg_base_addr,
      ## which `movq`s the incoming pointer through.)
      if ent.ek == 2 or ent.ek == 3 or ent.ek == 4 or ent.ek == 5 { is_agg = true }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  if is_agg {
    emit_agg_base_addr(ent, sb)
    push_str(sb, "  pushq %rax\n")
  } else {
    emit_gas(e, sb, cx, a, nl)
  }
}

## STACK-ARG tier (System V AMD64, >6 args). The first 6 integer/pointer value arguments go in
## %rdi %rsi %rdx %rcx %r8 %r9; arguments 7..N are passed ON THE STACK. At the callee they live
## at POSITIVE offsets above the saved %rbp — arg7 at 16(%rbp), arg8 at 24(%rbp), … (16 = 8 for
## the return address + 8 for the saved %rbp). The ABI requires arg7 (the first stack arg) at the
## LOWEST address, so the stack args are PUSHED IN REVERSE (argN first, …, arg7 last) — after the
## pushes arg7 is on top (lowest address).
##
## ALIGNMENT: the ABI requires %rsp ≡ 0 (mod 16) at the `call` instruction. This emitter's pushes
## are otherwise balanced (every operand pushed is popped), so a call sequence does NOT change
## %rsp's residue — EXCEPT the stack args, which leave `8 * nstack` bytes pushed across the call.
## To keep %rsp's 16-byte residue INVARIANT across the call (so whatever alignment held before is
## restored after), an ODD number of stack args gets ONE 8-byte pad pushed FIRST (so the bytes
## pushed before the `call`, pad + nstack words, total an EVEN word count = a 16-byte multiple).
## After the call the same `8 * (nstack + pad)` bytes are reclaimed with `addq`.
##
## The number of stack arguments for `nvals` value arguments: max(0, nvals - 6).
nstack_args := fn(nvals : usize) -> usize {
  if nvals > 6 { return nvals - 6 }
  0
}

## SRET calls reserve %rdi for the hidden result pointer, so only five ordinary GPR argument slots
## remain (%rsi..%r9). The lean call splitter is still value-index based; this shifted helper covers
## the all-integer sret shape while the full classed ABI splitter remains a later roadmap slice.
nstack_args_shift := fn(nvals : usize, sc_shift : usize) -> usize {
  cap := if sc_shift != 0 { 5 } else { 6 }
  if nvals > cap { return nvals - cap }
  0
}

## The 8-byte alignment pad word count (0 or 1) for `nvals` value arguments — 1 when the number
## of stack args is odd (so pad + nstack is even = 16-byte aligned), else 0.
align_pad := fn(nvals : usize) -> usize {
  ns := nstack_args(nvals)
  if ns % 2 != 0 { return 1 }
  0
}

align_pad_shift := fn(nvals : usize, sc_shift : usize) -> usize {
  ns := nstack_args_shift(nvals, sc_shift)
  if ns % 2 != 0 { return 1 }
  0
}

## Lower a call's value arguments into the System V locations and leave %rsp positioned for the
## `call` (the caller emits the `call <label>` after, then `emit_call_cleanup`). `args_head` is
## the arena-linked `Arg` list; `base` leading args are SKIPPED (1 for a generic call's erased
## comptime type arg, 0 otherwise); `nvals` is the number of VALUE args (total args − base).
##
## Layout: (1) if `nstack` is odd, push one 8-byte alignment pad; (2) push the STACK args
## (indices 6..nvals-1) IN REVERSE — value arg `nvals-1` first … value arg 6 last — so arg7 ends
## up on top (lowest address = 16(%rbp) at the callee); (3) lower the REGISTER args (indices
## 0..5) onto the stack and pop them into %rdi..%r9 in reverse SysV order. Steps (2)/(3) each
## leave one net stack word per `emit_arg`; the register pops are balanced, so the stack args
## stay correctly laid out on top for the `call`.
## The slot offset of the str-materialization temp block to use for VALUE argument index `i`
## (0-based, over the call's value args; the temp pool base is `cx.str_tmp`). A str-literal arg
## gets its OWN distinct 2-word block within the call — the (j-th str-literal-arg)-th block, where
## `j` = the number of str-literal value args BEFORE index `i`. Returns -1 if arg `i` is not a str
## literal (then `emit_arg` ignores it) or the pool is unallocated. Distinct blocks per str-literal
## arg are required because their addresses are all live on the stack simultaneously at the `call`.
## The SOURCE argument index of the `i`-th VALUE argument, given the erased comptime-type-argument
## index `skip` (-1 = no type arg, so value `i` = source `i`; else the type arg at source index
## `skip` is skipped, so value `i` maps past it). Generalizes the old "leading `base` skip" (which
## only handled the type arg at source index 0) to a type parameter at ANY position — e.g.
## `allocate(in out self, T : type, size, align)` erases source index 1, so value args are 0,2,3.
arg_src_idx := fn(i : usize, skip : i64, skip2 : i64, skip3 : i64) -> usize {
  ## Walk source indices, skipping the erased comptime type-arg position(s) `skip` (and `skip2`/`skip3`,
  ## -1 = none — a 2-type-param generic `map(T, U, …)` erases the two leading args, a 3-type-param
  ## `Result::map(T, E, U, …)` three), counting the value args; the `i`-th value arg's source index is
  ## returned. For a single skip this is byte-identical to the old `i >= skip ? i+1 : i`; -1 = identity.
  mut src := 0
  mut cnt := 0
  mut guard := 0
  while guard < 256 {
    is_skip := (skip >= 0 and src == usize(skip)) or (skip2 >= 0 and src == usize(skip2)) or (skip3 >= 0 and src == usize(skip3))
    if is_skip {
      src += 1
    } else {
      if cnt == i { return src }
      cnt += 1
      src += 1
    }
    guard += 1
  }
  src
}

str_arg_tmp_off := fn(args_head : ptr(mut Arg), skip : i64, skip2 : i64, skip3 : i64, i : usize, cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena) -> i64 {
  ei := arg_expr_at(args_head, arg_src_idx(i, skip, skip2, skip3), a)
  ## a str LITERAL / `str_at(…)` view / `bytes(<literal|str_at>)` argument consumes a 2-word temp
  ## block (same predicate as `scan_call_str_args`, so the pool is sized + indexed consistently).
  if not arg_str_temp(ei, cx.src, cx.decls, a) { return -1 }
  if cx.str_tmp < 0 { return -1 }
  ## count the str-value args before index `i` — that is this arg's block index `j`
  mut j := 0
  for k in 0..i {
    ek := arg_expr_at(args_head, arg_src_idx(k, skip, skip2, skip3), a)
    if arg_str_temp(ek, cx.src, cx.decls, a) { j = j + 1 }
  }
  cx.str_tmp + i64(j * 2)   ## block `j` occupies 2 words at str_tmp + j*2
}

## Resolve a call's target to its decl index (mirrors `emit_mangled_call`'s resolution): a
## qualified `mod::f` resolves `f` by its TAIL name preferring the head module; a bare `f`
## resolves preferring the caller's module. -1 if no matching decl (an intrinsic / unknown call).
## Used to read the callee's parameter modes for the out-scalar by-reference ABI.
resolve_callee_idx := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), cs : usize, cl : usize, ms : usize, ml : usize) -> i64 {
  cp := colon_pos(src, cs, cl)
  if cp >= 0 {
    hl := usize(cp)
    ts := cs + hl + 2
    tl := cl - hl - 2
    return callee_decl_idx_qual(decls, src, ts, tl, cs, hl, ms, ml)
  }
  callee_decl_idx(decls, src, cs, cl, ms, ml)
}

## Is parameter `pidx` (0-based, over the callee's FULL param list incl. a generic's leading
## comptime type param) of the callee at decl index `cidx` an OUT-SCALAR — `pmode == 2` AND a
## scalar type (not array/struct/enum/str/ptr-to-aggregate)? Such a param is bound BY REFERENCE
## (ek 8), so the caller passes the ADDRESS of the argument local, not its value. Mirrors the
## type tests in `bind_param`'s scalar fallthrough. false for `cidx < 0` (unknown callee) or any
## aggregate `out` param (those already pass by reference via their type branch).
callee_out_scalar := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena, cidx : i64, pidx : usize) -> bool {
  if cidx < 0 { return false }
  d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(cidx))))
  mut pp := d.params_head
  mut k := 0
  while pp != 0 {
    pm := deref(param_p(pp))
    if k == pidx {
      if pm.pmode != 2 { return false }
      if struct_decl_of(decls, src, pm.ts, pm.tl) >= 0 { return false }
      if enum_decl_of(decls, src, pm.ts, pm.tl) >= 0 { return false }
      if str_at((src + pm.ts), pm.tl) == "str" { return false }
      if str_at((src + pm.ts), pm.tl) == "ptr" and (enum_decl_of(decls, src, pm.pps, pm.ppl) >= 0 or struct_decl_of(decls, src, pm.pps, pm.ppl) >= 0) { return false }
      return true
    }
    k += 1
    pp = pm.next
  }
  false
}

## A scalar callee parameter cannot consume a two-word str element. Most str-element consumers are
## routed through `view_temp_arg` (which deliberately materializes the pair), but that route is shape
## based and otherwise cannot see the callee's expected type. Keep this call-edge fence here so an
## invalid `take_u64(s[0])` cannot pass a pair address as if it were a u64 value.
callee_param_is_scalar_value := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), cidx : i64, pidx : usize) -> bool {
  if cidx < 0 { return false }
  d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(cidx))))
  mut pp := d.params_head
  mut k := 0
  while pp != 0 {
    pm := deref(param_p(pp))
    if k == pidx {
      if struct_decl_of(decls, src, pm.ts, pm.tl) >= 0 { return false }
      if enum_decl_of(decls, src, pm.ts, pm.tl) >= 0 { return false }
      if str_at((src + pm.ts), pm.tl) == "str" { return false }
      pbn := base_type_name(src, pm.ts, pm.tl)
      if pbn.n != 0 and str_at((src + pbn.s), pbn.n) == "Slice" { return false }
      if pm.tl > 0 and (str_at((src + pm.ts), 1) == "[" or str_at((src + pm.ts), 1) == "(") { return false }
      if str_at((src + pm.ts), pm.tl) == "ptr" and (enum_decl_of(decls, src, pm.pps, pm.ppl) >= 0 or struct_decl_of(decls, src, pm.pps, pm.ppl) >= 0) { return false }
      return true
    }
    k += 1
    pp = pm.next
  }
  false
}

## Is a call argument syntactically a str element? `eek == 4` covers fixed `[str; N]` and
## `Slice(str)` PARAM entries; `slice_var_is_str_elem` covers the returned/constructed Slice VALUE
## entry (`ek == 2`) whose element metadata is recovered from its `Slice(str)` type span.
call_arg_is_str_elem := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> bool {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Index(base, idx) => {
      if slice_var_is_str_elem(base, cx) { return true }
      deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, index_base_entry(base, cx.slots, cx.src))).eek == 4
    }
    _ => { false }
  }
}

reject_scalar_str_elem_arg := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx), cidx : i64, pidx : usize) {
  if callee_param_is_scalar_value(cx.decls, cx.src, cidx, pidx) and call_arg_is_str_elem(e, cx) {
    match deref(e) {
      Expr::Index(base, idx) => {
        bsp := var_name_span(base)
        if bsp.n != 0 { lower_show_src_line(cx.src, bsp.s) }
      }
      _ => {}
    }
    panic("selfhost: a Slice(str) element is a two-word `{ptr,len}` value and cannot be passed to a scalar parameter (the source line above); bind it or pass it to a str parameter")
  }
}

## Pass an OUT-SCALAR argument: the address of the argument's local (a place — a `Var`). For an
## ordinary scalar local this is `leaq` its slot; for an argument that is ITSELF an out-scalar
## param (ek 8, the slot holds a pointer) it forwards that pointer — both via `emit_agg_base_addr`'s
## is_ref dual. (A non-place out argument is invalid; fall back to the value path so it still lowers.)
emit_out_scalar_arg := fn(e : ptr(Expr), in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena, in out nl : usize) {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Var(s, n) => {
      ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, s, n)))
      emit_agg_base_addr(ent, sb)
      push_str(sb, "  pushq %rax\n")
    }
    _ => { emit_gas(e, sb, cx, a, nl) }
  }
}

emit_call_args := fn(args_head : ptr(mut Arg), skip : i64, skip2 : i64, skip3 : i64, nvals : usize, in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, cx : ptr(LCtx), in out nl : usize) {
  a := arena_of(cx)
  ## AGGREGATE-VALUE args (§8, no silent miscompiles): each struct/enum/tuple LITERAL or struct-RETURNING
  ## call arg materializes into a DISTINCT agg-temp slice (bump allocator, `agg_alloc`) and is passed by
  ## reference — so 2+ in one call no longer alias. SAVE the bump pointer here and RESTORE it after the
  ## args are lowered (below): this call's slices are reclaimed for the next call, while a NESTED call's
  ## args correctly stack ABOVE this call's still-live slices (its own save captured the higher point).
  aggsave := cx.agg_next
  ## the callee decl index for out-scalar arg detection — captured into a LOCAL up front, since
  ## lowering a nested call argument (emit_arg → emit_gas → a further emit_call_args) overwrites
  ## `cx.call_cidx`. -1 = unknown callee (no out-scalar passing).
  cidx := cx.call_cidx
  ## FN-6 — the fn-VALUE TYPE of an INDIRECT call (0 = an ordinary named call), captured into a LOCAL
  ## and CLEARED for the same reason as `cidx`: lowering a nested call ARGUMENT re-enters here and
  ## would otherwise inherit this call's fn type. It supplies the ARGUMENT classes the parser dropped
  ## when it kept only the bare `fn` token for the value's type — without them an `f64` argument rode a
  ## GPR while the callee read %xmm (a silent wrong value whenever %xmm0 did not happen to hold it).
  ift := cx.ind_fn_fmask
  cx.ind_fn_fmask = 0
  ## SRET: this call returns a struct > 7 words. `sc` = the caller's destination slot base (its ADDRESS
  ## goes in the hidden %rdi arg), captured into a LOCAL then CLEARED so a nested argument-call is not
  ## itself treated as sret. `sc_shift` = 1 shifts every real reg-arg to %rsi.. (leaving %rdi for the
  ## result ptr). -1 / 0 for an ordinary call → the byte-identical existing path.
  sc := cx.sret_call
  cx.sret_call = -1
  sc_shift := if sc >= 0 { 1 } else { 0 }
  ## `skip` is the SOURCE index of the erased comptime type argument (-1 = none); value arg `i`
  ## maps to source arg `arg_src_idx(i, skip, skip2, skip3)` (skips past a type arg at ANY position, not only 0).
  reg_cap := if sc_shift != 0 { 5 } else { 6 }
  ## alignment pad (one word) when the stack-arg count is odd
  if align_pad_shift(nvals, sc_shift) != 0 { push_str(sb, "  subq $8, %rsp\n") }
  ## stack args (indices 6..nvals-1) pushed in REVERSE so arg7 lands at the lowest address
  mut k := nvals
  while k > reg_cap {
    k = k - 1
    ea := arg_expr_at(args_head, arg_src_idx(k, skip, skip2, skip3), a)
    reject_scalar_str_elem_arg(ea, cx, cidx, arg_src_idx(k, skip, skip2, skip3))
    if callee_out_scalar(cx.decls, cx.src, a, cidx, arg_src_idx(k, skip, skip2, skip3)) {
      emit_out_scalar_arg(ea, sb, cx, a, nl)
    } else {
      emit_arg(ea, sb, cx, a, nl, str_arg_tmp_off(args_head, skip, skip2, skip3, k, cx, a))
    }
  }
  ## register args (indices 0..min(nvals,6)-1): lower onto the stack, then pop in reverse
  nreg := if nvals > reg_cap { reg_cap } else { nvals }
  for i in 0..nreg {
    ra := arg_expr_at(args_head, arg_src_idx(i, skip, skip2, skip3), a)
    reject_scalar_str_elem_arg(ra, cx, cidx, arg_src_idx(i, skip, skip2, skip3))
    if callee_out_scalar(cx.decls, cx.src, a, cidx, arg_src_idx(i, skip, skip2, skip3)) {
      emit_out_scalar_arg(ra, sb, cx, a, nl)
    } else {
      emit_arg(ra, sb, cx, a, nl, str_arg_tmp_off(args_head, skip, skip2, skip3, i, cx, a))
    }
  }
  ## Pop the register args (in reverse — top of stack = the last arg) into their SysV registers.
  ## INTEGER-ONLY callees (the entire self-host source + any all-int call) take the byte-identical
  ## all-GPR loop `arg i → argreg(i)`, so the fixpoint is untouched. A callee with ANY float param
  ## uses the class-aware routing: floats consume xmm0.. and integers consume %rdi.. with INDEPENDENT
  ## counters (the per-arg index = how many SAME-class value args precede it).
  if not call_has_float_param(cx, cidx, ift) {
    mut j := nreg
    while j != 0 {
      j = j - 1
      push_str(sb, "  popq ")
      emit_argreg(sb, j + sc_shift)          ## +1 under sret: real args ride %rsi.. (%rdi = result ptr)
      push_str(sb, "\n")
    }
  } else {
    mut j := nreg
    while j != 0 {
      j = j - 1
      if call_param_is_float(cx, cidx, ift, arg_src_idx(j, skip, skip2, skip3), j) {
        mut si := 0
        mut t := 0
        while t < j {
          if call_param_is_float(cx, cidx, ift, arg_src_idx(t, skip, skip2, skip3), t) { si = si + 1 }
          t += 1
        }
        push_str(sb, "  popq %rax\n  movq %rax, ")
        emit_xmmreg(sb, si)
        push_str(sb, "\n")
      } else {
        mut gj := 0
        mut u := 0
        while u < j {
          if not call_param_is_float(cx, cidx, ift, arg_src_idx(u, skip, skip2, skip3), u) { gj = gj + 1 }
          u += 1
        }
        ## `+ sc_shift` for the SAME reason as the all-GPR loop above: an sret call (and the `dyn`
        ## fat-pair call, which reuses the sret shift to free %rdi for the environment pointer) rides
        ## its INTEGER args from %rsi on. The SSE index is NOT shifted — %xmm0.. stay free.
        push_str(sb, "  popq ")
        emit_argreg(sb, gj + sc_shift)
        push_str(sb, "\n")
      }
    }
  }
  ## SRET: after the real args are in %rsi.., load the destination LOCAL's word-0 ADDRESS into %rdi
  ## (the hidden result pointer). The callee writes the whole struct through it (down-growing:
  ## word k at -(k*8)(%rdi)) straight into the caller's slots. (≤5 real integer args; see the note.)
  if sc >= 0 {
    push_str(sb, "  leaq -")
    push_int(sb, i64((sc + 1) * 8))
    push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rdi\n")
  }
  ## reclaim this call's agg-temp slices (the by-ref args are now in registers/stack, still pointing at
  ## the materialized values, which stay valid through the caller's `call`; nothing materializes between).
  cx.agg_next = aggsave
}

## Reclaim the bytes pushed for stack arguments + alignment pad after a `call` (System V — the
## caller cleans up its stack args). Emits `addq $(8 * (nstack + pad)), %rsp` when there were any
## stack args; a no-op for ≤6 value args (the register-only path is byte-identical).
emit_call_cleanup := fn(in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, nvals : usize) {
  nwords := nstack_args(nvals) + align_pad(nvals)
  if nwords != 0 {
    push_str(sb, "  addq $")
    push_int(sb, i64(nwords * 8))
    push_str(sb, ", %rsp\n")
  }
}

emit_call_cleanup_shift := fn(in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, nvals : usize, sc_shift : usize) {
  nwords := nstack_args_shift(nvals, sc_shift) + align_pad_shift(nvals, sc_shift)
  if nwords != 0 {
    push_str(sb, "  addq $")
    push_int(sb, i64(nwords * 8))
    push_str(sb, ", %rsp\n")
  }
}

## An `EnumLit`-detection result: whether the expression is an enum-variant construction, and
## (if so) the enum TYPE name span. Carried out of `enum_lit_info` so the deref-`match` stays
## a function-body match over a pointer PARAM (the lowerable shape — see `struct_lit_info`).
EInfo := struct { is_e : bool, es : usize, el : usize }

## Is `e` an enum-variant construction `E.V(…)`? If so, also report the enum TYPE name span.
enum_lit_info := fn(e : ptr(Expr)) -> EInfo {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::EnumLit(es, el, vs, vl, np, phead) => { EInfo(is_e = true, es = es, el = el) }
    _ => { EInfo(is_e = false, es = 0, el = 0) }
  }
}
## The FULL parts of an enum-variant construction `E.V(payload…)` — enum type span, variant name span,
## payload arg count, and payload `Arg`-list head — for laying an ENUM-valued mutable global into `.data`
## (`[disc, payload…, pad]`). Ptr-param match (a local-Expr `match deref` degenerates).
EFull := struct { is_e : bool, es : usize, el : usize, vs : usize, vl : usize, np : usize, phead : ptr(mut Arg) }
enum_lit_full := fn(e : ptr(Expr)) -> EFull {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::EnumLit(es, el, vs, vl, np, phead) => { EFull(is_e = true, es = es, el = el, vs = vs, vl = vl, np = np, phead = phead) }
    _ => { EFull(is_e = false, es = 0, el = 0, vs = 0, vl = 0, np = 0, phead = 0) }
  }
}
## Is `e` a `Call` expression. Kept beside the AST-shape probes used by literal slot synthesis so
## callers can guard call-only classifiers before passing arbitrary payload expressions to them.
expr_is_call := fn(e : ptr(Expr)) -> bool {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, na, ah) => { true }
    _ => { false }
  }
}
## True iff `v` is a struct literal `S(f0, …)` whose EVERY field is exactly ONE WORD (a scalar TYPE) —
## checked by the struct's total word count equalling its field count, so word index k == field index k.
## This is TYPE-based (not value-based): a `str` field (2 words) or a nested-struct field is caught even
## when its value is a plain VAR, so the DerefAssign fast path never mis-stores a wide field (deferred).
slit_scalar_fields := fn(v : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> bool {
  si := struct_lit_info(v)
  if si.is_s == false { return false }
  nw := struct_words(cx.decls, cx.src, si.ss, si.sl, deref(cx.mar))
  nw == struct_lit_nf(v)
}
## True iff `v` is an enum literal `E.V(p0, …)` whose EVERY payload is a SCALAR value (not a struct /
## str / nested-enum literal) — the case the DerefAssign pointer-store fast path handles; a complex
## payload is deferred to the existing slow path (no regression).
elit_scalar_payloads := fn(v : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> bool {
  ef := enum_lit_full(v)
  if ef.is_e == false { return false }
  mut g := ef.phead
  mut ok := true
  while g != 0 {
    ga := deref(arg_p(g))
    ## reject a struct / nested-enum / str payload (a str is 2 words: `is_str_operand` catches a literal,
    ## a var, and `sub(…)`) — any of those is >1 word and would mis-store at the 1-word-per-payload path.
    if struct_lit_info(ga.e).is_s or enum_lit_info(ga.e).is_e or is_str_operand(ga.e, cx) { ok = false }
    g = ga.next
  }
  ok
}

## A `StructLit`-detection result: whether the expression is a struct construction, and (if
## so) the struct's TYPE name span. Carried out of `struct_lit_info` so the `match` on the
## deref'd `Expr` stays a FUNCTION-BODY-level match over a pointer PARAM — the lowerable shape
## (matching `deref(ptr)` on a pointer extracted as an enum-pattern binding is NOT lowered
## yet, so the deref-match is isolated into these helper functions that take the pointer
## directly, mirroring `emit_gas`'s own top-level `match deref(e)`).
SInfo := struct { is_s : bool, ss : usize, sl : usize }

## Is `e` a struct construction `S(…)`? If so, also report the struct TYPE name span.
struct_lit_info := fn(e : ptr(Expr)) -> SInfo {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::StructLit(ss, sl, nf, fhead) => { SInfo(is_s = true, ss = ss, sl = sl) }
    _ => { SInfo(is_s = false, ss = 0, sl = 0) }
  }
}

## Map a type-name span (typically a struct/enum LITERAL's name) to a "clean" instance-tag span —
## its DECLARATION's name span (`P := struct …`, whose `P` is followed by ` :=`, not by the
## literal's `(fields)`). A generic type-arg inferred from a literal `P(x = …)` points its name span
## right before `(`, so `typearg_at` mis-reads the field list as type-args: two `P` tags then fail
## `inst_targ_eq` and emit a DUPLICATE `__P` symbol. Resolving to the paren-free decl name makes the
## tag canonical, so all `P` instances dedup regardless of how `T` was inferred. Returns the input
## unchanged for a non-nominal name (a primitive, or an as-yet-undeclared type).
type_lit_tag := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize) -> CSpan {
  sd := struct_decl_of(decls, src, s, n)
  if sd >= 0 { d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(sd)))); return CSpan(s = d.name_start, n = d.name_len) }
  ed := enum_decl_of(decls, src, s, n)
  if ed >= 0 { d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(ed)))); return CSpan(s = d.name_start, n = d.name_len) }
  CSpan(s = s, n = n)
}

## The arena-linked field-value (`Arg`) list head of a struct literal `S(f = v, …)`, or 0. Lets a
## caller read a struct literal's FIRST field value (`arg_expr_at(struct_lit_fields(e), 0, a)`) —
## e.g. the enum-return convention returning a struct payload's word 0. Isolated deref-match.
struct_lit_fields := fn(e : ptr(Expr)) -> usize {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::StructLit(ss, sl, nf, fhead) => { fhead }
    _ => { 0 }
  }
}
## The FIELD (argument) count of a struct literal `S(f0, …, f_{nf-1})`, or 0. The number of ARGS — NOT
## the number of WORDS (a nested-struct field is one arg but several words) — so a caller can iterate a
## struct lit's fields without overrunning the `Arg` list. Isolated deref-match.
struct_lit_nf := fn(e : ptr(Expr)) -> usize {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::StructLit(ss, sl, nf, fhead) => { nf }
    _ => { 0 }
  }
}
## Word width of a raw union or ordinary struct value. Union values use the existing struct-like slot
## class, but their storage has no discriminant and `struct_words` intentionally does not recognize the
## union declaration. Keep this distinction in one helper so whole-value copies and aggregate-field
## extraction cannot silently emit zero words for a union.
aggregate_words := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize, a : rt::Arena) -> usize {
  if is_union_decl(decls, src, s, n) { return union_words(decls, src, s, n, a) }
  struct_words(decls, src, s, n, a)
}

## Emit the `.data` cells of a mutable-global aggregate INITIALIZER, RECURSIVELY: a struct literal emits
## its fields in order (a nested struct field recurses — so `[a, b, q]` for `Outer(Inner(a,b), q)`); a
## float literal is a `.double`; anything else a `.quad` of its comptime integer value. Iterating by
## FIELD count (`struct_lit_nf`), not word count, is what avoids overrunning the `Arg` list on a nested
## struct (`struct_words` counts nested words but there are fewer args). Ptr-param recursion.
emit_global_data_cells := fn(e : ptr(Expr), in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) {
  sli := struct_lit_info(e)
  sti := str_lit_info(e)
  ali := array_lit_info(e)
  if sli.is_s {
    nfa := struct_lit_nf(e)
    mut k := 0
    while k < nfa {
      emit_global_data_cells(arg_expr_at(struct_lit_fields(e), k, a), sb, decls, src, a)
      k += 1
    }
  } else if ali.is_a {
    ## an ARRAY FIELD (`xs = [10, 20, 12]`) — emit each element cell in order (recursively, so a
    ## struct/str element lays out correctly), matching the top-level array-global emission and the
    ## `g.xs[i]` read (element `i` at `field_word_offset(xs) + i`).
    mut ak := 0
    while ak < ali.nel {
      emit_global_data_cells(arg_expr_at(ali.ehead, ak, a), sb, decls, src, a)
      ak += 1
    }
  } else if sti.is_s {
    ## a `str` FIELD initializer (`name = "hi"`) — a 2-word `{ptr, len}` cell: the `.rodata` label
    ## address then the byte length (matching a str local's {ptr, len} slot pair and the field read).
    push_str(sb, "  .quad ")
    push_lstr(sb, sti.lbl)
    push_str(sb, "\n  .quad ")
    push_int(sb, i64(sti.sl))
    push_str(sb, "\n")
  } else {
    ef := enum_lit_full(e)
    fls := float_lit_span(e)
    if ef.is_e {
      if is_union_decl(decls, src, ef.es, ef.el) {
        ## RAW UNION initializer: one payload at offset zero, no discriminant. Multi-payload members are
        ## deliberately unsupported here because §6.3 only says they are additive and gives no layout
        ## contract (gap). The payload's actual width is recovered from the member declaration so
        ## wide struct payloads are padded to the union's max width rather than shifting later fields.
        if ef.np != 1 { panic("selfhost: multi-payload union global initializer is unsupported until the spec defines payload layout") }
        pay := arg_expr_at(ef.phead, 0, a)
        emit_global_data_cells(pay, sb, decls, src, a)
        pty := variant_payload_type(decls, src, ef.es, ef.el, ef.vs, ef.vl, a)
        mut pw : usize = 1
        if pty.n != 0 { pw = field_words(decls, src, pty.s, pty.n, 1, a) }
        uw := union_words(decls, src, ef.es, ef.el, a)
        mut pad := pw
        while pad < uw {
          push_str(sb, "  .quad 0\n")
          pad += 1
        }
      } else {
      ## an ENUM FIELD initializer (`c = Col.G(40)`) — `[disc, payload…, pad]` to the enum's full
      ## `1 + max_arity` word width (like a top-level enum global), so the enum field occupies its
      ## real width in `.data` and the following field's cells align (else it was one `.quad` and the
      ## next field overwrote the payload — the global-struct-with-enum-field silent miscompile).
      push_str(sb, "  .quad ")
      push_int(sb, variant_index(decls, src, ef.es, ef.el, ef.vs, ef.vl, a))
      push_str(sb, "\n")
      mut pk := 0
      while pk < ef.np {
        push_str(sb, "  .quad ")
        push_int(sb, global_init_value(arg_expr_at(ef.phead, pk, a), decls, src))
        push_str(sb, "\n")
        pk += 1
      }
      emxw := enum_inst_words(decls, src, ef.es, ef.el, a)
      mut padk := ef.np
      while padk < emxw {
        push_str(sb, "  .quad 0\n")
        padk += 1
      }
      }
    } else if fls.n != 0 {
      push_str(sb, "  .double ")
      push_str(sb, str_at((src + fls.s), fls.n))
      push_str(sb, "\n")
    } else {
      push_str(sb, "  .quad ")
      push_int(sb, global_init_value(e, decls, src))
      push_str(sb, "\n")
    }
  }
}

## A `StrLit`-detection result: whether the expression is a string literal, and (if so) its
## inner-bytes source span `[ss, ss+sl)` + label index. Carried out of `str_lit_info` so the
## deref-`match` stays a function-body match over a pointer PARAM (the lowerable shape, like
## `struct_lit_info`). A `str` LOCAL (`name := "…"`) is laid out like a 2-field struct — a
## {ptr, len} pair — at two contiguous frame slots (ptr at `off`, len at `off+1`); `ek == 4`
## marks it. `len`/`str_eq` read those words (or a literal's pair) directly.
StrInfo := struct { is_s : bool, ss : usize, sl : usize, lbl : usize }
str_lit_info := fn(e : ptr(Expr)) -> StrInfo {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::StrLit(ss, sl, lbl) => { StrInfo(is_s = true, ss = ss, sl = sl, lbl = lbl) }
    _ => { StrInfo(is_s = false, ss = 0, sl = 0, lbl = 0) }
  }
}

## Is the expression a `Call` to the str-VIEW builtin `sub(s, start, len)` — i.e. a str-PRODUCING
## call? `sub` forms a substring view `{s.ptr + start, len}` over an existing str operand. Detected
## by the callee name span (read via `str_at`) so the result is treated as a str value EVERYWHERE a
## str expression flows: `str_eq(sub(…), lit)` (`emit_str_pair`'s `Call` arm), and `name := sub(…)`
## (a str-slot binding + a {ptr, len} store). The deref-`match` on the expr pointer stays in this
## pointer-PARAM helper (the lowerable shape). `cx` carries `src` so the callee name can be read.
is_str_call := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> bool {
  is_sub_call(e, cx.src)
}

## The `src`-only form of the str-VIEW-call detector (used by `collect_slots`, which has `src` but no
## `LCtx`): is `e` a `Call` to `sub`? The deref-`match` stays in this pointer-PARAM helper.
is_sub_call := fn(e : ptr(Expr), src : ptr(u8)) -> bool {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, args_head) => { str_at((src + cs), cl) == "sub" }
    _ => { false }
  }
}

## Is `e` a `bytes(x)` call — the str→`[u8]` view (appendix 160 §3.5)? `str` and `[u8]` share the
## `{ptr, len}` representation, so `bytes(x)` is a str-VALUED expression: a `name := bytes(x)`
## binding reserves a str slot, and `emit_str_pair` emits `x`'s pair. (`src/` uses `bytes` only in
## the `bytes(x)[i]` index form, so this classification never fires for the fixpoint build.)
is_bytes_call := fn(e : ptr(Expr), src : ptr(u8)) -> bool {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, args_head) => { nargs == 1 and str_at((src + cs), cl) == "bytes" }
    _ => { false }
  }
}

## An `ArrayLit`-detection result: whether the expression is an array literal, and (if so) its
## element COUNT. Carried out of `array_lit_info` so the deref-`match` stays a function-body
## match over a pointer PARAM (the lowerable shape, like `struct_lit_info`). An array LOCAL
## (`name := [e0, …]`) is laid out as N contiguous word-sized frame slots (element `i` at slot
## `off + i`, byte offset -(off + i + 1) * 8(%rbp)); `ek == 5` marks the base slot.
ArrInfo := struct { is_a : bool, nel : usize, ehead : ptr(mut Arg) }
array_lit_info := fn(e : ptr(Expr)) -> ArrInfo {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::ArrayLit(nel, ehead) => { ArrInfo(is_a = true, nel = nel, ehead = ehead) }
    _ => { ArrInfo(is_a = false, nel = 0, ehead = 0) }
  }
}

## ARRAY-OF-AGGREGATE element-type detection. Given an array literal's element-list head
## `ehead`, inspect the FIRST element to determine the element kind + (for an aggregate) its
## type span; the element STRIDE in words follows from the type (struct = nfields, enum =
## 1 + max_arity, scalar = 1). All elements of a literal share a type (the parser enforces a
## homogeneous literal positionally), so the first element decides the layout. `eek`: 0 a
## word-sized scalar element, 2 a struct element, 3 an enum element. `ess`/`esl` carry the
## element TYPE name span (0/0 for a scalar element). The deref-match on the first element's
## Expr stays inside `struct_lit_info`/`enum_lit_info` (pointer-PARAM helpers — the lowerable
## shape). `decls` resolves the stride from the type span.
## Is `e` a FLOAT literal? (a direct-PARAM deref-match helper, isolated like `var_name_span`.)
expr_is_floatlit := fn(e : ptr(Expr)) -> bool {
  mut res := false
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::FloatLit(fs, fl) => { res = true }
    _ => {}
  }
  res
}
## The source-text span of a `FloatLit` (`{0,0}` if not one) — for a FLOAT global's `.data` `.double`
## cell and for recognizing a float-valued global. Ptr-param match.
float_lit_span := fn(e : ptr(Expr)) -> CSpan {
  mut res := CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::FloatLit(fs, fl) => { res = CSpan(s = fs, n = fl) }
    _ => {}
  }
  res
}
## True if `[s, n)` names a mutable FLOAT-valued global (`mut F := 3.14`) — its `Var` reads/arithmetic
## take the float (xmm) path, and its `.data` cell is a `.double`.
is_mut_float_global := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize) -> bool {
  mgv := mut_global_value(decls, src, s, n)
  if unchecked bitcast(usize, mgv) == 0 { return false }
  float_lit_span(mgv).n != 0
}
## Is `e` an unconstrained INTEGER LITERAL (`Expr::Num`)? A `{}`-template `print` hole filled by a
## bare int literal renders via the non-generic `print_one_int` (its default type is `i64`, §7.1),
## so no type-argument span is needed. Ptr-param match (a local-Expr `match deref` degenerates).
expr_is_numlit := fn(e : ptr(Expr)) -> bool {
  mut res := false
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Num(v, s, n) => { res = true }
    Expr::BoolLit(v) => { res = true }   ## a bool literal is a word-sized 0/1 constant — same hole path
    _ => {}
  }
  res
}
## Is `e` an ARITHMETIC `Bin` (`+ - * / %` etc. — an INTEGER-valued expression, NOT a comparison
## `== < …` (bool), logical `and`/`or`/`not`, or other kind)? A `{}`-`print` hole filled by such an
## expression (`a + b`) has no type span / is not a var-or-literal, so it defaults to the `i64`
## renderer (`print_one_int`, §7.1) — else it rendered NOTHING. Comparison ops 20/24/25/26/27/28 and
## logical 40/41/42 are excluded (they are bool, not int).
expr_is_arith_bin := fn(e : ptr(Expr)) -> bool {
  mut res := false
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Bin(op, l, r) => {
      if op != 20 and op != 24 and op != 25 and op != 26 and op != 27 and op != 28 and op != 40 and op != 41 and op != 42 { res = true }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  res
}
## The integer value of an `Expr::Num` initializer (else 0) — the `.data` `.quad` initializer of a
## mutable module global (`mut COUNTER := 42`). Ptr-param match (a local-Expr `match deref` degenerates).
## The COMPTIME value of a mutable-global initializer: a literal, a module CONSTANT reference (resolved
## to its value), or an arithmetic combination thereof (`mut G := 6 * 7` / `mut G := BASE + 2` /
## `mut G := 0 - 5`). Recursively evaluated so the `.data` `.quad` gets the folded integer; a
## non-constant / unsupported shape yields 0. Ptr-param match + ptr-param recursion (a local-Expr
## `match deref` degenerates to ek 0). Ops mirror the comptime `apply` set (+ - * / %).
global_init_value := fn(e : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8)) -> i64 {
  mut res := 0
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Num(v, s, n) => { res = i64(v) }
    Expr::BoolLit(v) => { res = i64(v) }
    Expr::Var(s, n) => {
      mcv := module_const_value(decls, src, s, n)
      if unchecked bitcast(usize, mcv) != 0 { res = global_init_value(mcv, decls, src) }
    }
    Expr::Bin(op, l, r) => {
      lv := global_init_value(l, decls, src)
      rv := global_init_value(r, decls, src)
      if op == 16 { res = unchecked (lv + rv) }
      if op == 17 { res = unchecked (lv - rv) }
      if op == 18 { res = unchecked (lv * rv) }
      if op == 19 { res = unchecked (lv / rv) }
      if op == 29 { res = unchecked (lv % rv) }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  res
}

## The byte-array variant of a global initializer. The ordinary scalar-global image deliberately keeps
## its historical `global_init_value` behavior (including seed/fixpoint output); only an explicitly
## typed byte array needs to peel the parser's `unchecked(0 - N)` wrapper around a negative literal.
global_byte_init_value := fn(e : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8)) -> i64 {
  mut res := global_init_value(e, decls, src)
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Unchecked(inner) => { res = global_init_value(inner, decls, src) }
    _ => {}
  }
  res
}

AElem := struct { eek : u8, ess : usize, esl : usize, stride : usize }

## BYTE-PACKED ARRAY ELEMENT KINDS. These are deliberately separate from eek 0: the latter is the
## historical WORD array ABI and changing its address/load semantics would silently rewrite every
## existing local array. A packed byte array is selected only from an explicit `[u8|i8|bits8; N]`
## annotation; inferred literals retain the old word layout until their type is known.
is_byte_array_eek := fn(eek : u8) -> bool {
  eek == 8 or eek == 10 or eek == 11
}
byte_type_eek := fn(src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize) -> u8 {
  t := str_at((src + s), n)
  if t == "u8" { return 8 }
  if t == "i8" { return 10 }
  if t == "bits8" { return 11 }
  0
}

## Recover the byte element kind of an explicitly typed local fixed array. Array literals themselves
## carry no scalar type annotation, so this source-side query is the authority for `[u8; N]` storage.
fixed_array_byte_eek := fn(src : ptr(u8), ns : usize, nl : usize) -> u8 {
  lt := local_type_span(src, ns, nl)
  if lt.n == 0 { return 0 }
  es := array_elem_span(src, lt.s, lt.n)
  if es.n == 0 { return 0 }
  byte_type_eek(src, es.s, es.n)
}

## `slots` (a `ptr(SVec)`) resolves a POINTER element `[ptr(mut b0), …]` to its pointee struct span;
## it may be 0 (the IR-scan caller has no frame yet) — the ptr-element branch then folds to the scalar
## default, byte-identical (an AddrOf element is never const, so the IR barrier rejects it anyway).
arr_elem_info := fn(ehead : ptr(mut Arg), src : ptr(u8), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), a : rt::Arena, slots : ptr(SVec)) -> AElem {
  mut res := AElem(eek = 0, ess = 0, esl = 0, stride = 1)
  if ehead == 0 { return res }
  ga := deref(arg_p(ehead))
  si := struct_lit_info(ga.e)
  ei := enum_lit_info(ga.e)
  ti := str_lit_info(ga.e)
  if si.is_s {
    ## §8 ARRAY-OF-@packed (spec Types §8) — DEFERRED, fail LOUD. The array-of-aggregate machinery is
    ## WORD-granular: element `i` lives at `base - i * estride * 8` (estride in WORDS) and `arr[i].f`
    ## resolves via `field_word_offset` (word offsets, `movq` loads). A `@packed` struct element needs a
    ## BYTE-precise element stride (`packed_struct_bytes`, e.g. 5 — the C-ABI layout for a struct array)
    ## and BYTE-precise field offsets (`packed_field_byte_offset`, sized `movb`/`movl`) — a byte-granular
    ## addressing distinct from every `estride * 8` site. Emitting the word path here would SILENTLY
    ## MISCOMPILE (`arr[i]` at byte 8i not 5i, and `.f` at the wrong word), so REJECT it — a fail-loud is
    ## the discipline (a valid binary with a wrong result is the forbidden outcome). Gated on `is_packed`
    ## → NEUTRAL for every non-packed struct array (byte-identical) and for the self-build (no `@packed`
    ## in `src/`/`lib/`). Removing this panic is the entry point for a real byte-strided packed-array tier.
    if is_packed(decls, src, si.ss, si.sl) { panic("selfhost: an array whose element is a @packed struct is not supported (byte-precise element stride + packed field offsets would need byte-granular array addressing, a deferred slice); rejected rather than silently miscompiled to a word-padded layout") }
    if std_struct_has_byte_layout(decls, src, si.ss, si.sl, a) { panic("selfhost: an array whose element is a standard-layout byte aggregate is not yet supported (byte-precise element stride + aggregate consumers need a dedicated tier); rejected rather than silently miscompiled to a word-padded layout") }
    nf := struct_words(decls, src, si.ss, si.sl, a)
    res = AElem(eek = 2, ess = si.ss, esl = si.sl, stride = nf)
  } else if ei.is_e {
    mx := enum_inst_words(decls, src, ei.es, ei.el, a)
    res = AElem(eek = 3, ess = ei.es, esl = ei.el, stride = 1 + mx)
  } else if ti.is_s {
    ## a `str` element (`["fn", "return", …]`) — each element is a 2-word {ptr, len} value
    ## (eek = 4, stride 2). No nominal type span (str is structural), so ess/esl stay 0.
    res = AElem(eek = 4, ess = 0, esl = 0, stride = 2)
  } else if expr_is_floatlit(ga.e) {
    ## a FLOAT element (`[1.5, 2.5]`) — one word like an integer element (stride 1), but tagged
    ## `eek = 9` so an `xs[i]` read is recognized as an `f64` value (the aggregate-copy paths key on
    ## eek 2/3/4 and treat 9 as a plain word, so storage/read are unchanged from a scalar array).
    res = AElem(eek = 9, ess = 0, esl = 0, stride = 1)
  } else if array_lit_info(ga.e).is_a {
    ## a TUPLE / nested-ARRAY element (`[(a, b), …]`, a tuple is built as an `ArrayLit`) — an aggregate
    ## of `nel` scalar words; stride = its component count, `eek = 5` (the tuple/array element kind).
    ## `xs[i].N` (parsed `Index(Index(xs, i), N)`) reads component N at the strided element base.
    tai := array_lit_info(ga.e)
    if tai.nel != 0 and array_lit_info(arg_expr_at(tai.ehead, 0, a)).is_a { panic("selfhost: nested array/tuple aggregate storage is not yet byte-aware; rejected rather than silently using a word stride") }
    res = AElem(eek = 5, ess = 0, esl = 0, stride = tai.nel)
  } else if unchecked bitcast(usize, slots) != 0 and addr_struct_span(ga.e, slots, src).n != 0 {
    ## a POINTER element `[ptr(mut b0), …]` (`AddrOf(<struct local>)`, spec MEM-7) — one word (the
    ## pointer value), stride 1 like a scalar element, but tagged `eek = 7` (mirroring the ek-7
    ## pointer-to-struct SLOT kind) and carrying the POINTEE struct span so a later
    ## `s := arr[..]; deref(s[i]).field` selects the field through the element pointer. Storage and the
    ## scalar `arr[i]`/`s[i]` read are UNCHANGED from a scalar element (1 word); only the inline
    ## deref-field select keys on `eek == 7` + the pointee span. Gated on a non-null `slots` + a
    ## struct-local pointee, so every existing array-literal element kind binds byte-identically (no
    ## `[ptr(…); N]` literal in `src/`/`lib/` → fixpoint-neutral for the self-build).
    ase := addr_struct_span(ga.e, slots, src)
    res = AElem(eek = 7, ess = ase.s, esl = ase.n, stride = 1)
  }
  res
}

## Bind an ARRAY local: the base name takes slot `off` (ek = 5) and `nel` element words occupy
## slots `off .. off+nel` contiguously — `nel` anonymous filler slots are reserved after the
## base so no later local overlaps the element words. (Element 0 shares the base slot `off`;
## the base slot IS element 0's storage — so `nel` total words, the base + nel-1 fillers... but
## we reserve `nel` fillers AFTER the base to keep the indexing uniform with struct/enum: the
## base slot holds element 0, fillers hold elements 1..nel. To keep element `i` at slot off+i
## with NO overlap we reserve `nel - 1` fillers; reserve `nel` would waste one — but reserving
## the base + nel fillers (nel+1 words) is harmless and simplest, matching bind_struct_slot's
## base-plus-nfields shape.) Re-assignment of an already-bound array local reuses its base.
## `el` is the element-layout descriptor (kind + type span + stride in words); a word-sized
## array passes `eek = 0, stride = 1` (the pre-existing layout, byte-identical). The base slot
## records the element TYPE span (`sns`/`snl`), stride and element-kind; `nel * stride` element
## words are reserved after the base.
bind_array_slot := fn(in out slots : SVec, src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize, nel : usize, el : AElem) {
  ## I11: reject a rebinding whose existing reservation is NARROWER than this one needs (see
  ## `require_reservation`) — the wide store would land on the neighbouring locals' slots.
  require_reservation(ptr(slots), src, s, n, nel * el.stride + 1)
  existing := slot_of(ptr(slots), src, s, n)
  if existing >= 0 { return }
  ## For a SCALAR (eek 0) / FLOAT (eek 9) element array, `sns`/`snl` are NOT a type span
  ## (el.ess/el.esl are 0 for those); reuse `snl` to record the element COUNT `nel` so
  ## `for x in <array>` knows the static length (the array has no runtime length). Aggregate /
  ## str elements (eek 2/3/4) keep `sns`/`snl` = the element TYPE span. The one generic reader
  ## of a Var's `snl` (`expr_type_span`, overload resolution) skips array slots (`ek == 5`).
  mut asns := el.ess
  mut asnl := el.esl
  if el.eek == 0 or el.eek == 8 or el.eek == 9 or el.eek == 10 or el.eek == 11 or el.eek == 12 { asns = 0; asnl = nel }
  ## §4 UP-GROWING: reserve nel*stride element FILLER words FIRST (below the base), then the base name
  ## at the TOP (see bind_struct_slot). Element `i` word `j` is then at slot `off - (i*stride + j)`;
  ## `agg_arr_fill_count` scans these anonymous fillers BACKWARD from the base.
  mut nw := nel * el.stride
  if el.eek == 12 { nw = el.stride }
  if is_byte_array_eek(el.eek) { nw = (nel + 7) / 8 }
  for i in 0..nw {
    fo := svec_len(ptr(slots))
    svec_push(slots, SlotEntry(ns = 0, nl = 0, off = fo, sns = 0, snl = 0, ek = 0, estride = 1, eek = 0, is_ref = false))
  }
  off := svec_len(ptr(slots))
  mut estr := el.stride
  if el.eek == 12 { estr = 1 }
  svec_push(slots, SlotEntry(ns = s, nl = n, off = off, sns = asns, snl = asnl, ek = 5, estride = estr, eek = el.eek, is_ref = false))
}


## The comptime element COUNT `N` of a FIXED array `[T; N]` named by `base` — a local that OWNS its
## storage (slot `ek == 5`, `is_ref == false`). Spec Types §6.4 ("`N` is a compile-time value") and
## Control-Flow §5.4 (the upper bound of `a[..]` is "a `[T; N]` array's OWN LENGTH" = the comptime
## `N`); §160 §2.4 arrays are iterable by that length. Returns -1 when `base` is NOT such a fixed
## array — a SLICE view/param (`ek == 5` + `is_ref`, a runtime {ptr,len}), a `str`, a struct field,
## or an unbound / non-Var base — so a slice/str `.len` keeps its runtime word read BYTE-IDENTICAL
## (the neutrality lever). `N` folds to a compile-time immediate (zero cost, I2): a scalar/float
## element array records `N` in the slot's `snl` (bind_array_slot), an aggregate/str/ptr element
## array recovers it as filler-words ÷ element-stride (`agg_arr_fill_count`).
fixed_arr_len := fn(base : ptr(Expr), slots : ptr(SVec), src : ptr(u8)) -> i64 {
  bv := var_name_span(base)
  if bv.n == 0 { return -1 }
  if slot_of(slots, src, bv.s, bv.n) < 0 { return -1 }
  si := entry_of(slots, src, bv.s, bv.n)
  ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, slots, si))
  if ent.ek != 5 { return -1 }
  if ent.is_ref { return -1 }
  if ent.eek == 0 or ent.eek == 8 or ent.eek == 9 or ent.eek == 10 or ent.eek == 11 { return i64(ent.snl) }
  if ent.estride == 0 { return -1 }
  i64(agg_arr_fill_count(slots, si) / ent.estride)
}

## Parse the element COUNT `N` from an array type-annotation span `[T; N]` (recovered by
## `local_type_span`) — scan for the `;` (byte 59), then read the trailing decimal number. 0 if the
## span holds no `; N` (not an array annotation). Bytes read directly from the source via a `ptr(u8)`.
parse_arr_len := fn(src : ptr(u8), ts : usize, tl : usize) -> usize {
  ## Read via `bytes(str)[i]` (a Slice(u8) byte view) — NOT `deref(bitcast(ptr(u8), ...))`, which the
  ## lean lower reads as a WORD (the bitcast target type is discarded), so `;` (byte 59) was missed.
  bs := bytes(str_at((src + ts), tl))
  mut i := 0
  mut sp := 0
  mut found := false
  while i < tl {
    if bs[i] == 59 { sp = i + 1; found = true }
    i += 1
  }
  if found == false { return 0 }
  mut j := sp
  mut n := 0
  mut any := false
  mut going := true
  while going and j < tl {
    b := bs[j]
    if b >= 48 and b <= 57 { n = n * 10 + usize(b - 48); any = true; j = j + 1 }
    else if b == 32 and any == false { j = j + 1 }
    else { going = false }
  }
  n
}

## The ELEMENT TYPE span of an array type `[T; N]` — the text between `[` and `;` (or `]`), trimmed of
## surrounding spaces. `[u64; 3]` → `u64`. 0/0 if the span isn't an array. Used to mangle an array
## type-arg (`Array_<T>_<N>`) and to bind the element type in `display`'s array unroll.
array_elem_span := fn(src : ptr(u8), ts : usize, tl : usize) -> CSpan {
  if tl < 2 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  if str_at((src + ts), 1) != "[" { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  mut es := ts + 1
  while es < ts + tl and str_at((src + es), 1) == " " { es = es + 1 }
  mut ee := es
  while ee < ts + tl and str_at((src + ee), 1) != ";" and str_at((src + ee), 1) != "]" { ee = ee + 1 }
  mut et := ee
  while et > es and str_at((src + et - 1), 1) == " " { et = et - 1 }
  CSpan(s = es, n = et - es)
}

## WORD WIDTH of a fixed-array TYPE span used as an enum payload. The enum layout module predates
## array-shaped payloads and intentionally keeps its generic enum sizer independent; the match-root
## path needs the concrete payload width here so an enum local owns the complete inline array block.
## Returns 0 when `ts..ts+tl` is not a fixed array or has no resolvable element width.
array_payload_words := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), ts : usize, tl : usize, a : rt::Arena) -> usize {
  ae := array_elem_span(src, ts, tl)
  if ae.n == 0 { return 0 }
  nel := parse_arr_len(src, ts, tl)
  mut stride := 1
  if struct_decl_of(decls, src, ae.s, ae.n) >= 0 {
    stride = struct_words(decls, src, ae.s, ae.n, a)
  } else if enum_decl_of(decls, src, ae.s, ae.n) >= 0 {
    stride = 1 + enum_inst_words(decls, src, ae.s, ae.n, a)
  } else if str_at((src + ae.s), ae.n) == "str" {
    stride = 2
  }
  if nel == 0 or stride == 0 { return 0 }
  nel * stride
}

## The concrete payload width of a non-generic enum TYPE. This is the lower-side companion to the
## layout module's legacy enum sizer: ordinary struct/enum/str payloads retain the existing answer,
## while a direct `[T; N]` payload contributes N * sizeof(T). Generic instances continue through the
## established `enum_inst_words` substitution path.
enum_type_payload_words := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize, a : rt::Arena) -> usize {
  ebn := base_type_name(src, s, n)
  di := enum_decl_of(decls, src, ebn.s, ebn.n)
  if di < 0 { return 0 }
  d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(di))))
  if d.is_generic { return enum_inst_words(decls, src, s, n, a) }
  mut f := d.fields_head
  mut mx := 0
  while f != 0 {
    fd := deref(fld_p(f))
    mut pw := fd.arity
    if fd.arity == 1 {
      apw := array_payload_words(decls, src, fd.ts, fd.tl, a)
      if apw != 0 { pw = apw }
      else if struct_decl_of(decls, src, fd.ts, fd.tl) >= 0 { pw = struct_words(decls, src, fd.ts, fd.tl, a) }
      else if enum_decl_of(decls, src, fd.ts, fd.tl) >= 0 { pw = 1 + enum_inst_words(decls, src, fd.ts, fd.tl, a) }
      else if str_at((src + fd.ts), fd.tl) == "str" { pw = 2 }
    }
    if pw > mx { mx = pw }
    f = fd.next
  }
  mx
}

## The concrete payload width of an enum literal's selected variant when that payload is an array.
## This is kept separate from `enum_type_payload_words`: the latter sizes a whole enum value, while
## this one lets constructor/return emitters recognize the one aggregate argument without guessing
## from the literal expression alone.
enum_lit_array_payload_words := fn(v : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> usize {
  ef := enum_lit_full(v)
  if ef.is_e == false or ef.np != 1 { return 0 }
  pty := variant_payload_type(decls, src, ef.es, ef.el, ef.vs, ef.vl, a)
  array_payload_words(decls, src, pty.s, pty.n, a)
}

## Bind a STR local: the base name takes slot `off` (ek = 4) and a second filler word (the
## len) occupies slot `off+1`, so a {ptr, len} pair lives at two contiguous frame slots.
## Re-assignment of an already-bound str local reuses its base slot (no new reservation).
bind_str_slot := fn(in out slots : SVec, src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize) {
  ## I11: reject a rebinding whose existing reservation is NARROWER than this one needs (see
  ## `require_reservation`) — the wide store would land on the neighbouring locals' slots.
  require_reservation(ptr(slots), src, s, n, 2)
  existing := slot_of(ptr(slots), src, s, n)
  if existing >= 0 { return }
  ## §4 UP-GROWING: the len FILLER word first (below the base), then the base (ptr) at the TOP — so
  ## word 0 = ptr = `ent.off` (lowest addr), word 1 = len = `ent.off - 1` (higher addr).
  fo := svec_len(ptr(slots))
  svec_push(slots, SlotEntry(ns = 0, nl = 0, off = fo, sns = 0, snl = 0, ek = 0, estride = 1, eek = 0, is_ref = false))
  off := svec_len(ptr(slots))
  svec_push(slots, SlotEntry(ns = s, nl = n, off = off, sns = 0, snl = 0, ek = 4, estride = 1, eek = 0, is_ref = false))
}

## typed-slice: if `v` is `Slice(<array-local Var>, lo, hi)` whose base is a frame-local
## SCALAR/FLOAT-element array (ek 5, eek 0/9), the element STRIDE (else 0). A raw-array slice is a
## TYPED view — distinct from a `str` byte-view — so it must index element-wise, not byte-wise.
slice_arr_stride := fn(v : ptr(Expr), slots : ptr(SVec), src : ptr(u8)) -> usize {
  match deref(v) {
    Expr::Slice(base, lo, hi) => {
      bv := var_name_span(base)
      if bv.n == 0 { return 0 }
      ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, slots, entry_of(slots, src, bv.s, bv.n)))
      ## scalar/float (eek 0/9), struct/enum (eek 2/3) AND str (eek 4) element arrays can be sliced as
      ## a TYPED array-slice VIEW (ek 5 is_ref, stride = element word count). A `[str; N]` local slice
      ## must take THIS path — the str-byte-view path (`emit_slice_pair`) would mis-read the 2-word str
      ## elements as a flat byte buffer. `s[i]` then reads element `lo+i`'s {ptr,len} stride-aware.
      if ent.ek == 5 and (ent.eek == 0 or is_byte_array_eek(ent.eek) or ent.eek == 9 or ent.eek == 2 or ent.eek == 3 or ent.eek == 4 or ent.eek == 7) { return ent.estride }
      0
    }
    _ => { 0 }
  }
}
## The base array's element KIND (`eek`) for a `Slice(<array-local>, lo, hi)` — so a float-element
## slice (eek 9) is read through the xmm path, a struct/enum element (eek 2/3) by reference. 0 if not
## an array-base slice.
slice_arr_eek := fn(v : ptr(Expr), slots : ptr(SVec), src : ptr(u8)) -> u8 {
  match deref(v) {
    Expr::Slice(base, lo, hi) => {
      bv := var_name_span(base)
      if bv.n == 0 { return 0 }
      ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, slots, entry_of(slots, src, bv.s, bv.n)))
      if ent.ek == 5 { return ent.eek }
      0
    }
    _ => { 0 }
  }
}
## The base array's ELEMENT TYPE span for a struct/enum-element `Slice(...)` — carried into the slice
## slot's `sns`/`snl` so `s[i].field` resolves the field through the element type. 0/0 for a
## scalar/float element (no nominal type; those use the snl==1 runtime-len marker instead).
slice_arr_espan := fn(v : ptr(Expr), slots : ptr(SVec), src : ptr(u8)) -> CSpan {
  match deref(v) {
    Expr::Slice(base, lo, hi) => {
      bv := var_name_span(base)
      if bv.n == 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
      ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, slots, entry_of(slots, src, bv.s, bv.n)))
      if ent.ek == 5 and (ent.eek == 2 or ent.eek == 3 or ent.eek == 7) { return CSpan(s = ent.sns, n = ent.snl) }
      CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
    }
    _ => { CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  }
}

## Recover the element type from a PARAMETER written `name : Slice(T)` without widening
## `SlotEntry`/`LCtx`. The parser stores generic parameter types as the bare head (`Slice`), so this
## source scan supplies the erased `T` where the lower needs aggregate stride/field metadata.
## Is the binding at name span `s`/`n` an ANNOTATED declaration (`name : T` — a PARAM, or any typed
## binding) rather than an inferred LOCAL (`name := <value>`)? A source-scan past the name: the next
## non-space byte is `:` AND the one after it is not `=`. Used to tell a struct/enum-element slice
## LOCAL (`s := arr[lo..hi]` — its slot words hold {data ptr, len} DIRECTLY) from a slice / array
## PARAM (whose slot holds a POINTER to the caller's block), which the slot entry alone cannot
## distinguish for an aggregate element (both carry the element TYPE span in `sns`/`snl`, so the
## scalar-element `snl == 1 && sns == 0` marker is unavailable). Same source-scan technique as
## `slice_param_elem_span` / `local_is_mut`.
slot_name_is_annotated := fn(src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize) -> bool {
  mut p := s + n
  end := p + 512
  mut c := str_at((src + p), 1)
  while p < end and (c == " " or c == "\n" or c == "\t" or c == "\r") { p = p + 1; c = str_at((src + p), 1) }
  if c != ":" { return false }
  return str_at((src + p + 1), 1) != "="
}

slice_param_elem_span := fn(src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize) -> CSpan {
  mut p := s + n
  end := p + 512
  mut c := str_at((src + p), 1)
  while p < end and (c == " " or c == "\n" or c == "\t" or c == "\r") { p = p + 1; c = str_at((src + p), 1) }
  if c != ":" { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  p = p + 1
  c = str_at((src + p), 1)
  while p < end and (c == " " or c == "\n" or c == "\t" or c == "\r") { p = p + 1; c = str_at((src + p), 1) }
  if str_at((src + p), 6) != "Slice(" {
    ## §7.2 slice-VARIADIC `name : ...T` — the element type follows `...`. Recover `T`'s span so a
    ## `...Pt` / `...E` param reads its struct/enum element exactly like a `Slice(Pt)` param (field
    ## resolution + the by-ref for-loop). A bare `...` (the §7.1 comptime variadic — no element type,
    ## `)` right after) yields 0/0, unchanged from before → fixpoint-neutral for the lib `args : ...`.
    if str_at((src + p), 3) == "..." {
      vs := p + 3
      mut ve := vs
      mut vc := str_at((src + ve), 1)
      while ve < end and vc != ")" and vc != "," and vc != " " and vc != "\n" and vc != "\t" and vc != "\r" { ve = ve + 1; vc = str_at((src + ve), 1) }
      if ve == vs { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
      return CSpan(s = vs, n = ve - vs)
    }
    return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
  }
  es := p + 6
  mut ee := es
  while ee < end and str_at((src + ee), 1) != ")" { ee = ee + 1 }
  if ee == end { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  if ee == es { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  CSpan(s = es, n = ee - es)
}

## Types §9.4 / §7.2 — the VIEW kind of a plain `Var` used as a VALUE: `4` = a `str`, `5` = a
## `Slice(T)`, `0` = anything else. A view is the 2-word `{ptr, len}` value `emit_str_pair`
## materializes; a `t := <view var>` copy must reserve BOTH destination words and store BOTH. Without
## this the copy fell to the generic SCALAR store: it kept word 0 only, so `t.len` read whatever the
## neighbouring slot held (0) and — for a str/slice PARAM, whose slot holds a POINTER to the caller's
## pair rather than the pair itself — even the DATA POINTER was wrong. Both SILENT.
##
## An ARRAY param `a : [T; N]` is `ek 5` + `is_ref` too but is NOT a view (its slot points at raw
## element data; there is no len word), so it must be excluded. A scalar/float/str-element slice is
## told apart by the `snl == 1` runtime-len marker (an array param records `snl == 0`); a
## struct/enum/ptr-element slice spends `sns`/`snl` on the element TYPE span instead, so it is told
## apart by being either an INFERRED local (`s := arr[lo..hi]`, unannotated) or an annotation that
## source-scans to a real `Slice(…)` / `...T` — the same discrimination `emit_str_pair` already uses
## on the read side, so a Var this reports as a view is exactly a Var it can emit as a pair.
view_var_kind := fn(v : ptr(Expr), slots : ptr(SVec), src : ptr(u8)) -> u8 {
  vn := var_name_span(v)
  if vn.n == 0 { return 0 }
  ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, slots, entry_of(slots, src, vn.s, vn.n)))
  if streq(src, ent.ns, ent.nl, vn.s, vn.n) == false { return 0 }
  if ent.ek == 4 { return 4 }
  if ent.ek == 5 and ent.is_ref {
    if ent.eek == 2 or ent.eek == 3 or ent.eek == 7 {
      if slot_name_is_annotated(src, ent.ns, ent.nl) == false { return 5 }
      if slice_param_elem_span(src, ent.ns, ent.nl).n != 0 { return 5 }
    } else if ent.snl == 1 { return 5 }
  }
  0
}

## Types §9.4 — is the binding `<ns,nl>` a DIRECT 2-word view LOCAL, i.e. a slot whose two frame words
## hold the `{ptr, len}` pair themselves (`emit_pair_field_store`'s destination shape: ptr at word 0,
## len at word 1)? A `str` LOCAL (`ek 4`, not by-ref) or a slice VIEW local (`ek 5`, the direct-read
## shape `emit_str_pair`'s Var arm recognizes). A str/slice PARAM (`is_ref`, its slot holds a POINTER
## to the caller's pair) is NOT — writing the pair into its frame words would clobber the pointer —
## and neither is an array local/param. Gates the view-copy store, so a destination that is anything
## else keeps its existing (unchanged) lowering.
view_dest_is_local := fn(slots : ptr(SVec), src : ptr(u8), ns : usize, nl : usize) -> bool {
  ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, slots, entry_of(slots, src, ns, nl)))
  if streq(src, ent.ns, ent.nl, ns, nl) == false { return false }
  if ent.ek == 4 { return ent.is_ref == false }
  if ent.ek == 5 and ent.is_ref {
    if ent.snl == 1 and ent.sns == 0 { return true }
    if ent.eek == 2 or ent.eek == 3 or ent.eek == 7 {
      if slot_name_is_annotated(src, ent.ns, ent.nl) == false { return true }
    }
  }
  false
}

## Bind a TYPED ARRAY-SLICE view `s := arr[lo..hi]` (§4 typed-slice): a pointer-backed array (ek 5,
## `is_ref` — word 0 holds the data pointer, so `s[i]` indexes through it stride-aware via
## `emit_index_addr`) plus a runtime LEN word (word 1, read by `.len`). `snl = 1` marks "a slice with
## a runtime len word" — distinguishing it from a by-ref array PARAM (whose word 1 is another local),
## so `.len` reads word 1 only for a real slice.
bind_slice_slot := fn(in out slots : SVec, src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize, stride : usize, eek : u8, ess : usize, esl : usize) {
  ## I11: reject a rebinding whose existing reservation is NARROWER than this one needs (see
  ## `require_reservation`) — the wide store would land on the neighbouring locals' slots.
  require_reservation(ptr(slots), src, s, n, 2)
  existing := slot_of(ptr(slots), src, s, n)
  if existing >= 0 { return }
  ## A SCALAR/FLOAT-element slice (no nominal element type) marks its runtime len with `snl == 1` (so
  ## `.len` / emit_str_pair read the len word directly). A STRUCT/ENUM-element slice instead carries
  ## the element TYPE span in `sns`/`snl` so `s[i].field` resolves; its `.len`/str-context is not
  ## exercised (structs aren't strings) so the marker isn't needed there.
  mut sns := 0
  mut snl := 1
  ## eek 2/3 (struct/enum element) AND eek 7 (POINTER-to-struct element) carry the element TYPE span in
  ## `sns`/`snl` (for eek 7 that is the POINTEE struct span) so `s[i].field` / `deref(s[i]).field`
  ## resolves; a scalar/float slice keeps the `snl == 1` runtime-len marker.
  if eek == 2 or eek == 3 or eek == 7 { sns = ess; snl = esl }
  ## §4 UP-GROWING: the runtime-len FILLER word first (below the base), then the base (data ptr) at the
  ## TOP — word 0 = ptr = `ent.off`, word 1 = len = `ent.off - 1`.
  fo := svec_len(ptr(slots))
  svec_push(slots, SlotEntry(ns = 0, nl = 0, off = fo, sns = 0, snl = 0, ek = 0, estride = 1, eek = 0, is_ref = false))
  off := svec_len(ptr(slots))
  svec_push(slots, SlotEntry(ns = s, nl = n, off = off, sns = sns, snl = snl, ek = 5, estride = stride, eek = eek, is_ref = true))
}

## Bind a POINTER-TO-STRUCT local (`ek = 7`): a single word holding the pointer, carrying the
## POINTEE struct's type span (`ss`/`sl`) so a later `s := deref(p)` knows what to copy. Bound by
## a `p := ptr(<struct local>)` binding (the dual of `ek = 6`, a pointer-to-enum param).
bind_ptrstruct_slot := fn(in out slots : SVec, src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize, ss : usize, sl : usize) {
  existing := slot_of(ptr(slots), src, s, n)
  if existing >= 0 { return }
  off := svec_len(ptr(slots))
  svec_push(slots, SlotEntry(ns = s, nl = n, off = off, sns = ss, snl = sl, ek = 7, estride = 1, eek = 0, is_ref = false))
}

## Emit code pushing a string operand's {ptr, len} pair onto the stack — `ptr` first (deeper),
## then `len` (on top). The operand `e` is either a `StrLit` (ptr = `leaq .Lstr<idx>(%rip)`,
## len = the immediate byte count) or a `str` `Var` local (ptr = its slot word, len = the next
## word). The deref-`match` on `e` stays here (a direct PARAM) — the lowerable shape. A
## non-str operand pushes two zero words (not exercised by the supported grammar). A `Call` to the
## `sub(s, start, len)` view builtin is materialized by `emit_sub_pair` (recursively pushing the
## source str's pair, then `ptr += start`, `len := len`). `a`/`nl` are threaded so `sub`'s index/len
## arg EXPRESSIONS can be lowered.
## `<f>.name` where `f` is the ACTIVE comptime-for loop var (`cf_var`), currently bound to the
## member `cf_fld` — the field NAME as a compile-time `str`. `display`'s aggregate arm renders
## `{ <name> = value, … }` via `push_str(sb, f.name)`. True when `e` is `Field(Var(cf_var), "name")`.
is_cf_name_ref := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> bool {
  if cx.cf_var_l == 0 { return false }
  mut res := false
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Field(base, fs, fl) => {
      if str_at((cx.src + fs), fl) == "name" {
        vn := var_name_span(base)
        if vn.n != 0 and streq(cx.src, vn.s, vn.n, cx.cf_var_s, cx.cf_var_l) { res = true }
      }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  res
}

## The rodata name span (source offset + len) that a `<var>.name` comptime read resolves to: a
## struct-field name (`cf_fld_*`, when the base is the field comptime-for var `cf_var`) or a VARIANT
## name (`cf_curvar_*`, when the base is the variant comptime-for var `cf_vloop`, inside a variant
## arm body — `display`'s enum arm). {0,0} when `e` is not such a read. Both resolve to `.Lfld<offset>`
## in .rodata; source offsets are unique per occurrence, so field + variant names share the label
## scheme without collision. Purely a cx-aware refinement of `is_cf_name_ref`.
cf_name_span := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> CSpan {
  mut rs := 0
  mut rn := 0
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Field(base, fs, fl) => {
      if str_at((cx.src + fs), fl) == "name" {
        vn := var_name_span(base)
        if vn.n != 0 {
          if cx.cf_var_l != 0 and streq(cx.src, vn.s, vn.n, cx.cf_var_s, cx.cf_var_l) { rs = cx.cf_fld_s; rn = cx.cf_fld_l }
          else if cx.cf_vloop_l != 0 and cx.cf_curvar_l != 0 and streq(cx.src, vn.s, vn.n, cx.cf_vloop_s, cx.cf_vloop_l) { rs = cx.cf_curvar_s; rn = cx.cf_curvar_l }
        }
      }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  CSpan(s = rs, n = rn)
}

## The mutability bit of `<f>.mutable` for the ACTIVE comptime field descriptor (Comptime §5.1). Field
## mutability is a source-level marker, so recover it from the current field name exactly as the parser
## recovers local mutability. -1 means this is not an active mutable query.
cf_mutable_value := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> i64 {
  if cx.cf_var_l == 0 { return 0 - 1 }
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Field(base, fs, fl) => {
      if str_at((cx.src + fs), fl) != "mutable" { return 0 - 1 }
      vn := var_name_span(base)
      if vn.n == 0 or not streq(cx.src, vn.s, vn.n, cx.cf_var_s, cx.cf_var_l) { return 0 - 1 }
      if local_is_mut(cx.src, cx.cf_fld_s) { return 1 }
      return 0
    }
    _ => { return 0 - 1 }
  }
}

## Reject an unknown member on the ACTIVE comptime `Field` descriptor. The descriptor schema is closed
## by CT-6: `name`, `type`, `offset`, and `mutable`. If this reaches ordinary runtime field lowering,
## the erased loop variable has no slot and the old fallback silently emitted zero — a direct I11 breach.
cf_unknown_member := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> bool {
  if cx.cf_var_l == 0 { return false }
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Field(base, fs, fl) => {
      vn := var_name_span(base)
      if vn.n == 0 or not streq(cx.src, vn.s, vn.n, cx.cf_var_s, cx.cf_var_l) { return false }
      nm := str_at((cx.src + fs), fl)
      not (nm == "name" or nm == "type" or nm == "offset" or nm == "mutable")
    }
    _ => { false }
  }
}

## The byte OFFSET of `<f>.offset` for the ACTIVE comptime field descriptor (Comptime §5.1). The
## descriptor is erased: use the same layout calculators as the value paths, selecting the packed,
## standard byte-array, or ordinary word-granular representation. `compfor_target_type` resolves the
## header's own `typeinfo(X)` argument through the enclosing generic instance, so a field derive over a
## second type parameter cannot accidentally use `cx.it`. -1 means this is not an active offset read.
cf_offset_value := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena) -> i64 {
  if cx.cf_var_l == 0 { return 0 - 1 }
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Field(base, fs, fl) => {
      if str_at((cx.src + fs), fl) != "offset" { return 0 - 1 }
      vn := var_name_span(base)
      if vn.n == 0 or not streq(cx.src, vn.s, vn.n, cx.cf_var_s, cx.cf_var_l) { return 0 - 1 }
      ct := compfor_target_type(cx, cx.cf_var_s, cx.cf_var_l)
      if ct.n == 0 { return 0 - 1 }
      ## THE ORACLE (`lower_layout::layout_kind`) picks the tier — one decision shared with the
      ## a64/rv64/wat duals and with `size`/`align`/`struct_words`/`iface`, so they cannot drift.
      lk := layout_kind(cx.decls, cx.src, ct.s, ct.n, a)
      if layout_kind_is_packed(lk) {
        return packed_field_byte_offset(cx.decls, cx.src, ct.s, ct.n, cx.cf_fld_s, cx.cf_fld_l, a)
      }
      if layout_kind_is_byte(lk) {
        return standard_field_byte_offset(cx.decls, cx.src, ct.s, ct.n, cx.cf_fld_s, cx.cf_fld_l, a)
      }
      fwo := field_word_offset(cx.decls, cx.src, ct.s, ct.n, cx.cf_fld_s, cx.cf_fld_l, a)
      if fwo >= 0 { return fwo * 8 }
      return 0 - 1
    }
    _ => { return 0 - 1 }
  }
}

## Is `e` a `<var>.payload` read on the comptime-for-variant loop var (`cf_vloop`)? `display`'s enum
## arm folds `comptime match var.payload { Some(_) => …, None => … }` on the CURRENT variant's arity;
## this recognises its scrutinee. False outside a variant unroll (`cf_vloop_l == 0`).
is_cf_payload_ref := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> bool {
  if cx.cf_vloop_l == 0 { return false }
  mut res := false
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Field(base, fs, fl) => {
      if str_at((cx.src + fs), fl) == "payload" {
        vn := var_name_span(base)
        if vn.n != 0 and streq(cx.src, vn.s, vn.n, cx.cf_vloop_s, cx.cf_vloop_l) { res = true }
      }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  res
}

## Is `e` a `typeinfo(p)` where `p` is the CURRENT enum-arm payload binding (`cf_pay`)? `display`'s
## enum arm dispatches `comptime match typeinfo(p)` to bracket a multi-component (tuple) payload
## differently from a scalar one. When true the fold keys the kind off `cf_pay_ty` (the payload's
## type), not the instance type `cx.it`.
is_cf_payload_typeinfo := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena) -> bool {
  if cx.cf_pay_l == 0 { return false }
  mut res := false
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, na, ah) => {
      if str_at((cx.src + cs), cl) == "typeinfo" {
        vn := var_name_span(arg_expr_at(ah, 0, a))
        if vn.n != 0 and streq(cx.src, vn.s, vn.n, cx.cf_pay_s, cx.cf_pay_l) { res = true }
      }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  res
}

## Is `e` a call through an EXPRESSION CALLEE (FN-6 — argument 0 IS the callee; see `ast::ecallee_mark`)?
## Its callee NAME SPAN is BORROWED from the callee chain's root place, so every name-keyed classifier
## answers about that place instead of about the call. Wherever such an answer would pick a non-scalar
## delivery convention, the node has to be caught by this probe first.
is_ecallee_call := fn(e : ptr(Expr)) -> bool {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, na, ah) => { ecallee_is(cs) }
    _ => { false }
  }
}

## Types §7 — is `e` a two-word `{ptr, len}` VIEW VALUE with no frame home, i.e. a view that
## `emit_str_pair` can materialize but that is NOT a place with its own read arms? A str LITERAL, the
## `sub(…)` / `str_at(…)` / `bytes(…)` view builtins, and a range sub-view. A `Var` / FIELD / INDEX
## base is deliberately excluded: those are PLACES whose `.len`/`.ptr` reads already work, and routing
## them here would change emission for the whole self-host build.
view_pair_base := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> bool {
  if str_lit_info(e).is_s { return true }
  if is_sub_call(e, cx.src) { return true }
  if is_strat_call(e, cx.src) { return true }
  if is_bytes_call(e, cx.src) { return true }
  is_slice_expr(e)
}

emit_str_pair := fn(e : ptr(Expr), in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena, in out nl : usize) {
  ## FN-6 — a call through an EXPRESSION CALLEE cannot deliver a `str` here. The str-return convention
  ## (ptr/%rax + len/%rdx) is selected by `str_ret_call`, which resolves the callee by NAME — this node
  ## borrows the root place's name, so a genuinely `str`-returning fn value answers FALSE and falls to
  ## the `_` default below, pushing an EMPTY pair: a silent wrong value. `emit_ecallee_call` rejects a
  ## non-scalar return class, but `len`/`bytes`/`sub`/`str_eq`/`print`/`return <str>` reach their operand
  ## THROUGH here without ever entering it, so the same reject has to stand at this door too.
  if is_ecallee_call(e) { panic("selfhost: FN-6 - a call through an expression callee cannot produce a `str` operand; bind the callee to a name first (`g := <callee>` then `g(args)`)") }
  ## `unchecked` and `bitcast` are transparent for a view value. `str_at` is implemented in the
  ## standard library as `unchecked bitcast(str, slice)`, so rejecting these wrappers would turn a
  ## supported two-word view into a false positive. Re-enter the same pair emitter so the inner local,
  ## call, or literal still goes through its ordinary materialization arm; an inner scalar continues
  ## to fail at the default below rather than being guessed as a pair.
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Unchecked(inner) => { emit_str_pair(inner, sb, cx, a, nl); return }
    Expr::Bitcast(inner, bs, bn) => { emit_str_pair(inner, sb, cx, a, nl); return }
    _ => {}
  }
  ## `f.name`/`var.name` — the comptime field/variant NAME (a compile-time `str`): its bytes live at
  ## `.Lfld<offset>` (emitted in .rodata, keyed on the field/variant decl's name offset). Push the pair.
  cns := cf_name_span(e, cx)
  if cns.n != 0 {
    push_str(sb, "  leaq ")
    push_lfld(sb, cns.s)
    push_str(sb, "(%rip), %rax\n  pushq %rax\n  movq $")
    push_int(sb, i64(cns.n))
    push_str(sb, ", %rax\n  pushq %rax\n")
    return
  }
  ## `bytes(x)` — the str→`[u8]` view (identical `{ptr, len}` repr). Emit `x`'s pair directly.
  mut bvp := bytes_view_arg(e, cx.src, a)
  if unchecked bitcast(usize, bvp) != 0 {
    ## A bytes-view source may be an immutable module constant. Resolve only at the builtin boundary
    ## so ordinary `str` operations keep their established behavior while immediate/bound/initializer
    ## `bytes(global)` all reuse this pair emitter.
    bvn := var_name_span(bvp)
    if bvn.n != 0 {
      bcm := module_const_value(cx.decls, cx.src, bvn.s, bvn.n)
      if unchecked bitcast(usize, bcm) != 0 and (str_lit_info(bcm).is_s or is_bytes_call(bcm, cx.src)) {
        bvp = bcm
      }
    }
    emit_str_pair(bvp, sb, cx, a, nl)
    return
  }
  if is_strat_call(e, cx.src) {
    emit_strat_pair(e, sb, cx, a, nl)
    return
  }
  if is_str_call(e, cx) {
    emit_sub_pair(e, sb, cx, a, nl)
    return
  }
  ## a str-returning CALL `f(…)` (e.g. `return read_cmdline(a)` / `return read_proc(…)`): the call
  ## leaves ptr/%rax, len/%rdx (the str-return convention — `emit_struct_value`'s Call arm emits it).
  ## Push them as the pair (ptr deeper, len top), like the `StrLit`/`Var` cases. Without this a str
  ## call fell to the `_` default (`pushq $0` twice) → an EMPTY str, so any `return <str-call>` (or a
  ## str-call as a `str_eq` operand) yielded a 0-length string — the §1.0 CLI argv-read returned 0.
  if str_ret_call(e, cx.decls, cx.src, deref(cx.mar)) or generic_inferred_str_ret(e, cx, a) {
    emit_struct_value(e, sb, cx, a)
    push_str(sb, "  pushq %rax\n  pushq %rdx\n")
    return
  }
  ## a RANGE-SLICE `base[lo..hi]` (`Expr::Slice`) is a str sub-view — emit its {ptr, len} pair.
  if is_slice_expr(e) {
    emit_slice_pair(e, sb, cx, a, nl)
    return
  }
  ## A module-level CONST struct has no `.data` address. Fold a direct or nested field to its literal
  ## value, then let the existing literal arm emit the pair. Mutable globals take the `.data` path below;
  ## locals/PARAMs take `str_field_place`, so this branch is restricted to the const-value resolver.
  cfp := field_place_parts(e)
  if unchecked bitcast(usize, cfp.base) != 0 {
    cfv := const_field_value(e, cx.decls, cx.src, a)
    if unchecked bitcast(usize, cfv) != 0 {
      cstr := const_rhs(cfv, cx.decls, cx.src)
      if str_lit_info(cstr).is_s {
        emit_str_pair(cstr, sb, cx, a, nl)
        return
      }
    }
  }
  ## `xs[i].key` — a str FIELD of an ARRAY-of-STRUCT element: the {ptr, len} sit at the element field
  ## address (`emit_idx_field_addr`) and the next word down. Push ptr (deeper), len (top).
  sae := str_field_arr_elem(e, cx, a)
  if sae.found {
    emit_idx_field_addr(sae.arr, sae.idx, sae.woff * 8, sb, cx, nl)   ## str field addr → %rax
    push_str(sb, "  movq (%rax), %rbx\n  pushq %rbx\n  movq 8(%rax), %rbx\n  pushq %rbx\n")
    return
  }
  ## Types §9.4 — `g.name`, a `str` FIELD of a struct used as a str VALUE (a `str_eq` operand, a
  ## `sub`/`bytes` source, a call ARGUMENT). It matched none of the arms below (the base is a `Field`,
  ## not a `Var`) and fell to the `_` default: `pushq $0` twice = an EMPTY string, so `str_eq(g.name,
  ## "…")` was always false and every str-field value flowed as "" — a SILENT MISCOMPILE. Push the
  ## field's {ptr, len} with the SAME slot math the `.ptr`/`.len` field read uses (`str_field_place`):
  ## an INLINE struct local reads its two frame words directly, a BY-REFERENCE struct param loads the
  ## caller's word-0 pointer and reads the field's words at the ASCENDING pointee offsets.
  sfpv := str_field_place(e, cx, a)
  if sfpv.found {
    if sfpv.is_ref {
      push_str(sb, "  movq -")
      push_int(sb, i64((sfpv.off + 1) * 8))
      push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rax\n  movq ")
      push_int(sb, i64(sfpv.fldoff * 8))
      push_str(sb, "(%rax), %rbx\n  pushq %rbx\n  movq ")
      push_int(sb, i64((sfpv.fldoff + 1) * 8))
      push_str(sb, "(%rax), %rbx\n  pushq %rbx\n")
    } else {
      push_str(sb, "  movq -")
      push_int(sb, i64((sfpv.off + 1) * 8))
      push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rax\n  pushq %rax\n  movq -")
      push_int(sb, i64(sfpv.off * 8))
      push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rax\n  pushq %rax\n")
    }
    return
  }
  ## A mutable GLOBAL struct has no frame slot. Reuse the global-place walk used by scalar nested
  ## field reads, but deliver the final `str` as its two ascending `.data` words. This covers both the
  ## direct `G.name` issue shape and a deeper `G.q.name` chain.
  gsp := global_place(e, cx, a)
  if gsp.found and gsp.tl != 0 and str_at((cx.src + gsp.ts), gsp.tl) == "str" {
    push_str(sb, "  movq ")
    emit_global_label(sb, cx.decls, cx.src, gsp.gs, gsp.gn)
    push_str(sb, "+")
    push_int(sb, gsp.off * 8)
    push_str(sb, "(%rip), %rax\n  pushq %rax\n  movq ")
    emit_global_label(sb, cx.decls, cx.src, gsp.gs, gsp.gn)
    push_str(sb, "+")
    push_int(sb, (gsp.off + 1) * 8)
    push_str(sb, "(%rip), %rax\n  pushq %rax\n")
    return
  }
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::StrLit(ss, sl, lbl) => {
      push_str(sb, "  leaq ")
      push_lstr(sb, lbl)
      push_str(sb, "(%rip), %rax\n  pushq %rax\n  movq $")
      push_int(sb, i64(sl))
      push_str(sb, ", %rax\n  pushq %rax\n")
    }
    Expr::Var(s, n) => {
      gmv := mut_global_value(cx.decls, cx.src, s, n)
      if unchecked bitcast(usize, gmv) != 0 and str_lit_info(gmv).is_s {
        ## a str MUTABLE GLOBAL (`mut S := "hi"`) — NO frame slot; its {ptr, len} live in `.data` at
        ## `LABEL+0` (word 0 = the rodata-bytes ptr) and `LABEL+8` (word 1 = len). Load both from the
        ## label and push as the {ptr, len} pair (ptr deeper, len on top). Was ABSENT → this fell to the
        ## str-LOCAL `else` below, reading garbage frame slots (`.len` → 0, `bytes(S)[i]` → segfault).
        push_str(sb, "  movq ")
        emit_global_label(sb, cx.decls, cx.src, s, n)
        push_str(sb, "(%rip), %rax\n  pushq %rax\n  movq ")
        emit_global_label(sb, cx.decls, cx.src, s, n)
        push_str(sb, "+8(%rip), %rax\n  pushq %rax\n")
        return
      }
      ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, s, n)))
      ## A typed byte fixed-array is also a valid `bytes(x)` source. Its storage is a packed byte
      ## run, not the two-word `str` view that the generic LOCAL branch below reads. Materialize the
      ## same {ptr, len} view that `emit_index_addr` uses: a local's first filler byte is at `off`,
      ## while a fixed-array PARAM's slot carries the caller's data pointer. `sns` records N for the
      ## by-ref fixed-array ABI; `snl` records N for a packed local. Keep this ahead of the generic
      ## `is_ref`/LOCAL arms so `bytes([u8; N])` cannot dereference frame metadata as a pointer/len pair.
      if ent.ek == 5 and is_byte_array_eek(ent.eek) {
        if ent.is_ref {
          push_str(sb, "  movq -")
          push_int(sb, i64((ent.off + 1) * 8))
          push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rax\n  pushq %rax\n  movq $")
          push_int(sb, i64(ent.sns))
          push_str(sb, ", %rax\n  pushq %rax\n")
        } else {
          push_str(sb, "  leaq -")
          push_int(sb, i64(ent.off * 8))
          push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rax\n  pushq %rax\n  movq $")
          push_int(sb, i64(ent.snl))
          push_str(sb, ", %rax\n  pushq %rax\n")
        }
        return
      }
      ## A STRUCT/ENUM-element slice LOCAL (`s := arr[lo..hi]` over `[Pt; N]`, eek 2/3) stores its
      ## {data ptr, len} in its two words exactly like a scalar-element slice view, but carries the
      ## element TYPE span in `sns`/`snl` instead of the `snl == 1` runtime-len marker — so it must take
      ## the DIRECT two-word read too. Without it `s.len()` double-deref'd and returned `8(data_ptr)` =
      ## the first element's SECOND word (a silent WRONG length). A slice/array PARAM (whose slot holds a
      ## POINTER to the caller's {ptr,len} block) is told apart by its `name : T` ANNOTATION — the slot
      ## entry alone cannot distinguish the two — and keeps the by-ref branch below (byte-identical).
      slice_view_local := ent.ek == 5 and ent.is_ref and (ent.eek == 2 or ent.eek == 3) and slot_name_is_annotated(cx.src, ent.ns, ent.nl) == false
      if (ent.ek == 5 and ent.is_ref and ent.snl == 1 and ent.sns == 0) or slice_view_local {
        ## a TYPED ARRAY-SLICE local VIEW (§4, `sns == 0`): word 0 = the data pointer, word 1 = the runtime
        ## len — both stored directly in frame slots (the slice's len is materialized, not derived). Emit
        ## them as the {ptr, len} pair so `s.len` (which reads the len word) works. `s[i]` → emit_index_addr.
        ## A slice PARAM (`sns == 1`, a POINTER to the caller's {ptr,len} block) is NOT this — it falls to the
        ## by-ref branch below, which derefs the slot (ptr @ `(%rax)`, len @ `8(%rax)`) exactly as needed.
        push_str(sb, "  movq -")
        push_int(sb, (ent.off + 1) * 8)
        push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rax\n  pushq %rax\n  movq -")
        push_int(sb, ent.off * 8)
        push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rax\n  pushq %rax\n")
      } else if ent.is_ref {
        ## a str BY-REF PARAM: the slot holds a POINTER to the caller's str word 0 (its ptr
        ## word). Load that pointer, then deref: ptr = `(%rax)`, len = `-8(%rax)` (the
        ## down-growing {ptr, len} layout — len lives one word past the ptr, at a LOWER addr).
        push_str(sb, "  movq -")
        push_int(sb, (ent.off + 1) * 8)
        push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rax\n  movq (%rax), %rbx\n  pushq %rbx\n  movq 8(%rax), %rbx\n  pushq %rbx\n")
      } else {
        ## a str LOCAL: ptr word (slot off), then len word (slot off+1), read directly.
        push_str(sb, "  movq -")
        push_int(sb, (ent.off + 1) * 8)
        push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rax\n  pushq %rax\n  movq -")
        push_int(sb, ent.off * 8)
        push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rax\n  pushq %rax\n")
      }
    }
    ## `deref(p)` where `p` points at a §7 VIEW — the pointee IS the two-word `{ptr, len}` pair, so
    ## read BOTH words at the ASCENDING pointee offsets (`(%rax)` / `8(%rax)`), exactly the layout the
    ## by-ref str PARAM arm above and the str-element ARRAY arm below use. WITHOUT this arm a
    ## `deref(<pointer to a str>)` fell to the `_` default and pushed an EMPTY pair, so a generic
    ## container's element read (`alloc::vec::at` at `T = str`) silently returned a 0-length string
    ## with a null pointer — a wrong value, not a trap.
    Expr::Deref(dvp) => {
      emit_gas(dvp, sb, cx, a, nl)
      push_str(sb, "  popq %rax\n  movq (%rax), %rbx\n  pushq %rbx\n  movq 8(%rax), %rbx\n  pushq %rbx\n")
    }
    Expr::Index(ibase, iidx) => {
      ## `xs[i]` on a str-ELEMENT array (`[str; N]`, eek 4, estride 2) or a `Slice(str)` VALUE:
      ## `emit_index_addr` computes element i's word-0 (ptr) address stride-aware; the len is one word
      ## past it in the arena's UPWARD layout. Push ptr (deeper) then len (top), the str-pair shape.
      ## A non-str array index is not a str value (falls to the empty-pair default).
      if call_arg_is_str_elem(e, cx) {
        emit_index_addr(ibase, iidx, sb, cx, a, nl)
        push_str(sb, "  movq (%rax), %rbx\n  pushq %rbx\n  movq 8(%rax), %rbx\n  pushq %rbx\n")
      } else {
        push_str(sb, "  pushq $0\n  pushq $0\n")
      }
    }
    ## The default is still reached by the implicit (dead) TAIL of every `-> str` fn whose body ends
    ## in an explicit `return`: the parser's `Num(-1)` sentinel reaches the trailing return emitter,
    ## which asks for a pair it will never use. Preserve that dead path, but never turn a LIVE view
    ## operand into an empty string. Types §7 says the view is the two-word value, and I11 permits a
    ## deterministic reject where this lowerer cannot materialize it — a silent zero is neither.
    _ => {
      if not expr_is_no_tail(e) {
        off := comptime_cond_src_off(e)
        if off != 0 { lower_show_src_line(cx.src, off) }
        panic("selfhost: live str/view expression has no lowering path; bind it to a local first or add a supported view-place arm")
      }
      push_str(sb, "  pushq $0\n  pushq $0\n")
    }
  }
}

## Emit the {ptr, len} pair for a substring VIEW `sub(s, start, len)` — a str expression result, in
## the SAME stack shape `emit_str_pair` produces (ptr deeper, len on top), so a `sub` view plugs in
## anywhere a str expression flows (`str_eq` operand, a `name := sub(…)` binding). Lowering:
##   (1) push the SOURCE str's pair via `emit_str_pair` (arg 0 — a `StrLit`/`Var`/nested `sub`);
##   (2) pop its len (discard) and its ptr into %rax;
##   (3) lower the `start` expr (arg 1) → pop into %rbx → `addq %rbx, %rax` (the view's base ptr);
##       push it (deeper word);
##   (4) lower the `len` expr (arg 2) → it is already on the stack (top word) — the view's len.
## So the final stack is [base+start (deep), len (top)] = the view pair. NO bounds check (the toy
## grammar is `unchecked`-style, like `byte_at`). The deref-`match` reading the args stays in
## `arg_expr_at`. This MIRRORS real Alatyr's `str_at(base, len)` raw-bytes→str view, generalized to
## an offset+length over an existing str so keyword classification reads `str_eq(sub(src, ts, tl), "fn")`.
emit_sub_pair := fn(e : ptr(Expr), in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena, in out nl : usize) {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, args_head) => {
      a0 := arg_expr_at(args_head, 0, a)   ## the source str
      a1 := arg_expr_at(args_head, 1, a)   ## start offset (bytes)
      a2 := arg_expr_at(args_head, 2, a)   ## view length (bytes)
      emit_str_pair(a0, sb, cx, a, nl)     ## stack: [sptr (deep), slen (top)]
      push_str(sb, "  popq %rbx\n")   ## discard slen; stack: [sptr (top)] (KEPT on stack —
      ## lowering the `start` expr below may clobber registers, so sptr must live on the stack)
      emit_gas(a1, sb, cx, a, nl)          ## stack: [sptr, start (top)]
      ## base = sptr + start: pop start → %rbx, pop sptr → %rax, add, push the base (deep word)
      push_str(sb, "  popq %rbx\n  popq %rax\n  addq %rbx, %rax\n  pushq %rax\n")
      emit_gas(a2, sb, cx, a, nl)          ## len on the stack (top) — the view pair is complete
    }
    _ => { push_str(sb, "  pushq $0\n  pushq $0\n") }
  }
}

## Emit the two-word store for a substring binding `name := sub(s, start, len)` — the view's
## {ptr, len} pair (materialized by `emit_sub_pair`) stored into the str local's reserved slots:
## ptr at slot `base`, len at slot `base+1` (the same layout `emit_str_assign` uses for a literal).
emit_sub_assign := fn(v : ptr(Expr), base : i64, in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena, in out nl : usize) {
  emit_sub_pair(v, sb, cx, a, nl)   ## stack: [ptr (deep), len (top)]
  ## pop len (top) → slot base+1, then ptr → slot base
  push_str(sb, "  popq %rax\n  movq %rax, -")
  push_int(sb, base * 8)
  push_str(sb, "(%rbp)\n  popq %rax\n  movq %rax, -")
  push_int(sb, (base + 1) * 8)
  push_str(sb, "(%rbp)\n")
}

## Is `e` a RANGE-SLICE expression `base[lo..hi]` (parsed as `Expr::Slice`)? A str sub-VIEW, a
## 2-word str value — sized + lowered like a `sub(…)` call wherever a str expression flows.
is_slice_expr := fn(e : ptr(Expr)) -> bool {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Slice(b, lo, hi) => { true }
    _ => { false }
  }
}

## Emit the {ptr, len} pair for a range slice `base[lo..hi]` — the SAME stack shape `emit_sub_pair`
## produces (ptr deeper, len on top). Semantically `sub(base, lo, hi - lo)`:
##   (1) push the SOURCE str's pair (`emit_str_pair` on `base` — a str `Var`/`StrLit`/nested view);
##   (2) discard its len, add `lo` to its ptr → the view's base ptr (kept on the stack, deep word);
##   (3) push `hi - lo` (evaluate `hi`, then `lo`, subtract) → the view's len (top word).
## The bounds are re-evaluated where needed (they are pure in every use — vars / `s.len` / literals),
## mirroring the desugar `sub(base, lo, hi - lo)`. NO bounds check (the `unchecked` toy grammar).
emit_slice_pair := fn(e : ptr(Expr), in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena, in out nl : usize) {
  ## Types §9.4 — a range slice whose base is a raw ARRAY local (`xs : [T; N]`, `xs[lo..hi]`) is a
  ## TYPED array-slice VIEW, not a str BYTE view: its data pointer is element 0's ADDRESS advanced by
  ## `lo * stride * 8`, whereas the str path below reads the base's word-0 VALUE and adds `lo` bytes.
  ## The BINDING path already dispatches on exactly this test (`emit_array_slice_assign` vs
  ## `emit_slice_assign`); the PAIR path did not, so every non-binding use of an array slice — a
  ## `Slice(T)` struct-FIELD store (`S(v = xs[0..3])`), a by-value `-> Slice(T)` return — took the str
  ## reading and stored `xs`'s first ELEMENT as the "pointer": `st.v[i]` then read from address 10, and
  ## `.len` (which is `hi - lo` either way) still looked right — a SILENT MISCOMPILE. Routing it here,
  ## the ONE choke point every pair-valued slice flows through, makes both spellings agree.
  ## `src/`+`lib/` slice only strs/str-views (`ek` 4 / scalar-ptr bases, `slice_arr_stride == 0`), so
  ## this never fires there → fixpoint-neutral.
  if slice_arr_stride(e, cx.slots, cx.src) != 0 {
    emit_arr_slice_pair(e, sb, cx, a, nl)
    return
  }
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Slice(base, lo, hi) => {
      emit_str_pair(base, sb, cx, a, nl)   ## stack: [sptr (deep), slen (top)]
      push_str(sb, "  popq %rbx\n")        ## discard slen; stack: [sptr (top)]
      emit_gas(lo, sb, cx, a, nl)          ## stack: [sptr, lo (top)]
      push_str(sb, "  popq %rbx\n  popq %rax\n  addq %rbx, %rax\n  pushq %rax\n")  ## base ptr = sptr+lo
      emit_gas(hi, sb, cx, a, nl)          ## stack: [baseptr, hi (top)]
      emit_gas(lo, sb, cx, a, nl)          ## stack: [baseptr, hi, lo (top)]
      push_str(sb, "  popq %rbx\n  popq %rax\n  subq %rbx, %rax\n  pushq %rax\n")  ## len = hi - lo
    }
    _ => { push_str(sb, "  pushq $0\n  pushq $0\n") }
  }
}

## Emit the {ptr, len} pair for a TYPED ARRAY-SLICE `arr[lo..hi]` (`arr` a raw `[T; N]` local /
## by-ref array param) in the SAME stack shape `emit_slice_pair` produces (ptr deeper, len on top):
## the data pointer is element 0's ADDRESS advanced by `lo * stride * 8` (stride-aware, matching
## `emit_index_addr`'s element read) and the length is `hi - lo`. The PAIR dual of
## `emit_array_slice_assign` (which stores the same two words into a local's slots) — used wherever an
## array slice flows as a VALUE with no frame home: a `Slice(T)` struct-FIELD store, a by-value
## `-> Slice(T)` return. No bounds check (the same `unchecked` view discipline as the assign form).
emit_arr_slice_pair := fn(e : ptr(Expr), in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena, in out nl : usize) {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Slice(arr, lo, hi) => {
      av := var_name_span(arr)
      aent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, av.s, av.n)))
      if is_byte_array_eek(aent.eek) and aent.is_ref == false {
        push_str(sb, "  leaq -")
        push_int(sb, i64(aent.off * 8))
        push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rax\n")
      } else {
        emit_agg_base_addr(aent, sb)            ## &arr element-0 → %rax
      }
      push_str(sb, "  pushq %rax\n")
      emit_gas(lo, sb, cx, a, nl)             ## lo → stack
      push_str(sb, "  popq %rcx\n  imulq $")
      if is_byte_array_eek(aent.eek) { push_int(sb, 1) } else { push_int(sb, i64(aent.estride) * 8) }
      push_str(sb, ", %rcx\n  popq %rax\n  addq %rcx, %rax\n  pushq %rax\n")   ## ptr = elem0 + lo*stride*8
      emit_gas(hi, sb, cx, a, nl)             ## hi → stack
      emit_gas(lo, sb, cx, a, nl)             ## lo → stack
      push_str(sb, "  popq %rbx\n  popq %rax\n  subq %rbx, %rax\n  pushq %rax\n")   ## len = hi - lo
    }
    _ => { push_str(sb, "  pushq $0\n  pushq $0\n") }
  }
}

## Emit the two-word store for a range-slice binding `name := base[lo..hi]` — the view's {ptr, len}
## pair (from `emit_slice_pair`) into the str local's slots (ptr at `base+1`, len at `base+2`).
emit_slice_assign := fn(v : ptr(Expr), base : i64, in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena, in out nl : usize) {
  emit_slice_pair(v, sb, cx, a, nl)   ## stack: [ptr (deep), len (top)]
  push_str(sb, "  popq %rax\n  movq %rax, -")
  push_int(sb, base * 8)
  push_str(sb, "(%rbp)\n  popq %rax\n  movq %rax, -")
  push_int(sb, (base + 1) * 8)
  push_str(sb, "(%rbp)\n")
}

## Emit the two-word store for a TYPED ARRAY-SLICE binding `s := arr[lo..hi]` (§4 typed-slice): the
## data pointer at word 0 = the base array's element-0 address minus `lo * stride * 8` (the elements
## grow DOWN from element 0, matching `emit_index_addr`'s `ptr - i*stride*8` read), and the runtime
## length `hi - lo` at word 1. `s[i]` then reads element `lo+i` through the pointer, stride-aware.
emit_array_slice_assign := fn(v : ptr(Expr), base : i64, in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena, in out nl : usize) {
  match deref(v) {
    Expr::Slice(arr, lo, hi) => {
      av := var_name_span(arr)
      aent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, av.s, av.n)))
      if is_byte_array_eek(aent.eek) and aent.is_ref == false {
        push_str(sb, "  leaq -")
        push_int(sb, i64(aent.off * 8))
        push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rax\n")
      } else {
        emit_agg_base_addr(aent, sb)            ## &arr element-0 → %rax
      }
      push_str(sb, "  pushq %rax\n")
      emit_gas(lo, sb, cx, a, nl)             ## lo → stack
      push_str(sb, "  popq %rcx\n  imulq $")
      if is_byte_array_eek(aent.eek) { push_int(sb, 1) } else { push_int(sb, i64(aent.estride) * 8) }
      push_str(sb, ", %rcx\n  popq %rax\n  addq %rcx, %rax\n  movq %rax, -")   ## ptr = elem0 - lo*stride*8
      push_int(sb, (base + 1) * 8)
      push_str(sb, "(%rbp)\n")
      emit_gas(hi, sb, cx, a, nl)             ## hi → stack
      emit_gas(lo, sb, cx, a, nl)             ## lo → stack
      push_str(sb, "  popq %rbx\n  popq %rax\n  subq %rbx, %rax\n  movq %rax, -")   ## len = hi - lo
      push_int(sb, base * 8)
      push_str(sb, "(%rbp)\n")
    }
    _ => {}
  }
}

## Is `e` a `Call` to the spec str builtin `str_at(base, len)` — the raw-bytes→str view that
## constructs a `str` {ptr = base, len = len} from an ABSOLUTE byte pointer + length? Like `sub`
## it is a str-PRODUCING call (a 2-word str value), so it must be sized + lowered as a str wherever
## a str expression flows. The self-host lower has NO `str_at` fn to call (it is a Stage-0 builtin),
## so every pass's `str_at(...)` is recognized + INLINED here, like `byte_at`/`str_eq`/`sub`.
is_strat_call := fn(e : ptr(Expr), src : ptr(u8)) -> bool {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, args_head) => { str_at((src + cs), cl) == "str_at" }
    _ => { false }
  }
}

## Emit the {ptr, len} pair for `str_at(base, len)` in the SAME stack shape `emit_str_pair`
## produces (ptr deeper, len on top): lower arg 0 (the absolute base pointer) → push, then arg 1
## (the length) → push. No source-str deref (unlike `sub`, whose base is `s.ptr + start`); `str_at`'s
## base is already the absolute address. So a `str_at` view plugs in anywhere a str expression flows.
emit_strat_pair := fn(e : ptr(Expr), in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena, in out nl : usize) {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, args_head) => {
      a0 := arg_expr_at(args_head, 0, a)   ## the absolute base pointer (usize)
      a1 := arg_expr_at(args_head, 1, a)   ## the byte length (usize)
      ## arg0 is a POINTER (`str_at`'s base): `base + off` addressing (incl. a REBASED comptime-name
      ## handle whose `src + off` wraps back to the real pointer) is address arithmetic, NOT a checked
      ## integer `+` — suppress the overflow guard (I11 / CG-8) for it, the ONE central point that
      ## covers every inlined `str_at((src + off), …)` across all passes. Restored for the length arg.
      ov := cx.vchk
      cx.vchk = false
      emit_gas(a0, sb, cx, a, nl)          ## stack: [ptr (top)]
      cx.vchk = ov
      emit_gas(a1, sb, cx, a, nl)          ## stack: [ptr (deep), len (top)] — the str pair
    }
    _ => { push_str(sb, "  pushq $0\n  pushq $0\n") }
  }
}

## Emit the two-word store for `name := str_at(base, len)` — the view's {ptr, len} pair stored
## into the str local's reserved slots (ptr at slot `base`, len at slot `base+1`), like `emit_sub_assign`.
emit_strat_assign := fn(v : ptr(Expr), base : i64, in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena, in out nl : usize) {
  emit_strat_pair(v, sb, cx, a, nl)   ## stack: [ptr (deep), len (top)]
  push_str(sb, "  popq %rax\n  movq %rax, -")
  push_int(sb, base * 8)
  push_str(sb, "(%rbp)\n  popq %rax\n  movq %rax, -")
  push_int(sb, (base + 1) * 8)
  push_str(sb, "(%rbp)\n")
}

## Resolve a field read `base.f` to the frame slot index holding field `f` (or -1 if the
## base is not a struct `Var`). The base struct `Var` resolves to its slot entry (base
## offset + struct TYPE span), the field name `[fs, fs+fl)` maps to its declaration-order
## index via the struct layout, and the field slot is `base_off + field_index`. The
## deref-match on `base` lives here (a pointer PARAM) — the lowerable shape.
field_slot := fn(base : ptr(Expr), fs : usize, fl : usize, cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> i64 {
  match deref(base) {
    Expr::Var(s, n) => {
      ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, s, n)))
      fi := field_word_offset(cx.decls, cx.src, ent.sns, ent.snl, fs, fl, deref(cx.mar))
      i64(ent.off) - fi
    }
    ## NESTED field `<ib>.<ifield>.<fs>` (struct-in-struct): the slot of `<ib>.<ifield>` (recursively)
    ## plus the offset of `<fs>` within that inner field's STRUCT type. Resolves arbitrary nesting.
    Expr::Field(ib, ifs, ifl) => {
      inner := field_slot(ib, ifs, ifl, cx)
      ibt := base_struct_span(ib, cx)
      ift := field_type_span(cx.decls, cx.src, ibt.s, ibt.n, ifs, ifl, deref(cx.mar))
      fi := field_word_offset(cx.decls, cx.src, ift.s, ift.n, fs, fl, deref(cx.mar))
      if inner < 0 or fi < 0 { -1 } else { inner - fi }
    }
    _ => { -1 }
  }
}

## FN-10 — resolve `o.f(args)` (the parser desugars it to a UFCS `Call(f, [o, args…])`) to an INDIRECT
## call through a fn-VALUE STRUCT FIELD. Returns the frame slot holding `o`'s field `f` (a code-pointer
## word) when the receiver `base` is an INLINE struct local whose field `f` is typed `fn(…)`; -1
## otherwise (→ the ordinary UFCS / direct-call path). Gated three ways — the receiver must be a `Var`
## naming an INLINE (non-by-ref) STRUCT local, and its field `f` must exist with a type spelled `fn(…)`
## — so a normal UFCS method call (no such field) and a non-struct receiver both fall through, and a
## by-ref struct param (whose field lives behind a pointer) is deferred. `src/`+`lib/` declare no
## fn-typed field, so this never fires there → the self-host GAS is byte-identical → the TOOL-1 fixpoint.
fn_field_call_slot := fn(base : ptr(Expr), fs : usize, fl : usize, cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> i64 {
  vn := var_name_span(base)
  if vn.n == 0 { return -1 }
  ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, vn.s, vn.n)))
  if ent.snl == 0 { return -1 }            ## receiver is not a struct local
  if ent.is_ref { return -1 }              ## by-ref struct param — field-indirect deferred
  fts := field_type_span(cx.decls, cx.src, ent.sns, ent.snl, fs, fl, deref(cx.mar))
  if fts.n < 3 { return -1 }               ## no such field (or too short to be a fn-type)
  if str_at((cx.src + fts.s), 3) != "fn(" { return -1 }   ## the field is not a fn-value type
  i64(ent.off) - field_word_offset(cx.decls, cx.src, ent.sns, ent.snl, fs, fl, deref(cx.mar))
}

## Split a nested-field PLACE `o.i.v` (`Field(<base>, fs, fl)`) into its base expression + the LAST
## field's name span, so `FieldPathAssign` can resolve the whole place via `field_slot(base, fs, fl)`
## (which walks the nested base recursively). Isolated pointer-PARAM deref-match (the lowerable shape).
FPParts := struct { base : ptr(Expr), fs : usize, fl : usize }

## Resolve a scalar field projection rooted at an INLINE standard-byte struct LOCAL. The ordinary
## `field_slot` resolver is word-based, which is correct for the old tier but wrong after a byte array
## has compressed an earlier field: `o.inner.tail` must use the accumulated §6.1 byte offset, not the
## word-model count of `o.data`. This bounded path keeps by-reference/pointer roots on their existing
## routes; those ABI/address forms are separate S3(a) consumers.
##
## THE ROOT is gated on the ORACLE (`layout_kind_is_byte`), not on `std_struct_has_direct_byte_layout`
## alone: the latter answers TRUE for a `@packed` struct that happens to carry a byte array, so the
## bare predicate silently stole `@packed` roots from `emit_packed_load`/`emit_packed_assign` and read
## them at §6.1 offsets instead of the packed byte cursor (measured on a64/rv64/wasm:
## `@packed { data : [u8;3], inner }` read `o.inner.a` as `data[1]`, a WRONG VALUE where the previous
## compiler was correct). `layout_kind` decides PACKED before BYTE, which is the whole point of it.
##
## EVERY HOP uses `layout_field_offset_bytes`, which reads each link in the tier that link is actually
## WRITTEN in. Since CLAYOUT S3(b) there is ONE byte-precise whole-value writer, so that is §6.1
## bytes for the byte-layout root AND for every nested child in that writer's domain
## (`std_struct_is_byte_writable`), plus `field_word_offset * 8` for a word-granular child, where the
## two models coincide. A child in neither set has NO answer and its consumer stays fail-loud. Under
## S3(a) the word arm had to refuse every non-word-granular child, because x86_64 built such a child
## with `emit_struct_assign` (child words 0/1 = bytes 0/8) while its §6.1 offsets are 0/2 — so
## `o.inner.b` read byte 10 and returned 0 while this compiler's own a64/rv64/wat answered 22.
StdFieldPath := struct { ok : bool, root : usize, bo : i64, ts : usize, tl : usize }

## CLAYOUT S3(d) — THE BYTE-PRECISE ARRAY-ELEMENT PLACE RESOLVER, and it is deliberately the same
## walk as `standard_field_path` above with an `Index` root instead of a `Var` root. That is the whole
## design: an array element is a byte-layout struct value at `base + i*stride`, so once the stride is
## byte-precise (`slot_elem_stride_bytes`) the field path inside it is the SAME per-hop query every
## other reader uses (`layout_field_offset_bytes`), and nothing here recomputes an offset.
##
## The difference from `standard_field_path` is only WHERE the value lives: a `Var` root has a static
## frame displacement, so its consumers use `emit_packed_load` / `emit_packed_store`; an `Index` root's
## address depends on the index at run time, so its consumers compute the element address into %rax
## (`emit_index_addr`) and use the register-relative `emit_packed_load_rax` / `emit_packed_store_rax`.
## `arr`/`idx` are carried so the caller can emit that address without re-matching the chain.
##
## ROOT GATE: `std_array_elem_byte_tier` (`lower_layout`) — the element must be a byte-tier struct in
## the ONE byte-precise whole-value writer's domain. A WORD-tier element answers false and keeps the
## pre-existing word-offset composition byte-identically; that is not a lesser path but the CORRECT one
## for it, because a word-tier element is written by the word-per-field constructor. Confusing the two
## is exactly the defect this slice closes, in the other direction.
##
## `ok = false` means "not this shape" and every consumer then falls through to its existing arms, one
## of which is `resolve_idx_field_place`'s fence — so a byte-tier element OUTSIDE the domain still
## traps rather than being composed here (I11).
StdIdxPath := struct { ok : bool, arr : ptr(Expr), idx : ptr(Expr), bo : i64, ts : usize, tl : usize }



## CLAYOUT S3(d) — the DEPTH-1 form of `std_idx_path`, taking the place's PIECES rather than its
## expression: `xs[i].f`. It exists because `Stmt::IndexFieldAssign` is destructured by the parser into
## (array, index, field-name, value) and never reassembled, so the write arm has no `Field` node to walk.
## Same two decisions, same order, same queries — the element must be in `std_array_elem_byte_tier`'s
## domain and the field must have a §6.1 offset — so the depth-1 write cannot disagree with the depth-1
## read that goes through `std_idx_path`.
StdIdxOne := struct { ok : bool, bo : i64, ts : usize, tl : usize }

## The root `Var` of a field chain (`p.q.name`). Kept separate from `field_base_var`: the latter is
## deliberately depth-1 because several old callers need a direct `match` shape, while this value path
## must recover the root without making those callers re-enter a nested match under the frozen seed.
field_root_var := fn(e : ptr(Expr)) -> CSpan {
  mut res := CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Var(s, n) => { res = CSpan(s = s, n = n) }
    Expr::Field(base, fs, fl) => { res = field_root_var(base) }
    _ => {}
  }
  res
}

## The cumulative WORD offset of a field chain from its root struct (`p.q.name` = q's offset + name's
## offset). `base_struct_span` supplies the type at each hop, so this uses the same field layout query as
## every other word-tier reader instead of reconstructing declaration indices here. `-1` means the chain
## is not a plain Var-rooted struct path.
field_path_word_offset := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena) -> i64 {
  mut res := 0 - 1
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Var(s, n) => { res = 0 }
    Expr::Field(base, fs, fl) => {
      bt := base_struct_span(base, cx)
      if bt.n != 0 {
        fwo := field_word_offset(cx.decls, cx.src, bt.s, bt.n, fs, fl, a)
        if fwo >= 0 {
          parent := field_path_word_offset(base, cx, a)
          if parent >= 0 { res = parent + fwo }
        }
      }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  res
}

## Is `base` the place `g.name` where `g` is a STRUCT LOCAL/PARAM and `name` a 2-word `{ptr, len}`
## VIEW field — `str` or a `Slice(T)` instantiation (Types §9.4: the same pair repr)? Used by the
## `Field` arm to read a str VALUE operand. The root may be a nested plain field path (`p.q.name`):
## `off`/`fldoff` carry the cumulative word offset so inline locals and by-reference params use the same
## ascending pair layout as a one-level field. `found`=false means the base is not a struct-local view
## field.
StrFldPlace := struct { found : bool, is_ref : bool, off : usize, fldoff : usize }
str_field_place := fn(base : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena) -> StrFldPlace {
  mut res := StrFldPlace(found = false, is_ref = false, off = 0, fldoff = 0)
  fp := field_place_parts(base)                          ## base == Field(g, name) → (g, "name")
  if unchecked bitcast(usize, fp.base) == 0 { return res }
  bv := field_root_var(base)                              ## g may be the root of p.q.name
  if bv.n == 0 { return res }
  ei := entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, bv.s, bv.n)
  se := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, ei))
  if not streq(cx.src, se.ns, se.nl, bv.s, bv.n) { return res }   ## entry_of returns 0 on miss — verify
  if se.ek != 2 { return res }                           ## g must be a struct (inline local or by-ref param)
  bt := base_struct_span(fp.base, cx)
  fts := field_type_span(cx.decls, cx.src, bt.s, bt.n, fp.fs, fp.fl, a)
  if fts.n == 0 { return res }
  ## Types §9.4 — the 2-word `{ptr, len}` VIEW field. `str` IS that pair, and so is a `Slice(T)`
  ## instantiation (the library pair `struct { ptr : ptr(T), len : usize }`, Stdlib §3.5 / appendix
  ## 160): identical repr, identical slot math, so both take this place. Without the `Slice` case a
  ## `v : Slice(T)` FIELD matched no arm — `x.v.len` on a BY-REFERENCE struct param fell to
  ## `field_slot`, whose `Var` arm computes `ent.off - fi` off a slot that holds a POINTER (not the
  ## inline struct) and read a garbage frame word (in practice a folded 0) — a SILENT MISCOMPILE
  ## whose documented workaround was to extract the field first (`w := x.v; w.len`). For an INLINE
  ## struct LOCAL the two routes agree word-for-word (`field_slot` = `se.off - fwo - 1` for `len`,
  ## this place's `-sfoff*8` = the same slot), so only the by-ref read changes. `src/` and `lib/`
  ## declare NO `Slice(T)` struct field, so the added case is dormant there → fixpoint-neutral.
  ftb := base_type_name(cx.src, fts.s, fts.n)
  if str_at((cx.src + fts.s), fts.n) != "str" and str_at((cx.src + ftb.s), ftb.n) != "Slice" { return res }
  fwo := field_path_word_offset(base, cx, a)
  if fwo < 0 { return res }
  if se.is_ref {
    res = StrFldPlace(found = true, is_ref = true, off = se.off, fldoff = usize(fwo))
  } else {
    ## Types §9.4 — a struct local's word `k` lives at slot `base - k` (its slots DESCEND as the word
    ## index rises; `emit_struct_assign` writes word k at `-(base - k + 1)*8`). So the str field's
    ## word-0 slot is `se.off - fwo`, NOT `se.off + fwo`: the `+` read `fwo` words on the WRONG SIDE of
    ## the struct, so a `str` field at a NON-ZERO word offset (`S { n : u64, v : str }` → `b.v.len`)
    ## returned a neighbouring slot — a SILENT MISCOMPILE. A leading str field (`fwo == 0`, every
    ## pre-existing fixture) is byte-identical either way, which is why it went unnoticed.
    res = StrFldPlace(found = true, is_ref = false, off = se.off - usize(fwo), fldoff = 0)
  }
  res
}

## Is `base` the place `g.field` where `g` is a MUTABLE-GLOBAL struct? Returns the global's name span +
## the field's WORD offset within `g`'s `.data`. Used by the `Index` arm to read `g.xs[i]` (an ARRAY
## field of a global struct): the element sits at `LABEL + (off + i)*8(%rip)`. Any field type (the
## caller applies index semantics); `found`=false ⇒ base is not a global-struct field.
GFldOff := struct { found : bool, gs : usize, gn : usize, off : i64 }
global_field_off := fn(base : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena) -> GFldOff {
  mut res := GFldOff(found = false, gs = 0, gn = 0, off = 0)
  fp := field_place_parts(base)
  if unchecked bitcast(usize, fp.base) == 0 { return res }
  bv := field_base_var(fp.base)
  if not bv.ok { return res }
  if not is_module_mut_global(cx.decls, cx.src, bv.s, bv.n) { return res }
  mgv := mut_global_value(cx.decls, cx.src, bv.s, bv.n)
  if unchecked bitcast(usize, mgv) == 0 { return res }
  gsli := struct_lit_info(mgv)
  if not gsli.is_s { return res }
  fwo := field_word_offset(cx.decls, cx.src, gsli.ss, gsli.sl, fp.fs, fp.fl, a)
  if fwo < 0 { return res }
  res = GFldOff(found = true, gs = bv.s, gn = bv.n, off = fwo)
  res
}

## The `.data` PLACE of an arbitrary field chain rooted at a mutable-global struct — a `Var(g)` (g a
## mut-global struct) → {off 0, type = g's struct}, and `base.f` → resolve `base` to a struct place,
## then add `field_word_offset(basetype, f)` and carry the field's type forward. This generalizes the
## 1-/2-level global-field reads/writes to ANY depth (`STATE.a.b.c`). `found`=false if the root is not
## a mut-global struct or any intermediate link is not a struct field. The `ts`/`tl` span is the type
## AT the resolved place (the final field's type), so the caller can gate on scalar-vs-aggregate.
## Arms are if/else (NOT early `return`, which is dead inside a match arm under the seed).
GPlace := struct { found : bool, gs : usize, gn : usize, off : i64, ts : usize, tl : usize }
global_place := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena) -> GPlace {
  z := GPlace(found = false, gs = 0, gn = 0, off = 0, ts = 0, tl = 0)
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Var(s, n) => {
      mgv := mut_global_value(cx.decls, cx.src, s, n)
      if unchecked bitcast(usize, mgv) == 0 { z }
      else {
        gsli := struct_lit_info(mgv)
        if gsli.is_s { GPlace(found = true, gs = s, gn = n, off = 0, ts = gsli.ss, tl = gsli.sl) }
        else {
          ## A raw-union global is initialized from a constructor literal but has no discriminant.
          ## Carry the declared union type so a later `.member.field` path can use the normal union
          ## offset/type machinery rather than falling back to a frame load.
          gei := enum_lit_full(mgv)
          if gei.is_e and is_union_decl(cx.decls, cx.src, gei.es, gei.el) {
            GPlace(found = true, gs = s, gn = n, off = 0, ts = gei.es, tl = gei.el)
          } else { z }
        }
      }
    }
    Expr::Field(base, fs, fl) => {
      pb := global_place(base, cx, a)
      if not pb.found or pb.tl == 0 or (struct_decl_of(cx.decls, cx.src, pb.ts, pb.tl) < 0 and not is_union_decl(cx.decls, cx.src, pb.ts, pb.tl)) { z }
      else {
        fwo := field_word_offset(cx.decls, cx.src, pb.ts, pb.tl, fs, fl, a)
        if fwo < 0 { z }
        else {
          fts := field_type_span(cx.decls, cx.src, pb.ts, pb.tl, fs, fl, a)
          GPlace(found = true, gs = pb.gs, gn = pb.gn, off = pb.off + fwo, ts = fts.s, tl = fts.n)
        }
      }
    }
    _ => { z }
  }
}

## Is the type span `[ts, ts+tl)` a SCALAR (not a user struct/enum, not str)? A scalar place is a
## single-word `.data` read/store; an aggregate place needs multi-word delivery (deferred).
global_place_scalar := fn(ts : usize, tl : usize, cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> bool {
  if tl == 0 { return false }
  if enum_decl_of(cx.decls, cx.src, ts, tl) >= 0 { return false }
  if struct_decl_of(cx.decls, cx.src, ts, tl) >= 0 { return false }
  if str_at((cx.src + ts), tl) == "str" { return false }
  true
}

## Store a MULTI-WORD aggregate value `fv` into a mutable-global's `.data` at `LABEL + off*8` ASCENDING:
## an enum (`[disc, payload…]`), a str (`{ptr, len}`), or a struct (its field words). Materialize `fv`
## into the match scratch (same down-growing layout for all: word `j` at `-(base+1+j)*8`), then copy
## `nw` words up to `.data`. Returns true if `[ts, ts+tl)` is one of those aggregate kinds (else false
## → the caller uses its scalar / default path). Only a LITERAL `fv` materializes, as in the local
## field-write path; a non-literal is a no-op. Shared by the 1-level `FieldAssign` and the nested
## `FieldPathAssign` global-write paths (any depth). `nw` uses STATEMENT-form assignment — a chained
## `if/else if/else` VALUE expression mis-lowers its middle arm under the seed (garbage → runaway loop).
emit_global_agg_store := fn(fv : ptr(Expr), gs : usize, gn : usize, off : i64, ts : usize, tl : usize, in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena, in out nl : usize) -> bool {
  mut is_enum := false
  mut is_str := false
  mut is_struct := false
  if enum_decl_of(cx.decls, cx.src, ts, tl) >= 0 { is_enum = true }
  else if str_at((cx.src + ts), tl) == "str" { is_str = true }
  else if struct_decl_of(cx.decls, cx.src, ts, tl) >= 0 { is_struct = true }
  mut done := false
  if is_enum or is_str or is_struct {
    mut nw : usize = 2
    if is_enum { nw = 1 + enum_inst_words(cx.decls, cx.src, ts, tl, a) }
    else if is_struct { nw = struct_words(cx.decls, cx.src, ts, tl, a) }
    tb := usize(cx.tslot) + cx.mdepth * cx.swidth + cx.swidth - 1
    if is_enum { emit_enum_assign(fv, i64(tb), sb, cx, a, nl) }
    else if is_struct { emit_struct_assign(fv, i64(tb), sb, cx, a, nl) }
    else { emit_str_assign(fv, i64(tb), sb) }
    push_str(sb, "  leaq ")
    emit_global_label(sb, cx.decls, cx.src, gs, gn)
    push_str(sb, "(%rip), %rbx\n")
    mut j := 0
    while j < nw {
      push_str(sb, "  movq -")
      push_int(sb, i64((tb - j + 1) * 8))
      push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rax\n  movq %rax, ")
      push_int(sb, (off + i64(j)) * 8)
      push_str(sb, "(%rbx)\n")
      j += 1
    }
    done = true
  }
  done
}

## Whole-value assignment to a MUTABLE-GLOBAL aggregate (`G = <value>` where `G` is a struct / enum /
## str / array module global). The bare-name `Stmt::Assign` global-write path stored a SINGLE word
## (`emit_gas; popq %rax; movq %rax, LABEL`) — correct for a scalar global, but for a multi-word
## aggregate global it dropped every word past word 0 (and even word 0 was an ADDRESS, not a value, for
## a struct/array/str LITERAL RHS) — a silent miscompile. Route the aggregate cases here:
##   - struct / enum literal RHS → `emit_global_agg_store` (materialize into scratch, copy ALL words up
##     to `.data` ascending — the same machinery the field-write path uses);
##   - str literal RHS → materialize `{ptr, len}` into scratch, copy BOTH words;
##   - scalar-element array literal RHS → store each element to `LABEL + k*8`.
## A NON-literal aggregate RHS (a fn-return, a var, a deref…) would need the per-RHS-kind local
## materialization dispatch that the Assign body has for a frame destination; rather than SILENTLY drop
## words, FAIL LOUD (Priority-1). Returns true iff `G` is an aggregate global (the caller then skips the
## scalar path); false for a scalar global (unchanged fast path → fixpoint-neutral: every mut global the
## compiler itself whole-assigns is a scalar).
emit_mut_global_whole_assign := fn(gs : usize, gn : usize, v : ptr(Expr), in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena, in out nl : usize) -> bool {
  gv := mut_global_value(cx.decls, cx.src, gs, gn)
  if unchecked bitcast(usize, gv) == 0 { return false }
  ## STRUCT global — materialize the RHS literal into the WIDTH-SAFE per-function agg-temp pool
  ## (the width scan includes this global when it is referenced by the function), so word `k` at slot
  ## `base-k` never underflows the frame, then copy
  ## all `nw` words up to `.data` ascending. (The match-scratch band `emit_global_agg_store` uses is only
  ## `swidth` wide — it underflows for a struct wider than the scratch.)
  if struct_lit_info(gv).is_s {
    si := struct_lit_info(v)
    if not si.is_s { panic("selfhost: whole-value assignment of a NON-LITERAL aggregate to a mutable STRUCT global is unsupported (would silently drop words) — assign fields individually, or copy through a local") }
    if cx.agg_next < 0 { panic("selfhost: whole-value assignment to a mutable STRUCT global has no aggregate-temp pool in this context") }
    nw := struct_words(cx.decls, cx.src, si.ss, si.sl, a)
    gaoff := agg_alloc(cx)
    emit_struct_assign(v, gaoff, sb, cx, a, nl)
    push_str(sb, "  leaq ")
    emit_global_label(sb, cx.decls, cx.src, gs, gn)
    push_str(sb, "(%rip), %rbx\n")
    mut j := 0
    while j < nw {
      push_str(sb, "  movq -")
      push_int(sb, i64((gaoff - i64(j) + 1) * 8))
      push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rax\n  movq %rax, ")
      push_int(sb, i64(j * 8))
      push_str(sb, "(%rbx)\n")
      j += 1
    }
    return true
  }
  ## ENUM global — `[disc, payload…]` into the agg pool, copy all words up (same width-safe path).
  if enum_lit_info(gv).is_e {
    ei := enum_lit_info(v)
    if not ei.is_e { panic("selfhost: whole-value assignment of a NON-LITERAL enum to a mutable ENUM global is unsupported (would silently drop payload words) — copy through a local") }
    if cx.agg_next < 0 { panic("selfhost: whole-value assignment to a mutable ENUM global has no aggregate-temp pool in this context") }
    nw := 1 + enum_inst_words(cx.decls, cx.src, ei.es, ei.el, a)
    gaoff := agg_alloc(cx)
    emit_enum_assign(v, gaoff, sb, cx, a, nl)
    push_str(sb, "  leaq ")
    emit_global_label(sb, cx.decls, cx.src, gs, gn)
    push_str(sb, "(%rip), %rbx\n")
    mut j := 0
    while j < nw {
      push_str(sb, "  movq -")
      push_int(sb, i64((gaoff - i64(j) + 1) * 8))
      push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rax\n  movq %rax, ")
      push_int(sb, i64(j * 8))
      push_str(sb, "(%rbx)\n")
      j += 1
    }
    return true
  }
  ## STR global — a bare `mut S := "…"` module global. Its READ side is itself broken (a bare str
  ## global's `.len` reads 0 and indexing its bytes faults — the top-level str-global init/read path,
  ## distinct from the working str-FIELD-of-struct-global path), so a whole-value str-global assignment
  ## has no correct observable result. FAIL LOUD rather than emit a word-copy against a broken read
  ## (Priority-1). src/ + lib/ declare no bare str globals → dormant, fixpoint-neutral.
  if str_lit_info(gv).is_s {
    panic("selfhost: whole-value assignment to a mutable STR global is unsupported (the bare str-global read path is itself broken — `.len` reads 0) — use a str FIELD of a struct global, or a local")
  }
  ## ARRAY global (whole reassign) — only a SCALAR-element array literal is handled here (store each
  ## element to `LABEL + k*8`); an array-of-aggregate or a non-literal array fails loud.
  if array_lit_info(gv).is_a {
    ai := array_lit_info(v)
    if not ai.is_a { panic("selfhost: whole-value assignment of a NON-LITERAL array to a mutable ARRAY global is unsupported (would silently drop elements) — assign elements individually, or copy through a local") }
    ## reject aggregate elements (multi-word) — the scalar-per-word store below would mis-lay them
    mut gel := ai.ehead
    while gel != 0 {
      ge := deref(arg_p(gel))
      if struct_lit_info(ge.e).is_s or enum_lit_info(ge.e).is_e or str_lit_info(ge.e).is_s or array_lit_info(ge.e).is_a {
        panic("selfhost: whole-value assignment of an array-of-AGGREGATE to a mutable ARRAY global is unsupported (would drop element words) — assign elements individually")
      }
      gel = ge.next
    }
    mut g := ai.ehead
    mut k := 0
    while g != 0 {
      ga := deref(arg_p(g))
      emit_gas(ga.e, sb, cx, a, nl)
      push_str(sb, "  popq %rax\n  leaq ")
      emit_global_label(sb, cx.decls, cx.src, gs, gn)
      push_str(sb, "(%rip), %rbx\n  movq %rax, ")
      push_int(sb, i64(k * 8))
      push_str(sb, "(%rbx)\n")
      k += 1
      g = ga.next
    }
    return true
  }
  false
}

## Is `e` the place `xs[i].key` where `xs` is an ARRAY-of-STRUCT (eek 2) and `key` a `str`-typed field
## of the element? The str's `{ptr, len}` live at the element field address (`emit_idx_field_addr` with
## the field's word offset) and the next word down (`-8`). Returns the array/index exprs + the field
## word offset. `found`=false ⇒ not a str field of an array-of-struct element. (Compound of the a[i].f
## element-field read and the str-field 2-word layout.)
StrArrElem := struct { found : bool, arr : ptr(Expr), idx : ptr(Expr), woff : i64 }
str_field_arr_elem := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena) -> StrArrElem {
  z := unchecked bitcast(ptr(Expr), 0)
  mut res := StrArrElem(found = false, arr = z, idx = z, woff = 0)
  fp := field_place_parts(e)                       ## e == Field(Index(xs,i), key) → (Index(xs,i), "key")
  if unchecked bitcast(usize, fp.base) == 0 { return res }
  ibx := field_base_index(fp.base)                 ## fp.base == Index(xs, i)?
  if not ibx.is_ix { return res }
  ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, index_base_entry(ibx.arr, cx.slots, cx.src)))
  if ent.eek != 2 { return res }                   ## element must be a struct (its sns/snl = struct type)
  fts := field_type_span(cx.decls, cx.src, ent.sns, ent.snl, fp.fs, fp.fl, a)
  if fts.n == 0 { return res }
  if str_at((cx.src + fts.s), fts.n) != "str" { return res }
  fwo := field_word_offset(cx.decls, cx.src, ent.sns, ent.snl, fp.fs, fp.fl, a)
  res = StrArrElem(found = true, arr = ibx.arr, idx = ibx.idx, woff = fwo)
  res
}

## AGGREGATE-PARAM tier — is a `Field`'s base a BY-REFERENCE struct param? Returns the base's
## slot ENTRY (so the caller can `emit_agg_base_addr` the word-0 pointer) and the field's
## declaration-order index `fi` (the field is at `-(fi*8)(%rax)` through the pointer). `is_ref`
## false (and a sentinel entry) when the base is not a by-ref `Var` — the caller falls back to
## the direct-frame-slot `field_slot` path. The deref-match on the base pointer PARAM stays here.
## Carries the base-var slot INDEX (`ent_idx`), NOT an embedded `SlotEntry` — a struct that embeds
## a 9-word SlotEntry is an 11-word value the ≤3-register struct-RETURN truncates (the same wide
## struct-value defect). Callers recover the entry with `deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, slots, ent_idx))`.
FBRef := struct { is_ref : bool, ent_idx : usize, fi : i64 }
field_base_ref := fn(base : ptr(Expr), fs : usize, fl : usize, cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> FBRef {
  mut res := FBRef(is_ref = false, ent_idx = 0, fi = 0)
  match deref(base) {
    Expr::Var(s, n) => {
      ix := entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, s, n)
      ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, ix))
      if ent.is_ref {
        fi := field_word_offset(cx.decls, cx.src, ent.sns, ent.snl, fs, fl, deref(cx.mar))
        res = FBRef(is_ref = true, ent_idx = ix, fi = fi)
      }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  res
}

## The frame slot of a struct LOCAL's field, resolved from the base var's name span
## `[bs, bs+bn)` and the field name span `[fs, fs+fl)` directly (the `FieldAssign` form,
## where the base is a name not an `Expr`). The base var's slot ENTRY gives the base offset
## + struct TYPE span; `field` maps to its declaration-order index — slot `base_off + index`.
## The same computation `field_slot` does for a READ, used here for the field-mutation STORE.
field_slot_of_name := fn(bs : usize, bn : usize, fs : usize, fl : usize, cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> i64 {
  ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, bs, bn)))
  fi := field_word_offset(cx.decls, cx.src, ent.sns, ent.snl, fs, fl, deref(cx.mar))
  i64(ent.off) - fi
}

## ARRAY-OF-STRUCT field access `a[i].f` — recover the `Index(arr, idx)` pieces from a
## `Field`'s base when that base is an `Index`. The deref-match on the base pointer PARAM stays
## here (the lowerable shape, mirroring `index_parts`). `is_ix` is false (both ptrs null) when
## the base is not an `Index` (a plain `Var`-struct field read, handled by `field_slot`).
FBIx := struct { is_ix : bool, arr : ptr(Expr), idx : ptr(Expr) }
field_base_index := fn(base : ptr(Expr)) -> FBIx {
  match deref(base) {
    Expr::Index(b, i) => { FBIx(is_ix = true, arr = b, idx = i) }
    _ => {
      z := unchecked bitcast(ptr(Expr), 0)
      FBIx(is_ix = false, arr = z, idx = z)
    }
  }
}

## Resolve `a[i].f`'s field index — `f`'s declaration-order index within the array's ELEMENT
## struct type (the base array slot's `sns`/`snl` element-type span). The caller scales it by 8
## for the byte offset. Isolated from `emit_idx_field_addr` to keep the emit signature within
## the argument-register budget (the field byte offset is passed pre-computed).
idx_field_index := fn(arr : ptr(Expr), idx : ptr(Expr), fs : usize, fl : usize, cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> i64 {
  ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, index_base_entry(arr, cx.slots, cx.src)))
  fib := field_index_base(arr, cx)
  if fib.is_fld {
    fent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, fib.ent_idx))
    afp := field_place_parts(arr)
    aft := field_type_span(cx.decls, cx.src, fent.sns, fent.snl, afp.fs, afp.fl, deref(cx.mar))
    aet := array_elem_span(cx.src, aft.s, aft.n)
    rr := field_word_offset(cx.decls, cx.src, aet.s, aet.n, fs, fl, deref(cx.mar))
    if rr < 0 { panic("selfhost: array-field element field not resolvable") }
    return rr
  }
  ## MIXED-KIND tuple: element N has its OWN struct/enum type (from `tcomps`), not the uniform first-
  ## component type — so `t.N.field` resolves the field within element N's actual type. Uniform tuples /
  ## arrays have no `tcomps` entry (`estride == 0` sentinel) → the uniform `ent.sns/snl` path (unchanged).
  tc := tcomp_find(cx, ent.off, usize(num_lit_value(idx)))
  mut r := field_word_offset(cx.decls, cx.src, ent.sns, ent.snl, fs, fl, deref(cx.mar))
  if tc.estride != 0 and tc.snl != 0 { r = field_word_offset(cx.decls, cx.src, tc.sns, tc.snl, fs, fl, deref(cx.mar)) }
  if r < 0 and ent.ek == 5 and ent.is_ref {
    eta := slice_param_elem_span(cx.src, ent.ns, ent.nl)
    if eta.n != 0 { r = field_word_offset(cx.decls, cx.src, eta.s, eta.n, fs, fl, deref(cx.mar)) }
  }
  ## SOUNDNESS (§8): a NEGATIVE offset means the field is not resolvable in the resolved element type —
  ## the element type is WRONG (the uniform first-component type, e.g. a mixed-kind tuple PARAM, which
  ## has no per-component `tcomps` entry). Fail LOUDLY rather than emit a bad `-1*8` offset (silent
  ## miscompile). A LOCAL mixed tuple resolves via `tcomps` (r >= 0); a homogeneous array resolves
  ## normally. Workaround for a mixed-kind tuple PARAM: pass its components separately / via a struct.
  if r < 0 { panic("selfhost: tuple/array element field not resolvable — mixed-kind tuple PARAM field access not yet supported (pass components separately or via a struct)") }
  r
}

## Emit the ADDRESS of an array-of-struct element field `a[i].f` into %rax: compute the element
## base address (`emit_index_addr` → %rax), then subtract `foff` (the precomputed field byte
## offset `field_index * 8`). The frame grows DOWN — field `f` of an element is at the element
## address MINUS `foff`, matching the struct field layout where field `i` is at slot
## `base_off + i` = a deeper/more-negative byte offset. Leaves the field address in %rax (no
## load). `foff` is precomputed (via `idx_field_index`) by the caller so this stays at 6 params
## (two `in out` must land in argument registers — the documented budget).
emit_idx_field_addr := fn(arr : ptr(Expr), idx : ptr(Expr), foff : i64, in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, cx : ptr(LCtx), in out nl : usize) {
  a := arena_of(cx)
  ## A STRUCT-element ARRAY GLOBAL has ascending `.data` layout and no frame slot. Resolve it before
  ## the frame-oriented `emit_index_addr` path, which would otherwise read slot 0. `foff` is already a
  ## byte offset from the element base; the global element stride must include every struct word.
  gavn := var_name_span(arr)
  if gavn.n != 0 {
    gamv := global_arr_value(cx.slots, cx.decls, cx.src, gavn.s, gavn.n)
    if unchecked bitcast(usize, gamv) != 0 {
      gali := array_lit_info(gamv)
      if gali.is_a and gali.nel > 0 {
        gge := struct_lit_info(arg_expr_at(gali.ehead, 0, a))
        if gge.is_s {
          gstride := struct_words(cx.decls, cx.src, gge.ss, gge.sl, a)
          emit_gas(idx, sb, cx, a, nl)                 ## index → stack
          push_str(sb, "  leaq ")
          emit_global_label(sb, cx.decls, cx.src, gavn.s, gavn.n)
          push_str(sb, "(%rip), %rax\n  popq %rcx\n")
          if cx.vchk {
            push_str(sb, "  cmpq $")
            push_int(sb, i64(gali.nel))
            push_str(sb, ", %rcx\n  jb 1f\n  ud2\n1:\n")
          }
          push_str(sb, "  imulq $")
          push_int(sb, i64(gstride * 8))
          push_str(sb, ", %rcx\n  addq %rcx, %rax\n")
          if foff != 0 {
            push_str(sb, "  addq $")
            push_int(sb, foff)
            push_str(sb, ", %rax\n")
          }
          return
        }
      }
    }
  }
  emit_index_addr(arr, idx, sb, cx, a, nl)   ## element base address → %rax
  ## field address = element address - field_offset (the frame grows down)
  push_str(sb, "  addq $")
  push_int(sb, foff)
  push_str(sb, ", %rax\n")
}

## COMBINED-OFFSET resolver for `arr[idx].agg.leaf` (Types §9.4 deeper fixed-array definite assignment) —
## an array-of-struct ELEMENT (`Index(arr, idx)`), an intermediate STRUCT field `agg`, then a SCALAR leaf
## field. Returns the array/index exprs + the COMBINED word offset `field_word_offset(elem, agg) +
## field_word_offset(agg_type, leaf)`. Both offsets are ASCENDING from the element base (the array-element
## field layout `emit_idx_field_addr`/`emit_index_addr` use), and `agg` occupies a contiguous word run
## within the element, so the leaf sits at `wagg + wleaf`. `found`=false when `base` is not
## `Field(Index(...), agg)` (a different place → a later read/write arm, or the conservative default).
## PANICS (fail loud, NEVER a silent bad offset / silent init) when the shape MATCHES but is unsupported:
## a non-plain-struct intermediate field (str/enum/union/slice), a MULTI-WORD leaf (str/struct/enum), or an
## unresolvable field. The 4-word result mirrors `StrArrElem` (within the ≤3-register struct-return budget).
DeepIdxField := struct { found : bool, arr : ptr(Expr), idx : ptr(Expr), woff : i64 }

## The struct TYPE span of a place `e` rooted at an array-of-struct ELEMENT (`Index(arr, idx)` with a
## struct element `eek == 2`) followed by ZERO or more plain-struct field hops, plus the ASCENDING
## cumulative word offset from the element base. `found`=false when the chain is NOT rooted at a
## struct-element `Index` (a different place → the caller falls through). PANICS (fail loud, never a
## silent bad offset) when a hop's containing type is not a plain struct or the field is unresolvable.
## Recursion over a DIRECT param (a function-body match, the lowerable shape — the nested call in the
## `Field` arm is a call, not a `match` inside a `match` arm). This is the N-hop generalization of the
## one-hop `arr[i].agg.leaf` resolver (Types §9.4 deeper fixed-array definite assignment).
IFPlace := struct { found : bool, tys : usize, tyn : usize, woff : i64 }

## The innermost `Index(arr, idx)` of a Field-chain place (descends through `Field` hops). `is_ix`
## false when there is no array-element base at the root. Kept a flat one-level match per level (a
## recursive call, not a nested match) — the deref-match lowerable shape.
deep_idx_arr := fn(e : ptr(Expr)) -> FBIx {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Index(b, i) => { FBIx(is_ix = true, arr = b, idx = i) }
    Expr::Field(ib, fs, fl) => { deep_idx_arr(ib) }
    _ => {
      z := unchecked bitcast(ptr(Expr), 0)
      FBIx(is_ix = false, arr = z, idx = z)
    }
  }
}

## COMBINED-OFFSET resolver for `arr[i].agg1.agg2….leaf` (Types §9.4) — an array-of-struct ELEMENT
## followed by ANY depth of intermediate plain-struct fields, then a SCALAR leaf field. Returns the
## array/index exprs + the COMBINED ascending word offset from the element base to the leaf. `found`
## =false when `base` is not a `Field(<chain rooted at an array-element Index>, …)` (a different place
## → a later read/write arm, or the conservative default). PANICS (fail loud, NEVER a silent bad offset
## / silent init) when the shape MATCHES but is unsupported: a non-plain-struct intermediate, a
## MULTI-WORD leaf (str/struct/enum), or an unresolvable field. Generalized from the former one-hop
## `Field(Index(...), agg)` matcher to N hops via `resolve_idx_field_place` (bugs: `xs[i].b.c.x`).
resolve_deep_idx_field := fn(base : ptr(Expr), fs : usize, fl : usize, cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> DeepIdxField {
  a := deref(cx.mar)
  z := unchecked bitcast(ptr(Expr), 0)
  mut res := DeepIdxField(found = false, arr = z, idx = z, woff = 0)
  ## `base` must itself be a `Field` chain (the whole read/write is depth >= 2); a bare `Index` base
  ## (depth-1 `arr[i].f`) is handled by the `fbi.is_ix` arm, not here.
  ip := field_place_parts(base)                    ## base == Field(inner_base, agg) → (inner_base, "agg")
  if unchecked bitcast(usize, ip.base) == 0 { return res }
  pl := resolve_idx_field_place(base, cx)          ## resolve the struct type + cumulative offset of `base`
  if pl.found == false { return res }
  lft := field_type_span(cx.decls, cx.src, pl.tys, pl.tyn, fs, fl, a)
  if lft.n == 0 { panic("selfhost: `arr[i].field…leaf` — leaf field not declared in the innermost struct") }
  if str_at((cx.src + lft.s), lft.n) == "str" or struct_decl_of(cx.decls, cx.src, lft.s, lft.n) >= 0 or enum_decl_of(cx.decls, cx.src, lft.s, lft.n) >= 0 {
    panic("selfhost: `arr[i].field…leaf` — a MULTI-WORD (str/struct/enum) leaf is not yet supported (bind the intermediate struct to a local, then read/write the leaf)")
  }
  wleaf := field_word_offset(cx.decls, cx.src, pl.tys, pl.tyn, fs, fl, a)
  if wleaf < 0 { panic("selfhost: `arr[i].field…leaf` — leaf field not resolvable in the innermost struct type") }
  di := deep_idx_arr(base)
  if di.is_ix == false { return res }
  res = DeepIdxField(found = true, arr = di.arr, idx = di.idx, woff = pl.woff + i64(wleaf))
  res
}

## Nested field place `<root>.f1.f2…` where `<root>` is a POINTER to a struct — a BY-REF struct PARAM
## (`Var`, ek 2 + is_ref: the slot holds the caller's struct pointer) or `deref(<ek-7 ptr var>)` (the
## slot holds the pointee address). Carries the root var's slot INDEX + the place's struct TYPE span +
## the ASCENDING cumulative word offset from the pointee base (the pointee-field layout `deref(p).f` /
## the depth-1 by-ref field read use). `found`=false when the root is neither shape (an INLINE struct
## local → the direct-frame `field_slot` path stays authoritative → fixpoint-neutral). Fails LOUD on a
## non-plain-struct / @packed intermediate (never a silent bad offset). Fixes depth>=2 `deref(p).b.pb`
## and by-ref `a.b.pb`, whose depth-1 forms are handled by the `dsb` / `bref` arms respectively.
PtrPlace := struct { found : bool, ent_idx : usize, tys : usize, tyn : usize, woff : i64 }
ptr_place_root := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> PtrPlace {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Var(s, n) => {
      ix := entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, s, n)
      ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, ix))
      ## a BY-REF struct PARAM only (ek 2 + is_ref); an INLINE struct local (ek 2, not ref) is NOT a
      ## pointer — it uses the direct-frame `field_slot` path (so this stays fixpoint-neutral).
      if ent.ek == 2 and ent.is_ref and ent.snl != 0 { return PtrPlace(found = true, ent_idx = ix, tys = ent.sns, tyn = ent.snl, woff = 0) }
      return PtrPlace(found = false, ent_idx = 0, tys = 0, tyn = 0, woff = 0)
    }
    Expr::Deref(inner) => {
      vs := var_name_span(inner)
      if vs.n == 0 { return PtrPlace(found = false, ent_idx = 0, tys = 0, tyn = 0, woff = 0) }
      ix := entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, vs.s, vs.n)
      ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, ix))
      if ent.ek != 7 or ent.snl == 0 { return PtrPlace(found = false, ent_idx = 0, tys = 0, tyn = 0, woff = 0) }
      return PtrPlace(found = true, ent_idx = ix, tys = ent.sns, tyn = ent.snl, woff = 0)
    }
    _ => { return PtrPlace(found = false, ent_idx = 0, tys = 0, tyn = 0, woff = 0) }
  }
}
ptr_place := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> PtrPlace {
  a := deref(cx.mar)
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Field(ib, fs, fl) => {
      inner := ptr_place(ib, cx)
      if inner.found == false { return PtrPlace(found = false, ent_idx = 0, tys = 0, tyn = 0, woff = 0) }
      if struct_decl_of(cx.decls, cx.src, inner.tys, inner.tyn) < 0 {
        panic("selfhost: nested field through a pointer where an intermediate field is not a plain struct is not yet supported (bind it to a local first)")
      }
      if is_packed(cx.decls, cx.src, inner.tys, inner.tyn) {
        panic("selfhost: nested field through a pointer into a @packed struct is not yet supported (word-offset math would mis-address the packed layout)")
      }
      fty := field_type_span(cx.decls, cx.src, inner.tys, inner.tyn, fs, fl, a)
      if fty.n == 0 { return PtrPlace(found = false, ent_idx = 0, tys = 0, tyn = 0, woff = 0) }
      wf := field_word_offset(cx.decls, cx.src, inner.tys, inner.tyn, fs, fl, a)
      if wf < 0 { panic("selfhost: nested field through a pointer — intermediate field not resolvable") }
      return PtrPlace(found = true, ent_idx = inner.ent_idx, tys = fty.s, tyn = fty.n, woff = inner.woff + i64(wf))
    }
    _ => { return ptr_place_root(e, cx) }
  }
}
## Resolve a DEPTH>=2 nested-field READ/WRITE `<ptr-root>.f1…leaf` (`base` a `Field` chain rooted at a
## by-ref struct param or `deref(ek-7 ptr)`), returning the root slot index + the ASCENDING word offset
## to the SCALAR leaf. `found`=false for a non-pointer root (→ `field_slot`) OR a depth-1 base (→ the
## `dsb`/`bref`/`p7` arms). Fails LOUD on a MULTI-WORD leaf / @packed / unresolvable field.
resolve_nested_ptr_field := fn(base : ptr(Expr), fs : usize, fl : usize, cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> PtrPlace {
  a := deref(cx.mar)
  mut nf := PtrPlace(found = false, ent_idx = 0, tys = 0, tyn = 0, woff = 0)
  ## depth>=2 only: `base` must itself be a `Field` (a `Var`/`Deref` base = depth-1).
  ip := field_place_parts(base)
  if unchecked bitcast(usize, ip.base) == 0 { return nf }
  pl := ptr_place(base, cx)
  if pl.found == false { return nf }
  if struct_decl_of(cx.decls, cx.src, pl.tys, pl.tyn) < 0 { return nf }
  if is_packed(cx.decls, cx.src, pl.tys, pl.tyn) {
    panic("selfhost: nested field through a pointer into a @packed struct is not yet supported (word-offset math would mis-address the packed layout)")
  }
  lft := field_type_span(cx.decls, cx.src, pl.tys, pl.tyn, fs, fl, a)
  if lft.n == 0 { return nf }
  if str_at((cx.src + lft.s), lft.n) == "str" or struct_decl_of(cx.decls, cx.src, lft.s, lft.n) >= 0 or enum_decl_of(cx.decls, cx.src, lft.s, lft.n) >= 0 {
    panic("selfhost: a MULTI-WORD (str/struct/enum) leaf of a nested field through a pointer is not yet supported (bind the inner struct to a local, then read the leaf)")
  }
  wleaf := field_word_offset(cx.decls, cx.src, pl.tys, pl.tyn, fs, fl, a)
  if wleaf < 0 { panic("selfhost: nested field through a pointer — leaf field not resolvable") }
  nf = PtrPlace(found = true, ent_idx = pl.ent_idx, tys = pl.tys, tyn = pl.tyn, woff = pl.woff + i64(wleaf))
  nf
}

## `xs[i].a.arr[j]` — an inner index into an ARRAY FIELD of an array-of-struct ELEMENT, after zero or
## more nested plain-struct fields. `base` (the outer Index's base) is a Field chain rooted at
## `Index(xs, i)`. Carries the array/index exprs of the ELEMENT, the array FIELD's cumulative word-0
## offset within the element, and the field's static length N (for the bounds check). `found`=false when
## `base` is not that shape. Fails LOUD when the shape matches but is unsupported: a non-array field, or
## a MULTI-WORD array-field element (str/struct/enum). Word-sized element only.
ArrFieldElem := struct { found : bool, arr : ptr(Expr), idx : ptr(Expr), woff : i64, elen : i64 }
arr_field_elem := fn(base : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> ArrFieldElem {
  z := unchecked bitcast(ptr(Expr), 0)
  mut res := ArrFieldElem(found = false, arr = z, idx = z, woff = 0, elen = 0)
  ## The existing deep resolver composes every Field hop from the array element to `base`, and carries
  ## the type and cumulative word offset at the terminal place. A plain `Field(Index(xs,i), arr)` is the
  ## one-hop case; `Field(Field(Index(xs,i), a), arr)` and deeper chains use the same result.
  pl := resolve_idx_field_place(base, cx)
  if pl.found == false { return res }
  ae := array_elem_span(cx.src, pl.tys, pl.tyn)
  if ae.n == 0 { panic("selfhost: `xs[i].field[j]` where `field` is not an array is not yet supported (bind the element struct to a local first)") }
  ## A plain STRUCT element is the one aggregate-leaf increment supported by this lane. Packed
  ## structs, enums, strings, tuples, and nested arrays retain the conservative fail-loud behavior:
  ## their byte/variant layout is not the word-address composition used by this path.
  if struct_decl_of(cx.decls, cx.src, ae.s, ae.n) >= 0 {
    if is_packed(cx.decls, cx.src, ae.s, ae.n) {
      panic("selfhost: `xs[i].field[j]` over a @packed aggregate array-field element is not yet supported")
    }
  } else if str_at((cx.src + ae.s), ae.n) == "str" or enum_decl_of(cx.decls, cx.src, ae.s, ae.n) >= 0 or str_at((cx.src + ae.s), 1) == "[" or str_at((cx.src + ae.s), 1) == "(" {
    panic("selfhost: `xs[i].field[j]` with a MULTI-WORD (str/struct/enum) array-field element is not yet supported (bind the element struct to a local first)")
  }
  di := deep_idx_arr(base)
  if di.is_ix == false { return res }
  ## `pl.woff` is the cumulative element→array-field offset, not merely the last field's offset.
  res = ArrFieldElem(found = true, arr = di.arr, idx = di.idx, woff = pl.woff, elen = i64(parse_arr_len(cx.src, pl.tys, pl.tyn)))
  res
}

## Walk a body statement list, binding a slot for every distinct local introduced by an
## `Assign` (and recursing into a `While` body). The params were already bound by the
## caller, so a re-seen name reuses its slot (`bind_slot` no-ops on a duplicate). Threaded
## `in out slots`; `head` is the arena-linked `Stmt` list head (0 = end).
## Bind a STRUCT local: the base name takes slot `off` (recording the struct TYPE span) and
## the struct's `nf` fields occupy slots `off .. off+nf` contiguously — so `nf` anonymous
## filler slots are reserved after the base so no later local overlaps them. Re-assignment
## of an already-bound struct local reuses its base slot (no new reservation).
bind_struct_slot := fn(in out slots : SVec, src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize, ss : usize, sl : usize, nf : usize) {
  ## I11: reject a rebinding whose existing reservation is NARROWER than this one needs (see
  ## `require_reservation`) — the wide store would land on the neighbouring locals' slots.
  require_reservation(ptr(slots), src, s, n, nf + 1)
  existing := slot_of(ptr(slots), src, s, n)
  if existing >= 0 { return }
  ## §4 UP-GROWING: reserve nf field FILLER slots FIRST (below the base), then the base name at the
  ## TOP of the block — so word 0 (= `ent.off`) is the LOWEST address and word k grows UP to it
  ## (`ent.off - k`). Fillers are anonymous (ns = nl = 0 never matches a real name); the base entry's
  ## svec INDEX still equals its `off` (both = top), so `svec_at(slots, ent.off)` stays valid.
  for i in 0..nf {
    fo := svec_len(ptr(slots))
    svec_push(slots, SlotEntry(ns = 0, nl = 0, off = fo, sns = 0, snl = 0, ek = 0, estride = 1, eek = 0, is_ref = false))
  }
  off := svec_len(ptr(slots))
  svec_push(slots, SlotEntry(ns = s, nl = n, off = off, sns = ss, snl = sl, ek = 2, estride = 1, eek = 0, is_ref = false))
}

## Bind an ENUM local: the base name takes slot `off` (recording the enum TYPE span + ek=3)
## and `1 + max_arity` words (discriminant + payload) occupy slots `off .. off+1+max_arity`
## contiguously — `nw` anonymous filler slots are reserved after the base so no later local
## overlaps the payload words. Re-assignment of an already-bound enum local reuses its base.
bind_enum_slot := fn(in out slots : SVec, src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize, ss : usize, sl : usize, nw : usize) {
  ## I11: reject a rebinding whose existing reservation is NARROWER than this one needs (see
  ## `require_reservation`) — the wide store would land on the neighbouring locals' slots.
  require_reservation(ptr(slots), src, s, n, nw + 1)
  existing := slot_of(ptr(slots), src, s, n)
  if existing >= 0 { return }
  ## §4 UP-GROWING: nw payload/disc FILLER slots FIRST, then the base name at the TOP (see bind_struct_slot).
  for i in 0..nw {
    fo := svec_len(ptr(slots))
    svec_push(slots, SlotEntry(ns = 0, nl = 0, off = fo, sns = 0, snl = 0, ek = 0, estride = 1, eek = 0, is_ref = false))
  }
  off := svec_len(ptr(slots))
  svec_push(slots, SlotEntry(ns = s, nl = n, off = off, sns = ss, snl = sl, ek = 3, estride = 1, eek = 0, is_ref = false, tmod_s = 0, tmod_l = 0))
}

## The larger of two word counts (a tiny helper for the str-temp-pool scan below).
imax := fn(x : usize, y : usize) -> usize { if x > y { x } else { y } }

## STRING tier — str-temp-pool SIZING. A str LITERAL passed as a call argument is materialized
## into a reserved 2-word frame block (`emit_arg` / `str_arg_tmp_off`); every str-literal arg of a
## SINGLE call needs its own block (their addresses are live on the stack simultaneously at the
## `call`). So `emit_fn` reserves `scan_str_arg_temps(body)` blocks = the MAX number of str-literal
## arguments over any single call reachable in the function body (statements + the trailing return
## expr). The walk is a structural max over the AST (every call's str-literal-arg count, AND the
## max from each argument's own sub-expression, since an arg may itself be a call). Over-counting is
## harmless frame padding — builtins (`str_eq`/`len`/`byte_at`) read their str operands directly via
## `emit_str_pair`, not the pool, but counting their args too just reserves a couple of unused words.
##
## Count the str-LITERAL arguments of ONE call's arena-linked `Arg` list head `head`.
scan_call_str_args := fn(src : ptr(u8), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), head : ptr(mut Stmt), a : rt::Arena) -> usize {
  mut g := head
  mut cnt := 0
  while g != 0 {
    ga := deref(arg_p(g))
    ## a str LITERAL / `str_at(base, len)` view / `bytes(<literal|str_at>)` argument needs a 2-word
    ## temp block (no frame home → materialized + passed by reference, see `emit_arg`).
    if arg_str_temp(ga.e, src, decls, a) { cnt = cnt + 1 }
    g = ga.next
  }
  cnt
}

## The max str-literal-argument count of any single call within expression `e` (including `e`
## itself if it is a `Call`, and recursing into every sub-expression / argument).
scan_str_arg_expr := fn(src : ptr(u8), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), e : ptr(Expr), a : rt::Arena) -> usize {
  mut m := 0
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Bin(op, l, r) => { m = imax(scan_str_arg_expr(src, decls, l, a), scan_str_arg_expr(src, decls, r, a)) }
    Expr::If(c, t, f) => { m = imax(scan_str_arg_expr(src, decls, c, a), imax(scan_str_arg_expr(src, decls, t, a), scan_str_arg_expr(src, decls, f, a))) }
    Expr::Match(scrut, head) => {
      m = scan_str_arg_expr(src, decls, scrut, a)
      mut arm := head
      while arm != 0 {
        am := deref(arm_p(arm))
        m = imax(m, scan_str_arg_expr(src, decls, am.body, a))
        m = imax(m, scan_str_arg_stmts(src, decls, am.body_stmts, a))
        arm = am.next
      }
    }
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, args_head) => {
      m = scan_call_str_args(src, decls, args_head, a)
      mut g := args_head
      while g != 0 {
        ga := deref(arg_p(g))
        m = imax(m, scan_str_arg_expr(src, decls, ga.e, a))
        g = ga.next
      }
    }
    Expr::StructLit(cs, cl, nf, fhead) => {
      mut g := fhead
      while g != 0 {
        ga := deref(arg_p(g))
        m = imax(m, scan_str_arg_expr(src, decls, ga.e, a))
        g = ga.next
      }
    }
    Expr::Field(base, fs, fl) => { m = scan_str_arg_expr(src, decls, base, a) }
    Expr::EnumLit(es, el, vs, vl, np, phead) => {
      mut g := phead
      while g != 0 {
        ga := deref(arg_p(g))
        m = imax(m, scan_str_arg_expr(src, decls, ga.e, a))
        g = ga.next
      }
    }
    Expr::AddrOf(p) => { m = scan_str_arg_expr(src, decls, p, a) }
    Expr::Deref(p) => { m = scan_str_arg_expr(src, decls, p, a) }
    Expr::ArrayLit(nel, ehead) => {
      mut g := ehead
      while g != 0 {
        ga := deref(arg_p(g))
        m = imax(m, scan_str_arg_expr(src, decls, ga.e, a))
        g = ga.next
      }
    }
    Expr::Index(base, idx) => { m = imax(scan_str_arg_expr(src, decls, base, a), scan_str_arg_expr(src, decls, idx, a)) }
    Expr::Try(inner) => { m = scan_str_arg_expr(src, decls, inner, a) }
    Expr::Unchecked(inner) => { m = scan_str_arg_expr(src, decls, inner, a) }
    Expr::Bitcast(inner, _bcs, _bcl) => { m = scan_str_arg_expr(src, decls, inner, a) }
    _ => {}
  }
  m
}

## The max str-literal-argument count of any single call within a statement list `head`.
scan_str_arg_stmts := fn(src : ptr(u8), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), head : ptr(mut Stmt), a : rt::Arena) -> usize {
  mut s := head
  mut m := 0
  while s != 0 {
    st := deref(stmt_p(Stmt, s))
    match st {
      Stmt::Assign(ns, nl, v, nx) => { m = imax(m, scan_str_arg_expr(src, decls, v, a)); s = nx }
      Stmt::While(c, b, nx) => { m = imax(m, imax(scan_str_arg_expr(src, decls, c, a), scan_str_arg_stmts(src, decls, b, a))); s = nx }
      Stmt::Loop(b, nx) => { m = imax(m, scan_str_arg_stmts(src, decls, b, a)); s = nx }
      Stmt::Unchecked(b, nx) => { m = imax(m, scan_str_arg_stmts(src, decls, b, a)); s = nx }
      Stmt::AllocWith(ae, b, nx) => { m = imax(m, scan_str_arg_stmts(src, decls, b, a)); s = nx }
      Stmt::Break(_bv, _bd, nx) => { s = nx }
      Stmt::Continue(_cd, nx) => { s = nx }
      Stmt::ExprStmt(e, nx) => { m = imax(m, scan_str_arg_expr(src, decls, e, a)); s = nx }
      Stmt::FieldAssign(bns, bnl, fns, fnl, fv, nx) => { m = imax(m, scan_str_arg_expr(src, decls, fv, a)); s = nx }
      Stmt::FieldPathAssign(pl, fpv, nx) => { m = imax(m, scan_str_arg_expr(src, decls, fpv, a)); s = nx }
      Stmt::Return(rv, nx) => { m = imax(m, scan_str_arg_expr(src, decls, rv, a)); s = nx }
      Stmt::DerefAssign(ptr, val, nx) => { m = imax(m, imax(scan_str_arg_expr(src, decls, ptr, a), scan_str_arg_expr(src, decls, val, a))); s = nx }
      Stmt::IndexAssign(ib, ii, iv, nx) => { m = imax(m, imax(scan_str_arg_expr(src, decls, ib, a), imax(scan_str_arg_expr(src, decls, ii, a), scan_str_arg_expr(src, decls, iv, a)))); s = nx }
      Stmt::IndexFieldAssign(fia, fii, ifs, ifl, fiv, nx) => { m = imax(m, imax(scan_str_arg_expr(src, decls, fia, a), imax(scan_str_arg_expr(src, decls, fii, a), scan_str_arg_expr(src, decls, fiv, a)))); s = nx }
      Stmt::If(c, th, el, nx) => { m = imax(m, imax(scan_str_arg_expr(src, decls, c, a), imax(scan_str_arg_stmts(src, decls, th, a), scan_str_arg_stmts(src, decls, el, a)))); s = nx }
      Stmt::Match(sc, ah, nx) => {
        m = imax(m, scan_str_arg_expr(src, decls, sc, a))
        mut arm := ah
        while arm != 0 {
          am := deref(arm_p(arm))
          m = imax(m, scan_str_arg_stmts(src, decls, am.body_stmts, a))
          arm = am.next
        }
        s = nx
      }
      Stmt::For(fns, fnl, flo, fhi, fb, nx) => { m = imax(m, imax(scan_str_arg_expr(src, decls, flo, a), scan_str_arg_stmts(src, decls, fb, a))); if unchecked bitcast(usize, fhi) != 0 { m = imax(m, scan_str_arg_expr(src, decls, fhi, a)) } ; s = nx }
      Stmt::CompIf(ccond, cthen, celse, nx) => { m = imax(m, imax(scan_str_arg_stmts(src, decls, cthen, a), scan_str_arg_stmts(src, decls, celse, a))); s = nx }
      Stmt::CompFor(cvs, cvl, civ, cb, nx) => { m = imax(m, scan_str_arg_stmts(src, decls, cb, a)); s = nx }
      Stmt::CompForRange(rvs, rvl, rlo, rhi, rb, nx) => { m = imax(m, scan_str_arg_stmts(src, decls, rb, a)); s = nx }
      Stmt::CompMatch(cmsc, cmah, nx) => { mut car : usize = cmah; while car != 0 { cam := deref(arm_p(car)); m = imax(m, scan_str_arg_stmts(src, decls, cam.body_stmts, a)); car = cam.next } ; s = nx }
    }
  }
  m
}

## Is `e` a Var naming a MUTABLE-GLOBAL aggregate (struct/array)? `emit_arg` copies such a global into the
## agg-temp pool before passing it by-ref (a global has no frame home), so it also consumes a pool slice.
is_global_agg_arg := fn(e : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> bool {
  vn := var_name_span(e)
  if vn.n == 0 { return false }
  gv := mut_global_value(decls, src, vn.s, vn.n)
  if unchecked bitcast(usize, gv) == 0 { return false }
  struct_lit_info(gv).is_s or array_lit_info(gv).is_a
}
## Is `e` an aggregate-VALUE call argument — a struct/enum/tuple LITERAL, a struct-RETURNING call, or a
## mutable-global aggregate Var — that `emit_arg` materializes into the agg-temp pool and passes by
## reference (Functions §4 ABI)? Each such arg consumes one distinct pool slice.
## `sret_ret_call` is counted alongside `struct_ret_call`: a WIDE (> 7-word) struct-returning call arg
## also materializes into a pool slice (it is the callee's hidden-result-pointer destination), so two of
## them in one call (`two(mk(), mk())`) need two DISTINCT blocks. Without it `aggpeak` stayed 0 and the
## single reserved block overflowed (a loud abort). `src/`+`lib/` pass no such arg → fixpoint-neutral.
## `enum_sret_ret_call` is counted for the SAME reason as `sret_ret_call`: a WIDE (disc + payload >
## 7-word) enum-returning call arg also materializes into a pool slice (it is the callee's hidden-result-
## pointer destination), so two of them in one call need two DISTINCT blocks. `src/`+`lib/` declare no
## wide-enum-returning fn → `aggpeak` is unchanged there → fixpoint-neutral.
arg_is_agg_value := fn(e : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> bool {
  require_agg_blocks(e, decls, src, a) != 0 or struct_lit_info(e).is_s or enum_lit_info(e).is_e or array_lit_info(e).is_a or struct_ret_call(e, decls, src, a) or sret_ret_call(e, decls, src, a) or enum_sret_ret_call(e, decls, src, a) or fixed_array_byte_return_len(e, decls, src, a) >= 1 or is_global_agg_arg(e, decls, src, a)
}

## WIDTH of one expression that can be MATERIALIZED into the aggregate-value pool. This is deliberately
## separate from `scan_agg_arg_*`: the latter answers HOW MANY blocks must be live (including nested-call
## depth), while this answers the width of EACH block. Keeping the two measurements separate avoids the
## old program-global upper bound and preserves the existing fail-loud block-count fence.
agg_value_words := fn(e : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> usize {
  ra := require_agg_parts(e, decls, src, a)
  if ra.ok {
    w := require_agg_words(ra.under, decls, src, a)
    if w == 0 { panic("selfhost: aggregate-value scratch width could not resolve checked aggregate type") }
    return w
  }
  sli := struct_lit_info(e)
  if sli.is_s {
    mut w := struct_words(decls, src, sli.ss, sli.sl, a)
    if w == 0 { w = 1 }
    return w
  }
  eli := enum_lit_info(e)
  if eli.is_e { return 1 + enum_inst_words(decls, src, eli.es, eli.el, a) }
  ali := array_lit_info(e)
  if ali.is_a {
    mut w := 0
    mut g := ali.ehead
    while g != 0 {
      ga := deref(arg_p(g))
      mut ew := agg_value_words(ga.e, decls, src, a)
      if ew == 0 { ew = 1 }
      w += ew
      g = ga.next
    }
    if w == 0 { w = 1 }
    return w
  }
  if fixed_array_byte_return_len(e, decls, src, a) >= 1 { return 1 }
  if struct_ret_call(e, decls, src, a) or sret_ret_call(e, decls, src, a) {
    cs := call_ret_struct_span(e, decls, src, a)
    w := struct_words(decls, src, cs.s, cs.n, a)
    if w == 0 { panic("selfhost: aggregate-value scratch width could not resolve struct-return call") }
    return w
  }
  if enum_sret_ret_call(e, decls, src, a) or enum_ret_call_d(e, decls, src, a) {
    es := call_ret_enum_span_d(e, decls, src, a)
    if es.n == 0 { panic("selfhost: aggregate-value scratch width could not resolve enum-return call") }
    return 1 + enum_inst_words(decls, src, es.s, es.n, a)
  }
  if str_ret_call(e, decls, src, a) { return 2 }
  if is_global_agg_arg(e, decls, src, a) {
    vn := var_name_span(e)
    gv := mut_global_value(decls, src, vn.s, vn.n)
    gs := struct_lit_info(gv)
    if gs.is_s { return struct_words(decls, src, gs.ss, gs.sl, a) }
    gai := array_lit_info(gv)
    if gai.is_a {
      mut w := 0
      mut gg := gai.ehead
      while gg != 0 { ge := deref(arg_p(gg)); mut ew := agg_value_words(ge.e, decls, src, a); if ew == 0 { ew = 1 }; w += ew; gg = ge.next }
      if w != 0 { return w }
    }
    panic("selfhost: mutable-global aggregate scratch width could not be resolved")
  }
  0
}

## Maximum aggregate-temp BLOCK WIDTH in one function's own body/value tree. A direct struct/enum/array
## literal is included because the same expression may be a call argument, a branch value, or a whole-value
## mutable-global store; over-reserving that local width is safe, but a declaration elsewhere in the program
## is intentionally invisible here. Mutable-global Vars are counted only at argument sites through
## `agg_value_words`, so merely reading a global does not tax the frame.
scan_agg_width_expr := fn(e : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> usize {
  mut m := 0
  if arg_is_agg_value(e, decls, src, a) or str_ret_call(e, decls, src, a) { m = agg_value_words(e, decls, src, a) }
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Bin(op, l, r) => { m = imax(m, imax(scan_agg_width_expr(l, decls, src, a), scan_agg_width_expr(r, decls, src, a))) }
    Expr::If(c, t, f) => { m = imax(m, imax(scan_agg_width_expr(c, decls, src, a), imax(scan_agg_width_expr(t, decls, src, a), scan_agg_width_expr(f, decls, src, a)))) }
    Expr::Match(scrut, head) => {
      m = imax(m, scan_agg_width_expr(scrut, decls, src, a))
      mut arm := head
      while arm != 0 { am := deref(arm_p(arm)); m = imax(m, scan_agg_width_expr(am.body, decls, src, a)); m = imax(m, scan_agg_width_stmts(am.body_stmts, decls, src, a)); arm = am.next }
    }
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, args_head) => { mut g := args_head; while g != 0 { ga := deref(arg_p(g)); m = imax(m, scan_agg_width_expr(ga.e, decls, src, a)); g = ga.next } }
    Expr::StructLit(cs, cl, nf, fhead) => { mut g := fhead; while g != 0 { ga := deref(arg_p(g)); m = imax(m, scan_agg_width_expr(ga.e, decls, src, a)); g = ga.next } }
    Expr::Field(base, fs, fl) => { m = imax(m, scan_agg_width_expr(base, decls, src, a)) }
    Expr::EnumLit(es, el, vs, vl, np, phead) => { mut g := phead; while g != 0 { ga := deref(arg_p(g)); m = imax(m, scan_agg_width_expr(ga.e, decls, src, a)); g = ga.next } }
    Expr::AddrOf(p) => { m = imax(m, scan_agg_width_expr(p, decls, src, a)) }
    Expr::Deref(p) => { m = imax(m, scan_agg_width_expr(p, decls, src, a)) }
    Expr::ArrayLit(nel, ehead) => { mut g := ehead; while g != 0 { ga := deref(arg_p(g)); m = imax(m, scan_agg_width_expr(ga.e, decls, src, a)); g = ga.next } }
    Expr::Index(base, idx) => { m = imax(m, imax(scan_agg_width_expr(base, decls, src, a), scan_agg_width_expr(idx, decls, src, a))) }
    Expr::Try(inner) => { m = imax(m, scan_agg_width_expr(inner, decls, src, a)) }
    Expr::Unchecked(inner) => { m = imax(m, scan_agg_width_expr(inner, decls, src, a)) }
    Expr::Bitcast(inner, _bcs, _bcl) => { m = imax(m, scan_agg_width_expr(inner, decls, src, a)) }
    _ => {}
  }
  m
}

scan_agg_width_stmts := fn(head : ptr(mut Stmt), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> usize {
  mut s := head
  mut m := 0
  while s != 0 {
    st := deref(stmt_p(Stmt, s))
    match st {
      Stmt::Assign(ns, nl, v, nx) => { m = imax(m, scan_agg_width_expr(v, decls, src, a)); s = nx }
      Stmt::While(c, b, nx) => { m = imax(m, imax(scan_agg_width_expr(c, decls, src, a), scan_agg_width_stmts(b, decls, src, a))); s = nx }
      Stmt::Loop(b, nx) => { m = imax(m, scan_agg_width_stmts(b, decls, src, a)); s = nx }
      Stmt::Unchecked(b, nx) => { m = imax(m, scan_agg_width_stmts(b, decls, src, a)); s = nx }
      Stmt::AllocWith(ae, b, nx) => { m = imax(m, imax(scan_agg_width_expr(ae, decls, src, a), scan_agg_width_stmts(b, decls, src, a))); s = nx }
      Stmt::Break(_bv, _bd, nx) => { s = nx }
      Stmt::Continue(_cd, nx) => { s = nx }
      Stmt::ExprStmt(e, nx) => { m = imax(m, scan_agg_width_expr(e, decls, src, a)); s = nx }
      Stmt::FieldAssign(bns, bnl, fns, fnl, fv, nx) => { m = imax(m, scan_agg_width_expr(fv, decls, src, a)); s = nx }
      Stmt::FieldPathAssign(pl, fpv, nx) => { m = imax(m, scan_agg_width_expr(fpv, decls, src, a)); s = nx }
      Stmt::Return(rv, nx) => { m = imax(m, scan_agg_width_expr(rv, decls, src, a)); s = nx }
      Stmt::DerefAssign(ptr, val, nx) => { m = imax(m, imax(scan_agg_width_expr(ptr, decls, src, a), scan_agg_width_expr(val, decls, src, a))); s = nx }
      Stmt::IndexAssign(ib, ii, iv, nx) => { m = imax(m, imax(scan_agg_width_expr(ib, decls, src, a), imax(scan_agg_width_expr(ii, decls, src, a), scan_agg_width_expr(iv, decls, src, a)))); s = nx }
      Stmt::IndexFieldAssign(fia, fii, ifs, ifl, fiv, nx) => { m = imax(m, imax(scan_agg_width_expr(fia, decls, src, a), imax(scan_agg_width_expr(fii, decls, src, a), scan_agg_width_expr(fiv, decls, src, a)))); s = nx }
      Stmt::If(c, th, el, nx) => { m = imax(m, imax(scan_agg_width_expr(c, decls, src, a), imax(scan_agg_width_stmts(th, decls, src, a), scan_agg_width_stmts(el, decls, src, a)))); s = nx }
      Stmt::Match(sc, ah, nx) => { m = imax(m, scan_agg_width_expr(sc, decls, src, a)); mut arm := ah; while arm != 0 { am := deref(arm_p(arm)); m = imax(m, scan_agg_width_stmts(am.body_stmts, decls, src, a)); arm = am.next }; s = nx }
      Stmt::For(fns, fnl, flo, fhi, fb, nx) => { m = imax(m, imax(scan_agg_width_expr(flo, decls, src, a), scan_agg_width_stmts(fb, decls, src, a))); if unchecked bitcast(usize, fhi) != 0 { m = imax(m, scan_agg_width_expr(fhi, decls, src, a)) }; s = nx }
      Stmt::CompIf(ccond, cthen, celse, nx) => { m = imax(m, imax(scan_agg_width_stmts(cthen, decls, src, a), scan_agg_width_stmts(celse, decls, src, a))); s = nx }
      Stmt::CompFor(cvs, cvl, civ, cb, nx) => { m = imax(m, scan_agg_width_stmts(cb, decls, src, a)); s = nx }
      Stmt::CompForRange(rvs, rvl, rlo, rhi, rb, nx) => { m = imax(m, scan_agg_width_stmts(rb, decls, src, a)); s = nx }
      Stmt::CompMatch(cmsc, cmah, nx) => { mut car : usize = cmah; while car != 0 { cam := deref(arm_p(car)); m = imax(m, scan_agg_width_stmts(cam.body_stmts, decls, src, a)); car = cam.next }; s = nx }
    }
  }
  m
}
## Count the aggregate-VALUE arguments of ONE call's arg list — each needs a distinct agg-temp slice
## (N in one call → N slices; sharing one slot aliases them, a §8 miscompile — see `emit_call_args`).
scan_call_agg_args := fn(src : ptr(u8), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), head : ptr(mut Stmt), a : rt::Arena) -> usize {
  mut g := head
  mut cnt := 0
  while g != 0 {
    ga := deref(arg_p(g))
    rb := require_agg_blocks(ga.e, decls, src, a)
    if rb != 0 { cnt = cnt + rb }
    else if arg_is_agg_value(ga.e, decls, src, a) { cnt = cnt + 1 }
    g = ga.next
  }
  cnt
}
## The max aggregate-VALUE-argument count of any single call within expr `e` — the structural twin of
## `scan_str_arg_expr`. Drives the agg-temp POOL reservation (`aggpeak` blocks). A flat max (not a
## nesting sum): deep agg-nesting that would exceed the reservation is caught by `emit_arg`'s defensive
## overflow panic (sound — a loud abort, never a silent miscompile), not by over-reserving here.
scan_agg_arg_expr := fn(src : ptr(u8), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), e : ptr(Expr), a : rt::Arena) -> usize {
  mut m := 0
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Bin(op, l, r) => { m = imax(scan_agg_arg_expr(src, decls, l, a), scan_agg_arg_expr(src, decls, r, a)) }
    Expr::If(c, t, f) => { m = imax(scan_agg_arg_expr(src, decls, c, a), imax(scan_agg_arg_expr(src, decls, t, a), scan_agg_arg_expr(src, decls, f, a))) }
    Expr::Match(scrut, head) => {
      m = scan_agg_arg_expr(src, decls, scrut, a)
      mut arm := head
      while arm != 0 {
        am := deref(arm_p(arm))
        m = imax(m, scan_agg_arg_expr(src, decls, am.body, a))
        m = imax(m, scan_agg_arg_stmts(src, decls, am.body_stmts, a))
        arm = am.next
      }
    }
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, args_head) => {
      ## Types §9.4 — NESTING SUMS, it does not max. `emit_call_args` saves the bump pointer and a
      ## NESTED call's args "stack ABOVE this call's still-live slices", so `sumf(bump(mk(1)))` needs
      ## `own (1, for bump's result) + the deepest arg subtree (1, for mk's result)` = 2 blocks. The old
      ## flat `imax` reserved 1 and `agg_alloc` aborted loudly ("call-arg temp pool overflow") on a
      ## perfectly ordinary nested wide-SRET call — aarch64 already compiles+runs the same program
      ## because `a64_aggval_words_e` counts TREE-WIDE. Identical whenever either term is 0 (a call with
      ## no aggregate-value arg, or one whose args hold no further aggregate-value call).
      mut own := scan_call_agg_args(src, decls, args_head, a)
      mut deepest := 0
      mut g := args_head
      while g != 0 { ga := deref(arg_p(g)); deepest = imax(deepest, scan_agg_arg_expr(src, decls, ga.e, a)); g = ga.next }
      ## A require call itself consumes its preserved-result block plus its predicate-copy block. The
      ## ordinary recursive walk still sees the source constructor as a nested aggregate expression and
      ## may over-reserve one block; over-reservation is harmless, under-reservation would alias the
      ## value with the predicate and violate §8.1.
      reqb := require_agg_blocks(e, decls, src, a)
      if reqb > own { own = reqb }
      m = own + deepest
    }
    Expr::StructLit(cs, cl, nf, fhead) => { mut g := fhead; while g != 0 { ga := deref(arg_p(g)); m = imax(m, scan_agg_arg_expr(src, decls, ga.e, a)); g = ga.next } }
    Expr::Field(base, fs, fl) => { m = scan_agg_arg_expr(src, decls, base, a) }
    Expr::EnumLit(es, el, vs, vl, np, phead) => { mut g := phead; while g != 0 { ga := deref(arg_p(g)); m = imax(m, scan_agg_arg_expr(src, decls, ga.e, a)); g = ga.next } }
    Expr::AddrOf(p) => { m = scan_agg_arg_expr(src, decls, p, a) }
    Expr::Deref(p) => { m = scan_agg_arg_expr(src, decls, p, a) }
    Expr::ArrayLit(nel, ehead) => { mut g := ehead; while g != 0 { ga := deref(arg_p(g)); m = imax(m, scan_agg_arg_expr(src, decls, ga.e, a)); g = ga.next } }
    Expr::Index(base, idx) => { m = imax(scan_agg_arg_expr(src, decls, base, a), scan_agg_arg_expr(src, decls, idx, a)) }
    Expr::Try(inner) => { m = scan_agg_arg_expr(src, decls, inner, a) }
    Expr::Unchecked(inner) => { m = scan_agg_arg_expr(src, decls, inner, a) }
    Expr::Bitcast(inner, _bcs, _bcl) => { m = scan_agg_arg_expr(src, decls, inner, a) }
    _ => {}
  }
  m
}
## The max aggregate-value-argument count of any single call within a statement list `head`.
scan_agg_arg_stmts := fn(src : ptr(u8), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), head : ptr(mut Stmt), a : rt::Arena) -> usize {
  mut s := head
  mut m := 0
  while s != 0 {
    st := deref(stmt_p(Stmt, s))
    match st {
      Stmt::Assign(ns, nl, v, nx) => { m = imax(m, scan_agg_arg_expr(src, decls, v, a)); s = nx }
      Stmt::While(c, b, nx) => { m = imax(m, imax(scan_agg_arg_expr(src, decls, c, a), scan_agg_arg_stmts(src, decls, b, a))); s = nx }
      Stmt::Loop(b, nx) => { m = imax(m, scan_agg_arg_stmts(src, decls, b, a)); s = nx }
      Stmt::Unchecked(b, nx) => { m = imax(m, scan_agg_arg_stmts(src, decls, b, a)); s = nx }
      Stmt::AllocWith(ae, b, nx) => { m = imax(m, scan_agg_arg_stmts(src, decls, b, a)); s = nx }
      Stmt::Break(_bv, _bd, nx) => { s = nx }
      Stmt::Continue(_cd, nx) => { s = nx }
      Stmt::ExprStmt(e, nx) => { m = imax(m, scan_agg_arg_expr(src, decls, e, a)); s = nx }
      Stmt::FieldAssign(bns, bnl, fns, fnl, fv, nx) => { m = imax(m, scan_agg_arg_expr(src, decls, fv, a)); s = nx }
      Stmt::FieldPathAssign(pl, fpv, nx) => { m = imax(m, scan_agg_arg_expr(src, decls, fpv, a)); s = nx }
      Stmt::Return(rv, nx) => { m = imax(m, scan_agg_arg_expr(src, decls, rv, a)); s = nx }
      Stmt::DerefAssign(ptr, val, nx) => { m = imax(m, imax(scan_agg_arg_expr(src, decls, ptr, a), scan_agg_arg_expr(src, decls, val, a))); s = nx }
      Stmt::IndexAssign(ib, ii, iv, nx) => { m = imax(m, imax(scan_agg_arg_expr(src, decls, ib, a), imax(scan_agg_arg_expr(src, decls, ii, a), scan_agg_arg_expr(src, decls, iv, a)))); s = nx }
      Stmt::IndexFieldAssign(fia, fii, ifs, ifl, fiv, nx) => { m = imax(m, imax(scan_agg_arg_expr(src, decls, fia, a), imax(scan_agg_arg_expr(src, decls, fii, a), scan_agg_arg_expr(src, decls, fiv, a)))); s = nx }
      Stmt::If(c, th, el, nx) => { m = imax(m, imax(scan_agg_arg_expr(src, decls, c, a), imax(scan_agg_arg_stmts(src, decls, th, a), scan_agg_arg_stmts(src, decls, el, a)))); s = nx }
      Stmt::Match(sc, ah, nx) => {
        m = imax(m, scan_agg_arg_expr(src, decls, sc, a))
        mut arm := ah
        while arm != 0 {
          am := deref(arm_p(arm))
          m = imax(m, scan_agg_arg_stmts(src, decls, am.body_stmts, a))
          arm = am.next
        }
        s = nx
      }
      Stmt::For(fns, fnl, flo, fhi, fb, nx) => { m = imax(m, imax(scan_agg_arg_expr(src, decls, flo, a), scan_agg_arg_stmts(src, decls, fb, a))); if unchecked bitcast(usize, fhi) != 0 { m = imax(m, scan_agg_arg_expr(src, decls, fhi, a)) } ; s = nx }
      Stmt::CompIf(ccond, cthen, celse, nx) => { m = imax(m, imax(scan_agg_arg_stmts(src, decls, cthen, a), scan_agg_arg_stmts(src, decls, celse, a))); s = nx }
      Stmt::CompFor(cvs, cvl, civ, cb, nx) => { m = imax(m, scan_agg_arg_stmts(src, decls, cb, a)); s = nx }
      Stmt::CompForRange(rvs, rvl, rlo, rhi, rb, nx) => { m = imax(m, scan_agg_arg_stmts(src, decls, rb, a)); s = nx }
      Stmt::CompMatch(cmsc, cmah, nx) => { mut car : usize = cmah; while car != 0 { cam := deref(arm_p(car)); m = imax(m, scan_agg_arg_stmts(src, decls, cam.body_stmts, a)); car = cam.next } ; s = nx }
    }
  }
  m
}

## The maximum runtime `match`-nesting DEPTH of an expression / statement list — how many `match`
## bodies can be simultaneously live along one path. A match on a by-ref/call scrutinee materializes
## into a `swidth`-word scratch level; nesting `d` deep needs `d` levels reserved so an inner match
## never clobbers an outer scrutinee still aliased by its arm binding. Counts EVERY runtime `Match` as
## a level (overcounting a local-var match — which needs no scratch — is safe: just extra frame words).
## A COMPTIME `CompMatch` folds to one arm at compile time (no runtime scrutinee) → NOT a level.
## Structural twin of `scan_str_arg_expr`/`scan_str_arg_stmts`; the `Match` arm returns `1 + inner`.
match_depth_expr := fn(e : ptr(Expr), a : rt::Arena) -> usize {
  mut m := 0
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Bin(op, l, r) => { m = imax(match_depth_expr(l, a), match_depth_expr(r, a)) }
    Expr::If(c, t, f) => { m = imax(match_depth_expr(c, a), imax(match_depth_expr(t, a), match_depth_expr(f, a))) }
    Expr::Match(scrut, head) => {
      mut inner := match_depth_expr(scrut, a)
      mut arm := head
      while arm != 0 {
        am := deref(arm_p(arm))
        inner = imax(inner, match_depth_expr(am.body, a))
        inner = imax(inner, match_depth_stmts(am.body_stmts, a))
        arm = am.next
      }
      m = 1 + inner
    }
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, args_head) => { mut g : usize = args_head; while g != 0 { ga := deref(arg_p(g)); m = imax(m, match_depth_expr(ga.e, a)); g = ga.next } }
    Expr::StructLit(cs, cl, nf, fhead) => { mut g : usize = fhead; while g != 0 { ga := deref(arg_p(g)); m = imax(m, match_depth_expr(ga.e, a)); g = ga.next } }
    Expr::Field(base, fs, fl) => { m = match_depth_expr(base, a) }
    Expr::EnumLit(es, el, vs, vl, np, phead) => { mut g : usize = phead; while g != 0 { ga := deref(arg_p(g)); m = imax(m, match_depth_expr(ga.e, a)); g = ga.next } }
    Expr::AddrOf(p) => { m = match_depth_expr(p, a) }
    Expr::Deref(p) => { m = match_depth_expr(p, a) }
    Expr::ArrayLit(nel, ehead) => { mut g : usize = ehead; while g != 0 { ga := deref(arg_p(g)); m = imax(m, match_depth_expr(ga.e, a)); g = ga.next } }
    Expr::Index(base, idx) => { m = imax(match_depth_expr(base, a), match_depth_expr(idx, a)) }
    Expr::Try(inner) => { m = match_depth_expr(inner, a) }
    Expr::Unchecked(inner) => { m = match_depth_expr(inner, a) }
    Expr::Bitcast(inner, _bcs, _bcl) => { m = match_depth_expr(inner, a) }
    _ => {}
  }
  m
}

match_depth_stmts := fn(head : ptr(mut Stmt), a : rt::Arena) -> usize {
  mut s := head
  mut m := 0
  while s != 0 {
    st := deref(stmt_p(Stmt, s))
    match st {
      Stmt::Assign(ns, nl, v, nx) => { m = imax(m, match_depth_expr(v, a)); s = nx }
      Stmt::While(c, b, nx) => { m = imax(m, imax(match_depth_expr(c, a), match_depth_stmts(b, a))); s = nx }
      Stmt::Loop(b, nx) => { m = imax(m, match_depth_stmts(b, a)); s = nx }
      Stmt::Unchecked(b, nx) => { m = imax(m, match_depth_stmts(b, a)); s = nx }
      Stmt::AllocWith(ae, b, nx) => { m = imax(m, match_depth_stmts(b, a)); s = nx }
      Stmt::Break(_bv, _bd, nx) => { s = nx }
      Stmt::Continue(_cd, nx) => { s = nx }
      Stmt::ExprStmt(e, nx) => { m = imax(m, match_depth_expr(e, a)); s = nx }
      Stmt::FieldAssign(bns, bnl, fns, fnl, fv, nx) => { m = imax(m, match_depth_expr(fv, a)); s = nx }
      Stmt::FieldPathAssign(pl, fpv, nx) => { m = imax(m, match_depth_expr(fpv, a)); s = nx }
      Stmt::Return(rv, nx) => { m = imax(m, match_depth_expr(rv, a)); s = nx }
      Stmt::DerefAssign(pe, val, nx) => { m = imax(m, imax(match_depth_expr(pe, a), match_depth_expr(val, a))); s = nx }
      Stmt::IndexAssign(ib, ii, iv, nx) => { m = imax(m, imax(match_depth_expr(ib, a), imax(match_depth_expr(ii, a), match_depth_expr(iv, a)))); s = nx }
      Stmt::IndexFieldAssign(fia, fii, ifs, ifl, fiv, nx) => { m = imax(m, imax(match_depth_expr(fia, a), imax(match_depth_expr(fii, a), match_depth_expr(fiv, a)))); s = nx }
      Stmt::If(c, th, el, nx) => { m = imax(m, imax(match_depth_expr(c, a), imax(match_depth_stmts(th, a), match_depth_stmts(el, a)))); s = nx }
      Stmt::Match(sc, ah, nx) => {
        mut inner := match_depth_expr(sc, a)
        mut arm := ah
        while arm != 0 {
          am := deref(arm_p(arm))
          inner = imax(inner, match_depth_expr(am.body, a))
          inner = imax(inner, match_depth_stmts(am.body_stmts, a))
          arm = am.next
        }
        m = imax(m, 1 + inner)
        s = nx
      }
      Stmt::For(fns, fnl, flo, fhi, fb, nx) => { m = imax(m, imax(match_depth_expr(flo, a), match_depth_stmts(fb, a))); if unchecked bitcast(usize, fhi) != 0 { m = imax(m, match_depth_expr(fhi, a)) } ; s = nx }
      Stmt::CompIf(ccond, cthen, celse, nx) => { m = imax(m, imax(match_depth_stmts(cthen, a), match_depth_stmts(celse, a))); s = nx }
      Stmt::CompFor(cvs, cvl, civ, cb, nx) => { m = imax(m, match_depth_stmts(cb, a)); s = nx }
      Stmt::CompForRange(rvs, rvl, rlo, rhi, rb, nx) => { m = imax(m, match_depth_stmts(rb, a)); s = nx }
      Stmt::CompMatch(cmsc, cmah, nx) => { mut car : usize = cmah; while car != 0 { cam := deref(arm_p(car)); m = imax(m, match_depth_stmts(cam.body_stmts, a)); car = cam.next } ; s = nx }
    }
  }
  m
}

## The inline-frame WIDTH of an `@inline` callee (decl `ci`) — how many scratch slots its expansion
## needs: its PARAM count (each param binds one arg slot). (Callee body-LOCALS are added when the
## statement-body inliner lands; for now a tail-expression `@inline` body uses only params.)
inline_callee_pcount := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), ci : i64, a : rt::Arena) -> usize {
  d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(ci))))
  mut c := 0
  mut p := d.params_head
  while p != 0 { pm := deref(param_p(p)); c = c + 1; p = pm.next }
  c
}
## The WORD width of a parameter's type: a user struct/enum spans several words (its layout);
## a scalar/float/ptr is one. Drives the inline-scratch layout for multi-word aggregate operands.
param_words := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), pm : Param, a : rt::Arena) -> usize {
  if str_at((src + pm.ts), pm.tl) == "str" { return 2 }
  bn := base_type_name(src, pm.ts, pm.tl)
  if bn.n != 0 and struct_decl_of(decls, src, bn.s, bn.n) >= 0 { return struct_words(decls, src, bn.s, bn.n, a) }
  if bn.n != 0 and enum_decl_of(decls, src, bn.s, bn.n) >= 0 { return 1 + enum_inst_words(decls, src, bn.s, bn.n, a) }
  1
}
## The total WORD count of an `@inline` callee's parameters (sum of `param_words`) — the inline-scratch
## reservation must hold every param's words, not just one per param. For an all-scalar callee (num.al's
## operators, every `@inline` call in `src/`) this equals the param count, so `src/` stays byte-identical.
## The total WORD count of an `@inline` callee's parameters (sum of `param_words`) — the inline-scratch
## reservation must hold every param's words, not just one per param. For an all-scalar callee (num.al's
## operators, every `@inline` call in `src/`) this equals the param count, so `src/` stays byte-identical.
## A COMPTIME VALUE parameter (TYP-10 slice B) contributes NOTHING — it is bound at expansion time,
## never delivered on the value stack.
inline_callee_pwords := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), ci : i64, a : rt::Arena) -> usize {
  d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(ci))))
  mut c := 0
  mut p := d.params_head
  while p != 0 { pm := deref(param_p(p)); if param_is_comptime(src, pm.ns) == false { c = c + param_words(decls, src, pm, a) }; p = pm.next }
  c
}
## Seed an inline-expansion slot table with the callee's PARAM bindings (TYP-10 slice B), so
## `collect_slots` over the body resolves a local copied FROM a param (`r : uint(N) = a` → the
## `x := <struct var>` aggregate reservation) — the table is built FRESH per expansion and
## otherwise cannot see the params (they live in the caller's table). One entry per NON-comptime
## param, mirroring the emit-time alias shape (struct → ek 2, enum → ek 3, else a typed scalar);
## a comptime param binds no slot. Returns the entry count pushed, so the caller skips/rebases
## the seeds. Byte-identical for pre-slice-B callees: their bodies never copied a param into an
## aggregate local, so the seeds change no binding decision (offsets are rebased away).
seed_inline_params := fn(in out slots : SVec, decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), ci : i64, a : rt::Arena) -> usize {
  d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(ci))))
  mut cnt := 0
  mut p := d.params_head
  while p != 0 {
    pm := deref(param_p(p))
    if param_is_comptime(src, pm.ns) == false {
      bn := base_type_name(src, pm.ts, pm.tl)
      mut sek : u8 = 0
      mut sns := bn.s
      mut snl := bn.n
      if str_at((src + pm.ts), pm.tl) == "str" {
        sek = 4
        sns = 0
        snl = 0
      } else if bn.n != 0 and struct_decl_of(decls, src, bn.s, bn.n) >= 0 { sek = 2 }
      else if bn.n != 0 and enum_decl_of(decls, src, bn.s, bn.n) >= 0 { sek = 3 }
      off := svec_len(ptr(slots))
      svec_push(slots, SlotEntry(ns = pm.ns, nl = pm.nl, off = off, sns = sns, snl = snl, ek = sek, estride = 1, eek = 0, is_ref = false, tmod_s = 0, tmod_l = 0))
      cnt += 1
    }
    p = pm.next
  }
  cnt
}
## The BODY-LOCAL slot count of an `@inline` callee — collect_slots over its body into a throwaway SVec
## (statement-body operators declare a local `out`). SVec is created + used LOCALLY (never returned —
## a 4-word SVec would truncate through the ≤3-register struct-return convention).
inline_callee_lcount := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), ci : i64, mar : ptr(mut rt::Arena)) -> usize {
  d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(ci))))
  mut s := SVec(base = 0, len = 0, cap = 0, arena = mar)
  svec_new(s, mar, 8)
  esub := Subst(gps = 0, gpl = 0, its = 0, itl = 0, gps2 = 0, gpl2 = 0, its2 = 0, itl2 = 0, gps3 = 0, gpl3 = 0, its3 = 0, itl3 = 0)
  ## slice B: seed the params first so a body local copied FROM a param reserves its aggregate
  ## words (the emit-side `collect_slots` in `emit_inline_body` is seeded identically — the two
  ## MUST agree, since this count drives the scratch reservation); the seeds are subtracted, so
  ## a callee whose body never copies a param reports exactly its former count (fixpoint-neutral).
  seedn := seed_inline_params(s, decls, src, ci, deref(mar))
  collect_slots(s, d.body_stmts, src, decls, deref(mar), mar, ptr(esub))
  svec_len(ptr(s)) - seedn
}
## Depth guard for the nested-footprint recursion (operator guards nest ~2 deep; a hypothetical
## routing cycle would otherwise loop). A module global avoids threading a depth param through
## `inline_frame_need`.
mut INL_NEST_DEPTH := 0
## The SUMMED inline footprint of `ci`'s BODY beyond `ci`'s own params/locals — the scratch a routed
## operator INSIDE `ci`'s body needs (2b: num.al's `+` guard's `if out < a` routes `<` to cmp.al::lt,
## which expands PAST `ci`'s frame because `emit_inline_body` advances `cx.inl_tmp`). Builds `ci`'s
## slot table (params typed + body-locals via `collect_slots`) so the nested operands' types resolve,
## then recurses `inline_frame_need` over `ci`'s body with THAT table. 0 at the depth cap / no nesting.
inline_callee_nested_need := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), ci : i64, a : rt::Arena, mar : ptr(mut rt::Arena)) -> usize {
  if INL_NEST_DEPTH >= 8 { return 0 }
  INL_NEST_DEPTH = INL_NEST_DEPTH + 1
  d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(ci))))
  mut sl := SVec(base = 0, len = 0, cap = 0, arena = mar)
  svec_new(sl, mar, 8)
  ## slice B: seed the params with their AGGREGATE-aware shapes (`seed_inline_params` — a struct
  ## param is ek 2, not the former flat typed-scalar entry) so a body local copied from a param
  ## reserves correctly here too; the operand-type reads (`operand_type_from_slots`) key on the
  ## recorded span, unchanged for every pre-slice-B callee.
  seed_inline_params(sl, decls, src, ci, a)
  nsub := Subst(gps = 0, gpl = 0, its = 0, itl = 0, gps2 = 0, gpl2 = 0, its2 = 0, itl2 = 0, gps3 = 0, gpl3 = 0, its3 = 0, itl3 = 0)
  collect_slots(sl, d.body_stmts, src, decls, a, mar, ptr(nsub))
  nd := inline_frame_need_stmts(d.body_stmts, decls, src, d.mod_start, d.mod_len, ptr(sl), a, mar)
  INL_NEST_DEPTH = INL_NEST_DEPTH - 1
  nd
}
## An operand expression's USER TYPE name span resolved from the SLOTS alone (a struct/enum `Var`
## reads its slot's recorded `sns`/`snl`) — the prologue-time (pre-emit) dual of `expr_type_span`,
## used by the frame-need walk to detect an operator-routed Bin without an `LCtx`. 0/0 when not a
## slot-typed Var (a literal, an un-inferable operand) → no operator reservation.
operand_type_from_slots := fn(e : ptr(Expr), slots : ptr(SVec), src : ptr(u8)) -> CSpan {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Var(s, n) => {
      if slot_of(slots, src, s, n) < 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
      ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, slots, entry_of(slots, src, s, n)))
      if ent.snl != 0 and ent.ek != 5 { return CSpan(s = ent.sns, n = ent.snl) }
      return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
    }
    ## a struct LITERAL operand carries its own type name span (the pre-emit dual of `expr_type_span`'s
    ## StructLit arm) — so `bin_operator_ret_struct` sizes an `r := S(..) <op> S(..)` binding as the
    ## operator's struct result and `inline_frame_need_expr` reserves the inline scratch for the route.
    Expr::StructLit(cs, cl, nf, fhead) => { return CSpan(s = cs, n = cl) }
    _ => { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  }
}
## Pass a type-name span through IFF it names an UNSIGNED built-in integer (`uN`/`usize`); 0/0
## otherwise. The one-directional filter on the `unchecked` peel below.
unsigned_ty_only := fn(t : CSpan, src : ptr(u8)) -> CSpan {
  if t.n == 0 { return t }
  tx := str_at((src + t.s), t.n)
  if tx == "u8" or tx == "u16" or tx == "u32" or tx == "u64" or tx == "usize" { return t }
  CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
}
## Do the two operand type spans of an ARITHMETIC `Bin` PROVE OPPOSITE SIGNEDNESS — one a built-in
## UNSIGNED integer (`uN`/`usize`), the other a built-in SIGNED integer (`iN`/`isize`)? Such a pair
## proves NOTHING about the result's signedness, so `infer_local_scalar_type` must record NO type for
## it. An operand with no recorded type (a bare literal, an unrecorded local), a float, and any
## signedness-COHERENT pair (`u64 + usize`, `i64 + i64`) all answer FALSE — those keep the existing
## reading, which the CG-6/I11 narrow-width classification depends on.
mixed_signedness_pair := fn(lt : CSpan, rt : CSpan, src : ptr(u8)) -> bool {
  if lt.n == 0 { return false }
  if rt.n == 0 { return false }
  lx := str_at((src + lt.s), lt.n)
  rx := str_at((src + rt.s), rt.n)
  lu := lx == "u8" or lx == "u16" or lx == "u32" or lx == "u64" or lx == "usize"
  ru := rx == "u8" or rx == "u16" or rx == "u32" or rx == "u64" or rx == "usize"
  ls := lx == "i8" or lx == "i16" or lx == "i32" or lx == "i64" or lx == "isize"
  rs := rx == "i8" or rx == "i16" or rx == "i32" or rx == "i64" or rx == "isize"
  (lu and rs) or (ls and ru)
}
## INFER the scalar type of an INFERRED local's init (`x := <v>`, no declared type) so its slot records
## the type — widening checked-verification coverage: a typed local routes its arithmetic to num.al's
## guarded operators. Resolves a `Var` (its slot's recorded type), a scalar CONVERSION `T(x)` (target
## type), and a `Call` (declared return type). Only a BUILT-IN scalar name is accepted (else 0/0 —
## leave the local untyped, as before); a struct/enum RHS is bound by the aggregate paths, not here.
infer_local_scalar_type := fn(v : ptr(Expr), slots : ptr(SVec), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> CSpan {
  mut r := CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
  match deref(v) {
    Expr::Var(s, n) => { r = operand_type_from_slots(v, slots, src) }
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, na, ah) => {
      cn := str_at((src + cs), cl)
      if conv_kind(cn) == 0 { r = CSpan(s = cs, n = cl) }
      else {
        if cn == "shl" or cn == "shr" or cn == "rotl" or cn == "rotr" {
          r = operand_type_from_slots(arg_expr_at(ah, 0, a), slots, src)
        } else {
          r = call_ret_ty_span(v, decls, src, a)
        }
      }
    }
    ## `x := s.f` — a struct-field read: the field's declared type (resolve the base struct's type
    ## from its slot, then the field type). `base` must be a struct-typed Var in the slot table.
    Expr::Field(base, fs, fl) => {
      bt := operand_type_from_slots(base, slots, src)
      if bt.n != 0 { r = field_type_span(decls, src, bt.s, bt.n, fs, fl, a) }
    }
    ## `x := a <arith> b` — an ARITHMETIC combination has the operand type (comparisons/booleans give
    ## `bool`, NOT the operand type, so they are excluded). First typed operand wins — EXCEPT for a
    ## pair that PROVES OPPOSITE SIGNEDNESS, which records nothing.
    ##
    ## Taking the first typed operand unconditionally made the answer depend on OPERAND ORDER, and it
    ## broke the proof-only discipline the whole `unchecked` peel rests on (see the `Expr::Unchecked`
    ## arm below). Over `w : u64 = 18446744073709551610` and `k : i64 = 6`:
    ##
    ##   s := unchecked (w + k) ; s < w   -> recorded `u64` -> UNSIGNED compare -> 3
    ##   s := unchecked (k + w) ; s < w   -> recorded `i64` -> filtered out by `unsigned_ty_only`
    ##                                       -> the always-signed default          -> 4
    ##
    ## Same program, operands flipped, different answer — and the `u64` reading moved the result
    ## signed -> unsigned for a pair containing a PROVABLY SIGNED member, i.e. WITHOUT proof. The
    ## three non-x86 backends already require BOTH operands proven (`a64_operand_unsigned`'s `Bin`
    ## arm and its rv64 / wat duals) and answer the signed reading for both orders; refusing a
    ## mixed-signedness pair here makes all four agree, and moves x86 in the SOUND direction (it can
    ## now only record a type the pair actually proves).
    ##
    ## Deliberately NOT a full "both operands typed" rule: an operand with no recorded type is a bare
    ## LITERAL (which has no signedness of its own and takes its partner's) or an unrecorded local,
    ## and refusing those would drop the recorded type for `x := a + 1` over a narrow `a` — which is
    ## exactly what the CG-6/I11 narrow-width wrap and the overflow guard classification read. A
    ## signedness-COHERENT pair (`u64 + usize`, `i64 + i64`) is likewise untouched.
    Expr::Bin(op, l, r2) => {
      if is_scalar_bin_op(op) {
        blt := operand_type_from_slots(l, slots, src)
        brt := operand_type_from_slots(r2, slots, src)
        if mixed_signedness_pair(blt, brt, src) == false {
          r = blt
          if r.n == 0 { r = brt }
        }
      }
    }
    ## `x := arr[i]` — an INDEX read binds `x` at the array's ELEMENT type, so a later `x + <literal>`
    ## (both operands non-index) is still width-classified (CG-6/I11). Only a real SCALAR-element array
    ## LOCAL (`ek == 5`, `eek == 0` or a byte-packed eek) qualifies; its element type is not in the slot (scalar arrays reuse
    ## `snl` for the count), so recover it from the declared `[T; N]` annotation. Aggregate/float/str
    ## elements and non-array bases fall through to 0/0 (unchanged).
    Expr::Index(base, idx) => {
      bv := var_name_span(base)
      if bv.n != 0 {
        bent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, slots, entry_of(slots, src, bv.s, bv.n)))
        if bent.ek == 5 and (bent.eek == 0 or is_byte_array_eek(bent.eek)) {
          ## scan the DECLARATION name span (`bent.ns`/`bent.nl`), not the use-site `bv` (whose next
          ## char is `[`, not the `:` annotation), to read the `[T; N]` type off `xs : [u8; N] = …`.
          lts := local_type_span(src, bent.ns, bent.nl)
          if lts.n != 0 and str_at((src + lts.s), 1) == "[" { r = array_elem_span(src, lts.s, lts.n) }
        }
      }
    }
    ## `x := unchecked (<init>)` — an `unchecked` SCOPE changes only the VERIFICATION mode of
    ## the expression it wraps, never its TYPE. Without this arm the wrapper swallowed the inferred
    ## type: `s := unchecked (w + d)` over `w, d : u64` bound `s` UNTYPED, so `is_unsigned_expr` could
    ## not prove it unsigned and `s < w` fell back to the always-SIGNED `setl` — a wrapped `u64` sum
    ## above 2^63 then compared as a negative number and `0 < 18446744073709551610` answered FALSE (a
    ## silent wrong value). Annotating the binding or dropping the `unchecked` both gave the right
    ## answer, which is exactly the shape of a dropped fact.
    ##
    ## STRICTLY ONE-DIRECTIONAL: a peeled type is recorded ONLY when it is UNSIGNED (`unsigned_ty_only`
    ## below). The reason is `bitcast`: the parser ERASES a scalar `bitcast(T, x)` (it is a pure
    ## reinterpretation, no code), so the inner expression of `unchecked bitcast(usize, n)` is just
    ## `n` — and propagating THAT type would record the SOURCE type `isize` for a value the program
    ## explicitly reinterpreted as unsigned, marking it signed for the division/overflow-guard
    ## classification. Refusing the signed direction keeps the always-signed default exactly where it
    ## was (it is what the compiler's own `isize` comparisons depend on — flipping that default to
    ## unsigned was tried on 2026-07-24 and broke self-reproduction) and moves an operand only where
    ## unsignedness is PROVEN.
    Expr::Unchecked(inner) => { r = unsigned_ty_only(infer_local_scalar_type(inner, slots, decls, src, a), src) }
    _ => {}
  }
  if r.n == 0 { return r }
  if is_builtin_num_name(str_at((src + r.s), r.n)) { return r }
  CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
}
## If `v` is `a <op> b` routing to a user operator (`@inline` or the non-inline fallback) whose
## RESULT is a struct, that struct's type name span (for sizing an `r := a <op> b` binding as the
## struct); else 0/0. `src/`'s Bins are all scalar → `operator_decl_idx` returns -1 → 0/0 → neutral.
## `slots` resolves the operand type.
bin_operator_ret_struct := fn(v : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), slots : ptr(SVec), a : rt::Arena) -> CSpan {
  match deref(v) {
    Expr::Bin(op, l, r) => {
      mut ots := operand_type_from_slots(l, slots, src)
      if ots.n == 0 { ots = operand_type_from_slots(r, slots, src) }
      opci := operator_decl_idx(decls, src, op, ots.s, ots.n, a)
      if opci < 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
      od := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(opci))))
      rbn := base_type_name(src, od.ret_ts, od.ret_tl)
      ## TYP-10 slice B: a GENERIC operator's declared return type is the UNBOUND `uint(N)` — its
      ## words would have to fold against the expansion-time binding, which does not exist at
      ## slot-collection time. The instance is fully determined by the OPERAND instead: when the
      ## return head is the operand's head (`uint(N)` over a `uint(192)` operand — the routing
      ## rule), size the result from the OPERAND's span, whose literal value argument re-reads
      ## from the source with no binding. A scalar/`bool` return keeps the 0/0 fallthrough.
      if od.is_generic and rbn.n != 0 and struct_decl_of(decls, src, rbn.s, rbn.n) >= 0 {
        ## slice C: canonicalize a TYPE-ALIAS operand (`u128`) to the alias's instance span
        ## (`uint(128)`) BEFORE the head match — its base head then equals the return head `uint`.
        ## Return the BASE-HEAD span (never the paren-carrying full span — `struct_decl_of`
        ## resolves by head name only): the head sits right before `(128)` in the alias decl's
        ## RHS, so the literal value argument re-reads from the source exactly like a direct
        ## `uint(128)` operand's span (a bare use-site `u128` has no `(128)` after it — returning
        ## the operand span itself re-hits the unbound-`N` read).
        oa := alias_rhs(decls, src, ots.s, ots.n)
        obt := base_type_name(src, oa.s, oa.n)
        if streq(src, rbn.s, rbn.n, obt.s, obt.n) { return CSpan(s = obt.s, n = obt.n) }
      }
      if rbn.n != 0 and struct_decl_of(decls, src, rbn.s, rbn.n) >= 0 { return rbn }
      return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
    }
    _ => { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  }
}
## The WIDEST inline-frame need over every `@inline` CALL — AND every operator-routed Bin (§2) — in an
## expr (0 if none). Drives the per-fn inline-scratch reservation. Recurses the expr tree (mirrors
## `match_depth_expr`). `>0` also means "this fn expands an @inline body" (so a fn without one reserves
## nothing → byte-identical → fixpoint-neutral). `slots` resolves an operator Bin's operand type.
inline_frame_need_expr := fn(e : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), ms : usize, ml : usize, slots : ptr(SVec), a : rt::Arena, mar : ptr(mut rt::Arena)) -> usize {
  mut m := 0
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, args_head) => {
      ci := inline_callee_idx(decls, src, cs, cl, ms, ml)
      if ci >= 0 { m = inline_callee_pwords(decls, src, ci, a) + inline_callee_lcount(decls, src, ci, mar) + inline_callee_nested_need(decls, src, ci, a, mar) }
      ## a comptime-VARIADIC callee is EXPANDED here too (§7.1): reserve its FIXED param words (the `...`
      ## rest contributes none) plus 1 — the +1 guarantees the reservation fires even for a 0-fixed-param
      ## variadic, and the callee's own body locals ride the fixed +8 slack `emit_fn` adds to the region.
      vci := variadic_callee_idx(decls, src, cs, cl, ms, ml, a)
      if vci >= 0 {
        vd := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(vci))))
        mut vw := 1
        mut vp := vd.params_head
        mut vpc := usize(vd.arity) - 1
        mut vk := 0
        while vp != 0 and vk < vpc { vpm := deref(param_p(vp)); vw = vw + param_words(decls, src, vpm, a); vp = vpm.next; vk += 1 }
        m = imax(m, vw)
      }
      mut g := args_head
      while g != 0 { ga := deref(arg_p(g)); m = imax(m, inline_frame_need_expr(ga.e, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar)); g = ga.next }
    }
    Expr::Bin(op, l, r) => {
      m = imax(inline_frame_need_expr(l, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar), inline_frame_need_expr(r, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar))
      ## an operator-routed Bin (`a <op> b` over a user/built-in type, or a built-in integer comparison
      ## desugared to cmp.al `lt`/`eq`) expands an `@inline` body — its total param WORDS add to the
      ## inline-scratch need exactly like an @inline call would.
      mut ots := operand_type_from_slots(l, slots, src)
      if ots.n == 0 { ots = operand_type_from_slots(r, slots, src) }
      opci := operator_decl_idx(decls, src, op, ots.s, ots.n, a)
      if opci >= 0 {
        ## TYP-10 slice B: a GENERIC operator's param/body-local words fold against the operand's
        ## comptime value argument — bind it (`N` → `192` for a `uint(192)` operand) around the
        ## sizing calls, popped right after; a concrete operator pushes nothing.
        didb := op_ct_bind(decls, src, opci, ots.s, ots.n, a)
        m = imax(m, inline_callee_pwords(decls, src, opci, a) + inline_callee_lcount(decls, src, opci, mar) + inline_callee_nested_need(decls, src, opci, a, mar))
        if didb { ct_bind_pop() }
      }
      cci := cmp_route_idx(decls, src, op, ots.s, ots.n, a)
      if cci >= 0 { m = imax(m, inline_callee_pwords(decls, src, cci, a) + inline_callee_lcount(decls, src, cci, mar) + inline_callee_nested_need(decls, src, cci, a, mar)) }
    }
    Expr::If(c, t, f) => { m = imax(inline_frame_need_expr(c, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar), imax(inline_frame_need_expr(t, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar), inline_frame_need_expr(f, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar))) }
    Expr::Match(scrut, head) => {
      m = inline_frame_need_expr(scrut, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar)
      mut arm := head
      while arm != 0 { am := deref(arm_p(arm)); m = imax(m, inline_frame_need_expr(am.body, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar)); m = imax(m, inline_frame_need_stmts(am.body_stmts, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar)); arm = am.next }
    }
    Expr::StructLit(cs, cl, nf, fhead) => { mut g : usize = fhead; while g != 0 { ga := deref(arg_p(g)); m = imax(m, inline_frame_need_expr(ga.e, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar)); g = ga.next } }
    Expr::EnumLit(es, el, vs, vl, np, phead) => { mut g : usize = phead; while g != 0 { ga := deref(arg_p(g)); m = imax(m, inline_frame_need_expr(ga.e, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar)); g = ga.next } }
    Expr::ArrayLit(nel, ehead) => { mut g : usize = ehead; while g != 0 { ga := deref(arg_p(g)); m = imax(m, inline_frame_need_expr(ga.e, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar)); g = ga.next } }
    Expr::Field(base, fs, fl) => { m = inline_frame_need_expr(base, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar) }
    Expr::Index(base, idx) => { m = imax(inline_frame_need_expr(base, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar), inline_frame_need_expr(idx, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar)) }
    Expr::AddrOf(p) => { m = inline_frame_need_expr(p, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar) }
    Expr::Deref(p) => { m = inline_frame_need_expr(p, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar) }
    Expr::Try(inner) => { m = inline_frame_need_expr(inner, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar) }
    Expr::Unchecked(inner) => { m = inline_frame_need_expr(inner, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar) }
    Expr::Bitcast(inner, _bcs, _bcl) => { m = inline_frame_need_expr(inner, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar) }
    _ => {}
  }
  m
}
inline_frame_need_stmts := fn(head : ptr(mut Stmt), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), ms : usize, ml : usize, slots : ptr(SVec), a : rt::Arena, mar : ptr(mut rt::Arena)) -> usize {
  mut s := head
  mut m := 0
  while s != 0 {
    st := deref(stmt_p(Stmt, s))
    match st {
      Stmt::Assign(ns, nl, v, nx) => { m = imax(m, inline_frame_need_expr(v, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar)); s = nx }
      Stmt::ExprStmt(e, nx) => { m = imax(m, inline_frame_need_expr(e, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar)); s = nx }
      Stmt::Return(rv, nx) => { m = imax(m, inline_frame_need_expr(rv, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar)); s = nx }
      Stmt::While(c, b, nx) => { m = imax(m, imax(inline_frame_need_expr(c, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar), inline_frame_need_stmts(b, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar))); s = nx }
      Stmt::Loop(b, nx) => { m = imax(m, inline_frame_need_stmts(b, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar)); s = nx }
      Stmt::Unchecked(b, nx) => { m = imax(m, inline_frame_need_stmts(b, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar)); s = nx }
      Stmt::AllocWith(ae, b, nx) => { m = imax(m, inline_frame_need_stmts(b, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar)); s = nx }
      Stmt::If(c, th, el, nx) => { m = imax(m, imax(inline_frame_need_expr(c, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar), imax(inline_frame_need_stmts(th, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar), inline_frame_need_stmts(el, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar)))); s = nx }
      Stmt::FieldAssign(bns, bnl, fns, fnl, fv, nx) => { m = imax(m, inline_frame_need_expr(fv, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar)); s = nx }
      Stmt::FieldPathAssign(pl, fpv, nx) => { m = imax(m, inline_frame_need_expr(fpv, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar)); s = nx }
      Stmt::DerefAssign(pe, val, nx) => { m = imax(m, imax(inline_frame_need_expr(pe, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar), inline_frame_need_expr(val, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar))); s = nx }
      Stmt::IndexAssign(ib, ii, iv, nx) => { m = imax(m, imax(inline_frame_need_expr(ib, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar), imax(inline_frame_need_expr(ii, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar), inline_frame_need_expr(iv, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar)))); s = nx }
      Stmt::IndexFieldAssign(fia, fii, ifs, ifl, fiv, nx) => { m = imax(m, imax(inline_frame_need_expr(fia, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar), imax(inline_frame_need_expr(fii, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar), inline_frame_need_expr(fiv, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar)))); s = nx }
      Stmt::For(fns, fnl, flo, fhi, fb, nx) => { m = imax(m, imax(inline_frame_need_expr(flo, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar), inline_frame_need_stmts(fb, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar))); if unchecked bitcast(usize, fhi) != 0 { m = imax(m, inline_frame_need_expr(fhi, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar)) } ; s = nx }
      Stmt::Match(sc, ah, nx) => {
        m = imax(m, inline_frame_need_expr(sc, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar))
        mut arm := ah
        while arm != 0 { am := deref(arm_p(arm)); m = imax(m, inline_frame_need_expr(am.body, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar)); m = imax(m, inline_frame_need_stmts(am.body_stmts, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar)); arm = am.next }
        s = nx
      }
      Stmt::Break(_bv, _bd, nx) => { s = nx }
      Stmt::Continue(_cd, nx) => { s = nx }
      Stmt::CompIf(ccond, cthen, celse, nx) => { m = imax(m, imax(inline_frame_need_stmts(cthen, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar), inline_frame_need_stmts(celse, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar))); s = nx }
      Stmt::CompFor(cvs, cvl, civ, cb, nx) => { m = imax(m, inline_frame_need_stmts(cb, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar)); s = nx }
      Stmt::CompForRange(rvs, rvl, rlo, rhi, rb, nx) => { m = imax(m, inline_frame_need_stmts(rb, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar)); s = nx }
      Stmt::CompMatch(cmsc, cmah, nx) => { mut car : usize = cmah; while car != 0 { cam := deref(arm_p(car)); m = imax(m, inline_frame_need_stmts(cam.body_stmts, decls, src, ms, ml, slots, a, mar)); car = cam.next } ; s = nx }
    }
  }
  m
}
## Bind a FLOAT (`f64`) local: one word, like a scalar, but tagged `ek == 9` so a later `Var` read /
## arithmetic dispatches the xmm path. (The bits ride a single frame slot — no special layout.)
bind_float_slot := fn(in out slots : SVec, src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize) {
  existing := slot_of(ptr(slots), src, s, n)
  if existing >= 0 { return }
  off := svec_len(ptr(slots))
  svec_push(slots, SlotEntry(ns = s, nl = n, off = off, sns = 0, snl = 0, ek = 9, estride = 1, eek = 0, is_ref = false))
}

## The COLLECT-time float detector (the slot-building dual of `is_float_expr`, which needs an `LCtx`):
## a float literal, a float `Var` (`ek == 9`, bound by an earlier statement), float arithmetic, or an
## `f64(...)`/`f32(...)` conversion. An integer cast yields an integer, so it is not float.
value_is_float := fn(e : ptr(Expr), slots : ptr(SVec), src : ptr(u8), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), a : rt::Arena) -> bool {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::FloatLit(fs, fl) => { true }
    Expr::Var(s, n) => {
      if slot_of(ptr(slots), src, s, n) < 0 {
        is_mut_float_global(decls, src, s, n)   ## a float-valued mutable global (no frame slot)
      } else {
        ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, slots, entry_of(slots, src, s, n)))
        ent.ek == 9
      }
    }
    Expr::Bin(op, l, r) => {
      mut r2 := false
      if is_float_bin_op(op) { r2 = value_is_float(l, slots, src, decls, a) or value_is_float(r, slots, src, decls, a) }
      r2
    }
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, na, ah) => { str_at((src + cs), cl) == "f64" or str_at((src + cs), cl) == "f32" }
    Expr::Field(base, fs, fl) => {
      bv := var_name_span(base)
      mut rf := false
      if bv.n != 0 {
        se := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, slots, entry_of(slots, src, bv.s, bv.n)))
        if se.ek == 2 or se.ek == 7 {
          fsp := field_type_span(decls, src, se.sns, se.snl, fs, fl, a)
          mut tn := str_at((src + fsp.s), fsp.n)
          rf = tn == "f64" or tn == "f32"
        }
      }
      rf
    }
    Expr::Index(base, idx) => {
      bv := var_name_span(base)
      mut ri := false
      if bv.n != 0 {
        se := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, slots, entry_of(slots, src, bv.s, bv.n)))
        if se.ek == 5 and se.eek == 9 { ri = true }
      }
      ri
    }
    _ => { false }
  }
}

## The enclosing generic INSTANCE's type substitution, threaded into `collect_slots` so a local
## bound from a call returning the type-parameter (`existing := deref(key_at(K, …)) -> ptr(mut K)`)
## can be sized as the CONCRETE instance type. 0-length `gps` (a non-generic / non-instance fn) → no
## substitution ever fires → byte-identical slot layout (src/ never binds such a local).
Subst := struct { gps : usize, gpl : usize, its : usize, itl : usize, gps2 : usize, gpl2 : usize, its2 : usize, itl2 : usize, gps3 : usize, gpl3 : usize, its3 : usize, itl3 : usize }

## The FULL type-text length starting at `ts`: the base name (`tl`) plus any balanced `(...)` generic
## arguments immediately following it. A fn `Decl`'s `ret_ts`/`ret_tl` records only the BASE name
## (`ptr`), but `ret_ts` points at the full type in the source, so `ptr(mut K)` is recovered by
## scanning the parens. Returns `tl` unchanged when no `(` follows (a plain `u64` return).
extend_type_span := fn(src : ptr(u8), ts : usize, tl : usize) -> usize {
  mut e := ts + tl
  if str_at((src + e), 1) != "(" { return tl }
  mut depth := 0
  mut go := true
  while go {
    c := str_at((src + e), 1)
    if c == "(" { depth = depth + 1; e = e + 1 }
    else if c == ")" { depth = depth - 1; e = e + 1; if depth == 0 { go = false } }
    else { e = e + 1 }
  }
  e - ts
}

## The pointee NAME span of a `ptr(...)` / `ptr(mut ...)` type — like `ptr_pointee_struct_span` but
## WITHOUT the struct check, so a type-parameter pointee (`ptr(mut K)`) is returned for substitution.
## `tl` must be the FULL type length (`extend_type_span`), not the truncated base name.
ptr_pointee_name := fn(src : ptr(u8), ts : usize, tl : usize) -> CSpan {
  if tl < 6 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  if str_at((src + ts), 4) != "ptr(" { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  mut ps := ts + 4
  mut pl := tl - 5
  if pl > 4 and str_at((src + ps), 4) == "mut " { ps = ps + 4; pl = pl - 4 }
  CSpan(s = ps, n = pl)
}

## Resolve `deref(<call>)` to a STRUCT span by the CALLEE's declared RETURN type (`ptr(mut P)`),
## mapping the pointee `P` through the callee's own type-parameters to the call's type-ARGUMENT at
## that position, then substituting the ENCLOSING instance's type-param (`gps`→`its`). Distinguishes
## `key_at(K,V,…) -> ptr(mut K)` (K → concrete struct) from `val_at(K,V,…) -> ptr(mut V)` (V, a
## different arg → not the struct key) — which the first-argument heuristic (`deref_call_struct_span`)
## cannot. `inner` is the deref'd Call expr (a typed PARAM, so the `match deref` lowers). 0/0 unless
## the resolved pointee is a known struct. src/'s deref-calls pass CONCRETE structs whose FIRST arg IS
## the return pointee → handled by `deref_call_struct_span` first → this never fires there.
call_ret_struct_span_sub := fn(inner : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena, gps : usize, gpl : usize, its : usize, itl : usize, gps2 : usize, gpl2 : usize, its2 : usize, itl2 : usize) -> CSpan {
  mut res := CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
  match deref(inner) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, ah) => {
      nm := callee_name_span(src, cs, cl)
      cnt := rt::vec_len(deref(decls))
      mut ci := 0 - 1
      th := name_hash(src, nm.s, nm.n)
      mut jc := dni_lo(cnt, th)
      jce := dni_hi(cnt, th)
      while jc < jce {
        i := dni_at(cnt, jc)
        jc = jc + 1
        if dnh_skip(cnt, i, th) == false {
          d := deref(decl_get(decls, i))
          if (d.kind == 1 or d.kind == 4) and streq(src, d.name_start, d.name_len, nm.s, nm.n) { ci = i64(i) }
        }
      }
      if ci >= 0 {
        d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(ci))))
        rfull := extend_type_span(src, d.ret_ts, d.ret_tl)
        pn := ptr_pointee_name(src, d.ret_ts, rfull)
        if pn.n != 0 {
          ## the pointee's position among the callee's params (a type-param `P : type`); the call's
          ## argument there names the concrete type-arg.
          mut ppos := 0 - 1
          mut pc := d.params_head
          mut pj := 0
          while pc != 0 {
            pm := deref(param_p(pc))
            if streq(src, pm.ns, pm.nl, pn.s, pn.n) { ppos = i64(pj) }
            pj += 1
            pc = pm.next
          }
          mut rn : CSpan = pn
          if ppos >= 0 and usize(ppos) < nargs {
            cta := arg_expr_at(ah, usize(ppos), a)
            vn := var_name_span(cta)
            if vn.n != 0 { rn = vn }
          }
          if gpl != 0 and streq(src, rn.s, rn.n, gps, gpl) { rn = CSpan(s = its, n = itl) }
          else if gpl2 != 0 and streq(src, rn.s, rn.n, gps2, gpl2) { rn = CSpan(s = its2, n = itl2) }
          if struct_decl_of(decls, src, rn.s, rn.n) >= 0 { res = rn }
        }
      }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  res
}

## `deref(<call>)` → the struct span resolved via the callee's RETURN type + instance substitution
## (`call_ret_struct_span_sub`); 0/0 otherwise. The return-type dual of `deref_call_struct_span`.
deref_call_ret_struct_span := fn(v : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena, gps : usize, gpl : usize, its : usize, itl : usize, gps2 : usize, gpl2 : usize, its2 : usize, itl2 : usize) -> CSpan {
  inner := deref_inner_expr(v)
  if unchecked bitcast(usize, inner) == 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  call_ret_struct_span_sub(inner, decls, src, a, gps, gpl, its, itl, gps2, gpl2, its2, itl2)
}

## The 0-based position of the PARAMETER named `[ts,tl)` in the fn `Decl` at index `ci`'s `params_head`
## (walks the `Param` list). -1 if not a parameter. Local analogue of `lower_layout::param_pos` (not
## imported here) — used by `subst_enum_ret_span` to map a return type-arg name to the call's argument.
callee_param_pos := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), ci : usize, src : ptr(u8), ts : usize, tl : usize, a : rt::Arena) -> i64 {
  d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), ci)))
  mut pc := d.params_head
  mut pj := 0
  mut res := 0 - 1
  while pc != 0 {
    pm := deref(param_p(pc))
    if streq(src, pm.ns, pm.nl, ts, tl) { res = i64(pj) }
    pj += 1
    pc = pm.next
  }
  res
}

## The `decls` index of the FN (or generic-fn) declaration named `nm`, or -1. The shared form of the
## name-hash bucket walk `call_ret_struct_span_sub` open-codes just above; no module context is
## consulted, matching that walk exactly.
fn_decl_idx_by_name := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), nm : CSpan) -> i64 {
  cnt := rt::vec_len(deref(decls))
  mut ci := 0 - 1
  th := name_hash(src, nm.s, nm.n)
  mut jc := dni_lo(cnt, th)
  jce := dni_hi(cnt, th)
  while jc < jce {
    i := dni_at(cnt, jc)
    jc = jc + 1
    if dnh_skip(cnt, i, th) == false {
      d := deref(decl_get(decls, i))
      if (d.kind == 1 or d.kind == 4) and streq(src, d.name_start, d.name_len, nm.s, nm.n) { ci = i64(i) }
    }
  }
  ci
}

## Types §7 / CLAYOUT S3(b) — the POINTEE type span of a CALL whose callee is declared
## `-> ptr(<opt mut> P)`, resolved by TYPE-PARAMETER **POSITION**.
##
## `P` is written in the CALLEE's scope. When it names one of the callee's own PARAMETERS (a
## `T : type` type-parameter), the concrete type is the call's ARGUMENT at THAT parameter's
## position — a positional mapping, never a match of the pointee NAME against anything outside the
## callee (two different `T`s can share a spelling, so a tail-name match would be unsound; that
## leniency is exactly what produced earlier silent defects). The resolved name is then carried
## through the ENCLOSING generic instance's substitution (`gps`→`its`, `gps2`→`its2`, `gps3`→`its3`)
## so a helper reached from inside another instance lands on the concrete type. A CONCRETE pointee
## (`-> ptr(mut str)`, `-> ptr(mut u64)`) is returned exactly as spelled.
##
## `{0,0}` means "not resolvable HERE": not a call, an unknown callee, a non-`ptr(…)` return, or the
## type-parameter's position carries no type-NAME argument (the type-argument was inferred rather
## than supplied). That last case is the one `deref_call_pointee_unresolved` turns into a LOCATED
## reject at the value site, because a one-word read of what may be a two-word view is a silent
## wrong value (I11).
call_ret_pointee_span := fn(v : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena, gps : usize, gpl : usize, its : usize, itl : usize, gps2 : usize, gpl2 : usize, its2 : usize, itl2 : usize, gps3 : usize, gpl3 : usize, its3 : usize, itl3 : usize) -> CSpan {
  mut res := CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
  match deref(v) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, ah) => {
      ci := fn_decl_idx_by_name(decls, src, callee_name_span(src, cs, cl))
      if ci >= 0 {
        d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(ci))))
        rfull := extend_type_span(src, d.ret_ts, d.ret_tl)
        pn := ptr_pointee_name(src, d.ret_ts, rfull)
        if pn.n != 0 {
          ppos := callee_param_pos(decls, usize(ci), src, pn.s, pn.n, a)
          mut rn : CSpan = pn
          mut ok := true
          if ppos >= 0 {
            ## the pointee IS a callee type-parameter: take the call's argument at ITS position
            ok = false
            if usize(ppos) < nargs {
              ## `type_arg_at` reads a bare `Var` type-arg AND a generic-instance one
              ## (`mk(Box(u64), …)` → the head span `Box`), so a well-formed call always resolves.
              vn := type_arg_at(ah, usize(ppos), a)
              if vn.n != 0 { rn = vn ; ok = true }
            }
          }
          if ok {
            if gpl != 0 and streq(src, rn.s, rn.n, gps, gpl) { rn = CSpan(s = its, n = itl) }
            else if gpl2 != 0 and streq(src, rn.s, rn.n, gps2, gpl2) { rn = CSpan(s = its2, n = itl2) }
            else if gpl3 != 0 and streq(src, rn.s, rn.n, gps3, gpl3) { rn = CSpan(s = its3, n = itl3) }
            res = rn
          }
        }
      }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  res
}

## Does the CALL `v` return `ptr(<callee type-parameter>)` while the call supplies no type-NAME
## argument at that parameter's position, so `call_ret_pointee_span` cannot say whether the pointee
## is a scalar or a two-word §7 VIEW? Reading such a `deref(<call>)` as one word is a silent wrong
## value whenever the pointee is a view, so the value sites turn this into a located reject (I11 —
## a trap is acceptable, a wrong value is not).
call_ret_pointee_unresolved := fn(v : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> bool {
  mut res := false
  match deref(v) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, ah) => {
      ci := fn_decl_idx_by_name(decls, src, callee_name_span(src, cs, cl))
      if ci >= 0 {
        d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(ci))))
        rfull := extend_type_span(src, d.ret_ts, d.ret_tl)
        pn := ptr_pointee_name(src, d.ret_ts, rfull)
        if pn.n != 0 {
          ppos := callee_param_pos(decls, usize(ci), src, pn.s, pn.n, a)
          if ppos >= 0 {
            res = true
            if usize(ppos) < nargs {
              vn := type_arg_at(ah, usize(ppos), a)
              if vn.n != 0 { res = false }
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  res
}

## Build a SUBSTITUTED enum-return type span for a Call `v` to a generic fn whose declared return type
## is a generic enum `Enum(<args>)` in which one or more top-level arg names the callee's OWN
## type-PARAMETER (`Opt(T)`, `Option(U)`). Each such arg is resolved to the concrete type the call
## supplies — the call's ARGUMENT at that parameter's position (a `Var` naming the type-arg) — then
## through the enclosing instance substitution (`gps`→`its` / `gps2`→`its2`). The resolved
## `Enum(concrete,…)` text is written into the PERSISTENT arena `synth` (the emit arena `p.mar`, which
## survives through emission); the returned CSpan's `s = addr - src` so every `str_at(src + s)` consumer
## (slot sizing, call-result staging, `match` payload-type) resolves it against the GLOBAL `src` by
## WRAPPING arithmetic (`src + (addr - src) == addr`, regardless of which region `addr` lives in). This
## is the roadmap's "record the SUBSTITUTED return type on the result binding" — a synthetic span in a
## persistent buffer. Returns 0/0 when NO return arg is a callee type-param (all concrete / not a
## generic enum) → the caller keeps the raw-span path → every EXISTING enum-returning binding is
## byte-identical. WITHOUT this, `om := wrap(Pt, …)` where `wrap -> Opt(T)` sized `om` as `Opt(T)` with
## `T` a scalar (disc + 1 word) → the caller staged only %rax/%rdx (dropping the struct payload's tail)
## and the `match` bound the payload as a scalar. Mirrors `call_ret_struct_span_sub` (the `ptr(mut K)`
## dual), generalized to an enum head + N type-args.
subst_enum_ret_span := fn(v : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena, synth : ptr(mut rt::Arena), gps : usize, gpl : usize, its : usize, itl : usize, gps2 : usize, gpl2 : usize, its2 : usize, itl2 : usize) -> CSpan {
  mut res := CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
  match deref(v) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, ah) => {
      nm := callee_name_span(src, cs, cl)
      cnt := rt::vec_len(deref(decls))
      ## Resolve the callee the SAME way as `generic_decl_of`/`ret_call_target`: prefer a QUALIFIED
      ## call's MODULE + arity match (`base::result::map`), else an arity match (`option::map`/4 vs
      ## `vec::map`/5), else last — so the substituted return span is read off the RIGHT decl.
      cpq := colon_pos(src, cs, cl)
      mut segcmp := false
      mut pms := 0
      mut pml := 0
      if cpq >= 0 { pms = cs; pml = usize(cpq); segcmp = true }
      mut ci := 0 - 1
      mut ci_arity := 0 - 1
      mut ci_mod := 0 - 1
      th := name_hash(src, nm.s, nm.n)
      mut jc := dni_lo(cnt, th)
      jce := dni_hi(cnt, th)
      while jc < jce {
        i := dni_at(cnt, jc)
        jc = jc + 1
        if dnh_skip(cnt, i, th) == false {
        d := deref(decl_get(decls, i))
        if d.kind == 1 and d.is_generic and streq(src, d.name_start, d.name_len, nm.s, nm.n) {
          ci = i64(i)
          if d.arity == nargs {
            ci_arity = i64(i)
            if segcmp and mod_head_matches(src, d.mod_start, d.mod_len, pms, pml) { ci_mod = i64(i) }
          }
        }
        }
      }
      if ci_mod >= 0 { ci = ci_mod }
      else if ci_arity >= 0 { ci = ci_arity }
      ## RECEIVER-KEYED redirect — align with `ret_call_target` so an IMPLICIT-UFCS multi-type-param
      ## method (`r.map(f)`, no arity match → last-declared, often the WRONG same-name overload) resolves
      ## to its true decl. `rcv` (the receiver's full declared type) is reused by PASS 2 to recover the
      ## type-params the omitted positional type-args can't supply. NEUTRAL: `recv_redirect_generic`
      ## returns its input unchanged whenever the pick's `self` already matches the receiver, and a 0/0
      ## receiver skips both the redirect and the PASS-2 recovery (every existing src/ binding).
      mut rcv := CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
      if ci >= 0 and nargs >= 1 {
        rcv = recv_full_emit(arg_expr_at(ah, 0, a), decls, src, a)
        if rcv.n != 0 {
          rcb := base_type_name(src, rcv.s, rcv.n)
          ci = recv_redirect_generic(decls, src, ci, nargs, rcb.s, rcb.n, a)
        }
      }
      if ci >= 0 {
        d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(ci))))
        hs := d.ret_ts
        hl := d.ret_tl
        if enum_decl_of(decls, src, hs, hl) >= 0 {
          ## PASS 1 (no allocation): does any top-level return type-arg name a callee type-param?
          mut anyp := false
          mut k := 0
          mut go := true
          while go {
            ta := typearg_at(src, hs, hl, k)
            if ta.n == 0 { go = false }
            else {
              if callee_param_pos(decls, usize(ci), src, ta.s, ta.n, a) >= 0 { anyp = true }
              k += 1
            }
          }
          if anyp {
            ## PASS 2: build `Enum(resolved,…)` into `synth` (bytes copied via `push_str`)
            mut msb := rt::strbuf(deref(synth), 256)
            hstr := str_at((src + hs), hl)
            push_str(msb, hstr)
            push_str(msb, "(")
            mut k2 := 0
            mut go2 := true
            while go2 {
              ta := typearg_at(src, hs, hl, k2)
              if ta.n == 0 { go2 = false }
              else {
                if k2 > 0 { push_str(msb, ", ") }
                mut rs := ta.s
                mut rn := ta.n
                pp := callee_param_pos(decls, usize(ci), src, ta.s, ta.n, a)
                mut resolved := false
                if pp >= 0 and usize(pp) < nargs {
                  cta := arg_expr_at(ah, usize(pp), a)
                  vn := var_name_span(cta)
                  ## a genuine TYPE arg (`u64`) — use it. A FN-VALUE arg (`step`) sitting at a type-param
                  ## position means the call OMITTED its type-args (implicit UFCS) and the fn rode into a
                  ## type-param slot — NOT the type; fall through to the receiver/fn-return recovery below.
                  if vn.n != 0 and is_fn_name(decls, src, vn.s, vn.n) == false { rs = vn.s; rn = vn.n; resolved = true }
                }
                if resolved == false and rcv.n != 0 {
                  ## IMPLICIT UFCS: this return type-arg's type-param has no positional TYPE arg. Resolve it
                  ## from the RECEIVER's type-args (its index among the callee's type-params), or — for the
                  ## mapper's OWN return type-param (`U`/`F`, absent from the receiver) — from the fn
                  ## ARGUMENT's declared return (`infer_map_targ3`). This makes `rm := r.map(step)` bind the
                  ## fully-substituted `Result(u64, u64)` (base + payload size), not `Result(U, E)`.
                  mut tpix := 0 - 1
                  mut ppx := d.params_head
                  mut tpc := 0
                  while ppx != 0 {
                    pmx := deref(param_p(ppx))
                    if str_at((src + pmx.ts), pmx.tl) == "type" {
                      if streq(src, pmx.ns, pmx.nl, ta.s, ta.n) { tpix = i64(tpc) }
                      tpc += 1
                    }
                    ppx = pmx.next
                  }
                  if tpix >= 0 {
                    rcb := base_type_name(src, rcv.s, rcv.n)
                    cc := implicit_targ_from_recv(decls, src, ci, rcv.s, rcv.n, usize(tpix), a)
                    if cc.n != 0 { rs = cc.s; rn = cc.n }
                    else {
                      fu := infer_map_targ3(decls, src, ah, nargs, rcb.s, rcb.n, a)
                      if fu.n != 0 { rs = fu.s; rn = fu.n }
                    }
                  }
                }
                if gpl != 0 and streq(src, rs, rn, gps, gpl) { rs = its; rn = itl }
                else if gpl2 != 0 and streq(src, rs, rn, gps2, gpl2) { rs = its2; rn = itl2 }
                astr := str_at((src + rs), rn)
                push_str(msb, astr)
                k2 += 1
              }
            }
            push_str(msb, ")")
            base_abs := unchecked bitcast(usize, rt::strbuf_base(msb))
            ## REBASE the synthesized-name buffer pointer into a `src`-relative handle: `base_abs - src`
            ## is POINTER DIFFERENCE (underflows to a huge two's-complement offset when base_abs < src),
            ## deliberately modular — route through `rt::off`, not a checked integer `-` (I11 / CG-8).
            ## `(src + s)` later wraps it back. This is THE origin of every rebased span.
            res = CSpan(s = unchecked (base_abs - src), n = hl)   ## INTENTIONAL modular underflow — rebase seam
          }
        }
      }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  res
}

## The STRUCT dual of `subst_enum_ret_span`, restricted to a generic `Slice(<typeparam>)` return whose
## element resolves to a MULTI-WORD struct — e.g. `omap_values(K, V, …) -> Slice(V)` called with a
## struct `V`. The element type-arg (`V`) is resolved to the concrete type the call supplies (its arg at
## `V`'s parameter position), then through the enclosing instance substitution, and a synthetic
## `Slice(<concrete>)` name is written into the PERSISTENT `synth` arena (like `subst_enum_ret_span`), so a
## `vs := omap_values(…)` binding records the SUBSTITUTED element (`Slice(Rec)`), not the un-substituted
## param (`Slice(V)`). That lets `slice_val_struct_espan` recover the concrete element for the whole-element
## copy `v := vs[i]`. 0/0 unless the return HEAD is `Slice` AND its element resolves to a `>1`-word struct
## (so `Slice(scalar)` / a non-struct element keeps the raw span → byte-identical for every existing bind).
subst_slice_ret_span := fn(v : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena, synth : ptr(mut rt::Arena), gps : usize, gpl : usize, its : usize, itl : usize, gps2 : usize, gpl2 : usize, its2 : usize, itl2 : usize) -> CSpan {
  mut res := CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
  match deref(v) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, ah) => {
      ci := ret_call_target(decls, src, cs, cl, nargs, ah, a)
      if ci >= 0 {
        d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(ci))))
        hs := d.ret_ts
        hl := d.ret_tl
        bn := base_type_name(src, hs, hl)
        if str_at((src + bn.s), bn.n) == "Slice" {
          ta := typearg_at(src, hs, hl, 0)
          if ta.n != 0 {
            mut rs := ta.s
            mut rn := ta.n
            pp := callee_param_pos(decls, usize(ci), src, ta.s, ta.n, a)
            if pp >= 0 and usize(pp) < nargs {
              cta := arg_expr_at(ah, usize(pp), a)
              vn := var_name_span(cta)
              if vn.n != 0 { rs = vn.s; rn = vn.n }
            }
            if gpl != 0 and streq(src, rs, rn, gps, gpl) { rs = its; rn = itl }
            else if gpl2 != 0 and streq(src, rs, rn, gps2, gpl2) { rs = its2; rn = itl2 }
            if struct_decl_of(decls, src, rs, rn) >= 0 and struct_words(decls, src, rs, rn, a) > 1 {
              mut msb := rt::strbuf(deref(synth), 128)
              push_str(msb, "Slice(")
              push_str(msb, str_at((src + rs), rn))
              push_str(msb, ")")
              base_abs := unchecked bitcast(usize, rt::strbuf_base(msb))
              ## rebase the synthesized name into a `src`-relative handle (modular pointer diff, wrapped
              ## back by `src + s` at every consumer). The length is the BASE head length `hl` ("Slice"),
              ## NOT the full string — so `base_type_name` reads the head and `typearg_at` (which starts at
              ## `s + n`) lands exactly on the synthesized `(<elem>)`. This is the `subst_enum_ret_span` seam.
              res = CSpan(s = unchecked (base_abs - src), n = hl)
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  res
}

## ---- GENERIC-STRUCT INSTANTIATION AT AN AGGREGATE TYPE-ARG (Types §9.4). A generic type FUNCTION
## `Box := fn(T : type) -> type { struct { v : T } }` applied at an AGGREGATE (`Box(P)`, `Box(Option(u64))`)
## is WIDER than the un-substituted `struct { v : T }` (which sizes `T` as one scalar word). Layout
## already substitutes through `subst_field_ty` — but ONLY when the span it is handed carries the
## instantiation's `(…)` type-args resolved to CONCRETE types. Inside a generic fn's instance the
## application is written `Box(T)` (the callee's own type-param), and at the CALL SITE the callee's
## declared return is likewise `Box(T)` — both size the field as one word, so the value was moved
## one word wide: a SILENT truncated struct (and, at an ENUM type-arg, a GARBAGE discriminant whose
## `match` matched NO arm). The three helpers below synthesize the resolved application text.

## The FULL source extent of a possibly-parenthesized type APPLICATION whose HEAD span is `[s, s+n)`.
## A generic type reference keeps only its HEAD in the AST (`Option`, `Box`); its `(…)` arguments stay
## intact in `src` right after it (the `typearg_at` seam). Extend the span across that BALANCED paren
## group so the text can be COPIED VERBATIM (`Option(u64)`) — a bare-head copy would truncate it to
## `Option` and lose the instantiation. Returns `[s, s+n)` unchanged when no `(` follows (a bare name,
## and every span `typearg_at` already returns whole). Bounded so a malformed/unterminated group
## cannot spin.
full_app_span := fn(src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize) -> CSpan {
  if n == 0 { return CSpan(s = s, n = n) }
  if str_at(((src + s) + n), 1) != "(" { return CSpan(s = s, n = n) }
  mut i := n + 1
  mut depth := 0
  mut go := true
  while go {
    c := str_at(((src + s) + i), 1)
    if c == "(" { depth = depth + 1 }
    else if c == ")" {
      if depth == 0 { go = false } else { depth = depth - 1 }
    }
    i += 1
    if i > 512 { go = false }             ## bound: a malformed group never spins
  }
  CSpan(s = s, n = i)
}

## Is the type span `[s, s+n)` an AGGREGATE (struct / enum / `str`)? This is exactly the condition
## under which substituting it for a type-PARAMETER CHANGES layout (a scalar type-arg sizes as one
## word, identically to the un-substituted `T`) — so it is the GATE on every substitution below, and
## the reason they are byte-identical for every scalar-arg instantiation already in `src/` and `lib/`.
ty_is_agg_span := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize) -> bool {
  if n == 0 { return false }
  bn := base_type_name(src, s, n)
  if struct_decl_of(decls, src, bn.s, bn.n) >= 0 { return true }
  if enum_decl_of(decls, src, bn.s, bn.n) >= 0 { return true }
  str_at((src + bn.s), bn.n) == "str"
}

## Map a bare TYPE-name span through the enclosing generic INSTANCE's 3-way substitution
## (`gps→its` / `gps2→its2` / `gps3→its3`) — the shared core of the inline `cx.gp_*` maps. Returns
## the input unchanged when it names no type-parameter.
subst3_ty := fn(src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize, gps : usize, gpl : usize, its : usize, itl : usize, gps2 : usize, gpl2 : usize, its2 : usize, itl2 : usize, gps3 : usize, gpl3 : usize, its3 : usize, itl3 : usize) -> CSpan {
  if gpl != 0 and itl != 0 and streq(src, s, n, gps, gpl) { return CSpan(s = its, n = itl) }
  if gpl2 != 0 and itl2 != 0 and streq(src, s, n, gps2, gpl2) { return CSpan(s = its2, n = itl2) }
  if gpl3 != 0 and itl3 != 0 and streq(src, s, n, gps3, gpl3) { return CSpan(s = its3, n = itl3) }
  CSpan(s = s, n = n)
}

## The STRUCT dual of `subst_param_enum_span`: given a generic-STRUCT APPLICATION span `[s, s+n)`
## (`Box(T)`) appearing INSIDE a generic fn's instance, and that instance's 3-way Subst, synthesize
## the RESOLVED application (`Box(P)` / `Box(Option(u64))`) into the PERSISTENT `synth` arena. The
## returned CSpan is rebased `src`-relative with the HEAD length, so `base_type_name` reads the head
## and `typearg_at` (which starts at `s + n`) lands exactly on the synthesized `(…)` — the same seam
## `subst_enum_ret_span` / `subst_slice_ret_span` use. 0/0 — the RAW span kept, byte-identical —
## unless the head is a GENERIC struct decl AND some top-level type-arg substitutes to an AGGREGATE.
subst_inst_struct_span := fn(s : usize, n : usize, decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), synth : ptr(mut rt::Arena), gps : usize, gpl : usize, its : usize, itl : usize, gps2 : usize, gpl2 : usize, its2 : usize, itl2 : usize, gps3 : usize, gpl3 : usize, its3 : usize, itl3 : usize) -> CSpan {
  mut res := CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
  if n == 0 { return res }
  bn := base_type_name(src, s, n)
  di := struct_decl_of(decls, src, bn.s, bn.n)
  if di < 0 { return res }
  d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(di))))
  if d.is_generic == false { return res }
  ## PASS 1 (no allocation): does any top-level type-arg MAP to an AGGREGATE? Only then does the
  ## instantiated layout differ from the raw span's, so only then is a synthesized span warranted.
  mut anyp := false
  mut k := 0
  mut go := true
  while go {
    ta := typearg_at(src, bn.s, bn.n, k)
    if ta.n == 0 { go = false }
    else {
      r := subst3_ty(src, ta.s, ta.n, gps, gpl, its, itl, gps2, gpl2, its2, itl2, gps3, gpl3, its3, itl3)
      if r.s != ta.s and ty_is_agg_span(decls, src, r.s, r.n) { anyp = true }
      k += 1
    }
  }
  if anyp == false { return res }
  ## PASS 2: build `G(<mapped>, …)` into `synth`. A mapped arg is copied through `full_app_span` so a
  ## generic-instance type-arg (`Option(u64)`, recorded as the bare head) keeps its own `(…)`.
  mut msb := rt::strbuf(deref(synth), 192)
  push_str(msb, str_at((src + bn.s), bn.n))
  push_str(msb, "(")
  mut k2 := 0
  mut go2 := true
  while go2 {
    ta := typearg_at(src, bn.s, bn.n, k2)
    if ta.n == 0 { go2 = false }
    else {
      if k2 > 0 { push_str(msb, ", ") }
      r := subst3_ty(src, ta.s, ta.n, gps, gpl, its, itl, gps2, gpl2, its2, itl2, gps3, gpl3, its3, itl3)
      fa := full_app_span(src, r.s, r.n)
      push_str(msb, str_at((src + fa.s), fa.n))
      k2 += 1
    }
  }
  push_str(msb, ")")
  base_abs := unchecked bitcast(usize, rt::strbuf_base(msb))
  res = CSpan(s = unchecked (base_abs - src), n = bn.n)   ## INTENTIONAL modular underflow — rebase seam
  res
}

## The CALL-SITE dual of `subst_inst_struct_span` (and the generic-STRUCT-head generalization of
## `subst_slice_ret_span`): a call to a GENERIC fn whose declared return type is a generic-struct
## APPLICATION at least one of whose top-level type-args names the callee's OWN type-PARAMETER
## (`mkbox := fn(T : type, x : T) -> Box(T)` called `mkbox(P, p)`). Each such arg is resolved to the
## type the CALL supplies at that parameter's position, and the resolved `Box(P)` is synthesized into
## `synth` (rebased, head-length — the same seam). Lets `bx := mkbox(P, p)` reserve + stage the FULL
## instantiated width instead of the one word the un-substituted `Box(T)` sizes to. 0/0 unless the
## return head is a GENERIC struct AND some type-arg resolves to an AGGREGATE, so every existing
## struct-returning call keeps its raw span (byte-identical → fixpoint-neutral).
subst_gen_struct_ret_span := fn(v : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena, synth : ptr(mut rt::Arena)) -> CSpan {
  mut res := CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
  match deref(v) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, ah) => {
      ci := ret_call_target(decls, src, cs, cl, nargs, ah, a)
      if ci >= 0 {
        d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(ci))))
        if d.is_generic and d.ret_tl != 0 {
          bn := base_type_name(src, d.ret_ts, d.ret_tl)
          sdi := struct_decl_of(decls, src, bn.s, bn.n)
          if sdi >= 0 {
            sd := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(sdi))))
            if sd.is_generic {
              ## PASS 1 (no allocation): does any return type-arg name a callee type-param whose CALL
              ## argument is an aggregate type? Only then does the instantiated layout differ.
              mut anyp := false
              mut k := 0
              mut go := true
              while go {
                ta := typearg_at(src, bn.s, bn.n, k)
                if ta.n == 0 { go = false }
                else {
                  pp := callee_param_pos(decls, usize(ci), src, ta.s, ta.n, a)
                  if pp >= 0 and usize(pp) < nargs {
                    cta := type_arg_at(ah, usize(pp), a)
                    if cta.n != 0 and ty_is_agg_span(decls, src, cta.s, cta.n) { anyp = true }
                  }
                  k += 1
                }
              }
              if anyp {
                mut msb := rt::strbuf(deref(synth), 192)
                push_str(msb, str_at((src + bn.s), bn.n))
                push_str(msb, "(")
                mut k2 := 0
                mut go2 := true
                while go2 {
                  ta := typearg_at(src, bn.s, bn.n, k2)
                  if ta.n == 0 { go2 = false }
                  else {
                    if k2 > 0 { push_str(msb, ", ") }
                    mut rs := ta.s
                    mut rn := ta.n
                    pp := callee_param_pos(decls, usize(ci), src, ta.s, ta.n, a)
                    if pp >= 0 and usize(pp) < nargs {
                      cta := type_arg_at(ah, usize(pp), a)
                      if cta.n != 0 { fa := full_app_span(src, cta.s, cta.n) ; rs = fa.s ; rn = fa.n }
                    }
                    push_str(msb, str_at((src + rs), rn))
                    k2 += 1
                  }
                }
                push_str(msb, ")")
                base_abs := unchecked bitcast(usize, rt::strbuf_base(msb))
                res = CSpan(s = unchecked (base_abs - src), n = bn.n)   ## rebase seam
              }
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  res
}

## `subst_inst_struct_span` keyed on the EMIT context: the RESOLVED generic-struct application span for
## a type reference written inside the current monomorphization INSTANCE (`Box(T)` → `Box(P)`), or the
## span UNCHANGED when nothing substitutes (a non-generic head, a scalar type-arg, a non-instance emit)
## — so every existing span consumer is byte-identical.
inst_struct_span_cx := fn(s : usize, n : usize, cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> CSpan {
  r := subst_inst_struct_span(s, n, cx.decls, cx.src, cx.mar, cx.gp_s, cx.gp_l, cx.it_s, cx.it_l, cx.gp2_s, cx.gp2_l, cx.it2_s, cx.it2_l, cx.gp3_s, cx.gp3_l, cx.it3_s, cx.it3_l)
  if r.n != 0 { return r }
  CSpan(s = s, n = n)
}

## The PARAM dual of `subst_enum_ret_span`: given a PARAM's type span `[pts, pts+ptl)` and the 3-way
## instance Subst (`gps/gpl → its/itl`, `gps2 → its2`, `gps3 → its3`), synthesize the SUBSTITUTED enum
## instance span when the param type is a GENERIC enum `G(<typeparam>…)` at least one of whose top-level
## type-args resolves to a MULTI-WORD struct. Written into the PERSISTENT `synth` arena (rebased to a
## `src`-relative handle, head-length `= G`'s name so `typearg_at` lands on the synthesized `(<elem>…)`),
## so `bind_param`'s enum branch can bind `self : Option(V)` as the concrete `Option(Rec)` — else the
## enum-value param slot kept the raw `Option(V)` span (`typearg_at` → `V` → `agg_words("V") == 1`), so
## `materialize_ref_enum`/`enum_inst_words` sized the payload ONE word (truncation) and the `match`
## bound the payload as a scalar. Returns 0/0 unless the param is a generic enum AND some type-arg
## substitutes to a `>=2`-word struct → a scalar / 1-word / non-generic param keeps its raw span
## (byte-identical for every existing enum-value param). The seam of `subst_slice_ret_span`, keyed on a
## type span instead of a Call, with the type-args resolved directly through the Subst (they name the
## callee's OWN type-params, so no receiver/fn-return recovery is needed).
subst_param_enum_span := fn(pts : usize, ptl : usize, decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena, synth : ptr(mut rt::Arena), gps : usize, gpl : usize, its : usize, itl : usize, gps2 : usize, gpl2 : usize, its2 : usize, itl2 : usize, gps3 : usize, gpl3 : usize, its3 : usize, itl3 : usize) -> CSpan {
  mut res := CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
  bn := base_type_name(src, pts, ptl)
  if enum_decl_of(decls, src, bn.s, bn.n) < 0 { return res }
  ## a GENERIC instance carries top-level type-args right after the head; a bare `E` has none.
  ta0 := typearg_at(src, bn.s, bn.n, 0)
  if ta0.n == 0 { return res }
  ## PASS 1 (no allocation): does any resolved type-arg become a `>=2`-word struct?
  mut anywide := false
  mut k := 0
  mut go := true
  while go {
    ta := typearg_at(src, bn.s, bn.n, k)
    if ta.n == 0 { go = false }
    else {
      mut rs := ta.s
      mut rn := ta.n
      if gpl != 0 and streq(src, rs, rn, gps, gpl) { rs = its; rn = itl }
      else if gpl2 != 0 and streq(src, rs, rn, gps2, gpl2) { rs = its2; rn = itl2 }
      else if gpl3 != 0 and streq(src, rs, rn, gps3, gpl3) { rs = its3; rn = itl3 }
      if struct_decl_of(decls, src, rs, rn) >= 0 and struct_words(decls, src, rs, rn, a) >= 2 { anywide = true }
      k += 1
    }
  }
  if anywide == false { return res }
  ## PASS 2: build `G(resolved,…)` into `synth` (bytes copied via `push_str`).
  mut msb := rt::strbuf(deref(synth), 128)
  push_str(msb, str_at((src + bn.s), bn.n))
  push_str(msb, "(")
  mut k2 := 0
  mut go2 := true
  while go2 {
    ta := typearg_at(src, bn.s, bn.n, k2)
    if ta.n == 0 { go2 = false }
    else {
      if k2 > 0 { push_str(msb, ", ") }
      mut rs := ta.s
      mut rn := ta.n
      if gpl != 0 and streq(src, rs, rn, gps, gpl) { rs = its; rn = itl }
      else if gpl2 != 0 and streq(src, rs, rn, gps2, gpl2) { rs = its2; rn = itl2 }
      else if gpl3 != 0 and streq(src, rs, rn, gps3, gpl3) { rs = its3; rn = itl3 }
      push_str(msb, str_at((src + rs), rn))
      k2 += 1
    }
  }
  push_str(msb, ")")
  base_abs := unchecked bitcast(usize, rt::strbuf_base(msb))
  ## rebase into a `src`-relative handle (modular pointer diff, wrapped back by `src + s`); length is
  ## the BASE head `bn.n` so `typearg_at` (starting at `s + n`) lands on the synthesized `(<elem>…)`.
  res = CSpan(s = unchecked (base_abs - src), n = bn.n)   ## INTENTIONAL modular underflow — rebase seam
  res
}

## The Ok-payload STRUCT type span of a `<call>?` whose callee returns `Result(Struct, E)` — else 0/0.
## A binding `x := <call>?` (the fallible-pass shape `tv := check_expr(v)?`) yields the Ok payload; when
## that payload is a STRUCT, `x` must be bound as that struct so `x.field` resolves to `x`'s base. Without
## this `x` was a bare scalar (no type span), and `x.tag` mis-resolved to `base-1` — reading a stale slot
## (the check_stmts type-mismatch miscompile: `dt.tag != tv.tag` read garbage → spurious/missed rejects).
try_ok_struct_span := fn(v : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> CSpan {
  match deref(v) {
    Expr::Try(inner) => {
      es := call_ret_enum_span_d(inner, decls, src, a)
      if es.n == 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
      ta := typearg_at(src, es.s, es.n, 0)
      if ta.n != 0 and struct_decl_of(decls, src, ta.s, ta.n) >= 0 { return CSpan(s = ta.s, n = ta.n) }
      return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
    }
    Expr::Unchecked(inner) => { return try_ok_struct_span(inner, decls, src, a) }
    Expr::Bitcast(inner, _bcs, _bcl) => { return try_ok_struct_span(inner, decls, src, a) }
    _ => { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  }
}

## ---- FN-11: type-erased `dyn fn(T…)->R` closure = a two-word {code, env} fat pair over EXPLICIT
## user storage (Functions §1.6 / Memory §6.3). `dyn` is a keyword type-constructor; construction is the
## prelude `dyn_over(ptr(mut store))` (store = a static closure held in a named place); the call `d(args)`
## is an indirect call through `code`, passing `env` leading. x86_64; single scalar capture in this slice.

## Is the local-annotation type text `[s, s+n)` a `dyn fn(…)->R` type (leading `dyn` keyword)?
is_dyn_type := fn(src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize) -> bool {
  if n < 4 { return false }
  if str_at((src + s), 3) != "dyn" { return false }
  c := str_at((src + s + 3), 1)
  c == " " or c == "\t" or c == "\n" or c == "\r"
}
## The number of USER parameters in a `dyn fn(T0, …)->R` annotation — top-level types inside `fn( )`
## (0 if the parameter list is empty). Depth-1 commas separate the params.
dyn_type_nuser := fn(src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize) -> usize {
  mut i := 0
  while i < n and str_at((src + s + i), 1) != "(" { i = i + 1 }
  if i >= n { return 0 }
  i = i + 1
  mut depth := 1
  mut commas := 0
  mut sawany := false
  while i < n and depth > 0 {
    c := str_at((src + s + i), 1)
    if c == "(" { depth = depth + 1 }
    else if c == ")" { depth = depth - 1 }
    else if c == "," and depth == 1 { commas = commas + 1 }
    else if depth == 1 and c != " " and c != "\t" and c != "\n" and c != "\r" { sawany = true }
    i = i + 1
  }
  if sawany == false { return 0 }
  commas + 1
}
## Is `e` a `dyn_over(...)` construction call?
is_dyn_over_call := fn(e : ptr(Expr), src : ptr(u8)) -> bool {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, np, ah) => { str_at((src + cs), cl) == "dyn_over" }
    _ => { false }
  }
}
## The Var span inside an `AddrOf(Var)` (`ptr(mut store)` → `store`), else empty. A single match kept
## standalone so the enclosing call helper never nests a deref-match on a bound child.
addr_inner_var_span := fn(e : ptr(Expr)) -> CSpan {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::AddrOf(inner) => { var_name_span(inner) }
    _ => { CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  }
}
## The STORE place name of a `dyn_over(ptr(mut store))` — arg0 is `AddrOf(Var store)`.
dyn_over_store_span := fn(e : ptr(Expr), a : rt::Arena) -> CSpan {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, np, ah) => { addr_inner_var_span(arg_expr_at(ah, 0, a)) }
    _ => { CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  }
}
## FnRef node parts (is-a-FnRef + its `fnpos` and module span).
FnRefInfo := struct { is_r : bool, fnpos : usize, ms : usize, ml : usize }
pub fnref_info := fn(e : ptr(Expr)) -> FnRefInfo {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::FnRef(fnpos, fms, fml) => { FnRefInfo(is_r = true, fnpos = fnpos, ms = fms, ml = fml) }
    _ => { FnRefInfo(is_r = false, fnpos = 0, ms = 0, ml = 0) }
  }
}
## The lifted-lambda decl index for `fnpos` (sentinel name_len==0, name_start==fnpos), or -1.
lam_idx_by_fnpos := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), fnpos : usize) -> i64 {
  cnt := rt::vec_len(deref(decls))
  mut res : i64 = 0 - 1
  mut i := 0
  while i < cnt {
    d := deref(decl_get(decls, i))
    if d.name_len == 0 and d.name_start == fnpos { res = i64(i) }
    i = i + 1
  }
  res
}
## The NAME span of a lifted lambda's LAST parameter (the single trailing capture, this slice), given
## the lambda decl index; empty if it has no params.
lam_last_param_span := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), lidx : usize) -> CSpan {
  d := deref(decl_get(decls, lidx))
  mut pp := d.params_head
  mut r := CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
  while pp != 0 {
    pm := deref(param_p(pp))
    r = CSpan(s = pm.ns, n = pm.nl)
    pp = pm.next
  }
  r
}
## FN-11 multi-capture: the NAME span of the lifted lambda's param at 0-based index `idx` (empty if out
## of range). The trailing capture params are the LAST `ncap` of these, in append (capture) order.
lam_param_span_at := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), lidx : usize, idx : usize) -> CSpan {
  d := deref(decl_get(decls, lidx))
  mut pp := d.params_head
  mut i := 0
  mut r := CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
  while pp != 0 {
    pm := deref(param_p(pp))
    if i == idx { r = CSpan(s = pm.ns, n = pm.nl) }
    i = i + 1
    pp = pm.next
  }
  r
}
## FN-11 multi-capture: the USER-argument count of the `dyn fn(…)->R` local built by
## `dyn_over(ptr(mut <store>))` for the store name `[s,n)` — read off that dyn local's type annotation.
## -1 if no such dyn_over binding exists. FLAT scan (the store binding, its `dyn_over`, and the dyn
## local sit in the same block); mirrors `stmts_have_dyn_over`'s inline `st = nx` advance.
dyn_store_nuser := fn(head : ptr(mut Stmt), src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize, a : rt::Arena) -> i64 {
  mut st := head
  mut res : i64 = 0 - 1
  while st != 0 {
    stx := deref(stmt_p(Stmt, st))
    match stx {
      Stmt::Assign(ans, anl, av, anx) => {
        if is_dyn_over_call(av, src) {
          sv := dyn_over_store_span(av, a)
          if sv.n != 0 and streq(src, sv.s, sv.n, s, n) {
            lt := local_type_span(src, ans, anl)
            if lt.n != 0 and is_dyn_type(src, lt.s, lt.n) { res = i64(dyn_type_nuser(src, lt.s, lt.n)) }
          }
        }
        st = anx
      }
      Stmt::While(c, b, nx) => { st = nx }
      Stmt::FieldAssign(bns, bnl, fns, fnl, fv, nx) => { st = nx }
      Stmt::FieldPathAssign(pl, fpv, nx) => { st = nx }
      Stmt::Return(rv, nx) => { st = nx }
      Stmt::If(c, th, el, nx) => { st = nx }
      Stmt::Match(sc, ah, nx) => { st = nx }
      Stmt::For(fns, fnl, lo, hi, b, nx) => { st = nx }
      Stmt::DerefAssign(p, dv, nx) => { st = nx }
      Stmt::IndexAssign(b, ix, iv, nx) => { st = nx }
      Stmt::IndexFieldAssign(b, ix, ifs, ifl, iv, nx) => { st = nx }
      Stmt::Loop(b, nx) => { st = nx }
      Stmt::Unchecked(b, nx) => { st = nx }
      Stmt::AllocWith(ae, b, nx) => { st = nx }
      Stmt::Break(_bv, _bd, nx) => { st = nx }
      Stmt::Continue(_cd, nx) => { st = nx }
      Stmt::ExprStmt(e, nx) => { st = nx }
      Stmt::CompIf(c, th, el, nx) => { st = nx }
      Stmt::CompFor(vs, vl, iv, b, nx) => { st = nx }
      Stmt::CompForRange(vs, vl, lo, hi, b, nx) => { st = nx }
      Stmt::CompMatch(sc, ah, nx) => { st = nx }
    }
  }
  res
}
## Does the statement list `head` bind `d := dyn_over(ptr(mut <name>))` for the store name `[s,n)`?
## (A FLAT scan of the current list — the store binding and its `dyn_over` sit in the same block.)
## The advance sets `st = nx` INSIDE each arm (a tail-match returning a `ptr` mis-lowers under the lean
## seed — mirror `collect_slots`' inline advance instead).
stmts_have_dyn_over := fn(head : ptr(mut Stmt), src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize, a : rt::Arena) -> bool {
  mut st := head
  mut res := false
  while st != 0 {
    stx := deref(stmt_p(Stmt, st))
    match stx {
      Stmt::Assign(ans, anl, av, anx) => {
        if is_dyn_over_call(av, src) {
          sv := dyn_over_store_span(av, a)
          if sv.n != 0 and streq(src, sv.s, sv.n, s, n) { res = true }
        }
        st = anx
      }
      Stmt::While(c, b, nx) => { st = nx }
      Stmt::FieldAssign(bns, bnl, fns, fnl, fv, nx) => { st = nx }
      Stmt::FieldPathAssign(pl, fpv, nx) => { st = nx }
      Stmt::Return(rv, nx) => { st = nx }
      Stmt::If(c, th, el, nx) => { st = nx }
      Stmt::Match(sc, ah, nx) => { st = nx }
      Stmt::For(fns, fnl, lo, hi, b, nx) => { st = nx }
      Stmt::DerefAssign(p, dv, nx) => { st = nx }
      Stmt::IndexAssign(b, ix, iv, nx) => { st = nx }
      Stmt::IndexFieldAssign(b, ix, ifs, ifl, iv, nx) => { st = nx }
      Stmt::Loop(b, nx) => { st = nx }
      Stmt::Unchecked(b, nx) => { st = nx }
      Stmt::AllocWith(ae, b, nx) => { st = nx }
      Stmt::Break(_bv, _bd, nx) => { st = nx }
      Stmt::Continue(_cd, nx) => { st = nx }
      Stmt::ExprStmt(e, nx) => { st = nx }
      Stmt::CompIf(c, th, el, nx) => { st = nx }
      Stmt::CompFor(vs, vl, iv, b, nx) => { st = nx }
      Stmt::CompForRange(vs, vl, lo, hi, b, nx) => { st = nx }
      Stmt::CompMatch(sc, ah, nx) => { st = nx }
    }
  }
  res
}
## Bind a `dyn`-typed local as a two-word {code, env} fat pair (ek 11). Layout mirrors `bind_str_slot`:
## the env FILLER first (higher address), then the base (code, word 0 at `off`).
bind_dyn_slot := fn(in out slots : SVec, src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize) {
  ## I11: reject a rebinding whose existing reservation is NARROWER than this one needs (see
  ## `require_reservation`) — the wide store would land on the neighbouring locals' slots.
  require_reservation(ptr(slots), src, s, n, 2)
  existing := slot_of(ptr(slots), src, s, n)
  if existing >= 0 { return }
  fo := svec_len(ptr(slots))
  svec_push(slots, SlotEntry(ns = 0, nl = 0, off = fo, sns = 0, snl = 0, ek = 0, estride = 1, eek = 0, is_ref = false))
  off := svec_len(ptr(slots))
  svec_push(slots, SlotEntry(ns = s, nl = n, off = off, sns = 0, snl = 0, ek = 11, estride = 1, eek = 0, is_ref = false))
}
## Bind a static-closure STORE local (the env storage, ek 12) as `ncap` capture words (one per captured
## value; §4 UP-GROWING: the named base = env word 0 at the LOW slot, then `ncap-1` anonymous fillers for
## words 1.. below it). Stash the lifted lambda's `fnpos`/module span so `dyn_over` can build the adapter
## (sns = fnpos, snl = module-start, estride = module-len) and the capture COUNT in `eek` so the store +
## adapter agree on how many words to move.
bind_dyn_store_slot := fn(in out slots : SVec, src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize, fnpos : usize, fms : usize, fml : usize, ncap : usize) {
  existing := slot_of(ptr(slots), src, s, n)
  if existing >= 0 { return }
  off := svec_len(ptr(slots))
  svec_push(slots, SlotEntry(ns = s, nl = n, off = off, sns = fnpos, snl = fms, ek = 12, estride = fml, eek = u8(ncap), is_ref = false))
  mut j := 1
  while j < ncap {
    fo := svec_len(ptr(slots))
    svec_push(slots, SlotEntry(ns = 0, nl = 0, off = fo, sns = 0, snl = 0, ek = 0, estride = 1, eek = 0, is_ref = false))
    j = j + 1
  }
}

## Append the full instance TYPE text of a generic-enum LITERAL value `v` (`E.V(payload…)`) into `msb`,
## recursing into an aggregate (enum-literal) payload — so `Option.Some(Option.Some(42))` writes the text
## `Option(Option(T))`. For a NON-generic enum, or a payload that is not itself an aggregate literal, the
## corresponding type-param slot keeps the enum's OWN param NAME (`T`/`E`), which stays a 1-word scalar
## leaf — exactly right for slot sizing (`enum_inst_words`) and for the outer `match`'s payload dispatch
## (only the top-level type-arg's BASE name is consulted to decide the binding kind). A struct/enum
## literal or aggregate VAR payload writes its full source type; anything else writes nothing.
append_lit_type := fn(in out msb : strbuf::StrBuf, v : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena, slots : ptr(SVec)) {
  ef := enum_lit_full(v)
  if ef.is_e == false {
    si := struct_lit_info(v)
    if si.is_s { push_str(msb, str_at((src + si.ss), si.sl)) }
    return
  }
  push_str(msb, str_at((src + ef.es), ef.el))            ## the enum BASE name (`Option`)
  di := enum_decl_of(decls, src, ef.es, ef.el)
  if di < 0 { return }
  d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(di))))
  if d.is_generic == false { return }
  ## Locate this variant's (single-component) payload FIELD to learn which type-PARAM the payload binds.
  mut vf := d.fields_head
  mut vfts := 0
  mut vftl := 0
  while vf != 0 {
    fd := deref(fld_p(vf))
    if streq(src, fd.ns, fd.nl, ef.vs, ef.vl) and fd.arity >= 1 { vfts = fd.ts; vftl = fd.tl }
    vf = fd.next
  }
  bound_pi := param_pos(decls, usize(di), src, vfts, vftl, a)   ## the param index this variant's payload binds (-1 if none)
  ## Emit one type-arg per type-PARAM, in declaration order: the param the payload binds resolves to the
  ## payload arg's (recursively synthesized) type when the arg is itself an aggregate literal; every other
  ## param (and a scalar payload) keeps the param's own NAME.
  push_str(msb, "(")
  mut p := d.params_head
  mut pi := 0
  while p != 0 {
    pm := deref(param_p(p))
    if pi > 0 { push_str(msb, ", ") }
    mut done := false
    if i64(pi) == bound_pi {
      arg0 := arg_expr_at(ef.phead, 0, a)
      va := var_agg_info(arg0, slots, src)
      if enum_lit_full(arg0).is_e or struct_lit_info(arg0).is_s {
        append_lit_type(msb, arg0, decls, src, a, slots)
        done = true
      } else if va.ek == 2 or va.ek == 3 {
        push_str(msb, str_at((src + va.s), va.n))
        done = true
      } else if enum_ret_call_d(arg0, decls, src, a) {
        ## A bare `Option.Some(inner())` has no payload type at the constructor spelling. Use the
        ## enum-returning CALL's declared result as the same type witness that the emitter uses for
        ## register staging, so the inferred outer instance remains `Option(E)` rather than bare
        ## `Option(T)`.
        ces := call_ret_enum_span_d(arg0, decls, src, a)
        if ces.n != 0 { push_str(msb, str_at((src + ces.s), ces.n)); done = true }
      }
    }
    if done == false { push_str(msb, str_at((src + pm.ns), pm.nl)) }
    pi += 1
    p = pm.next
  }
  push_str(msb, ")")
}
## Synthesize the FULL instance TYPE span of a generic-enum LITERAL `v` into the persistent `synth` arena,
## returning a `src`-relative rebased handle whose length is the BASE-head length (the `subst_enum_ret_span`
## seam — `base_type_name` reads the head, `typearg_at` starts at `s + n` and lands on the synthesized
## `(…)`). `0/0` when `v` is not a generic-enum literal, so a caller keeps the bare span (byte-identical).
synth_lit_type := fn(v : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena, synth : ptr(mut rt::Arena), slots : ptr(SVec)) -> CSpan {
  ef := enum_lit_full(v)
  if ef.is_e == false { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  di := enum_decl_of(decls, src, ef.es, ef.el)
  if di < 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(di))))
  if d.is_generic == false { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  mut msb := rt::strbuf(deref(synth), 256)
  append_lit_type(msb, v, decls, src, a, slots)
  base_abs := unchecked bitcast(usize, rt::strbuf_base(msb))
  ## rebase the synthesized text into a `src`-relative handle (modular pointer diff — the `subst_enum_ret_span`
  ## seam); length = the base-head (`ef.el`) so `typearg_at` scans the trailing `(…)`.
  CSpan(s = unchecked (base_abs - src), n = ef.el)
}

## Bind an explicitly uninitialized local from its declared type rather than the parser's zero sentinel.
## This keeps a later whole-aggregate assignment inside the correctly sized frame slot. Arrays are left to
## the dedicated array-default/definite-assignment slice; scalar, struct, enum, raw-union, and str types
## all have established slot binders here.
bind_uninit_slot := fn(in out slots : SVec, src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize, decls : ptr(rt::Vec), a : rt::Arena) {
  lts := local_type_span(src, s, n)
  if lts.n == 0 { bind_slot(slots, src, s, n); return }
  if str_at((src + lts.s), 1) == "[" {
    nel := parse_arr_len(src, lts.s, lts.n)
    esp := array_elem_span(src, lts.s, lts.n)
    beek := byte_type_eek(src, esp.s, esp.n)
    if esp.n != 0 and beek != 0 {
      bind_array_slot(slots, src, s, n, nel, AElem(eek = beek, ess = 0, esl = 0, stride = 1))
    } else {
      if nel != 0 and esp.n != 0 {
        if struct_decl_of(decls, src, esp.s, esp.n) >= 0 {
          ## §8 ARRAY-OF-@packed — the uninitialized declared-type path has no array literal for
          ## `arr_elem_info` to inspect. Its fallback is word-strided, so reject the same deferred
          ## byte-strided form here rather than reserving a word layout that silently miscompiles.
          if is_packed(decls, src, esp.s, esp.n) { panic("selfhost: an array whose element is a @packed struct is not supported (byte-precise element stride + packed field offsets would need byte-granular array addressing, a deferred slice); rejected rather than silently miscompiled to a word-padded layout") }
          bind_array_slot(slots, src, s, n, nel, AElem(eek = 2, ess = esp.s, esl = esp.n, stride = struct_words(decls, src, esp.s, esp.n, a)))
        } else {
          if enum_decl_of(decls, src, esp.s, esp.n) >= 0 {
            bind_array_slot(slots, src, s, n, nel, AElem(eek = 3, ess = esp.s, esl = esp.n, stride = 1 + enum_inst_words(decls, src, esp.s, esp.n, a)))
          } else {
            bind_slot_typed(slots, src, s, n, lts.s, lts.n)
          }
        }
      } else {
        bind_slot_typed(slots, src, s, n, lts.s, lts.n)
      }
    }
  } else {
    if struct_decl_of(decls, src, lts.s, lts.n) >= 0 {
      bind_struct_slot(slots, src, s, n, lts.s, lts.n, struct_words(decls, src, lts.s, lts.n, a))
    } else {
      if enum_decl_of(decls, src, lts.s, lts.n) >= 0 {
        if is_union_decl(decls, src, lts.s, lts.n) {
          bind_struct_slot(slots, src, s, n, lts.s, lts.n, union_words(decls, src, lts.s, lts.n, a))
        } else {
          bind_enum_slot(slots, src, s, n, lts.s, lts.n, 1 + enum_inst_words(decls, src, lts.s, lts.n, a))
        }
      } else {
        if str_at((src + lts.s), lts.n) == "str" {
          bind_str_slot(slots, src, s, n)
        } else {
          bind_slot_typed(slots, src, s, n, lts.s, lts.n)
        }
      }
    }
  }
}


## Append a fresh ".L<n>" label name to `sb` (no newline, no colon) and return the next
## counter value. Callers use it both to define a label (`.L<n>:`) and to reference it in
## a jump (`jmp .L<n>`) — the same number for both, threaded through `nl`.
emit_label := fn(in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, n : usize) {
  push_str(sb, ".L")
  push_int(sb, i64(n))
}

## Resolve `ptr(<place>)`'s place to a frame-slot offset when the place is a local `Var`
## (the only AddrOf place this slice supports — `leaq` its frame slot). Returns the slot offset
## or -1 if the inner expression is not a `Var`. The deref-`match` on the inner pointer PARAM
## stays here (a direct param) — the lowerable shape (mirroring `struct_lit_info`/`field_slot`;
## matching `deref(ptr)` on an enum-pattern-bound pointer is not lowered, so it is isolated).
addr_var_slot := fn(p : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> i64 {
  match deref(p) {
    Expr::Var(s, n) => { slot_of(cx.slots, cx.src, s, n) }
    _ => { -1 }
  }
}


## A `match` scrutinee analysis: whether the scrutinee is an enum-typed `Var` local, and (if
## so) its base slot + enum TYPE name span (to resolve variant names → discriminant indices
## and payload slots). The deref-match on the scrutinee pointer PARAM stays here — the
## lowerable shape (like `struct_lit_info`/`field_slot`).
## `is_ref` marks an enum scrutinee reached **through a pointer** (a by-reference enum
## parameter, `bind_param`'s enum case): `base` is then the slot holding the POINTER, not an
## inline enum — the match materializes the pointee's words into the scratch temp first.
ScrutInfo := struct { is_e : bool, base : usize, es : usize, el : usize, is_ref : bool, tmod_s : usize, tmod_l : usize }

## Append the System V integer ARGUMENT register for parameter/argument index `i` (0-based)
## directly to `sb`: %rdi %rsi %rdx %rcx %r8 %r9. Index 6+ has NO register — arguments 7+ are
## passed ON THE STACK (see `emit_call_args` / `emit_fn`'s param binding / `nstack_args`). Every
## caller guards `i < 6`, so the out-of-range placeholder is never reached.
##
## Emits the literal DIRECTLY rather than RETURNING a `str` consumed by `emit_argreg(sb, i)`:
## a str-returning call passed as a by-ref `str` argument has no `emit_arg` materialization case
## (only str literals + `str_at` views are materialized into a temp + passed by address), so it
## fell to the bare-expression path and pushed the str's data POINTER as the by-ref pointer —
## `sb_str` then read `len` at `-8(ptr)` (garbage) / crashed on a null. Direct emission sidesteps
## both the str-return ABI and the str-returning-call-arg gap.
emit_argreg := fn(in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, i : usize) {
  match i {
    0 => { push_str(sb, "%rdi") }
    1 => { push_str(sb, "%rsi") }
    2 => { push_str(sb, "%rdx") }
    3 => { push_str(sb, "%rcx") }
    4 => { push_str(sb, "%r8") }
    5 => { push_str(sb, "%r9") }
    _ => { push_str(sb, "%rdi") }
  }
}

## The i-th SSE argument register (`%xmm0`..`%xmm7`) — the float dual of `emit_argreg`. SysV passes
## floating-point arguments in xmm0.. and counts them INDEPENDENTLY of the integer argument registers.
emit_xmmreg := fn(in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, i : usize) {
  match i {
    0 => { push_str(sb, "%xmm0") }
    1 => { push_str(sb, "%xmm1") }
    2 => { push_str(sb, "%xmm2") }
    3 => { push_str(sb, "%xmm3") }
    4 => { push_str(sb, "%xmm4") }
    5 => { push_str(sb, "%xmm5") }
    6 => { push_str(sb, "%xmm6") }
    7 => { push_str(sb, "%xmm7") }
    _ => { push_str(sb, "%xmm0") }
  }
}

## The k-th STRUCT-RETURN register, extending the %rax:%rdx convention to up to 7 words so a wide
## (4-7 word) struct (`IVLayout`/`ScrutInfo`/`AElem`/`StrInfo`) is returned in registers rather
## than truncated to 3 words (the self-host lower has no sret). Words 0-2 ride %rax/%rdx/%rcx
## (the established convention), words 3-6 ride the caller-saved %r8/%r9/%r10/%r11 — safe because
## the deliver (at the callee's return) and the receive (right after the `call`) are adjacent with
## no intervening clobber. Emitted directly (not a `str` return — see `emit_argreg`).
emit_retreg := fn(in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, k : usize) {
  match k {
    0 => { push_str(sb, "%rax") }
    1 => { push_str(sb, "%rdx") }
    2 => { push_str(sb, "%rcx") }
    3 => { push_str(sb, "%r8") }
    4 => { push_str(sb, "%r9") }
    5 => { push_str(sb, "%r10") }
    _ => { push_str(sb, "%r11") }
  }
}

## The i-th argument's `Expr` pointer from an arena-linked `Arg` list (`head`, 0 = end), or 0
## if out of range. Used by the `len`/`str_eq` builtin lowering to reach their str operands.

## Lower `str_eq(a, b)` — a byte-by-byte string equality builtin. The two str operands' {ptr,
## len} pairs are pushed (`emit_str_pair`), then popped into registers (b on top): blen→%rcx,
## bptr→%rdx, alen→%rsi, aptr→%rdi. If the lengths differ the result is 0; otherwise a counted
## loop compares byte `i` of each (`movzbq (%rdi,%r8), %rax` vs `(%rdx,%r8)`), short-circuiting
## to 0 on the first mismatch; falling through the loop yields 1. The 0/1 result is pushed.
## Fresh labels via `nl` so nested `str_eq` never collide.
## Whether expr `e` is a STR-valued operand (so `e == e2` / `e != e2` must compare CONTENT, not the
## scalar {ptr} word). A `StrLit`, a `str_at`/`sub` view, a str-returning call, or a `Var` bound as a
## str local (ek 4). Used to route `==`/`!=` on strings to the byte-compare instead of a ptr compare
## (the self-host lower otherwise compared str by IDENTITY — two equal strings at different source
## offsets mis-compared, breaking every `streq`-based name lookup at a non-binding occurrence).
is_str_operand := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> bool {
  si := str_lit_info(e)
  if si.is_s { return true }
  if is_strat_call(e, cx.src) { return true }
  if is_sub_call(e, cx.src) { return true }
  if is_slice_expr(e) { return true }
  if str_ret_call(e, cx.decls, cx.src, deref(cx.mar)) { return true }
  vn := var_name_span(e)
  if vn.n != 0 {
    ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, vn.s, vn.n)))
    ## `entry_of` returns slot 0 for a module-global or otherwise unbound name. Check the
    ## recovered entry's name before using its kind; otherwise any scalar global can inherit the
    ## string kind of an unrelated first local and route a numeric expression into `emit_str_pair`.
    if streq(cx.src, ent.ns, ent.nl, vn.s, vn.n) and ent.ek == 4 { return true }
  }
  ## Reuse the call-edge classifier for every direct str-element index: fixed `[str; N]` arrays and
  ## typed `Slice(str)` views both carry `eek == 4`, while a nominal `Slice(str)` VALUE return is
  ## recovered by `slice_var_is_str_elem`. Without this, `s[0] == "x"` fell through to scalar
  ## lowering even though `emit_str_pair` already knew how to materialize the pair.
  if call_arg_is_str_elem(e, cx) { return true }
  ## `deref(p)` where `p` points at a §7 VIEW — the pointee IS the two-word `{ptr, len}` pair, which
  ## `emit_str_pair`'s `Deref` arm materializes. Recognizing it here is what lets an ENUM PAYLOAD carry
  ## the whole pair (`payload_agg_words` → 2): a generic container's fallible element read
  ## (`alloc::vec::get` at `T = str`, `Option(T).Some(deref(elem))`) otherwise delivered ONLY the
  ## pointer word and the caller read a garbage length — a wrong value.
  if deref_view_span_cx(e, cx).n != 0 { return true }
  false
}

## The byte-compare equality of two already-resolved str operand exprs — pushes 1 (equal) / 0. The
## core of both the `str_eq(a,b)` builtin and the `==`/`!=` operator over str operands.
emit_str_eq_core := fn(a0 : ptr(Expr), a1 : ptr(Expr), in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena, in out nl : usize) {
  emit_str_pair(a0, sb, cx, a, nl)
  emit_str_pair(a1, sb, cx, a, nl)
  lne := nl          ## not-equal (result 0)
  ldone := nl + 1    ## done
  lloop := nl + 2    ## loop head
  nl += 3
  ## pop b (top) then a: blen, bptr, alen, aptr
  push_str(sb, "  popq %rcx\n  popq %rdx\n  popq %rsi\n  popq %rdi\n")
  ## lengths must match
  push_str(sb, "  cmpq %rcx, %rsi\n  jne ")
  emit_label(sb, lne)
  push_str(sb, "\n")
  push_str(sb, "  movq $0, %r8\n")
  emit_label(sb, lloop)
  push_str(sb, ":\n  cmpq %rsi, %r8\n  jge ")
  emit_label(sb, ldone)
  push_str(sb, "\n")
  push_str(sb, "  movzbq (%rdi,%r8), %rax\n  movzbq (%rdx,%r8), %r9\n  cmpq %r9, %rax\n  jne ")
  emit_label(sb, lne)
  push_str(sb, "\n  addq $1, %r8\n  jmp ")
  emit_label(sb, lloop)
  push_str(sb, "\n")
  emit_label(sb, lne)
  push_str(sb, ":\n  pushq $0\n  jmp ")
  lend := nl
  nl += 1
  emit_label(sb, lend)
  push_str(sb, "\n")
  emit_label(sb, ldone)
  push_str(sb, ":\n  pushq $1\n")
  emit_label(sb, lend)
  push_str(sb, ":\n")
}

emit_str_eq := fn(args_head : ptr(mut Arg), in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena, in out nl : usize) {
  a0 := arg_expr_at(args_head, 0, a)
  a1 := arg_expr_at(args_head, 1, a)
  emit_str_eq_core(a0, a1, sb, cx, a, nl)
}

## If `e` is a struct payload (a struct LITERAL or a struct-typed VAR), its word count; 0 otherwise.
## Used by `emit_enum_value` to decide whether a variant payload rides more than one return register.
payload_agg_words := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena) -> usize {
  si := struct_lit_info(e)
  if si.is_s { return struct_words(cx.decls, cx.src, si.ss, si.sl, deref(cx.mar)) }
  va := var_agg_info(e, cx.slots, cx.src)
  if va.ek == 2 { return struct_words(cx.decls, cx.src, va.s, va.n, deref(cx.mar)) }
  ## a `str` payload (`Some("x")` / `Some(strvar)` / `Some(trim(s))`) is a 2-word {ptr, len} — without
  ## this it fell to the scalar path and only the ptr (word 0) reached the payload register, so
  ## `Option(str)` (and any str-payload variant) truncated the len. `is_str_operand` covers a str
  ## literal / `str_at` / sub / slice / a str-returning call / a str Var (ek 4).
  if is_str_operand(e, cx) { return 2 }
  0
}

## The WORD count of an ENUM-VALUED payload expression — an `EnumLit` ctor (`Err.Bad(x)`) or an
## enum `Var` local — as `1 + enum_inst_words` of its enum type; 0 if the payload is not an enum
## value. The enum dual of `payload_agg_words`: lets a nested-enum variant payload (`R.Fail(Err.Bad(x))`)
## ride the payload registers WHOLE (disc + its own words) rather than being truncated to word 0 (the
## inner discriminant), which dropped every word past the first (the inner payload → garbage).
payload_enum_words := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena) -> usize {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::EnumLit(es, el, vs, vl, np, phead) => {
      if enum_decl_of(cx.decls, cx.src, es, el) >= 0 { return 1 + enum_inst_words(cx.decls, cx.src, es, el, a) }
      0
    }
    Expr::Var(s, n) => {
      va := var_agg_info(e, cx.slots, cx.src)
      if va.ek == 3 { return 1 + enum_inst_words(cx.decls, cx.src, va.s, va.n, a) }
      0
    }
    _ => { 0 }
  }
}

## The WORD count of an ENUM-VALUED CALL payload. A call has no local slot or literal node from which
## `payload_enum_words` can recover the inner type, but its declared return span is the exact witness.
## Keep this beside the literal/Var helper so every enum payload source uses the same register-width
## calculation. The register-return slice is deliberately capped at six payload words: an outer enum
## would otherwise exceed the seven-word register convention and must use the separate SRET path.
payload_call_enum_words := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena) -> usize {
  if expr_is_call(e) == false { return 0 }
  if enum_ret_call_d(e, cx.decls, cx.src, a) == false { return 0 }
  esp := call_ret_enum_span_d(e, cx.decls, cx.src, a)
  if esp.n == 0 { return 0 }
  ew := 1 + enum_inst_words(cx.decls, cx.src, esp.s, esp.n, a)
  if ew > 6 { panic("selfhost: an enum CALL used as an enum payload exceeds the register-return budget; wide nested payloads need the SRET path") }
  ew
}

## Guard the remaining unsupported enum-payload CALL shapes. A resolved enum return now has a complete
## width witness and is handled by the shared call staging/store paths; unresolved generic calls must
## still fail before scalar lowering can silently retain only the inner discriminant.
reject_unsupported_enum_call_payload := fn(es : usize, el : usize, vs : usize, vl : usize, e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena) {
  if expr_is_call(e) == false { return }
  ## A resolved enum-returning CALL now has a complete register-width witness and is handled by the
  ## shared payload shift in `emit_enum_value`; keep the old guard only for unresolved/unsupported
  ## generic cases where falling back to one scalar word would still be unsound.
  if payload_call_enum_words(e, cx, a) > 0 { return }
  eb := base_type_name(cx.src, es, el)
  et := name_tail(cx.src, eb.s, eb.n)
  if str_at((cx.src + et.s), et.n) != "Option" and str_at((cx.src + et.s), et.n) != "Result" { return }
  pty := variant_payload_type(cx.decls, cx.src, es, el, vs, vl, a)
  mut is_tryable := false
  mut is_named_enum := false
  if pty.n != 0 {
    pb := base_type_name(cx.src, pty.s, pty.n)
    pt := name_tail(cx.src, pb.s, pb.n)
    is_tryable = str_at((cx.src + pt.s), pt.n) == "Option" or str_at((cx.src + pt.s), pt.n) == "Result"
  }
  ## A bare `Option.Some(...)` has no concrete type-argument span at the constructor site, so the
  ## generic `T` from `variant_payload_type` is unresolved. The call's declared return is the missing
  ## witness (`inner() -> Result(...)`); use the same resolver as enum-call result staging.
  if is_tryable == false and is_named_enum == false {
    rty := call_ret_enum_span_d(e, cx.decls, cx.src, a)
    if rty.n != 0 {
      rb := base_type_name(cx.src, rty.s, rty.n)
      rt := name_tail(cx.src, rb.s, rb.n)
      is_tryable = str_at((cx.src + rt.s), rt.n) == "Option" or str_at((cx.src + rt.s), rt.n) == "Result"
      if is_tryable == false and enum_decl_of(cx.decls, cx.src, rb.s, rb.n) >= 0 {
        is_named_enum = enum_inst_words(cx.decls, cx.src, rty.s, rty.n, a) > 0
      }
    }
  }
  if is_tryable or is_named_enum {
    panic("selfhost: an Option/Result enum CALL payload has no resolvable complete layout; bind the inner value first or use a supported register-return shape")
  }
}

## The STRUCT word count of a variant `[vs,vn)`'s payload when the payload EXPRESSION is a call (so
## `payload_agg_words` can't read it), resolved from the variant's DECLARED payload type
## (`variant_payload_type`) substituted through the enclosing instance's type-params (`gp`/`gp2`).
## 0 if the payload is not a >0-word struct. So `Option(U).Some(f(v))` in a `U = Pt` instance reports
## Pt's 2 words → the enum ctor stages the fn value's struct return whole (not truncated to one word).
payload_call_struct_words := fn(es : usize, el : usize, vs : usize, vl : usize, cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena) -> usize {
  pty := variant_payload_type(cx.decls, cx.src, es, el, vs, vl, a)
  if pty.n == 0 { return 0 }
  mut rs := pty.s
  mut rn := pty.n
  if cx.gp_l != 0 and cx.it_l != 0 and streq(cx.src, rs, rn, cx.gp_s, cx.gp_l) { rs = cx.it_s; rn = cx.it_l }
  else if cx.gp2_l != 0 and cx.it2_l != 0 and streq(cx.src, rs, rn, cx.gp2_s, cx.gp2_l) { rs = cx.it2_s; rn = cx.it2_l }
  if struct_decl_of(cx.decls, cx.src, rs, rn) >= 0 { return struct_words(cx.decls, cx.src, rs, rn, a) }
  0
}

## Materialize an ENUM-VALUED expression into the two-register return convention: the
## discriminant word in %rax, the first payload word in %rdx. This is how an enum/aggregate
## value is delivered out of a function (an enum return), consumed by a call's caller (a
## `match` over a call result), and tested by the `?` operator. (≤2-word enums — one
## discriminant + one payload word — which is the toy convention: success = variant 0 with a
## single payload word.) Three sources of an enum value:
##  - an enum-typed `Var` LOCAL — read its discriminant slot into %rax, its payload slot
##    (`base+1`) into %rdx;
##  - an `EnumLit` `E.V(p)` construction — load the variant's declaration index into %rax,
##    lower the (first) payload expression and pop it into %rdx (a nullary variant → %rdx 0);
##  - a `Call` to an enum-returning fn — lower the args, `call`, and the callee already left
##    %rax (disc) / %rdx (payload) per this same convention. No `pushq %rax` (unlike the
##    generic Call lowering) — the registers ARE the result.
## The deref-`match` on the expr pointer stays here (a direct PARAM) — the lowerable shape.
emit_enum_value := fn(e : ptr(Expr), in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena, in out nl : usize) {
  ## An enum-underlying require alias has the same register representation as its enum, but its
  ## constructor must preserve/check the complete value before those registers are exposed. Keep this
  ## before the ordinary enum source match so a require CALL never falls through to the scalar-word
  ## call arm or to the old null-enum fallback.
  if require_agg_parts(e, cx.decls, cx.src, a).ok {
    emit_require_agg_value(e, sb, cx, a, nl)
    return
  }
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Var(s, n) => {
      ## deliver the enum local's words into the return registers: disc (word 0) → %rax, payload
      ## word i → `emit_retreg(i+1)` (%rdx, %rcx, …). The word COUNT is `1 + max-payload` of the
      ## enum type — a single-payload-word enum emits exactly %rax + %rdx (byte-identical to the
      ## former 2-register convention); a wider payload (e.g. `Option(Pt)`, a 2-word struct) rides
      ## the extra registers so it is not truncated across the return (the register dual of a
      ## multi-word struct return; `emit_retreg` covers up to %r11 = disc + 6 payload words).
      ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, s, n)))
      tw := 1 + enum_type_payload_words(cx.decls, cx.src, ent.sns, ent.snl, deref(cx.mar))
      if ent.is_ref {
        ## a BY-REFERENCE enum PARAM (a generic `id(T, v) -> T` returning its enum param): the slot
        ## holds a POINTER to the caller's enum word 0; load it (into %r11 = retreg 6, free for ≤7
        ## words) and deliver word k from `-(k*8)(%r11)` (down-growing pointee) — the enum dual of the
        ## struct is_ref return. Without this the disc/payload came back as the pointer + garbage.
        push_str(sb, "  movq -")
        push_int(sb, i64((ent.off + 1) * 8))
        push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %r11\n")
        for k in 0..tw {
          push_str(sb, "  movq ")
          push_int(sb, i64(k * 8))
          push_str(sb, "(%r11), ")
          emit_retreg(sb, k)
          push_str(sb, "\n")
        }
      } else {
        for k in 0..tw {
          push_str(sb, "  movq -")
          push_frame_word(sb, ent.off, k)
          push_str(sb, "(%rbp), ")
          emit_retreg(sb, k)
          push_str(sb, "\n")
        }
      }
    }
    Expr::EnumLit(es, el, vs, vl, np, phead) => {
      disc := variant_index(cx.decls, cx.src, es, el, vs, vl, deref(cx.mar))
      ## a MULTI-word STRUCT payload (`Some(v)` / `Some(Pt(x,y))`, v a >1-word struct) rides the
      ## payload registers %rdx, %rcx, … (word i → `emit_retreg(i+1)`) so it is not truncated to
      ## word 0. Computed only for a single-arg payload; a scalar / 1-word payload keeps the exact
      ## former single-register (%rdx) emission (byte-identical). The discriminant → %rax LAST (the
      ## payload lowering clobbers %rax).
      mut pw := 0
      mut pcall := false
      mut parray := false
      if phead != 0 and np == 1 {
        ga0 := deref(arg_p(phead))
        reject_unsupported_enum_call_payload(es, el, vs, vl, ga0.e, cx, a)
        apw := enum_lit_array_payload_words(e, cx.decls, cx.src, a)
        if apw != 0 {
          if apw > 6 { panic("selfhost: an enum array payload wider than 6 words needs the wide enum result-pointer path") }
          if array_lit_info(ga0.e).is_a == false { panic("selfhost: an enum array payload must be constructed from an array literal in a register-returning value") }
          if cx.agg_tmp < 0 { panic("selfhost: an enum array payload needs an aggregate-value temp block") }
          aoff := agg_alloc(cx)
          emit_array_assign(ga0.e, i64(aoff), sb, cx, nl)
          for k in 0..apw {
            push_str(sb, "  movq -")
            push_frame_word(sb, aoff, k)
            push_str(sb, "(%rbp), ")
            emit_retreg(sb, k + 1)
            push_str(sb, "\n")
          }
          pw = apw
          parray = true
        } else {
          pw = payload_agg_words(ga0.e, cx, a)
        }
        if parray == false and pw == 0 and expr_is_call(ga0.e) {
          cw := payload_call_struct_words(es, el, vs, vl, cx, a)
          if cw > 1 { pw = cw; pcall = true }
        }
        ## a nested-ENUM payload (`R.Fail(Err.Bad(x))` / `R.Fail(e)`, e an enum local): the inner enum
        ## value spans `1 + its own arity` words. `emit_enum_value(ga0.e)` leaves them in retreg(0..ew-1)
        ## just like a struct-returning call, so reuse the `pcall` SHIFT path (word k → payload reg k+1),
        ## delivering the inner disc + payload whole instead of only its word 0. Include the
        ## single-word/nullary enum case: its discriminant is still the payload value, and scalar
        ## fallthrough emits zero instead of the nested variant's actual discriminant.
        if pw == 0 {
          ew := payload_enum_words(ga0.e, cx, a)
          if ew > 0 { pw = ew; pcall = true }
        }
        ## An enum-returning CALL has the same whole-value register shape as an enum Var/literal, but
        ## its width is recoverable only from the callee's declared return type. Without this witness
        ## `Err(make_e())`/`Some(inner())` fell through to scalar emission and retained only the inner
        ## discriminant. The shared pcall shift then moves disc + every payload word one register up.
        if pw == 0 {
          ew := payload_call_enum_words(ga0.e, cx, a)
          if ew > 0 { pw = ew; pcall = true }
        }
      }
      if parray {
        ## The array literal was materialized above; its words already occupy payload return registers.
      } else if pcall {
        ## Multi-word aggregate/enum CALL payload (`Some(f(v))`, `Some(inner())`): emit the call (its
        ## whole return lands in retreg(0..pw-1) = %rax:%rdx:%rcx:…), then SHIFT each word up one
        ## register (word k → payload reg k+1) so the payload rides %rdx.. and %rax is free for the
        ## outer discriminant. The Call arm below leaves the registers (no push).
        ga := deref(arg_p(phead))
        emit_enum_value(ga.e, sb, cx, a, nl)
        mut j := pw
        while j > 0 {
          push_str(sb, "  movq ")
          emit_retreg(sb, j - 1)
          push_str(sb, ", ")
          emit_retreg(sb, j)
          push_str(sb, "\n")
          j = j - 1
        }
        ## the discriminant → %rax is emitted by the shared tail below (%rax still holds the call's
        ## word 0 here, but the shift already copied it to %rdx, so the tail overwrite is safe).
      } else if pw > 1 {
        ga := deref(arg_p(phead))
        if is_str_operand(ga.e, cx) {
          ## str payload: `emit_str_pair` pushes ptr (deeper) then len (top); pop len → payload word 1
          ## (retreg 2 = %rcx), ptr → payload word 0 (retreg 1 = %rdx). The discriminant → %rax follows.
          emit_str_pair(ga.e, sb, cx, a, nl)
          push_str(sb, "  popq ")
          emit_retreg(sb, 2)
          push_str(sb, "\n  popq ")
          emit_retreg(sb, 1)
          push_str(sb, "\n")
        } else {
        psl := struct_lit_info(ga.e)
        if psl.is_s {
          ## struct LITERAL payload: lower fields onto the stack, pop into %rdx.. in reverse (field
          ## k → `emit_retreg(k+1)`), matching `emit_struct_value`'s StructLit register order.
          fh := struct_lit_fields(ga.e)
          for k in 0..pw { fk := arg_expr_at(fh, k, a); emit_gas(fk, sb, cx, a, nl) }
          for j in 0..pw {
            push_str(sb, "  popq ")
            emit_retreg(sb, pw - j)
            push_str(sb, "\n")
          }
        } else {
          ## struct VAR payload: copy each of its slot words into a payload register (word k → retreg k+1).
          vn := var_name_span(ga.e)
          pent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, vn.s, vn.n)))
          if pent.is_ref {
            ## a BY-REFERENCE struct PARAM payload (`Option(Rec).Some(v)`, v a by-ref param): the slot
            ## holds a POINTER to the caller's struct word 0, so LOAD it (into %r11 = retreg 6, free for
            ## the ≤6 payload words that ride %rdx..%r10) and deliver pointee word k from `-(k*8)(%r11)`
            ## — the enum-payload dual of `emit_struct_value`'s is_ref Var arm. Without this the frame-word
            ## read underflowed `push_frame_word` (a by-ref param's slot `off` is small → `off - k` is a
            ## checked-usize underflow → `ud2` at COMPILE time) / read the pointer itself as word 0.
            push_str(sb, "  movq -")
            push_int(sb, i64((pent.off + 1) * 8))
            push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %r11\n")
            for k in 0..pw {
              push_str(sb, "  movq ")
              push_int(sb, i64(k * 8))
              push_str(sb, "(%r11), ")
              emit_retreg(sb, k + 1)
              push_str(sb, "\n")
            }
          } else {
            for k in 0..pw {
              push_str(sb, "  movq -")
              push_frame_word(sb, pent.off, k)
              push_str(sb, "(%rbp), ")
              emit_retreg(sb, k + 1)
              push_str(sb, "\n")
            }
          }
        }
        }
      } else if np >= 2 {
        ## a MULTI-FIELD payload (`E.B(a, b)`, np >= 2): deliver EACH payload field into a consecutive
        ## payload register (field k → payload reg k+1 = %rdx, %rcx, %r8, …). The `pcall`/`pw > 1` paths
        ## above ride a SINGLE multi-word aggregate payload (`Some(struct)`), gated to `np == 1`; a
        ## multi-SCALAR-field variant fell to the single-%rdx `else` below and SILENTLY DROPPED fields
        ## 1..N on a direct `return E.B(a, b)` (only the tag + field 0 reached the caller — a Priority-1
        ## silent miscompile). The local-store path `emit_enum_assign` already stores every field; this
        ## is its return-register dual, matching the enum VAR return (`emit_enum_value`'s Var arm delivers
        ## all `1 + enum_inst_words` words), so the caller's existing all-words staging reads it correctly.
        ## Push every field in order, then pop into %rdx.. in REVERSE (field 0 → %rdx); the discriminant →
        ## %rax follows (the shared tail). Fail loud on a multi-word AGGREGATE field (a struct/str/enum
        ## field among the scalars → an unmodeled mixed register layout) rather than truncate, and on a
        ## payload wider than the 6-register budget (%rdx..%r11).
        mut allscalar := true
        mut gs := phead
        mut cnt := 0
        while gs != 0 {
          gsa := deref(arg_p(gs))
          if payload_agg_words(gsa.e, cx, a) != 0 or payload_enum_words(gsa.e, cx, a) != 0 { allscalar = false }
          cnt += 1
          gs = gsa.next
        }
        if allscalar == false { panic("selfhost: a multi-field enum variant with a multi-word aggregate field returned by value is unsupported") }
        if cnt > 6 { panic("selfhost: an enum variant with more than 6 payload words returned by value exceeds the return-register budget") }
        mut gp := phead
        while gp != 0 { gpa := deref(arg_p(gp)); emit_gas(gpa.e, sb, cx, a, nl); gp = gpa.next }
        mut jj := 0
        while jj < cnt {
          push_str(sb, "  popq ")
          emit_retreg(sb, usize(cnt - jj))
          push_str(sb, "\n")
          jj += 1
        }
      } else {
        ## payload word 0 → %rdx (a nullary variant leaves %rdx 0); lower the first payload (if
        ## any) BEFORE moving the discriminant into %rax (the payload lowering clobbers %rax).
        if phead != 0 {
          ga := deref(arg_p(phead))
          psi := struct_lit_info(ga.e)
          if psi.is_s {
            ## a 1-word STRUCT-literal payload (`Ok(Ty(tag=…))`): return the struct's WORD 0 (its
            ## FIRST field) in %rdx — `emit_gas` of a whole struct pushes a placeholder `$0`.
            f0 := arg_expr_at(struct_lit_fields(ga.e), 0, a)
            if unchecked bitcast(usize, f0) != 0 { emit_gas(f0, sb, cx, a, nl) } else { push_str(sb, "  pushq $0\n") }
            push_str(sb, "  popq %rdx\n")
          } else {
            emit_gas(ga.e, sb, cx, a, nl)
            push_str(sb, "  popq %rdx\n")
          }
        } else {
          push_str(sb, "  movq $0, %rdx\n")
        }
      }
      push_str(sb, "  movq $")
      push_int(sb, disc)
      push_str(sb, ", %rax\n")
    }
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, args_head) => {
      ## lower the args into the SysV locations (args 1-6 in registers, args 7+ on the stack via
      ## `emit_call_args`), `call` — the callee leaves disc/%rax + payload/%rdx (this
      ## convention), then reclaim any stack args. No `pushq %rax` — the registers ARE the result.
      ## A generic callee returning an enum (`push -> Result(usize, AllocError)`) routes to its
      ## mangled instance via `emit_call_dispatch` (was a typetag-less bare label).
      ## An INDIRECT call through a fn-VALUE param whose result is an enum (`return f(v)`, `f : fn(T)
      ## -> Option(U)`): load the code pointer from `f`'s slot into %rax and `call *%rax`; the callee
      ## leaves disc/%rax + payload/%rdx (the enum convention), so no pushq — the registers ARE the
      ## result. Without this the callee `f` mis-resolved as a named module fn (undefined label).
      ## `src/` has no fn-value call returning an enum → the direct path is byte-identical (fixpoint).
      fvcs := slot_of(cx.slots, cx.src, cs, cl)
      ## FN-10 — the same INDIRECT call one level out: the code pointer is a fn-VALUE STRUCT FIELD
      ## (`match o.g(x) { … }`, desugared to `Call(g, [o, x…])`). The receiver is ERASED (`skip = 0`,
      ## `nargs - 1` value args) exactly as at the scalar field site. Checked FIRST so a member call
      ## reaches the FIELD even when a same-named local shadows it (member access wins), mirroring the
      ## scalar site's ordering. Without it the call fell to `emit_call_dispatch` → a direct `call
      ## <module>__g` to a nonexistent label; before the enum CLASS existed it never got here at all
      ## and the match dispatched on the raw pushed word (a silent wrong arm).
      ffcv := if nargs >= 1 { fn_field_call_slot(arg_expr_at(args_head, 0, a), cs, cl, cx) } else { 0 - 1 }
      if ffcv >= 0 {
        cx.call_cidx = 0 - 1
        cx.ind_fn_fmask = fn_field_param_fmask(arg_expr_at(args_head, 0, a), cs, cl, cx)
        emit_call_args(args_head, 0, 0 - 1, 0 - 1, nargs - 1, sb, cx, nl)
        push_str(sb, "  movq -")
        push_int(sb, (ffcv + 1) * 8)
        push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rax\n  call *%rax\n")
        emit_call_cleanup(sb, nargs - 1)
      } else if fvcs >= 0 {
        cx.call_cidx = -1
        ## FLOAT ARGUMENT CLASS (FN-6 / SysV) — the same fn VALUE, so the same mask as the scalar
        ## indirect-call site; only the RESULT convention differs here (disc/%rax + payload/%rdx).
        cx.ind_fn_fmask = fnval_param_fmask(cs, cl, cx)
        emit_call_args(args_head, -1, -1, -1, nargs, sb, cx, nl)
        push_str(sb, "  movq -")
        push_int(sb, (fvcs + 1) * 8)
        push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rax\n  call *%rax\n")
        emit_call_cleanup(sb, nargs)
      } else {
        emit_call_dispatch(cs, cl, nargs, args_head, sb, cx, a, nl)
      }
    }
    ## `if cond { then } else { els }` yielding an enum: lower the cond, branch, and recurse
    ## into each branch (each leaves the enum in %rax/%rdx). Fresh else/end labels via `nl`.
    Expr::If(c, t, f) => {
      lelse := nl
      lend := nl + 1
      nl += 2
      emit_gas(c, sb, cx, a, nl)
      push_str(sb, "  popq %rax\n  cmpq $0, %rax\n  je ")
      emit_label(sb, lelse)
      push_str(sb, "\n")
      emit_enum_value(t, sb, cx, a, nl)   ## then — enum in %rax/%rdx
      push_str(sb, "  jmp ")
      emit_label(sb, lend)
      push_str(sb, "\n")
      emit_label(sb, lelse)
      push_str(sb, ":\n")
      emit_enum_value(f, sb, cx, a, nl)   ## else — enum in %rax/%rdx
      emit_label(sb, lend)
      push_str(sb, ":\n")
    }
    Expr::Field(fbase, ffs, ffl) => {
      ## An ENUM-typed FIELD returned BY VALUE (`unbox := fn(T : type, b : Box(T)) -> T { return b.v }`
      ## at `T = Option(u64)`) — the enum dual of `emit_struct_value`'s Field arm: deliver the field's
      ## `1 + payload` words into the return registers (disc → %rax, payload word i → `emit_retreg(i+1)`),
      ## read through the base POINTER for a by-ref base (word k at `+(k*8)`) or from the base's frame
      ## words. Without this arm an enum `Field` fell to the `_` fallback (disc 0, payload 0), so a
      ## two-arm `match` on the result matched NEITHER arm. Gated on the field's type resolving to an
      ## ENUM — every other `Field` keeps the byte-identical fallback below.
      fbt := base_struct_span(fbase, cx)
      fft := field_type_span(cx.decls, cx.src, fbt.s, fbt.n, ffs, ffl, deref(cx.mar))
      ffbn := base_type_name(cx.src, fft.s, fft.n)
      mut ffw := 0
      if fbt.n != 0 and fft.n != 0 and enum_decl_of(cx.decls, cx.src, ffbn.s, ffbn.n) >= 0 { ffw = 1 + enum_inst_words(cx.decls, cx.src, fft.s, fft.n, deref(cx.mar)) }
      if ffw == 0 {
        push_str(sb, "  movq $0, %rax\n  movq $0, %rdx\n")
        return
      }
      ## CORRECT-OR-TRAP: an unresolvable place, or one wider than the return-register budget, is a
      ## compile-time panic — never a silently truncated / all-zero enum.
      if ffw > 7 { panic("selfhost: an enum field wider than the 7-word return-register budget returned by value is unsupported — bind it to a local and return that") }
      ffa := agg_field_of(fbase, ffs, ffl, cx)
      if ffa.ok == false { panic("selfhost: returning this enum FIELD by value is unsupported — its place could not be resolved; bind it to a local (`c := <field>`) and return that") }
      if ffa.is_ref {
        aent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, ffa.ent_idx))
        emit_agg_base_addr(aent, sb)
        if ffa.fi != 0 { push_str(sb, "  addq $") ; push_int(sb, ffa.fi * 8) ; push_str(sb, ", %rax\n") }
        push_str(sb, "  movq %rax, %r11\n")
        for k in 0..ffw {
          push_str(sb, "  movq ")
          push_int(sb, i64(k * 8))
          push_str(sb, "(%r11), ")
          emit_retreg(sb, k)
          push_str(sb, "\n")
        }
      } else {
        for k in 0..ffw {
          push_str(sb, "  movq -")
          push_int(sb, (ffa.slot - i64(k) + 1) * 8)
          push_str(sb, "(%rbp), ")
          emit_retreg(sb, k)
          push_str(sb, "\n")
        }
      }
    }
    _ => {
      ## any other expression in enum position is not part of the supported grammar — emit a
      ## null enum (disc 0, payload 0) so the output still assembles.
      push_str(sb, "  movq $0, %rax\n  movq $0, %rdx\n")
    }
  }
}

## Whether a kind-1 fn `Decl` declares an ENUM return type (its `-> R` type name span resolves
## to a kind-3 enum `Decl`). Such a fn delivers its return value via the two-register enum
## convention (`emit_enum_value` → %rax disc / %rdx payload) rather than a scalar in %rax.
fn_returns_enum := fn(d : Decl, decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8)) -> bool {
  if d.ret_tl == 0 { return false }
  enum_decl_of(decls, src, d.ret_ts, d.ret_tl) >= 0
}

## Whether a kind-1 fn declares a 1..7-WORD STRUCT return type — delivered via the register-return
## convention (word k → %rax/%rdx/%rcx/…, `emit_retreg`). A 1-word struct rides %rax (SysV integer
## class), like a scalar, but is BOUND as a struct so `p.field` reads it (a 1-field struct returned by
## value used to fall to the scalar path → `p.x` read 0). Larger structs (> 7 words) need sret.
fn_returns_struct := fn(d : Decl, decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> bool {
  if d.ret_tl == 0 { return false }
  if struct_decl_of(decls, src, d.ret_ts, d.ret_tl) < 0 { return false }
  w := struct_words(decls, src, d.ret_ts, d.ret_tl, a)
  return w >= 1 and w <= 7               ## words 0..6 → %rax:%rdx:%rcx:%r8:%r9:%r10:%r11 (emit_retreg)
}

## Whether a kind-1 fn returns a struct TOO WIDE for the register convention (> 7 words) — delivered
## via SRET: the caller passes a hidden result-pointer as arg 0 (%rdi), the callee writes the whole
## struct through it + returns the pointer in %rax. Disjoint from `fn_returns_struct` (2..7 words), so
## the register-return path is unchanged for every existing fn (nothing in src/lib returns > 7 words).
fn_returns_sret := fn(d : Decl, decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> bool {
  if d.ret_tl == 0 { return false }
  if struct_decl_of(decls, src, d.ret_ts, d.ret_tl) < 0 { return false }
  return struct_words(decls, src, d.ret_ts, d.ret_tl, a) >= 8
}

## Whether a kind-1 fn returns an ENUM too wide for the register convention (disc + payload > 7
## words, i.e. beyond `emit_retreg`'s %rax..%r11) — delivered via SRET, the same hidden-result-pointer
## path as a wide struct. `fn_returns_enum` stays TRUE (it IS an enum), so `match`/`?` logic elsewhere
## is unchanged; only the RETURN emit + the CALL binding switch to the pointer path. Nothing in src/lib
## returns a >7-word enum, so the register-return path is byte-identical there (fixpoint-neutral).
fn_returns_enum_sret := fn(d : Decl, decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> bool {
  if fn_returns_enum(d, decls, src) == false { return false }
  return (1 + enum_type_payload_words(decls, src, d.ret_ts, d.ret_tl, a)) >= 8
}

## Whether a kind-1 fn declares a TUPLE return type `(T0, T1, …)` — the return-type span starts with
## `(` (the parser captures the balanced-paren span). A tuple is an N-word aggregate delivered via the
## SAME register-return convention as a small struct (word k → %rax/%rdx/%rcx/…). Disjoint from
## `fn_returns_struct` (a named struct never starts with `(`), so all existing returns are unchanged;
## `src/` returns no tuples → fixpoint-neutral.
fn_returns_tuple := fn(d : Decl, src : ptr(u8)) -> bool {
  if d.ret_tl == 0 { return false }
  str_at((src + d.ret_ts), 1) == "("
}
## The COMPONENT count (= word count, scalar components) of a tuple return-type span `(T0, …, TN)`:
## count the top-level (paren-depth 1) commas + 1. A single-component `(T)` has 0 commas → 1 word.
tuple_words := fn(src : ptr(u8), ts : usize, tl : usize) -> usize {
  mut depth := 0
  mut commas := 0
  mut i := 0
  while i < tl {
    c := str_at((src + ts + i), 1)
    if c == "(" { depth = depth + 1 }
    else if c == ")" { depth = depth - 1 }
    else if c == "," and depth == 1 { commas = commas + 1 }
    i += 1
  }
  commas + 1
}
## Whether a kind-1 fn returns a TUPLE too WIDE for the register-return convention (>= 8 components,
## i.e. beyond `emit_retreg`'s %rax..%r11) — delivered via SRET, the same hidden-result-pointer path a
## wide struct / wide enum takes: the caller passes the destination's word-0 ADDRESS in %rdi and the
## callee writes every component through it. `fn_returns_tuple` stays TRUE (it IS a tuple), so the
## <= 7-component register path is byte-identical; only the >= 8 case switches. Without this a wide
## tuple return read components back from `emit_retreg(k)` past %r11 — a SILENT garbage/0 tail.
## `lib/`'s only tuple returns are the 2-word `overflowing_*` pairs and `src/` returns none, so this
## is dormant for the self-host build (fixpoint-neutral).
fn_returns_tuple_sret := fn(d : Decl, src : ptr(u8)) -> bool {
  if fn_returns_tuple(d, src) == false { return false }
  return tuple_words(src, d.ret_ts, d.ret_tl) >= 8
}

## Whether a kind-1 fn declares a `str` return type — a 2-word {ptr, len} value delivered via the
## SAME two-register convention as a 2-word struct (ptr/%rax, len/%rdx). The str dual of
## `fn_returns_struct`; a fn like `svec_str_get`/`module_name` that `return`s a str value.
fn_returns_str := fn(d : Decl, src : ptr(u8)) -> bool {
  if d.ret_tl == 0 { return false }
  str_at((src + d.ret_ts), d.ret_tl) == "str"
}

## Whether a GENERIC identity-return call is instantiated at `str`: the declared result is exactly the
## callee's `: type` parameter, and the corresponding explicit type argument is the two-word byte view.
## This is deliberately narrower than treating every generic result as a str — generic scalar and
## aggregate returns keep their existing classifiers and ABI paths.
generic_str_return_instance := fn(d : Decl, args_head : ptr(mut Arg), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> bool {
  if not d.is_generic or d.ret_tl == 0 { return false }
  mut p := d.params_head
  mut pi := 0
  while p != 0 {
    pm := deref(param_p(p))
    if str_at((src + pm.ts), pm.tl) == "type" {
      if streq(src, d.ret_ts, d.ret_tl, pm.ns, pm.nl) {
        ta := arg_expr_at(args_head, pi, a)
        if unchecked bitcast(usize, ta) == 0 { return false }
        tv := deref_var_info(ta)
        return tv.is_v and str_at((src + tv.s), tv.n) == "str"
      }
    }
    p = pm.next
    pi += 1
  }
  false
}

## Whether a kind-1 fn declares a SLICE return `Slice(T)` (spec §7.2). A slice is a 2-word {ptr, len}
## value delivered via the SAME two-register convention as a `str`/2-word struct (ptr/%rax, len/%rdx).
## The parser records only the return-type HEAD (`Slice`); the `(elem)` follows in `src`. Used by the
## epilogue to route the RETURN of a slice through the str-pair materializer (`emit_str_pair` handles a
## slice `Var`/by-ref param) — but ONLY when the `Slice` type-decl is ABSENT (else the return is already
## classified as a 2-word STRUCT and delivered by `emit_struct_value`, byte-identically). The callee dual
## of the caller-side `slice_ret_call` binding.
fn_returns_slice := fn(d : Decl, src : ptr(u8)) -> bool {
  if d.ret_tl == 0 { return false }
  bn := base_type_name(src, d.ret_ts, d.ret_tl)
  str_at((src + bn.s), bn.n) == "Slice"
}

## Whether a PARAM rides an SSE argument register (xmm) under SysV: a plain by-value (`in`, pmode 0)
## `f64`/`f32`. An `out`/`in out` (pmode 2) or array (pmode 1) param passes an ADDRESS in a GPR even
## when its element type is float, so it is NOT an SSE param. The single source of truth for the
## float-vs-integer classification, shared by the call-site routing and the prologue spill.
param_is_float_sse := fn(pm : Param, src : ptr(u8)) -> bool {
  if pm.pmode != 0 { return false }
  mut tn := str_at((src + pm.ts), pm.tl)
  return tn == "f64" or tn == "f32"
}

## Whether a fn declares an `f64`/`f32` return type — delivered in %xmm0 (the SysV SSE return
## register) rather than a scalar in %rax. The caller of such a fn reads its result from %xmm0.
fn_returns_float := fn(d : Decl, src : ptr(u8)) -> bool {
  if d.ret_tl == 0 { return false }
  mut rn := str_at((src + d.ret_ts), d.ret_tl)
  return rn == "f64" or rn == "f32"
}

## Does the fn at decl index `cidx` return a float? (false for an unknown callee, `cidx < 0`.)
callee_returns_float := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), cidx : i64) -> bool {
  if cidx < 0 { return false }
  d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(cidx))))
  return fn_returns_float(d, src)
}

## Is the `pidx`-th param (full param-list index) of the callee at `cidx` a float? Mirrors the param
## walk in `callee_out_scalar`. false for an unknown callee / out-of-range index.
callee_param_is_float := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena, cidx : i64, pidx : usize) -> bool {
  if cidx < 0 { return false }
  d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(cidx))))
  mut pp := d.params_head
  mut k := 0
  mut res := false
  while pp != 0 {
    pm := deref(param_p(pp))
    if k == pidx and param_is_float_sse(pm, src) { res = true }
    pp = pm.next
    k += 1
  }
  res
}

## Does the callee at `cidx` have ANY float param? GATES the float-aware argument routing in
## `emit_call_args` — when false (every all-integer call, incl. the entire self-host source) the
## byte-identical all-GPR pop loop runs unchanged, so the TOOL-1 fixpoint is unaffected.
callee_has_float_param := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena, cidx : i64) -> bool {
  if cidx < 0 { return false }
  d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(cidx))))
  mut pp := d.params_head
  mut res := false
  while pp != 0 {
    pm := deref(param_p(pp))
    if param_is_float_sse(pm, src) { res = true }
    pp = pm.next
  }
  res
}

## Does a GENERIC callee declare its return as its OWN comptime TYPE PARAMETER, with this call supplying
## `f64`/`f32` for it? (`idf(f64, 10.5)` where `idf := fn(T : type, x : T) -> T`.) The monomorphized
## INSTANCE carries the value in the ordinary word-sized bits-in-%rax convention (its `-> T` is not an
## SSE return and its `x : T` is not an SSE parameter), so only the CONSUMER's classification is missing:
## without this a surrounding `u64(…)` read the f64 BITS as an integer — the silent 0. The `Arg` list is
## walked directly (no arena handle needed — `arg_expr_at` ignores its own).
gen_ret_targ_float := fn(d : Decl, head : ptr(mut Arg), src : ptr(u8)) -> bool {
  if d.ret_tl == 0 { return false }
  mut pp := d.params_head
  mut k : i64 = 0
  mut ti : i64 = 0 - 1
  while pp != 0 {
    pm := deref(param_p(pp))
    if str_at((src + pm.ts), pm.tl) == "type" and streq(src, pm.ns, pm.nl, d.ret_ts, d.ret_tl) { ti = k }
    pp = pm.next
    k = k + 1
  }
  if ti < 0 { return false }
  mut g := head
  mut j : i64 = 0
  mut r := false
  while g != 0 {
    ga := deref(arg_p(g))
    if j == ti {
      vn := var_name_span(ga.e)
      if vn.n != 0 {
        tn := str_at((src + vn.s), vn.n)
        if tn == "f64" or tn == "f32" { r = true }
      }
    }
    g = ga.next
    j = j + 1
  }
  r
}

## Is `e` a `Call` to a float-returning fn? (the slot-collection / `Stmt::Assign` dual of
## `str_ret_call` — a `name := f(…)` whose `f` returns `f64` binds `name` as a float local, ek 9.)
float_ret_call := fn(e : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8)) -> bool {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, args_head) => {
      mut res := false
      cnt := rt::vec_len(deref(decls))
      ## `nm` (and its `colon_pos` source scan) is HOISTED out of the loop — it does not depend on `i`,
      ## and recomputing it per decl was the profile's single hottest leaf.
      nm := callee_name_span(src, cs, cl)
      th := name_hash(src, nm.s, nm.n)
      mut jc := dni_lo(cnt, th)
      jce := dni_hi(cnt, th)
      while jc < jce {
        i := dni_at(cnt, jc)
        jc = jc + 1
        if dnh_skip(cnt, i, th) == false {
        d := deref(decl_get(decls, i))
        if d.kind == 1 and streq(src, d.name_start, d.name_len, nm.s, nm.n) {
          if fn_returns_float(d, src) { res = true }
          ## a GENERIC `-> T` callee instantiated at `f64`/`f32` (see `gen_ret_targ_float`). `src/`
          ## instantiates no generic with a float type argument → never fires → fixpoint-neutral.
          if d.is_generic and gen_ret_targ_float(d, args_head, src) { res = true }
        }
        }
      }
      res
    }
    _ => { false }
  }
}

## A TRYABLE-unwrap base of a `Field` read: `<recv>?` (Try) or `<recv>.expect(msg)` (a Call to the
## `expect` protocol method). `kind` 0 = neither, 1 = `?`, 2 = `expect`. Split into a helper so each
## destructure is a single-level function-body match (the nested-match gotcha).
TryField := struct { kind : u8, recv : ptr(Expr), msg : ptr(Expr) }
tryable_field_recv := fn(base : ptr(Expr), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> TryField {
  z := unchecked bitcast(ptr(Expr), 0)
  mut res := TryField(kind = 0, recv = z, msg = z)
  match deref(base) {
    Expr::Try(inner) => { res = TryField(kind = 1, recv = inner, msg = z) }
    Expr::Unchecked(inner) => { res = tryable_field_recv(inner, src, a) }
    Expr::Bitcast(inner, _bcs, _bcl) => { res = tryable_field_recv(inner, src, a) }
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, args_head) => {
      if str_at((src + cs), cl) == "expect" and nargs >= 2 {
        r0 := arg_expr_at(args_head, 0, a)
        m1 := arg_expr_at(args_head, 1, a)
        res = TryField(kind = 2, recv = r0, msg = m1)
      }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  res
}

## The declared RETURN-TYPE span of a `Call` expression's callee (generic or plain); 0/0 otherwise.
## Used to recover a tryable receiver's `Result(Ok, Err)` / `Option(Ok)` type so its Ok-payload
## struct + a following field can be resolved (`allocate(…)?.idx` — payload `Handle`, field `idx`).
call_ret_ty_span := fn(recv : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> CSpan {
  match deref(recv) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, ah) => {
      mut idx := generic_decl_of(decls, src, cs, cl, 0, 0, nargs, ah, a)
      if idx < 0 { idx = resolve_callee_idx(decls, src, cs, cl, 0, 0) }
      ## `T(v)` user CONVERSION-CONSTRUCTOR (Types §4.6): resolve the @convert fn so its declared
      ## RETURN type `T` types the call result (field-type recovery, scalar bindings). Neutral — see
      ## `convert_callee_idx` (filters builtin/brand, so src/lib's `char(n)` never resolves here).
      if idx < 0 { idx = convert_callee_idx(decls, src, cs, cl) }
      if idx < 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
      d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(idx))))
      return CSpan(s = d.ret_ts, n = d.ret_tl)
    }
    _ => { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  }
}

## The discriminant of the SUCCESS variant of a tryable enum span `[s,n)` for the `?` operator
## (Control-Flow §8.2 — success is `Some` (Option) / `Ok` (Result); failure `None`/`Err` early-returns
## the enclosing fn). The SHIPPED stdlib Option is `enum { None, Some(T) }` (Some = variant 1) while the
## compiler-INTERNAL Option (ast.al) is `enum { Some(T), None }` (Some = variant 0), and Result is
## `enum { Ok(T), Err(E) }` (Ok = variant 0) in BOTH — so `?` MUST resolve the success discriminant by
## variant NAME, never assume variant 0. (Assuming 0 mis-tested a stdlib `Some(x)` as failure and
## propagated the whole enum verbatim, so a later `Option.Some(<expr>)` in the same fn was discarded.)
## Returns 0 when the type is unresolved or is neither Some- nor Ok-bearing (the toy default — and the
## self-host source's Option is Some=0 / Result is Ok=0, so every `?` in src/ resolves to 0 → the
## self-build stays byte-identical / fixpoint-neutral).
try_success_disc := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize, a : rt::Arena) -> i64 {
  ebn := base_type_name(src, s, n)
  di := enum_decl_of(decls, src, ebn.s, ebn.n)
  if di < 0 { return 0 }
  d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(di))))
  mut f := d.fields_head
  mut idx := 0
  mut res := 0
  while f != 0 {
    fd := deref(fld_p(f))
    nm := str_at((src + fd.ns), fd.nl)
    if nm == "Some" { res = idx }
    if nm == "Ok" { res = idx }
    idx += 1
    f = fd.next
  }
  res
}

## The tryable ENUM type span of a `?` operand `inner` — a call's declared return type
## (`call_ret_ty_span`), or, for `?` on an enum LOCAL, that local's declared type span. Feeds
## `try_success_disc`; a 0/0 span (unresolved) folds the success disc to the 0 default.
try_operand_ty_span := fn(inner : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena) -> CSpan {
  match deref(inner) {
    Expr::Var(vs, vn) => {
      ei := entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, vs, vn)
      ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, ei))
      return CSpan(s = ent.sns, n = ent.snl)
    }
    _ => { return call_ret_ty_span(inner, cx.decls, cx.src, a) }
  }
}

## Emit the `call` for a `Call` whose RESULT is an AGGREGATE (enum / struct) left in the return
## registers — the aggregate-return dual of the scalar-return generic branch in the main `Expr::Call`
## arm. A generic callee (`generic_decl_of >= 0`) is lowered to its mangled INSTANCE
## `<module>__<fn>__<typetag>` with the comptime type-arg erased (like the scalar path); otherwise a
## plain mangled call. Leaves the result in %rax (+%rdx for a 2-word value) — NO push, matching the
## register-return convention of `emit_enum_value` / `emit_struct_value`. Fixpoint-safe: `src/` makes
## no generic call whose result flows through these aggregate-return arms (such a call would already
## emit an undefined typetag-less label and fail to link — but `src/` links), so the generic branch
## never fires for `src/` → byte-identical.

## NOTE (TYP-6): the aggregate-VALUE-into-scalar-SINK soundness nets that formerly lived in the
## emit path (a struct/enum argument to a scalar parameter — `is_agg_value_var` + `check_agg_arg_scalar_
## param` — plus the return / local / field / index / reassign sinks below) have MOVED UP into
## `sema::check_program` (now run on the build path), which rejects the class post-overload-resolution
## with a located diagnostic, in BOTH directions. Only the `T(aggregate)` builtin-CONVERSION net (in
## `emit_call_dispatch`'s value-position arm) remains here — sema does not yet model conversions.
emit_call_dispatch := fn(cs : usize, cl : usize, nargs : usize, args_head : ptr(mut Arg), in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena, in out nl : usize) {
  ## USER CONVERSION-CONSTRUCTOR `T(v)` (Types §4.6 / TYP-6): the callee `T` is a type name with an
  ## in-scope `@convert fn(…) -> T` — dispatch to that fn by ITS OWN (mangled) name, not `T` (there is
  ## no fn named `T`). The result is delivered in the return registers (%rax/%rdx…), matching the
  ## aggregate-return convention of this fn. Fixpoint-neutral: `convert_callee_idx` filters builtin/brand,
  ## so no `src/lib` call resolves here (its only @convert `to_char` targets the brand `char`).
  cvi0 := convert_callee_idx(cx.decls, cx.src, cs, cl)
  if cvi0 >= 0 {
    cvd := deref(decl_get(cx.decls, usize(cvi0)))
    cx.call_cidx = cvi0
    sc_shift0 := if cx.sret_call >= 0 { 1 } else { 0 }
    emit_call_args(args_head, -1, -1, -1, nargs, sb, cx, nl)
    push_str(sb, "  call ")
    emit_mangled_call(sb, cx.src, cvd.name_start, cvd.name_len, cvd.mod_start, cvd.mod_len, cx.decls)
    push_str(sb, "\n")
    emit_call_cleanup_shift(sb, nargs, sc_shift0)
    return
  }
  mut gi := generic_decl_of(cx.decls, cx.src, cs, cl, cx.mod_s, cx.mod_l, nargs, args_head, a)
  ## RECEIVER-KEYED SELECTION (spec FN-7), mirror of the value-position Call arm: an aggregate/enum-
  ## returning UFCS method (`m := r.map(f)` / `r.ok()` / `r.and_then(f)` / `r.map_err(g)`) whose name is
  ## shared with `base/option` must pick the `Result` candidate, not the last-declared. No-op when the
  ## resolved decl already matches the receiver → fixpoint-neutral for `src/`.
  if gi >= 0 and nargs >= 1 {
    ar_ty := expr_type_span(arg_expr_at(args_head, 0, a), cx)
    if ar_ty.n != 0 {
      ar_bt := base_type_name(cx.src, ar_ty.s, ar_ty.n)
      gi = recv_redirect_generic(cx.decls, cx.src, gi, nargs, ar_bt.s, ar_bt.n, a)
    }
  }
  ## a CONCRETE overload matching by arity+first-arg type WINS over a same-name generic (see the
  ## value-position Call arm) — route it to the non-generic path.
  if gi >= 0 {
    gat := arg_type_name(args_head, cx, a)
    if nongen_type_match(cx.decls, cx.src, cs, cl, nargs, gat.s, gat.n, a) { gi = 0 - 1 }
  }
  if gi >= 0 {
    tpi := tparam_idx(cx.decls, gi, cx.src, a)
    mut ta := type_arg_at(args_head, tpi, a)
    if is_fn_name(cx.decls, cx.src, ta.s, ta.n) { ta = CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
    ## a TUPLE type-arg `(T0, T1, …)` — recover its `(…)` source span (mono keys on it).
    if ta.n == 0 {
      tt := tuple_typearg_span(arg_expr_at(args_head, tpi, a), cx.src, a)
      if tt.n != 0 { ta = tt }
    }
    if ta.n == 0 and cx.cf_var_l != 0 {
      ct_a0 := arg_expr_at(args_head, tpi, a)
      ct_fp := field_var_parts(ct_a0)
      if ct_fp.ok and streq(cx.src, ct_fp.os, ct_fp.on, cx.cf_var_s, cx.cf_var_l) and str_at((cx.src + ct_fp.fs), ct_fp.fl) == "type" {
        ta = CSpan(s = cx.cf_ty_s, n = cx.cf_ty_l)
      }
    }
    if cx.gp_l != 0 and cx.it_l != 0 and streq(cx.src, ta.s, ta.n, cx.gp_s, cx.gp_l) {
      ta = CSpan(s = cx.it_s, n = cx.it_l)
    } else if cx.gp2_l != 0 and cx.it2_l != 0 and streq(cx.src, ta.s, ta.n, cx.gp2_s, cx.gp2_l) {
      ta = CSpan(s = cx.it2_s, n = cx.it2_l)
    } else if cx.gp3_l != 0 and cx.it3_l != 0 and streq(cx.src, ta.s, ta.n, cx.gp3_s, cx.gp3_l) {
      ta = CSpan(s = cx.it3_s, n = cx.it3_l)
    }
    ntpc := tparam_count(cx.decls, gi, cx.src, a)
    mut ta2 := CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
    mut tpi2 := -1
    if ntpc >= 2 {
      tpi2 = i64(tparam_idx2(cx.decls, gi, cx.src, a))
      ta2 = type_arg_at(args_head, usize(tpi2), a)
      if is_fn_name(cx.decls, cx.src, ta2.s, ta2.n) { ta2 = CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
      if cx.gp_l != 0 and cx.it_l != 0 and streq(cx.src, ta2.s, ta2.n, cx.gp_s, cx.gp_l) { ta2 = CSpan(s = cx.it_s, n = cx.it_l) }
      else if cx.gp2_l != 0 and cx.it2_l != 0 and streq(cx.src, ta2.s, ta2.n, cx.gp2_s, cx.gp2_l) { ta2 = CSpan(s = cx.it2_s, n = cx.it2_l) }
      else if cx.gp3_l != 0 and cx.it3_l != 0 and streq(cx.src, ta2.s, ta2.n, cx.gp3_s, cx.gp3_l) { ta2 = CSpan(s = cx.it3_s, n = cx.it3_l) }
    }
    mut ta3 := CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
    mut tpi3 := -1
    if ntpc >= 3 {
      tpi3 = i64(tparam_idx3(cx.decls, gi, cx.src, a))
      ta3 = type_arg_at(args_head, usize(tpi3), a)
      if is_fn_name(cx.decls, cx.src, ta3.s, ta3.n) { ta3 = CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
      if cx.gp_l != 0 and cx.it_l != 0 and streq(cx.src, ta3.s, ta3.n, cx.gp_s, cx.gp_l) { ta3 = CSpan(s = cx.it_s, n = cx.it_l) }
      else if cx.gp2_l != 0 and cx.it2_l != 0 and streq(cx.src, ta3.s, ta3.n, cx.gp2_s, cx.gp2_l) { ta3 = CSpan(s = cx.it2_s, n = cx.it2_l) }
      else if cx.gp3_l != 0 and cx.it3_l != 0 and streq(cx.src, ta3.s, ta3.n, cx.gp3_s, cx.gp3_l) { ta3 = CSpan(s = cx.it3_s, n = cx.it3_l) }
    }
    ## IMPLICIT type-arg (`hash(key)`, T omitted): infer T from the value arg's type; erase nothing.
    gdd := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(cx.decls), usize(gi))))
    mut did_impl := false
    ## IMPLICIT payload type-arg (an arm-body `g(p, …)` where arg 0 is the current variant PAYLOAD
    ## binding `cf_pay` — `display`'s enum arm `display(p, sb)`, `derive`'s `hash(p)`): the payload's
    ## own type `cf_pay_ty` IS the type-arg, the payload is a VALUE arg (nothing erased). Mirrors the
    ## `emit_call_dispatch` impl_pay case; the payload's frame slot records no type, so the general
    ## `infer_implicit_targ` (via `expr_type_span`) can't recover it — this cf_pay path can.
    if cx.cf_pay_l != 0 and ntpc == 1 {
      ip_a0 := arg_expr_at(args_head, 0, a)
      ip_vn := var_name_span(ip_a0)
      if ip_vn.n != 0 and streq(cx.src, ip_vn.s, ip_vn.n, cx.cf_pay_s, cx.cf_pay_l) {
        ta = CSpan(s = cx.cf_pay_ty_s, n = cx.cf_pay_ty_l); did_impl = true
      }
    }
    if did_impl == false and ntpc == 1 and gdd.arity != 0 and nargs == gdd.arity - ntpc {
      iti := infer_implicit_targ(gi, args_head, cx, a)
      if iti.n != 0 { ta = iti; did_impl = true }
    }
    ## IMPLICIT UFCS on a 2-or-3-type-param aggregate/enum-returning method. `r.ok()`/`r.unwrap()` take
    ## BOTH type-params from the receiver's declared type. `o.map(f)`/`r.map(f)`/`r.and_then(f)`/
    ## `r.map_err(g)` take all-but-one from the receiver and the LAST — the MAPPER's return type-param
    ## (`U`/`F`, absent from the receiver's type) — from the fn ARGUMENT's declared return
    ## (`infer_map_targ3`). Erase nothing; the guarded `nvals` below avoids the `nargs - ntpc` usize
    ## underflow. `src/` spells its type-args → never fires → fixpoint-neutral.
    if did_impl == false and (ntpc == 2 or ntpc == 3) and gdd.arity != 0 and nargs == gdd.arity - ntpc {
      rcv_ty := recv_full_emit(arg_expr_at(args_head, 0, a), cx.decls, cx.src, a)
      if rcv_ty.n != 0 {
        rb := base_type_name(cx.src, rcv_ty.s, rcv_ty.n)
        c0 := implicit_targ_from_recv(cx.decls, cx.src, gi, rcv_ty.s, rcv_ty.n, 0, a)
        c1 := implicit_targ_from_recv(cx.decls, cx.src, gi, rcv_ty.s, rcv_ty.n, 1, a)
        c2 := implicit_targ_from_recv(cx.decls, cx.src, gi, rcv_ty.s, rcv_ty.n, 2, a)
        fu := infer_map_targ3(cx.decls, cx.src, args_head, nargs, rb.s, rb.n, a)
        mut e0 := c0
        mut e1 := c1
        mut e2 := c2
        if e0.n == 0 { e0 = fu }
        if e1.n == 0 { e1 = fu }
        if e2.n == 0 { e2 = fu }
        if ntpc == 2 and e0.n != 0 and e1.n != 0 {
          ta = e0
          ta2 = e1
          did_impl = true
        }
        if ntpc == 3 and e0.n != 0 and e1.n != 0 and e2.n != 0 {
          ta = e0
          ta2 = e1
          ta3 = e2
          did_impl = true
        }
      }
    }
    ## Any OTHER implicit-UFCS call to a multi-type-parameter method whose type-args cannot all be
    ## recovered here (a lambda mapper with no decl entry; a receiver bound from a generic call whose
    ## type is not tracked). Fail loud with the explicit-type-arg workaround rather than emit a broken
    ## call / underflow `nvals`.
    if did_impl == false and nargs < ntpc {
      panic("selfhost: an implicit-UFCS call to a multi-type-parameter Result/Option method can't infer all its type arguments here (e.g. map/and_then/map_err — the mapper return type; or a receiver bound from a generic call whose type is not tracked) — call it with explicit type args, e.g. `map(T, E, U, r, f)` / `unwrap(T, E, r)`")
    }
    ## An implicit-UFCS call omits ALL type-args (`nargs < ntpc`), so compute `nargs - ntpc` only on the
    ## non-implicit branch (else the CHECKED usize subtraction underflows → `ud2`) and erase nothing.
    mut nvals := if did_impl { nargs } else { nargs - ntpc }
    mut eff_tpi := if did_impl { 0 - 1 } else { i64(tpi) }
    eff_tpi2 := if did_impl { 0 - 1 } else { tpi2 }
    eff_tpi3 := if did_impl { 0 - 1 } else { tpi3 }
    cx.call_cidx = gi
    sc_shift := if cx.sret_call >= 0 { 1 } else { 0 }
    emit_call_args(args_head, eff_tpi, eff_tpi2, eff_tpi3, nvals, sb, cx, nl)
    push_str(sb, "  call ")
    gd := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(cx.decls), usize(gi))))
    emit_generic_label(sb, cx.src, gd.mod_start, gd.mod_len, gd.name_start, gd.name_len, ta.s, ta.n, ta2.s, ta2.n, ta3.s, ta3.n)
    push_str(sb, "\n")
    emit_call_cleanup_shift(sb, nvals, sc_shift)
  } else {
    cx.call_cidx = resolve_callee_idx(cx.decls, cx.src, cs, cl, cx.mod_s, cx.mod_l)
    ## C-ABI FOREIGN CALL (spec 150 §FN-9): the callee is `@abi(c)` — lay the args by the SysV C
    ## register-classing (aggregates ≤ 16 bytes by VALUE in int / xmm regs) instead of our internal
    ## by-reference pass, then `call` the (extern) symbol. A struct RETURN ≤ 16 bytes arrives in the
    ## classed result registers (%rax/%rdx INTEGER, %xmm0/%xmm1 SSE); `emit_abi_c_ret_agg` normalizes
    ## it into the %rax:%rdx retreg convention this arm's callers (the struct-return store loop) read.
    ## Register-only in this increment (no sret / stack cleanup). Gated on `callee_decl_is_abi_c` → no
    ## self-host call reaches here → fixpoint byte-identical. (An `@abi(c)` fn is never overloaded, so
    ## no `emit_overload_suffix`.)
    if callee_decl_is_abi_c(cx.decls, cx.src, cx.call_cidx) {
      c_area := emit_abi_c_call_args(args_head, nargs, cx.call_cidx, sb, cx, nl)
      push_str(sb, "  call ")
      emit_mangled_call(sb, cx.src, cs, cl, cx.mod_s, cx.mod_l, cx.decls)
      push_str(sb, "\n")
      if c_area != 0 {
        push_str(sb, "  addq $")
        push_int(sb, i64(c_area * 8))
        push_str(sb, ", %rsp\n")
      }
      emit_abi_c_ret_agg(cx.decls, cx.src, a, cx.call_cidx, sb)
      return
    }
    sc_shift := if cx.sret_call >= 0 { 1 } else { 0 }
    emit_call_args(args_head, -1, -1, -1, nargs, sb, cx, nl)
    push_str(sb, "  call ")
    emit_mangled_call(sb, cx.src, cs, cl, cx.mod_s, cx.mod_l, cx.decls)
    emit_overload_suffix(sb, cs, cl, args_head, cx, a)
    push_str(sb, "\n")
    emit_call_cleanup_shift(sb, nargs, sc_shift)
  }
}

## Deliver a 2-word struct value into %rax (field 0) / %rdx (field 1) — the struct dual of
## `emit_enum_value`, used by a 2-word-struct-returning fn's return (and the same `if`-yields path).
## True if struct `[s, s+n)` has a field whose type is a NON-str MULTI-WORD type (an enum, nested
## struct, or array — `field_words > 1` and not `str`). Such a field can't be delivered by the
## per-field str/scalar push in `emit_struct_value`'s StructLit arm, so that struct is returned via
## the materialize-then-word-deliver path instead. A str-only or all-scalar struct returns false
## (keeps the existing per-field path — byte-identical for the self-host build).
##
## GENERIC INSTANCE (`Box(P)`, `Box := fn(T : type) -> type { struct { v : T } }`): the span carries
## `(…)` type-args, so the decl lookup must STRIP them (`base_type_name`, exactly as `struct_words` /
## `field_word_offset` do) — an unstripped `Box(P)` matched no decl, so this returned FALSE and the
## multi-word field `v : P` was pushed as ONE word: a SILENT truncated struct return. And the per-field
## width must be read through `subst_field_ty`, so a type-PARAM field (`v : T`) sizes as the instance's
## type-arg. Both are aggregate-GATED inside `subst_field_ty` (a scalar type-arg keeps the raw param),
## so every non-generic struct — and every scalar-arg instance — answers byte-identically.
## Does the struct literal `e` have a field VALUE that is a BY-REFERENCE aggregate — a struct/enum
## PARAM, whose slot holds a POINTER instead of the aggregate's own frame words? The per-field
## `emit_gas` push in `emit_struct_value`'s StructLit arm delivers such a value as the POINTER, so the
## field read back an ADDRESS. For a MULTI-word field the `swf > nf` width gate already routes the
## literal through the materialize path; a ONE-word aggregate field (`Box(Box(u64))`, whose `v` is a
## 1-word `Box(u64)`) has `swf == nf` and slipped through — a silent ADDRESS-for-value. This is the
## missing half of that gate.
lit_has_byref_agg_value := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena) -> bool {
  nfv := struct_lit_nf(e)
  fhv := struct_lit_fields(e)
  mut res := false
  for k in 0..nfv {
    fk := arg_expr_at(fhv, k, a)
    vn := var_name_span(fk)
    if vn.n != 0 {
      ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, vn.s, vn.n)))
      if streq(cx.src, ent.ns, ent.nl, vn.s, vn.n) and ent.is_ref and (ent.ek == 2 or ent.ek == 3) { res = true }
    }
  }
  res
}
struct_has_nonstr_multiword_field := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize, a : rt::Arena) -> bool {
  sbn := base_type_name(src, s, n)
  di := struct_decl_of(decls, src, sbn.s, sbn.n)
  if di < 0 { return false }
  d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(di))))
  mut f := d.fields_head
  mut res := false
  while f != 0 {
    fd := deref(fld_p(f))
    eff := subst_field_ty(decls, src, s, n, fd.ts, fd.tl, a)
    efw := eff_field_wsize(decls, src, s, n, fd.ts, fd.tl, fd.wsize, a)
    fw := field_words(decls, src, eff.s, eff.n, efw, a)
    if fw > 1 and str_at((src + eff.s), eff.n) != "str" { res = true }
    f = fd.next
  }
  res
}
## BYTES return value emitter for the bounded internal `[u8; N]` ABI. The local/parameter path
## reads the packed byte block in the same low-address/low-byte order used by `emit_array_assign` and
## `emit_index_addr`; a nested call already leaves its one packed word in %rax and is normalized onto
## that same register. Other expression shapes are deliberately rejected here: adding a guessed
## temporary or array-literal layout would widen this lane beyond the one-word ABI proof.
emit_byte_array_return_value := fn(e : ptr(Expr), in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena, in out nl : usize) {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Var(s, n) => {
      ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, s, n)))
      if ent.ek != 5 or ent.eek != 8 { panic("selfhost: bounded [u8; N] return needs a packed byte-array local or parameter") }
      if ent.is_ref {
        push_str(sb, "  movq -")
        push_int(sb, (ent.off + 1) * 8)
        push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rax\n  movq (%rax), %rax\n")
      } else {
        push_str(sb, "  movq -")
        push_int(sb, ent.off * 8)
        push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rax\n")
      }
    }
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, args_head) => {
      emit_call_dispatch(cs, cl, nargs, args_head, sb, cx, a, nl)
    }
    Expr::Num(v, s, n) => {
      ## A function with only explicit returns carries the parser's no-tail sentinel here. All
      ## reachable paths already jump to the epilogue; emit a harmless unreachable carrier so the
      ## bounded return emitter remains total without treating a source numeric value as an array.
      if v == -1 and s == 0 and n == 0 { push_str(sb, "  pushq $0\n  popq %rax\n") }
      else { panic("selfhost: bounded [u8; N] return does not accept a scalar expression") }
    }
    _ => { panic("selfhost: bounded [u8; N] return supports only a packed byte-array local/parameter or a bounded byte-array call result") }
  }
}

emit_struct_value := fn(e : ptr(Expr), in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena, in out nl : usize) {
  ## `R(v)` is represented as a Call but has no scalar stack result. In a small aggregate return
  ## context, materialize/check it through the dedicated aggregate path before the ordinary Call arm.
  if require_agg_parts(e, cx.decls, cx.src, a).ok {
    emit_require_agg_value(e, sb, cx, a, nl)
    return
  }
  match deref(e) {
    ## Types §9.4 — a RANGE-SLICE expression returned BY VALUE from a `-> Slice(T)` / `-> str` fn
    ## (`return xs[0..4]`). `Expr::Slice` had NO arm here, so the value fell to the `_` default and the
    ## return registers were left untouched: the caller's `r.len`/`r[i]` read stale registers (in
    ## practice 0) — a SILENT MISCOMPILE, and the sibling spellings (`return Slice(u64)(ptr = …, len =
    ## …)`, `return s` for a slice PARAM) both worked, so they disagreed. `emit_slice_pair` already
    ## materializes the {ptr, len} pair for BOTH base kinds (a raw ARRAY base → the stride-aware
    ## element-0 address; a str/str-view base → the byte view); pop it into the same 2-word return
    ## convention `str` and a slice `Var` use (ptr/%rax, len/%rdx).
    Expr::Slice(sbase, slo, shi) => {
      emit_slice_pair(e, sb, cx, a, nl)               ## stack: [ptr (deep), len (top)]
      push_str(sb, "  popq %rdx\n  popq %rax\n")
    }
    Expr::Var(s, n) => {
      ## deliver each of the struct local's `sw` words into the return registers
      ## (word k at slot `ent.off + k` → `emit_retreg(k)`): the %rax:%rdx:%rcx:%r8:%r9:%r10:%r11
      ## convention for 2..7-word structs.
      ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, s, n)))
      ## A runtime-len SLICE (`ek == 5`, `is_ref`, `snl == 1` — a `Slice(T)` view LOCAL, `sns == 0`, or a
      ## `Slice(T)` PARAM whose slot points at the caller's {ptr, len} block, `sns == 1`) returned BY
      ## VALUE. Its two words are NOT a declared struct's frame words, so `struct_words` on the slot's
      ## span yields 0 and the loops below delivered NOTHING: `get := fn(s : Slice(u64)) -> Slice(u64)
      ## { return s }` left %rax/%rdx untouched, so the caller's `r.len` read a stale register — a
      ## SILENT MISCOMPILE reachable as soon as the `Slice` type-decl is in scope (the no-decl program
      ## rides the separate `slice_ret_call` path). `emit_str_pair` already materializes exactly this
      ## {ptr, len} pair for BOTH slot shapes (direct frame words / deref the block); pop it into the
      ## 2-word return convention (ptr/%rax, len/%rdx), the same one `str` uses (Types §9.4).
      ## Gated to the runtime-len marker `snl == 1`, so a by-ref fixed-ARRAY param (`snl == 0`) and
      ## every declared-struct slot are byte-identical → fixpoint-neutral.
      if ent.ek == 5 and ent.is_ref and ent.snl == 1 {
        emit_str_pair(e, sb, cx, a, nl)                 ## stack: [ptr (deep), len (top)]
        push_str(sb, "  popq %rdx\n  popq %rax\n")
        return
      }
      sw := struct_words(cx.decls, cx.src, ent.sns, ent.snl, deref(cx.mar))
      if ent.is_ref {
        ## a BY-REFERENCE struct PARAM (a generic `id(T, v) -> T` returning its param): the slot holds
        ## a POINTER to the caller's struct word 0, so LOAD it (into %r11 = retreg 6, free for the ≤7-
        ## word register-return convention), then deliver word k from `-(k*8)(%r11)` (down-growing
        ## pointee). Reading in order means the k==6 read into %r11 (its own retreg) is last, after the
        ## pointer is no longer needed. Without this the Var arm returned the pointer + adjacent frame.
        push_str(sb, "  movq -")
        push_int(sb, i64((ent.off + 1) * 8))
        push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %r11\n")
        for k in 0..sw {
          push_str(sb, "  movq ")
          push_int(sb, i64(k * 8))
          push_str(sb, "(%r11), ")
          emit_retreg(sb, k)
          push_str(sb, "\n")
        }
      } else {
        for k in 0..sw {
          push_str(sb, "  movq -")
          push_frame_word(sb, ent.off, k)
          push_str(sb, "(%rbp), ")
          emit_retreg(sb, k)
          push_str(sb, "\n")
        }
      }
    }
    Expr::StructLit(cs, cl, nf, fhead) => {
      ## FAIL LOUD on the SILENT-ZERO case of an UNRESOLVABLE construction head — the
      ## return-position dual of the `emit_struct_assign` guard: an undeclared generic-constructor
      ## head (`X(128)(words = […])`, erased to a bare `StructLit`) pushes a `$0` word per
      ## ARRAY-LITERAL field, a SILENT zero return. An all-scalar bare head keeps the positional
      ## per-field delivery (the `Slice(u64)(ptr = p, len = n)` shape, as in `emit_struct_assign`).
      mut badrv := false
      if struct_decl_of(cx.decls, cx.src, cs, cl) < 0 {
        mut grv := fhead
        while grv != 0 {
          garv := deref(arg_p(grv))
          if array_lit_info(garv.e).is_a { badrv = true }
          grv = garv.next
        }
      }
      if badrv { panic("selfhost: a struct construction's head does not resolve to a declared struct type — an unknown type constructor (e.g. `X(128)(words = […])` with no `X` declared) cannot materialize an array-literal field; declare the type or fix the name") }
      ## A struct with a MULTI-WORD field that isn't a plain str (an enum / nested struct / array
      ## field — `struct_words > field_count` and the per-field push below only knows str + scalar):
      ## materialize the whole literal into the aggregate scratch via `emit_struct_assign` (which
      ## stores every field kind correctly), then deliver its words to the return registers word-by-
      ## word (like the Var arm). Without this an enum field was pushed as ONE word (payload dropped).
      ## Types §9.4: inside a generic INSTANCE the construction head is written over the callee's own
      ## type parameter (`Box(T)(v = x)`); resolve it to the instantiation (`Box(P)`) so the width and
      ## the multi-word-field test see the REAL layout. Unchanged for every other head.
      irs := inst_struct_span_cx(cs, cl, cx)
      swf := struct_words(cx.decls, cx.src, irs.s, irs.n, a)
      ## …and route through it TOO when a field VALUE is a by-reference aggregate PARAM, which the
      ## width gate misses for a ONE-word aggregate field (see `lit_has_byref_agg_value`). Correct-or-
      ## trap: without a reserved aggregate scratch there is nowhere to materialize, so panic rather
      ## than emit the address.
      mut matz := swf > nf and struct_has_nonstr_multiword_field(cx.decls, cx.src, irs.s, irs.n, a)
      if matz == false and lit_has_byref_agg_value(e, cx, a) {
        if cx.agg_tmp < 0 { panic("selfhost: a struct literal returned by value with a by-reference aggregate field value has no aggregate scratch to materialize into") }
        matz = true
      }
      if matz {
        emit_struct_assign(e, cx.agg_tmp, sb, cx, a, nl)
        for k in 0..swf {
          push_str(sb, "  movq -")
          push_int(sb, i64((cx.agg_tmp - i64(k) + 1) * 8))
          push_str(sb, "(%rbp), ")
          emit_retreg(sb, k)
          push_str(sb, "\n")
        }
        return
      }
      ## lower each field's WORDS onto the stack in declaration/word order, then pop into the return
      ## registers in REVERSE (top of stack = the last word) so WORD i lands in `emit_retreg(i)`
      ## (word 0 → %rax, … the 2..7-word convention). A `str` field is 2 words ({ptr, len}); a scalar
      ## field is 1. For an ALL-SCALAR struct this is byte-identical to the old per-field loop (word
      ## count == field count), so the self-host build (flat return structs) is unaffected.
      ## Types §9.4 — resolve the decl off the INSTANCE head's BASE NAME (`irs`, stripped of `(…)`):
      ## `struct_decl_of` matches by tail name, so a parenthesized generic head (`Box(str)`) missed and
      ## left `fd == 0`, which made EVERY field look scalar (a 2-word `str` field rode ONE register —
      ## a silent truncation of the returned struct). A plain `S(…)` head strips to itself → unchanged.
      irsb := base_type_name(cx.src, irs.s, irs.n)
      di := struct_decl_of(cx.decls, cx.src, irsb.s, irsb.n)
      mut fd := 0
      if di >= 0 {
        ddd := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(cx.decls), usize(di))))
        fd = ddd.fields_head
      }
      mut sw := 0
      for k in 0..nf {
        fk := arg_expr_at(fhead, k, a)
        mut is_str := false
        if fd != 0 {
          fdn := deref(fld_p(fd))
          ## the SUBSTITUTED field type (a `v : T` param field of `Box(str)` IS a str) — the raw span
          ## `T` missed the probe and pushed one word.
          effr := subst_field_ty(cx.decls, cx.src, irs.s, irs.n, fdn.ts, fdn.tl, a)
          if str_at((cx.src + effr.s), effr.n) == "str" { is_str = true }
          fd = fdn.next
        }
        sti := str_lit_info(fk)
        if is_str and sti.is_s {
          ## a str-literal field → push {ptr, len} as two words (ptr word 0, len word 1)
          push_str(sb, "  leaq ")
          push_lstr(sb, sti.lbl)
          push_str(sb, "(%rip), %rax\n  pushq %rax\n  movq $")
          push_int(sb, i64(sti.sl))
          push_str(sb, ", %rax\n  pushq %rax\n")
          sw += 2
        } else if is_str {
          ## a NON-LITERAL str field value (a str `Var`/param, a `sub`/`str_at`/`bytes` view, a range
          ## slice, a str-returning call): `emit_str_pair` pushes ptr (deep) then len (top) — exactly
          ## the two words the literal arm above pushes. Was `emit_gas` → ONE word (the ptr, or 0 for a
          ## by-ref str param), so `mk(x)` returned a str field with a GARBAGE len: a SILENT MISCOMPILE.
          emit_str_pair(fk, sb, cx, a, nl)
          sw += 2
        } else {
          emit_gas(fk, sb, cx, a, nl)
          sw += 1
        }
      }
      for j in 0..sw {
        k := sw - 1 - j
        push_str(sb, "  popq ")
        emit_retreg(sb, k)
        push_str(sb, "\n")
      }
    }
    Expr::ArrayLit(nel, ehead) => {
      ## a TUPLE value `(e0, …, eN)` (built as an ArrayLit) returned by value — deliver each element
      ## into the return registers exactly like a StructLit's fields (element k → `emit_retreg(k)`,
      ## the %rax:%rdx:%rcx:… convention). Scalar-word elements only. `return (a, b)`.
      for k in 0..nel {
        ek := arg_expr_at(ehead, k, a)
        emit_gas(ek, sb, cx, a, nl)
      }
      for j in 0..nel {
        k := nel - 1 - j
        push_str(sb, "  popq ")
        emit_retreg(sb, k)
        push_str(sb, "\n")
      }
    }
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, args_head) => {
      ## FN-6 INDIRECT CALL through a fn VALUE returning a STRUCT (`w := fv(x)`, `fv : fn(u64) -> W`):
      ## `cs/cl` names a LOCAL/PARAM slot holding a code pointer, not a fn — a `call *%rax` through the
      ## slot, with the SAME return convention the direct call uses. For a 1..7-word struct the callee
      ## leaves word k in `emit_retreg(k)` (the caller reads them back); for a WIDE (>= 8-word) struct
      ## the caller has published the destination's slot base on `cx.sret_call`, and `emit_call_args`
      ## turns that into the hidden result pointer in %rdi (real args shifted to %rsi.., hence
      ## `emit_call_cleanup_shift`). Without this arm the call fell to `emit_call_dispatch`, which
      ## emitted a DIRECT `call <module>__fv` to a nonexistent label — or, when a same-named fn did
      ## exist, called the wrong target. `src/`+`lib/` make no indirect call → byte-identical (fixpoint).
      ## Gated on the CLASSIFIER (which requires `ret_call_target < 0` — no named fn resolves — AND a
      ## fn-value struct return), so a DIRECT struct-returning call shadowed by a same-named local is
      ## still dispatched directly: the whole self-host corpus keeps the byte-identical direct path.
      fvsv := if ind_call_ret_struct_words(e, cx.decls, cx.src, a) != 0 { slot_of(cx.slots, cx.src, cs, cl) } else { 0 - 1 }
      if fvsv >= 0 {
        cx.call_cidx = 0 - 1
        sc_sv := if cx.sret_call >= 0 { 1 } else { 0 }
        cx.ind_fn_fmask = fnval_param_fmask(cs, cl, cx)
        emit_call_args(args_head, 0 - 1, 0 - 1, 0 - 1, nargs, sb, cx, nl)
        push_str(sb, "  movq -")
        push_int(sb, (fvsv + 1) * 8)
        push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rax\n  call *%rax\n")
        emit_call_cleanup_shift(sb, nargs, sc_sv)
        return
      }
      ## a tail call that itself returns a 2-word struct: the callee leaves it in %rax/%rdx.
      ## A generic callee returning a struct (`new -> Vec(T)`) routes to its mangled instance via
      ## `emit_call_dispatch` (was a typetag-less bare label).
      emit_call_dispatch(cs, cl, nargs, args_head, sb, cx, a, nl)
    }
    Expr::Deref(inner) => {
      ## `return deref(p)` (a struct-returning fn whose value is a pointee read) — deref-COPY the
      ## pointee's words into the return registers. The pointee uses the down-growing aggregate
      ## layout (matching the `deref(p).field` READ and the `DerefAssign` STORE): word k at
      ## `-(k*8)(p)`. Load the POINTER into %rax — from a VAR's slot (works for an ek-7 ptr-to-struct
      ## param AND an ek-0 ptr-to-struct LOCAL born of a `bitcast(ptr(S),…)`, both of which hold the
      ## pointer at slot word 0), or from a `deref(<call>)`'s call result (e.g. `deref(node_ptr(…))`).
      ## Then read word 2 (`-16`) → %rcx and word 1 (`-8`) → %rdx FIRST, then word 0 (offset 0) →
      ## %rax LAST (it clobbers the pointer). The word COUNT comes from the fn's RETURN type
      ## (`cx.ret_ss`/`cx.ret_sl`) — the only reliable source, since the parser discards the
      ## `bitcast` target / local annotation so a `p` local carries no struct span. Without this arm a
      ## fn like `ivec_at` (`return deref(p)`, `p` a bitcast LOCAL) returned {0,0} and the self-host
      ## mono loop read every instance record as zero (the bug-#7 fixpoint miscompile).
      dv := deref_var_span(e)
      if dv.n != 0 {
        pent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, dv.s, dv.n)))
        push_str(sb, "  movq -")
        push_int(sb, i64((pent.off + 1) * 8))
        push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rax\n")
      } else {
        emit_gas(inner, sb, cx, a, nl)
        push_str(sb, "  popq %rax\n")
      }
      ## %rax holds the pointer. Read words sw-1 .. 1 into their return registers (down-growing
      ## pointee: word k at -(k*8)(%rax)), THEN word 0 (offset 0) into %rax LAST — it clobbers the
      ## pointer. Word count from the fn's RETURN type (cx.ret_ss/ret_sl).
      sw := struct_words(cx.decls, cx.src, cx.ret_ss, cx.ret_sl, deref(cx.mar))
      mut kk := sw
      while kk > 1 {
        kk = kk - 1
        push_str(sb, "  movq ")
        push_int(sb, i64(kk * 8))
        push_str(sb, "(%rax), ")
        emit_retreg(sb, kk)
        push_str(sb, "\n")
      }
      push_str(sb, "  movq (%rax), %rax\n")
    }
    Expr::Field(fbase, ffs, ffl) => {
      ## A STRUCT-typed FIELD returned BY VALUE (`unbox := fn(T : type, b : Box(T)) -> T { return b.v }`)
      ## — deliver the field's words into the return registers (word k → `emit_retreg(k)`), read from the
      ## field's PLACE: through the base POINTER for a by-ref base (`emit_agg_base_addr` + the field's
      ## word offset, word k at `+(k*8)`, exactly as the `Var` `is_ref` arm above), else straight from the
      ## base's frame words (word k at slot `fslot - k`). Without this a struct `Field` fell to the `_`
      ## fallback, which writes `$0` into %rax/%rdx: a SILENT ZERO struct return. Gated on the field's
      ## type resolving to a STRUCT — every other `Field` (scalar / `str` / unresolvable) keeps the
      ## byte-identical fallback, so `src/`+`lib/` are untouched.
      fbt := base_struct_span(fbase, cx)
      fft := field_type_span(cx.decls, cx.src, fbt.s, fbt.n, ffs, ffl, deref(cx.mar))
      ffbn := base_type_name(cx.src, fft.s, fft.n)
      mut ffw := 0
      if fbt.n != 0 and fft.n != 0 and struct_decl_of(cx.decls, cx.src, ffbn.s, ffbn.n) >= 0 { ffw = struct_words(cx.decls, cx.src, fft.s, fft.n, deref(cx.mar)) }
      if ffw == 0 {
        push_str(sb, "  movq $0, %rax\n  movq $0, %rdx\n")
        return
      }
      ## CORRECT-OR-TRAP: a struct field whose place cannot be resolved, or one wider than the
      ## register-return budget, is a compile-time panic — never a silently truncated value.
      if ffw > 7 { panic("selfhost: an aggregate field wider than the 7-word return-register budget returned by value is unsupported — bind it to a local and return that") }
      ffa := agg_field_of(fbase, ffs, ffl, cx)
      if ffa.ok == false { panic("selfhost: returning this struct FIELD by value is unsupported — its place could not be resolved; bind it to a local (`c := <field>`) and return that") }
      if ffa.is_ref {
        aent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, ffa.ent_idx))
        emit_agg_base_addr(aent, sb)
        if ffa.fi != 0 { push_str(sb, "  addq $") ; push_int(sb, ffa.fi * 8) ; push_str(sb, ", %rax\n") }
        push_str(sb, "  movq %rax, %r11\n")
        for k in 0..ffw {
          push_str(sb, "  movq ")
          push_int(sb, i64(k * 8))
          push_str(sb, "(%r11), ")
          emit_retreg(sb, k)
          push_str(sb, "\n")
        }
      } else {
        for k in 0..ffw {
          push_str(sb, "  movq -")
          push_int(sb, (ffa.slot - i64(k) + 1) * 8)
          push_str(sb, "(%rbp), ")
          emit_retreg(sb, k)
          push_str(sb, "\n")
        }
      }
    }
    _ => { push_str(sb, "  movq $0, %rax\n  movq $0, %rdx\n") }
  }
}

## SRET return: write the whole (>7-word) struct value `e` through the hidden result-pointer (saved in
## `cx.sret_slot`), then leave that pointer in %rax (the SysV sret return value). The result buffer is
## the caller's destination LOCAL, whose word `k` sits at a LOWER address as `k` grows (frame slots
## descend), so word `k` is stored at `-(k*8)(ptr)` — the same DOWN-growing pointee layout the field
## read + `DerefAssign` use. The pointer is reloaded from its slot per field (a field value that is a
## CALL would clobber a held register). Handles the common `return S9(…)` (StructLit) + `return v`
## (Var); other value forms (a tail sret call / `deref(p)`) are deferred.
emit_struct_to_sret := fn(e : ptr(Expr), in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena, in out nl : usize) {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::StructLit(cs, cl, nf, fhead) => {
      for k in 0..nf {
        fk := arg_expr_at(fhead, k, a)
        emit_gas(fk, sb, cx, a, nl)                       ## field value on the stack
        push_str(sb, "  popq %rcx\n  movq -")
        push_int(sb, i64((cx.sret_slot + 1) * 8))
        push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rax\n  movq %rcx, ")
        push_int(sb, i64(k * 8))
        push_str(sb, "(%rax)\n")
      }
    }
    Expr::Var(s, n) => {
      ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, s, n)))
      ## the word count comes from the VALUE's own struct span when the slot records one; a slot with no
      ## recorded type (snl == 0) falls back to the FN's return type (`cx.ret_ss`/`cx.ret_sl`) — the value
      ## returned from an sret fn always HAS that type. Without the fallback `struct_words(0, 0)` yields 0
      ## and the copy loop below writes NOTHING (a silent all-zero result).
      mut sw := if ent.snl != 0 { struct_words(cx.decls, cx.src, ent.sns, ent.snl, deref(cx.mar)) } else { struct_words(cx.decls, cx.src, cx.ret_ss, cx.ret_sl, deref(cx.mar)) }
      ## a wide TUPLE return (`return t`, `t` a tuple LOCAL): a tuple local is an ARRAY slot whose
      ## `snl` records the element COUNT, not a type span — `struct_words` on it is meaningless. The
      ## component count comes from the FN's tuple return span (`cx.ret_ss`/`cx.ret_sl`). Gated on
      ## `cx.ret_tuple` → every struct/enum sret return keeps its exact word count (neutral).
      if cx.ret_tuple { sw = tuple_words(cx.src, cx.ret_ss, cx.ret_sl) }
      if ent.is_ref {
        ## a BY-REFERENCE wide-struct PARAM (`idf := fn(v : S9) -> S9 { return v }`) — an aggregate param
        ## is passed as a POINTER (`is_ref`), so its slot holds an ADDRESS, not the struct's own frame
        ## words. Load that pointer (%r10) alongside the hidden result pointer (%r11) and copy word k from
        ## `k*8(%r10)` to `k*8(%r11)` (both sides use the up-growing pointee layout). Without this arm the
        ## frame-word path below read `-(off - k + 1)*8(%rbp)`, whose `off - k` UNDERFLOWED for a param in
        ## a low slot → a checked-arith trap that crashed the compiler itself (SIGILL). This mirrors
        ## `emit_rv_sret_store`'s param arm on rv64 and `emit_struct_value`'s `is_ref` dual.
        push_str(sb, "  movq -")
        push_int(sb, i64((ent.off + 1) * 8))
        push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %r10\n  movq -")
        push_int(sb, i64((cx.sret_slot + 1) * 8))
        push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %r11\n")
        for k in 0..sw {
          push_str(sb, "  movq ")
          push_int(sb, i64(k * 8))
          push_str(sb, "(%r10), %rcx\n  movq %rcx, ")
          push_int(sb, i64(k * 8))
          push_str(sb, "(%r11)\n")
        }
      } else {
        push_str(sb, "  movq -")
        push_int(sb, i64((cx.sret_slot + 1) * 8))
        push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %r11\n")
        for k in 0..sw {
          push_str(sb, "  movq -")
          push_frame_word(sb, ent.off, k)
          push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rcx\n  movq %rcx, ")
          push_int(sb, i64(k * 8))
          push_str(sb, "(%r11)\n")
        }
      }
    }
    Expr::ArrayLit(nel, ehead) => {
      ## a wide TUPLE value `(e0, …, eN)` (built as an ArrayLit) returned through the hidden result
      ## pointer — the tuple dual of the StructLit arm: lower component k, then store it at `k*8(ptr)`
      ## (the same up-growing result-buffer layout the caller's destination words use). The pointer is
      ## reloaded per component (a component that is a CALL would clobber a held register). Without
      ## this arm a >= 8-component tuple return fell to the `_` catch-all. Dormant for the self-build.
      for k in 0..nel {
        ek := arg_expr_at(ehead, k, a)
        emit_gas(ek, sb, cx, a, nl)
        push_str(sb, "  popq %rcx\n  movq -")
        push_int(sb, i64((cx.sret_slot + 1) * 8))
        push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rax\n  movq %rcx, ")
        push_int(sb, i64(k * 8))
        push_str(sb, "(%rax)\n")
      }
    }
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, args_head) => {
      ## TAIL-FORWARD a wide-struct-returning CALL (`fwd := fn() -> S9 { return mk() }`). Both this fn and
      ## the callee deliver via the hidden result pointer, and the inner call needs a DESTINATION for its
      ## own hidden pointer. Materialize into a fresh agg-temp block (publish its base on `cx.sret_call`,
      ## exactly like a `c := mk()` binding does with the destination local's base), then copy the words
      ## through THIS fn's result pointer. Without a destination the inner call was emitted with NO hidden
      ## pointer at all and this arm fell to `_ => {}` — the result buffer was never written (a SILENT
      ## all-zero struct). `src/`'s one wide-returning fn (`tcomp_find`) returns a literal / a local, never
      ## a call, so this arm never fires on the self-host build (fixpoint-neutral).
      ## A callee returning a 1..7-word struct in the RETURN REGISTERS also lands here (an `@abi(c)` fn
      ## whose SysV MEMORY return forces sret even in the register range): stage word k from `emit_retreg(k)`
      ## through the stack (all 7 retregs may be live, so no register is free to hold the result pointer
      ## alongside them) and store it through the result pointer.
      if sret_ret_call(e, cx.decls, cx.src, a) {
        if cx.agg_tmp < 0 { panic("selfhost: a wide (> 7-word) struct return of a call result needs the aggregate-value temp pool, which this function does not have") }
        csp := call_ret_struct_span(e, cx.decls, cx.src, a)
        cw := struct_words(cx.decls, cx.src, csp.s, csp.n, deref(cx.mar))
        aggsave := cx.agg_next
        toff := agg_alloc(cx)
        ovsc := cx.sret_call
        cx.sret_call = toff
        emit_struct_value(e, sb, cx, a, nl)
        cx.sret_call = ovsc
        cx.agg_next = aggsave
        push_str(sb, "  movq -")
        push_int(sb, i64((cx.sret_slot + 1) * 8))
        push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %r11\n")
        for k in 0..cw {
          push_str(sb, "  movq -")
          push_frame_word(sb, usize(toff), k)
          push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rcx\n  movq %rcx, ")
          push_int(sb, i64(k * 8))
          push_str(sb, "(%r11)\n")
        }
      } else if struct_ret_call(e, cx.decls, cx.src, a) {
        rsp := call_ret_struct_span(e, cx.decls, cx.src, a)
        rw := struct_words(cx.decls, cx.src, rsp.s, rsp.n, deref(cx.mar))
        emit_struct_value(e, sb, cx, a, nl)
        for k in 0..rw {
          push_str(sb, "  pushq ")
          emit_retreg(sb, k)
          push_str(sb, "\n")
        }
        push_str(sb, "  movq -")
        push_int(sb, i64((cx.sret_slot + 1) * 8))
        push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %r11\n")
        for j in 0..rw {
          k := rw - 1 - j
          push_str(sb, "  popq %rcx\n  movq %rcx, ")
          push_int(sb, i64(k * 8))
          push_str(sb, "(%r11)\n")
        }
      } else {
        panic("selfhost: a wide (> 7-word) struct return delivers a CALL result whose callee returns neither by hidden result pointer nor in the return registers — bind the call to a local first (`t := f(…)` then `return t`)")
      }
    }
    Expr::Num(nv, ns, nn) => {
      ## the parser's NO-TRAILING-VALUE sentinel (`Num(-1)`): a body that ends in an explicit `return`
      ## still carries a `d.value`, and `emit_fn` emits it unconditionally after the body statements
      ## (dead code past the epilogue jump). Nothing to deliver — and NOT a fail-loud case.
    }
    _ => {
      ## CORRECT-OR-TRAP: any other value form in a wide-struct return position has no writer for
      ## the hidden result buffer, so it used to leave the caller's destination UNTOUCHED — a silent
      ## garbage/zero struct. Fail at COMPILE time instead of shipping a wrong value.
      panic("selfhost: this value form is not yet delivered through a wide (> 7-word) struct return's hidden result pointer — bind it to a local first (`t := <value>` then `return t`)")
    }
  }
  ## the sret return value IS the result pointer
  push_str(sb, "  movq -")
  push_int(sb, i64((cx.sret_slot + 1) * 8))
  push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rax\n")
}

## SRET return of a WIDE enum (disc + payload > 7 words): write the enum value through the hidden
## result-pointer (`cx.sret_slot`) — disc at word 0, payload word i at word `1+i` (`-(k*8)(ptr)`, the
## down-growing pointee layout the `match` reader expects) — then leave the pointer in %rax. The
## register-staging `emit_enum_value` can't be reused (it tops out at %r11 = 7 words), so words go
## STRAIGHT to memory. Handles `return E.V(<struct lit>)` / `E.V(a, b, …)` (EnumLit) and `return v`
## (an enum Var); the pointer is reloaded per word (a field value that is a call would clobber it).
emit_enum_to_sret := fn(e : ptr(Expr), in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena, in out nl : usize) {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::EnumLit(es, el, vs, vl, np, phead) => {
      disc := variant_index(cx.decls, cx.src, es, el, vs, vl, deref(cx.mar))
      ## payload → words 1.. (emitted BEFORE the disc, which is written last).
      if np == 1 {
        ga := deref(arg_p(phead))
        psl := struct_lit_info(ga.e)
        va := var_agg_info(ga.e, cx.slots, cx.src)
        if psl.is_s {
          fh := struct_lit_fields(ga.e)
          pw := struct_words(cx.decls, cx.src, psl.ss, psl.sl, deref(cx.mar))
          for k in 0..pw {
            fk := arg_expr_at(fh, k, a)
            emit_gas(fk, sb, cx, a, nl)
            push_str(sb, "  popq %rcx\n  movq -")
            push_int(sb, i64((cx.sret_slot + 1) * 8))
            push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rax\n  movq %rcx, ")
            push_int(sb, i64((1 + k) * 8))
            push_str(sb, "(%rax)\n")
          }
        } else if va.ek == 2 {
          vn := var_name_span(ga.e)
          pent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, vn.s, vn.n)))
          pw := struct_words(cx.decls, cx.src, va.s, va.n, deref(cx.mar))
          for k in 0..pw {
            push_str(sb, "  movq -")
            push_frame_word(sb, pent.off, k)
            push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rcx\n  movq -")
            push_int(sb, i64((cx.sret_slot + 1) * 8))
            push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rax\n  movq %rcx, ")
            push_int(sb, i64((1 + k) * 8))
            push_str(sb, "(%rax)\n")
          }
        } else {
          emit_gas(ga.e, sb, cx, a, nl)
          push_str(sb, "  popq %rcx\n  movq -")
          push_int(sb, i64((cx.sret_slot + 1) * 8))
          push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rax\n  movq %rcx, 8(%rax)\n")
        }
      } else if np > 1 {
        ## multiple scalar payload components → words 1..np
        for k in 0..np {
          gk := arg_expr_at(phead, k, a)
          emit_gas(gk, sb, cx, a, nl)
          push_str(sb, "  popq %rcx\n  movq -")
          push_int(sb, i64((cx.sret_slot + 1) * 8))
          push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rax\n  movq %rcx, ")
          push_int(sb, i64((1 + k) * 8))
          push_str(sb, "(%rax)\n")
        }
      }
      push_str(sb, "  movq $")
      push_int(sb, disc)
      push_str(sb, ", %rcx\n  movq -")
      push_int(sb, i64((cx.sret_slot + 1) * 8))
      push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rax\n  movq %rcx, (%rax)\n")
    }
    Expr::Var(s, n) => {
      ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, s, n)))
      etw := 1 + enum_inst_words(cx.decls, cx.src, ent.sns, ent.snl, deref(cx.mar))
      push_str(sb, "  movq -")
      push_int(sb, i64((cx.sret_slot + 1) * 8))
      push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %r11\n")
      for k in 0..etw {
        push_str(sb, "  movq -")
        push_frame_word(sb, ent.off, k)
        push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rcx\n  movq %rcx, ")
        push_int(sb, i64(k * 8))
        push_str(sb, "(%r11)\n")
      }
    }
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, args_head) => {
      ## TAIL-FORWARD a wide-enum-returning CALL (`fwd := fn(k : u64) -> W { return mk(k) }`, W a
      ## `1 + payload >= 8`-word enum). BOTH this fn and the callee deliver through the hidden result
      ## pointer, and the inner call needs a DESTINATION for its own. Materialize into a fresh agg-temp
      ## block (publish its base on `cx.sret_call`, exactly like a `w := mk(k)` binding does with the
      ## destination local's base — `emit_call_args` then hands the block's ADDRESS down as the hidden
      ## %rdi), then copy the `{disc, payload…}` words through THIS fn's result pointer. The enum dual of
      ## `emit_struct_to_sret`'s tail-forward arm, and the x86_64 answer to aarch64's `A64_SRET_DST_IND`
      ## one-shot (a64 hands its OWN spilled x8 down; x86 stages + copies, reusing the proven struct
      ## wiring). Was the silent `_ => {}` below: the inner call got NO hidden pointer at all and the
      ## caller's destination was never written — `main` read an all-zero enum and exited 0.
      if enum_sret_ret_call(e, cx.decls, cx.src, a) {
        if cx.agg_tmp < 0 { panic("selfhost: a wide (disc + payload > 7-word) enum return of a call result needs the aggregate-value temp pool, which this function does not have") }
        eci := call_ret_enum_span_d(e, cx.decls, cx.src, a)
        ecw := 1 + enum_inst_words(cx.decls, cx.src, eci.s, eci.n, deref(cx.mar))
        eaggsave := cx.agg_next
        etoff := agg_alloc(cx)
        eovsc := cx.sret_call
        cx.sret_call = etoff
        emit_enum_value(e, sb, cx, a, nl)
        cx.sret_call = eovsc
        cx.agg_next = eaggsave
        push_str(sb, "  movq -")
        push_int(sb, i64((cx.sret_slot + 1) * 8))
        push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %r11\n")
        for k in 0..ecw {
          push_str(sb, "  movq -")
          push_frame_word(sb, usize(etoff), k)
          push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rcx\n  movq %rcx, ")
          push_int(sb, i64(k * 8))
          push_str(sb, "(%r11)\n")
        }
      } else if enum_ret_call_d(e, cx.decls, cx.src, a) {
        ## a callee that delivers its enum in the RETURN REGISTERS (word k in `emit_retreg(k)`) while
        ## THIS fn returns via the hidden pointer — the register/sret seam a generic or `@abi(c)` return
        ## type can open. Stage the words through the stack (all 7 retregs may be live, so no register is
        ## free to hold the result pointer alongside them), then store each through the result pointer.
        rci := call_ret_enum_span_d(e, cx.decls, cx.src, a)
        rcw := 1 + enum_inst_words(cx.decls, cx.src, rci.s, rci.n, deref(cx.mar))
        emit_enum_value(e, sb, cx, a, nl)
        for k in 0..rcw {
          push_str(sb, "  pushq ")
          emit_retreg(sb, k)
          push_str(sb, "\n")
        }
        push_str(sb, "  movq -")
        push_int(sb, i64((cx.sret_slot + 1) * 8))
        push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %r11\n")
        for j in 0..rcw {
          k := rcw - 1 - j
          push_str(sb, "  popq %rcx\n  movq %rcx, ")
          push_int(sb, i64(k * 8))
          push_str(sb, "(%r11)\n")
        }
      } else {
        panic("selfhost: a wide (disc + payload > 7-word) enum return delivers a CALL result whose callee returns neither by hidden result pointer nor in the return registers — bind the call to a local first (`t := f(…)` then `return t`)")
      }
    }
    Expr::Num(nv, ns, nn) => {
      ## the parser's NO-TRAILING-VALUE sentinel (`Num(-1)`): a body that ends in an explicit `return`
      ## still carries a `d.value`, and `emit_fn` emits it unconditionally after the body statements
      ## (dead code past the epilogue jump). Nothing to deliver — and NOT a fail-loud case.
    }
    _ => {
      ## CORRECT-OR-TRAP: any other value form in a wide-enum return position has no writer for
      ## the hidden result buffer, so it used to leave the caller's destination UNTOUCHED — a silent
      ## all-zero enum (which reads as variant 0 with a zero payload). Fail at COMPILE time instead.
      panic("selfhost: this value form is not yet delivered through a wide (disc + payload > 7-word) enum return's hidden result pointer — bind it to a local first (`t := <value>` then `return t`)")
    }
  }
  push_str(sb, "  movq -")
  push_int(sb, i64((cx.sret_slot + 1) * 8))
  push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rax\n")
}

## Is `e` an enum-valued expression (an `EnumLit` ctor, or a `Call` to an enum-returning fn,
## or an enum-typed `Var` local)? Used so a `return <e>` / trailing `<e>` whose enclosing fn
## returns an enum delivers via `emit_enum_value`, and a `match <Call>` over an enum result
## stages the call into a temp. The deref-match stays here (a direct PARAM).
expr_is_enum := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> bool {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::EnumLit(es, el, vs, vl, np, phead) => { true }
    Expr::Var(s, n) => {
      ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, s, n)))
      ent.ek == 3
    }
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, args_head) => {
      ci := enum_ret_call(e, cx)
      ci
    }
    Expr::Try(inner) => { false }
    Expr::Unchecked(inner) => { expr_is_enum(inner, cx) }
    Expr::Bitcast(inner, _bcs, _bcl) => { expr_is_enum(inner, cx) }
    _ => { false }
  }
}

## For an aggregate-var COPY `x := <v>`: if `v` is a `Var` bound as a by-value struct (ek 2) or enum
## (ek 3), report its kind + the struct/enum TYPE span so `x` can be reserved + word-copied. Returns
## ek 0 for anything else (a pointer-to-enum ek 6 / ptr-to-struct ek 7 is a 1-word POINTER copied by
## the scalar path — treating it as a multi-word aggregate would clobber neighbouring slots).
## If `v` is `Field(Var(s), f)` where `s` is a LOCAL struct (ek 2) and `f` is a STRUCT (kind 2) or
## ENUM (kind 3) field, report the field's aggregate kind + type span + the base struct's slot offset
## + the field's word offset — so `x := s.f` reserves `x` as that aggregate and the emit copies the
## field's words in (an aggregate-field extract; the scalar path drops all but word 0 → silent 0).
## kind 0 for anything else. `a` = the AST/decl arena.
## `isr` = the BASE struct is a BY-REFERENCE param (its slot holds a POINTER to the caller's word 0,
## pointee word k at `+k*8`), not an inline frame local (word k at slot `off - k`). The emit needs the
## two shapes apart; reading a by-ref base's frame slots as if they were the struct's words produced a
## bogus offset that tripped the compiler's own checked arithmetic — a `ud2` SIGILL with NO diagnostic.
FieldAgg := struct { kind : u8, s : usize, n : usize, boff : usize, fwo : i64, isr : bool }

## The GLOBAL-base dual of `field_read_agg`: `x := STATE.f` where STATE is a mutable-global struct and
## `f` is a struct/enum field. Reports the field's aggregate kind + type span + the global's name span
## (for the `.data` label) + the field's word offset. The emit copies the field's words from `.data`
## (ASCENDING: `LABEL + (fwo+k)*8`). kind 0 for anything else.
GFieldAgg := struct { kind : u8, s : usize, n : usize, gs : usize, gn : usize, fwo : i64 }
global_field_agg := fn(v : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> GFieldAgg {
  mut r := GFieldAgg(kind = 0, s = 0, n = 0, gs = 0, gn = 0, fwo = 0)
  match deref(v) {
    Expr::Field(base, fs, fl) => {
      bvn := var_name_span(base)
      if bvn.n != 0 {
        gmv := mut_global_value(decls, src, bvn.s, bvn.n)
        if unchecked bitcast(usize, gmv) != 0 {
          gsli := struct_lit_info(gmv)
          if gsli.is_s {
            ft := field_type_span(decls, src, gsli.ss, gsli.sl, fs, fl, a)
            if ft.n != 0 {
              ftb := base_type_name(src, ft.s, ft.n)
              fwo := field_word_offset(decls, src, gsli.ss, gsli.sl, fs, fl, a)
              if fwo >= 0 {
                if is_union_decl(decls, src, ftb.s, ftb.n) { r = GFieldAgg(kind = 2, s = ft.s, n = ft.n, gs = bvn.s, gn = bvn.n, fwo = fwo) }
                else if struct_decl_of(decls, src, ftb.s, ftb.n) >= 0 { r = GFieldAgg(kind = 2, s = ft.s, n = ft.n, gs = bvn.s, gn = bvn.n, fwo = fwo) }
                else if enum_decl_of(decls, src, ftb.s, ftb.n) >= 0 { r = GFieldAgg(kind = 3, s = ft.s, n = ft.n, gs = bvn.s, gn = bvn.n, fwo = fwo) }
              }
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  r
}

VarAgg := struct { ek : u8, s : usize, n : usize }
var_agg_info := fn(v : ptr(Expr), slots : ptr(SVec), src : ptr(u8)) -> VarAgg {
  vn := var_name_span(v)
  if vn.n == 0 { return VarAgg(ek = 0, s = 0, n = 0) }
  ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, slots, entry_of(slots, src, vn.s, vn.n)))
  if streq(src, ent.ns, ent.nl, vn.s, vn.n) {
    if ent.ek == 2 { return VarAgg(ek = 2, s = ent.sns, n = ent.snl) }
    if ent.ek == 3 { return VarAgg(ek = 3, s = ent.sns, n = ent.snl) }
  }
  return VarAgg(ek = 0, s = 0, n = 0)
}

## `bitcast(<UserStruct>, v)` — the aggregate→aggregate reinterpret the parser PRESERVES as an
## `Expr::Bitcast` carrying the TARGET struct-NAME span (only for a bare non-scalar target). Returns
## that struct span when `v` is such a node whose target resolves to a struct decl; {0,0} otherwise —
## so the sub-word-POINTER `Bitcast` (its span names a scalar pointee) and any non-struct target fall
## through to the identity path. Neutral: `src/` bitcasts only to scalars/pointers, never a struct.
bitcast_struct_target_direct := fn(v : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8)) -> CSpan {
  match deref(v) {
    Expr::Bitcast(inner, ts, tl) => {
      if tl != 0 and struct_decl_of(decls, src, ts, tl) >= 0 { return CSpan(s = ts, n = tl) }
      CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
    }
    _ => { CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  }
}
bitcast_struct_target := fn(v : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8)) -> CSpan {
  match deref(v) {
    Expr::Unchecked(inner) => { bitcast_struct_target_direct(inner, decls, src) }
    _ => { bitcast_struct_target_direct(v, decls, src) }
  }
}
## Like `bitcast_struct_target` but for a POINTER-to-struct bitcast target: if `v` (seeing through an
## `unchecked` wrapper) is `bitcast(ptr(<Struct>), x)` / `bitcast(ptr(mut <Struct>), x)`, return the
## POINTEE struct's type span; 0/0 otherwise. Used to infer `ek = 7` for a bare
## `vp := unchecked bitcast(ptr(Struct), addr)` local (NO type annotation) so `deref(vp)` / `vp.f`
## resolve the aggregate THROUGH the pointer rather than reading zeros. The annotated form
## (`vp : ptr(Struct) = …`) recovers the pointee from `local_type_span`; this recovers it from the
## bitcast TARGET when the local is inferred.
bitcast_ptrstruct_span := fn(v : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> CSpan {
  match deref(v) {
    Expr::Unchecked(inner) => { return bitcast_ptrstruct_span(inner, decls, src, a) }
    Expr::Bitcast(inner, ts, tl) => {
      if tl != 0 { return ptr_pointee_struct_span(src, ts, tl, decls, a) }
      return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
    }
    _ => { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  }
}

## P0-NESTED-DEREF-BITCAST — a pointer-to-struct root can be either a collected `ek == 7` local
## (`deref(p)`) or a parser-preserved pointer bitcast (`deref(bitcast(ptr(S), ...))`). The existing
## nested pointer resolver only recognizes `Var` at the latter root, so the exact bitcast form fell
## through to `field_slot` and pushed zero. Keep this root recovery separate from `PtrPlace`: the latter
## is deliberately word-tier and carries a frame slot index, while this path needs the actual pointer
## expression plus a byte offset for a standard byte-layout pointee.
ByteDerefRoot := struct { ok : bool, p : ptr(Expr), ts : usize, tl : usize }
bitcast_deref_root := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> ByteDerefRoot {
  z := unchecked bitcast(ptr(Expr), 0)
  mut res := ByteDerefRoot(ok = false, p = z, ts = 0, tl = 0)
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Deref(p) => {
      ## `bitcast_ptrstruct_span` sees through `Unchecked` and the preserved Bitcast target. For a
      ## named pointer local it returns zero, so recover that established `ek == 7` slot shape too.
      bt := bitcast_ptrstruct_span(p, cx.decls, cx.src, deref(cx.mar))
      if bt.n != 0 { res = ByteDerefRoot(ok = true, p = p, ts = bt.s, tl = bt.n) }
      vn := var_name_span(p)
      if vn.n != 0 {
        ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, vn.s, vn.n)))
        if ent.ek == 7 and ent.snl != 0 { res = ByteDerefRoot(ok = true, p = p, ts = ent.sns, tl = ent.snl) }
      }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  res
}

## The byte-layout counterpart of `resolve_nested_ptr_field`, bounded to the two proven roots above.
## A root whose layout is word-tier returns `ok = false`, leaving the existing word resolver in charge.
## A packed root/intermediate is rejected rather than composed with standard offsets. `bo` is the
## cumulative byte displacement from the pointer root; `ts`/`tl` are the terminal field type.
ByteDerefPath := struct { ok : bool, p : ptr(Expr), bo : i64, ts : usize, tl : usize }
bitcast_deref_path := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> ByteDerefPath {
  z := unchecked bitcast(ptr(Expr), 0)
  mut res := ByteDerefPath(ok = false, p = z, bo = 0, ts = 0, tl = 0)
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Deref(p) => {
      r := bitcast_deref_root(e, cx)
      if r.ok {
        if is_packed(cx.decls, cx.src, r.ts, r.tl) {
          panic("selfhost: nested field through a pointer to a @packed struct is not yet supported (byte-precise pointer field addressing is deferred)")
        }
        if layout_kind_is_byte(layout_kind(cx.decls, cx.src, r.ts, r.tl, deref(cx.mar))) {
          res = ByteDerefPath(ok = true, p = r.p, bo = 0, ts = r.ts, tl = r.tl)
        }
      }
    }
    Expr::Field(base, fs, fl) => {
      p := bitcast_deref_path(base, cx)
      if p.ok {
        if is_packed(cx.decls, cx.src, p.ts, p.tl) {
          panic("selfhost: nested field through a pointer into a @packed struct is not yet supported (byte-precise pointer field addressing is deferred)")
        }
        bo := layout_field_offset_bytes(cx.decls, cx.src, p.ts, p.tl, fs, fl, deref(cx.mar))
        ft := field_type_span(cx.decls, cx.src, p.ts, p.tl, fs, fl, deref(cx.mar))
        if bo >= 0 and ft.n != 0 { res = ByteDerefPath(ok = true, p = p.p, bo = p.bo + bo, ts = ft.s, tl = ft.n) }
      }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  res
}
## The inner VALUE of an `Expr::Bitcast` (the reinterpreted source), or `v` itself when not a Bitcast.
bitcast_inner_expr_direct := fn(v : ptr(Expr)) -> ptr(Expr) {
  match deref(v) {
    Expr::Bitcast(inner, ts, tl) => { inner }
    _ => { v }
  }
}
bitcast_inner_expr := fn(v : ptr(Expr)) -> ptr(Expr) {
  match deref(v) {
    Expr::Unchecked(inner) => { bitcast_inner_expr_direct(inner) }
    _ => { bitcast_inner_expr_direct(v) }
  }
}

## Resolve a `ptr( [mut] <pointee> )` type span (`ts`/`tl`) to a CONCRETE struct span, substituting a
## type-PARAM pointee through the enclosing generic INSTANCE's Subst (`gps`→`its`, `gps2`→`its2`,
## `gps3`→`its3`). The subst-aware sibling of `ptr_pointee_struct_span`: a concrete pointee
## (`ptr(mut Rec)`) needs no subst; a type-param pointee (`ptr(mut V)` inside an `OMap(K,V)` method)
## resolves `V` → the monomorphization type (a struct → the words survive). 0/0 unless the substituted
## pointee is a KNOWN struct. Neutral for a non-generic / scalar pointee (`gpl==0` or `usize` → no
## struct → 0/0, identical to `ptr_pointee_struct_span`).
subst_ptr_pointee_struct_span := fn(src : ptr(u8), ts : usize, tl : usize, decls : ptr(rt::Vec), a : rt::Arena, gps : usize, gpl : usize, its : usize, itl : usize, gps2 : usize, gpl2 : usize, its2 : usize, itl2 : usize, gps3 : usize, gpl3 : usize, its3 : usize, itl3 : usize) -> CSpan {
  pn := ptr_pointee_name(src, ts, tl)
  if pn.n == 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  mut rs := pn.s
  mut rl := pn.n
  if gpl != 0 and streq(src, pn.s, pn.n, gps, gpl) { rs = its; rl = itl }
  else if gpl2 != 0 and streq(src, pn.s, pn.n, gps2, gpl2) { rs = its2; rl = itl2 }
  else if gpl3 != 0 and streq(src, pn.s, pn.n, gps3, gpl3) { rs = its3; rl = itl3 }
  if struct_decl_of(decls, src, rs, rl) >= 0 { return CSpan(s = rs, n = rl) }
  CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
}

## The subst-aware sibling of `bitcast_ptrstruct_span`: infer the pointee struct of a bare
## `vp := unchecked bitcast(ptr(mut V), addr)` local where `V` is the enclosing generic fn's
## type-param (resolved to the instance struct via the Subst). Subsumes the concrete case (no subst
## fires → same span as `bitcast_ptrstruct_span`), so it replaces it at the collect_slots call site.
bitcast_ptrstruct_span_sub := fn(v : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena, gps : usize, gpl : usize, its : usize, itl : usize, gps2 : usize, gpl2 : usize, its2 : usize, itl2 : usize, gps3 : usize, gpl3 : usize, its3 : usize, itl3 : usize) -> CSpan {
  match deref(v) {
    Expr::Unchecked(inner) => { return bitcast_ptrstruct_span_sub(inner, decls, src, a, gps, gpl, its, itl, gps2, gpl2, its2, itl2, gps3, gpl3, its3, itl3) }
    Expr::Bitcast(inner, ts, tl) => {
      if tl != 0 { return subst_ptr_pointee_struct_span(src, ts, tl, decls, a, gps, gpl, its, itl, gps2, gpl2, its2, itl2, gps3, gpl3, its3, itl3) }
      return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
    }
    _ => { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  }
}

## Words of the pointee struct when `p` is a `Var` bound as a pointer-to-struct (ek 7); 0 otherwise.
## Lets a `deref(vd) = deref(vs)` store copy the WHOLE pointee struct (both pointers ek 7) — the
## pointee→pointee dual of the by-ref struct store. A scalar pointer (`ptr(mut usize)`, ek 0) → 0, so
## the unchanged scalar-store path handles `src/`'s `deref(dp) = deref(sp)` → fixpoint-neutral.
ptr_var_struct_words := fn(p : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> usize {
  vn := var_name_span(p)
  if vn.n == 0 { return 0 }
  ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, vn.s, vn.n)))
  if ent.ek != 7 { return 0 }
  struct_words(cx.decls, cx.src, ent.sns, ent.snl, deref(cx.mar))
}

## Is `e` an `if`/`match` BRANCH expression? Used by the `DerefAssign` store to fail LOUD on
## `deref(p) = <if/match …>` into a MULTI-WORD pointee — the scalar store path would move only word 0
## (a silent word-drop). Single-level match (the nested-match idiom).
expr_is_branch := fn(e : ptr(Expr)) -> bool {
  mut r := false
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::If(c, t, f) => { r = true }
    Expr::Match(sc, h) => { r = true }
    _ => {}
  }
  r
}

## If `base` is a `bytes(<s>)` call — the spec str→`[u8]` byte view (appendix 160 §3.5) — return
## its str argument so `bytes(s)[i]` can lower as a BYTE read; otherwise a null `Expr` pointer.
## The `match deref` stays in this helper: a nested deref-`match` inside another match arm does
## not lower (Stage-0 "variant-pattern match is not lowered yet"), the same reason the other
## deref-matches are isolated into pointer-PARAM helpers.
bytes_view_arg := fn(base : ptr(Expr), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> ptr(Expr) {
  mut r := unchecked bitcast(ptr(Expr), 0)
  match deref(base) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, ah) => {
      if nargs == 1 and str_at((src + cs), cl) == "bytes" { r = arg_expr_at(ah, 0, a) }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  r
}

## Does argument `e` need a 2-word str TEMP block (materialized + passed by reference)? A str
## LITERAL or a `str_at(…)` view does; and — SEEING THROUGH the `bytes(x)` view — so does
## `bytes(<literal>)` / `bytes(str_at(…))`, since `bytes` is a no-op `{ptr,len}` view and the
## inner is emitted via the same temp (`emit_arg` recurses into the `bytes` arg with this off).
## A `bytes(<var>)` needs NO temp (the var has a frame home). Both the temp-pool SIZER
## (`scan_call_str_args`) and the per-arg OFFSET (`str_arg_tmp_off`) use this so they agree.
arg_str_temp := fn(e : ptr(Expr), src : ptr(u8), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), a : rt::Arena) -> bool {
  mut inner := e
  bva := bytes_view_arg(e, src, a)
  if unchecked bitcast(usize, bva) != 0 { inner = bva }
  si := str_lit_info(inner)
  if si.is_s or is_strat_call(inner, src) or is_name_field_ref(e, src) { return true }
  ## a str-CONSTANT `Var` argument (`MSG := "…"`) is materialized like a str literal → it too needs a
  ## 2-word temp block, so the pool is sized + indexed consistently (scan + offset agree).
  vn := var_name_span(e)
  if vn.n != 0 {
    mcv := module_const_value(decls, src, vn.s, vn.n)
    if unchecked bitcast(usize, mcv) != 0 and str_lit_info(mcv).is_s { return true }
  }
  false
}

## Syntactic `<var>.name` — a comptime FIELD-NAME read (`display`'s `f.name`), a compile-time `str`.
## Used by the str-temp SIZING (`scan_call_str_args`) + OFFSET (`str_arg_tmp_off`), which have no
## `LCtx`, so the test is purely syntactic; the whole codebase's only `.name` field access is the
## comptime-for `f.name` (structs spell their fields `name_start`/`name_len`), so this never
## mis-fires on a real struct field and is fixpoint-neutral for `src/` (which has no `.name` read).
is_name_field_ref := fn(e : ptr(Expr), src : ptr(u8)) -> bool {
  mut res := false
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Field(base, fs, fl) => {
      if str_at((src + fs), fl) == "name" {
        vn := var_name_span(base)
        if vn.n != 0 { res = true }
      }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  res
}

## If `e` is an aggregate `Var` of more than one word, return that word count (so a `deref(p) = e`
## store copies the WHOLE aggregate, not just word 0); 1 otherwise (the scalar single-word store).
## A STRUCT `Var` (ek 2) sizes via `struct_words`; an ENUM `Var` (ek 3) — incl. a by-ref enum
## PARAM like `snode`'s `val : Stmt` — sizes via `1 + enum_max_arity` (disc + max payload words).
## Without the enum case a `deref(node_ptr(…)) = val` stored only the by-ref POINTER (1 word) into
## the node, leaving the rest of the Stmt zero → the self-emitted parser produced empty AST nodes
## (the TOOL-1 Stage2 emit_rodata_stmts hang).
deref_store_words := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> usize {
  vn := var_name_span(e)
  if vn.n == 0 { return 1 }
  ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, vn.s, vn.n)))
  if ent.ek == 2 { return struct_words(cx.decls, cx.src, ent.sns, ent.snl, deref(cx.mar)) }
  if ent.ek == 3 { return 1 + enum_inst_words(cx.decls, cx.src, ent.sns, ent.snl, deref(cx.mar)) }
  return 1
}

## Recover the pointee name of a pointer local. An annotated local is covered by `local_type_span`;
## an inferred local needs the source spelling of the preserved `bitcast(ptr(T), …)` target because
## `Stmt::Assign` deliberately carries no type field. This is intentionally limited to the exact
## `name := [unchecked ]bitcast(ptr([mut ]T), …)` shape: an unresolved pointer keeps the old word-sized
## fallback rather than guessing a width. The source scan is the same bounded recovery used by
## `byte_ptr_local` and does not change any ordinary scalar slot layout.
## CLAYOUT S3(b): a local bound from a CALL returning `ptr(mut T)` carries no `bitcast` in its
## source and no annotation, so the scan below finds nothing. `bind_ptrview_slot` records the
## ALREADY-RESOLVED §7 view pointee on such a slot (the eek-6 marker); read it first — it is the
## concrete type, so no further substitution applies.
local_ptr_pointee_span := fn(cx : ptr(LCtx), s : usize, n : usize) -> CSpan {
  return slot_ptr_pointee_span(cx.slots, cx.src, s, n)
}
slot_ptr_pointee_span := fn(slots : ptr(SVec), src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize) -> CSpan {
  ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, slots, entry_of(slots, src, s, n)))
  if streq(src, ent.ns, ent.nl, s, n) == false { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  if ent.ek == 0 and ent.eek == 6 and ent.snl != 0 { return CSpan(s = ent.sns, n = ent.snl) }
  lts := local_type_span(src, ent.ns, ent.nl)
  if lts.n != 0 {
    if str_at((src + lts.s), 4) == "ptr(" {
      mut ps := lts.s + 4
      mut pl := lts.n - 5
      if pl > 4 and str_at((src + ps), 4) == "mut " { ps = ps + 4 ; pl = pl - 4 }
      return CSpan(s = ps, n = pl)
    }
    return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
  }
  mut p := ent.ns + ent.nl
  end := p + 512
  mut c := str_at((src + p), 1)
  while p < end and (c == " " or c == "\n" or c == "\t" or c == "\r") {
    p = p + 1
    c = str_at((src + p), 1)
  }
  if str_at((src + p), 2) != ":=" { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  p = p + 2
  c = str_at((src + p), 1)
  while p < end and (c == " " or c == "\n" or c == "\t" or c == "\r") {
    p = p + 1
    c = str_at((src + p), 1)
  }
  if str_at((src + p), 9) == "unchecked" {
    p = p + 9
    c = str_at((src + p), 1)
    while p < end and (c == " " or c == "\n" or c == "\t" or c == "\r") {
      p = p + 1
      c = str_at((src + p), 1)
    }
  }
  if str_at((src + p), 8) != "bitcast(" { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  p = p + 8
  ts := p
  mut depth := 0
  mut done := false
  while p < end and done == false {
    c = str_at((src + p), 1)
    if c == "(" { depth = depth + 1 }
    else if c == ")" {
      if depth > 0 { depth = depth - 1 }
    } else if c == "," and depth == 0 { done = true }
    if done == false { p = p + 1 }
  }
  if done == false { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  mut te := p
  while te > ts {
    c = str_at((src + te - 1), 1)
    if c == " " or c == "\n" or c == "\t" or c == "\r" { te = te - 1 }
    else { break }
  }
  if te <= ts or str_at((src + ts), 4) != "ptr(" { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  mut ps2 := ts + 4
  mut pl2 := te - ts - 5
  if pl2 > 4 and str_at((src + ps2), 4) == "mut " { ps2 = ps2 + 4 ; pl2 = pl2 - 4 }
  CSpan(s = ps2, n = pl2)
}

## The pointee TYPE span of a `deref(p)` PLACE (a `deref(p) = v` destination, or the pointer of a
## `deref(p)` READ), resolved THROUGH the enclosing generic instance. Inside `Vec(T)`'s `push` the
## destination is `elem := bitcast(ptr(mut T), …)`, whose SOURCE spelling is the type-PARAMETER `T`;
## the monomorphized instance binds it to the concrete type-argument, so a `Vec(str)` element store
## must see `str`.
##
## CLAYOUT S3(b): the pointer is not always a local. The sibling containers reach an element
## through a HELPER CALL declared `-> ptr(mut T)` (`dq_elem`, `val_at`, `omap_val_elem`,
## `oset_elem`), and at that use site the local-slot / bitcast-source recovery above sees nothing.
## `call_ret_pointee_span` recovers it from the CALLEE's declared return type, mapping the pointee
## type-parameter to the call's type ARGUMENT by POSITION. That single addition is what lets a
## `Deque(str)` / `HashMap(K, str)` / `omap` / `oset` element be the two-word pair it is.
##
## `{0,0}` when the pointee cannot be recovered — the caller then keeps its existing path.
deref_dest_pointee_span := fn(p : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> CSpan {
  vn := var_name_span(p)
  if vn.n == 0 {
    cp := call_ret_pointee_span(p, cx.decls, cx.src, deref(cx.mar), cx.gp_s, cx.gp_l, cx.it_s, cx.it_l, cx.gp2_s, cx.gp2_l, cx.it2_s, cx.it2_l, cx.gp3_s, cx.gp3_l, cx.it3_s, cx.it3_l)
    return cp
  }
  pt := local_ptr_pointee_span(cx, vn.s, vn.n)
  if pt.n == 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  if cx.gp_l != 0 and streq(cx.src, pt.s, pt.n, cx.gp_s, cx.gp_l) { return CSpan(s = cx.it_s, n = cx.it_l) }
  if cx.gp2_l != 0 and streq(cx.src, pt.s, pt.n, cx.gp2_s, cx.gp2_l) { return CSpan(s = cx.it2_s, n = cx.it2_l) }
  if cx.gp3_l != 0 and streq(cx.src, pt.s, pt.n, cx.gp3_s, cx.gp3_l) { return CSpan(s = cx.it3_s, n = cx.it3_l) }
  return pt
}

## `deref(<pointer>)` used as a VALUE whose pointee is a §7 VIEW — the view TYPE span, or {0,0}. The
## emit-side dual of `deref_view_pointee_span` (which runs at slot-collection time). One resolver,
## every pointer shape: a bitcast/annotated local, an eek-6 call-derived pointer local, or the call
## itself, with the pointee mapped by TYPE-PARAMETER POSITION.
deref_view_span_cx := fn(v : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> CSpan {
  inner := deref_inner_expr(v)
  if unchecked bitcast(usize, inner) == 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  pt := deref_dest_pointee_span(inner, cx)
  if pt.n == 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  if is_view_type(cx.src, pt.s, pt.n) == false { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  pt
}

## The pointee BYTE WIDTH (1 / 2 / 4 / 8) that a `deref` LOAD/STORE through pointer expression `p`
## must move, or 8 when the pointee is word-sized / unknown (keep the full `movq`). Sees THROUGH an
## `Unchecked` wrapper and reads the parser-preserved `Expr::Bitcast(_, ps, pl)` pointee span (the
## parser records it ONLY for a known sub-word pointee, so `scalar_byte_size` returns 1/2/4 there);
## every other shape → 8. Isolated pointer-PARAM helper — a nested `match deref(x)` in another arm
## does not lower, hence this stands alone (per the self-host lower idiom).
deref_pointee_bytes := fn(p : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> usize {
  mut w := 8
  match deref(p) {
    Expr::Unchecked(inner) => { w = deref_pointee_bytes(inner, cx) }
    Expr::Bitcast(inner, ps, pl) => { w = scalar_byte_size(cx.src, ps, pl) }
    Expr::Var(vs, vn) => {
      pty := local_ptr_pointee_span(cx, vs, vn)
      if pty.n != 0 { w = scalar_byte_size(cx.src, pty.s, pty.n) }
      else if byte_ptr_local(cx, vs, vn) { w = 1 }
    }
    ## `q := s.ptr; deref(q)` — the DATA pointer of a `str` / byte-element view is a `ptr(u8)`
    ## (Types §9.4: `str` is `{ptr : ptr(u8), len}`), so `deref(q)` reads ONE BYTE. The slot carries no
    ## pointee type (a `.ptr` read binds an untyped scalar — `infer_local_scalar_type` only records
    ## BUILT-IN NUMERIC names), so the width fell to the `movq` default: `deref(q)` returned the first
    ## EIGHT bytes of the string as one integer. `u64(deref(q))` still looked right through an exit code
    ## (mod 256 = the first byte), while `if deref(q) != 104` compared the full word and took the WRONG
    ## branch — two spellings disagreeing on the same read, plus a 7-byte OVER-READ past a short str.
    ## `byte_ptr_local` recovers the pointee by SOURCE-SCANNING the binding (the established
    ## `local_type_span` / `slot_name_is_annotated` idiom) — it fires only for an INFERRED `q := <view>.ptr`
    ## local, and neither `src/` nor `lib/` binds one → fixpoint-neutral.
    Expr::Var(vs, vn) => { if byte_ptr_local(cx, vs, vn) { w = 1 } }
    _ => {}
  }
  w
}

## Is the local named `[s, n)` bound as `q := <view>.ptr`, where `<view>` is a `str` / byte-element
## view — i.e. is `q` a `ptr(u8)`? SOURCE-SCAN from the slot's DECLARATION name span (`ent.ns`/`ent.nl`,
## the same anchor `slot_name_is_annotated` uses): the next non-space bytes must be `:=`, then an
## IDENTIFIER, then `.ptr`, then a terminator. The identifier's own slot decides: `ek == 4` is a `str`
## local/param/view (`str` IS `[u8]`, appendix 160 §3.5); an `ek == 2` `Slice(u8)` value and an `ek == 5`
## byte-element slice are the `Slice` duals. Anything else (a `ptr(u64)`-yielding `.ptr`, an annotated
## binding, a non-`.ptr` RHS) → false → the unchanged word-sized `deref`.
byte_ptr_local := fn(cx : ptr(LCtx), s : usize, n : usize) -> bool {
  ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, s, n)))
  if streq(cx.src, ent.ns, ent.nl, s, n) == false { return false }
  if ent.ek != 0 { return false }
  mut p := ent.ns + ent.nl
  end := p + 512
  mut c := str_at((cx.src + p), 1)
  while p < end and (c == " " or c == "\n" or c == "\t" or c == "\r") { p = p + 1; c = str_at((cx.src + p), 1) }
  if str_at((cx.src + p), 2) != ":=" { return false }
  p = p + 2
  c = str_at((cx.src + p), 1)
  while p < end and (c == " " or c == "\n" or c == "\t" or c == "\r") { p = p + 1; c = str_at((cx.src + p), 1) }
  vs := p
  mut stop := false
  while p < end and stop == false {
    ch := str_at((cx.src + p), 1)
    if ch == "." { stop = true }
    else if ch == " " or ch == "\n" or ch == "\t" or ch == "\r" or ch == ";" or ch == "}" or ch == "(" or ch == "[" { return false }
    else { p = p + 1 }
  }
  if p == vs or stop == false { return false }
  if str_at((cx.src + p), 4) != ".ptr" { return false }
  ## the RHS must be EXACTLY `<ident>.ptr` — a longer chain (`a.ptr.b`, `a.ptr + k`) is not this shape.
  t := str_at((cx.src + p + 4), 1)
  if t != "\n" and t != ";" and t != " " and t != "}" { return false }
  vent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, vs, p - vs)))
  if streq(cx.src, vent.ns, vent.nl, vs, p - vs) == false { return false }
  if vent.ek == 4 { return true }
  bn := base_type_name(cx.src, vent.sns, vent.snl)
  if vent.ek == 2 and str_at((cx.src + bn.s), bn.n) == "Slice" {
    eta := typearg_at(cx.src, vent.sns, vent.snl, 0)
    if eta.n != 0 {
      etx := str_at((cx.src + eta.s), eta.n)
      if etx == "u8" or etx == "bits8" { return true }
    }
  }
  false
}

## Is the pointee of `p` a SIGNED sub-word integer (`i8`/`i16`/`i32`) — so a `deref` LOAD must
## SIGN-extend (`movsbq`/`movswq`/`movslq`) instead of zero-extend? Sees through `Unchecked`; reads
## the parser-preserved `Bitcast` pointee NAME span and tests its leading `i` (the parser records the
## span ONLY for a known sub-word pointee, so an `i`-prefixed name here is exactly i8/i16/i32).
## `u8`/`u16`/`u32`/`bool`/`char`/`f32`/word-sized/unknown → false (zero-extend / word `movq`).
## Isolated pointer-PARAM helper (the `match deref(p)` idiom), mirroring `deref_pointee_bytes`.
deref_pointee_signed := fn(p : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> bool {
  mut sg := false
  match deref(p) {
    Expr::Unchecked(inner) => { sg = deref_pointee_signed(inner, cx) }
    Expr::Bitcast(inner, ps, pl) => { sg = pl != 0 and str_at((cx.src + ps), 1) == "i" }
    Expr::Var(vs, vn) => {
      pty := local_ptr_pointee_span(cx, vs, vn)
      if pty.n != 0 { sg = str_at((cx.src + pty.s), 1) == "i" }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  sg
}

## Emit the `deref` LOAD move for a `w`-byte pointee (pointer already in %rax; result extended into
## %rax): a KNOWN sub-word width narrows — SIGN-extending for a signed pointee (`sg`,
## `movsbq`/`movswq`/`movslq`) else ZERO-extending (`movzbq`/`movzwq`/`movl`) — otherwise the full
## `movq`. A dedicated `in out sb` emit fn (the `emit_packed_load_rax` idiom) — an else-if chain
## INLINE in the huge emit arm mis-lowers (every branch fires), but a standalone emit fn lowers
## correctly; keep the branches FLAT `if`s.
emit_deref_load := fn(in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, w : usize, sg : bool) {
  if w == 1 and sg { push_str(sb, "  movsbq (%rax), %rax\n") }
  if w == 1 and not sg { push_str(sb, "  movzbq (%rax), %rax\n") }
  if w == 2 and sg { push_str(sb, "  movswq (%rax), %rax\n") }
  if w == 2 and not sg { push_str(sb, "  movzwq (%rax), %rax\n") }
  if w == 4 and sg { push_str(sb, "  movslq (%rax), %rax\n") }
  if w == 4 and not sg { push_str(sb, "  movl (%rax), %eax\n") }
  if w != 1 and w != 2 and w != 4 { push_str(sb, "  movq (%rax), %rax\n") }
}

## Emit the `deref` STORE move (%rbx through the %rax pointer), narrowed to a KNOWN sub-word width
## (`movb`/`movw`/`movl`) or the full `movq`. Standalone emit fn (see `emit_deref_load`).
emit_deref_store := fn(in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, w : usize) {
  if w == 1 { push_str(sb, "  movb %bl, (%rax)\n") }
  if w == 2 { push_str(sb, "  movw %bx, (%rax)\n") }
  if w == 4 { push_str(sb, "  movl %ebx, (%rax)\n") }
  if w != 1 and w != 2 and w != 4 { push_str(sb, "  movq %rbx, (%rax)\n") }
}

## The inner `Var` of an `AddrOf` (`ptr(x)`) expression, or 0/0 if `e` is not `ptr(<Var>)`. Used
## so a `p := ptr(s)` binding can record p as a pointer-to-struct when s is a struct local.
addr_var_span := fn(e : ptr(Expr)) -> CSpan {
  mut res := CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::AddrOf(inner) => { res = var_name_span(inner) }
    _ => {}
  }
  res
}

## If `e` is `ptr(<struct-local>)`, the pointee struct's TYPE span (so `p := ptr(s)` binds p as a
## pointer-to-struct, `ek = 7`); 0/0 otherwise.
addr_struct_span := fn(e : ptr(Expr), slots : ptr(SVec), src : ptr(u8)) -> CSpan {
  av := addr_var_span(e)
  if av.n == 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, slots, entry_of(slots, src, av.s, av.n)))
  if ent.ek != 2 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  CSpan(s = ent.sns, n = ent.snl)
}

## The inner `Var` of a `Deref` (`deref(p)`) expression, or 0/0 if `e` is not `deref(<Var>)`.
deref_var_span := fn(e : ptr(Expr)) -> CSpan {
  mut res := CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Deref(inner) => { res = var_name_span(inner) }
    _ => {}
  }
  res
}

## If `e` is `deref(<pointer-to-struct local>)` (a `p` bound as `ek = 7`), the pointee struct's
## TYPE span (so `s := deref(p)` binds s as that struct + copies the words); 0/0 otherwise.
deref_struct_span := fn(e : ptr(Expr), slots : ptr(SVec), src : ptr(u8)) -> CSpan {
  dv := deref_var_span(e)
  if dv.n == 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, slots, entry_of(slots, src, dv.s, dv.n)))
  if ent.ek != 7 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  CSpan(s = ent.sns, n = ent.snl)
}

## The inner expression of a `deref(<e>)` (0-pointer if `e` is not a `Deref`). Used to lower the
## pointer-producing call of a `deref(get(…))` read, AND to inspect it without a NESTED match
## (the seed compiler does not lower a variant `match` nested inside a `match` arm).
deref_inner_expr := fn(e : ptr(Expr)) -> ptr(Expr) {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Deref(inner) => { return inner }
    _ => { return unchecked bitcast(ptr(Expr), 0) }
  }
}

## The base-`Var` span + field-name span of `deref(<Var>.<field>)` (e.g. `ab := deref(v.arena)`).
## `ok` false unless `e` is exactly `Deref(Field(Var(o), f))`. Split out so each destructure is a
## single-level function-body match (the nested-match gotcha): `deref_inner_expr` peels the Deref,
## then one `Field` match, then `field_base_var` peels the base Var.
## The base VAR of a `Field`'s base — either a plain `Var(o)` (`o` a struct LOCAL, `via_ptr` false) or
## `Deref(Var(o))` (`o` a POINTER-to-struct param/local, `via_ptr` true — `deref(o).f`). Split into a
## TYPED-PARAM helper so the deref-match lowers (the nested-match gotcha).
FldBaseVP := struct { ok : bool, s : usize, n : usize, via_ptr : bool }
field_base_vp := fn(base : ptr(Expr)) -> FldBaseVP {
  mut res := FldBaseVP(ok = false, s = 0, n = 0, via_ptr = false)
  match deref(base) {
    Expr::Var(s, n) => { res = FldBaseVP(ok = true, s = s, n = n, via_ptr = false) }
    Expr::Deref(inner) => {
      vs := var_name_span(inner)
      if vs.n != 0 { res = FldBaseVP(ok = true, s = vs.s, n = vs.n, via_ptr = true) }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  res
}

DerefFieldParts := struct { ok : bool, os : usize, on : usize, fs : usize, fl : usize, via_ptr : bool }
## The `Var`+field parts of a `Field(Var(o), f)` node — taken as a TYPED PARAM `f : ptr(Expr)` so
## the `match deref(f)` lowers correctly. (Matching `deref(<a local bound from a call>)` mis-lowers:
## the lean lower discards the call-result's element type, so the deref reads a wrong-tagged value —
## the documented "read a field off deref(<call>) via a typed accessor / param, not inline" landmine.)
field_var_parts := fn(f : ptr(Expr)) -> DerefFieldParts {
  mut res := DerefFieldParts(ok = false, os = 0, on = 0, fs = 0, fl = 0, via_ptr = false)
  match deref(f) {
    Expr::Field(base, fs, fl) => {
      bv := field_base_vp(base)
      if bv.ok { res = DerefFieldParts(ok = true, os = bv.s, on = bv.n, fs = fs, fl = fl, via_ptr = bv.via_ptr) }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  res
}
deref_field_parts := fn(e : ptr(Expr)) -> DerefFieldParts {
  inner := deref_inner_expr(e)
  if unchecked bitcast(usize, inner) == 0 { return DerefFieldParts(ok = false, os = 0, on = 0, fs = 0, fl = 0, via_ptr = false) }
  return field_var_parts(inner)
}

## If `e` is `deref(<struct-local>.<field>)` where the field's declared type is `ptr(<opt mut> Struct)`,
## the POINTEE struct's TYPE span (so `ab := deref(v.arena)` binds `ab` as that struct + copies the
## words from the pointee, exactly like `deref(<call>)`); 0/0 otherwise. Unblocks the allocator-borne
## containers' `ab := deref(v.arena)` idiom (`vec_base`/`push`/`at`) — `deref_struct_span` handles only
## a pointer-typed LOCAL, not a pointer-typed struct FIELD.
deref_field_struct_span := fn(e : ptr(Expr), slots : ptr(SVec), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> CSpan {
  dfp := deref_field_parts(e)
  if dfp.ok == false { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, slots, entry_of(slots, src, dfp.os, dfp.on)))
  ## `deref(o.f)` — `o` a struct LOCAL (ek 2); or `deref(deref(o).f)` — `o` a POINTER-to-struct
  ## (ek 7, `via_ptr`), e.g. `vec_base`'s `deref(deref(v).arena)`. Either way `ent.sns` is the struct
  ## whose field `f` (a `ptr(<opt mut> Struct)`) is derefed; return that pointee's struct span.
  if dfp.via_ptr { if ent.ek != 7 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) } } else { if ent.ek != 2 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) } }
  fts := field_type_span(decls, src, ent.sns, ent.snl, dfp.fs, dfp.fl, a)
  ptr_pointee_struct_span(src, fts.s, fts.n, decls, a)
}

## The pointee struct span of a BARE `o.f` field read whose field `f` is a `ptr(<opt mut> Struct)` — so
## `n := node.next` (node a struct LOCAL/param, next a `ptr(Node)` field) binds `n` as a pointer-to-struct
## (ek 7) and a later `deref(n).field` resolves through it (linked-list / tree traversal via a struct ptr
## field). Without this `n` fell to the scalar binding and `deref(n)` had no pointee type → read `0`.
## `field_var_parts` peels `Field(Var(o), f)` (also `Field(Deref(Var(o)), f)` — `o` a ptr param, via_ptr).
field_ptrstruct_span := fn(e : ptr(Expr), slots : ptr(SVec), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> CSpan {
  fvp := field_var_parts(e)
  if fvp.ok == false { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, slots, entry_of(slots, src, fvp.os, fvp.on)))
  if fvp.via_ptr { if ent.ek != 7 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) } } else { if ent.ek != 2 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) } }
  fts := field_type_span(decls, src, ent.sns, ent.snl, fvp.fs, fvp.fl, a)
  ptr_pointee_struct_span(src, fts.s, fts.n, decls, a)
}

## The pointee struct span of a `deref(<struct>.<field>)` whose field is a `ptr(<opt mut> Struct)` — so a
## DIRECT `deref(o.p).f` field read (no intermediate `n := o.p` binding) resolves the pointee. Peels the
## Deref, then reuses `field_ptrstruct_span` on the inner `Field`. The read dual of `deref_field_struct_span`
## (which handles the `x := deref(o.p)` BINDING); unblocks the idiomatic linked-list / tree walk spelled
## inline (`deref(node.next).val`) without first binding the field to a local. 0/0 if `e` is not that shape.
deref_field_ptrstruct_span := fn(e : ptr(Expr), slots : ptr(SVec), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> CSpan {
  inner := deref_inner_expr(e)
  if unchecked bitcast(usize, inner) == 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  return field_ptrstruct_span(inner, slots, decls, src, a)
}

## The base `Var` span of an `Index(<Var>, idx)` (0/0 if the index base is not a plain `Var`). Isolated
## as a flat one-level match (the nested-match gotcha) so `deref_index_ptrstruct_span` can peel it.
index_var_span := fn(e : ptr(Expr)) -> CSpan {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Index(b, i) => { var_name_span(b) }
    _ => { CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  }
}

## If `e` is `deref(s[i])` where `s` is an ek-5 slice/array whose element is a POINTER-to-struct
## (`eek == 7` — bound by a `[ptr(mut b0), …]` literal), the POINTEE struct's TYPE span — so an inline
## `deref(s[i]).field` selects the field through the element pointer (loaded by the scalar `s[i]` read).
## 0/0 otherwise. The array/slice dual of `deref_struct_span` (a plain ek-7 `Var`) and
## `deref_field_ptrstruct_span` (a `ptr(Struct)` FIELD). Peels Deref → Index → the base Var's slot.
deref_index_ptrstruct_span := fn(e : ptr(Expr), slots : ptr(SVec), src : ptr(u8)) -> CSpan {
  inner := deref_inner_expr(e)
  if unchecked bitcast(usize, inner) == 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  ib := index_var_span(inner)
  if ib.n == 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, slots, entry_of(slots, src, ib.s, ib.n)))
  if streq(src, ent.ns, ent.nl, ib.s, ib.n) and ent.ek == 5 and ent.eek == 7 { return CSpan(s = ent.sns, n = ent.snl) }
  CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
}

## Fail-loud guard for the GENERIC form `deref(s[i]).field` where `s` is a `Slice(ptr(mut T))` PARAM:
## the pointee `T` is erased in the param's source text (`slice_param_elem_span` recovers only
## "ptr(mut T)") AND the param binds as a plain scalar-named slice (base name "Slice", ek 0) with no
## per-instance mono pointee binding — so the pointee struct span cannot be resolved WITHOUT unbounded
## new mono machinery. Returns true when the base is `Deref(Index(<Var>))` and the Var's DECLARED param
## type is `Slice(ptr(…))`. The caller PANICS on true rather than emit a wrong `pushq $0` (the cardinal
## rule: never a silent miscompile). Only consulted when `dix.n == 0`, so the concrete
## `[ptr(mut b0), …]` case (eek 7, a LOCAL with no annotation → `slice_param_elem_span` = 0/0) is handled
## upstream and never reaches here.
deref_index_ptr_param_unresolved := fn(e : ptr(Expr), slots : ptr(SVec), src : ptr(u8)) -> bool {
  inner := deref_inner_expr(e)
  if unchecked bitcast(usize, inner) == 0 { return false }
  ib := index_var_span(inner)
  if ib.n == 0 { return false }
  ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, slots, entry_of(slots, src, ib.s, ib.n)))
  if streq(src, ent.ns, ent.nl, ib.s, ib.n) == false { return false }
  eta := slice_param_elem_span(src, ent.ns, ent.nl)   ## element text of the declared `s : Slice(<…>)`
  if eta.n < 4 { return false }
  str_at((src + eta.s), 4) == "ptr("
}

## The pointee struct span of a BARE `s[i]` element read where `s` is an eek-7 pointer-element slice —
## so `p := s[i]` binds `p` as a pointer-to-struct (ek 7) and `deref(p).field` resolves through it.
## The Deref-free dual of `deref_index_ptrstruct_span` (used at the whole-element bound-local BIND site).
index_elem_ptrstruct_span := fn(e : ptr(Expr), slots : ptr(SVec), src : ptr(u8)) -> CSpan {
  ib := index_var_span(e)
  if ib.n == 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, slots, entry_of(slots, src, ib.s, ib.n)))
  if streq(src, ent.ns, ent.nl, ib.s, ib.n) and ent.ek == 5 and ent.eek == 7 { return CSpan(s = ent.sns, n = ent.snl) }
  CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
}

## ============ POINTER-TO-POINTER-to-struct (depth-2 nested deref) ============
## If `e` is `ptr(mut <x>)` where `x` is a POINTER-to-struct local/param (ek 7), the ULTIMATE pointee
## struct span (`x`'s pointee) — so `pp := ptr(mut p)` (p : ptr(mut Box)) records that `pp` is a
## `ptr(mut ptr(mut Box))` and `deref(deref(pp)).field` resolves the field. 0/0 unless `e` is `AddrOf`
## of an ek-7 var. A SCALAR/u64 double pointer (inner `x` is ek 0, not ek 7) yields 0/0 — unchanged.
addr_ptrptr_struct_span := fn(e : ptr(Expr), slots : ptr(SVec), src : ptr(u8)) -> CSpan {
  av := addr_var_span(e)
  if av.n == 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, slots, entry_of(slots, src, av.s, av.n)))
  if streq(src, ent.ns, ent.nl, av.s, av.n) and ent.ek == 7 { return CSpan(s = ent.sns, n = ent.snl) }
  CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
}

## `pp` is bound as a 1-word SCALAR (ek 0 — same store/read as any pointer word: it holds the address of
## the inner pointer's slot), MARKED `eek == 1` with the ULTIMATE pointee struct span in `sns`/`snl`. The
## marker is invisible to every existing reader (all `eek` reads are gated on `ek == 5`), so `pp` behaves
## as a plain scalar everywhere except the two depth-2 resolvers below. A distinct kind is deliberately
## NOT used: `pp` reads/writes exactly as a scalar pointer, and staying ek 0 means the SINGLE-deref
## resolvers (`deref_struct_span`, which gate on `ek == 7`) never misfire on it → `p2 := deref(pp)` is
## resolved solely by the dedicated branch below, not mistaken for a struct copy.
bind_ptrptrstruct_slot := fn(in out slots : SVec, src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize, ss : usize, sl : usize) {
  existing := slot_of(ptr(slots), src, s, n)
  if existing >= 0 { return }
  off := svec_len(ptr(slots))
  svec_push(slots, SlotEntry(ns = s, nl = n, off = off, sns = ss, snl = sl, ek = 0, estride = 1, eek = 1, is_ref = false))
}

## CLAYOUT S3(b) — `p := <call returning ptr(mut <§7 view>)>`. `p` is bound as a 1-word SCALAR
## (ek 0 — a pointer word reads and writes exactly like any other), MARKED `eek == 6` with the
## RESOLVED pointee VIEW type span in `sns`/`snl`. The marker follows the eek-1 precedent above: every
## existing `eek` reader is gated on `ek == 5`, so `p` behaves as a plain scalar everywhere except
## `slot_ptr_pointee_span`, which hands the span to the `deref(p)` read and the `deref(p) = v` store so
## both move the TWO words a view is. Without it a `slot := dq_elem(T, ptr(d), i)` element pointer at
## `T = str` stored (and read back) ONE word — a silent wrong value (I11).
bind_ptrview_slot := fn(in out slots : SVec, src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize, ss : usize, sl : usize) {
  existing := slot_of(ptr(slots), src, s, n)
  if existing >= 0 { return }
  off := svec_len(ptr(slots))
  svec_push(slots, SlotEntry(ns = s, nl = n, off = off, sns = ss, snl = sl, ek = 0, estride = 1, eek = 6, is_ref = false, tmod_s = 0, tmod_l = 0))
}

## The pointee TYPE span of an arbitrary POINTER EXPRESSION, at slot-collection time (the `collect_slots`
## dual of `deref_dest_pointee_span`, which needs a full `LCtx`). A `Var` resolves through its slot (the
## eek-6 view marker, an annotation, or the preserved `bitcast(ptr(T), …)` source) and is then carried
## through the enclosing instance substitution; anything else is tried as a CALL whose declared return
## type is `ptr(<callee type-parameter>)`, resolved by POSITION. {0,0} when unresolvable.
ptr_expr_pointee_span := fn(pe : ptr(Expr), slots : ptr(SVec), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena, sub : ptr(Subst)) -> CSpan {
  vn := var_name_span(pe)
  if vn.n == 0 {
    return call_ret_pointee_span(pe, decls, src, a, sub.gps, sub.gpl, sub.its, sub.itl, sub.gps2, sub.gpl2, sub.its2, sub.itl2, sub.gps3, sub.gpl3, sub.its3, sub.itl3)
  }
  pt := slot_ptr_pointee_span(slots, src, vn.s, vn.n)
  if pt.n == 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  if sub.gpl != 0 and streq(src, pt.s, pt.n, sub.gps, sub.gpl) { return CSpan(s = sub.its, n = sub.itl) }
  if sub.gpl2 != 0 and streq(src, pt.s, pt.n, sub.gps2, sub.gpl2) { return CSpan(s = sub.its2, n = sub.itl2) }
  if sub.gpl3 != 0 and streq(src, pt.s, pt.n, sub.gps3, sub.gpl3) { return CSpan(s = sub.its3, n = sub.itl3) }
  return pt
}

## I11 loud boundary — `deref(f(…))` (or `deref(f(…)) = v`) where `f` is declared `-> ptr(<its own
## type PARAMETER>)` and the call does NOT name that type argument at its position. The pointee is
## then unknowable at the use site: a scalar pointee reads one word, a §7 VIEW pointee reads two, and
## picking either silently produces a wrong value for the other. Reject instead (a trap is acceptable,
## a wrong value never is) — the workaround is to pass the type argument explicitly.
deref_call_pointee_unresolved := fn(v : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> bool {
  inner := deref_inner_expr(v)
  if unchecked bitcast(usize, inner) == 0 { return false }
  call_ret_pointee_unresolved(inner, decls, src, a)
}

## The LOCATED half of that boundary: write the offending source line to stderr (the lean lower's only
## diagnostic channel, `lower_show_src_line`), then reject. `ce` is the CALL whose returned pointee
## could not be resolved.
reject_call_pointee_unresolved := fn(ce : ptr(Expr), src : ptr(u8)) {
  ci := call_callee_span(ce)
  if ci.n != 0 { lower_show_src_line(src, ci.s) }
  panic("selfhost: this call returns `ptr(<type parameter>)` and does not name that type argument at its position (the source line above), so the pointee cannot be resolved — a scalar pointee is ONE word and a `str`/`[T]` view pointee is TWO, and reading/storing the wrong width is a silent wrong value. Pass the type argument explicitly (`f(str, ...)`).")
}

## `deref(<call>)` whose callee's returned pointee is unresolvable — the located reject, given the
## DEREF expression (the call inside it is what gets pointed at).
reject_deref_call_pointee := fn(v : ptr(Expr), src : ptr(u8)) {
  inner := deref_inner_expr(v)
  if unchecked bitcast(usize, inner) == 0 { return }
  reject_call_pointee_unresolved(inner, src)
}

## `p := <call returning `ptr(<opt mut> <§7 view>)`>` — the view TYPE span the pointer points at, or
## {0,0}. Gates the eek-6 pointer-to-view binding (`bind_ptrview_slot`).
call_view_pointee_bind := fn(v : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena, sub : ptr(Subst)) -> CSpan {
  pt := call_ret_pointee_span(v, decls, src, a, sub.gps, sub.gpl, sub.its, sub.itl, sub.gps2, sub.gpl2, sub.its2, sub.itl2, sub.gps3, sub.gpl3, sub.its3, sub.itl3)
  if pt.n == 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  if is_view_type(src, pt.s, pt.n) == false { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  pt
}

## `x := deref(<pointer to a §7 VIEW>)` — the view TYPE span, or {0,0} when `x` is not that. Drives the
## `bind_str_slot` binding of a container's element read (`v := deref(val_at(K, V, m, a, i))` at
## `V = str`), which before this bound ONE scalar word and read a garbage length.
deref_view_pointee_span := fn(v : ptr(Expr), slots : ptr(SVec), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena, sub : ptr(Subst)) -> CSpan {
  inner := deref_inner_expr(v)
  if unchecked bitcast(usize, inner) == 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  pt := ptr_expr_pointee_span(inner, slots, decls, src, a, sub)
  if pt.n == 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  if is_view_type(src, pt.s, pt.n) == false { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  pt
}

## If `e` is `deref(deref(pp))` where `pp` is the pointer-to-pointer-to-struct scalar (eek-1 marker), the
## ULTIMATE pointee struct span — so an inline `deref(deref(pp)).field` READ/WRITE selects the field. The
## VALUE `deref(deref(pp))`'s pointer (`&Box`) is produced by the ordinary nested-`Deref` value path
## (`emit_gas(deref_inner_expr(e))`); this only supplies the pointee TYPE. Depth-2 only — a deeper nest
## (`deref(deref(deref(x)))`) is not resolved (0/0 → falls to the fail-loud / pushq $0 default).
deref_deref_struct_span := fn(e : ptr(Expr), slots : ptr(SVec), src : ptr(u8)) -> CSpan {
  inner := deref_inner_expr(e)                 ## e == Deref(<inner>); inner should be Deref(Var pp)
  if unchecked bitcast(usize, inner) == 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  iv := deref_var_span(inner)                  ## inner == Deref(Var pp) → pp span
  if iv.n == 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, slots, entry_of(slots, src, iv.s, iv.n)))
  if streq(src, ent.ns, ent.nl, iv.s, iv.n) and ent.ek == 0 and ent.eek == 1 and ent.snl != 0 { return CSpan(s = ent.sns, n = ent.snl) }
  CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
}

## If `e` is `deref(pp)` where `pp` is the pointer-to-pointer-to-struct scalar (eek-1 marker), the pointee
## `ptr(mut Struct)`'s STRUCT span — so a bound-local `p2 := deref(pp)` is inferred as an ek-7
## pointer-to-struct (and `deref(p2).field` then resolves). 0/0 otherwise.
deref_ptrptr_pointee_span := fn(e : ptr(Expr), slots : ptr(SVec), src : ptr(u8)) -> CSpan {
  iv := deref_var_span(e)                      ## e == Deref(Var pp) → pp span
  if iv.n == 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, slots, entry_of(slots, src, iv.s, iv.n)))
  if streq(src, ent.ns, ent.nl, iv.s, iv.n) and ent.ek == 0 and ent.eek == 1 and ent.snl != 0 { return CSpan(s = ent.sns, n = ent.snl) }
  CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
}

## NOTE: there is deliberately NO fail-loud for the GENERIC bound-local `p := s[i]` over a
## `Slice(ptr(mut T))` PARAM. Binding `p` as a plain SCALAR (the raw pointer word) is the CORRECT and
## USED representation for the idiomatic pass-the-element-pointer-ACROSS-A-CALL pattern (`chp := chans[i];
## try_recv(T, chp)` in `lib/std/channel.al`'s `select_recv` — a passing e2e). Only a subsequent INLINE
## `deref(p).field` would read 0, and at that scalar-deref site `p` is indistinguishable from any other
## untyped scalar (pre-existing `pushq $0` behaviour) — so a bind-site fail-loud would wrongly reject the
## working call idiom. The CONCRETE bound-local IS inferred (`index_elem_ptrstruct_span`); the generic
## inline-deref is covered at the DIRECT `deref(s[i]).field` read/write sites instead.

## The pointee struct span of a plain `Var` that already binds a POINTER-to-struct (ek 7 — a `ptr(Struct)`
## param or local). Lets `cur := head` (head a `ptr(Node)` param) copy the ek-7 pointee so `deref(cur).field`
## resolves — the walk-cursor of a linked-list/tree traversal. 0/0 if the Var is not an ek-7 slot.
var_ptrstruct_span := fn(e : ptr(Expr), slots : ptr(SVec), src : ptr(u8)) -> CSpan {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Var(s, n) => {
      ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, slots, entry_of(slots, src, s, n)))
      if streq(src, ent.ns, ent.nl, s, n) and ent.ek == 7 { return CSpan(s = ent.sns, n = ent.snl) }
      CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
    }
    _ => { CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  }
}

## A typed pointer to the `T`-record at arena address `h` (the decls/AST handle = the down-growing
## top-of-block address). `T : type` arg0 lets `call_first_struct_span` recognize `deref(decl_at(T,h))`
## as a struct read (the `get(T,…)` shape), so the read deref-COPIES the record into a local.
decl_at := fn(T : type, h : usize) -> ptr(T) { return unchecked bitcast(ptr(T), h) }
## a direct typed accessor for decl `i` (encapsulates the usize-handle recovery).
decl_get := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), i : usize) -> ptr(Decl) { hh := rt::vec_get(deref(decls), i) ; return decl_at(Decl, hh) }
## A typed pointer to the AST node at arena OFFSET `h` — the lean replacement for `get(T, a,
## Handle(T)(idx=h))` (the self-host lower cannot compile the generic `get`/`Handle`/`scoped`
## allocator surface, only this `bitcast` accessor over the arena base; mirrors `parser::node_ptr`).
## 8-aligned bump over the arena (returns the new region's OFFSET) — the lean replacement for the
## generic `allocate`, used by `SVec`/`IVec` (the SlotEntry/Inst vectors). Mirrors `parser::node_alloc`.
node_alloc := fn(in out a : rt::Arena, sz : usize) -> usize {
  rem := a.off % 8
  mut aligned := a.off
  if rem != 0 { aligned = a.off + (8 - rem) }
  if aligned + sz > a.cap { panic("lower: out of memory") }
  a.off = aligned + sz
  return aligned
}

## Allocate + initialize one AST node from a VALUE (mirroring `parser::newnode`/`gnode`): the value
## is stored as a PARAM, which the Rust seed lowers as a struct/enum copy through the node pointer —
## an INLINE `deref(node_ptr(…)) = Expr.X(…)` is "storing a non-scalar through a pointer", unlowered.
## Used by the UFCS desugar (build a receiver `Var` + a `Call` node at emit time).
mk_expr := fn(in out a : rt::Arena, val : Expr) -> usize {
  idx := node_alloc(a, 64)
  deref(node_ptr(Expr, a, idx)) = val
  idx
}
mk_arg := fn(in out a : rt::Arena, val : Arg) -> ptr(mut Arg) {
  idx := node_alloc(a, 64)
  p := node_ptr(Arg, a, idx)
  deref(p) = val
  p
}
mk_arm := fn(in out a : rt::Arena, val : Arm) -> ptr(mut Arm) {
  idx := node_alloc(a, 96)
  p := node_ptr(Arm, a, idx)
  deref(p) = val
  p
}
## Overwrite an EXISTING node in place from a VALUE param (same seed-lowered param→pointer copy as
## `mk_arg`/`mk_expr`; an inline `deref(node_ptr(…)) = Expr.X(…)` at the store site is unlowered). Used
## by the §5.1 default filler to relink an arg tail (`set_arg`) and rewrite a `Call`'s count/head
## (`set_expr`).
set_arg := fn(in out a : rt::Arena, h : usize, val : Arg) {
  deref(arg_p(h)) = val
}
set_expr := fn(e : ptr(Expr), val : Expr) {
  deref(unchecked bitcast(ptr(mut Expr), e)) = val
}

## If `e` is a `Call` whose FIRST argument is a type naming a struct, that struct's TYPE span;
## 0/0 otherwise. A single flat match (kept out of an enclosing match arm by the composition below).
call_first_struct_span := fn(e : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> CSpan {
  mut res := CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, args_head) => {
      if nargs >= 1 {
        a0 := arg_expr_at(args_head, 0, a)
        tn := var_name_span(a0)
        if tn.n != 0 and struct_decl_of(decls, src, tn.s, tn.n) >= 0 {
          res = CSpan(s = tn.s, n = tn.n)
        }
      }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  res
}

## If `e` is `deref(<call>(T, …))` whose first argument is a struct type — the arena read shape
## `deref(get(T, arena, handle))` the passes use — return that struct's TYPE span; else 0/0.
## Composed from `deref_inner_expr` + `call_first_struct_span` (two flat matches, no nesting).
deref_call_struct_span := fn(e : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> CSpan {
  inner := deref_inner_expr(e)
  if unchecked bitcast(usize, inner) == 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  fs := call_first_struct_span(inner, decls, src, a)
  ## FALLBACK (§4): the first-arg heuristic (node_ptr/decl_at/get(S, …) convention) doesn't fire
  ## for a plain user fn `get(ptr(b)) -> ptr(Struct)`; resolve the pointee from the callee's declared
  ## RETURN type instead, so `deref(get(…)).f` reads the field inline (and `s := deref(get(…))` copies).
  if fs.n != 0 { return fs }
  call_ret_ptrstruct_ret_span(inner, decls, src, a)
}

## The ENUM dual of `call_first_struct_span`: if `e` is a `Call` whose first argument is an ENUM
## type name (`node_ptr(Stmt, …)`), return that enum's TYPE span; else 0/0.
call_first_enum_span := fn(e : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> CSpan {
  mut res := CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, args_head) => {
      if nargs >= 1 {
        a0 := arg_expr_at(args_head, 0, a)
        tn := var_name_span(a0)
        if tn.n != 0 and enum_decl_of(decls, src, tn.s, tn.n) >= 0 {
          res = CSpan(s = tn.s, n = tn.n)
        }
      }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  res
}

## The ENUM dual of `deref_call_struct_span`: if `e` is `deref(<call>(E, …))` whose first argument
## is an ENUM type — the arena read shape `deref(stmt_p(Stmt, handle))` the lower's own
## `match st` walk uses — return that enum's TYPE span; else 0/0. Without this a `st := deref(
## stmt_p(Stmt, s))` binding sized `st` as a SCALAR, so `match st` was not recognized as an
## enum match (scrut_enum_info needs `ek == 3`) and its arm payload variables collided with the
## function's own locals (the TOOL-1 Stage2 emit_rodata_stmts `s = nx` self-assign hang).
deref_call_enum_span := fn(e : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> CSpan {
  inner := deref_inner_expr(e)
  if unchecked bitcast(usize, inner) == 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  fe := call_first_enum_span(inner, decls, src, a)
  if fe.n != 0 { return fe }
  ## FALLBACK (§6 ptr-typing): the first-arg-type-name convention (node_ptr(E, …)) didn't fire; resolve
  ## the pointee ENUM from the callee's declared RETURN type instead, so `st := deref(stmt_p(Stmt, h))` (an
  ## identity accessor `fn(p : ptr(mut E)) -> ptr(mut E)`) still binds `st` as an enum local. Mirrors
  ## `deref_call_struct_span`'s struct return-type fallback.
  call_ret_ptrenum_ret_span(inner, decls, src, a)
}
call_ret_enum_span := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> CSpan {
  mut res := CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, args_head) => {
      ## Resolve the callee EXACTLY like the `enum_ret_call` gate (both via `ret_call_target`, which does
      ## the mod/arity/ntpc tiers + the RECEIVER-KEYED redirect) so a direct `match r.map(step)` reads the
      ## RIGHT same-name overload's return enum (`Result`, not the last-declared `Option`/`Vec`). Was a
      ## last-declared-name scan that disagreed with the gate for an implicit-UFCS multi-type-param method.
      ci := ret_call_target(cx.decls, cx.src, cs, cl, nargs, args_head, deref(cx.mar))
      if ci >= 0 {
        d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(cx.decls), usize(ci))))
        res = CSpan(s = d.ret_ts, n = d.ret_tl)
      } else {
        ## FN-6/FN-10 INDIRECT: the enum type of a call through a fn VALUE / a fn-value struct FIELD
        ## (`enum_ret_call`'s span dual — the same two resolvers, in the same order).
        if fnfield_call_ret_enum(e, cx) { res = fnfield_call_ret_span(e, cx) }
        else if ind_call_ret_enum(e, cx.decls, cx.src, deref(cx.mar)) { res = ind_call_ret_span(e, cx.decls, cx.src, deref(cx.mar)) }
      }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  res
}

## The resolved declaration index for a DIRECT enum-returning call, or -1 for an indirect call / a
## non-call. The return TYPE span itself is not enough to resolve generic payload arguments: a callee
## declared in `wide` may spell `Result(u64, Error)`, and that bare `Error` is nominally `wide::Error`
## even when the call site is the anonymous root. Keep the declaration identity beside the existing
## span path so the direct-match staging can publish the callee's naming module while it sizes and
## dispatches the returned enum. Indirect calls have no declaration module to carry here and retain
## their existing behavior (located reject remains preferable to guessing).
call_ret_enum_target := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> i64 {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, args_head) => { ret_call_target(cx.decls, cx.src, cs, cl, nargs, args_head, deref(cx.mar)) }
    _ => { 0 - 1 }
  }
}
## FAIL-LOUD GUARD for a DIRECT `match <call>` scrutinee (`match recv.map(f) { … }`) whose generic
## return enum's payload resolves to a MULTI-WORD type. The direct call-scrutinee staging sizes the
## scratch from the RAW (param-generic) return span — payload counted as 1 word via `enum_max_arity` —
## so a multi-word payload (a struct produced by the mapper, or a multi-word receiver payload) is
## TRUNCATED to its word 0: a silent miscompile. The BOUND form (`m := recv.map(f); match m { … }`)
## records the fully-SUBSTITUTED span (`collect_slots` → `subst_enum_ret_span`) and works correctly, so
## reject the direct form with that workaround rather than emit the truncating read. NEUTRAL: a
## CONCRETE-return call (every src/lib direct-`match`) carries no return type-parameter, so
## `subst_enum_ret_span` returns 0/0 → the guard never fires; a scalar/word payload (`.map(step)`, T1-T3)
## has substituted size == raw size → also never fires.
direct_match_multiword_reject := fn(scrut : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) {
  raw := call_ret_enum_span(scrut, cx)
  if raw.n == 0 { return }
  sub := subst_enum_ret_span(scrut, cx.decls, cx.src, deref(cx.mar), cx.mar, cx.gp_s, cx.gp_l, cx.it_s, cx.it_l, cx.gp2_s, cx.gp2_l, cx.it2_s, cx.it2_l)
  if sub.n == 0 { return }
  rw := enum_inst_words(cx.decls, cx.src, raw.s, raw.n, deref(cx.mar))
  sw := enum_inst_words(cx.decls, cx.src, sub.s, sub.n, deref(cx.mar))
  if sw > rw { panic("selfhost: a direct `match recv.map(f)` / `.and_then(f)` / `.map_err(g)` whose payload is a MULTI-WORD type truncates the scrutinee to its first word — bind the result to a local first: `m := recv.map(f); match m { … }`") }
}

## Is the fn decl whose name is `[name_s, name_s+name_l)` marked `@convert` (Types §4.6 / TYP-6)? The
## marker sits in VALUE position after `:=` (`to_char := @convert fn(…)`) and the parser DISCARDS it
## (parser.al ~3070), so recover it by a FORWARD source scan — past the name, skip whitespace, expect
## `:=`, skip whitespace, check for `@convert`. Mirrors `fn_is_inline` (which scans BACKWARD for a decl
## prefix); here the marker is a value-position effector, so the scan runs forward. Only called on real
## fn decls (a body always follows), so reading a few bytes past `:=` is in-bounds.
fn_is_convert := fn(src : ptr(u8), name_s : usize, name_l : usize) -> bool {
  mut p := name_s + name_l
  mut sc := true
  while sc {
    c := str_at((src + p), 1)
    if c == " " or c == "\n" or c == "\t" or c == "\r" { p = p + 1 } else { sc = false }
  }
  if str_at((src + p), 2) != ":=" { return false }
  p = p + 2
  mut s2 := true
  while s2 {
    c := str_at((src + p), 1)
    if c == " " or c == "\n" or c == "\t" or c == "\r" { p = p + 1 } else { s2 = false }
  }
  str_at((src + p), 8) == "@convert"
}
## The decl index of the SINGLE in-scope `@convert` fn whose RETURN type's base name == the callee
## `[cs, cl)` (Types §4.6 dispatch step 2 / TYP-6), else -1. Builtin lattice conversions (`conv_kind >=
## 0`) and BRAND constructions (`brand_underlying`) keep PRIORITY — a `T(v)` that rides either is
## short-circuited here, so it never resolves to a @convert (this is what makes `char(n)`, a brand,
## stay the brand path → fixpoint-neutral: src/lib's only @convert `to_char` targets the brand `char`).
## Two @convert to the same target are an AMBIGUITY error (fail-loud, no guessing).
## The §3 scope rank of a `@convert` declaration's module as seen from the module being lowered. A
## declaration in NO module (a single-file program, `mod_len == 0`) and a compile with no published
## module both rank 0, so every candidate ties there and the historical flat-ambiguity behaviour is
## preserved verbatim — that is the shape `src/`+`lib/` compile in, hence fixpoint-neutral.
convert_scope_rank := fn(src : ptr(u8), as_ : usize, al : usize) -> i64 {
  if lower_layout::type_ref_mod_on() == false { return 0 }
  ml := lower_layout::type_ref_mod_l()
  if ml == 0 { return 0 }
  callee_mod_rank(src, as_, al, lower_layout::type_ref_mod_s(), ml)
}

convert_callee_idx := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), cs : usize, cl : usize) -> i64 {
  if conv_kind(str_at((src + cs), cl)) >= 0 { return 0 - 1 }
  bu := brand_underlying(decls, src, cs, cl)
  if bu.n != 0 { return 0 - 1 }
  cnt := rt::vec_len(deref(decls))
  mut found := 0 - 1
  ## Modules §3 (TYPE-ANCESTOR): a `@convert` is a FUNCTION declaration, so which ones are IN SCOPE
  ## at this construction is the same question a bare call asks — `callee_mod_rank` answers it (this
  ## module, then its ancestors nearest-first, -1 for a sibling / a descendant / an unrelated module).
  ## Without the rank, two `@convert` to the same target in unrelated modules were a flat AMBIGUITY
  ## even when §3 lets the naming module see exactly ONE of them (measured: a child constructing
  ## through its ancestor's `@convert` was rejected as ambiguous because a sibling declared one too).
  ## The fail-loud is PRESERVED for a genuine tie — two candidates at the SAME best rank.
  mut bestr := 0 - 2                     ## below EVERY rank, including the -1 "not on the chain"
  mut j := 0
  je := dcv_hi(cnt)
  while j < je {
    i := dcv_at(cnt, j)
    d := deref(decl_get(decls, i))
    mut isc := false
    if dic_live(cnt) { isc = dic_is_convert(cnt, i) } else { if d.is_fn and fn_is_convert(src, d.name_start, d.name_len) { isc = true } }
    if isc {
      rb := base_type_name(src, d.ret_ts, d.ret_tl)
      if rb.n != 0 and streq(src, rb.s, rb.n, cs, cl) {
        r := convert_scope_rank(src, d.mod_start, d.mod_len)
        if found < 0 or r > bestr { bestr = r; found = i64(i) }
        else if r == bestr { panic("selfhost: ambiguous @convert to target type (TYP-6)") }
      }
    }
    j += 1
  }
  found
}

## Like `convert_callee_idx` but WITHOUT the builtin-conv-name bail — the in-scope `@convert fn(U) -> T`
## whose RETURN type is `T` (which MAY be a builtin scalar name, e.g. `@convert fn(c : Celsius) -> u64`).
## Used ONLY when the conversion operand is a user AGGREGATE (struct/enum), where `u64(structval)` is a
## legitimate user conversion rather than the scalar-lattice identity. The brand bail stays (a brand
## `Id(v)` reinterprets, never dispatches). No self-host `@convert` returns a builtin name → neutral.
convert_callee_idx_incl_builtin := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), cs : usize, cl : usize) -> i64 {
  bu := brand_underlying(decls, src, cs, cl)
  if bu.n != 0 { return 0 - 1 }
  cnt := rt::vec_len(deref(decls))
  mut found := 0 - 1
  ## Modules §3 (TYPE-ANCESTOR): a `@convert` is a FUNCTION declaration, so which ones are IN SCOPE
  ## at this construction is the same question a bare call asks — `callee_mod_rank` answers it (this
  ## module, then its ancestors nearest-first, -1 for a sibling / a descendant / an unrelated module).
  ## Without the rank, two `@convert` to the same target in unrelated modules were a flat AMBIGUITY
  ## even when §3 lets the naming module see exactly ONE of them (measured: a child constructing
  ## through its ancestor's `@convert` was rejected as ambiguous because a sibling declared one too).
  ## The fail-loud is PRESERVED for a genuine tie — two candidates at the SAME best rank.
  mut bestr := 0 - 2                     ## below EVERY rank, including the -1 "not on the chain"
  mut j := 0
  je := dcv_hi(cnt)
  while j < je {
    i := dcv_at(cnt, j)
    d := deref(decl_get(decls, i))
    mut isc := false
    if dic_live(cnt) { isc = dic_is_convert(cnt, i) } else { if d.is_fn and fn_is_convert(src, d.name_start, d.name_len) { isc = true } }
    if isc {
      rb := base_type_name(src, d.ret_ts, d.ret_tl)
      if rb.n != 0 and streq(src, rb.s, rb.n, cs, cl) {
        r := convert_scope_rank(src, d.mod_start, d.mod_len)
        if found < 0 or r > bestr { bestr = r; found = i64(i) }
        else if r == bestr { panic("selfhost: ambiguous @convert to target type (TYP-6)") }
      }
    }
    j += 1
  }
  found
}

## The kind-1 decl index a `Call`'s tail name resolves to, PREFERRING a param arity matching the
## call's `nargs` (else the last same-name decl) — so the `*_ret_call` classifiers below pick the
## SAME overload the call dispatches to (`option::map`/4 vs `result::map`/5 vs `vec::map`/5), rather
## than classifying ANY same-name decl (which let `struct_ret_call` fire on `vec::map` and mis-size an
## `Option`-returning `map`). Falls back to last-match when no arity matches (implicit-type-arg calls /
## single-arity names) so `src/`'s per-module same-name generics stay byte-identical.
ret_call_target := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), cs : usize, cl : usize, nargs : usize, args_head : ptr(mut Arg), a : rt::Arena) -> i64 {
  cnt := rt::vec_len(deref(decls))
  nm := callee_name_span(src, cs, cl)
  ## Resolve to the SAME decl `generic_decl_of` dispatches to, so the result-binding CLASSIFIER (enum
  ## vs struct vs str) agrees with the emitted call. Priority: a QUALIFIED call's MODULE + arity match
  ## (`base::result::map`); then a leading-type-arg-COUNT match (`map(u64,u64,u64,…)` → `result::map`/3
  ## over the same-arity `vec::map`/2); then arity; then last-match.
  cpq := colon_pos(src, cs, cl)
  mut segcmp := false
  mut pms := 0
  mut pml := 0
  if cpq >= 0 { pms = cs; pml = usize(cpq); segcmp = true }
  ltac := leading_type_arg_count(args_head, decls, src, a)
  mut res := 0 - 1
  mut res_arity := 0 - 1
  mut res_ntpc := 0 - 1
  mut res_mod := 0 - 1
  th := name_hash(src, nm.s, nm.n)
  mut j := dni_lo(cnt, th)
  je := dni_hi(cnt, th)
  while j < je {
    i := dni_at(cnt, j)
    if DNH == 0 or DNH_N != cnt or rt::rec_get(unchecked bitcast(ptr(mut u8), DNH), i) == th {
    d := deref(decl_get(decls, i))
    if d.kind == 1 and streq(src, d.name_start, d.name_len, nm.s, nm.n) {
      res = i64(i)
      if d.arity == nargs {
        res_arity = i64(i)
        if ltac != 0 and d.is_generic and tparam_count(decls, i64(i), src, a) == ltac { res_ntpc = i64(i) }
        if segcmp and mod_head_matches(src, d.mod_start, d.mod_len, pms, pml) { res_mod = i64(i) }
      }
    }
    }
    j += 1
  }
  mut fin := 0 - 1
  if res_mod >= 0 { fin = res_mod }
  else if res_ntpc >= 0 { fin = res_ntpc }
  else if res_arity >= 0 { fin = res_arity }
  else { fin = res }
  ## RECEIVER-KEYED redirect (mirror the value-position emit path's `recv_redirect_generic`): a UFCS
  ## call whose name resolved to a candidate whose `self` base does NOT match the receiver — but a
  ## same-name candidate's DOES — is re-selected, so the result-binding CLASSIFIER (enum/struct/str)
  ## agrees with the emitted call. This is the ONLY way an IMPLICIT-UFCS multi-type-param method
  ## (`r.map(f)`, no arity match → `res` = last-declared, often the WRONG same-name overload) reaches
  ## its true decl. NEUTRAL: `recv_redirect_generic` returns its input unchanged whenever the current
  ## pick's `self` already matches the receiver (every existing src/ UFCS call), and a 0/0 receiver
  ## (unresolvable Var) skips the redirect entirely.
  if fin >= 0 and nargs >= 1 {
    rcv := recv_full_emit(arg_expr_at(args_head, 0, a), decls, src, a)
    if rcv.n != 0 {
      rb := base_type_name(src, rcv.s, rcv.n)
      fin = recv_redirect_generic(decls, src, fin, nargs, rb.s, rb.n, a)
    }
  }
  if fin >= 0 { return fin }
  ## No kind-1 fn matches the callee name — try a user CONVERSION-CONSTRUCTOR `T(v)` (Types §4.6 /
  ## TYP-6): the callee is a TYPE name `T` with an in-scope `@convert fn(…) -> T`. Resolving to that
  ## fn's decl lets the `*_ret_call` classifiers (struct/enum/str/tuple) + `call_ret_*_span` size the
  ## `c := T(v)` binding by the @convert's RETURN type. Fires only when no same-name fn exists AND a
  ## @convert matches — src/lib's `char(n)` (a brand) is filtered inside `convert_callee_idx` → neutral.
  if res < 0 {
    cvi := convert_callee_idx(decls, src, cs, cl)
    if cvi >= 0 { return cvi }
  }
  res
}

## Is `e` a `Call` to a fn whose declared return type is an enum? The deref-match stays here
## (a direct PARAM); resolves the callee name to its kind-1 `Decl` and checks `fn_returns_enum`.
enum_ret_call := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> bool {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, args_head) => {
      ci := ret_call_target(cx.decls, cx.src, cs, cl, nargs, args_head, deref(cx.mar))
      ## FN-6 INDIRECT: no named fn resolves → it may be a call through a fn VALUE whose declared/
      ## bound return type is an enum. Classify it so `match f(…)` stages disc/%rax + payload/%rdx
      ## instead of dispatching on the raw pushed word (a silent wrong arm + garbage payload).
      if ci < 0 {
        if fnfield_call_ret_enum(e, cx) { return true }
        return ind_call_ret_enum(e, cx.decls, cx.src, deref(cx.mar))
      }
      d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(cx.decls), usize(ci))))
      fn_returns_enum(d, cx.decls, cx.src)
    }
    _ => { false }
  }
}

## `decls`/`src` versions of the enum-returning-call predicate + its enum-type span — so
## `collect_slots` (no `cx`) can reserve `r := enum_call()` as an enum and `emit_stmts` can
## receive the result (disc/%rax, payload/%rdx) into its slots. The passes use this pervasively
## (`pr := parse_program(…)` then `match pr { … }`).
enum_ret_call_d := fn(e : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> bool {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, args_head) => {
      ci := ret_call_target(decls, src, cs, cl, nargs, args_head, a)
      if ci < 0 { return ind_call_ret_enum(e, decls, src, a) }   ## FN-6 INDIRECT — see `enum_ret_call`
      d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(ci))))
      fn_returns_enum(d, decls, src)
    }
    _ => { false }
  }
}
call_ret_enum_span_d := fn(e : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> CSpan {
  mut res := CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, args_head) => {
      ci := ret_call_target(decls, src, cs, cl, nargs, args_head, a)
      if ci >= 0 {
        d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(ci))))
        res = CSpan(s = d.ret_ts, n = d.ret_tl)
      } else {
        ## FN-6 INDIRECT — see `enum_ret_call` (the fn-VALUE return class).
        if ind_call_ret_enum(e, decls, src, a) { res = ind_call_ret_span(e, decls, src, a) }
      }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  res
}

## Is `e` a `Call` to a fn that returns a 2-WORD STRUCT? (the caller receives it from %rax/%rdx
## into the binding's two slots). The struct dual of `enum_ret_call`. Takes `decls`/`src`
## directly (not `cx`) so both `collect_slots` (slot reservation) and `emit_stmts` can call it.
struct_ret_call := fn(e : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> bool {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, args_head) => {
      ci := ret_call_target(decls, src, cs, cl, nargs, args_head, a)
      if ci < 0 {
        ## FN-6 INDIRECT: a call through a fn VALUE returning a 1..7-word struct — the register-return
        ## convention, exactly as the direct call it forwards to (`ind_call_ret_span`).
        iw := ind_call_ret_struct_words(e, decls, src, a)
        return iw >= 1 and iw <= 7
      }
      d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(ci))))
      fn_returns_struct(d, decls, src, a)
    }
    _ => { false }
  }
}

## Is `e` a `Call` to a fn whose declared return type is a SLICE `Slice(T)` (spec §7.2 / Types §9.4)?
## A slice is returned BY VALUE as a 2-word {ptr, len} aggregate (ptr/%rax, len/%rdx), so `r := f(…)`
## must bind `r` as an ek-5 TYPED SLICE (runtime-len view) so `r.len`, `r[i]` and `for x in r` read it
## as a slice — NOT as a bare scalar (which dropped `.ptr`/`.len`, the silent-0 miscompile). The parser
## records ONLY the return-type HEAD (`Slice`) in `ret_ts`/`ret_tl`; the `(elem)` args follow in `src`
## and are recovered by `typearg_at` (the proven bare-span read). This fires ONLY where `struct_ret_call`
## does NOT — i.e. the `Slice` type-decl is ABSENT from `decls` (a program with no prelude/lib import,
## the §7.2 bare `-> Slice(T)` case) — because it is placed AFTER `struct_ret_call` in the dispatch, so
## every existing lib slice-return (`omap_values`/`as_slice`/…, whose `Slice` decl IS present) keeps its
## current struct-bound lowering byte-for-byte (fixpoint- and e2e-neutral).
slice_ret_call := fn(e : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> bool {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, args_head) => {
      ci := ret_call_target(decls, src, cs, cl, nargs, args_head, a)
      if ci < 0 { return false }
      d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(ci))))
      if d.ret_tl == 0 { return false }
      bn := base_type_name(src, d.ret_ts, d.ret_tl)
      str_at((src + bn.s), bn.n) == "Slice"
    }
    _ => { false }
  }
}

## Recover a complete fixed-array return span. The parser records only the `[` head in Decl.ret_ts/ret_tl
## for a multi-token array return (`-> [T; N]`), unlike tuple/qualified return forms. Keep this recovery
## local to the lower fence: changing parser-wide return spans would widen a high-conflict surface for a
## boundary that only needs to reject direct indexing. The bounded scan handles nested array element types
## and stops at the matching `]`; malformed/incomplete spans remain unknown and follow existing paths.
fixed_array_return_span := fn(src : ptr(u8), ts : usize, tl : usize) -> CSpan {
  if tl == 0 or str_at((src + ts), 1) != "[" { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  mut i := ts
  mut depth := 0
  mut done := false
  mut end := 0
  while i < ts + 256 and not done {
    c := str_at((src + i), 1)
    if c == "[" { depth += 1 }
    else if c == "]" {
      if depth != 0 { depth -= 1 }
      if depth == 0 { end = i + 1; done = true }
    }
    i += 1
  }
  if done { CSpan(s = ts, n = end - ts) } else { CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
}

## Is `e` a direct call returning a fixed array `[T; N]`? The array return ABI is not implemented, so
## an index directly on that call has no frame-owned array from which `emit_index_addr` can safely
## compute an element address. Keep this classifier separate from `slice_ret_call`: a Slice result has
## its own `{ptr,len}` return convention and existing local/view lowering, while a fixed-array result
## must remain a located build reject until the complete `[T; N]` return ABI lands.
fixed_array_ret_call := fn(e : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> bool {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, args_head) => {
      ci := ret_call_target(decls, src, cs, cl, nargs, args_head, a)
      if ci < 0 { return false }
      d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(ci))))
      rs := fixed_array_return_span(src, d.ret_ts, d.ret_tl)
      rs.n != 0 and array_elem_span(src, rs.s, rs.n).n != 0
    }
    _ => { false }
  }
}

## BYTES bounded return ABI. A `[u8; N]` result with 1 <= N <= 8 is the one fixed-array shape
## whose already-proven packed local representation fits one internal integer return word: byte 0 is
## the low byte at the array's data address and the whole word returns in %rax. Keep this predicate
## separate from `fixed_array_ret_call`: every other array (including `[i8; N]`, `[bits8; N]`, wider
## arrays and zero-length arrays) remains on the existing located reject path rather than guessing an
## ABI or silently truncating a result.
fixed_array_byte_return_len_span := fn(src : ptr(u8), ts : usize, tl : usize) -> i64 {
  if tl == 0 or str_at((src + ts), 1) != "[" { return -1 }
  mut rs := CSpan(s = ts, n = tl)
  if tl == 1 {
    full := fixed_array_return_span(src, ts, tl)
    if full.n != 0 { rs = full }
  } else if str_at((src + ts), 1) == "[" {
    full := fixed_array_return_span(src, ts, tl)
    if full.n != 0 { rs = full }
  }
  ae := array_elem_span(src, rs.s, rs.n)
  if ae.n == 0 or byte_type_eek(src, ae.s, ae.n) != 8 { return -1 }
  nel := parse_arr_len(src, rs.s, rs.n)
  if nel == 0 or nel > 8 { return -1 }
  i64(nel)
}

## The call-site dual of `fixed_array_byte_return_len_span`: recover the callee's complete return
## span before asking for the bounded byte ABI. A non-resolved/indirect call stays unsupported here;
## it must not inherit a guessed one-word return convention.
fixed_array_byte_return_len := fn(e : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> i64 {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, args_head) => {
      ci := ret_call_target(decls, src, cs, cl, nargs, args_head, a)
      if ci >= 0 {
        d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(ci))))
        n := fixed_array_byte_return_len_span(src, d.ret_ts, d.ret_tl)
        if n >= 1 { return n }
      }
      ## A return-forwarding call can carry a parser-erased `[` head through the ordinary
      ## call-type resolver even when the name/arity classifier above cannot recover the
      ## concrete declaration at this nested consumer site. Keep this fallback typed: it
      ## only admits the same declared `[u8; N]`, 1 <= N <= 8, carrier and otherwise returns
      ## to the existing fixed-array located reject.
      rt := call_ret_ty_span(e, decls, src, a)
      fixed_array_byte_return_len_span(src, rt.s, rt.n)
    }
    _ => { -1 }
  }
}

## The ELEMENT layout (`eek`/`stride` + the element type span `ess`/`esl`) of a `-> Slice(T)`-returning
## call, so `collect_slots` can `bind_slice_slot` the result. The element `T` is read via `typearg_at`
## (the return-type head `Slice` is followed by `(T)` in `src`), then resolved: a callee type-PARAM
## element (`-> Slice(V)`) maps through the caller's value-arg at `V`'s parameter position and then
## through the enclosing instance `Subst` (the same seam as `subst_slice_ret_span`). Classified like a
## `Slice(T)` PARAM element (struct→eek 2, enum→eek 3, str→eek 4, float→eek 9, else scalar→eek 0).
## `is_slice = false` when `e` is not such a call.
SliceRetInfo := struct { is_slice : bool, eek : u8, stride : usize, ess : usize, esl : usize }
slice_ret_elem := fn(e : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena, gps : usize, gpl : usize, its : usize, itl : usize, gps2 : usize, gpl2 : usize, its2 : usize, itl2 : usize) -> SliceRetInfo {
  mut res := SliceRetInfo(is_slice = false, eek = 0, stride = 1, ess = 0, esl = 0)
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, ah) => {
      ci := ret_call_target(decls, src, cs, cl, nargs, ah, a)
      if ci >= 0 {
        d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(ci))))
        if d.ret_tl != 0 {
          bn := base_type_name(src, d.ret_ts, d.ret_tl)
          if str_at((src + bn.s), bn.n) == "Slice" {
            ta := typearg_at(src, d.ret_ts, d.ret_tl, 0)
            if ta.n != 0 {
              mut rs := ta.s
              mut rn := ta.n
              ## a callee type-PARAM element (`-> Slice(V)`) → the caller's value-arg at V's position.
              pp := callee_param_pos(decls, usize(ci), src, ta.s, ta.n, a)
              if pp >= 0 and usize(pp) < nargs {
                cta := arg_expr_at(ah, usize(pp), a)
                vn := var_name_span(cta)
                if vn.n != 0 { rs = vn.s; rn = vn.n }
              }
              ## then through the enclosing instance substitution (Slice(T) → Slice(<concrete>)).
              if gpl != 0 and streq(src, rs, rn, gps, gpl) { rs = its; rn = itl }
              else if gpl2 != 0 and streq(src, rs, rn, gps2, gpl2) { rs = its2; rn = itl2 }
              mut eek : u8 = 0
              mut stride := 1
              mut esdi : i64 = 0 - 1
              mut eedi : i64 = 0 - 1
              if rn < 64 { esdi = struct_decl_of(decls, src, rs, rn); eedi = enum_decl_of(decls, src, rs, rn) }
              esl := str_at((src + rs), rn)
              if esdi >= 0 { eek = 2; stride = struct_words(decls, src, rs, rn, a) }
              else if eedi >= 0 { eek = 3; stride = 1 + enum_inst_words(decls, src, rs, rn, a) }
              else if esl == "str" { eek = 4; stride = 2 }
              else if type_is_float(decls, src, rs, rn) { eek = 9; stride = 1 }
              else { eek = 0; stride = 1 }
              res = SliceRetInfo(is_slice = true, eek = eek, stride = stride, ess = rs, esl = rn)
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  res
}

## The SUBSTITUTED return-STRUCT span of a GENERIC call whose declared return type IS its type
## parameter (`id(T, v) -> T` called `id(P, …)` → the type-arg `P`), at ANY width. 0/0 unless the
## callee is a generic fn whose `-> R` is exactly its first type parameter and the leading type-arg
## resolves to a known struct. The WIDTH split lives in the two wrappers below: `gen_ret_struct_span`
## (1..7 words → the register-return convention) and `gen_ret_sret_span` (>= 8 words → SRET).
gen_ret_targ_struct_span := fn(e : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> CSpan {
  mut res := CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, ah) => {
      nm := callee_name_span(src, cs, cl)
      cnt := rt::vec_len(deref(decls))
      ## name→decl INDEX walk (see `DNI`): the candidate list is ascending, so `i` visits the same
      ## decls in the same order the full `0..cnt` scan did; `streq` below stays the arbiter.
      th := name_hash(src, nm.s, nm.n)
      mut jc := dni_lo(cnt, th)
      jce := dni_hi(cnt, th)
      mut i := 0
      while jc < jce {
        i = dni_at(cnt, jc)
        jc = jc + 1
        d := deref(decl_get(decls, i))
        if d.kind == 1 and d.is_generic and streq(src, d.name_start, d.name_len, nm.s, nm.n) {
          mut tps := 0
          mut tpl := 0
          mut pp := d.params_head
          while pp != 0 {
            pm := deref(param_p(pp))
            if tpl == 0 and str_at((src + pm.ts), pm.tl) == "type" { tps = pm.ns; tpl = pm.nl }
            pp = pm.next
          }
          if tpl != 0 and d.ret_tl != 0 and streq(src, d.ret_ts, d.ret_tl, tps, tpl) {
            mut ta := type_arg_span(ah, a)
            ## A GENERIC-INSTANCE type argument (`id(Box(u64), b)`) parses as a `Call`, which
            ## `type_arg_span` (bare `Var` only) misses → the binding fell to a bare scalar and the
            ## struct's tail words were dropped. `type_arg_at` returns the instance HEAD (its `(…)`
            ## follows in source); `is_fn_name` + the struct-decl gate reject a non-type argument.
            if ta.n == 0 {
              tac := type_arg_at(ah, 0, a)
              if tac.n != 0 and is_fn_name(decls, src, tac.s, tac.n) == false { ta = tac }
            }
            if ta.n != 0 and struct_decl_of(decls, src, ta.s, ta.n) >= 0 { res = CSpan(s = ta.s, n = ta.n) }
          }
        }
        i += 1
      }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  res
}

## The SUBSTITUTED return-struct span of a GENERIC call whose declared return type IS its type
## parameter (`id(T, v) -> T` called `id(P, …)` → the type-arg `P`). Lets the caller bind
## `p := id(P, …)` as that struct; the instance delivers it in %rax/%rdx (its effective return type
## resolves to the struct via emit_fn's substitution). 0/0 unless the callee is a generic fn whose
## `-> R` is exactly its first type parameter and the leading type-arg is a known struct (1..7 words).
gen_ret_struct_span := fn(e : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> CSpan {
  ta := gen_ret_targ_struct_span(e, decls, src, a)
  if ta.n == 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  tw := struct_words(decls, src, ta.s, ta.n, a)
  if tw >= 1 and tw <= 7 { return ta }
  CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
}

## The WIDE (>= 8-word, SRET) dual of `gen_ret_struct_span`: a `c := mk(S, …)` where the GENERIC callee
## returns its type parameter and the type-arg is a struct TOO WIDE for the register-return budget
## (words 0..6 = %rax..%r11). The caller allocates `c`, passes its word-0 ADDRESS as the hidden %rdi and
## the instance writes the whole struct through it — the same SRET path the CONCRETE wide return
## (`sret_ret_call`) takes. Without this the generic return took neither path (`fn_returns_sret` can't
## resolve the type-parameter return span), so `c` was sized as a bare scalar and read back from the
## return registers: a SILENT 0/garbage for every field. 0/0 for a 1..7-word type-arg (that is
## `gen_ret_struct_span`'s register path, byte-identical) → fixpoint-neutral (`src/` has no such call).
gen_ret_sret_span := fn(e : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> CSpan {
  ta := gen_ret_targ_struct_span(e, decls, src, a)
  if ta.n == 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  if struct_words(decls, src, ta.s, ta.n, a) >= 8 { return ta }
  CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
}

## The IMPLICIT-UFCS counterpart of `gen_ret_struct_span` (Hunk C): an `r := o.unwrap()` / `r := o.expect(m)`
## whose callee returns its type-param `T` (`unwrap`/`expect` -> `T`) but is spelled UFCS — the `T` type-arg
## is OMITTED, so the receiver `o` is a VALUE arg. `gen_ret_struct_span` reads the EXPLICIT type-arg (arg 0),
## which for the UFCS form is the receiver Var (not a struct) → 0/0 → the binding is sized as a scalar,
## dropping the payload's high words (a silent truncation). Recover `T` from the RECEIVER's SLOT type
## instead (`Option(Rec)` → `Rec`, read from the enum slot the earlier `o := getit()` bound), so the binding
## is sized as the full struct. Gated to the implicit shape (`nargs == arity - ntpc`) and a `>= 2`-word
## (`<= 7`, register-returned) struct payload — mirroring `infer_implicit_targ`'s emit-side re-tag gate so
## the instance label (`unwrap__Rec`) and the binding size agree; a scalar / 1-word payload returns 0/0
## (byte-identical to before, so `src/`'s scalar `.expect(…).idx` is untouched → fixpoint-neutral).
ufcs_ret_struct_span := fn(e : ptr(Expr), slots : ptr(SVec), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> CSpan {
  mut res := CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, ah) => {
      nm := callee_name_span(src, cs, cl)
      cnt := rt::vec_len(deref(decls))
      ## name→decl INDEX walk (see `DNI`): the candidate list is ascending, so `i` visits the same
      ## decls in the same order the full `0..cnt` scan did; `streq` below stays the arbiter.
      th := name_hash(src, nm.s, nm.n)
      mut jc := dni_lo(cnt, th)
      jce := dni_hi(cnt, th)
      mut i := 0
      while jc < jce {
        i = dni_at(cnt, jc)
        jc = jc + 1
        d := deref(decl_get(decls, i))
        if res.n == 0 and d.kind == 1 and d.is_generic and streq(src, d.name_start, d.name_len, nm.s, nm.n) {
          mut tps := 0
          mut tpl := 0
          mut ntpc := 0
          mut pp := d.params_head
          while pp != 0 {
            pm := deref(param_p(pp))
            if str_at((src + pm.ts), pm.tl) == "type" { if tpl == 0 { tps = pm.ns; tpl = pm.nl } ; ntpc += 1 }
            pp = pm.next
          }
          ## the callee returns its FIRST type-param, AND every type-arg is omitted (the implicit-UFCS
          ## shape) → the value-param index equals the arg index, so `jm`/`arg_expr_at` line up.
          if tpl != 0 and d.ret_tl != 0 and streq(src, d.ret_ts, d.ret_tl, tps, tpl) and d.arity != 0 and nargs == d.arity - usize(ntpc) {
            mut jm := 0
            mut pc := d.params_head
            while pc != 0 {
              pm := deref(param_p(pc))
              if str_at((src + pm.ts), pm.tl) == "type" { pc = pm.next }
              else {
                if res.n == 0 and param_type_has_tparam(src, pm.ts, pm.tl, tps, tpl) {
                  rvn := var_name_span(arg_expr_at(ah, usize(jm), a))
                  if rvn.n != 0 and slot_of(slots, src, rvn.s, rvn.n) >= 0 {
                    rent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, slots, entry_of(slots, src, rvn.s, rvn.n)))
                    if rent.snl != 0 {
                      pbn := base_type_name(src, pm.ts, pm.tl)
                      mut kk := 0
                      mut tpos := 0 - 1
                      mut gk := true
                      while gk {
                        pta := typearg_at(src, pbn.s, pbn.n, usize(kk))
                        if pta.n == 0 { gk = false }
                        else { if streq(src, pta.s, pta.n, tps, tpl) { tpos = i64(kk); gk = false } ; kk += 1 }
                      }
                      if tpos >= 0 {
                        rbn := base_type_name(src, rent.sns, rent.snl)
                        payl := typearg_at(src, rbn.s, rbn.n, usize(tpos))
                        if payl.n != 0 and struct_decl_of(decls, src, payl.s, payl.n) >= 0 {
                          tw := struct_words(decls, src, payl.s, payl.n, a)
                          if tw >= 2 and tw <= 7 { res = CSpan(s = payl.s, n = payl.n) }
                        }
                      }
                    }
                  }
                }
                jm += 1
                pc = pm.next
              }
            }
          }
        }
        i += 1
      }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  res
}

## The ENUM dual of `gen_ret_struct_span`: the substituted return-ENUM span of a generic call whose
## `-> R` is its type parameter and whose leading type-arg is an ENUM (`id(Opt, …)`). Lets the caller
## bind `o := id(Opt, …)` as that enum; the instance delivers it via the disc/%rax + payload/%rdx
## convention. 0/0 otherwise.
gen_ret_enum_span := fn(e : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> CSpan {
  mut res := CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, ah) => {
      nm := callee_name_span(src, cs, cl)
      cnt := rt::vec_len(deref(decls))
      ## name→decl INDEX walk (see `DNI`): the candidate list is ascending, so `i` visits the same
      ## decls in the same order the full `0..cnt` scan did; `streq` below stays the arbiter.
      th := name_hash(src, nm.s, nm.n)
      mut jc := dni_lo(cnt, th)
      jce := dni_hi(cnt, th)
      mut i := 0
      while jc < jce {
        i = dni_at(cnt, jc)
        jc = jc + 1
        d := deref(decl_get(decls, i))
        if d.kind == 1 and d.is_generic and streq(src, d.name_start, d.name_len, nm.s, nm.n) {
          mut tps := 0
          mut tpl := 0
          mut pp := d.params_head
          while pp != 0 {
            pm := deref(param_p(pp))
            if tpl == 0 and str_at((src + pm.ts), pm.tl) == "type" { tps = pm.ns; tpl = pm.nl }
            pp = pm.next
          }
          if tpl != 0 and d.ret_tl != 0 and streq(src, d.ret_ts, d.ret_tl, tps, tpl) {
            mut ta := type_arg_span(ah, a)
            ## A GENERIC-INSTANCE type argument (`unbox(Option(u64), b)`) parses as a `Call`, which
            ## `type_arg_span` (bare `Var` only) misses — the binding then fell to a bare SCALAR and a
            ## `match` on it compared every arm against discriminant 0, so a two-arm match ran NEITHER
            ## arm. `type_arg_at` returns the instance HEAD (`Option`, with its `(u64)` following in
            ## source). Filtered by `is_fn_name` (a value Call in an inferred type-param slot) and by
            ## the enum-decl gate below, so a non-type argument still yields 0/0.
            if ta.n == 0 {
              tac := type_arg_at(ah, 0, a)
              if tac.n != 0 and is_fn_name(decls, src, tac.s, tac.n) == false { ta = tac }
            }
            if ta.n != 0 and enum_decl_of(decls, src, ta.s, ta.n) >= 0 { res = CSpan(s = ta.s, n = ta.n) }
          }
        }
        i += 1
      }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  res
}

## Is `e` a `Call` to a fn returning a TUPLE `(…)`? The caller receives it from %rax/%rdx/… into the
## binding's N word slots (an array-shaped local). The tuple dual of `struct_ret_call`.
tuple_ret_call := fn(e : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> bool {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, args_head) => {
      ci := ret_call_target(decls, src, cs, cl, nargs, args_head, a)
      if ci < 0 { return false }
      d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(ci))))
      fn_returns_tuple(d, src)
    }
    _ => { false }
  }
}
## Is `e` a `Call` to the atomic COMPARE-AND-SWAP builtin (`atomic::cas_strong` / `atomic::cas_weak`)?
## It returns a 2-tuple `(current : T, succeeded : bool)`, so `r := atomic::cas_*(…)` binds `r` as a
## 2-word tuple (`r.0` current, `r.1` succeeded) and the Assign emits `cmpxchg` directly into its slots.
## Ptr-param match (a local-Expr `match deref` degenerates to ek 0).
is_cas_call := fn(e : ptr(Expr), src : ptr(u8)) -> bool {
  mut res := false
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, args_head) => {
      nm := str_at((src + cs), cl)
      res = nm == "atomic::cas_strong" or nm == "atomic::cas_weak"
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  res
}
## The COMPONENT (word) count of a `Call` to a tuple-returning fn — so `t := mk()` reserves `t` as
## an N-word tuple local. 0 if not such a call.
call_ret_tuple_words := fn(e : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> usize {
  mut res := 0
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, args_head) => {
      ci := ret_call_target(decls, src, cs, cl, nargs, args_head, a)
      if ci >= 0 {
        d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(ci))))
        if fn_returns_tuple(d, src) { res = tuple_words(src, d.ret_ts, d.ret_tl) }
      }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  res
}

## Is `e` a `Call` to a fn returning a struct > 7 words (SRET)? The caller allocates the destination
## LOCAL, passes its address as the hidden %rdi arg, and the callee writes the whole struct through it
## (so no register-copy after the call). The wide-struct dual of `struct_ret_call`.
sret_ret_call := fn(e : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> bool {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, args_head) => {
      ci := ret_call_target(decls, src, cs, cl, nargs, args_head, a)
      if ci < 0 {
        ## FN-6 INDIRECT: a call through a fn VALUE returning a struct >= 8 words. Without the class
        ## the hidden result pointer was never wired — the callee wrote through whatever %rdi held
        ## (the first user argument), i.e. a SEGFAULT. Now the destination local's address rides %rdi
        ## exactly as for the direct call (`emit_call_args`'s `cx.sret_call` path).
        return ind_call_ret_struct_words(e, decls, src, a) >= 8
      }
      d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(ci))))
      fn_returns_sret(d, decls, src, a)
    }
    _ => { false }
  }
}

## Is `e` a `Call` to a fn returning a WIDE enum (disc + payload > 7 words, SRET)? The caller allocates
## the destination enum LOCAL (via `enum_ret_call_d` in collect_slots — unchanged), passes its address
## as the hidden %rdi, and the callee writes the whole enum through it. The wide-enum dual of `sret_ret_call`.
enum_sret_ret_call := fn(e : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> bool {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, args_head) => {
      ci := ret_call_target(decls, src, cs, cl, nargs, args_head, a)
      if ci < 0 { return false }
      d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(ci))))
      fn_returns_enum_sret(d, decls, src, a)
    }
    _ => { false }
  }
}

## Is `e` a `Call` to a fn that returns a `str`? (the caller binds the result into a 2-word str
## slot, receiving ptr/%rax + len/%rdx). The str dual of `struct_ret_call` — used by `collect_slots`
## (slot reservation) and the Assign path so `nm := module_name(p)` / `p := svec_str_get(…)` are
## sized + stored as a str, not a scalar (which would drop the len and corrupt by-ref str reads).
## Types §7 / Comptime §3.3 — a call to a GENERIC whose RETURN type IS its own comptime type
## parameter, with that type argument OMITTED and INFERRED as `str` (`id(s)` for `s : str`, through
## `id := fn(T : type, v : T) -> T`). `str_ret_call` reads the type argument POSITIONALLY out of the
## call's own arguments (`generic_str_return_instance`), so an INFERRED one is invisible to it: the
## call then fell to the scalar path and handed the callee its data POINTER as a by-reference block
## pointer. Before the type-argument inference landed that shape SIGSEGV'd (loud); with inference it
## would have become an EMPTY string instead — a silent wrong value, a strictly worse outcome, which
## is why this predicate lands WITH the inference. Resolves the callee exactly as the emitter does
## and asks the same `infer_implicit_targ`, so all three agree on the instance.
generic_inferred_str_ret := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena) -> bool {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, args_head) => {
      gi := generic_decl_of(cx.decls, cx.src, cs, cl, cx.mod_s, cx.mod_l, nargs, args_head, a)
      if gi < 0 { return false }
      gd := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(cx.decls), usize(gi))))
      if gd.is_generic == false { return false }
      if gd.ret_tl == 0 { return false }
      mut tpn_s := 0
      mut tpn_l := 0
      mut ntp := 0
      mut pc := gd.params_head
      while pc != 0 {
        pm := deref(param_p(pc))
        if str_at((cx.src + pm.ts), pm.tl) == "type" { tpn_s = pm.ns; tpn_l = pm.nl; ntp += 1 }
        pc = pm.next
      }
      if ntp != 1 { return false }
      if tpn_l == 0 { return false }
      if streq(cx.src, gd.ret_ts, gd.ret_tl, tpn_s, tpn_l) == false { return false }
      if gd.arity == 0 { return false }
      if nargs != gd.arity - 1 { return false }        ## the type argument must be OMITTED
      iti := infer_implicit_targ(gi, args_head, cx, a)
      iti.n != 0 and str_at((cx.src + iti.s), iti.n) == "str"
    }
    _ => { false }
  }
}

str_ret_call := fn(e : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> bool {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, args_head) => {
      ci := ret_call_target(decls, src, cs, cl, nargs, args_head, a)
      if ci < 0 { return false }
      d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(ci))))
      fn_returns_str(d, src) or generic_str_return_instance(d, args_head, src, a)
    }
    _ => { false }
  }
}

## Is a module-level GLOBAL's initializer a runtime CALL returning an AGGREGATE (struct / wide struct /
## enum / wide enum / tuple / str / slice / bounded fixed [u8;N])? A global initializer must be a COMPILE-TIME constant — the
## `.data` cells are emitted from the literal, and nothing runs before `_start`. A CALL is not folded,
## so such a global used to allocate ZEROED `.data` (or no storage at all) and the initializer never
## ran: `G.a` silently read 0 (a SILENT miscompile). The SCALAR case is already fail-loud (the value is
## inlined at each use → an `undefined reference` at `ld`); this makes the AGGREGATE case loud too.
## Deciding what a *runtime* global initializer should MEAN (a start-up initializer phase? an
## initialization order?) is a spec question — until it is decided, reject rather than miscompile.
global_init_agg_call := fn(v : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> bool {
  if struct_ret_call(v, decls, src, a) { return true }
  if sret_ret_call(v, decls, src, a) { return true }
  if enum_ret_call_d(v, decls, src, a) { return true }
  if enum_sret_ret_call(v, decls, src, a) { return true }
  if tuple_ret_call(v, decls, src, a) { return true }
  if str_ret_call(v, decls, src, a) { return true }
  if fixed_array_byte_return_len(v, decls, src, a) >= 1 { return true }
  return slice_ret_call(v, decls, src, a)
}

## The struct-TYPE name span of a `Call` to a 2-word-struct-returning fn (so a `q := call()`
## binding reserves `q` as that struct). 0/0 if not such a call. Mirrors `call_ret_enum_span`.
call_ret_struct_span := fn(e : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> CSpan {
  mut res := CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, args_head) => {
      ci := ret_call_target(decls, src, cs, cl, nargs, args_head, a)
      if ci >= 0 {
        d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(ci))))
        res = CSpan(s = d.ret_ts, n = d.ret_tl)
      } else {
        ## FN-6 INDIRECT: the struct type of a call through a fn VALUE (`struct_ret_call`/
        ## `sret_ret_call`'s span dual) — so the destination local is sized + typed from it.
        if ind_call_ret_struct_words(e, decls, src, a) != 0 { res = ind_call_ret_span(e, decls, src, a) }
      }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  res
}

## The struct-TYPE span + cumulative word offset of an expression that is a scalar-leaf FIELD
## PROJECTION off a struct-returning CALL result (Types §9.4) — the by-value dual of `ptr_place`
## for a call whose returned struct lands in the %rax:%rdx:%rcx:… registers. Recurses down the
## `Field` chain; the ROOT `Call` arm resolves the returned struct's span (a concrete `-> Struct`
## via `call_ret_struct_span` when `struct_ret_call`, else the GENERIC `id(T, v) -> T` shape via
## `gen_ret_struct_span` — the same resolver the BINDING path `r := id(P,…); r.b` uses). Each
## `Field` hop adds the field's word offset (`field_word_offset`) and advances the running struct
## span to that field's substituted type (`field_type_span`). `found`=false when the root is not
## such a call (→ the existing `<call>.field` single-field arm / `field_slot` default stays
## authoritative → fixpoint-neutral). Fails LOUD on a non-plain-struct / unresolvable intermediate
## field (never a silent bad offset). Fixes `mk().b.c` (chained field off a by-value struct call)
## and `id(P, …).b` (field off a GENERIC struct-returning call), both of which fell to `pushq $0`.
CallChainPlace := struct { found : bool, root : ptr(Expr), tys : usize, tyn : usize, woff : i64 }
call_chain_place := fn(e : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> CallChainPlace {
  z := unchecked bitcast(ptr(Expr), 0)
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Field(ib, fs, fl) => {
      inner := call_chain_place(ib, decls, src, a)
      if inner.found == false { return CallChainPlace(found = false, root = z, tys = 0, tyn = 0, woff = 0) }
      if struct_decl_of(decls, src, inner.tys, inner.tyn) < 0 {
        panic("selfhost: a field projection off a struct-returning call whose intermediate field is not a plain struct is not lowered (bind the call result to a local first)")
      }
      fty := field_type_span(decls, src, inner.tys, inner.tyn, fs, fl, a)
      if fty.n == 0 { return CallChainPlace(found = false, root = z, tys = 0, tyn = 0, woff = 0) }
      wf := field_word_offset(decls, src, inner.tys, inner.tyn, fs, fl, a)
      if wf < 0 { panic("selfhost: a field projection off a struct-returning call names an intermediate field the struct does not declare") }
      return CallChainPlace(found = true, root = inner.root, tys = fty.s, tyn = fty.n, woff = inner.woff + i64(wf))
    }
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, ah) => {
      if struct_ret_call(e, decls, src, a) {
        csp := call_ret_struct_span(e, decls, src, a)
        return CallChainPlace(found = true, root = e, tys = csp.s, tyn = csp.n, woff = 0)
      }
      gsp := gen_ret_struct_span(e, decls, src, a)
      if gsp.n != 0 { return CallChainPlace(found = true, root = e, tys = gsp.s, tyn = gsp.n, woff = 0) }
      return CallChainPlace(found = false, root = z, tys = 0, tyn = 0, woff = 0)
    }
    _ => { return CallChainPlace(found = false, root = z, tys = 0, tyn = 0, woff = 0) }
  }
}

## The FULL return-type span of a DIRECT struct/enum-returning CALL used as a UFCS receiver
## (`find(1).unwrap()` — the receiver `find(1)` is a call with NO name to resolve a slot type from,
## so `recv_full_emit`/`recv_full_pre` return 0/0). An enum-returning call → its `-> Option(u64)`
## span; a concrete struct-returning call → `call_ret_struct_span`; a generic `id(T,v)->T` call →
## `gen_ret_struct_span`. 0/0 for a non-aggregate / non-call receiver (the Var-name / `recv_full_*`
## resolution stays authoritative → fixpoint-neutral). Lets the implicit-UFCS type-arg inference tag
## `.unwrap()`/`.expect()` on a call receiver by its CONCRETE payload type.
recv_call_full_span := fn(e : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> CSpan {
  if enum_ret_call_d(e, decls, src, a) { return call_ret_enum_span_d(e, decls, src, a) }
  if struct_ret_call(e, decls, src, a) { return call_ret_struct_span(e, decls, src, a) }
  gsp := gen_ret_struct_span(e, decls, src, a)
  if gsp.n != 0 { return gsp }
  CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
}

## The concrete type-argument at type-parameter `[tpn_s,tpn_l)`'s POSITION within a UFCS receiver's
## resolved type. `pts/ptl` = the param's generic type span (`Option(T)`); `rts/rtl` = the receiver's
## concrete type span (`Option(u64)`). Finds `T`'s index among the param type's type-args, then reads
## the same-index type-arg off the receiver type (→ `u64`). 0/0 if `T` is not a top-level type-arg of
## the param type or the receiver type has no arg there. The shared kernel of the emit/collect implicit
## type-arg inference for a call-receiver UFCS method (`.unwrap()`/`.expect()`).
recv_payload_targ := fn(src : ptr(u8), pts : usize, ptl : usize, tpn_s : usize, tpn_l : usize, rts : usize, rtl : usize) -> CSpan {
  pbn := base_type_name(src, pts, ptl)
  mut kk := 0
  mut tpos := 0 - 1
  mut gk := true
  while gk {
    pta := typearg_at(src, pbn.s, pbn.n, usize(kk))
    if pta.n == 0 { gk = false }
    else { if streq(src, pta.s, pta.n, tpn_s, tpn_l) { tpos = i64(kk); gk = false } ; kk += 1 }
  }
  if tpos < 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  rbn := base_type_name(src, rts, rtl)
  payl := typearg_at(src, rbn.s, rbn.n, usize(tpos))
  CSpan(s = payl.s, n = payl.n)
}

## Is the type span `[s, s+n)` a CONCRETE SCALAR type tag — a bare type NAME (no `(`, so not a generic
## instance / unsubstituted `Handle(T)`) that is NOT str / struct / enum (a 1-word scalar like `u64`)?
## Gates the call-receiver UFCS type-arg inference to the ONLY shape it lowers correctly (`find(1).
## unwrap()` → `T = u64`): a parenthesized (`Handle(T)`/`Vec(u64)`) or aggregate payload is DEFERRED to
## the pre-existing path so it stays byte-identical (no regression).
is_concrete_scalar_tag := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize, a : rt::Arena) -> bool {
  if n == 0 { return false }
  mut i := 0
  while i < n {
    if str_at((src + s + i), 1) == "(" { return false }
    i += 1
  }
  if str_at((src + s), n) == "str" { return false }
  if struct_decl_of(decls, src, s, n) >= 0 { return false }
  if enum_decl_of(decls, src, s, n) >= 0 { return false }
  true
}

## The pointee STRUCT-type span of a `Call` to a GENERIC fn whose return type HEAD is `ptr`
## (`node_ptr(T, …) -> ptr(mut T)` / `decl_at(T, h) -> ptr(T)`), where the pointee `T` is the
## call's TYPE ARGUMENT (arg 0). 0/0 if not such a call OR the type arg is not a struct. So
## `ep := node_ptr(Arg, …)` binds `ep` as a POINTER-TO-STRUCT local (ek 7) carrying Arg's type —
## then a following `eold := deref(ep)` is recognized by `deref_struct_span` and copies Arg's
## words, so `eold.e` resolves. WITHOUT this `ep` sized as a SCALAR (no pointee type), `eold`
## read garbage, and the AST list-update path (`ep := node_ptr(Arg,…); eold := deref(ep);
## deref(ep) = Arg(e = eold.e, next = …)`) corrupted the head node's `e` field. Gated on the
## callee being GENERIC (first param `T : type`) so only `node_ptr`/`decl_at` match — `arg_expr_at`
## (-> ptr(Expr), not generic) / `strbuf_base` (-> ptr(mut u8)) / the `p_*` parser fns are excluded.
call_ret_ptrstruct_span := fn(e : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> CSpan {
  mut res := CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, args_head) => {
      cnt := rt::vec_len(deref(decls))
      mut isptr := false
      ## `nm` HOISTED out of the loop (see `float_ret_call`) + the name→decl index walk.
      nm := callee_name_span(src, cs, cl)
      th := name_hash(src, nm.s, nm.n)
      mut jc := dni_lo(cnt, th)
      jce := dni_hi(cnt, th)
      while jc < jce {
        i := dni_at(cnt, jc)
        jc = jc + 1
        if dnh_skip(cnt, i, th) == false {
          d := deref(decl_get(decls, i))
          if d.kind == 1 and d.is_generic and streq(src, d.name_start, d.name_len, nm.s, nm.n) {
            if str_at((src + d.ret_ts), d.ret_tl) == "ptr" { isptr = true }
          }
        }
      }
      if isptr {
        ta := type_arg_span(args_head, a)
        if ta.n != 0 and struct_decl_of(decls, src, ta.s, ta.n) >= 0 {
          res = CSpan(s = ta.s, n = ta.n)
        }
      }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  res
}

## The POINTEE struct span of a call to a NON-generic fn whose DECLARED return type is
## `ptr(<opt mut> Struct)` (`get -> ptr(Box)`). The dual of `call_ret_ptrstruct_span` (which
## handles the GENERIC node_ptr/decl_at machinery, where the pointee is a type-ARG). Lets an
## INFERRED `p := get(…)` bind `p` as a pointer-to-struct (ek 7) so a later `deref(p).f` / `p.f`
## reads the field THROUGH the pointer (preserved pointee types through pointer-
## returning calls). The annotated form (`p : ptr(Box) = …`) already resolved via the local type
## span; this recovers the same pointee for the un-annotated binding. 0/0 if the callee isn't a
## non-generic fn returning `ptr(Struct)`.
call_ret_ptrstruct_ret_span := fn(e : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> CSpan {
  mut res := CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, args_head) => {
      cnt := rt::vec_len(deref(decls))
      nm := callee_name_span(src, cs, cl)
      for i in 0..cnt {
        d := deref(decl_get(decls, i))
        if d.kind == 1 and not d.is_generic and streq(src, d.name_start, d.name_len, nm.s, nm.n) {
          ## The declared return type records only the BASE name (`ptr`); recover the FULL
          ## `ptr(<opt mut> P)` from the source, then take the pointee name. Accept it only when the
          ## pointee is a known STRUCT decl (so a scalar-pointee return stays a plain scalar bind).
          rfull := extend_type_span(src, d.ret_ts, d.ret_tl)
          pn := ptr_pointee_name(src, d.ret_ts, rfull)
          if pn.n != 0 and struct_decl_of(decls, src, pn.s, pn.n) >= 0 { res = pn }
        }
      }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  res
}

## The GENERIC counterpart of `call_ret_ptrstruct_ret_span`: if `e` is a `Call` to a GENERIC fn whose
## declared return type is `ptr(<opt mut> T)` where `T` is the callee's TYPE PARAMETER (`get(T : type, a,
## h) -> scoped ptr(mut T)`), resolve `T` to the call's concrete type ARGUMENT and — when that is a known
## STRUCT — return its span; else 0/0. Lets `p := get(P, ar, h)` bind `p` as a pointer-to-struct (ek 7) so
## `deref(p).x` resolves the field through the pointer (the value-side dual is `gen_ret_struct_span`).
gen_ret_ptrstruct_span := fn(e : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> CSpan {
  mut res := CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, ah) => {
      nm := callee_name_span(src, cs, cl)
      cnt := rt::vec_len(deref(decls))
      ## name→decl INDEX walk (see `DNI`): the candidate list is ascending, so `i` visits the same
      ## decls in the same order the full `0..cnt` scan did; `streq` below stays the arbiter.
      th := name_hash(src, nm.s, nm.n)
      mut jc := dni_lo(cnt, th)
      jce := dni_hi(cnt, th)
      mut i := 0
      while jc < jce {
        i = dni_at(cnt, jc)
        jc = jc + 1
        d := deref(decl_get(decls, i))
        if d.kind == 1 and d.is_generic and streq(src, d.name_start, d.name_len, nm.s, nm.n) {
          mut tps := 0
          mut tpl := 0
          mut pp := d.params_head
          while pp != 0 {
            pm := deref(param_p(pp))
            if tpl == 0 and str_at((src + pm.ts), pm.tl) == "type" { tps = pm.ns; tpl = pm.nl }
            pp = pm.next
          }
          ## the declared return records the BASE name `ptr`; recover the full `ptr(<mut> T)` + its pointee,
          ## and accept only when the pointee IS the type param — then resolve `T` to the type argument.
          if tpl != 0 and d.ret_tl != 0 {
            rfull := extend_type_span(src, d.ret_ts, d.ret_tl)
            pn := ptr_pointee_name(src, d.ret_ts, rfull)
            if pn.n != 0 and streq(src, pn.s, pn.n, tps, tpl) {
              ta := type_arg_span(ah, a)
              if ta.n != 0 and struct_decl_of(decls, src, ta.s, ta.n) >= 0 { res = ta }
            }
          }
        }
        i += 1
      }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  res
}

## The pointee STRUCT span of ANY `Call` that returns `ptr(<opt mut> Struct)` — the union of the three
## ptr-returning-call shapes: GENERIC node_ptr/decl_at (pointee = type ARG, `call_ret_ptrstruct_span`),
## a NON-generic fn declaring `ptr(Struct)` (`call_ret_ptrstruct_ret_span`), and a GENERIC fn declaring
## `ptr(mut T)` where `T` is a type PARAM resolved to the type arg (`gen_ret_ptrstruct_span`, e.g.
## `get(S, ar, h)`). 0/0 if `e` is not a call returning a pointer-to-struct. Used to lower a field read
## taken DIRECTLY off a pointer-returning call (`f(x).b`) — see the `<call: ptr(Struct)>.field` arm — so
## it reads the field THROUGH the returned pointer instead of the `pushq $0` fallthrough (a silent 0).
call_ptrstruct_field_span := fn(e : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> CSpan {
  r0 := call_ret_ptrstruct_span(e, decls, src, a)
  if r0.n != 0 { return r0 }
  r1 := call_ret_ptrstruct_ret_span(e, decls, src, a)
  if r1.n != 0 { return r1 }
  gen_ret_ptrstruct_span(e, decls, src, a)
}

## The ENUM dual of `call_ret_ptrstruct_ret_span` (§6 ptr-typing): if `e` is a `Call` to a non-generic
## fn whose declared RETURN type is `ptr(<opt mut> E)` for a known ENUM `E`, return `E`'s TYPE span; else
## 0/0. Lets a `st := deref(<identity accessor>(h))` binding — where the accessor returns `ptr(mut Stmt)`
## but takes the handle as arg 0 (so the node_ptr FIRST-ARG-TYPE-NAME convention does NOT fire) — still
## bind `st` as an enum local. The enum counterpart of the struct return-type fallback, needed once the
## `Stmt`-handle reads route through `stmt_p(Stmt, h)` instead of `stmt_p(Stmt, h)`.
call_ret_ptrenum_ret_span := fn(e : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> CSpan {
  mut res := CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, args_head) => {
      cnt := rt::vec_len(deref(decls))
      nm := callee_name_span(src, cs, cl)
      th := name_hash(src, nm.s, nm.n)
      mut jc := dni_lo(cnt, th)
      jce := dni_hi(cnt, th)
      while jc < jce {
        i := dni_at(cnt, jc)
        jc = jc + 1
        if dnh_skip(cnt, i, th) == false {
          d := deref(decl_get(decls, i))
          if d.kind == 1 and not d.is_generic and streq(src, d.name_start, d.name_len, nm.s, nm.n) {
            rfull := extend_type_span(src, d.ret_ts, d.ret_tl)
            pn := ptr_pointee_name(src, d.ret_ts, rfull)
            if pn.n != 0 and enum_decl_of(decls, src, pn.s, pn.n) >= 0 { res = pn }
          }
        }
      }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  res
}

## GENERICS tier — MONOMORPHIZATION. A generic fn call `f(T, a, b)` (where `f`'s first
## param is `T : type`) is specialized per concrete type `T`. Lower:
##  - finds the callee's `Decl` is generic (`generic_decl_of`),
##  - reads the TYPE ARGUMENT (arg 0, an ident naming a type) lexeme as the instance TYPETAG,
##  - emits the call to the mangled instance `<callee-module>__<fn>__<typetag>`,
##  - passes ONLY the value arguments (args 1..) in the System V registers — the type
##    argument is COMPTIME and consumes NO register (erased at runtime).
## A pre-pass (`emit_program`) collects every distinct (generic-fn, typetag) pair and emits one
## specialized fn-definition copy per pair; an uninstantiated generic emits no code.

## The callee's bare FUNCTION-NAME span — the TAIL after `::` for a qualified call `mod::f`
## (so `f`), or the whole span for an unqualified call. Generic resolution + instance-label
## mangling match on this tail (mirroring nameres, which keys a cross-module callee by its tail
## name); the head module is split off separately by `emit_mangled_call`.
callee_name_span := fn(src : ptr(u8), cs : usize, cl : usize) -> CSpan {
  cp := colon_pos(src, cs, cl)
  if cp >= 0 {
    hl := usize(cp)                       ## head length (the module name)
    return CSpan(s = cs + hl + 2, n = cl - hl - 2)   ## skip `::`
  }
  CSpan(s = cs, n = cl)
}

## Is expr `e` a bare type-NAME (a leading comptime type-argument) — a `Var` naming a builtin scalar
## (`conv_kind` ≥ 0, i.e. `u64`/`i64`/`usize`/…/`f64`/`f32`), `bool`/`char`/`bitsN`, or a struct/enum
## decl? A value arg (a literal, a call, a fn-value `Var` like `inc`) is NOT a type name. Used to count
## the LEADING type-args a call spells, disambiguating same-arity same-name generics by type-param count.
is_type_name := fn(e : ptr(Expr), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> bool {
  vn := var_name_span(e)
  if vn.n == 0 { return false }
  nm := str_at((src + vn.s), vn.n)
  if conv_kind(nm) >= 0 { return true }
  if nm == "bool" or nm == "char" { return true }
  if vn.n >= 4 and str_at((src + vn.s), 4) == "bits" { return true }
  if struct_decl_of(decls, src, vn.s, vn.n) >= 0 { return true }
  if enum_decl_of(decls, src, vn.s, vn.n) >= 0 { return true }
  false
}

## The number of LEADING arguments of a call that are bare type-NAMES (`is_type_name`), stopping at
## the first non-type arg. `map(u64, u64, u64, r, f)` → 3; `map(u64, u64, arena, s, f)` → 2. Equals the
## type-parameter count of the intended overload, so a same-arity cross-module collision
## (`result::map`/ntpc 3 vs `vec::map`/ntpc 2) resolves to the one whose `tparam_count` matches.
leading_type_arg_count := fn(args_head : ptr(mut Arg), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> usize {
  mut g := args_head
  mut c := 0
  mut go := true
  while g != 0 and go {
    ga := deref(arg_p(g))
    if is_type_name(ga.e, decls, src, a) { c = c + 1; g = ga.next } else { go = false }
  }
  c
}

## The rank of a candidate declaration's module `[as_, al)` as seen from the naming module
## `[ms, ml)`. `mod_head_matches` (the caller's OWN module, or the LAST-SEGMENT lib-path leniency)
## sits ABOVE the whole ancestor chain: ranks are module-path LENGTHS, so `ml + 1` outranks every
## ancestor. Below it the §3 chain, nearest-first, via `mod_anc_rank_from`. -1 = §3 makes the module
## unnameable from here (a sibling, a descendant, an unrelated module). One helper so the plain-call
## and the GENERIC-call resolvers order candidates by exactly the same rule.
callee_mod_rank := fn(src : ptr(u8), as_ : usize, al : usize, ms : usize, ml : usize) -> i64 {
  if mod_head_matches(src, as_, al, ms, ml) { return i64(ml) + 1 }
  mod_anc_rank_from(src, as_, al, ms, ml)
}

## Find a generic kind-1 fn `Decl` whose name matches the callee's bare fn name; -1 if none /
## not generic. A CROSS-MODULE qualified callee `mod::f` matches a generic `f` by its TAIL name
## (`callee_name_span` strips the `mod::`), so a generic stdlib fn called across modules
## (`alloc::vec::push(T, …)`) is monomorphized like a same-module generic call — the instance
## label `<callee-module>__<fn>__<typetag>` is produced consistently at both the definition
## (the decl's own module) and the call site (`emit_mangled_call` splits the head module).
generic_decl_of := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), cs : usize, cl : usize, ms : usize, ml : usize, nargs : usize, args_head : ptr(mut Arg), a : rt::Arena) -> i64 {
  cnt := rt::vec_len(deref(decls))
  nm := callee_name_span(src, cs, cl)
  ## SAME-NAME disambiguation: if the DECL of this tail name in the RESOLVED module is NON-generic,
  ## the call resolves to THAT (not a cross-module generic of the same name) → NOT a generic call →
  ## -1. Fixes `alloc::map`'s NON-generic `get(m,key)` being conflated with `base/alloc`'s GENERIC
  ## `get(T,a,h)`. The resolved module is: the QUALIFIED callee's HEAD (`alloc::map` of
  ## `alloc::map::get`, compared segment-aware since decls store the MANGLED `alloc__map`), else the
  ## CALLER's module (`ms`/`ml`, a bare call). ONLY a NON-generic hit short-circuits; a GENERIC hit
  ## (every module's own `node_ptr`) falls through to the by-tail match, preserving last-match
  ## resolution → `src/`'s per-module generics are byte-identical (fixpoint-safe).
  cpq := colon_pos(src, cs, cl)
  mut pms := ms
  mut pml := ml
  mut segcmp := false
  if cpq >= 0 { pms = cs; pml = usize(cpq); segcmp = true }
  th := name_hash(src, nm.s, nm.n)
  if pml != 0 {
    mut j0 := dni_lo(cnt, th)
    je0 := dni_hi(cnt, th)
    while j0 < je0 {
      i := dni_at(cnt, j0)
      j0 += 1
      if DNH == 0 or DNH_N != cnt or rt::rec_get(unchecked bitcast(ptr(mut u8), DNH), i) == th {
      d := deref(decl_get(decls, i))
      if (d.kind == 1 or d.kind == 4) and d.is_generic == false and streq(src, d.name_start, d.name_len, nm.s, nm.n) {
        mut mm := false
        if segcmp { mm = mod_head_matches(src, d.mod_start, d.mod_len, pms, pml) } else { mm = streq(src, d.mod_start, d.mod_len, pms, pml) }
        ## A QUALIFIED call (`alloc::map::get`) short-circuits to the named non-generic authoritatively.
        ## A BARE same-module call only short-circuits when the non-generic decl's ARITY matches — so
        ## `derive`'s recursive struct `hash(f.type, v.(f))` (2 args) is NOT captured by the concrete
        ## `hash(str)` overload (arity 1) in the same module → it stays generic (mangles `hash__u64`).
        if mm and (segcmp or d.arity == nargs) { return -1 }
      }
      }
    }
  }
  ## Among same-tail-name GENERICS, PREFER (in order): (1) a QUALIFIED call's MODULE + arity match
  ## (`base::result::map` → `base__result`'s `map`, not `alloc__vec`'s same-arity `map`); (2) an arity
  ## match (`option::map`/4 vs `vec::map`/5 for a bare call); (3) the last-declared (the original
  ## behavior). A bare call never sets `res_mod` (segcmp false) → falls to arity/last, and a
  ## single-arity name has `res_arity == res` → byte-identical for `src/`'s per-module generics.
  ## `ltac` = the number of leading type-NAME args the call spells; a same-arity candidate whose
  ## type-param COUNT equals it is preferred (routes a bare `map(u64,u64,u64,…)` to `result::map`/3
  ## over the same-arity `vec::map`/2). 0 = the call spells no leading types (an implicit-type-arg or
  ## all-value call) → the ntpc tie-break is skipped so those paths are unchanged.
  ## Modules §3 for a BARE generic call: rank the same-name generics by the caller's own module and
  ## then its ANCESTOR chain, nearest first, and let ONLY the nearest nameable module's candidates
  ## take part in the tie-breaks below. Without it the last tier was the LAST-declared candidate, so
  ## a bare generic call bound to whichever module happened to come last in declaration order — a
  ## wrong value, measured: `geo::child` calling bare `gid` emitted `call zother__gid__u64` and the
  ## program returned 22 instead of 42. `bestr < 0` means no candidate is nameable from here (an
  ## alias, a listed projection, a lib copy whose module is not recoverable here) — the historical
  ## last-match behavior then stands verbatim, which is the leniency the whole compiler leans on.
  mut bestr := 0 - 1
  if segcmp == false {
    mut jr := dni_lo(cnt, th)
    jre := dni_hi(cnt, th)
    while jr < jre {
      i := dni_at(cnt, jr)
      jr += 1
      if DNH == 0 or DNH_N != cnt or rt::rec_get(unchecked bitcast(ptr(mut u8), DNH), i) == th {
        d := deref(decl_get(decls, i))
        if d.kind == 1 and d.is_generic and streq(src, d.name_start, d.name_len, nm.s, nm.n) {
          r := callee_mod_rank(src, d.mod_start, d.mod_len, ms, ml)
          if r > bestr { bestr = r }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  ## §4.1.1 — nothing on the ancestor chain, so honour what the calling module SAID it means
  ## (`(… node_ptr …) := lower_ctx`, `f := M::g`). Only consulted on the path that used to guess.
  mut bms := 0
  mut bml := 0
  if segcmp == false and bestr < 0 {
    bh := binding_head_span(decls, src, nm.s, nm.n, ms, ml)
    bms = bh.s
    bml = bh.n
  }
  ltac := leading_type_arg_count(args_head, decls, src, a)
  mut res := -1
  mut res_arity := 0 - 1
  mut res_ntpc := 0 - 1
  mut res_mod := 0 - 1
  mut j := dni_lo(cnt, th)
  je := dni_hi(cnt, th)
  while j < je {
    i := dni_at(cnt, j)
    j += 1
    if DNH == 0 or DNH_N != cnt or rt::rec_get(unchecked bitcast(ptr(mut u8), DNH), i) == th {
    d := deref(decl_get(decls, i))
    if d.kind == 1 and d.is_generic and streq(src, d.name_start, d.name_len, nm.s, nm.n)
       and (bestr < 0 or callee_mod_rank(src, d.mod_start, d.mod_len, ms, ml) == bestr)
       and (bml == 0 or mod_head_matches(src, d.mod_start, d.mod_len, bms, bml)) {
      res = i64(i)
      if d.arity == nargs {
        res_arity = i64(i)
        if ltac != 0 and tparam_count(decls, i64(i), src, a) == ltac { res_ntpc = i64(i) }
        if segcmp and mod_head_matches(src, d.mod_start, d.mod_len, pms, pml) { res_mod = i64(i) }
      }
    }
    }
  }
  if res_mod >= 0 { return res_mod }
  if res_ntpc >= 0 { return res_ntpc }
  if res_arity >= 0 { return res_arity }
  res
}

## The base type name of a decl's FIRST value (non-`type`) parameter — a UFCS/method call's `self`
## receiver. 0/0 for a non-fn, no value param, or `gi < 0`. Drives receiver-typed selection among
## same-name cross-module generics (spec FN-7: "UFCS method dispatch is resolution keyed on the receiver").
self_param_base := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), gi : i64, a : rt::Arena) -> CSpan {
  if gi < 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(gi))))
  mut p := d.params_head
  while p != 0 {
    pm := deref(param_p(p))
    if str_at((src + pm.ts), pm.tl) == "type" { p = pm.next }
    else { return base_type_name(src, pm.ts, pm.tl) }
  }
  CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
}
## Infer the concrete type-arg span for the `which`-th (0-based) type-param of an IMPLICIT-UFCS
## generic call from the RECEIVER's concrete type. `Result(u64, u64)` receiver + `self : Result(T, E)`
## → param 0 (`T`) = `u64`, param 1 (`E`) = `u64`. Locates the type-param name in the callee's `self`
## param type-arg list, then reads the receiver type's type-arg at that SAME position — yielding the
## REAL concrete tag (so the instance is `base__result__unwrap__u64__u64`, the proven explicit-call
## instance) rather than a bogus receiver-name tag. 0/0 when it can't be located. `recv_ts/recv_tl` =
## the receiver's full type text (`Result(u64, u64)`).
implicit_targ_from_recv := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), gi : i64, recv_ts : usize, recv_tl : usize, which : usize, a : rt::Arena) -> CSpan {
  if gi < 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(gi))))
  mut p := d.params_head
  mut tpi := 0
  mut tpn_s := 0
  mut tpn_l := 0
  mut found_tp := false
  while p != 0 {
    pm := deref(param_p(p))
    if str_at((src + pm.ts), pm.tl) == "type" {
      if tpi == which { tpn_s = pm.ns; tpn_l = pm.nl; found_tp = true }
      tpi += 1
    }
    p = pm.next
  }
  if found_tp == false { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  p = d.params_head
  while p != 0 {
    pm := deref(param_p(p))
    if str_at((src + pm.ts), pm.tl) == "type" { p = pm.next }
    else {
      if param_type_has_tparam(src, pm.ts, pm.tl, tpn_s, tpn_l) {
        sbn := base_type_name(src, pm.ts, pm.tl)
        mut k := 0
        mut pos := 0 - 1
        mut go := true
        while go {
          pta := typearg_at(src, sbn.s, sbn.n, usize(k))
          if pta.n == 0 { go = false }
          else { if streq(src, pta.s, pta.n, tpn_s, tpn_l) { pos = i64(k); go = false } ; k += 1 }
        }
        if pos >= 0 {
          rbn := base_type_name(src, recv_ts, recv_tl)
          return typearg_at(src, rbn.s, rbn.n, usize(pos))
        }
      }
      p = pm.next
    }
  }
  CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
}
## Recover the ONE type-parameter of an implicit-UFCS `map`/`and_then`/`map_err` (`U`/`F`) that is NOT
## present in the receiver's type — it comes from the MAPPER fn ARGUMENT's declared return. The parser
## discards a fn-value PARAM's return, so read the ARGUMENT fn's return instead: the fn arg is the LAST
## value arg (`self, f`); look it up by name in the decl table and read its `ret_ts`/`ret_tl`. When that
## return's BASE type equals the receiver's base (the `and_then` shape `f : fn(T) -> Result(U, E)` — the
## fn returns the SAME generic as the receiver), the type-param is the return's FIRST type-arg; otherwise
## (`map`/`map_err`'s `f : fn(T) -> U`) it IS the whole return span. Only a NAMED fn arg is recovered (a
## lambda has no decl entry) → 0/0 otherwise (caller keeps its fail-loud). `src/` spells its type-args →
## never reached → fixpoint-neutral.
infer_map_targ3 := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), args_head : ptr(mut Arg), nargs : usize, rcv_bs : usize, rcv_bn : usize, a : rt::Arena) -> CSpan {
  if nargs == 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  fe := arg_expr_at(args_head, nargs - 1, a)
  fv := var_name_span(fe)
  if fv.n == 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  nm := last_seg(src, fv.s, fv.n)
  cnt := rt::vec_len(deref(decls))
  mut rs := 0
  mut rn := 0
  for i in 0..cnt {
    d := deref(decl_get(decls, i))
    if d.is_fn and d.ret_tl != 0 and streq(src, d.name_start, d.name_len, nm.s, nm.n) { rs = d.ret_ts; rn = d.ret_tl }
  }
  if rn == 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  rb := base_type_name(src, rs, rn)
  if streq(src, rb.s, rb.n, rcv_bs, rcv_bn) {
    ta := typearg_at(src, rs, rn, 0)
    return CSpan(s = ta.s, n = ta.n)
  }
  CSpan(s = rs, n = rn)
}
## Re-select a generic `gi` (resolved by name/arity/last-declared) to the same-name generic whose
## `self` (first value param) base type matches the receiver's base type `rbs/rbl` — spec FN-7's
## receiver-keyed UFCS/argument-type resolution. NEUTRAL by construction: fires ONLY when the
## already-chosen decl's self type does NOT match the receiver yet another same-name candidate's does,
## so every currently-correct call (incl. per-module duplicate generics whose self types are identical
## — `node_ptr`/`decl_at`) is untouched. Fixes a bare/UFCS `Result` method (`r.unwrap()`) resolving to
## the same-name `Option` method (which was simply the last-declared). Candidate arity must be viable
## for the call: an exact arity match (explicit type args) OR the implicit-UFCS shape `arity - ntpc`.
recv_redirect_generic := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), gi : i64, nargs : usize, rbs : usize, rbl : usize, a : rt::Arena) -> i64 {
  if gi < 0 { return gi }
  if rbl == 0 { return gi }
  csb := self_param_base(decls, src, gi, a)
  if streq(src, csb.s, csb.n, rbs, rbl) { return gi }
  gd := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(gi))))
  cnt := rt::vec_len(deref(decls))
  mut best := gi
  for i in 0..cnt {
    d := deref(decl_get(decls, i))
    if d.kind == 1 and d.is_generic and streq(src, d.name_start, d.name_len, gd.name_start, gd.name_len) {
      sb := self_param_base(decls, src, i64(i), a)
      if sb.n != 0 and streq(src, sb.s, sb.n, rbs, rbl) {
        ntpc := tparam_count(decls, i64(i), src, a)
        if d.arity == nargs or (d.arity >= ntpc and d.arity - ntpc == nargs) { best = i64(i) }
      }
    }
  }
  best
}

## The source-parameter INDEX of a generic fn's comptime TYPE parameter (`x : type`). Scans the
## decl's `Param` list for the one whose type annotation is the literal `type`; returns its 0-based
## position (0 if none found — the common leading-`T : type` case). Generalizes the old hard-coded
## "the type parameter is param 0": `allocate := fn(in out self, T : type, …)` has it at index 1.
tparam_idx := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), gi : i64, src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> usize {
  if gi < 0 { return 0 }
  d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(gi))))
  mut p := d.params_head
  mut i := 0
  mut res := 0
  mut found := false
  while p != 0 {
    pm := deref(param_p(p))
    if found == false and str_at((src + pm.ts), pm.tl) == "type" { res = i; found = true }
    i += 1
    p = pm.next
  }
  res
}

## How many comptime `: type` parameters a generic decl has (1 for `id(T,…)`, 2 for
## `map(T, U, …)` / `HashMap(K, V, …)`). Drives the two-type-arg mono machinery: a count of 2
## erases + binds + mangles BOTH type-args.
tparam_count := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), gi : i64, src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> usize {
  if gi < 0 { return 0 }
  d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(gi))))
  mut p := d.params_head
  mut c := 0
  while p != 0 {
    pm := deref(param_p(p))
    if str_at((src + pm.ts), pm.tl) == "type" { c = c + 1 }
    p = pm.next
  }
  c
}

## The SOURCE-PARAM index of the SECOND `: type` parameter (0-based); returns 0 if the decl has
## fewer than two. For `map(T, U : type, …)` this is 1 (T at 0, U at 1).
tparam_idx2 := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), gi : i64, src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> usize {
  if gi < 0 { return 0 }
  d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(gi))))
  mut p := d.params_head
  mut i := 0
  mut seen := 0
  mut res := 0
  while p != 0 {
    pm := deref(param_p(p))
    if str_at((src + pm.ts), pm.tl) == "type" {
      seen += 1
      if seen == 2 { res = i }
    }
    i += 1
    p = pm.next
  }
  res
}
## The source-param INDEX of a generic fn's THIRD comptime `: type` parameter (`Result::map`'s `U` in
## `map(T, E, U, …)`). Mirrors `tparam_idx2` (the 3rd `: type` param). 0 if fewer than 3.
tparam_idx3 := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), gi : i64, src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> usize {
  if gi < 0 { return 0 }
  d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(gi))))
  mut p := d.params_head
  mut i := 0
  mut seen := 0
  mut res := 0
  while p != 0 {
    pm := deref(param_p(p))
    if str_at((src + pm.ts), pm.tl) == "type" {
      seen += 1
      if seen == 3 { res = i }
    }
    i += 1
    p = pm.next
  }
  res
}

## The TYPE-ARGUMENT lexeme span of a generic call's arg at source index `ti` (an ident naming the
## concrete type, e.g. `u64`); 0/0 if that arg is neither a bare `Var` nor a generic-instance type
## reference. NOTE: the returned span may be a SPURIOUS fn-name (a value `Call` sitting in an
## inferred type-param position, `allocate(…).expect(…)`); callers gate that out with `is_fn_name`
## (a legitimate type argument never names a function). Kept arena-by-value + no `decls`/`src` so the
## arg count stays within the 6 SysV registers (adding them corrupts the arena).
type_arg_at := fn(args_head : ptr(mut Arg), ti : usize, a : rt::Arena) -> CSpan {
  e := arg_expr_at(args_head, ti, a)
  if unchecked bitcast(usize, e) == 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  dv := deref_var_info(e)
  if dv.is_v { return CSpan(s = dv.s, n = dv.n) }
  ## A GENERIC-INSTANCE type argument (`display(Option(u64), …)`): the type-arg parses as a
  ## `Call("Option", [u64])` (no `.Variant` → not an enum-ctor; no second `(` → not a generic-inst;
  ## falls into the ordinary call path). Return the callee's BARE HEAD span (`Option`) — positioned
  ## in source right before its `(u64)`, so `typearg_at`/`variant_payload_type`/`enum_decl_of` re-read
  ## the `(…)` args from source AND the mangled label stays paren-free.
  ci := call_callee_span(e)
  if ci.n != 0 { return CSpan(s = ci.s, n = ci.n) }
  return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
}

## The parts of a `Call` expr (callee span + arg-list head), via a ptr-PARAM match (a `match deref`
## on a LOCAL `ptr(Expr)` degenerates — the ek-0 landmine — so callers pass the expr here).
CallParts := struct { is_call : bool, cs : usize, cl : usize, na : usize, ah : ptr(mut Arg) }
call_parts := fn(p : ptr(Expr)) -> CallParts {
  match deref(p) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, na, ah) => { CallParts(is_call = true, cs = cs, cl = cl, na = na, ah = ah) }
    _ => { CallParts(is_call = false, cs = 0, cl = 0, na = 0, ah = 0) }
  }
}
## The callee (head) name span of a `Call` expr, else 0/0.
call_callee_span := fn(p : ptr(Expr)) -> CSpan {
  match deref(p) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, na, ah) => { CSpan(s = cs, n = cl) }
    _ => { CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  }
}

## The SOURCE SPAN of a GENERIC-INSTANCE type argument — `Slice(u8)`, `Option(u64)`, `Vec(Pair)`,
## `Result(u64, E)` — written where a TYPE is expected. Such a type expression parses as an
## `Expr::Call(head, args)`, and every layout query keys on the FULL `Head(Args…)` text
## (`base_type_name` strips the `(…)` for the decl lookup while `typearg_at`/`subst_field_ty`/
## `enum_inst_words` re-read the args to substitute), so recover `[head … ')']` inclusive.
## `{0,0}` when `e` is not a call-shaped type expression.
##
## WITHOUT this, `size(Slice(u8))` / `size(Option(u64))` had no type name at all: `var_name_span`
## answered `{0,0}` and the fold fell through to the SCALAR default 8 — a silent wrong value for a
## 16-byte view pair (§7) and a 16-byte tag+payload `Option(u64)` (§6.2). The bound on both scans is
## deliberate: a malformed span returns `{0,0}` (the caller then fails LOUD) rather than running away.
generic_inst_typearg_span := fn(e : ptr(Expr), src : ptr(u8)) -> CSpan {
  if unchecked bitcast(usize, e) == 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, na, ah) => {
      if na == 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
      if cl == 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
      ## the head's `(` — only whitespace may sit between the callee lexeme and it.
      mut i := cs + cl
      mut op := 0
      mut lim := cs + cl + 8
      while op == 0 and i < lim {
        c := str_at((src + i), 1)
        if c == "(" { op = i }
        else if c == " " or c == "\n" or c == "\t" or c == "\r" { i = i + 1 }
        else { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
      }
      if op == 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
      ## forward at paren depth to the matching `)`.
      mut depth := 0
      mut j := op
      mut cp := 0
      mut lim2 := op + 4096
      while cp == 0 and j < lim2 {
        c := str_at((src + j), 1)
        if c == "(" { depth = depth + 1 }
        else if c == ")" {
          depth = depth - 1
          if depth == 0 { cp = j }
        }
        if cp == 0 { j = j + 1 }
      }
      if cp == 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
      return CSpan(s = cs, n = cp + 1 - cs)
    }
    _ => { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  }
}

## The leftmost SOURCE position of a type-expression `e` (a tuple type-arg's element) — the start
## of its `Var` lexeme or `Call` head. Used to anchor the backward scan to a tuple type-arg's `(`.
tuple_first_start := fn(e : ptr(Expr)) -> usize {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Var(s, n) => { s }
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, na, ah) => { cs }
    _ => { 0 }
  }
}

## A TUPLE type-argument `(T0, T1, …)` used where a type is expected (`display((u64, u64), t, sb)`)
## parses as an `ArrayLit` (the tuple-literal node, §parser). The mono machinery keys instances on a
## SOURCE SPAN, so recover the `(…)` span: anchor on the FIRST element's start, scan back to the
## opening `(`, then forward at paren depth to the matching `)`. Returns `[open, close]`; 0/0 if `e`
## is not an `ArrayLit`. `typearg_at(src, span.s, 0, j)` then reads the j-th component off this span
## (the `(` sits at position 0), and `emit_type_arg_tag` mangles it to a paren-free `Tuple_…` symbol.
tuple_typearg_span := fn(e : ptr(Expr), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> CSpan {
  ## Defensive: a nullary call whose resolved generic index has a type-param position beyond the (empty)
  ## argument list hands us a NULL Expr here — never deref it (a compiler-crash guard; the real fix keeps
  ## such calls non-generic in `nongen_type_match`, so this is belt-and-suspenders and never fires on `src/`).
  if unchecked bitcast(usize, e) == 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::ArrayLit(nel, ehead) => {
      e0 := arg_expr_at(ehead, 0, a)
      if unchecked bitcast(usize, e0) == 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
      s0 := tuple_first_start(e0)
      if s0 == 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
      ## scan back from the first element to the opening bracket — `(` (TUPLE type `(T0, …)`) or
      ## `[` (ARRAY type `[T; N]`). Both parse as an ArrayLit; the bracket kind decides the mangling.
      mut op := s0
      mut back := true
      mut is_br := false
      while back and op > 0 {
        op = op - 1
        c := str_at((src + op), 1)
        if c == "(" { is_br = false; back = false }
        else if c == "[" { is_br = true; back = false }
      }
      ## scan forward at bracket depth to the matching close (`)` for a tuple, `]` for an array).
      mut depth := 0
      mut i := op
      mut cl := op
      mut fwd := true
      while fwd {
        c := str_at((src + i), 1)
        mut isopen := false
        mut isclose := false
        if is_br {
          if c == "[" { isopen = true } else if c == "]" { isclose = true }
        } else {
          if c == "(" { isopen = true } else if c == ")" { isclose = true }
        }
        if isopen { depth = depth + 1 }
        else if isclose {
          depth = depth - 1
          if depth == 0 { cl = i; fwd = false }
        }
        i += 1
      }
      return CSpan(s = op, n = cl - op + 1)
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
}

## Does the name span `[s, s+n)` denote a FUNCTION declaration (a `fn`, kind 1/4/5) — as opposed to a
## type (struct/enum) or a built-in scalar (no decl)? Used to reject a spurious type-argument that is
## really a value call's callee (`allocate` from `allocate(…).expect(…)`): a legitimate type argument
## (`u64`, `Option`, a user struct) never names a fn, so dropping fn-named spans is safe + precise.
## The LAST `::`-separated segment of `[s, s+n)` (`alloc::hashmap::hashmap_insert` → `hashmap_insert`);
## the span unchanged when unqualified. `name_tail` strips only the FIRST segment, so a 3-segment
## qualified callee needs this to match a bare decl name.
last_seg := fn(src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize) -> CSpan {
  mut start := 0
  mut i := 0
  while i + 1 < n {
    if str_at((src + s + i), 1) == ":" and str_at((src + s + i + 1), 1) == ":" { start = i + 2 }
    i += 1
  }
  CSpan(s = s + start, n = n - start)
}

is_fn_name := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize) -> bool {
  if n == 0 { return false }
  nm := last_seg(src, s, n)
  cnt := rt::vec_len(deref(decls))
  mut res := false
  for i in 0..cnt {
    d := deref(decl_get(decls, i))
    if d.is_fn and streq(src, d.name_start, d.name_len, nm.s, nm.n) { res = true }
  }
  res
}

## The TYPE-ARGUMENT lexeme span of a generic call's arg 0 (an ident naming the concrete
## type, e.g. `u64`). Returns its source span as an `NSpan` (start, len); 0/0 if arg 0 is not
## a bare `Var` (a well-formed generic call always passes a type ident first).
type_arg_span := fn(args_head : ptr(mut Arg), a : rt::Arena) -> CSpan {
  e := arg_expr_at(args_head, 0, a)
  if unchecked bitcast(usize, e) == 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  ## extract the type-arg ident's span via `deref_var_info` (a function-body `match deref(p)` over a
  ## pointer PARAM, bound ek 6 = ptr-to-enum, so the dispatch resolves) — NOT an inline `match
  ## deref(e)` over the LOCAL `e`: `e := arg_expr_at(…)` returns `ptr(Expr)` but `collect_slots` has
  ## no ptr-to-ENUM-call binding, so `e` got ek 0 (scalar) and the inline match degenerated to a
  ## non-enum dispatch → empty span → every generic call/instance mangled `<fn>__` (no type tag),
  ## collapsing the distinct monomorphizations (`node_ptr__Stmt`/…__Expr) into one and diverging
  ## from a.out's `<fn>__<Type>`. The seed lower reads `svec_at` etc. via this; the bug gated §1.
  dv := deref_var_info(e)
  if dv.is_v { return CSpan(s = dv.s, n = dv.n) }
  return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
}

## Walk the AST post-order, emitting x86_64 GAS for a stack machine into `sb`. The result
## of every expression is left **on the stack** (the stack-machine model). `cx` is the
## per-function context (source base + name→slot map); the arena `a` resolves `Match` arm
## handles; `nl` is the fresh-label counter, threaded `in out` so nested `if`/`match`
## allocate distinct labels.
##
## - `Num` loads the immediate and pushes it.
## - `Var` reads its frame slot (`movq -<off>(%rbp), %rax ; pushq %rax`).
## - `Bin` lowers both children, pops them into `%rax`/`%rbx`, applies the operator, and
##   pushes the result. A comparison (20/24/25/26/27/28) is materialized as a 0/1 boolean
##   in `%rax` via `cmpq %rbx, %rax` + the matching `setcc` + `movzbq`.
## - `If` lowers the condition, pops it, branches on zero to an else-label, lowers the
##   `then` (pushed), jumps to an end-label, then under the else-label lowers `els`
##   (pushed); both arms leave their value on the stack at the end-label.
## - `Match` lowers the scrutinee into a register kept on the stack; for each arm it
##   compares the scrutinee to the arm's literal (a wildcard always matches) and jumps to
##   that arm's body label; each body lowers (leaving its value on the stack) then jumps to
##   a shared end-label. First-match-wins.
## A scalar CONVERSION callee, by name: -1 = not a conversion, 0 = to-INTEGER (`usize`/`u64`/…/`i64`),
## 1 = to-FLOAT (`f64`/`f32`). Integer→integer lowers to IDENTITY (the bits already live in a 64-bit
## word, `unchecked`); a float instruction is emitted only when the source OR target is a float.
conv_kind := fn(name : str) -> i64 {
  if name == "f64" or name == "f32" { return 1 }
  if name == "usize" or name == "isize" or name == "u8" or name == "u16" or name == "u32" or name == "u64" or name == "i8" or name == "i16" or name == "i32" or name == "i64" { return 0 }
  return -1
}
## Is a to-integer conversion `name(x)` NARROWING — a sub-native width (u8/u16/u32/i8/i16/i32)? The
## native widths (u64/i64/usize/isize) are word-wide, so their conversion is identity.
is_narrowing_conv := fn(name : str) -> bool {
  name == "u8" or name == "u16" or name == "u32" or name == "i8" or name == "i16" or name == "i32"
}
## Emit the width-narrowing of the integer value in %rax for `name(x)`: ZERO-extend the low N bits for
## an unsigned `uN`, SIGN-extend for a signed `iN` — the wrap/truncation semantics (`u8(810)` → 42,
## `i8(200)` → -56). Native widths emit nothing (the bits already fill the word).
emit_int_narrow_reg := fn(in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, name : str) {
  if name == "u8" { push_str(sb, "  movzbq %al, %rax\n") }
  else if name == "u16" { push_str(sb, "  movzwq %ax, %rax\n") }
  else if name == "u32" { push_str(sb, "  movl %eax, %eax\n") }
  else if name == "i8" { push_str(sb, "  movsbq %al, %rax\n") }
  else if name == "i16" { push_str(sb, "  movswq %ax, %rax\n") }
  else if name == "i32" { push_str(sb, "  movslq %eax, %rax\n") }
}

emit_shift_width_guard := fn(e : ptr(Expr), in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, cx : ptr(LCtx)) {
  t := expr_type_span(e, cx)
  if t.n == 0 {
    push_str(sb, "  cmpq $64, %rbx\n  jb 1f\n  ud2\n1:\n")
    return
  }
  bn := base_type_name(cx.src, t.s, t.n)
  nm := str_at((cx.src + bn.s), bn.n)
  mut done := false
  if nm == "u8" or nm == "i8" { push_str(sb, "  cmpq $8, %rbx\n  jb 1f\n  ud2\n1:\n"); done = true }
  if nm == "u16" or nm == "i16" { push_str(sb, "  cmpq $16, %rbx\n  jb 1f\n  ud2\n1:\n"); done = true }
  if nm == "u32" or nm == "i32" { push_str(sb, "  cmpq $32, %rbx\n  jb 1f\n  ud2\n1:\n"); done = true }
  if done == false { push_str(sb, "  cmpq $64, %rbx\n  jb 1f\n  ud2\n1:\n") }
}

emit_shift_narrow_result := fn(e : ptr(Expr), in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, cx : ptr(LCtx)) {
  t := expr_type_span(e, cx)
  if t.n != 0 {
    bn := base_type_name(cx.src, t.s, t.n)
    nm := str_at((cx.src + bn.s), bn.n)
    if is_narrowing_conv(nm) { emit_int_narrow_reg(sb, nm) }
  }
}

## True if the type named `[ts, ts+tl)` is a floating-point scalar — `f64`/`f32` directly, or a USER
## BRAND whose underlying is `f64`/`f32` (`F := brand(f64)`). So a float-underlying brand param/local
## is tagged `ek == 9` (the xmm path) like a native float — otherwise `display`'s `f64(v)` peel on a
## brand value mis-lowers as `cvtsi2sd` (int→float CONVERSION of the raw bits).
type_is_float := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), ts : usize, tl : usize) -> bool {
  nm := str_at((src + ts), tl)
  if nm == "f64" or nm == "f32" { return true }
  bu := brand_underlying(decls, src, ts, tl)
  if bu.n != 0 {
    un := str_at((src + bu.s), bu.n)
    return un == "f64" or un == "f32"
  }
  false
}

## Does the expression yield an `f64` VALUE (so its arithmetic / a surrounding conversion must use the
## xmm path)? A float literal; a `Var` whose slot is a float local (`ek == 9`); float ARITHMETIC
## (`+`/`-`/`*`/`/` over a float operand); or an `f64(...)`/`f32(...)` conversion. An integer cast
## (`u64(...)` etc.) yields an INTEGER, so it is NOT float even over a float argument.
is_float_expr := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> bool {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::FloatLit(fs, fl) => { true }
    Expr::Var(s, n) => {
      if slot_of(cx.slots, cx.src, s, n) < 0 {
        ## a float-valued mutable GLOBAL (no frame slot) reads/arithmetics on the float (xmm) path.
        is_mut_float_global(cx.decls, cx.src, s, n)
      } else {
        ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, s, n)))
        ent.ek == 9
      }
    }
    Expr::Bin(op, l, r) => {
      mut r2 := false
      if is_float_bin_op(op) { r2 = is_float_expr(l, cx) or is_float_expr(r, cx) }
      r2
    }
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, na, ah) => {
      mut cn := str_at((cx.src + cs), cl)
      mut rc := cn == "f64" or cn == "f32"
      if not rc { rc = float_ret_call(e, cx.decls, cx.src) }
      ## FN-6 — an INDIRECT call through a fn VALUE (`h(10.5)`, `h` a local/param code pointer): the
      ## callee has no `Decl`, so `float_ret_call` cannot see its return type. Recover it from the fn
      ## value's declared type / bound target (`fnval_call_ret_float`) — else a surrounding `u64(…)`
      ## read the f64 BITS as an integer (the silent 0). Gated on the callee naming a real frame SLOT,
      ## so an ordinary named call keeps the byte-identical path (`src/` has no fn-value call).
      if not rc and slot_of(cx.slots, cx.src, cs, cl) >= 0 { rc = fnval_call_ret_float(cs, cl, cx) }
      ## FN-10 dual — `o.f(args)` through a fn-VALUE STRUCT FIELD (the parser's UFCS desugar puts the
      ## receiver at arg 0), whose field type spells the return.
      if not rc and na >= 1 { rc = fn_field_call_ret_float(arg_expr_at(ah, 0, arena_of(cx)), cs, cl, cx) }
      rc
    }
    ## a struct FIELD whose declared type is `f64`/`f32` (the base is a struct local `ek == 2` or a
    ## ptr-to-struct `ek == 7`; both carry the struct type span in `sns`/`snl`).
    Expr::Field(base, fs, fl) => {
      bv := var_name_span(base)
      mut rf := false
      if bv.n != 0 {
        se := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, bv.s, bv.n)))
        if se.ek == 2 or se.ek == 7 {
          fsp := field_type_span(cx.decls, cx.src, se.sns, se.snl, fs, fl, deref(cx.mar))
          mut tn := str_at((cx.src + fsp.s), fsp.n)
          rf = tn == "f64" or tn == "f32"
        }
      }
      rf
    }
    ## an ARRAY element `xs[i]` whose element kind is float (`ek == 5` array slot, `eek == 9`).
    Expr::Index(base, idx) => {
      bv := var_name_span(base)
      mut ri := false
      if bv.n != 0 {
        se := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, bv.s, bv.n)))
        if se.ek == 5 and se.eek == 9 { ri = true }
        ## MIXED-KIND tuple element `t.N` (`t : (u64, f64)`): the component's float-ness lives in its
        ## per-component `tcomps` entry (the base slot's uniform `eek` is not 9), keyed by (base off, N).
        ## Consult it for a numeric index so the f64 component reads + arithmetics on the xmm path
        ## (`addsd`), not the integer `addq` over its raw bits. A uniform float tuple/array has no
        ## tcomps entry (handled above); a non-float component's entry has `eek != 9` → stays integer.
        if not ri and se.ek == 5 and expr_is_numlit(idx) {
          tc := tcomp_find(cx, se.off, usize(num_lit_value(idx)))
          if tc.estride != 0 and tc.eek == 9 { ri = true }
        }
      }
      ri
    }
    _ => { false }
  }
}

## Is expression `e` of a SIGNED integer type (`i8`/`i16`/`i32`/`i64`/`isize`)? Used to pick signed
## (`idivq`) vs unsigned (`divq`) division/modulo. An UNKNOWN type (a bare local whose `: T` annotation
## the lean parser discards, an untyped literal) is treated as UNSIGNED — Alatyr's scalar default —
## so `h % cap` over `u64`/`usize` operands (e.g. a hash reduction with the high bit set) uses the
## UNSIGNED `divq`, not the signed `idivq` that read the huge value as negative and returned a negative
## remainder (an out-of-range index → OOB slot read). Signed division stays `idivq` for real `iN` operands.
## Is `e` the literal `0`? A `Num(0)` LEFT operand makes `0 - x` / `0 + x` / `0 * x` a NEGATION or
## identity — never an overflow — so the checked guard (I11 / CG-8) is skipped for it. This is what
## makes the pervasive `0 - N` negative/sentinel idiom (`mut off := 0 - 1`) not trap under the `-` guard.
expr_is_zero := fn(e : ptr(Expr)) -> bool {
  mut r := false
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Num(v, s, n) => { if v == 0 { r = true } }
    _ => {}
  }
  r
}
## Is `e` a POSITIVE integer literal? A division BY such a literal can neither divide by zero nor be
## the signed `MIN / -1` overflow, so both checked division guards (CG-13, below) are PROVABLY
## unnecessary and are elided — the optimization CG-13 explicitly sanctions ("a semantics-preserving
## optimization may still eliminate a provably unnecessary guard — e.g. a divisor proven non-zero").
## `v > 0`, NOT `v != 0`: a 64-bit hex literal such as `0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF` parses to the i64 `-1`, and
## a negative literal must KEEP the `MIN / -1` guard. A literal `0`, a `0 - N` negation (a `Bin`, not a
## `Num`) and every non-literal divisor are not provable here and keep both guards.
expr_is_pos_num := fn(e : ptr(Expr)) -> bool {
  mut r := false
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Num(v, s, n) => { if v > 0 { r = true } }
    _ => {}
  }
  r
}
## Is `e` a UNARY NEGATION `-x`? The parser desugars unary `-x` to `Unchecked(Bin(17, Num(0), x))`
## (a 2's-complement wrapping `0 - x`), so a NEGATED VALUE used as an operand of an enclosing `+`/`-`
## (`30 + -a`, `10 + -3`) rides in as this exact shape. Its runtime word is a large unsigned pattern
## (`-5` = `2^64-5`), so an UNSIGNED overflow guard (`jnc`) on the enclosing add sees a spurious CARRY
## and traps (`ud2`) even though the SIGNED sum (`30 + (-5) = 25`) fits with no signed overflow. The
## caller uses this to pick the SIGNED guard (`jno`) for a `+`/`-` whose operand is a negation, so
## `30 + -a` yields 25 (wrapping subtraction) instead of a spurious checked-overflow trap.
## Does `inner` have the `Bin(17, Num(0), _)` shape of a unary-minus body? A SINGLE-match helper —
## keeping this OUT of `expr_is_uneg`'s `Unchecked` arm avoids the seed's nested-`match`-on-a-bound-
## child miscompile (a `match deref(child)` inside another match arm mis-lowers on the frozen seed).
uneg_body_ok := fn(inner : ptr(Expr)) -> bool {
  mut r := false
  match deref(inner) {
    Expr::Bin(op, l, rr) => { if op == 17 { r = expr_is_zero(l) } }
    _ => {}
  }
  r
}
expr_is_uneg := fn(e : ptr(Expr)) -> bool {
  mut r := false
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Unchecked(inner) => { r = uneg_body_ok(inner) }
    _ => {}
  }
  r
}
## Recover the declared array type of `Field(Var, field)` for the indexed-field signedness lane. Keep
## the variant destructure in a standalone one-level helper: the frozen seed mis-lowers a nested match
## over `Expr::Field` when it is placed inside another expression match arm.
index_field_array_type_span := fn(base : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> CSpan {
  mut res := CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
  match deref(base) {
    Expr::Field(owner, fs, fl) => {
      bt := base_struct_span(owner, cx)
      if bt.n != 0 { res = field_type_span(cx.decls, cx.src, bt.s, bt.n, fs, fl, deref(cx.mar)) }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  res
}

## Recover the STATIC integer element type of a fixed-array LOCAL, BY-REFERENCE PARAM, or declared
## array FIELD index for the signedness scans.
## `bind_array_slot` stores a scalar array's LENGTH in `snl`, not its element type, so the ordinary
## `expr_type_span` path deliberately returns 0/0 for native-width `[u64; N]` / `[i64; N]` elements.
## That neutrality was useful for overload/routing, but it left `is_unsigned_expr` and `is_signed_expr`
## unable to classify `xs[i]`: two indexed operands silently used the SIGNED comparison default, and
## native-width signed array elements used the UNSIGNED division default. Read the declaration only for
## a fixed-array slot and only return a builtin integer span; Slice/variadic, unknown, aggregate, and
## other unsupported forms remain unresolved and keep their existing conservative behavior.
index_signedness_type_span := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> CSpan {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Index(base, idx) => {
      mut lt := CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
      bv := var_name_span(base)
      if bv.n != 0 {
        si := slot_of(cx.slots, cx.src, bv.s, bv.n)
        if si >= 0 {
          ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, bv.s, bv.n)))
          if ent.ek == 5 { lt = local_type_span(cx.src, ent.ns, ent.nl) }
        }
      } else {
        ## A declared array FIELD has no SlotEntry of its own: recover the field's
        ## `[T; N]` annotation from the owning struct, keeping this lane limited to
        ## `Index(Field(Var, field), index)` rather than global/variadic/aggregate bases.
        lt = index_field_array_type_span(base, cx)
      }
      if lt.n == 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
      es := array_elem_span(cx.src, lt.s, lt.n)
      if es.n != 0 and is_builtin_int_name(str_at((cx.src + es.s), es.n)) { return es }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
}
## Recover the declared builtin integer element type of a module-level fixed-array global index. Global
## values have no SlotEntry, so use the module Decl's name to find its source `: [T; N]` annotation.
## Keep this lane separate from locals/params/fields and require a bare Var base; inferred globals,
## aggregate/non-builtin elements, variadic forms, and unknown names remain unresolved and preserve the
## conservative signedness fallback.
global_array_signedness_type_span := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> CSpan {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Index(base, idx) => {
      bv := var_name_span(base)
      if bv.n != 0 and slot_of(cx.slots, cx.src, bv.s, bv.n) < 0 {
        cnt := rt::vec_len(deref(cx.decls))
        mut i := 0
        mut found := CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
        while i < cnt {
          d := deref(decl_get(cx.decls, i))
          if d.is_fn == false and d.kind == 0 and d.ret_tl == 0 and d.arity == 0 and streq(cx.src, d.name_start, d.name_len, bv.s, bv.n) {
            lt := local_type_span(cx.src, d.name_start, d.name_len)
            if lt.n != 0 {
              es := array_elem_span(cx.src, lt.s, lt.n)
              if es.n != 0 and is_builtin_int_name(str_at((cx.src + es.s), es.n)) { found = CSpan(s = es.s, n = es.n) }
            }
          }
          i += 1
        }
        if found.n != 0 { return found }
      }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
}
## Recover the declared builtin integer element type of a typed `Slice(T)` LOCAL index. A range-view
## local is a two-word `ek == 5` slot with `is_ref == true`, `sns == 0`, and `snl == 1`; unlike a
## `Slice(T)` PARAM it has the local-view runtime marker rather than the parameter pair marker. Keep this
## lane local-only and scalar-only: parameters use the helper below, while slice-variadic, global/field
## bases, aggregate elements, and non-builtin elements remain unresolved and keep the conservative
## signed comparison fallback.
slice_local_signedness_type_span := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> CSpan {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Index(base, idx) => {
      bv := var_name_span(base)
      if bv.n == 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
      ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, bv.s, bv.n)))
      if ent.ek != 5 or ent.is_ref == false or ent.sns != 0 or ent.snl != 1 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
      lt := local_type_span(cx.src, ent.ns, ent.nl)
      if lt.n < 7 or str_at((cx.src + lt.s), 6) != "Slice(" { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
      es := lt.s + 6
      mut ee := es
      end := lt.s + lt.n
      while ee < end and str_at((cx.src + ee), 1) != ")" { ee += 1 }
      if ee == es or ee >= end { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
      if is_builtin_int_name(str_at((cx.src + es), ee - es)) { return CSpan(s = es, n = ee - es) }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
}
## Recover the declared builtin integer element type of a concrete `Slice(T)` PARAM index.
## `bind_param` records the runtime pair marker (`sns == 1`, `snl == 1`) instead of T in the
## slot, so `array_elem_span(local_type_span(...))` cannot see the element declaration here. Keep
## this bridge PARAM-only and syntax-gated to `Slice(`: a typed-slice LOCAL has `sns == 0`, while
## slice-variadic params must remain outside this lane. Unknown, aggregate, and non-builtin elements
## stay unresolved and retain the conservative signed comparison fallback.
slice_param_signedness_type_span := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> CSpan {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Index(base, idx) => {
      bv := var_name_span(base)
      if bv.n == 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
      ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, bv.s, bv.n)))
      if ent.ek != 5 or ent.is_ref == false or ent.sns != 1 or ent.snl != 1 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
      es := slice_param_elem_span(cx.src, ent.ns, ent.nl)
      if es.n != 0 and is_builtin_int_name(str_at((cx.src + es.s), es.n)) { return es }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
}
## Recover the declared INTEGER type of a direct pointer-field read `deref(p).f` for the
## signedness scans only. `expr_type_span` intentionally leaves this field shape unresolved because
## the aggregate/read paths have their own pointer-aware resolvers; without this narrow bridge,
## `is_unsigned_expr`/`is_signed_expr` see 0/0 and silently choose the conservative fallback for a
## field that is already proven by the pointer parameter's `ek == 7` + struct span. The helper is
## deliberately limited to `deref(Var).field`: nested derefs, pointer-producing calls, generic-erased
## pointees, and aggregate/layout recovery remain unresolved rather than guessing.
signedness_ptr_field_type_span := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> CSpan {
  mut res := CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Field(base, fs, fl) => {
      pv := deref_var_span(base)
      if pv.n != 0 {
        ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, pv.s, pv.n)))
        if ent.ek == 7 { res = field_type_span(cx.decls, cx.src, ent.sns, ent.snl, fs, fl, deref(cx.mar)) }
      }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  res
}
is_signed_expr := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> bool {
  mut t := expr_type_span(e, cx)
  if t.n == 0 { t = signedness_ptr_field_type_span(e, cx) }
  if t.n == 0 { t = index_signedness_type_span(e, cx) }
  if t.n == 0 { t = global_array_signedness_type_span(e, cx) }
  if t.n == 0 { t = slice_local_signedness_type_span(e, cx) }
  if t.n == 0 { t = slice_param_signedness_type_span(e, cx) }
  if t.n == 0 { return false }
  bn := base_type_name(cx.src, t.s, t.n)
  tx := str_at((cx.src + bn.s), bn.n)
  tx == "i8" or tx == "i16" or tx == "i32" or tx == "i64" or tx == "isize"
}
## POSITIVE, CONSERVATIVE unsignedness test (the DUAL of `is_signed_expr`): is `e` PROVABLY an
## unsigned integer type (`u8`/`u16`/`u32`/`u64`/`usize`)? Used ONLY to pick the UNSIGNED ordering
## setcc (`setb`/`seta`/`setbe`/`setae`) in the non-inline binary-op fallback below, in place of the
## always-signed `setl`/… default. An UNKNOWN type (0/0 span — a literal, an unrecorded local, an
## `iN`) returns FALSE, so an unproven operand keeps the SIGNED default: a MISS just leaves the latent
## bug for that site (safe), while never mis-flipping a signed comparison to unsigned (which would
## mis-lower the compiler's own `isize` comparisons that go negative → break self-build).
is_unsigned_expr := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> bool {
  mut t := expr_type_span(e, cx)
  if t.n == 0 { t = signedness_ptr_field_type_span(e, cx) }
  if t.n == 0 { t = index_signedness_type_span(e, cx) }
  if t.n == 0 { t = global_array_signedness_type_span(e, cx) }
  if t.n == 0 { t = slice_local_signedness_type_span(e, cx) }
  if t.n == 0 { t = slice_param_signedness_type_span(e, cx) }
  if t.n == 0 { return unsigned_thru_unchecked(e, cx) }
  bn := base_type_name(cx.src, t.s, t.n)
  tx := str_at((cx.src + bn.s), bn.n)
  tx == "u8" or tx == "u16" or tx == "u32" or tx == "u64" or tx == "usize"
}
## The two SHAPES that carry an operand's unsignedness but record no type span of their own, so
## `expr_type_span` answers 0/0 for them and `is_unsigned_expr` could not prove them unsigned:
##
##   • `unchecked (<inner>)` — a VERIFICATION scope. It changes the overflow-checking mode
##     of the expression it wraps, NEVER its type, so the inner's signedness IS the expression's.
##     `unchecked (w + d) < w` over `w, d : u64` therefore fell back to the always-SIGNED `setl`, and
##     the wrapped sum (a word above 2^63) compared as NEGATIVE: `0 < 18446744073709551610` answered
##     FALSE — a silent wrong value.
##   • an ARITHMETIC `Bin` (`+ - * / %`, and the bit ops) whose BOTH operands are provably unsigned:
##     the result of unsigned arithmetic is unsigned. Comparisons are excluded (they yield `bool`).
##
## Both directions are PROOF-ONLY — they can only move an operand signed → unsigned, never the
## reverse — so the always-signed default still covers every operand the type scan misses, including
## the compiler's own `isize` comparisons that go negative. (Flipping that DEFAULT to unsigned was
## tried on 2026-07-24 and broke self-reproduction; this is the opposite move.)
unsigned_thru_unchecked := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> bool {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Unchecked(inner) => { is_unsigned_expr(inner, cx) }
    Expr::Bin(op, l, r) => {
      if is_scalar_bin_op(op) {
        is_unsigned_expr(l, cx) and is_unsigned_expr(r, cx)
      } else { false }
    }
    _ => { false }
  }
}
## Is `e` a bare INTEGER LITERAL (`Expr::Num`)? An integer literal carries NO type span
## (`expr_type_span` returns 0/0), so `is_unsigned_expr` can never prove it unsigned — which is why
## `n >= 10` over a `u64` fell back to the SIGNED `setge` and read every value at or above 2^63 as
## negative (at `n = 2^63`: `n >= 10` FALSE, `n > 1` FALSE, `n < 100` TRUE). A literal has no
## signedness of its own; it takes the type of its partner, and its WORD is the same either way — so
## pairing it with a PROVABLY unsigned operand cannot change what the literal means, only how the
## OTHER operand is read. That covers the whole literal range in one test, including a literal at or
## above 2^63 (`m < 18446744073709551615`, a `Num` whose `i64` view is `-1`), which under the signed
## setcc compared as `5 < -1` → FALSE.
## There is NO negative-literal case to exclude: the grammar has no negative literal — the parser
## desugars unary `-x` to `Unchecked(Bin(17, Num(0), x))`, which is a `Bin`, not a `Num`, so `x < -1`
## never reaches this test and keeps the signed default. (A comptime fold CAN leave a `Num` negative,
## but only where its partner is provably unsigned does this fire, and there the value's `uN` reading
## — the wrapped word — is the one the comparison must use anyway. Emission over `src/` + `lib/` is
## byte-identical whether or not negative `Num`s are admitted: no such pair exists in the tree.)
expr_is_int_lit := fn(e : ptr(Expr)) -> bool {
  mut r := false
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Num(v, s, n) => { r = true }
    _ => {}
  }
  r
}
## An ORDERING comparison (`<`/`>`/`<=`/`>=`) is PROVABLY UNSIGNED iff BOTH operands are provably
## unsigned, OR one operand is provably unsigned and the other is a bare integer LITERAL (which has no
## type span of its own and takes its partner's type). Requiring the NON-LITERAL side to be PROVEN
## unsigned keeps the guard's conservative character — it only ever moves signed → unsigned, never the
## reverse — so the always-signed default still covers every operand the type scan misses (the
## compiler's own `isize` comparisons among them; flipping the DEFAULT to unsigned was tried on
## 2026-07-24 and broke self-reproduction). `0 < u64::MAX` (both `u64`) and `n >= 10` (`u64` vs a
## literal) are then both compared with the unsigned setcc and evaluate TRUE.
is_unsigned_cmp := fn(l : ptr(Expr), r : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> bool {
  ul := is_unsigned_expr(l, cx)
  ur := is_unsigned_expr(r, cx)
  if ul and ur { return true }
  if ul and expr_is_int_lit(r) { return true }
  if ur and expr_is_int_lit(l) { return true }
  false
}

## Is `base` a direct Slice(T) value whose `.len` field is a `usize`? Most slice params and range-view
## locals use the compact ek-5 view slot, but a constructed `Slice(T)(…)` can still be retained as the
## generic two-word struct slot (ek-2). Recognise both exact representations; no arbitrary aggregate
## field is treated as unsigned.
slice_len_base_is_view := fn(base : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> bool {
  if view_var_kind(base, cx.slots, cx.src) == 5 { return true }
  vn := var_name_span(base)
  if vn.n == 0 { return false }
  ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, vn.s, vn.n)))
  if ent.ek != 2 { return false }
  bt := base_type_name(cx.src, ent.sns, ent.snl)
  str_at((cx.src + bt.s), bt.n) == "Slice"
}

## A range `for` has no declared expression for its loop variable, so its guard must take the
## comparison domain from the upper bound. `Slice(T).len` is a `usize`, but the view representation
## intentionally carries only the runtime `{ptr,len}` marker and therefore has no type span for the
## ordinary scan. Recover exactly this direct view-field shape; every other unresolved bound keeps the
## signed fallback. This deliberately does not broaden the ordinary comparison scan or classify an
## otherwise unresolved range bound.
range_bound_is_unsigned := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> bool {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Field(base, fs, fl) => {
      if str_at((cx.src + fs), fl) == "len" and slice_len_base_is_view(base, cx) { return true }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  false
}

## PER-SIGNATURE OVERLOAD MANGLING (§1.4-A-5 deeper). A module may define several NON-generic fns
## with the SAME name but different parameter TYPES (e.g. `base::cmp`'s `eq`/`lt` for u64/i64/u32/…).
## They all mangle to one `<module>__<name>` label → the assembler rejects the duplicate symbols. So
## an OVERLOAD SET (2+ same-name/same-module non-generic decls) gets a per-signature label SUFFIX —
## the FIRST parameter's type name (the overloads are homogeneous, so it discriminates) — on BOTH the
## definition and the call. A call recovers its target overload from the first argument whose type it
## can infer (`arg_type_name`). `src/` has NO reached overload set (its `decl_at`/`node_ptr`/… dups are
## per-MODULE → distinct labels already), so `overload_set_count` returns < 2 for every `src/` name →
## no suffix → fixpoint byte-identical.

## Count NON-generic fn (kind 1) / syscall-fn (kind 4) decls named (ns,nl) in module (ms,ml).
overload_set_count := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), ns : usize, nl : usize, ms : usize, ml : usize) -> usize {
  cnt := rt::vec_len(deref(decls))
  mut c := 0
  th := name_hash(src, ns, nl)
  mut j := dni_lo(cnt, th)
  je := dni_hi(cnt, th)
  while j < je {
    i := dni_at(cnt, j)
    if DNH == 0 or DNH_N != cnt or rt::rec_get(unchecked bitcast(ptr(mut u8), DNH), i) == th {
      d := deref(decl_get(decls, i))
      if (d.kind == 1 or d.kind == 4) and d.is_generic == false and streq(src, d.name_start, d.name_len, ns, nl) and streq(src, d.mod_start, d.mod_len, ms, ml) {
        c += 1
      }
    }
    j += 1
  }
  c
}

## The base type name span of a decl's FIRST parameter (the overload-set discriminator). 0/0 if none.
first_param_type_span := fn(d : Decl, a : rt::Arena, src : ptr(u8)) -> CSpan {
  if d.params_head == 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  p0 := deref(param_p(d.params_head))
  base_type_name(src, p0.ts, p0.tl)
}

## Emit a type name span with the pointer-width aliases normalized (`usize`→`u64`, `isize`→`i64`)
## so a def parameter typed `u64` and a call argument inferred as `usize` reach the SAME label.
emit_norm_type := fn(in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize) {
  t := str_at((src + s), n)
  if t == "usize" {
    push_str(sb, "u64")
  } else {
    if t == "isize" {
      push_str(sb, "i64")
    } else {
      push_str(sb, t)
    }
  }
}

## Infer a scalar/aggregate expression's TYPE name span (for overload resolution): a `Var` from its
## slot's recorded type (`sns`/`snl` — set for a scalar VALUE param by `bind_slot_typed`, and for an
## aggregate by its binding), a `Field` from the base struct's field type, a `Call` from its declared
## return type. 0/0 when unknown (a literal, an unrecorded local) — the caller tries the next argument.
## `field_type_span` threads `deref(cx.mar)` (the struct-decl arena) exactly as the existing
## Field-read paths do. The Arg-node walk (`arg_type_name`) uses the separate EMIT arena instead.
expr_type_span := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> CSpan {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Var(s, n) => {
      ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, s, n)))
      ## An array slot (`ek == 5`, scalar/float element) reuses `snl` for its element COUNT, not a
      ## type span — skip it here so overload resolution never reads the count as a type name.
      if streq(cx.src, ent.ns, ent.nl, s, n) and ent.snl != 0 and ent.ek != 5 { return CSpan(s = ent.sns, n = ent.snl) }
      return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
    }
    Expr::Field(base, fs, fl) => {
      bt := base_struct_span(base, cx)
      if bt.n != 0 { return field_type_span(cx.decls, cx.src, bt.s, bt.n, fs, fl, deref(cx.mar)) }
      return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
    }
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, na, ah) => {
      ## A scalar CONVERSION `T(x)` (int/float) has the STATIC result type `T` — its callee name IS
      ## the type. This is the first strand of scalar type tracking: it lets `is_signed_expr` classify
      ## `i64(x)` / `u64(x)` correctly (Verification §, the signedness prerequisite).
      if conv_kind(str_at((cx.src + cs), cl)) >= 0 { return CSpan(s = cs, n = cl) }
      return call_ret_ty_span(e, cx.decls, cx.src, deref(cx.mar))
    }
    ## An INDEX read `xs[i]` of a SCALAR-element array (`ek == 5, eek == 0`) carries the array's
    ## DECLARED element type (CG-6 / I11): without it a narrow element (`u8`/…) read as an operand
    ## classified NATIVE-width, so its per-width overflow trap was silently dropped (`xs[i] + xs[j]`
    ## on a `[u8; N]` never trapped). Recover the element type NEUTRALLY by a source-scan of the
    ## array local's declaration — `local_type_span` → the `[u8; N]` span, `array_elem_span` → `u8`
    ## (the slot's `snl` holds the element COUNT for a scalar array, not a type span). GATED to a
    ## genuinely NARROW element (`is_narrowing_conv`: u8/u16/u32/i8/i16/i32): a native-width element
    ## (u64/usize/i64) returns 0/0 exactly as before, so the u64/usize-heavy corpus stays
    ## fixpoint-neutral (no new classification/routing for native-width index reads).
    Expr::Index(base, idx) => {
      bvn := var_name_span(base)
      if bvn.n == 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
      ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, bvn.s, bvn.n)))
      if streq(cx.src, ent.ns, ent.nl, bvn.s, bvn.n) and ent.ek == 5 and (ent.eek == 0 or is_byte_array_eek(ent.eek)) {
        lt := local_type_span(cx.src, ent.ns, ent.nl)
        if lt.n != 0 {
          es := array_elem_span(cx.src, lt.s, lt.n)
          if es.n != 0 and is_narrowing_conv(str_at((cx.src + es.s), es.n)) { return CSpan(s = es.s, n = es.n) }
        }
      }
      return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
    }
    ## A struct LITERAL `S(f = v, …)` operand — its STATIC type IS the constructor's name span
    ## `S` (`str_at((src+cs), cl)` reads exactly `S`, the `(` is past `cl`). Without this a routed
    ## operator over a struct-LITERAL operand (`u128(..) * u128(..)`) mis-typed to 0/0, the route
    ## missed, and the built-in scalar op ran over the struct's words (SIGILL / wrong value).
    Expr::StructLit(cs, cl, nf, fhead) => { return CSpan(s = cs, n = cl) }
    _ => { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  }
}

## The type-name discriminator for an overloaded CALL: the first argument whose type is inferable.
## `a` is the EMIT arena where the Arg nodes live (NOT `cx.mar` — using the wrong arena to resolve
## an Arg handle yields a garbage pointer → segfault).
arg_type_name := fn(head : ptr(mut Stmt), cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena) -> CSpan {
  mut g := head
  while g != 0 {
    ga := deref(arg_p(g))
    t := expr_type_span(ga.e, cx)
    if t.n != 0 { return t }
    g = ga.next
  }
  CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
}

## True if a NON-generic fn named (the tail of) `cs`/`cl` has arity == `nargs` AND its first parameter
## type == `(ats, atl)` — i.e. the call matches a CONCRETE overload by arity + first-arg type, so it
## must resolve to that overload, NOT a same-name GENERIC (implicit-type-arg) fn. Lets `cmp::eq(u64,u64)`
## win over `derive::eq(T,a,b)` for `eq(x:u64, y:u64)`, while `hash(key:u64)` still routes to the generic
## `derive::hash` (the concrete `hash(str)` mismatches u64). 0-len `(ats,atl)` → no match (be generic).
nongen_type_match := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), cs : usize, cl : usize, nargs : usize, ats : usize, atl : usize, a : rt::Arena) -> bool {
  nm := callee_name_span(src, cs, cl)
  cnt := rt::vec_len(deref(decls))
  ## A NULLARY call (`f()`, no args → no first-arg type to key on) resolves to a CONCRETE same-name
  ## overload of arity 0 over any same-name GENERIC. Without this a user's nullary fn whose name
  ## collides with a loaded generic of higher arity (e.g. `get := fn() -> …` vs `base/alloc`'s generic
  ## `get(T, a, h)`) mis-resolves to that generic in `generic_decl_of`'s arity-blind last-declared
  ## fallback, then `arg_expr_at(<empty args>, tparam_idx)` yields a NULL Expr that `tuple_typearg_span`
  ## dereferences → a compiler SEGFAULT. `src/` has no such nullary/generic name collision (it would
  ## crash the build otherwise) so this never fires on the self-build → fixpoint-neutral.
  th := name_hash(src, nm.s, nm.n)
  clo := dni_lo(cnt, th)
  chi := dni_hi(cnt, th)
  if nargs == 0 {
    mut jc := clo
    while jc < chi {
      j := dni_at(cnt, jc)
      jc = jc + 1
      if dnh_skip(cnt, j, th) == false {
        d := deref(decl_get(decls, j))
        if (d.kind == 1 or d.kind == 4) and d.is_generic == false and d.arity == 0 and streq(src, d.name_start, d.name_len, nm.s, nm.n) { return true }
      }
    }
    return false
  }
  if atl == 0 { return false }
  mut kc := clo
  while kc < chi {
    i := dni_at(cnt, kc)
    kc = kc + 1
    if dnh_skip(cnt, i, th) == false {
      d := deref(decl_get(decls, i))
      if (d.kind == 1 or d.kind == 4) and d.is_generic == false and d.arity == nargs and streq(src, d.name_start, d.name_len, nm.s, nm.n) {
        fp := first_param_type_span(d, a, src)
        if fp.n != 0 and streq(src, fp.s, fp.n, ats, atl) { return true }
      }
    }
  }
  false
}

## IMPLICIT type-argument inference at EMIT time for a generic call `f(v)` with the leading
## `T : type` OMITTED. Find `f`'s value parameter whose type IS its type-param and infer `T` from the
## corresponding call argument's type via `expr_type_span` (a Var reads its SLOT type — a scalar VALUE
## param in a mono instance carries the SUBSTITUTED concrete type, so `hash(key)` in
## `hashmap_insert__u64` infers `u64`, matching the pre-pass instance). 0/0 when not inferable. `src/`
## spells its type-args (never implicit) → never fires → fixpoint-neutral.
## Comptime §3.3 / Types §7 — the DECLARED type span of a VIEW value argument (`str` / `Slice(T)`),
## recovered from the binding's own `: str` / `: Slice(T)` source annotation. A view slot records the
## view KIND (`ek 4` for a `str`, the slice-view shape for a `[T]`) and never a type SPAN, so
## `expr_type_span` answers 0/0 for it and an OMITTED comptime type argument was left un-inferred —
## whereupon the caller took the VALUE argument itself for the erased type argument, tagged the
## instance with that argument's own NAME text and passed NOTHING: `println(s)` for a `str` local
## rendered `0`, a SILENT wrong value (I11). `block_decl_type` hands the collect-time pre-pass the
## same annotation span, so both sides mangle the identical `…__str` instance. Restricted to a VIEW
## type so no other implicit-type-argument call's resolution moves.
view_arg_ty_span := fn(e : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> CSpan {
  view_str_ty_span(e, cx.slots, cx.src)
}

## The slots-only core of `view_arg_ty_span`, shared with the SLOT-BINDING side (`collect_slots` has
## `slots`/`src` but no `LCtx`), so the binding's size and the call site's instance tag are decided by
## one rule. `str` ONLY: a `Slice(T)` annotation is a view too, but its span TEXT (`Slice(u8)`) mangles
## to a label with parentheses that `as` rejects and the EXPLICIT spelling reaches its instance by a
## different route — an inferred `Slice(T)` would trade one silent defect for a broken build, so it
## stays on the fail-loud "supply the type argument explicitly" boundary.
view_str_ty_span := fn(e : ptr(Expr), slots : ptr(SVec), src : ptr(u8)) -> CSpan {
  vn := var_name_span(e)
  if vn.n == 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  if view_var_kind(e, slots, src) == 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, slots, entry_of(slots, src, vn.s, vn.n)))
  if streq(src, ent.ns, ent.nl, vn.s, vn.n) == false { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  lt := local_type_span(src, ent.ns, ent.nl)
  if lt.n == 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  if str_at((src + lt.s), lt.n) == "str" { return CSpan(s = lt.s, n = lt.n) }
  CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
}

## Types §7 / Comptime §3.3 — is `e` a call to a GENERIC whose declared return type IS its own
## comptime type parameter, with that type argument OMITTED and inferable as `str` from a value
## argument (`t := id(s)` for `s : str`, through `id := fn(T : type, v : T) -> T`)? The SLOT-BINDING
## dual of `generic_inferred_str_ret`: `str_ret_call`/`gen_ret_targ_struct_span` both read the type
## argument POSITIONALLY, so an inferred one is invisible and the binding sized `t` as ONE scalar word
## (the returned data pointer), dropping the length — every later use of `t` then read a view whose
## length came from a neighbouring slot. Mirrors `gen_ret_targ_struct_span`'s decl walk exactly, with
## the "type argument present and a struct" test replaced by "type argument ABSENT and the T-typed
## value argument is a declared `str` view".
gen_ret_str_infer := fn(e : ptr(Expr), slots : ptr(SVec), decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), a : rt::Arena) -> bool {
  mut res := false
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Call(cs, cl, nargs, ah) => {
      nm := callee_name_span(src, cs, cl)
      cnt := rt::vec_len(deref(decls))
      th := name_hash(src, nm.s, nm.n)
      mut jc := dni_lo(cnt, th)
      jce := dni_hi(cnt, th)
      mut i := 0
      while jc < jce {
        i = dni_at(cnt, jc)
        jc = jc + 1
        d := deref(decl_get(decls, i))
        if d.kind == 1 and d.is_generic and streq(src, d.name_start, d.name_len, nm.s, nm.n) {
          mut tps := 0
          mut tpl := 0
          mut ntp := 0
          mut pp := d.params_head
          while pp != 0 {
            pm := deref(param_p(pp))
            if str_at((src + pm.ts), pm.tl) == "type" {
              ntp += 1
              if tpl == 0 { tps = pm.ns; tpl = pm.nl }
            }
            pp = pm.next
          }
          if ntp == 1 and tpl != 0 and d.ret_tl != 0 and streq(src, d.ret_ts, d.ret_tl, tps, tpl) and d.arity != 0 and nargs == d.arity - 1 {
            ## the type argument is OMITTED — find the value argument declared `T` and ask whether it
            ## is a `str` VIEW binding (the same rule the emit side's inference applies).
            mut vj := 0
            mut pv := d.params_head
            while pv != 0 {
              pmv := deref(param_p(pv))
              if str_at((src + pmv.ts), pmv.tl) == "type" {
                pv = pmv.next
              } else {
                if streq(src, pmv.ts, pmv.tl, tps, tpl) and view_str_ty_span(arg_expr_at(ah, vj, a), slots, src).n != 0 { res = true }
                vj += 1
                pv = pmv.next
              }
            }
          }
        }
        i += 1
      }
    }
    _ => {}
  }
  res
}

infer_implicit_targ := fn(gi : i64, args_head : ptr(mut Arg), cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena) -> CSpan {
  gd := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(cx.decls), usize(gi))))
  mut tpn_s := 0
  mut tpn_l := 0
  mut pc := gd.params_head
  while pc != 0 {
    pm := deref(param_p(pc))
    if str_at((cx.src + pm.ts), pm.tl) == "type" { tpn_s = pm.ns; tpn_l = pm.nl }
    pc = pm.next
  }
  if tpn_l == 0 { return CSpan(s = 0, n = 0) }
  mut j := 0
  pc = gd.params_head
  while pc != 0 {
    pm := deref(param_p(pc))
    if str_at((cx.src + pm.ts), pm.tl) == "type" {
      pc = pm.next
    } else {
      bn := base_type_name(cx.src, pm.ts, pm.tl)
      if streq(cx.src, bn.s, bn.n, tpn_s, tpn_l) {
        ## Try EVERY value param whose type is T, returning the first arg whose type IS inferable —
        ## so `eq(existing, key)` (existing a local → 0/0; key a param → K→concrete) resolves via
        ## `key`, not the un-inferable `existing`. Un-inferable first arg falls through to the next.
        ae := arg_expr_at(args_head, j, a)
        r := expr_type_span(ae, cx)
        if r.n != 0 { return r }
        ## a struct/enum LITERAL arg carries its own type name (no `expr_type_span`) — recover it so `T`
        ## infers from a literal value arg (`g(P(…))` → `T = P`). Must match `infer_implicit_pre` so the
        ## emit-time resolution and the collect-time instance tag agree.
        sli := struct_lit_info(ae)
        if sli.is_s { return type_lit_tag(cx.decls, cx.src, sli.ss, sli.sl) }
        eli := enum_lit_info(ae)
        if eli.is_e { return type_lit_tag(cx.decls, cx.src, eli.es, eli.el) }
        ## Types §7 — a VIEW value argument carries its type in its ANNOTATION, not in its slot.
        vwt := view_arg_ty_span(ae, cx)
        if vwt.n != 0 { return vwt }
      }
      j += 1
      pc = pm.next
    }
  }
  ## PASS 2 — the UFCS-RECEIVER case: no value param is DIRECTLY typed `T`, but one is a generic
  ## `G(…T…)` (e.g. `self : Option(T)`). `o.expect(msg)` desugars to the implicit `expect(o, msg)`
  ## with the leading `T : type` OMITTED — so the receiver `o` is a VALUE arg, NOT the erased type
  ## argument. Return its span (a Var name — the SAME tag the pre-pass `type_arg_at` fallback records
  ## for this call, so the collected instance and this call site agree on the mangled label). The
  ## non-zero return flips the caller's `impl_pay` so NOTHING is erased and the receiver reaches the
  ## `self` param. Reached only when PASS 1 found no direct-`T` param, so every currently-inferring
  ## call is byte-identical; `src/` spells its type-args → infer never runs for it → fixpoint-safe.
  mut j2 := 0
  pc = gd.params_head
  while pc != 0 {
    pm := deref(param_p(pc))
    if str_at((cx.src + pm.ts), pm.tl) == "type" {
      pc = pm.next
    } else {
      if param_type_has_tparam(cx.src, pm.ts, pm.tl, tpn_s, tpn_l) {
        rae := arg_expr_at(args_head, j2, a)
        vn := var_name_span(rae)
        if vn.n != 0 {
          ## MULTI-WORD PAYLOAD RE-TAG (Hunk C): a UFCS `o.expect(m)` / `o.unwrap()` on a generic
          ## `Option(T)` / `Result(T, E)` desugars with `T` omitted, so the receiver `o` is a VALUE arg.
          ## For a SCALAR / 1-word payload the instance is tagged by the receiver's NAME (fall through to
          ## the `vn` return below — byte-identical to before). But when `T` resolves to a MULTI-WORD
          ## aggregate, tagging by the name sizes the bare `T` as ONE word → the payload is truncated.
          ## Recover the CONCRETE payload type span (`payl`, e.g. `Rec`) from the receiver's resolved type
          ## and return THAT instead, so `o.unwrap()` mangles to the SAME `unwrap__Rec` instance the prefix
          ## `unwrap(Rec, o)` produces (`subst_param_enum_span` then sizes/delivers the full struct). The
          ## collect-time pre-pass (`infer_implicit_pre`) mirrors this exact `payl` recovery so the two
          ## agree on the mangled label. Gated to `>= 2` words → the scalar / 1-word `Handle` extraction
          ## the compiler ITSELF does (`allocate(…).expect(…).idx`) is untouched → fixpoint-neutral.
          rty := expr_type_span(rae, cx)
          if rty.n != 0 {
            pbn := base_type_name(cx.src, pm.ts, pm.tl)
            mut kk := 0
            mut tpos := 0 - 1
            mut gk := true
            while gk {
              pta := typearg_at(cx.src, pbn.s, pbn.n, usize(kk))
              if pta.n == 0 { gk = false }
              else { if streq(cx.src, pta.s, pta.n, tpn_s, tpn_l) { tpos = i64(kk); gk = false } ; kk += 1 }
            }
            if tpos >= 0 {
              rbn := base_type_name(cx.src, rty.s, rty.n)
              payl := typearg_at(cx.src, rbn.s, rbn.n, usize(tpos))
              if payl.n != 0 and agg_words(cx.decls, cx.src, payl.s, payl.n, deref(cx.mar)) >= 2 {
                return CSpan(s = payl.s, n = payl.n)
              }
            }
          }
          return CSpan(s = vn.s, n = vn.n)
        }
        ## CALL-RECEIVER UFCS (`find(1).unwrap()`): the receiver is a struct/enum-returning CALL, not a
        ## Var — so it has NO name to tag by. Handle ONLY the exact new shape: a DIRECT enum-returning call
        ## whose payload at `T`'s position is a CONCRETE SCALAR (a bare type name, 1 word — `u64`). Return
        ## that as the type-arg tag so `did_impl` flips (nothing erased; the result is materialized + passed
        ## by reference as `self` via `emit_arg`'s `enum_ret_call_d` arm). Was `vn.n == 0` → fell through →
        ## 0/0 → the receiver was treated as the erased type-arg and DROPPED (`call unwrap` with no args →
        ## segfault). DEFER (return 0/0, DO NOT tag) for every other call-receiver shape — a generic /
        ## parenthesized payload (`Handle(T)`, `Vec(u64)`), a multi-word payload, or a base mismatch
        ## (`Result` via `Option`): those already have a working pre-existing lowering (`allocate(...).
        ## expect(...)`) or fail loud downstream (a `Result` receiver redirects to `result::unwrap`/ntpc==2
        ## → the existing "explicit type args" panic), so deferring keeps them BYTE-IDENTICAL (no
        ## regression). `infer_implicit_pre` mirrors this gate. `src/` binds such results to a local (a Var
        ## receiver) → this branch never fires for it → fixpoint-neutral.
        rc := recv_call_full_span(rae, cx.decls, cx.src, deref(cx.mar))
        if rc.n != 0 {
          pbase := base_type_name(cx.src, pm.ts, pm.tl)
          rbase := base_type_name(cx.src, rc.s, rc.n)
          if streq(cx.src, pbase.s, pbase.n, rbase.s, rbase.n) {
            cpayl := recv_payload_targ(cx.src, pm.ts, pm.tl, tpn_s, tpn_l, rc.s, rc.n)
            if cpayl.n != 0 and is_concrete_scalar_tag(cx.decls, cx.src, cpayl.s, cpayl.n, deref(cx.mar)) { return cpayl }
          }
        }
      }
      j2 += 1
      pc = pm.next
    }
  }
  CSpan(s = 0, n = 0)
}

## Does the (possibly generic) type span `[ts, ts+tl)` mention the type-parameter named
## `[tpn_s, tpn_s+tpn_l)` as one of its TOP-LEVEL type-arguments? `Option(T)` / `Result(T, E)` do;
## a bare `T` does NOT (that is the direct case PASS 1 handles). Drives `infer_implicit_targ`'s
## UFCS-receiver PASS 2. `typearg_at` re-parses the actual bytes after the base name, so it works on
## both a source span and a rebased/synthesized span.
param_type_has_tparam := fn(src : ptr(u8), ts : usize, tl : usize, tpn_s : usize, tpn_l : usize) -> bool {
  bn := base_type_name(src, ts, tl)
  ## a param type span is the BASE name (`Option`), its `(…)` args following in source; a bare name
  ## (no `(` after the base) is the direct case PASS 1 handles → not a nested type-param.
  if str_at((src + bn.s + bn.n), 1) != "(" { return false }
  mut k := 0
  mut res := false
  mut go := true
  while go {
    ta := typearg_at(src, bn.s, bn.n, usize(k))
    if ta.n == 0 { go = false }
    else {
      if streq(src, ta.s, ta.n, tpn_s, tpn_l) { res = true; go = false }
      k += 1
    }
  }
  res
}

## Append the per-signature SUFFIX for an overloaded CALL (right after `emit_mangled_call`): resolve
## the callee decl (bare or qualified), and if its (name, module) is an overload set, append the
## inferred argument type. A no-op when the callee is not overloaded (every `src/` call). `a` is the
## EMIT arena (the Arg nodes' arena — the emit_gas / emit_call_dispatch `a` param).
emit_overload_suffix := fn(in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, cs : usize, cl : usize, args_head : ptr(mut Arg), cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena) {
  cp := colon_pos(cx.src, cs, cl)
  mut idx := -1
  if cp >= 0 {
    hl := usize(cp)
    idx = callee_decl_idx(cx.decls, cx.src, cs + hl + 2, cl - hl - 2, cs, hl)
  } else {
    idx = callee_decl_idx(cx.decls, cx.src, cs, cl, cx.mod_s, cx.mod_l)
  }
  if idx < 0 { return }
  cd := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(cx.decls), usize(idx))))
  if overload_set_count(cx.decls, cx.src, cd.name_start, cd.name_len, cd.mod_start, cd.mod_len) < 2 { return }
  ## Resolve the TARGET overload by the complete argument signature (§1 item 3), then emit ITS full
  ## parameter-type suffix — matching the definition side. When resolution is ambiguous (too many
  ## arguments untyped) no suffix is emitted, matching the pre-existing "cannot infer" behavior.
  mut nargs := 0
  mut ga := args_head
  while ga != 0 { an := deref(arg_p(ga)); nargs = nargs + 1; ga = an.next }
  ridx := overload_resolve_idx(cx.decls, cx.src, cd.name_start, cd.name_len, cd.mod_start, cd.mod_len, nargs, args_head, cx, a)
  if ridx < 0 { return }
  rd := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(cx.decls), usize(ridx))))
  emit_sig_suffix(sb, cx.src, rd, deref(cx.mar))
}

## Compare two type-NAME spans after pointer-width normalization (`usize`→`u64`, `isize`→`i64`),
## so a parameter typed `u64` and an argument inferred `usize` are considered the same type.
norm_type_str := fn(src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize) -> str {
  t := str_at((src + s), n)
  if t == "usize" { return "u64" }
  if t == "isize" { return "i64" }
  t
}
norm_type_eq := fn(src : ptr(u8), s0 : usize, n0 : usize, s1 : usize, n1 : usize) -> bool {
  norm_type_str(src, s0, n0) == norm_type_str(src, s1, n1)
}

## Emit a decl's FULL per-signature suffix `__<t0>_<t1>_…` (each parameter's normalized base type).
## Both the definition and the resolved CALL emit this for the SAME decl, so overloads differing in
## ANY parameter position get distinct, matching labels (§1 item 3). `a` is the arena holding the
## decl's `Param` nodes (the AST/mono arena — `deref(p.mar)` on the def side, `deref(cx.mar)` on a call).
emit_sig_suffix := fn(in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, src : ptr(u8), d : Decl, a : rt::Arena) {
  push_str(sb, "__")
  mut p := d.params_head
  mut first := true
  while p != 0 {
    pm := deref(param_p(p))
    bn := base_type_name(src, pm.ts, pm.tl)
    if first { first = false } else { push_str(sb, "_") }
    emit_norm_type(sb, src, bn.s, bn.n)
    p = pm.next
  }
}

## Does candidate overload `d` match the call's arguments? Each argument whose type is INFERABLE
## (`expr_type_span`) must equal the corresponding parameter's normalized base type; an argument
## whose type is not inferable (a bare literal) matches any parameter (a wildcard). Parameters live
## in `deref(cx.mar)`; the Arg nodes live in the emit arena `a`.
overload_args_match := fn(d : Decl, args_head : ptr(mut Arg), cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena) -> bool {
  mut p := d.params_head
  mut g := args_head
  while p != 0 and g != 0 {
    pm := deref(param_p(p))
    an := deref(arg_p(g))
    at := expr_type_span(an.e, cx)
    if at.n != 0 {
      pb := base_type_name(cx.src, pm.ts, pm.tl)
      if not norm_type_eq(cx.src, at.s, at.n, pb.s, pb.n) { return false }
    } else if arg_is_num_literal(an.e) {
      ## a bare integer/bool literal defaults to an integer type — it can only match an integer
      ## scalar parameter, never a struct / enum / str / float one. So `h(2, 10)` selects the
      ## `h(u64, u64)` overload over `h(u64, A)` / `h(u64, B)` (both structs), rather than being an
      ## ambiguous wildcard. A NON-literal uninferable arg stays a wildcard (match anything).
      pb := base_type_name(cx.src, pm.ts, pm.tl)
      if not is_int_scalar_type(cx.src, pb.s, pb.n) { return false }
    } else if arg_is_float_literal(an.e) {
      ## a bare float literal (`2.0`) mirrors the integer case — it can only match a FLOAT scalar
      ## parameter, so `g(2.0)` selects `g(f64)` over `g(u64)` rather than matching both (which left
      ## the call ambiguous → no suffix → an undefined bare callee label at link).
      pb := base_type_name(cx.src, pm.ts, pm.tl)
      if not is_float_scalar_type(cx.src, pb.s, pb.n) { return false }
    } else if str_lit_info(an.e).is_s {
      ## a bare STRING literal (`"ok"`) mirrors the integer/float cases: it carries no type span
      ## (`expr_type_span` is 0/0 for a `StrLit`), so without this it matched EVERY parameter as a
      ## wildcard — leaving `f("ok")` over the overload set `f(u64)` / `f(str)` AMBIGUOUS (two
      ## candidates), which emits NO signature suffix and so a BARE callee label `<mod>__f` that
      ## matches neither definition (`…__f__u64` / `…__f__str`) → an `undefined reference` at link
      ## (Functions §1.4-A-5 per-signature overload mangling). A string literal can bind ONLY to a
      ## `str` parameter, so restricting it here resolves the set to the `str` overload.
      pb := base_type_name(cx.src, pm.ts, pm.tl)
      if str_at((cx.src + pb.s), pb.n) != "str" { return false }
    }
    p = pm.next
    g = an.next
  }
  true
}

## Is `e` a bare integer / bool literal (its type is the default integer, not yet inferable to a
## specific width) — the arguments that overload resolution treats as integer-scalar-only wildcards?
arg_is_num_literal := fn(e : ptr(Expr)) -> bool {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Num(v, s, n) => { true }
    Expr::BoolLit(v) => { true }
    _ => { false }
  }
}

## Is `e` a bare FLOAT literal (`2.0`)? Its type is not yet a specific width but it can bind ONLY to a
## float scalar parameter — the float mirror of `arg_is_num_literal`, so an overload set discriminated
## solely by float-vs-non-float (`g(u64)` / `g(f64)` called `g(2.0)`) resolves instead of staying an
## ambiguous wildcard (which emitted a bare, undefined callee label).
arg_is_float_literal := fn(e : ptr(Expr)) -> bool {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::FloatLit(fs, fl) => { true }
    _ => { false }
  }
}

## Is the type name `[s, s+n)` a built-in INTEGER / bool scalar (the types a bare integer literal
## may bind to)? Excludes struct / enum / str / ptr / float — matching how a default-int literal
## lowers as a word-sized integer.
is_int_scalar_type := fn(src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize) -> bool {
  t := str_at((src + s), n)
  t == "u64" or t == "i64" or t == "usize" or t == "isize" or t == "u32" or t == "i32"
    or t == "u16" or t == "i16" or t == "u8" or t == "i8" or t == "bool" or t == "char"
}

## Is the type name `[s, s+n)` a built-in FLOAT scalar (`f32`/`f64`) — the types a bare float literal
## may bind to? The float mirror of `is_int_scalar_type`.
is_float_scalar_type := fn(src : ptr(u8), s : usize, n : usize) -> bool {
  t := str_at((src + s), n)
  t == "f64" or t == "f32"
}

## Resolve an overloaded CALL to the UNIQUE decl index in the overload set (same name + module,
## non-generic, arity == nargs) whose parameter types match every inferable argument (§1 item 3).
## Returns -1 when none or more than one candidate matches (ambiguous → no distinguishing suffix).
overload_resolve_idx := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), ns : usize, nl : usize, ms : usize, ml : usize, nargs : usize, args_head : ptr(mut Arg), cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena) -> i64 {
  cnt := rt::vec_len(deref(decls))
  mut found := 0 - 1
  mut nfound := 0
  mut i := 0
  while i < cnt {
    d := deref(decl_get(decls, i))
    if (d.kind == 1 or d.kind == 4) and d.is_generic == false and d.arity == nargs and streq(src, d.name_start, d.name_len, ns, nl) and streq(src, d.mod_start, d.mod_len, ms, ml) {
      if overload_args_match(d, args_head, cx, a) { found = i64(i); nfound = nfound + 1 }
    }
    i += 1
  }
  if nfound == 1 { return found }
  0 - 1
}

## Is `name` a recognised x86_64 instruction-intrinsic mnemonic (`x86_64.<mnem>`.3 / §80)?
## num.al's `@inline` scalar operators name these inside a `comptime if target.arch == Arch.x86_64`
## (folded TRUE on the lean x86_64 target), so only the x86_64 mnemonics ever reach emit.
is_arch_mnemonic := fn(name : str) -> bool {
  return name == "addq" or name == "subq" or name == "imulq" or name == "addl" or name == "subl"
    or name == "idivq" or name == "iremq" or name == "divq" or name == "remq"
    or name == "mulhiq" or name == "imulhiq"
    or name == "shlq" or name == "shrq" or name == "sarq"
    or name == "andq" or name == "orq" or name == "xorq"
    or name == "addsd" or name == "subsd" or name == "mulsd" or name == "divsd"
    or name == "addss" or name == "subss" or name == "mulss" or name == "divss"
    or name == "sete" or name == "setb" or name == "setl"
    or name == "feqsd" or name == "fltsd" or name == "feqss" or name == "fltss"
}

## Lower an x86_64 instruction intrinsic `x86_64.<mnem>(dest, src)` to real machine code (A-7 /
## §80.3). Each is a two-operand op that MUTATES `dest` (arg 0 — a scalar local lvalue) with
## `src` (arg 1) in place: load `dest` → %rax, `src` → %rbx, apply the mnemonic, store the result
## back to `dest`'s frame slot. Signedness / high-half / float variants pick the exact instruction
## (`idivq`/`divq`, `iremq`/`remq`, `imulhiq`/`mulhiq`, `addsd`/…). The `…ss` (f32) forms reuse the
## `…sd` (f64) shape under the P1b f32≈f64 approximation. Additive + fixpoint-safe: `src/` names no
## intrinsic (it uses the built-in operators), so this only fires on an explicit `x86_64.*` call.
emit_arch_intr := fn(mn_s : usize, mn_l : usize, phead : ptr(mut Arg), in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena, in out nl : usize) {
  mn := str_at((cx.src + mn_s), mn_l)
  a0 := deref(arg_p(phead))
  dst := a0.e
  a1 := deref(arg_p(a0.next))
  srce := a1.e
  dv := var_name_span(dst)
  ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, dv.s, dv.n)))
  off := i64((ent.off + 1) * 8)
  is_flt := mn == "addsd" or mn == "subsd" or mn == "mulsd" or mn == "divsd" or mn == "addss" or mn == "subss" or mn == "mulss" or mn == "divss"
  is_icmp := mn == "sete" or mn == "setb" or mn == "setl"
  is_fcmp := mn == "feqsd" or mn == "fltsd" or mn == "feqss" or mn == "fltss"
  if is_icmp {
    ## 3-operand integer COMPARISON `set*(out, a, b)`: out = (a <cc> b). Lower a,b onto the stack,
    ## `cmpq %rbx(b), %rax(a)`, materialize the 0/1 boolean via the matching setcc (sete/setb-unsigned/
    ## setl-signed), zero-extend into %rax. The shared store writes it to `out`'s slot.
    a2 := deref(arg_p(a1.next))
    emit_gas(srce, sb, cx, a, nl)
    emit_gas(a2.e, sb, cx, a, nl)
    push_str(sb, "  popq %rbx\n  popq %rax\n  cmpq %rbx, %rax\n")
    if mn == "sete" { push_str(sb, "  sete %al\n") }
    if mn == "setb" { push_str(sb, "  setb %al\n") }
    if mn == "setl" { push_str(sb, "  setl %al\n") }
    push_str(sb, "  movzbq %al, %rax\n")
  } else if is_fcmp {
    ## 3-operand FLOAT comparison `feqsd`/`fltsd` (+ `…ss` under P1b f32≈f64): `ucomisd %xmm1(b),
    ## %xmm0(a)` then a NaN-UNORDERED-safe setcc — equal/less is FALSE when unordered, so AND the
    ## setcc (sete/setb) with `setnp` (not-parity), exactly the built-in ordered-compare shape.
    a2 := deref(arg_p(a1.next))
    emit_gas(srce, sb, cx, a, nl)
    emit_gas(a2.e, sb, cx, a, nl)
    push_str(sb, "  popq %rax\n  movq %rax, %xmm1\n  popq %rax\n  movq %rax, %xmm0\n  ucomisd %xmm1, %xmm0\n")
    if mn == "feqsd" or mn == "feqss" { push_str(sb, "  sete %al\n") }
    if mn == "fltsd" or mn == "fltss" { push_str(sb, "  setb %al\n") }
    push_str(sb, "  setnp %cl\n  andb %cl, %al\n  movzbq %al, %rax\n")
  } else if is_flt {
    ## f64/f32 SSE: bounce the two bit patterns through xmm (left → %xmm0, right → %xmm1), apply the
    ## scalar-double op, move the result bits back to %rax (the same GP↔xmm dance the built-in uses).
    emit_gas(srce, sb, cx, a, nl)
    push_str(sb, "  movq -")
    push_int(sb, off)
    push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rax\n  movq %rax, %xmm0\n  popq %rax\n  movq %rax, %xmm1\n")
    if mn == "addsd" or mn == "addss" { push_str(sb, "  addsd %xmm1, %xmm0\n") }
    if mn == "subsd" or mn == "subss" { push_str(sb, "  subsd %xmm1, %xmm0\n") }
    if mn == "mulsd" or mn == "mulss" { push_str(sb, "  mulsd %xmm1, %xmm0\n") }
    if mn == "divsd" or mn == "divss" { push_str(sb, "  divsd %xmm1, %xmm0\n") }
    push_str(sb, "  movq %xmm0, %rax\n")
  } else {
    emit_gas(srce, sb, cx, a, nl)                    ## src → stack
    push_str(sb, "  movq -")
    push_int(sb, off)
    push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rax\n  popq %rbx\n")       ## dest → %rax, src → %rbx
    if mn == "addq" { push_str(sb, "  addq %rbx, %rax\n") }
    if mn == "subq" { push_str(sb, "  subq %rbx, %rax\n") }
    if mn == "imulq" { push_str(sb, "  imulq %rbx, %rax\n") }
    if mn == "addl" { push_str(sb, "  addl %ebx, %eax\n") }
    if mn == "subl" { push_str(sb, "  subl %ebx, %eax\n") }
    ## signed divide/remainder: sign-extend %rax into %rdx:%rax (`cqto`), `idivq` → quo %rax, rem %rdx.
    if mn == "idivq" { push_str(sb, "  cqto\n  idivq %rbx\n") }
    if mn == "iremq" { push_str(sb, "  cqto\n  idivq %rbx\n  movq %rdx, %rax\n") }
    ## unsigned divide/remainder: zero %rdx, `divq` → quo %rax, rem %rdx.
    if mn == "divq" { push_str(sb, "  xorq %rdx, %rdx\n  divq %rbx\n") }
    if mn == "remq" { push_str(sb, "  xorq %rdx, %rdx\n  divq %rbx\n  movq %rdx, %rax\n") }
    ## high 64 bits of the full 128-bit product (%rdx after the 1-operand multiply).
    if mn == "mulhiq" { push_str(sb, "  mulq %rbx\n  movq %rdx, %rax\n") }
    if mn == "imulhiq" { push_str(sb, "  imulq %rbx\n  movq %rdx, %rax\n") }
    ## BIT SHIFTS (`shl`/`shr`/`sar` — logical/arithmetic; spec: operations in call form, no glyph):
    ## the shift COUNT must be in `%cl` (the only variable-count shift register), so move the count
    ## (%rbx) → %cl, then shift %rax. `shlq`/`shrq` (logical), `sarq` (arithmetic — sign-preserving).
    if mn == "shlq" { push_str(sb, "  movb %bl, %cl\n  shlq %cl, %rax\n") }
    if mn == "shrq" { push_str(sb, "  movb %bl, %cl\n  shrq %cl, %rax\n") }
    if mn == "sarq" { push_str(sb, "  movb %bl, %cl\n  sarq %cl, %rax\n") }
    ## BITWISE and/or/xor (register form) — the operand kernel bit ops in call form.
    if mn == "andq" { push_str(sb, "  andq %rbx, %rax\n") }
    if mn == "orq" { push_str(sb, "  orq %rbx, %rax\n") }
    if mn == "xorq" { push_str(sb, "  xorq %rbx, %rax\n") }
  }
  ## store the result (%rax) back into `dest`'s frame slot; push it too (the intrinsic's value in
  ## expression position — discarded when it is a bare statement, as num.al's operators use it).
  push_str(sb, "  movq %rax, -")
  push_int(sb, off)
  push_str(sb, "(%rbp)\n  pushq %rax\n")
}


## The WORD width of an operator/inline callee's RESULT (a user struct → its layout words, else 1).
operator_result_words := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), ci : i64, a : rt::Arena) -> usize {
  d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(decls), usize(ci))))
  rbn := base_type_name(src, d.ret_ts, d.ret_tl)
  if rbn.n != 0 and struct_decl_of(decls, src, rbn.s, rbn.n) >= 0 { return struct_words(decls, src, rbn.s, rbn.n, a) }
  1
}

## Push ALL words of an inline-operator OPERAND onto the value stack (word 0 first / at the bottom),
## returning the count pushed. A struct/enum `Var` pushes its slot words; a nested operator-routed Bin
## expands (its result's words land on the stack); anything else is a 1-word scalar via `emit_gas`.
emit_operand_words := fn(e : ptr(Expr), in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena, in out nl : usize) -> usize {
  ## A `bytes(…)` view and a string literal are already proven two-word values. Keep them on the
  ## same `{ptr,len}` choke point used by ordinary call arguments; the inline caller supplies the
  ## addressable scratch home implicitly by copying the two pushed words into its inline frame.
  bva := bytes_view_arg(e, cx.src, a)
  if unchecked bitcast(usize, bva) != 0 {
    emit_str_pair(e, sb, cx, a, nl)
    return 2
  }
  if str_lit_info(e).is_s {
    emit_str_pair(e, sb, cx, a, nl)
    return 2
  }
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Var(s, n) => {
      if slot_of(cx.slots, cx.src, s, n) >= 0 {
        ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, s, n)))
        if ent.ek == 4 {
          emit_str_pair(e, sb, cx, a, nl)
          return 2
        }
        if ent.ek == 2 or ent.ek == 3 {
          mut w := 0
          if ent.ek == 2 { w = struct_words(cx.decls, cx.src, ent.sns, ent.snl, deref(cx.mar)) }
          else { w = 1 + enum_inst_words(cx.decls, cx.src, ent.sns, ent.snl, deref(cx.mar)) }
          ## A BY-REFERENCE aggregate PARAM (`is_ref`): its frame slot holds a POINTER to the caller's
          ## word 0, NOT the words inline. Load that pointer (`emit_agg_base_addr`), then push word k
          ## from `+(k*8)(ptr)` — the pointee layout `emit_struct_value` / `field_base_ref` read. Without
          ## this the words were read straight off the frame slot: the compiler crashed (a small param
          ## `off` underflowed `push_frame_word`'s checked `(off - k + 1)`) or pushed the pointer + garbage.
          if ent.is_ref {
            emit_agg_base_addr(ent, sb)
            push_str(sb, "  movq %rax, %rbx\n")
            mut k := 0
            while k < w {
              push_str(sb, "  movq ")
              push_int(sb, i64(k * 8))
              push_str(sb, "(%rbx), %rax\n  pushq %rax\n")
              k += 1
            }
            return w
          }
          mut k := 0
          while k < w {
            push_str(sb, "  movq -")
            push_frame_word(sb, ent.off, k)
            push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rax\n  pushq %rax\n")
            k += 1
          }
          return w
        }
      }
      emit_gas(e, sb, cx, a, nl)
      return 1
    }
    ## A struct LITERAL operand (`S(f = v, …)`): deliver its words via `emit_struct_value` (into the
    ## return registers, handling every field kind), then push them onto the value stack word 0 first —
    ## matching the aggregate-Var path. Enables a routed operator over a struct-literal operand
    ## (`u128(a=..,b=..) * x`). Scalar/str-field structs; a multiword-field literal rides
    ## `emit_struct_value`'s own agg-temp path (reserved only when in scope).
    Expr::StructLit(cs, cl, nf, fhead) => {
      w := struct_words(cx.decls, cx.src, cs, cl, a)
      emit_struct_value(e, sb, cx, a, nl)
      mut k := 0
      while k < w {
        push_str(sb, "  pushq ")
        emit_retreg(sb, k)
        push_str(sb, "\n")
        k += 1
      }
      return w
    }
    Expr::Bin(op, l, r) => {
      mut ots := expr_type_span(l, cx)
      if ots.n == 0 { ots = expr_type_span(r, cx) }
      opci := operator_decl_idx(cx.decls, cx.src, op, ots.s, ots.n, a)
      if opci >= 0 and cx.inl_tmp >= 0 {
        ## slice B: bind the generic operator's comptime value (`N` ← the operand's `192`) around
        ## the expansion AND the result-width read (both fold the operator's `uint(N)` spans).
        didb := op_ct_bind(cx.decls, cx.src, opci, ots.s, ots.n, a)
        emit_inline_binop(op, opci, l, r, sb, cx, a, nl)
        orw := operator_result_words(cx.decls, cx.src, opci, a)
        if didb { ct_bind_pop() }
        return orw
      }
      emit_gas(e, sb, cx, a, nl)
      return 1
    }
    _ => {
      emit_gas(e, sb, cx, a, nl)
      return 1
    }
  }
}

## Classify the aggregate parameter forms that the inline value-stack path can reason about. A nominal
## struct/enum and the structural `str` pair have proven word layouts; fixed arrays/tuples and other
## aggregate spellings remain a separate ABI surface. Returning 2 for those lets the call site reject
## them before a one-word fallback can silently miscompile an inline expansion.
inline_param_kind := fn(decls : ptr(rt::Vec), src : ptr(u8), pm : Param) -> u8 {
  if str_at((src + pm.ts), pm.tl) == "str" { return 1 }
  if pm.pmode == 1 { return 2 }
  if pm.tl != 0 and (str_at((src + pm.ts), 1) == "[" or str_at((src + pm.ts), 1) == "(") { return 2 }
  bn := base_type_name(src, pm.ts, pm.tl)
  if bn.n != 0 and struct_decl_of(decls, src, bn.s, bn.n) >= 0 { return 1 }
  if bn.n != 0 and enum_decl_of(decls, src, bn.s, bn.n) >= 0 { return 1 }
  0
}

## Expand an `@inline` callee (index `inl_ci` into `cx.decls`) at the call site (§3.1/§3.5) —
## the CALL entry point (arg list). Evaluate each scalar arg or ALL words of a proven aggregate onto
## the value stack, then hand off to `emit_inline_body` (shared with the operator-routing path) to bind
## params + emit the body.
## The caller guarantees `inl_ci >= 0 and cx.inl_tmp >= 0`.
emit_inline_call := fn(inl_ci : i64, args_head : ptr(mut Arg), in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena, in out nl : usize) {
  icd := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(cx.decls), usize(inl_ci))))
  ## evaluate ALL args onto the stack FIRST (arg 0 at the bottom), THEN pop them into the scratch
  ## param slots in REVERSE — so a nested `@inline` in a LATER arg, which transiently reuses the
  ## same scratch, cannot clobber an earlier arg's value (it is still on the stack).
  mut pp := icd.params_head
  mut pi := 0
  while pp != 0 {
    pm := deref(param_p(pp))
    if pm.pmode != 0 {
      panic("selfhost: @inline parameter mode/representation is unsupported; keep @inline parameters on the proven scalar or value-aggregate forms")
    }
    ae := arg_expr_at(args_head, pi, a)
    pk := inline_param_kind(cx.decls, cx.src, pm)
    if pk == 1 {
      got := emit_operand_words(ae, sb, cx, a, nl)
      want := param_words(cx.decls, cx.src, pm, a)
      if got != want { panic("selfhost: @inline aggregate argument shape is unsupported; the emitted word count does not match the parameter ABI") }
    } else if pk == 2 {
      panic("selfhost: @inline aggregate parameter shape is unsupported; use a proven nominal struct/enum or str value form")
    } else {
      emit_gas(ae, sb, cx, a, nl)
    }
    pp = pm.next
    pi += 1
  }
  emit_inline_body(inl_ci, sb, cx, a, nl)
}

## The routed integer operand type name, or an empty string for aggregate/float/non-integer operators.
## The operation-site guard uses this exact width so the shipped `num.al` body can remain a raw
## implementation for every integer overload.
routed_int_name := fn(l : ptr(Expr), r : ptr(Expr), cx : ptr(LCtx)) -> str {
  mut t := expr_type_span(l, cx)
  if t.n == 0 { t = expr_type_span(r, cx) }
  if t.n == 0 { return "" }
  b := base_type_name(cx.src, t.s, t.n)
  n := str_at((cx.src + b.s), b.n)
  if n == "u8" or n == "u16" or n == "u32" or n == "u64" or n == "usize" { return n }
  if n == "i8" or n == "i16" or n == "i32" or n == "i64" or n == "isize" { return n }
  ""
}

## Emit the checked arithmetic guard for an already-evaluated routed native integer pair. The caller has
## popped the right operand into %rbx and the left into %rax; this helper probes a temporary result for
## +/−/* so the actual routed body still receives the untouched operands. Division and remainder only
## need the pre-operation zero / MIN÷-1 tests. Every failure is the direct inline trap (CG-13), never the
## routed body's library logic.
emit_routed_int_guard := fn(op : i64, typ : str, l : ptr(Expr), r : ptr(Expr), in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, cx : ptr(LCtx)) {
  dsigned := is_signed_expr(l, cx) or is_signed_expr(r, cx)
  gsigned := dsigned or expr_is_uneg(l) or expr_is_uneg(r)
  narrow := typ == "u8" or typ == "u16" or typ == "u32" or typ == "i8" or typ == "i16" or typ == "i32"
  mut bits := 64
  if typ == "u8" or typ == "i8" { bits = 8 }
  if typ == "u16" or typ == "i16" { bits = 16 }
  if typ == "u32" or typ == "i32" { bits = 32 }
  if op == 16 {
    if narrow and (not expr_is_zero(l)) {
      push_str(sb, "  movq %rax, %rcx\n  addq %rbx, %rcx\n")
      if dsigned {
        if bits == 8 { push_str(sb, "  movsbq %cl, %rdx\n") }
        if bits == 16 { push_str(sb, "  movswq %cx, %rdx\n") }
        if bits == 32 { push_str(sb, "  movslq %ecx, %rdx\n") }
        push_str(sb, "  cmpq %rdx, %rcx\n  je 1f\n  ud2\n1:\n")
      } else {
        push_str(sb, "  movq %rcx, %rdx\n  shrq $")
        push_int(sb, bits)
        push_str(sb, ", %rdx\n  jz 1f\n  ud2\n1:\n")
      }
    } else if not expr_is_zero(l) {
      push_str(sb, "  movq %rax, %rcx\n  addq %rbx, %rcx\n")
      if gsigned { push_str(sb, "  jno 1f\n  ud2\n1:\n") }
      else { push_str(sb, "  jnc 1f\n  ud2\n1:\n") }
    }
  }
  if op == 17 {
    if narrow and (not expr_is_zero(l)) {
      push_str(sb, "  movq %rax, %rcx\n  subq %rbx, %rcx\n")
      if dsigned {
        if bits == 8 { push_str(sb, "  movsbq %cl, %rdx\n") }
        if bits == 16 { push_str(sb, "  movswq %cx, %rdx\n") }
        if bits == 32 { push_str(sb, "  movslq %ecx, %rdx\n") }
        push_str(sb, "  cmpq %rdx, %rcx\n  je 1f\n  ud2\n1:\n")
      } else {
        push_str(sb, "  movq %rcx, %rdx\n  shrq $")
        push_int(sb, bits)
        push_str(sb, ", %rdx\n  jz 1f\n  ud2\n1:\n")
      }
    } else if not expr_is_zero(l) {
      push_str(sb, "  movq %rax, %rcx\n  subq %rbx, %rcx\n")
      if gsigned { push_str(sb, "  jno 1f\n  ud2\n1:\n") }
      else { push_str(sb, "  jnc 1f\n  ud2\n1:\n") }
    }
  }
  if op == 18 {
    if narrow and (not expr_is_zero(l)) {
      push_str(sb, "  movq %rax, %rcx\n  imulq %rbx, %rcx\n")
      if dsigned {
        if bits == 8 { push_str(sb, "  movsbq %cl, %rdx\n") }
        if bits == 16 { push_str(sb, "  movswq %cx, %rdx\n") }
        if bits == 32 { push_str(sb, "  movslq %ecx, %rdx\n") }
        push_str(sb, "  cmpq %rdx, %rcx\n  je 1f\n  ud2\n1:\n")
      } else {
        push_str(sb, "  movq %rcx, %rdx\n  shrq $")
        push_int(sb, bits)
        push_str(sb, ", %rdx\n  jz 1f\n  ud2\n1:\n")
      }
    } else if not expr_is_zero(l) {
      if dsigned {
        push_str(sb, "  movq %rax, %rcx\n  imulq %rbx, %rcx\n  jno 1f\n  ud2\n1:\n")
      } else {
        ## `mulq` consumes %rax/%rdx, so preserve both source words while probing CF.
        push_str(sb, "  movq %rax, %rcx\n  movq %rbx, %rsi\n  mulq %rbx\n  jnc 1f\n  ud2\n1:\n  movq %rcx, %rax\n  movq %rsi, %rbx\n")
      }
    }
  }
  if (op == 19 or op == 29) and (not expr_is_pos_num(r)) {
    push_str(sb, "  testq %rbx, %rbx\n  jnz 1f\n  ud2\n1:\n")
    if dsigned {
      push_str(sb, "  cmpq $-1, %rbx\n  jne 1f\n  movq $-9223372036854775808, %rdx\n  cmpq %rdx, %rax\n  jne 1f\n  ud2\n1:\n")
    }
  }
}

## Expand an `@inline` BINARY OPERATOR callee at a `Bin(op, l, r)` site (§2) — evaluate the two
## operands (each ALL of its words, `emit_operand_words`) onto the value stack (left at the bottom),
## then hand off to `emit_inline_body`. This is the operator-overloading path: `a <op> b` over a user
## type routes through the user's `@inline` `op` function (`operator_decl_idx`) instead of the built-in
## scalar/float lowering. The caller guarantees `inl_ci >= 0 and cx.inl_tmp >= 0` and 2 params.
emit_inline_binop := fn(op : i64, inl_ci : i64, l : ptr(Expr), r : ptr(Expr), in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena, in out nl : usize) {
  emit_operand_words(l, sb, cx, a, nl)
  emit_operand_words(r, sb, cx, a, nl)
  ## A routed native integer operation still owns the compiler's checked guard. Preserve the two
  ## operand words while the guard probes them, then restore the exact inline-expander stack shape.
  routed_typ := routed_int_name(l, r, cx)
  if cx.vchk and routed_typ != "" {
    push_str(sb, "  popq %rbx\n  popq %rax\n")
    emit_routed_int_guard(op, routed_typ, l, r, sb, cx)
    push_str(sb, "  pushq %rax\n  pushq %rbx\n")
  }
  ## A routed operator body is an implementation detail, not the owner of the operation's checked
  ## guard (CG-13). Force its mode off so a stale/pre-existing `comptime if verify.checked` in a body
  ## cannot create a second failure mechanism; the operation-site guard above remains mode-sensitive.
  ov := cx.vchk
  cx.vchk = false
  emit_inline_body(inl_ci, sb, cx, a, nl)
  cx.vchk = ov
}

## Shared core: the params' argument WORDS are ALREADY on the value stack (param 0 word 0 at the
## bottom … the last param's last word on top — laid out CONTIGUOUSLY in push order, which is exactly
## the scratch order). Pop `totw` words into the scratch in REVERSE (so a nested expansion in a later
## operand cannot clobber an earlier one, still on the stack), alias each param name to its scratch
## slot (float → ek 9; user struct/enum → ek 2/3 with the type span, occupying its words inline), then
## emit the callee body with params resolving to the args. A TAIL-EXPRESSION body emits its value (a
## struct result is delivered via the return convention and its words pushed); a STATEMENT body (num.al
## operators) aliases the callee's own body-locals PAST the param words and redirects `return` to a
## fresh inline-end label. The result is left PUSHED on the stack (the emit_gas value-position ABI).
emit_inline_body := fn(inl_ci : i64, in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena, in out nl : usize) {
  icd := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(cx.decls), usize(inl_ci))))
  totw := inline_callee_pwords(cx.decls, cx.src, inl_ci, a)
  ## NESTED-INLINE base (2b): this callee's params + body-locals occupy [inl_tmp, inl_tmp+totw+lcnt);
  ## a routed operator INSIDE the body (a num.al guard's `if out < a` routes `<` to cmp.al::lt) must
  ## expand into scratch PAST them. So bind params/locals at the current base, then ADVANCE cx.inl_tmp
  ## by the words this frame uses before emitting the body, and RESTORE it after. `inline_frame_need`
  ## reserves the summed footprint over nesting (see `inline_callee_nested_need`), so the advanced base
  ## stays inside the reservation.
  ov_inl := cx.inl_tmp
  mut kk := totw
  while kk > 0 {
    kk = kk - 1
    push_str(sb, "  popq %rax\n  movq %rax, -")
    push_int(sb, i64((usize(cx.inl_tmp) - kk + 1) * 8))
    push_str(sb, "(%rbp)\n")
  }
  saved := svec_len(cx.slots)
  ## alias each param name → its scratch slot at its CUMULATIVE word offset. A float param → ek 9 (xmm
  ## path); a USER struct/enum param → ek 2/3 with the type span, occupying its `param_words` words
  ## inline so a field/aggregate use resolves; otherwise a plain scalar (ek 0).
  mut qp := icd.params_head
  mut cum := 0
  while qp != 0 {
    qm := deref(param_p(qp))
    bn := base_type_name(cx.src, qm.ts, qm.tl)
    mut pw := 0
    if param_is_comptime(cx.src, qm.ns) == false { pw = param_words(cx.decls, cx.src, qm, a) }
    ## TYP-10 slice B: a COMPTIME VALUE parameter (`comptime N : u64` on a generic operator) is
    ## bound at expansion time (`ct_bind_push` at the route site) — it consumes NO argument word
    ## and aliases NO scratch slot (`pw` stays 0 above), so the value params' cumulative offsets
    ## are exactly the operand words on the stack.
    if param_is_comptime(cx.src, qm.ns) { pw = 0 }
    else if type_is_float(cx.decls, cx.src, qm.ts, qm.tl) {
      svec_push(deref(cx.slots), SlotEntry(ns = qm.ns, nl = qm.nl, off = usize(cx.inl_tmp) - cum, sns = 0, snl = 0, ek = 9, estride = 1, eek = 0, is_ref = false))
    } else if str_at((cx.src + qm.ts), qm.tl) == "str" {
      svec_push(deref(cx.slots), SlotEntry(ns = qm.ns, nl = qm.nl, off = usize(cx.inl_tmp) - cum, sns = 0, snl = 0, ek = 4, estride = 1, eek = 0, is_ref = false))
    } else if bn.n != 0 and struct_decl_of(cx.decls, cx.src, bn.s, bn.n) >= 0 {
      svec_push(deref(cx.slots), SlotEntry(ns = qm.ns, nl = qm.nl, off = usize(cx.inl_tmp) - cum, sns = bn.s, snl = bn.n, ek = 2, estride = 1, eek = 0, is_ref = false))
    } else if bn.n != 0 and enum_decl_of(cx.decls, cx.src, bn.s, bn.n) >= 0 {
      svec_push(deref(cx.slots), SlotEntry(ns = qm.ns, nl = qm.nl, off = usize(cx.inl_tmp) - cum, sns = bn.s, snl = bn.n, ek = 3, estride = 1, eek = 0, is_ref = false, tmod_s = 0, tmod_l = 0))
    } else {
      svec_push(deref(cx.slots), SlotEntry(ns = qm.ns, nl = qm.nl, off = usize(cx.inl_tmp) - cum, sns = 0, snl = 0, ek = 0, estride = 1, eek = 0, is_ref = false))
    }
    cum += pw
    qp = qm.next
  }
  if icd.body_stmts == 0 {
    ## TAIL-EXPRESSION body — emit its value; params resolve to the scratch aliases. Advance the
    ## nested-inline base past this callee's params (a tail-expr body has no locals) so a routed
    ## operator in the value expands into fresh scratch.
    cx.inl_tmp = ov_inl - i64(totw)
    rbn := base_type_name(cx.src, icd.ret_ts, icd.ret_tl)
    if rbn.n != 0 and struct_decl_of(cx.decls, cx.src, rbn.s, rbn.n) >= 0 {
      ## an AGGREGATE result (`@inline + := fn(a, b) -> Vec2 { Vec2(…) }`): `emit_gas` would placeholder
      ## a StructLit as `pushq $0`, so deliver the result words via the struct-return convention
      ## (`emit_struct_value` → word k in `emit_retreg(k)`) and push them word 0 first. The caller
      ## stores the words (a 1-word result stores like a scalar).
      rw := struct_words(cx.decls, cx.src, rbn.s, rbn.n, deref(cx.mar))
      emit_struct_value(icd.value, sb, cx, a, nl)
      for k in 0..rw {
        push_str(sb, "  pushq ")
        emit_retreg(sb, k)
        push_str(sb, "\n")
      }
    } else {
      emit_gas(icd.value, sb, cx, a, nl)
    }
  } else {
    ## STATEMENT body — reserve+alias the callee's own body-LOCALS into the scratch PAST the params
    ## (a collect_slots over the callee body gives their names/offsets), then emit the body with the
    ## `return` REDIRECTED: point `cx.epi` at a fresh inline-end label + set the callee's scalar/float
    ## return convention, so `return X` delivers X (%rax / %xmm0) and jumps to inline-end (not the
    ## caller's epilogue). At inline-end, push the delivered value.
    mut ls := SVec(base = 0, len = 0, cap = 0, arena = cx.mar)
    svec_new(ls, cx.mar, 8)
    ## TYP-10 slice B: SEED the param bindings before collecting the body locals, so a local
    ## copied FROM a param (`r : uint(N) = a` in a generic operator) reserves its aggregate words
    ## (previously such a local fell to the scalar fallback — 1 word — and the field writes
    ## clobbered the neighbouring scratch). The seeds are SKIPPED below and the locals' offsets
    ## REBASED by the seed count, so a pre-slice-B body (which never copies a param into an
    ## aggregate local) gets byte-identical slots. Mirrors the reservation-side seeding
    ## (`inline_callee_lcount`) — the two MUST agree.
    lseed := seed_inline_params(ls, cx.decls, cx.src, inl_ci, a)
    lsub := Subst(gps = 0, gpl = 0, its = 0, itl = 0, gps2 = 0, gpl2 = 0, its2 = 0, itl2 = 0, gps3 = 0, gpl3 = 0, its3 = 0, itl3 = 0)
    collect_slots(ls, icd.body_stmts, cx.src, cx.decls, deref(cx.mar), cx.mar, ptr(lsub))
    lcnt := svec_len(ptr(ls)) - lseed
    mut li := 0
    mut lcum := 0
    while li < lcnt {
      le := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, ptr(ls), li + lseed))
      if le.nl != 0 {
        ## TYP-10 slice B: place the body locals WORD-DENSELY below the params. The former map
        ## (`… - le.off`) flipped the slot-table order, so a MULTI-WORD local (struct/enum/array/
        ## str — a body local copied from a param, e.g. `r : uint(N) = a`) had its word block
        ## (`off - k`) overlap the NEXT local's slot — a latent collision, hit as soon as an
        ## inline body held an aggregate local followed by another local. Each local now sits
        ## immediately below the previous one's extent (`lcum` = words placed). The word count
        ## is the anonymous-filler run before the base (+ the base word for a non-aggregate
        ## kind — a str/slice filler IS a payload word; a struct/enum/array filler run already
        ## counts every word). Byte-identical for a scalar-only body (`lcum` == `le.off`).
        mut lwc := 0
        mut lj := li + lseed
        while lj > 0 {
          pf := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, ptr(ls), lj - 1))
          ## independent guard-then-act `if`s (a `;`-pair inside an if-block with an `else` is
          ## not a parseable shape under the self-host parser).
          if pf.nl == 0 { lwc += 1 }
          if pf.nl == 0 { lj = lj - 1 }
          if pf.nl != 0 { lj = 0 }
        }
        if le.ek != 2 and le.ek != 3 and (le.ek != 5 or le.is_ref) { lwc += 1 }
        svec_push(deref(cx.slots), SlotEntry(ns = le.ns, nl = le.nl, off = usize(cx.inl_tmp) - totw - lcum, sns = le.sns, snl = le.snl, ek = le.ek, estride = le.estride, eek = le.eek, is_ref = false, tmod_s = le.tmod_s, tmod_l = le.tmod_l))
        lcum += lwc
      }
      li += 1
    }
    ov_epi := cx.epi
    ov_rf := cx.ret_float
    ov_re := cx.ret_enum
    ov_rst := cx.ret_struct
    ov_rt := cx.ret_tuple
    ov_rstr := cx.ret_str
    ov_rsr := cx.ret_sret
    iend := nl
    nl += 1
    srbn := base_type_name(cx.src, icd.ret_ts, icd.ret_tl)
    cstruct := srbn.n != 0 and struct_decl_of(cx.decls, cx.src, srbn.s, srbn.n) >= 0
    cfloat := type_is_float(cx.decls, cx.src, icd.ret_ts, icd.ret_tl) and not cstruct
    cx.epi = iend
    cx.ret_float = cfloat
    cx.ret_enum = false
    ## a STRUCT-returning statement body (`… -> Vec2 { mut r = a; …; return r }`): set `ret_struct` so
    ## each `return X` delivers the words via `emit_struct_value`'s retreg convention; at inline-end push
    ## them word 0 first (like the tail-StructLit path). A scalar/float body keeps the 1-word push.
    cx.ret_struct = cstruct
    cx.ret_tuple = false
    cx.ret_str = false
    cx.ret_sret = false
    ## Advance the nested-inline base past this callee's params + body-locals (bound above at the
    ## un-advanced base) so a routed operator inside the body gets fresh scratch; restored below.
    cx.inl_tmp = ov_inl - i64(totw) - i64(lcnt)
    emit_stmts(icd.body_stmts, sb, cx, nl)
    cx.epi = ov_epi
    cx.ret_float = ov_rf
    cx.ret_enum = ov_re
    cx.ret_struct = ov_rst
    cx.ret_tuple = ov_rt
    cx.ret_str = ov_rstr
    cx.ret_sret = ov_rsr
    emit_label(sb, iend)
    push_str(sb, ":\n")
    if cstruct {
      srw := struct_words(cx.decls, cx.src, srbn.s, srbn.n, deref(cx.mar))
      mut sk := 0
      while sk < srw {
        push_str(sb, "  pushq ")
        emit_retreg(sb, sk)
        push_str(sb, "\n")
        sk += 1
      }
    } else if cfloat {
      push_str(sb, "  movq %xmm0, %rax\n  pushq %rax\n")
    } else {
      push_str(sb, "  pushq %rax\n")
    }
  }
  cx.inl_tmp = ov_inl
  svec_truncate(deref(cx.slots), saved)
}

## Emit a call to a §7.2 SLICE-VARIADIC callee `f(xs : ...T)`. The callee is an ORDINARY runtime fn
## whose last param is a by-reference `[T]` slice; the call site GATHERS the trailing args into a
## contiguous stack block and passes a `{ptr, len}` slice as that param's argument. Layout, on the
## stack (down-growing), from lowest address up:
##   [ data[0] ][ data[1] ] … [ data[ng-1] ]   ← the gathered element words (ascending: element i at
##                                                data-ptr + i*8, matching the arena/vec `push` order
##                                                the by-ref slice read path expects)
##   [ block.ptr = &data[0] ][ block.len = ng ] ← the {ptr, len} block (word 0 = ptr at lower address,
##                                                word 1 = len, exactly the `emit_array_slice_assign`
##                                                shape a `Slice(T)` param derefs)
## The block's word-0 ADDRESS (`%rsp` after the pushes) is the slice arg value → the param's register.
## After the `call`, the whole `(ng + 2)`-word gather is reclaimed. Balanced (net %rsp change 0), so it
## composes with the value-stack model and NESTS (a slice-variadic call inside a gathered arg cleans up
## its own block). FIRST INCREMENT: NO leading fixed params (nfixed == 0), native scalar element,
## ≤6 gathered args (register-only), x86_64. A leading fixed param / >6 args / float element are
## follow-ups — fail-loud so there is never a silent miscompile.
emit_slice_variadic_call := fn(sv_ci : i64, cs : usize, cl : usize, args_head : ptr(mut Arg), nargs : usize, in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena, in out nl : usize) {
  scd := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(cx.decls), usize(sv_ci))))
  nfixed := usize(scd.arity) - 1
  ## §7.2 LEADING FIXED params (`fn(base : A, rest : ...T)`). The fixed args ride argreg(0..nfixed-1)
  ## and the gathered slice rides argreg(nfixed), so `nfixed <= 5` (the slice must still land in a GPR
  ## arg register). Each fixed param is restricted to a native NON-float SCALAR in this increment: the
  ## fixed-arg gather uses `emit_gas` (one word) + a pop into its register, so a struct/enum/float/by-ref
  ## fixed param (a different ABI) fails loud rather than silently miscompiling.
  if nfixed > 5 { panic("selfhost: §7.2 slice variadic with >5 leading fixed parameters is a follow-up") }
  mut fp := scd.params_head
  mut fpi := 0
  while fp != 0 and fpi < nfixed {
    fpm := deref(param_p(fp))
    fbn := base_type_name(cx.src, fpm.ts, fpm.tl)
    fis_struct := fbn.n != 0 and struct_decl_of(cx.decls, cx.src, fbn.s, fbn.n) >= 0
    fis_enum := fbn.n != 0 and enum_decl_of(cx.decls, cx.src, fbn.s, fbn.n) >= 0
    if fis_struct or fis_enum or type_is_float(cx.decls, cx.src, fpm.ts, fpm.tl) or fpm.pmode != 0 {
      panic("selfhost: §7.2 slice variadic leading fixed param must be a native non-float scalar in this increment")
    }
    fp = fpm.next
    fpi += 1
  }
  ng := nargs - nfixed
  ## NO `ng` CAP. Both gather paths below are loop-general in `ng`: the scalar path pushes `ng` words
  ## with a `while` loop, the aggregate path reserves `ng*estride*8` and fills element-by-element in a
  ## `while` loop; the reclaim (`addq $(ng+2)*8` / `$(ng*estride+2)*8`) is `ng`-parametric. The old
  ## `ng > 6` guard was purely conservative (a register-count intuition that does not apply — the
  ## gather never uses argument registers per-element). ALIGNMENT: this emitter keeps `%rsp`'s 16-byte
  ## residue invariant only across BALANCED sequences; the gather leaves `ng + 2` (or `ng*estride + 2`)
  ## words across the `call`, so an ODD total makes `%rsp` 8-off at the `call` — but that already held
  ## for the ≤6 path (e.g. `sum(10,20,12)`, ng == 3) and is harmless here: the callee is a pure Alatyr
  ## fn that reads the slice through an 8-aligned `%rsp`-derived data pointer (no libc / no 16-aligned
  ## SSE spill), so no padding is needed. Element kind (scalar / struct / enum) and leading-fixed-param
  ## count are still gated above; only the `ng` count is now unbounded.
  ## Determine the `...T` ELEMENT kind from the callee's LAST (rest) param. A native scalar/float
  ## element is ONE word per arg (the fast path below). A STRUCT/ENUM element is `stride` words per
  ## arg: reserve a contiguous ng*stride-word data block below %rsp, then fill element i (ascending)
  ## by materializing arg[nfixed+i] BY REFERENCE (`emit_arg` → a word-0 pointer, the aggregate call
  ## ABI) and copying its `stride` words (word k at +k*8) into data + (i*stride + k)*8. The rest of
  ## the sequence (the {ptr,len} block above the data, leading-fixed args, the call, the reclaim,
  ## the float-return route) mirrors the scalar path.
  mut rp := scd.params_head
  mut rpi := 0
  while rp != 0 and rpi < nfixed { rp = deref(param_p(rp)).next; rpi += 1 }
  rebn := base_type_name(cx.src, deref(param_p(rp)).ts, deref(param_p(rp)).tl)
  re_sdi := struct_decl_of(cx.decls, cx.src, rebn.s, rebn.n)
  re_edi := enum_decl_of(cx.decls, cx.src, rebn.s, rebn.n)
  mut estride := 0
  if re_sdi >= 0 { estride = struct_words(cx.decls, cx.src, rebn.s, rebn.n, a) }
  else if re_edi >= 0 { estride = 1 + enum_inst_words(cx.decls, cx.src, rebn.s, rebn.n, a) }
  if estride != 0 {
    aggsave := cx.agg_next
    push_str(sb, "  subq $")
    push_int(sb, i64(ng * estride * 8))
    push_str(sb, ", %rsp\n")
    mut ei := 0
    while ei < ng {
      emit_arg(arg_expr_at(args_head, nfixed + ei, a), sb, cx, a, nl, 0 - 1)
      push_str(sb, "  popq %rsi\n")
      mut ck := 0
      while ck < estride {
        push_str(sb, "  movq ")
        push_int(sb, i64(ck * 8))
        push_str(sb, "(%rsi), %rax\n  movq %rax, ")
        push_int(sb, i64((ei * estride + ck) * 8))
        push_str(sb, "(%rsp)\n")
        ck += 1
      }
      ei += 1
    }
    push_str(sb, "  movq %rsp, %rax\n  pushq $")
    push_int(sb, i64(ng))
    push_str(sb, "\n  pushq %rax\n")
    mut fai2 := 0
    while fai2 < nfixed {
      emit_gas(arg_expr_at(args_head, fai2, a), sb, cx, a, nl)
      fai2 += 1
    }
    mut fpj2 := nfixed
    while fpj2 != 0 {
      fpj2 = fpj2 - 1
      push_str(sb, "  popq ")
      emit_argreg(sb, fpj2)
      push_str(sb, "\n")
    }
    push_str(sb, "  movq %rsp, ")
    emit_argreg(sb, nfixed)
    push_str(sb, "\n  call ")
    emit_mangled_call(sb, cx.src, cs, cl, cx.mod_s, cx.mod_l, cx.decls)
    emit_overload_suffix(sb, cs, cl, args_head, cx, a)
    push_str(sb, "\n  addq $")
    push_int(sb, i64((ng * estride + 2) * 8))
    push_str(sb, ", %rsp\n")
    cx.agg_next = aggsave
    if callee_returns_float(cx.decls, cx.src, sv_ci) {
      push_str(sb, "  movq %xmm0, %rax\n  pushq %rax\n")
    } else {
      push_str(sb, "  pushq %rax\n")
    }
    return
  }
  ## gather the trailing args onto the stack in REVERSE (arg[nargs-1] pushed first) so the LAST push
  ## is arg[nfixed] — it lands at the lowest address (top of stack) = data[0], and element i sits at
  ## data-ptr + i*8 (ascending). Each arg is a native scalar → `emit_gas` leaves one word on the stack.
  mut gi := nargs
  while gi > nfixed {
    gi = gi - 1
    emit_gas(arg_expr_at(args_head, gi, a), sb, cx, a, nl)
  }
  ## build the {ptr, len} block above the data: ptr = &data[0] (current %rsp), len = ng.
  push_str(sb, "  movq %rsp, %rax\n")            ## %rax = &data[0]
  push_str(sb, "  pushq $")
  push_int(sb, i64(ng))                          ## block word 1 = len (higher address)
  push_str(sb, "\n  pushq %rax\n")               ## block word 0 = ptr (lower address); %rsp = &block
  ## LEADING FIXED args: evaluate them onto the stack (each `emit_gas` leaves one word), THEN pop them
  ## into argreg(0..nfixed-1) in reverse — the emit_call_args convention. Doing the FULL evaluation
  ## before ANY register is set means a fixed arg that is itself a call cannot clobber an already-placed
  ## arg register, and its own push/pop leaves the {ptr,len} block (at higher addresses) untouched. The
  ## nfixed pushes + nfixed pops are net-zero on %rsp, so %rsp = &block still holds for the slice below.
  mut fai := 0
  while fai < nfixed {
    emit_gas(arg_expr_at(args_head, fai, a), sb, cx, a, nl)
    fai += 1
  }
  mut fpj := nfixed
  while fpj != 0 {
    fpj = fpj - 1
    push_str(sb, "  popq ")
    emit_argreg(sb, fpj)
    push_str(sb, "\n")
  }
  push_str(sb, "  movq %rsp, ")                  ## the slice arg value = &block → the param's register
  emit_argreg(sb, nfixed)
  push_str(sb, "\n  call ")
  emit_mangled_call(sb, cx.src, cs, cl, cx.mod_s, cx.mod_l, cx.decls)
  emit_overload_suffix(sb, cs, cl, args_head, cx, a)
  push_str(sb, "\n  addq $")                     ## reclaim the (ng data + 2 block) words
  push_int(sb, i64((ng + 2) * 8))
  push_str(sb, ", %rsp\n")
  if callee_returns_float(cx.decls, cx.src, sv_ci) {
    push_str(sb, "  movq %xmm0, %rax\n  pushq %rax\n")
  } else {
    push_str(sb, "  pushq %rax\n")
  }
}

## Expand a comptime-VARIADIC callee (Functions §7.1) at the call site. The leading FIXED params bind
## to the leading args (evaluated onto the value stack, popped into scratch in reverse — exactly the
## `@inline` protocol); the trailing args form the comptime PACK, made available to the body via
## `cx.pack_args`. The body's `comptime for v in <pack>` (a `CompForRange` with a null hi) unrolls over
## that pack. `nfixed = arity - 1` (the last param is the `...` rest). The caller guarantees `cx.inl_tmp >= 0`.
emit_variadic_call := fn(var_ci : i64, args_head : ptr(mut Arg), in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena, in out nl : usize) {
  icd := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(cx.decls), usize(var_ci))))
  nfixed := usize(icd.arity) - 1
  mut pi := 0
  while pi < nfixed {
    emit_gas(arg_expr_at(args_head, pi, a), sb, cx, a, nl)
    pi += 1
  }
  emit_variadic_body(var_ci, args_head, nfixed, sb, cx, a, nl)
}

## Shared with `emit_variadic_call`: the FIXED params' arg words are already on the value stack. Pop them
## into scratch (reverse, like `emit_inline_body`), alias each fixed param to its slot, then point
## `cx.pack_args` at the first trailing (pack) arg + record the pack param name, reserve+alias the body's
## own locals PAST the fixed-param words, and emit the statement body with `return` redirected to a fresh
## inline-end label — so a variadic call lowers to straight-line code with its pack loop unrolled inline.
emit_variadic_body := fn(var_ci : i64, args_head : ptr(mut Arg), nfixed : usize, in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena, in out nl : usize) {
  icd := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(cx.decls), usize(var_ci))))
  ## fixed-param word total (the pack param contributes no scratch words — its args stay as Arg exprs)
  mut totw := 0
  mut cpp := icd.params_head
  mut cpi := 0
  while cpp != 0 and cpi < nfixed {
    cpm := deref(param_p(cpp))
    totw += param_words(cx.decls, cx.src, cpm, a)
    cpp = cpm.next
    cpi += 1
  }
  mut kk := totw
  while kk > 0 {
    kk = kk - 1
    push_str(sb, "  popq %rax\n  movq %rax, -")
    push_int(sb, i64((usize(cx.inl_tmp) - kk + 1) * 8))
    push_str(sb, "(%rbp)\n")
  }
  saved := svec_len(cx.slots)
  ## alias each FIXED param name → its scratch slot at its cumulative word offset (float ek 9 / struct ek 2
  ## / enum ek 3 / scalar ek 0 — the same classification as `emit_inline_body`).
  mut qp := icd.params_head
  mut cum := 0
  mut qi := 0
  while qp != 0 and qi < nfixed {
    qm := deref(param_p(qp))
    bn := base_type_name(cx.src, qm.ts, qm.tl)
    pw := param_words(cx.decls, cx.src, qm, a)
    if type_is_float(cx.decls, cx.src, qm.ts, qm.tl) {
      svec_push(deref(cx.slots), SlotEntry(ns = qm.ns, nl = qm.nl, off = usize(cx.inl_tmp) - cum, sns = 0, snl = 0, ek = 9, estride = 1, eek = 0, is_ref = false))
    } else if bn.n != 0 and struct_decl_of(cx.decls, cx.src, bn.s, bn.n) >= 0 {
      svec_push(deref(cx.slots), SlotEntry(ns = qm.ns, nl = qm.nl, off = usize(cx.inl_tmp) - cum, sns = bn.s, snl = bn.n, ek = 2, estride = 1, eek = 0, is_ref = false))
    } else if bn.n != 0 and enum_decl_of(cx.decls, cx.src, bn.s, bn.n) >= 0 {
      svec_push(deref(cx.slots), SlotEntry(ns = qm.ns, nl = qm.nl, off = usize(cx.inl_tmp) - cum, sns = bn.s, snl = bn.n, ek = 3, estride = 1, eek = 0, is_ref = false, tmod_s = 0, tmod_l = 0))
    } else {
      svec_push(deref(cx.slots), SlotEntry(ns = qm.ns, nl = qm.nl, off = usize(cx.inl_tmp) - cum, sns = 0, snl = 0, ek = 0, estride = 1, eek = 0, is_ref = false))
    }
    cum += pw
    qp = qm.next
    qi += 1
  }
  ## point the pack at the first trailing arg (the unroll reads `cx.pack_args`; a null-hi CompForRange is
  ## itself the pack marker, so the pack param name need not be threaded)
  ov_pa := cx.pack_args
  mut ah := args_head
  mut aidx := 0
  while aidx < nfixed and ah != 0 { ah = deref(arg_p(ah)).next; aidx += 1 }
  cx.pack_args = ah
  if icd.body_stmts == 0 {
    emit_gas(icd.value, sb, cx, a, nl)
  } else {
    mut ls := SVec(base = 0, len = 0, cap = 0, arena = cx.mar)
    svec_new(ls, cx.mar, 8)
    lsub := Subst(gps = 0, gpl = 0, its = 0, itl = 0, gps2 = 0, gpl2 = 0, its2 = 0, itl2 = 0, gps3 = 0, gpl3 = 0, its3 = 0, itl3 = 0)
    collect_slots(ls, icd.body_stmts, cx.src, cx.decls, deref(cx.mar), cx.mar, ptr(lsub))
    lcnt := svec_len(ptr(ls))
    mut li := 0
    while li < lcnt {
      le := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, ptr(ls), li))
      if le.nl != 0 {
        svec_push(deref(cx.slots), SlotEntry(ns = le.ns, nl = le.nl, off = usize(cx.inl_tmp) - totw - le.off, sns = le.sns, snl = le.snl, ek = le.ek, estride = le.estride, eek = le.eek, is_ref = false, tmod_s = le.tmod_s, tmod_l = le.tmod_l))
      }
      li += 1
    }
    ov_epi := cx.epi
    ov_rf := cx.ret_float
    ov_re := cx.ret_enum
    ov_rst := cx.ret_struct
    ov_rt := cx.ret_tuple
    ov_rstr := cx.ret_str
    ov_rsr := cx.ret_sret
    iend := nl
    nl += 1
    srbn := base_type_name(cx.src, icd.ret_ts, icd.ret_tl)
    cstruct := srbn.n != 0 and struct_decl_of(cx.decls, cx.src, srbn.s, srbn.n) >= 0
    cfloat := type_is_float(cx.decls, cx.src, icd.ret_ts, icd.ret_tl) and not cstruct
    cx.epi = iend
    cx.ret_float = cfloat
    cx.ret_enum = false
    cx.ret_struct = cstruct
    cx.ret_tuple = false
    cx.ret_str = false
    cx.ret_sret = false
    emit_stmts(icd.body_stmts, sb, cx, nl)
    cx.epi = ov_epi
    cx.ret_float = ov_rf
    cx.ret_enum = ov_re
    cx.ret_struct = ov_rst
    cx.ret_tuple = ov_rt
    cx.ret_str = ov_rstr
    cx.ret_sret = ov_rsr
    emit_label(sb, iend)
    push_str(sb, ":\n")
    if cstruct {
      srw := struct_words(cx.decls, cx.src, srbn.s, srbn.n, deref(cx.mar))
      mut sk := 0
      while sk < srw {
        push_str(sb, "  pushq ")
        emit_retreg(sb, sk)
        push_str(sb, "\n")
        sk += 1
      }
    } else if cfloat {
      push_str(sb, "  movq %xmm0, %rax\n  pushq %rax\n")
    } else {
      push_str(sb, "  pushq %rax\n")
    }
  }
  cx.pack_args = ov_pa
  svec_truncate(deref(cx.slots), saved)
}

pub emit_gas := fn(e : ptr(Expr), in out sb : strbuf::StrBuf, cx : ptr(LCtx), a : rt::Arena, in out nl : usize) {
  match deref(e) {
    Expr::Num(v, s, n) => {
      push_str(sb, "  movq $")
      push_int(sb, v)
      push_str(sb, ", %rax\n  pushq %rax\n")
    }
    ## a bool literal is a word-sized 0/1 constant — emit exactly like `Num` (immediate → push).
    Expr::BoolLit(v) => {
      push_str(sb, "  movq $")
      push_int(sb, v)
      push_str(sb, ", %rax\n  pushq %rax\n")
    }
    ## A FLOAT literal: load its IEEE-754 bits from the `.rodata` `.double` the assembler computed
    ## (labelled `.Lflt<start-offset>` — the source span start is a per-literal unique key, no
    ## counter needed), move them into a general register, and push (an `f64` value rides the ordinary
    ## word-sized value stack; only arithmetic/conversion reaches for the xmm regs).
    Expr::FloatLit(fss, fsl) => {
      push_str(sb, "  movsd ")
      push_lflt(sb, fss)
      push_str(sb, "(%rip), %xmm0\n  movq %xmm0, %rax\n  pushq %rax\n")
    }
    ## FN-6 — a reference to a LIFTED lambda as a value: push its code pointer `leaq
    ## <module>__lam<fnpos>(%rip)`. Calling it is the existing indirect-call path (`call *%rax`).
    Expr::FnRef(fnpos, fms, fml) => {
      push_str(sb, "  leaq ")
      emit_mangled_def(sb, cx.src, fms, fml, fnpos, 0)
      push_str(sb, "(%rip), %rax\n  pushq %rax\n")
    }
    ## An un-lifted `Lambda` — fail loud rather than silently miscompile (lift should have rewritten it).
    Expr::Lambda(fnpos, ph, rts, rtl, bh, val) => { panic("selfhost: lower — un-lifted lambda (FN-6: lambda in an unsupported expr position)") }
    ## A variable: read its frame slot at -(off + 1) * 8(%rbp), push it. An OUT-SCALAR param
    ## (ek 8, is_ref) holds a POINTER to the caller's scalar — load the pointer then deref it.
    Expr::Var(s, n) => {
      ## a bare identifier that is NOT a local/param slot AND names a FUNCTION (kind-1/4 decl) is a
      ## fn-VALUE (a function used as a value, e.g. `apply(add1, …)`): push its code pointer `leaq
      ## <module>__<fn>(%rip)` (the mangled fn label, module-resolved like a call). The parser keeps
      ## only the LAST segment of a qualified VALUE path (`hex::encode` → Var(encode)), so recover the
      ## complete path with the existing source-backed `gref_split` before resolving/emitting it. This
      ## lets a qualified value select `hex`' declaration even when a sibling module also exports an
      ## `encode`. Guarded on BOTH "not a slot" AND "is a fn decl" so a non-slot NON-fn ident (e.g. a
      ## module-level value ref) keeps the old slot-read path exactly → `src/` byte-identical → fixpoint
      ## unchanged.
      mut isfv := false
      mut isgl := false
      mut mcv : ptr(Expr) = unchecked bitcast(ptr(Expr), 0)
      fvspan := gref_split(cx.src, s, n)
      mut fvs := s
      mut fvn := n
      if fvspan.qual { fvs = fvspan.ms; fvn = fvspan.ns + fvspan.nl - fvspan.ms }
      if slot_of(cx.slots, cx.src, s, n) < 0 {
        if resolve_callee_idx(cx.decls, cx.src, fvs, fvn, cx.mod_s, cx.mod_l) >= 0 { isfv = true }
        else if is_module_mut_global(cx.decls, cx.src, s, n) { isgl = true }
        else { mcv = module_const_value(cx.decls, cx.src, s, n) }
      }
      if isfv {
        push_str(sb, "  leaq ")
        emit_mangled_call(sb, cx.src, fvs, fvn, cx.mod_s, cx.mod_l, cx.decls)
        push_str(sb, "(%rip), %rax\n  pushq %rax\n")
      } else if isgl {
        ## a MUTABLE module GLOBAL — read its current value from `.data`: `movq LABEL(%rip), %rax`.
        push_str(sb, "  movq ")
        emit_global_label(sb, cx.decls, cx.src, s, n)
        push_str(sb, "(%rip), %rax\n  pushq %rax\n")
      } else if unchecked bitcast(usize, mcv) != 0 {
        ## a module-level scalar CONSTANT — emit its compile-time value inline (no runtime storage).
        emit_gas(mcv, sb, cx, a, nl)
      } else {
        ent := deref(svec_at(SlotEntry, cx.slots, entry_of(cx.slots, cx.src, s, n)))
        ## An enum PARAM is passed by reference, so its frame word is the POINTER to the enum
        ## storage, not the discriminant itself. Scalar enum use (comparisons, arithmetic sinks,
        ## and forwarding to a scalar consumer) must read word 0 through that pointer; otherwise
        ## `fn(e : E) { e == E.C }` compares the address against the variant index and silently
        ## reports false for every variant. `emit_enum_value` already has the whole-value path for
        ## returns/matches; this is its scalar/discriminant dual. Ordinary enum locals keep the
        ## existing direct frame-word load.
        push_str(sb, "  movq -")
        push_int(sb, (ent.off + 1) * 8)
        if ent.ek == 8 or (ent.ek == 3 and ent.is_ref) {
          push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rax\n  movq (%rax), %rax\n  pushq %rax\n")
        } else {
          push_str(sb, "(%rbp), %rax\n  pushq %rax\n")
        }
      }
    }
    ## Boolean `and` (40) / `or` (41) — SHORT-CIRCUIT: the right operand is only evaluated
    ## when the left does not already decide the result. `and`: if left is false (0), the
    ## result is 0 without evaluating right; `or`: if left is true (!=0), the result is 1
    ## without evaluating right. Otherwise the result is the right operand normalized to 0/1
    ## (`cmpq $0` + `setne`). Fresh labels via `nl` so nested boolean ops never collide.
    Expr::Bin(op, l, r) => {
      if op == 40 {
        lshort := nl
        lend := nl + 1
        nl += 2
        emit_gas(l, sb, cx, a, nl)
        push_str(sb, "  popq %rax\n  cmpq $0, %rax\n  je ")
        emit_label(sb, lshort)
        push_str(sb, "\n")
        emit_gas(r, sb, cx, a, nl)
        push_str(sb, "  popq %rax\n  cmpq $0, %rax\n  setne %al\n  movzbq %al, %rax\n  jmp ")
        emit_label(sb, lend)
        push_str(sb, "\n")
        emit_label(sb, lshort)
        push_str(sb, ":\n  movq $0, %rax\n")
        emit_label(sb, lend)
        push_str(sb, ":\n  pushq %rax\n")
        return
      }
      if op == 41 {
        lshort := nl
        lend := nl + 1
        nl += 2
        emit_gas(l, sb, cx, a, nl)
        push_str(sb, "  popq %rax\n  cmpq $0, %rax\n  jne ")
        emit_label(sb, lshort)
        push_str(sb, "\n")
        emit_gas(r, sb, cx, a, nl)
        push_str(sb, "  popq %rax\n  cmpq $0, %rax\n  setne %al\n  movzbq %al, %rax\n  jmp ")
        emit_label(sb, lend)
        push_str(sb, "\n")
        emit_label(sb, lshort)
        push_str(sb, ":\n  movq $1, %rax\n")
        emit_label(sb, lend)
        push_str(sb, ":\n  pushq %rax\n")
        return
      }
      ## Boolean `not` (42): a prefix unary (`Bin(42, x, x)` — both slots are the same operand,
      ## so only the left is lowered). Lower the operand, pop it, set 1 iff it was 0 (`sete`).
      if op == 42 {
        emit_gas(l, sb, cx, a, nl)
        push_str(sb, "  popq %rax\n  cmpq $0, %rax\n  sete %al\n  movzbq %al, %rax\n  pushq %rax\n")
        return
      }
      ## §2 OPERATOR OVERLOADING — route `a <op> b` over a USER (struct/enum) type through its
      ## operator fn (`operator_decl_idx` — an `@inline` match wins, else a NON-inline operator fn
      ## falls back), expanding the body at the site (`emit_inline_binop`) with the same zero-cost
      ## lowering as a call. Built-in scalar/float/str operands fall through to the direct lowering
      ## below (so src/'s own arithmetic is unchanged → fixpoint-neutral).
      if cx.inl_tmp >= 0 {
        mut ots := expr_type_span(l, cx)
        if ots.n == 0 { ots = expr_type_span(r, cx) }
        opci := operator_decl_idx(cx.decls, cx.src, op, ots.s, ots.n, a)
        if opci >= 0 {
          ## slice B: bind the generic operator's comptime value (`N` ← the operand's value
          ## argument) around the expansion, popped after; a concrete operator pushes nothing.
          didb := op_ct_bind(cx.decls, cx.src, opci, ots.s, ots.n, a)
          emit_inline_binop(op, opci, l, r, sb, cx, a, nl)
          if didb { ct_bind_pop() }
          return
        }
      }
      ## `==` (20) / `!=` (28) on STR operands — compare CONTENT (byte-compare), not the scalar
      ## {ptr} word. Without this two equal strings at different source offsets mis-compared as
      ## unequal, breaking every `streq`-based name lookup (slot_of/entry_of/struct_decl_of) at a
      ## non-binding occurrence. `!=` is `==` negated (xor 1 over the 0/1 result).
      ## STRUCTURAL COMPARISON OF A `Slice(T)` VIEW (Stdlib §2.6, Types §6.4) — compare its CONTENT,
      ## never the `{ptr, len}` pair's IDENTITY. Three distinct silent wrong values lived here, all
      ## normal-exit (the one forbidden outcome), and all measured on the pre-fix compiler:
      ##   (1) a slice LOCAL fell to the scalar `cmpq` far below and compared WORD 0 — the DATA
      ##       POINTER. Two views over EQUAL contents at different addresses read UNEQUAL; two views
      ##       of the SAME buffer with DIFFERENT lengths read EQUAL; two EMPTY views read UNEQUAL.
      ##   (2) a slice PARAM was worse than word 0 — its slot holds a POINTER to the caller's
      ##       {ptr,len} block, so the compare answered on two BLOCK ADDRESSES (the slice twin of
      ##       `agg_cmp_param_not_address`). It also failed to BUILD: see `collect_ty_is_slice`.
      ##   (3) a slice EXPRESSION (`xs[0..3] == ys[0..3]`) was intercepted by the `str` route just
      ##       below — `is_str_operand` accepts every `Expr::Slice` — which BYTE-compares `len`
      ##       bytes. A typed slice's `len` counts ELEMENTS, so `[1,2,3]` vs `[1,9,3]` compared three
      ##       bytes of word 0 and read EQUAL.
      ## MUST precede the `str` route for case (3). `str` itself is untouched: a str local is
      ## `ek == 4` and a str sub-view has no array base, so `slice_view_cmp_kind` answers 0 for both
      ## and `emit_str_eq_core` — whose `len` really is a byte count — still owns them.
      if op == 20 or op == 24 or op == 25 or op == 26 or op == 27 or op == 28 {
        lsvk := slice_view_cmp_kind(l, cx, a)
        rsvk := slice_view_cmp_kind(r, cx, a)
        if lsvk != 0 or rsvk != 0 {
          ## ORDERING (`<`/`>`/`<=`/`>=`) over a slice is LEXICOGRAPHIC (Stdlib §2.6) and needs each
          ## element's proven SIGNEDNESS to pick its setcc — a runtime-len slice records only the
          ## element KIND, not its type. FAIL LOUD rather than keep the word-0 POINTER ordering that
          ## `a < b` silently answered.
          if op != 20 and op != 28 {
            panic("selfhost: ordering (< > <= >=) over a `Slice(T)` view is not yet supported (lexicographic order needs each element's proven signedness); compare with == / != or order the elements explicitly")
          }
          ## Not word-wise comparable (a float element — a bit compare mis-answers NaN and ±0; a
          ## struct / enum / `str` / pointer element, whose padding and unused payload words are
          ## indeterminate; a wider stride), OR a slice paired with a NON-slice operand (an array, a
          ## `str`, a scalar — a type error the lower cannot express, which silently compared two
          ## unrelated words). Every one of these fell through to the word-0 `cmpq`.
          if lsvk != 1 or rsvk != 1 {
            panic("selfhost: a bare == / != over a `Slice(T)` view is only supported between two views of SINGLE-WORD INTEGER elements (a float element needs the IEEE compare; a struct/enum/str/pointer element needs base::derive::eq; a slice compared against an array / str / scalar is not a structural comparison); compare the elements explicitly")
          }
          emit_slice_word_eq_core(l, r, sb, cx, a, nl)
          if op == 28 { push_str(sb, "  popq %rax\n  xorq $1, %rax\n  pushq %rax\n") }
          return
        }
      }
      if (op == 20 or op == 28) and is_str_operand(l, cx) {
        emit_str_eq_core(l, r, sb, cx, a, nl)
        if op == 28 { push_str(sb, "  popq %rax\n  xorq $1, %rax\n  pushq %rax\n") }
        return
      }
      ## FLOAT arithmetic (`+`/`-`/`*`/`/` over an `f64` operand) — pop the two bit patterns off the
      ## value stack into the xmm regs (right→%xmm1, left→%xmm0), apply the scalar-double op, move the
      ## result bits back to a general register and push.
      if is_float_bin_op(op) and (is_float_expr(l, cx) or is_float_expr(r, cx)) {
        emit_gas(l, sb, cx, a, nl)
        emit_gas(r, sb, cx, a, nl)
        push_str(sb, "  popq %rax\n  movq %rax, %xmm1\n  popq %rax\n  movq %rax, %xmm0\n")
        if op == 16 { push_str(sb, "  addsd %xmm1, %xmm0\n") }
        if op == 17 { push_str(sb, "  subsd %xmm1, %xmm0\n") }
        if op == 18 { push_str(sb, "  mulsd %xmm1, %xmm0\n") }
        if op == 19 { push_str(sb, "  divsd %xmm1, %xmm0\n") }
        push_str(sb, "  movq %xmm0, %rax\n  pushq %rax\n")
        return
      }
      ## FLOAT comparison (`<`/`<=`/`>`/`>=`/`==`/`!=` over an `f64` operand) — `ucomisd %xmm1, %xmm0`
      ## compares left(%xmm0) with right(%xmm1) and sets CF/ZF as for an UNSIGNED compare (left<right →
      ## CF=1), so the conditions use the unsigned set forms (`setb`/`seta`/…). NaN is UNORDERED —
      ## ucomisd then sets ZF=PF=CF=1, so the result must follow IEEE ordered semantics: every ordered
      ## relation (`<`/`<=`/`>`/`>=`/`==`) is FALSE when an operand is NaN, and `!=` is TRUE. The parity
      ## flag (PF=1 iff unordered) gates it: AND `setnp` for the ordered set, OR `setp` for `!=`.
      if (op == 20 or op == 24 or op == 25 or op == 26 or op == 27 or op == 28) and (is_float_expr(l, cx) or is_float_expr(r, cx)) {
        emit_gas(l, sb, cx, a, nl)
        emit_gas(r, sb, cx, a, nl)
        push_str(sb, "  popq %rax\n  movq %rax, %xmm1\n  popq %rax\n  movq %rax, %xmm0\n")
        push_str(sb, "  ucomisd %xmm1, %xmm0\n")
        if op == 28 {
          ## `!=` — true when not-equal OR unordered (NaN).
          push_str(sb, "  setne %al\n  setp %cl\n  orb %cl, %al\n")
        } else {
          if op == 24 { push_str(sb, "  setb %al\n") }
          if op == 26 { push_str(sb, "  setbe %al\n") }
          if op == 25 { push_str(sb, "  seta %al\n") }
          if op == 27 { push_str(sb, "  setae %al\n") }
          if op == 20 { push_str(sb, "  sete %al\n") }
          ## ordered relations are false when unordered (NaN) — AND with not-parity.
          push_str(sb, "  setnp %cl\n  andb %cl, %al\n")
        }
        push_str(sb, "  movzbq %al, %rax\n  pushq %rax\n")
        return
      }
      ## §2 item 3 — INTEGER COMPARISON via lib/base/cmp.al: route `a <cmp> b` over a built-in integer
      ## through cmp.al's `@inline` `lt`/`eq` (inlined), instead of the built-in `setl`/`setg` path. The
      ## op desugars: `==`→eq(a,b); `!=`→eq(a,b) negated; `<`→lt(a,b); `>`→lt(b,a); `<=`→lt(b,a) negated;
      ## `>=`→lt(a,b) negated. cmp.al picks the SIGNEDNESS per type (u64→`setb` unsigned, i64→`setl`
      ## signed) — so this also FIXES the built-in path's always-signed ordering of unsigned integers.
      if cx.inl_tmp >= 0 {
        mut cts := expr_type_span(l, cx)
        if cts.n == 0 { cts = expr_type_span(r, cx) }
        cci := cmp_route_idx(cx.decls, cx.src, op, cts.s, cts.n, a)
        if cci >= 0 {
          if op == 25 or op == 26 { emit_inline_binop(op, cci, r, l, sb, cx, a, nl) }   ## `>`/`<=`: swap operands
          else { emit_inline_binop(op, cci, l, r, sb, cx, a, nl) }
          if op == 28 or op == 26 or op == 27 { push_str(sb, "  popq %rax\n  xorq $1, %rax\n  pushq %rax\n") }   ## `!=`/`<=`/`>=`: negate
          return
        }
      }
      ## STRUCTURAL AGGREGATE COMPARISON (Stdlib §2.6): a bare comparison operator
      ## (`==`/`!=`/`<`/`>`/`<=`/`>=`) over a MULTI-WORD BY-VALUE AGGREGATE would otherwise reach the
      ## scalar fallthrough below — EVERY inline routing declined (no user `@inline op`, not a cmp.al
      ## built-in-integer `lt`/`eq`, not str, not float) — which materializes each operand with a SCALAR
      ## `emit_gas` + `cmpq %rbx, %rax`, comparing WORD 0 ONLY (`(5,7) == (5,9)` wrongly EQUAL). Instead,
      ## emit a CALL to the structural `base::derive::eq(T,a,b)` / `lt(T,a,b)` — the same monomorphized
      ## generic call the hashmap's `eq(existing, key)` uses (T inferred from the operand, aggregate args
      ## passed BY-REFERENCE). Map: `==`→eq; `!=`→eq negated; `<`→lt(a,b); `>`→lt(b,a); `<=`→not lt(b,a);
      ## `>=`→not lt(a,b). We reuse the ordinary generic-Call emit by SYNTHESIZING an `eq`/`lt` Call node
      ## (mirroring the UFCS desugar) so the SysV pad / by-ref arg materialization / result-push are all
      ## the proven path, and the emit label matches the mono pre-pass instance (`collect_insts_expr`'s
      ## Bin case registers the same instance). GATED to a Var operand whose SLOT is a by-value struct
      ## (`ek == 2`) or enum (`ek == 3`) of >1 word — a POINTER-to-aggregate (`ek == 7`, `while p != 0`)
      ## is a 1-word null/identity check (`agg_value_var_words` reads the SLOT kind, not the peeled type
      ## span, so it is NOT caught), and a single-word aggregate compares correctly by word 0. Requiring
      ## BOTH operands to be multi-word aggregate VARS side-steps slot TYPE-ALIASING in `src/` (a name
      ## bound as a struct in one arm but a scalar `lv == 1` in another → its right operand is non-agg →
      ## not routed). `src/`/`lib/` make no bare aggregate compare → the routing never fires there →
      ## fixpoint-neutral.
      if op == 20 or op == 24 or op == 25 or op == 26 or op == 27 or op == 28 {
        ## STRUCTURAL COMPARISON OF A BY-VALUE TUPLE / fixed-ARRAY (Stdlib §2.6, Types §6.4). A tuple
        ## parses as an `ArrayLit`, so it has no struct/enum DECL — `agg_value_var_words` reads the
        ## slot KIND (2 struct / 3 enum) and answers 0 for it, so `(5,7) == (5,9)` and `[5,7] == [5,9]`
        ## fell through to the scalar `cmpq` below and compared WORD 0 ONLY: both read EQUAL, a silent
        ## miscompile. (Exactly the same dropped fact produced the same bug on wat/a64/rv64, closed
        ## there by loud rejection because those backends have no `base::derive::eq`.) The structural
        ## `base::derive::eq` is NOT the vehicle here: its instance is keyed by a TYPE SPAN and an
        ## INFERRED tuple/array local (`a := (5, 7)`) has no type text anywhere in the source to key on
        ## — and its `_` fall-through arm would re-enter this very scalar path. For a block of
        ## SINGLE-WORD INTEGER components, componentwise equality IS word-for-word equality (the exact
        ## code the derive's unrolled per-component compare would produce), so emit it directly.
        ltw := tuple_value_var_words(l, cx)
        rtw := tuple_value_var_words(r, cx)
        if ltw > 1 or rtw > 1 {
          ## ORDERING (`<`/`>`/`<=`/`>=`) is LEXICOGRAPHIC (Stdlib §2.6) and therefore needs each
          ## component's SIGNEDNESS to pick its setcc — a scalar-element array records no element type
          ## in its slot (`snl` is the element COUNT). FAIL LOUD rather than keep a word-0 ordering.
          if op != 20 and op != 28 {
            panic("selfhost: ordering (< > <= >=) over a by-value TUPLE / ARRAY is not yet supported (lexicographic order needs each component's proven signedness); compare with == / != or order the components explicitly")
          }
          ## Mismatched widths cannot be a structural comparison at all (a type error the lower cannot
          ## express) — never emit a partial word compare.
          if ltw != rtw {
            panic("selfhost: a bare == / != over by-value TUPLE / ARRAY operands of DIFFERENT component counts is not a structural comparison")
          }
          emit_block_word_eq(sb, tuple_value_var_ref(l, cx), tuple_value_var_ref(r, cx), ltw, op == 28)
          return
        }
        ## A LOCAL fixed-array element of a plain multi-word STRUCT has a complete, stable address
        ## representation even though the expression itself is an Index rather than a Var. Compare the
        ## addressed words directly; materializing `xs[i]` as a by-reference derive argument is a
        ## different ABI path and is intentionally not used here. The emission lives in a helper so
        ## this already-large function keeps its self-hosted frame stable.
        lsw := index_plain_struct_elem_words(l, cx, a)
        rsw := index_plain_struct_elem_words(r, cx, a)
        if lsw > 1 or rsw > 1 {
          if lsw == 0 or rsw == 0 or lsw != rsw {
            panic("selfhost: a bare comparison over a multi-word aggregate array element requires two same-width local plain-struct elements")
          }
          emit_local_struct_index_eq(l, r, lsw, op == 28, sb, cx, nl)
          return
        }
        ## An INDEX read of a MULTI-WORD AGGREGATE element (`ps[0] == ps[1]` over `[P; 2]`) is a whole
        ## element, so the scalar fallthrough compares its word 0 alone — `P(1,2) == P(1,3)` read EQUAL.
        ## `base::derive::eq` is not reachable for it either: materializing `ps[i]` as a by-reference
        ## call ARGUMENT currently faults (`take(ps[0])` segfaults on its own), so routing there would
        ## trade a silent wrong value for a crash. FAIL LOUD until that materialization works.
        if index_agg_elem_words(l, cx) > 1 or index_agg_elem_words(r, cx) > 1 {
          panic("selfhost: a bare comparison over this whole multi-word AGGREGATE ARRAY ELEMENT is not yet supported (only plain scalar-field elements of concrete fixed-array params/locals are lowered); compare the fields explicitly")
        }
        ## A multi-word by-value tuple/array block that is NOT word-wise comparable (float / `str` /
        ## struct / enum / nested-tuple / pointer elements — see `tuple_value_var_block`), or paired
        ## with a non-block operand. Every one of these fell through to the word-0 `cmpq` below.
        if tuple_value_var_block(l, cx) or tuple_value_var_block(r, cx) {
          panic("selfhost: a bare comparison over a multi-word by-value TUPLE / ARRAY is only supported for SINGLE-WORD INTEGER components against another block of the same width (a str/float/struct/enum/nested-tuple component needs base::derive::eq, which has no type span to key on for an inferred tuple/array local); compare the components explicitly")
        }
        if agg_value_var_words(l, cx, a) > 1 and agg_value_var_words(r, cx, a) > 1 {
          use_lt := op == 24 or op == 25 or op == 26 or op == 27       ## ordering ops call `lt`; `==`/`!=` call `eq`
          ## ORDERING (`<`/`>`/`<=`/`>=`) over a PAYLOAD-carrying ENUM: `base::derive::lt`'s enum arm is a
          ## STATEMENT-form `comptime for v in typeinfo(T).variants { match a { … } }` whose emit is not yet
          ## implemented (the statement-form variant unroll interacts with the `comptime match typeinfo(T)`
          ## context + the >8-word `Decl`-copy landmine + nested variant matches). FAIL LOUD rather than emit
          ## an empty/garbage `lt` body → a SILENT wrong ordering. `==`/`!=` (eq) over enums work (below).
          ## `src/` makes no bare enum ordering → never fires there → fixpoint-neutral.
          if use_lt and (agg_value_var_is_enum(l, cx) or agg_value_var_is_enum(r, cx)) {
            panic("selfhost: ordering (< > <= >=) over a payload-carrying ENUM is not yet supported (base::derive::lt enum arm needs statement-form comptime-for-over-variants emit); compare with == / != or order the payload explicitly")
          }
          di := derive_cmp_decl(cx.decls, cx.src, use_lt)
          if di >= 0 {
            dd := deref(decl_get(cx.decls, usize(di)))
            ## `>` / `<=` compare `lt(b, a)` → the operands are swapped; everything else keeps `(a, b)`.
            swap := op == 25 or op == 26
            a0 := if swap { r } else { l }
            a1 := if swap { l } else { r }
            arg1 := mk_arg(deref(cx.mar), Arg(e = a1, next = 0))
            arg0 := mk_arg(deref(cx.mar), Arg(e = a0, next = arg1))
            cidx := mk_expr(deref(cx.mar), Expr.Call(dd.name_start, dd.name_len, 2, arg0))
            cptr := node_ptr(Expr, deref(cx.mar), cidx)
            emit_gas(cptr, sb, cx, a, nl)
            ## `!=` / `<=` / `>=` are the boolean NEGATION of `eq` / `lt(b,a)` / `lt(a,b)`.
            if op == 28 or op == 26 or op == 27 { push_str(sb, "  popq %rax\n  xorq $1, %rax\n  pushq %rax\n") }
            return
          }
          ## The structural derive `eq`/`lt` is not in scope (`lib/base/derive.al` not injected) — FAIL
          ## LOUD rather than fall to the word-0-only scalar `cmpq` (a silent miscompile). A user program
          ## that bare-compares aggregates gets `derive` injected (cli `needs_derive`); the self-host never
          ## reaches this gate (`src/` makes no bare aggregate compare) so this never fires there.
          panic("selfhost: a bare comparison over MULTI-WORD by-value struct/enum values needs base::derive::eq / lt in scope (compare via derive::eq(T,a,b) / lt, or ensure lib/base/derive.al is injected)")
        }
      }
      emit_gas(l, sb, cx, a, nl)
      emit_gas(r, sb, cx, a, nl)
      push_str(sb, "  popq %rbx\n  popq %rax\n")
      ## §4/I11: operand signedness + narrow-width, needed by the checked OVERFLOW guard (native-width
      ## `+`, below) AND by division signedness (op 19/29). Overflow traps at NATIVE width only; a
      ## narrow-typed op keeps its §4 value-model WRAP (the truncation below), not a trap.
      dsigned := is_signed_expr(l, cx) or is_signed_expr(r, cx)
      mut ovf_narrow := false
      if op == 16 or op == 17 or op == 18 {
        mut owt := expr_type_span(l, cx)
        if owt.n == 0 { owt = expr_type_span(r, cx) }
        if owt.n != 0 {
          obn := base_type_name(cx.src, owt.s, owt.n)
          if is_narrowing_conv(str_at((cx.src + obn.s), obn.n)) { ovf_narrow = true }
        }
      }
      ## a literal-`0` LEFT operand → negation/identity, never overflows → skip the guard (covers the
      ## `0 - N` negative/sentinel idiom pervasive in src/). Also the parser wraps unary `-x` in Unchecked.
      if expr_is_zero(l) { ovf_narrow = true }
      ## POINTER arithmetic (`ptr + int` / `ptr - ptr`) is ADDRESS arithmetic — unchecked BY TYPE (CG-8 /
      ## Memory §4.5), never an I11 integer overflow. When an operand is a pointer, skip the overflow guard,
      ## so a folded `src + off` (usize→ptr Phase 3: dropping the `rt::addr` helper) traps no more than the
      ## helper's own internal `unchecked` did. The narrow-width wrap below re-derives its own width, so a
      ## `ptr` (not a narrow int) never truncates. Detect via the operand's type-span base name `ptr`.
      if op == 16 or op == 17 {
        lpt := expr_type_span(l, cx)
        if lpt.n != 0 { lpb := base_type_name(cx.src, lpt.s, lpt.n); if str_at((cx.src + lpb.s), lpb.n) == "ptr" { ovf_narrow = true } }
        rpt := expr_type_span(r, cx)
        if rpt.n != 0 { rpb := base_type_name(cx.src, rpt.s, rpt.n); if str_at((cx.src + rpb.s), rpb.n) == "ptr" { ovf_narrow = true } }
      }
      ## A NEGATED operand (`30 + -a`) forces the SIGNED overflow check: `-a`'s runtime word is a
      ## large unsigned pattern, so the unsigned CARRY guard (`jnc`) would spuriously trap a sum that
      ## fits as a signed wrapping subtraction. `jno` sees `30 + (-5) = 25` as no signed overflow.
      gsigned := dsigned or expr_is_uneg(l) or expr_is_uneg(r)
      if op == 16 {
        push_str(sb, "  addq %rbx, %rax\n")
        ## CHECKED overflow (I11 / CG-8): trap when the native-width sum overflows — unsigned CARRY
        ## (`jnc`) or, for a signed `iN` operand, signed OVERFLOW (`jno`). Dropped under `unchecked`
        ## (cx.vchk false) and skipped for a narrow width (its wrap IS the value model). `ud2` faults.
        ## POINTER offsetting routes through `rt::addr` (unchecked), so address arithmetic never reaches here.
        if cx.vchk and (not ovf_narrow) {
          if gsigned { push_str(sb, "  jno 1f\n  ud2\n1:\n") } else { push_str(sb, "  jnc 1f\n  ud2\n1:\n") }
        }
      }
      if op == 17 {
        push_str(sb, "  subq %rbx, %rax\n")
        ## CHECKED underflow/overflow on `-` (I11 / CG-8): unsigned BORROW (`jnc`) or signed OVERFLOW
        ## (`jno`). Dropped under `unchecked`; narrow width wraps (value model). Pointer DIFFERENCE
        ## (`target - src`, the rebased-handle constructor) routes through `rt::off` (unchecked).
        if cx.vchk and (not ovf_narrow) {
          if gsigned { push_str(sb, "  jno 1f\n  ud2\n1:\n") } else { push_str(sb, "  jnc 1f\n  ud2\n1:\n") }
        }
      }
      if op == 18 {
        ## CHECKED overflow on `*` (I11 / CG-8): SIGNED via 2-operand `imulq` (sets OF on a >64-bit
        ## signed product → `jno`); UNSIGNED via 1-operand `mulq` (rdx:rax, sets CF iff the high half is
        ## nonzero → `jnc`). Dropped under `unchecked`; narrow width wraps. `imulq`/`mulq` agree on the
        ## low 64 bits. An INTENTIONAL wrapping `*` (FNV hash) is marked `unchecked` at its src site.
        if cx.vchk and (not ovf_narrow) {
          if dsigned { push_str(sb, "  imulq %rbx, %rax\n  jno 1f\n  ud2\n1:\n") } else { push_str(sb, "  mulq %rbx\n  jnc 1f\n  ud2\n1:\n") }
        } else {
          push_str(sb, "  imulq %rbx, %rax\n")
        }
      }
      ## NARROW-WIDTH WRAP (§4 value model): a built-in `+`/`-`/`*` whose operands are a narrow width
      ## (`uN`/`iN`, N < 64) must TRUNCATE the %rax result to that width — `200u8 + 100u8` is `44u8`, not
      ## the 64-bit 300. The NON-routed built-in path did not (it computed in 64 bits and only an explicit
      ## `uN(x)` conversion truncated → an untyped/typed narrow local silently held the un-wrapped value).
      ## A ROUTED width operator (§2, e.g. num.al's `addl`) truncates itself and returns before this path,
      ## so it is unaffected. Native widths (`u64`/`usize`/`i64`/`isize`) and unknown types emit nothing.
      if op == 16 or op == 17 or op == 18 {
        mut wt := expr_type_span(l, cx)
        if wt.n == 0 { wt = expr_type_span(r, cx) }
        if wt.n != 0 {
          wbn := base_type_name(cx.src, wt.s, wt.n)
          wnm := str_at((cx.src + wbn.s), wbn.n)
          if is_narrowing_conv(wnm) {
            ## CHECKED narrow-width OVERFLOW (I11 / CG-8): the 64-bit result in %rax must fit the narrow
            ## type, else TRAP — num.al's `if full > 255 { panic }`. UNSIGNED uN overflows iff any bit
            ## above bit N is set (`shr $N` nonzero); SIGNED iN overflows iff the sign-extension of the
            ## low N bits differs from the full value. Dropped under `unchecked`; skipped for a `0 - x`
            ## negation (`expr_is_zero`). The truncation below is then the `unchecked` value-model wrap.
            if cx.vchk and (not expr_is_zero(l)) {
              if wnm == "u8" { push_str(sb, "  movq %rax, %rcx\n  shrq $8, %rcx\n  jz 1f\n  ud2\n1:\n") }
              else if wnm == "u16" { push_str(sb, "  movq %rax, %rcx\n  shrq $16, %rcx\n  jz 1f\n  ud2\n1:\n") }
              else if wnm == "u32" { push_str(sb, "  movq %rax, %rcx\n  shrq $32, %rcx\n  jz 1f\n  ud2\n1:\n") }
              else if wnm == "i8" { push_str(sb, "  movsbq %al, %rcx\n  cmpq %rcx, %rax\n  je 1f\n  ud2\n1:\n") }
              else if wnm == "i16" { push_str(sb, "  movswq %ax, %rcx\n  cmpq %rcx, %rax\n  je 1f\n  ud2\n1:\n") }
              else if wnm == "i32" { push_str(sb, "  movslq %eax, %rcx\n  cmpq %rcx, %rax\n  je 1f\n  ud2\n1:\n") }
            }
            emit_int_narrow_reg(sb, wnm)
          }
        }
      }
      ## Divide `/` (19) / modulo `%` (29): pick SIGNED (`cqto; idivq`) only when an operand is a
      ## known signed `iN`; otherwise UNSIGNED (`xorq %rdx,%rdx; divq`) — the Alatyr scalar default.
      ## A `u64`/`usize` value with the high bit set (a hash) must reduce with `divq`, not `idivq`
      ## (which would treat it as negative → a negative/huge remainder → OOB index). `dsigned` is
      ## computed above (hoisted for the overflow guard).
      ## CHECKED DIVISION GUARDS (I11 / CG-13) — the whole checked-guard family fails through ONE
      ## mechanism, the direct inline trap (`ud2`), emitted BEFORE the operation. Without this the
      ## x86_64 hardware `#DE` faulted on its own and division-by-zero exited 136 (SIGFPE) while every
      ## other guard in the family exited 132 (SIGILL) — two observable failures for one guard family.
      ## Two conditions, both trapping through the same `ud2`:
      ##   (1) a ZERO divisor (both signednesses, `/` and `%`);
      ##   (2) `MIN / -1` for a SIGNED divide — the division-overflow member of the checked-overflow set.
      ## Dropped under `unchecked` (cx.vchk false → the operation takes its hardware-defined
      ## behaviour, which on x86_64 is the `#DE` fault, CG-7) and elided for a positive-literal divisor
      ## (`expr_is_pos_num`, provably neither condition). %rbx holds the fully-evaluated divisor and %rax
      ## the dividend; %rdx is dead scratch here — the divide below overwrites it (`cqto` / `xorq
      ## %rdx, %rdx`). Each `1f`/`1:` pair is self-contained (GAS resolves `1f` to the NEXT `1:`).
      if cx.vchk and (op == 19 or op == 29) and (not expr_is_pos_num(r)) {
        push_str(sb, "  testq %rbx, %rbx\n  jnz 1f\n  ud2\n1:\n")
        if dsigned { push_str(sb, "  cmpq $-1, %rbx\n  jne 1f\n  movq $-9223372036854775808, %rdx\n  cmpq %rdx, %rax\n  jne 1f\n  ud2\n1:\n") }
      }
      if op == 19 {
        if dsigned { push_str(sb, "  cqto\n  idivq %rbx\n") } else { push_str(sb, "  xorq %rdx, %rdx\n  divq %rbx\n") }
      }
      if op == 29 {
        if dsigned { push_str(sb, "  cqto\n  idivq %rbx\n  movq %rdx, %rax\n") } else { push_str(sb, "  xorq %rdx, %rdx\n  divq %rbx\n  movq %rdx, %rax\n") }
      }
      ## Bitwise `&` (34) / `|` (35) / `^` (36): the operands are already in %rax (left) /
      ## %rbx (right); apply the register-to-register form. No signedness — a pure bit op.
      if op == 34 { push_str(sb, "  andq %rbx, %rax\n") }
      if op == 35 { push_str(sb, "  orq %rbx, %rax\n") }
      if op == 36 { push_str(sb, "  xorq %rbx, %rax\n") }
      ## `^` on a NARROW operand: mask the result to the operand width (§4 value model). This matters for
      ## the `~x` desugar `x ^ (-1)` — the all-ones constant is 64-bit, so a narrow `~` would otherwise
      ## carry the high bits (`~5u8` = 0xFF…FA, not the `0xFA` = 250 that fits `u8`). A same-width `a ^ b`
      ## already fits so the mask is identity, and a native width (u64/usize/i64) masks to nothing →
      ## src/'s u64 bit-twiddling is byte-unchanged (fixpoint-neutral).
      if op == 36 {
        mut xwt := expr_type_span(l, cx)
        if xwt.n == 0 { xwt = expr_type_span(r, cx) }
        if xwt.n != 0 {
          xbn := base_type_name(cx.src, xwt.s, xwt.n)
          xnm := str_at((cx.src + xbn.s), xbn.n)
          if is_narrowing_conv(xnm) { emit_int_narrow_reg(sb, xnm) }
        }
      }
      ## Comparisons: compare %rax (left) to %rbx (right), set a 0/1 byte by the
      ## condition, zero-extend it back into %rax (signed conditions: l/g/le/ge/e/ne).
      if op == 20 { push_str(sb, "  cmpq %rbx, %rax\n  sete %al\n  movzbq %al, %rax\n") }
      ## ORDERING (`<`/`>`/`<=`/`>=`): SIGNED setcc (`setl`/…) is the DEFAULT — kept for every
      ## signed/unknown operand, so the compiler's own `isize` comparisons (which go negative) are
      ## byte-unchanged. When BOTH operands are PROVABLY unsigned (`is_unsigned_cmp`), emit the UNSIGNED
      ## setcc (`setb`/`seta`/`setbe`/`setae`) so a `u64`/`usize` comparison whose operands straddle
      ## 2^63 (`0 < u64::MAX`) is correct instead of reading the high-bit operand as negative.
      ucmp := is_unsigned_cmp(l, r, cx)
      if op == 24 { if ucmp { push_str(sb, "  cmpq %rbx, %rax\n  setb %al\n  movzbq %al, %rax\n") } else { push_str(sb, "  cmpq %rbx, %rax\n  setl %al\n  movzbq %al, %rax\n") } }
      if op == 25 { if ucmp { push_str(sb, "  cmpq %rbx, %rax\n  seta %al\n  movzbq %al, %rax\n") } else { push_str(sb, "  cmpq %rbx, %rax\n  setg %al\n  movzbq %al, %rax\n") } }
      if op == 26 { if ucmp { push_str(sb, "  cmpq %rbx, %rax\n  setbe %al\n  movzbq %al, %rax\n") } else { push_str(sb, "  cmpq %rbx, %rax\n  setle %al\n  movzbq %al, %rax\n") } }
      if op == 27 { if ucmp { push_str(sb, "  cmpq %rbx, %rax\n  setae %al\n  movzbq %al, %rax\n") } else { push_str(sb, "  cmpq %rbx, %rax\n  setge %al\n  movzbq %al, %rax\n") } }
      if op == 28 { push_str(sb, "  cmpq %rbx, %rax\n  setne %al\n  movzbq %al, %rax\n") }
      push_str(sb, "  pushq %rax\n")
    }
    ## `if cond { then } else { els }` — fresh else/end labels.
    Expr::If(c, t, f) => {
      lelse := nl
      lend := nl + 1
      nl += 2
      emit_gas(c, sb, cx, a, nl)
      ## cond on stack → pop, branch to else if it is zero (false)
      push_str(sb, "  popq %rax\n  cmpq $0, %rax\n  je ")
      emit_label(sb, lelse)
      push_str(sb, "\n")
      emit_gas(t, sb, cx, a, nl)   ## then — leaves its value on the stack
      push_str(sb, "  jmp ")
      emit_label(sb, lend)
      push_str(sb, "\n")
      emit_label(sb, lelse)
      push_str(sb, ":\n")
      emit_gas(f, sb, cx, a, nl)   ## else — leaves its value on the stack
      emit_label(sb, lend)
      push_str(sb, ":\n")
    }
    ## `loop { … }` in VALUE position (loop-as-expression, Control Flow §7.2): emit the identical loop
    ## structure as `Stmt::Loop` (top-label, body, back-edge, done-label) but mark the loop frame
    ## value-bearing (`loop_push(…, 1)`) so each `break <expr>` inside leaves its value on the stack. At
    ## the done-label exactly one value is present (every reachable exit is a value-bearing `break`) — the
    ## same stack discipline the `Expr::If` arm above relies on, so the consuming decl/assign/return pops
    ## it. A `loop` with no reachable `break` never reaches the done-label (type `Never`, §3).
    Expr::Loop(b) => {
      ltop := nl
      ldone := nl + 1
      nl += 2
      emit_label(sb, ltop)
      push_str(sb, ":\n")
      ob := cx.brk
      oc := cx.cont
      cx.brk = i64(ldone)
      cx.cont = i64(ltop)
      if cx.defer_active {
        defer_frame_push(cx)
        cx.loop_dframe[cx.loop_sp] = cx.defer_frame[cx.defer_sp - 1]
      }
      loop_push(cx, i64(ldone), i64(ltop), 1)   ## value-bearing frame
      emit_stmts(b, sb, cx, nl)
      loop_pop(cx)
      if cx.defer_active {
        emit_defer_chain(sb, cx, a, nl, cx.defer_frame[cx.defer_sp - 1])
        defer_frame_pop(cx)
      }
      cx.brk = ob
      cx.cont = oc
      push_str(sb, "  jmp ")
      emit_label(sb, ltop)
      push_str(sb, "\n")
      emit_label(sb, ldone)
      push_str(sb, ":\n")
    }
    ## `match scrut { lit => body ; … ; _ => body }` — first-match-wins. The scrutinee is
    ## lowered once and kept in %r12 (a callee-saved reg, preserved across the per-arm
    ## body emission); each arm compares %r12 to its literal and jumps to a fresh body
    ## label; each body leaves its value on the stack then jumps to a shared end label.
    Expr::Match(scrut, head) => {
      ## An enum scrutinee (an enum-typed `Var` local) dispatches on the discriminant word
      ## with payload binding — handled by `emit_enum_match`. A scrutinee that is a CALL to an
      ## enum-returning fn is STAGED first: the call leaves disc/%rax + payload/%rdx (the enum
      ## convention via `emit_enum_value`), which are stored into the reserved 2-word temp
      ## (`cx.tslot`); then `emit_enum_match` dispatches on the temp as a local. An integer
      ## scrutinee uses the value-compare path below.
      si := scrut_enum_info(scrut, cx)
      geg := try_global_enum_scrut(scrut, sb, cx)
      ## `match s.c` / `match cs[i]` over an enum FIELD / enum-array ELEMENT (value-match position):
      ## materialize the enum into the scratch, exactly as the statement-match path. Each helper emits
      ## only when it fires, and a scrutinee is at most one of global-var / field / index, so computing
      ## all three is safe (the others return an empty ScrutInfo without emitting).
      feg := try_field_enum_scrut(scrut, sb, cx)
      ieg := try_index_enum_scrut(scrut, sb, cx, nl)
      aeg := try_arrelem_field_enum_scrut(scrut, sb, cx, nl)
      if geg.is_e {
        ## `match <mutable enum GLOBAL>` — materialized into the scratch temp above; dispatch on it.
        emit_enum_match(head, geg, sb, cx, a, nl)
      } else if feg.is_e {
        emit_enum_match(head, feg, sb, cx, a, nl)
      } else if ieg.is_e {
        emit_enum_match(head, ieg, sb, cx, a, nl)
      } else if aeg.is_e {
        emit_enum_match(head, aeg, sb, cx, a, nl)
      } else if enum_ret_call(scrut, cx) {
        ## A direct call carries the RETURN DECLARATION's module even when its generic return span
        ## contains a bare type argument (`wide::fail() -> Result(u64, Error)`). Publish that owner
        ## while every operation below consumes the span: the generic enum sizer, payload resolver,
        ## and variant dispatch all recurse through the returned type's arguments. Without this
        ## scoped identity, the root call site asks for `Error` as `main::Error`, sees both sibling
        ## declarations, and takes the conservative AMBIGENUM reject. Indirect calls have no direct
        ## declaration owner and keep the existing context/located-failure behavior.
        owner_ci := call_ret_enum_target(scrut, cx)
        old_ton := lower_layout::type_ref_mod_on()
        old_tos := lower_layout::type_ref_mod_s()
        old_tol := lower_layout::type_ref_mod_l()
        mut call_owner_s := 0
        mut call_owner_l := 0
        if owner_ci >= 0 {
          owner_d := deref(decl_at(Decl, rt::vec_get(deref(cx.decls), usize(owner_ci))))
          call_owner_s = owner_d.mod_start
          call_owner_l = owner_d.mod_len
          lower_layout::set_type_ref_module(owner_d.mod_start, owner_d.mod_len, ROOT_MOD_S, ROOT_MOD_L)
        }
        direct_match_multiword_reject(scrut, cx)
        ci := call_ret_enum_span(scrut, cx)
        tbc := usize(cx.tslot) + cx.mdepth * cx.swidth + cx.swidth - 1   ## this match's scratch level
        ## the call leaves word k in `emit_retreg(k)` (disc/%rax, payload word i/%rdx..). Store EVERY
        ## word (not just disc + payload[0]) into the scratch temp — word k → slot `tbc+1-k` (the
        ## down-growing layout the local/materialize paths use) — so a nested multi-word enum payload
        ## (`R.Fail(Err.Bad(x))`) is staged whole, not truncated to its word 0 (the inner disc).
        etwc := 1 + enum_inst_words(cx.decls, cx.src, ci.s, ci.n, a)
        if etwc > tbc + 1 { panic("selfhost: match on a call returning an enum wider than the match scratch — bind the result to a local first (`t := f(…)` then `match t { … }`)") }
        if etwc >= 8 {
          ## a WIDE (disc + payload > 7-word, SRET) enum callee has NO return registers to stage from:
          ## it delivers through a hidden result POINTER. Hand it the scratch block's word-0 ADDRESS as
          ## that pointer (`cx.sret_call`, exactly as a `t := mk(…)` binding does) and it writes the whole
          ## {disc, payload…} block straight into the scratch — nothing to copy back. Without this the
          ## register loop below wired no pointer and read %rax..%r11 for a value that never rode them
          ## (a SEGFAULT / a wrong arm). `src/`+`lib/` declare no wide-enum-returning fn → dormant.
          ovmsc := cx.sret_call
          cx.sret_call = i64(tbc)
          emit_enum_value(scrut, sb, cx, a, nl)
          cx.sret_call = ovmsc
        } else {
        emit_enum_value(scrut, sb, cx, a, nl)   ## disc → %rax, payload word i → %rdx/%rcx/…
        for k in 0..etwc {
          push_str(sb, "  movq ")
          emit_retreg(sb, k)
